 that each person still has at least one like not in 'not_orderable' or one dislike in 'not_orderable'.\n\nLet's go through everyone's preferences:\n- Zac, Min, Nita, Amy like salmon but nobody dislikes it, so it's a strong candidate for 'orderable'.\n- Mia, Wei like couscous; nobody dislikes it; it's a good candidate for 'orderable'.\n- Jaya, Lan like biscuits, but Tom and Wei dislike them, but they both have other likes; it's a good choice for 'orderable'.\n- Mel, Joe, Gia, Kai like paneer, but Wei dislikes it; it's still fine for 'orderable'.\n- Raj, Aarav, Diya, Jaya, Pia, Jun, Geet like ramen, but no one who dislikes has an alternative dislike; it's in contention.\n- Om, Jaya, Leo like tacos. Rob, Geet, Tim have it as their only like, but since it's widely disliked, making it 'not_orderable' could work.\n\nIf we make 'tacos' 'not_orderable', then we satisfy those who dislike it (Sol already satisfied, and we do not conflict with any person who has only one like):\n\nOrderable: salmon, couscous, biscuits, paneer\nNot_orderable: pho, tacos\n\nReviewing all individuals' preferences:\n- Everyone who likes 'orderable' items (salmon, couscous, biscuits, paneer) is satisfied.\n- Everyone who dislikes 'not_orderable' items (pho, tacos) is satisfied.\n- Those not covered explicitly by likes must be covered by their dislikes in 'not_orderable'.\n- Since multiple people dislike ramen but have no other dislikes to satisfy their preferences, we have to accommodate by making sure it's not on the 'orderable' list.\n\nFinally, we output our solution in the required format:\n\n```python\norderable=['salmon', 'couscous', 'biscuits', 'paneer'], \nnot_orderable=['pho', 'tacos']\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 553, 'num_completion_tokens': 1028}
