 Rina. It is not universally disliked, but since it's not essential to any single person's satisfaction, it could potentially be 'not_orderable'.\n4. Pizza is a contentious item with multiple likes and dislikes.\n5. Pho is disliked by Dan, Xiu, Sam, and Ru, but liked by Gia and Yue.\n6. Ravioli is liked by many and disliked by Sky, Fay, and Mei. However, it seems essential to satisfy Kavi, Mina, Bo, Jaya, Anil, and Rina, so it should be in the 'orderable' list.\n7. Risotto is liked by quite a few but disliked only by Bo and Fay. This makes it a strong candidate for 'orderable'.\n8. Curry is liked and disliked by several people.\n9. Wonton is liked by a few but disliked by Fay.\n10. Omelette is liked by Jaya and Riya but disliked by Rina.\n\nOne effective approach is to place an item on the 'orderable' list if it is liked by someone and not disliked by anyone else or vice versa. Let's identify such items:\n\n- Ravioli is liked by Kavi, Mina, Bo, Jaya, Anil, and Rina and required to satisfy Kavi and is disliked only by Fay and Mei who can be satisfied otherwise (Fay by not ordering risotto and Mei by not ordering pizza). This makes ravioli 'orderable'.\n- Risotto is liked by Naina, Mina, Arun, Ru, and can be 'orderable' as it is essential for Ru and disliked by only Bo (who likes pizza but it's disliked by others) and Fay (who dislikes ravioli but it's essential for others).\n\nIf we make these two items 'orderable', we can now work to make everyone else satisfied:\n\n1. To satisfy Kavi, we have already included ravioli.\n2. To satisfy Naina, risotto can be included (cake isn't a good choice as it is disliked by many).\n3. To satisfy Mina, ravioli can be included.\n4. To satisfy Dan, we cannot include pizza; however, he doesn't like burger and many others do so burger can be 'not_orderable'.\n5. To satisfy Arun, we can include risotto.\n6. To satisfy Sky, ravioli should be 'orderable', and we've already planned that.\n7. To satisfy Tia, we cannot include pizza due to the dislikes, but since couscous is not essential for anyone, it can be 'not_orderable'.\n8. To satisfy Lei, pizza is 'not_orderable'.\n9. To satisfy Bo, ravioli has been included.\n10. To satisfy Mei, pizza cannot be included, but with ravioli 'orderable', she can be satisfied.\n11. To satisfy Jaya, ravioli has been included.\n12. To satisfy Riya, curry can be 'orderable'; if we don’t include burger, she is still satisfied.\n13. To satisfy Sam, cake cannot be 'orderable', but with curry 'orderable', she is still satisfied.\n14. To satisfy Ru, risotto is 'orderable', satisfying their requirement.\n15. To satisfy Anil, ravioli is 'orderable'.\n16. To satisfy Rina, ravioli is included.\n17. To satisfy Gia, ravioli is included, and given the dislike of cake by many, it's likely to be 'not_orderable'.\n18. To satisfy Fay, with ravioli 'orderable', they are satisfied.\n19. To satisfy Yue, curry can be 'orderable'.\n\nNotably, burger ends up being 'not_orderable' to satisfy Dan, Sky, and others. Given these considerations and the goal of satisfying all participants, we can keep ravioli and risotto as 'orderable', and burger, pizza, couscous as 'not_orderable'. Let's check if this fits everyone:\n\n- Dan dislikes burger ('not_orderable'), so he is satisfied with the choice.\n- All others who liked ravioli and/or risotto are satisfied due to their inclusion in 'orderable'.\n- Tia, who likes couscous and dislikes curry, isn't explicitly satisfied by these lists since couscous has to be not_orderable to satisfy others.\n- Curry is contentious but liked by some (Riya, Sam, and Yue); hence, it could be 'orderable'.\n\nIt seems we have an overriding conflict with Tia because if couscous is not_orderable, she isn't satisfied since pizza cannot be orderable due to dislikes by others. There doesn't appear to be a combination that satisfies everyone. Given this situation, we must output empty lists for both:\n\n```python\norderable=[], not_orderable=[]\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 457, 'num_completion_tokens': 1170}
