s: Ru, Wes, Meg, Joy, Raj, Lan, Liv, Leo, Ava, Rina, Tao\n   - Dislikes: Lex, Bo, Ivy, Ada, Zoe, Eva, Liz, Jim, Zac, Ben, Dan\n\n**Strategy:**\n1. The 'orderable' list must include at least one item each person likes, unless they have an item they dislike in the 'not_orderable' list.\n2. Ideally, we want to identify items that are predominantly disliked and put at least one of them in the 'not_orderable' list to satisfy many dislikers.\n\n**Analysis:**\n- Ravioli is liked by many but also disliked by many. It's a contentious item.\n- Pizza also has a lot of likes and dislikes.\n- Lobster also causes a split, but slightly fewer people dislike it.\n- Peach seems to be liked by many and only has a few dislikers.\n- Roti has a significant balance towards liking it with fewer dislikers.\n\n**Initial Thoughts:**\n- It seems that peach and roti have a higher number of people who like them and fewer dislikers, so they might be good candidates for the 'orderable' list.\n- Of the contentious items, ravioli and pizza are disliked a lot. We could potentially satisfy many individuals by placing one in 'not_orderable'.\n\nConsidering all this information, the goal is to try and satisfy every individual with a combination of likes in 'orderable' and dislikes in 'not_orderable'.\n\nLet's start to build the lists:\n- Since ravioli is a common dislike, if we put ravioli in the 'not_orderable' this will satisfy all who dislike it. Then, we need to ensure that all those disliking ravioli have something they like in the 'orderable'.\n- Peach appears to be relatively safe to put in 'orderable' because there are far fewer people who dislike it, and this could satisfy the likes of many.\n- Roti has some dislikes, but not as contentious as pizza and lobster.\n\nTrial combination:\n- not_orderable: ravioli (satisfies those who dislike it)\n- orderable: peach, roti (satisfies those who like them, assuming no conflicts)\n\nLet's review participant satisfaction with this trial combination:\n\n-