t because she doesn't like anything.\n\nStep 2: Satisfy the person with only dislikes.\n\nTo satisfy May, we could choose 'cheesecake', 'churros', or 'tandoori' for 'not_orderable'. We'll hold this decision and assess other constraints.\n\nStep 3: Avoid conflicts.\n\n- Gelato can't be 'not_orderable' because it's liked by Ping, Ian, Kavi, and Xiu.\n- Cheesecake can't be 'orderable' because it's disliked by more than one person but is liked by Sol, Sid, Xiu, and Kit.\n- Churros can't be 'orderable' because it's disliked by Sid and May but liked by Kavi, Jun, and Kai.\n- Tandoori is disliked by May but liked by several (Ping, Sid, Jun, Kai) so it can't be 'orderable'.\n- Korma is disliked by Ian and Kit but liked by Ping, Sol, and Xiu so it can't be 'orderable'.\n\nGiven these constraints and trying to satisfy everyone, let's make a decision:\n\n- Let's put 'churros' in 'not_orderable' because it is disliked by only Sid and May. Sid also dislikes churros but likes cheesecake, which has not yet been placed anywhere, so we can place 'cheesecake' in 'orderable' to satisfy Sid.\n\nSid is now satisfied with cheesecake being 'orderable' and churros being 'not_orderable'. May is satisfied with churros being 'not_orderable'.\n\nNow, let's see if we can still satisfy everyone:\n\nStep 4: Finalizing 'orderable' and 'not_orderable'\n\n- Since churros is 'not_orderable', we need to ensure that Kavi, Jun, and Kai are satisfied with other items. Kavi likes gelato, Jun and Kai like tandoori, both of which have not yet been placed, so let's add gelato and tandoori to 'orderable'.\n- We must keep gelato 'orderable' to satisfy Ping, Ian, Kavi, and Xiu.\n- For Ian, who dislikes cheesecake and korma, we can keep korma 'orderable' to satisfy him (this doesn't conflict with anything yet).\n- Sol dislikes gelato which is 'orderable', but likes korma and cheesecake. Cheesecake will be in 'orderable' to satisfy Sol.\n- Kit dislikes tandoori and korma. Tandoori has to be 'orderable' to satisfy