hich will satisfy the dislikes. Bo is the only one who likes "churros" and dislikes other items, so Bo will not be satisfied with a \'not_orderable\' "churros". However, he dislikes "ravioli" and "fries", which are liked by others, and we will need to ensure satisfaction for Bo in another way.\n\nWith "korma" in \'orderable\' and "churros" in \'not_orderable\', let\'s check if anyone is left unsatisfied:\n\n- Joy Likes "muffins", which no one dislikes, so we can place "muffins" on \'orderable\' unless someone dislikes them and has no other like, but no such person exists.\n- Ula Likes "muffins" and "ravioli", but dislikes "fries", which is liked by many. Ula is satisfied with "muffins" being \'orderable\'.\n- Kai, Leo, Fay, Amy, Taj, Ned all like "fries", but to satisfy Wes and Bo\'s dislikes, "fries" should be \'not_orderable\'. But no one only dislikes "fries" without an alternative to like, so this is fine.\n- Lee Likes "sushi", "muffins", and "ravioli". Since "muffins" can be made \'orderable\', Lee will be satisfied.\n- Wes Likes "sushi" but dislikes "ravioli" and "fries". With "fries" on \'not_orderable\', Wes will be satisfied.\n- Ava Likes "korma" and "fries" but dislikes "sushi". \'Orderable\' "korma" satisfies her.\n- Nita Likes "korma" and "ravioli", dislikes "fries". She’s satisfied with \'orderable\' "korma".\n- Bo Likes "churros", dislikes "ravioli" and "fries". To be satisfied, we cannot place both "fries" and "ravioli" on \'orderable\'; since we need to satisfy Bo\'s dislike, we put either “ravioli” or “fries” in \'not_orderable\'. However, placing "ravioli" in \'not_orderable\' would leave Ula unsatisfied, so we must place "fries" in \'not_orderable\'.\n- Gia Likes "korma" and "churros", dislikes "ravioli". She’s satisfied with \'orderable\' "korma".\n- Sue Likes "korma" and "muffins", dislikes "churros". \'Orderable\' "korma" and "muffins" satisfy her.\n- Li Likes "churros" and "sushi", dislikes "fries". \'Not_orderable\' "fries" satisfies her.\n\n