f placed on the 'not_orderable' list, individuals who like pho but do not have any other likes would be unsatisfied. Conversely, if placed on the 'orderable' list, those who dislike pho and have no other dislikes would be unsatisfied.\n   \n2. Since biscuits are liked and disliked by some, we cannot immediately place them into a list without conflict—similar analysis applies to the other food items.\n\nLet's start by grouping people based on conflicts and attempting to place foods with unanimous dislike or like into corresponding lists.\n\nStarting with pho, we notice a conflict. If pho is placed on the 'orderable' list, Zac will be satisfied, but Mina, Sam, Eva, Eli, Rohan, Jon, Chao, Jim, Val, Zan, Jaya, and Aarav will be unsatisfied. If pho is placed on the 'not_orderable' list, it would satisfy Mina, Sam, Eva, Eli, Rohan, Jon, Chao, Jim, Val, Zan, Jaya, and Aarav but leave Zac, Wei, Kim, and Neil potentially unsatisfied—all of whom like pho but also like another item.\n\nSince all individuals who dislike pho also dislike another item, placing pho on the 'orderable' list would satisfy all individuals who dislike pho (because their other dislikes can be placed in the 'not_orderable' list). Consequently, individuals who like pho will also be satisfied. \n\nThis analysis suggests pho should be placed on the 'orderable' list.\n\nNow we will analyze the other items:\n- Macaron is disliked by four individuals (Sam, Arun, Chao, and Jaya) and liked by several. We cannot satisfy all individuals if macaron is in 'orderable' since Sam and Arun dislike it but do not share a common like. The macaron must go into 'not_orderable'.\n- Biscuits are disliked by Mina and Sam but liked by Eva, Arun, Kim, Rohan, Jon, Cal, Riya, and Neil. Since Eva and Kim like both biscuits and macaron, and Arun, who dislikes macaron and likes biscuits, would be satisfied with biscuits being on 'orderable', we can add biscuits to 'orderable'.\n- Guacamole is liked by Zac, Arun, Kim, Jim, Isha, Neil, and Riya and is not disliked by anyone. We can put guacamole on the 'orderable' list.\n- Brownies are liked by several, including Zac, Wei, Cal, Val, Zan, Ava, Neil, and not disliked by anyone. We can also add brownies to the 'orderable' list.\n- Risotto is liked by Eli, Val, Ava, and Isha but disliked by Wei, Rohan, Jim, Zan, Chao, and Aarav. Since we need to satisfy their dislikes as well and nobody who dislikes risotto has a like intersecting with the orderable list, risotto should go into the 'not_orderable' list.\n- Soup is liked by Jon, Ava, Isha, and Jaya, and disliked by no one, making it suitable for 'orderable'.\n\nNow, let's compile the lists:\n\n- 'orderable': pho, guacamole, brownies, biscuits, soup\n- 'not_orderable': macaron, risotto\n\n### Final Solution:\nEvery individual is satisfied with at least one item they like in the 'orderable' list or one item they dislike in the 'not_orderable' list.\n\n```python\norderable=['pho', 'guacamole', 'brownies', 'biscuits', 'soup'], not_orderable=['macaron', 'risotto']\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 474, 'num_completion_tokens': 917}
