cakes.\n- Raj likes caramel, ravioli, biscuits.\n- Leo likes ravioli, biscuits.\n- Kim likes biscuits.\n- Zara likes biscuits, roti.\n- Nic likes biscuits.\n\nSince we have a mix of likes and some items like caramel and ravioli are liked by people who dislike what others like, it\'s challenging to find an item to put into \'orderable\' without clashing with the \'not_orderable\' list.\n\nGiven the constraint that all participants must be satisfied by the combination of the two lists, and recognizing that almost all foods are both liked and disliked by different individuals, it seems impossible to fulfill these conditions. No single food item or combination thereof will satisfy every person without conflict.\n\nTherefore, based on the rules and the complexity and interdependence of likes and dislikes among the group, the only logical outcome is that no such combination exists that satisfies everyone. Thus, we must output empty lists for both \'orderable\' and \'not_orderable\':\n\n```python\norderable = []\nnot_orderable = []\n```', 'num_prompt_tokens': 1194, 'num_completion_tokens': 1558}
