 are disliked by Jay, Geet, Rina, Liz, Qi, and Jun.\n- "pho" is liked by Lei, Lou, Val, and Qi, but is disliked by Fay, Aarav, and Diya.\n- "couscous" is liked by Bea, Eva, Veer, and Jim, but is disliked by Om, Eli, Rina, Feng, and Sal.\n\nNow, we need to find which items can be placed on the "orderable" list and which on the "not_orderable" list in order to satisfy everyone\'s preferences. The first step is to check if there are any items that everyone who likes them has something else they like that isn\'t as conflicting or if there is an item disliked by someone that no one else likes.\n\nFor example, consider the item with the most dislikes: "guacamole" has the most dislikes. If we place it on the "not_orderable" list, we will satisfy many people right away. But we have to check if there is at least one other item for each person who likes "guacamole" that can be put on the "orderable" list.\n\n- Mia likes ramen, which is disliked by fewer people than guacamole.\n- Lei likes waffles and pho, both are problematic but waffles might work since pho has fewer dislikers.\n- Sid dislikes tiramisu which is liked by fewer people than those who dislike guacamole.\n- Arun dislikes tiramisu as well, but also has lasagna, which is liked by fewer.\n- Bo likes fries, which is conflicting but less than guacamole.\n- Yan is the same as Mia, can be satisfied with frittata.\n- Vic dislikes lasagna, which is less conflicting.\n\nWe can put "guacamole" on the "not_orderable" list, satisfying many who dislike it. Then, for each person who likes guacamole, we try to find an alternative item to place on the "orderable" list or ensure that their disliked items appear on the "not_orderable" list:\n\n- "ramen" can be "orderable" for Mia.\n- "waffles" can be "orderable" for Lei.\n- "tiramisu" is disliked by Sid and can be "not_orderable."\n- "lasagna" can be "orderable" for Arun.\n- "fries" can be "orderable" for Bo.\n- "frittata" can be "orderable" for Yan.\n- "lasagna" can be "not_orderable" for Vic.\n\nConsidering this information, we\'ll proceed with satisfying the individual needs:\n\nSince "guacamole" is on the "not_orderable" list:\n- "ramen" must be "orderable" to satisfy Mia.\n- "waffles" must be "orderable" to satisfy Lei.\n- "tiramisu" must be "not_orderable" to satisfy Sid.\n- "lasagna" must be "orderable" to satisfy Arun.\n- "fries" must be "orderable" to satisfy Bo.\n- "frittata" must be "orderable" to satisfy Yan.\n- "lasagna" must be "not_orderable" to satisfy Vic, but this creates a conflict with Arun\'s satisfaction, hence the setup is becoming unworkable.\n\nGiven this issue, let\'s backtrack and reconsider an alternative approach. With the conflict identified between the requirements of Arun and Vic, we can see that "lasagna" cannot be placed on both "orderable" and "not_orderable" lists.\n\nWe need a different angle, possibly starting with another food item that is heavily disliked with fewer likes. Let\'s check on "frittata" as the next possible item based on dislikes against likes.\n\nIf we place "frittata" on the "not_orderable" list:\n- It satisfies Fay, Dan, Lee, Liz, Joe, Eli, and Aarav right off the bat who all dislike it.\n\nThose who like "frittata" but need satisfaction through other items are:\n- Om can be satisfied with "couscous," since he dislikes guacamole which will probably go on the "not_orderable" list.\n- Bea can be satisfied with "couscous" for the same reasons as Om.\n- Lou likes "fries" and "pho," which are less conflicting than frittata.\n- Eva likes "couscous," which is less conflicting.\n- Amy likes shawarma, which is conflicted but seems possible to satisfy.\n- Feng can also be satisfied by "shawarma."\n- Jim likes "fries," which can be potentially placed on the "orderable" list.\n\nThis seems promising, so let\'s proceed with "frittata" on the "not_orderable" list:\n\nWith "guacamole" likely heading to "not_orderable", "frittata" already decided, we can consider the following:\n\n- Om, Bea, and Eva can