slike it, and it would not be wise to add it to \'orderable\' given the heavy dislikes.\n6. "muffins" are liked by Lei, Om, Jay, Pat, Jim, and Jaya but disliked by Chao, Qi, and Tia. However, Chao likes "vindaloo" which we are avoiding, Qi dislikes "ravioli" which we are not ordering, and Tia dislikes "peach" which we are also avoiding. Hence, "muffins" can be added to \'orderable\', satisfying Lei, Om, Jay, Pat, Jim, and Jaya.\n7. "bread" is liked by Om, Chao, and Qi but disliked by Mia, Eli, Tim, and Jan. Since we have already satisfied Om through "muffins" and Chao and Qi through not adding "ravioli", we avoid adding "bread" to cater to dislikes.\n8. "burrito" should be in \'not_orderable\' because many who dislike it, such as Xiu, Jay, Lin, Ula, and potentially others, aren\'t easily satisfied by other likes, and it\'s less prominent in the likes. \n\nFinalizing the lists based on constraints and maximum satisfaction:\n\n- \'orderable\': ["shawarma", "couscous", "sushi", "muffins"]\n- \'not_orderable\': ["ravioli", "burrito"]\n\nI\'ll output the lists in the requested Python format:\n\n```python\norderable=["shawarma", "couscous", "sushi", "muffins"], not_orderable=["ravioli", "burrito"]\n```', 'num_prompt_tokens': 611, 'num_completion_tokens': 1836}
