 Net likes: 10 - 4 = +6\n\nBased on these tallies, we can make some initial decisions:\n\n1. Salmon is disliked by more people (9) than those who like it (5), so it\'s a good candidate for the \'not_orderable\' list. Also, everyone who likes salmon also likes at least one of cheese or poke, so they would still be satisfied if salmon is on the \'not_orderable\' list.\n   \n2. Tart is liked by the most number of people (10) with fewer dislikes (4). Each person who dislikes tart likes at least one other item. Hence, we can tentatively put tart on the \'orderable\' list.\n\n3. Cheese has a positive net like but has a significant number of dislikes. However, everyone who dislikes cheese likes either tart or salmon, so they would be satisfied with them being in the \'orderable\' or \'not_orderable\' lists respectively.\n\n4. Poke has a negative net like, and more people dislike it than like it. Also, everyone who likes poke likes at least one other item, so they would be satisfied if poke is on the \'not_orderable\' list.\n\nGiven these considerations, we can tentatively arrange the lists as follows:\n- \'orderable\': tart, cheese\n- \'not_orderable\': salmon, poke\n\nNow, let\'s see if all individuals are satisfied with these tentative lists:\n\n- Everyone who likes tart and has it on their likes list will be satisfied since it\'s on the \'orderable\' list.\n- Everyone who dislikes salmon will be satisfied with it on the \'not_orderable\' list.\n- The same goes for people who dislike poke with it being on the \'not_orderable\' list.\n- Everyone who likes cheese will be satisfied since it\'s on the \'orderable\' list.\n- And since individuals who dislike cheese like either tart or salmon, they would be satisfied as well.\n\nFinally, it looks like every individual\'s preferences are satisfied with the tentative arrangement we have made. Let\'s confirm by checking for any unsatisfied individuals.\n\nUpon careful review of each person:\n- Ned, Isha, Nic, Shan, Chen, Zac, Amy