ce they are single instances\n\nDislikes:\n- Tandoori: Jun, Kai, Arun, Meg\n- Omelette: Jun, Isha, Lan, Meg, Lee\n- Naan: Aarav, Ula, Pam, Kai, Yue\n- Pizza: Aarav, Isha, Ula, Nita\n- Hummus: Ru, Sue, Nita\n- Ravioli: Kai, Neil, Sue, Cal, Yue\n- Custard: Xan\n\nWe must ensure the 'orderable' list must contain at least one item liked by each individual or the 'not_orderable' list must contain at least one item disliked by each individual. No item can be in both lists, and there can be no conflicts between likes and dislikes for the items in the orderable list.\n\nWe can start by selecting items that are liked by some but disliked by no one. However, such items may not exist due to the complex interlink of likes and dislikes.\n\nSince ravioli is liked by the most people, we try to add it to 'orderable'. Then we need to make sure people who dislike ravioli (Kai, Neil, Sue, Cal, Yue) have something they dislike on the 'not_orderable' list or something they like on the 'orderable' list:\n\n- Kai dislikes naan and tandoori, but disliking more than one item doesn't help satisfy the condition.\n- Neil dislikes ravioli, but likes pizza and omelette.\n- Sue dislikes ravioli but likes custard.\n- Cal dislikes ravioli but likes hummus and naan.\n- Yue dislikes ravioli but likes naan.\n\nFrom our entries, it seems like naan could be a candidate for the 'orderable' list as it is liked by Cal and Yue who dislike ravioli. But we need to check others who dislike naan which are Aarav, Ula, Pam, Kai and Yue:\n\n- Aarav dislikes naan but likes omelette.\n- Ula dislikes naan but likes omelette.\n- Pam dislikes naan but likes pizza and hummus.\n- Kai dislike naan, and we don't have any other of their liked items to add to 'orderable'. So we have a conflict here.\n- Yue, however, is already satisfied by ravioli in the 'orderable' list.\n\nSo, there's a conflict for Kai, which leads us to explore other options. However, with so many interdependencies, it is clear that there is no single food item liked by some and disliked by no one. We therefore have to find items for the 'not_orderable' list that can satisfy as many people as possible.\n\nLet review and check dislikes and see if there is item that is disliked by everyone:\n\n- Tandoori is not a good candidate as it is liked by too many.\n- Omelette is liked by too many.\n- Naan is not possible due to the previous analysis (Kai presents a conflict).\n- Pizza has the same issue, as it's liked by several people.\n- Hummus is liked by several people.\n- Ravioli is liked by too many.\n- Custard is liked by several and only one person is against it.\n\nIn summary, none of the items can exclusively be placed in 'orderable' or 'not_orderable' to satisfy everyone based on the given preferences. We need to have one item that is liked by some but not disliked by anyone in the 'orderable' list to assure everyone will be satisfied, and such item does not exist here. Similarly, there is no single item that is disliked by everyone and not liked by someone to place in the 'not_orderable' list.\n\nTherefore, based on the set preferences, it is impossible to create two distinct lists that satisfy all the criteria given. As a result, the solution must be:\n\n```\norderable=[], not_orderable=[]\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 512, 'num_completion_tokens': 992}
