ody else as well, which also makes "poke" and "biscuits" safe for \'orderable\' since Leo will be satisfied with "peach" in \'not_orderable\'.\n\nHowever, putting "naan" in \'orderable\' will make Ping dissatisfied, and \'orderable\' would satisfy everyone else\'s preferences. So "naan" must also go into the \'not_orderable\' list.\n\nNow we\'ve made sure that everyone will be satisfied with our choices. Let\'s output our final solution:\n\n```python\norderable = [\'poke\', \'biscuits\']\nnot_orderable = [\'couscous\', \'peach\', \'naan\']\n```\n\nEvery person in our list is now satisfied because each person has at least one liked item in the \'orderable\' list or one disliked item in the \'not_orderable\' list.', 'num_prompt_tokens': 708, 'num_completion_tokens': 605}
