slike the \'orderable\' items by putting their dislikes into \'not_orderable\'. Lee, Li, and Ned all dislike "shrimps." Lee and Li also dislike "sorbet," as do many others, so we shall add \'sorbet\' to \'not_orderable\':\n- not_orderable: sorbet\n\nLet\'s continue and evaluate the remaining food items:\n- soup: Since it\'s liked by 14 participants and disliked by 9, if we put "soup" in \'not_orderable,\' those 14 need to have another like in \'orderable\' or a dislike in \'not_orderable.\' However, Qi, Raj, Ned, and Ru dislike "soup" and have no other dislikes to be satisfied with, so "soup" must be put into \'orderable.\' \n- orderable: shrimps, soup\n\nNext items to consider:\n- tart: Disliked by a large number (14). However, Neal, Jan, Qi, Raj, Ian, Oli, Ansh, Yue, Sid, Sia all like "tart" and would not be satisfied if it were in \'not_orderable\' unless they also have another item they like, which is now impossible because "shrimps" and "soup" are their only other likes and those are already in \'orderable\'. Therefore, "tart" cannot be placed in \'not_orderable.\'\n- tacos: Similarly, if we put "tacos" in \'not_orderable\', people like Jaya, Taj, Lin, Kai, Liz, Max, and Jun would not be satisfied unless they like "shrimps" or "soup," which isn\'t the case for all of them. So, "tacos" must also be in \'orderable\'.\n- orderable: shrimps, soup, tacos\n\nAt this point it appears everyone\'s likes can be satisfied with "shrimps," "soup," and "tacos" in the \'orderable\' list, and "sorbet" in the \'not_orderable\' list. Since "tart" is the last item and a good number of people who dislike "soup" and "tacos" would be dissatisfied if "tart" was in \'not_orderable\', we can also add "tart" to \'orderable\' to satisfy them:\n- orderable: shrimps, soup, tacos, tart\n- not_orderable: sorbet\n\nOne last check:\n- Every person who likes "shrimps," "soup," "tacos," or "tart" is satisfied because at least one of their liked items is in \'orderable\'.\n- Those who dislike "sorbet" are satisfied because "sorbet" is in \'not_orderable\'.\n\nAfter this thorough assessment, the final lists are:\norderable=[shrimps, soup, tacos, tart], not_orderable=[sorbet]', 'num_prompt_tokens': 702, 'num_completion_tokens': 1007}
Data Sample: {'num_vars': 7, 'num_clauses': 14, 'formula': [[3, -7, -4], [-4, -2, -1], [-6, -2, 7], [5, -3, 1], [5, -1, 6], [-1, 7, 5], [7, 4, -6], [5, -2, -1], [5, -4, -3], [1, 4, 5], [2, 7, 4], [2, 3, 4], [1, -2, 6], [5, 6, 4]], 'is_sat': True, 'preferences': 'Lee: Likes curry. Dislikes hummus, waffles. Pam: Dislikes waffles, wonton, peach. Wes: Likes hummus. Dislikes espresso, wonton. Raj: Likes kebab, peach. Dislikes curry. Ben: Likes kebab, espresso. Dislikes peach. Xiu: Likes hummus, kebab. Dislikes peach. Mei: Likes hummus, waffles. Dislikes espresso. Lin: Likes kebab. Dislikes wonton, peach. Tim: Likes kebab. Dislikes waffles, curry. Eva: Likes peach, waffles, kebab. Cal: Likes wonton, hummus, waffles. Ansh: Likes wonton, curry, waffles. Tara: Likes peach, espresso. Dislikes wonton. Lia: Likes kebab, espresso, waffles. ', 'menu_items': ['peach', 'wonton', 'curry', 'waffles', 'kebab', 'espresso', 'hummus'], 'gpt_out': "We'll need to go through these preferences step by step to identify which food items can be placed in the 'orderable' list and which in the 'not_orderable' list, ensuring that each person will be satisfied based on the criteria.\n\nFirst, we should identify any food items that are liked by some and disliked by others, because these items can directly satisfy or dissatisfy certain individuals depending on which list they're placed in. These conflicting foods might require some balancing. Also, foods that are universally liked or disliked could directly go into the 'orderable' and 'not_orderable' lists, respectively.\n\nLet's go through everyone's preferences, looking for:\n\n1. Foods that are only liked, never disliked - These can be safe to add to 'orderable'.\n2. Foods that are only disliked, never liked - These can be safe to add to 'not_orderable'.\n3. Foods that are liked by some and disliked by others - These will need careful consideration.\n\nAfter evaluating each person's preferences:\n\n**Likes only, no dislikes:**\n\n- There are no foods that are liked and not disliked by anyone.\n\n**Dislikes only, no likes:**\n\n- There are no foods that are disliked and not liked by anyone.\n\n**Conflicting Foods (liked by some, disliked by others):**\n\n- Curry: Liked by Lee, Ansh. Disliked by Raj, Tim.\n- Hummus: Liked by Wes, Xiu, Mei, Cal. Disliked by Lee.\n- Waffles: Liked by Mei, Eva, Cal, Ansh, Lia. Disliked by Lee, Pam, Tim.\n- Wonton: Liked by Pam, Wes, Cal, Ansh. Disliked by Lin, Tara.\n- Peach: Liked by Pam, Raj, Eva, Tara. Disliked by Xiu, Lin.\n- Kebab: Liked by Raj, Ben, Xiu, Lin, Tim, Eva, Lia. Disliked by no one.\n- Espresso: Liked by Wes, Ben, Tara, Lia. Disliked by Mei.\n\nSince there are no foods universally liked without dislike (and vice versa), we will have to look at the conflicting foods and make choices that satisfy as many individuals as possible based on their likes and dislikes.\n\nTo start our combination, we can safely add 'kebab' to the 'orderable' list, as all participants who mentioned 'kebab' liked it and no one disliked it. This will satisfy Raj, Ben, Xiu, Lin, Tim, Eva, and Lia.\n\nNow, for the remaining items, we have to find a way to satisfy each person. Let's try to find a solution that minimizes conflicts:\n\n- Wes likes hummus and dislikes espresso. Mei likes both hummus and waffles and dislikes espresso. Since espresso can't satisfy both Wes and Mei, we'll add hummus to the 'orderable' list to satisfy them, and we'll add espresso to the 'not_orderable' list since it has more dislikes (Wes and Mei) than likes (Ben, Tara, Lia).\n- Tara likes peach and espresso, and dislikes wonton. We already have espresso in the 'not_orderable' list, so we need to satisfy Tara with peach. We'll add peach to the 'orderable' list. This satisfies Raj, Eva, and Tara, although it will not satisfy Xiu and Lin, but they are already satisfied with kebab.\n- Lee likes curry, and dislikes hummus and waffles. We can't add curry to the 'orderable' list without unsatisfying Raj and Tim, but since we plan to add waffles to the 'not_orderable' list based on Lee's dislike and Pam's, this will also satisfy Lee.\n- Wonton is liked by Ansh and Cal but disliked by Lin and Tara. Since Tara is already satisfied by peach, we could add wonton to the 'orderable' list to satisfy Ansh and Cal.\n- Waffles must go into the 'not_orderable' list because more people (Lee, Pam, Tim) dislike them than those who like them (Mei, Eva, Cal, Ansh, Lia).\n\nOur lists will be:\n\norderable = [kebab, hummus, peach, wonton]\nnot_orderable = [espresso, waffles]\n\nThis selection will satisfy all individuals since:\n\n- Everyone who likes kebab is satisfied (Raj, Ben, Xiu, Lin, Tim, Eva, Lia)\n- Lee is satisfied (does not receive hummus or waffles)\n- Pam is satisfied by not getting waffles (since no one likes waffles exclusively, it's safe to put them in 'not_orderable')\n- Wes is satisfied with hummus (does not get espresso)\n- Raj is satisfied with kebab (does not get curry)\n- Ben gets kebab (and is okay with not getting espresso)\n- Xiu gets both kebab and hummus (and is okay with not getting peach)\n- Mei is satisfied with hummus (does not get espresso)\n- Lin is satisfied with kebab (despite the wonton)\n- Tim gets kebab and does not get waffles or curry (satisfying his condition)\n- Eva gets peach and kebab (and does not get waffles)\n- Cal gets hummus and wonton (and does not get waffles)\n- Ansh gets wonton and curry, but maybe not waffles (which is fine as only waffles disliked are in 'not_orderable')\n- Tara gets peach but not wonton (which is disliked)\n- Lia gets kebab and does not get espresso (which is disliked)\n\nBased on these considerations, we have found a viable solution that satisfies the criteria for the entire group. Here is the final solution in Python code format:\n\n```python\norderable = ['kebab', 'hummus', 'peach', 'wonton']\nnot_orderable = ['espresso', 'waffles']\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 365, 'num_completion_tokens': 1228}
