action by having 'espresso' in 'orderable'. \n2. Conversely, anyone disliking 'espresso' but liking 'sorbet' (e.g., Taj) will be satisfied with 'sorbet' on the 'orderable' list.\n3. Those who dislike both 'sorbet' and 'espresso' (e.g., Bo) need another item they dislike on the 'not_orderable' list, or they will not be satisfied.\n4. For those who like 'bruschetta', but dislike 'espresso', we can't satisfy them if 'espresso' is 'orderable', so 'bruschetta' must be on 'not_orderable'.\n5. For those liking 'tart' and disliking 'espresso', we can't satisfy them if 'espresso' is 'orderable', so 'tart' must be on 'not_orderable'.\n\nLet's try placing 'espresso' on the 'orderable' list and see if we can satisfy everyone:\n\n- 'Espresso' on the 'orderable' list: This satisfies Jun, Fay, Ty, Hui, Dan, Tara, Kavi, Ansh, Raj, Bao, and more.\n- 'Sorbet' on the 'orderable' list: This satisfies Ping, Jun, Ty, Hui, Taj, Ravi, May, Rob, Ivy, Val, Pat, Raj.\n\nNow, we need to make sure that Bo, Fay, Chen, Xan, Rohan, Zac, Kavi, Veer, and others who dislike 'sorbet' are satisfied by placing one of their disliked items on the 'not_orderable' list.\n\nSince 'espresso' is already on the 'orderable' list, 'tart' or 'bruschetta' must be on the 'not_orderable' list to satisfy people who dislike 'sorbet'. \n\nBased on Maslow's approach, let's address more critical dislikes first:\n\n- Bo, Xan, Zac, Nita, and Yue dislike 'bruschetta' and 'sorbet', so they need 'tart' on 'not_orderable' list to be satisfied.\n- Fay, Chen, Rohan, Dan, Val, and others dislike 'sorbet' but like 'tart', meaning they can not be satisfied with 'tart' on 'not_orderable', hence putting 'bruschetta' there would satisfy them.\n\nWith this in mind, everyone who dislikes 'sorbet' but likes 'espresso' is already satisfied because 'espresso' is on the 'orderable' list. So, our strategy should be to put 'bruschetta' on the 'not_orderable' list.\n\nLet's summarize the potential lists:\n\n- Orderable: 'espresso', 'sorbet'\n- 