ed.\n- Baklava is liked by the majority of the group.\n\nTo satisfy the most people, we could try putting ravioli and paneer on the 'orderable' list because they are liked by most and disliked by fewer.\n\nIf we put shrimps and vindaloo on the 'not_orderable' list, we cover the dislikes of many individuals too.\n\nThe controversial items here will be baklava and ravioli based on dislikes. However, since so many people like baklava, and fewer dislike it compared to ravioli, it seems safe to place baklava in the 'orderable' list.\n\nLet's test this configuration:\n- 'orderable': ravioli, paneer, baklava.\n- 'not_orderable': shrimps, vindaloo.\n\nWith ravioli, paneer, and baklava on the 'orderable' list, all individuals who like at least one of these items should be satisfied unless their dislikes include all three items (which does not appear to be the case).\n\nWith shrimps and vindaloo on the 'not_orderable' list, all individuals who dislike at least one of these items and do not have any satisfied likes among the 'orderable' items should be satisfied.\n\nProceeding with cross-checking:\n\n- Everyone who likes shrimps has another item they like in the 'orderable' list.\n- Those who like vindaloo either have an alternative they like on the 'orderable' list or have shrimps, which they dislike on the 'not_orderable' list.\n- Everyone who likes ravioli, paneer, or baklava, which are on the 'orderable' list, should be satisfied.\n\nCross-checking the dislikes and boundary cases, we can be confident that this list should satisfy everyone:\n\n- People who dislike vindaloo and shrimps should be satisfied as they are not in the 'orderable' list.\n- The individuals who dislike ravioli, paneer, or baklava can be satisfied because their dislikes are catered by the 'not_orderable' list, or they like at least one of the other items on the 'orderable' list.\n\nFinal output:\n\n```python\norderable=['ravioli', 'paneer', 'baklava'], not_orderable=['shrimps', 'vindaloo']\n```", 'num_