t accordingly if needed.\n\n- **Vegetables** are liked by many but disliked by a few. If we put vegetables in the 'orderable' list, Mia, Sal, and Bea won't be satisfied since they dislike vegetables.\n\n- **Toast** is also controversial, disliked by several members but liked by a few, so it would probably best fit in the 'not_orderable' list, satisfying those who dislike it.\n\nNext, we attempt to build the lists while trying to satisfy everyone. Here's one possible approach:\n\n1. Include sorbet in the 'orderable' list since it's liked by many.\n2. Include toast in the 'not_orderable' list since it's disliked by some and not overwhelmingly liked.\n\nNow we need to find at least one more item that can be included in the 'orderable' list to satisfy members who dislike sorbet or do not have mouse items liked yet.\n\n3. Kebab can be included in the 'orderable' list since it's liked by a significant number and disliked only by Mia, who can be satisfied by having vegetables in the 'not_orderable' list.\n\n4. Mia, Zac, Min, and Bea are left to be satisfied. Mia and Zac can be satisfied by placing vegetables in the 'not_orderable' list. Min and Bea can be satisfied with the presence of sorbet and toast, respectively, in the appropriate list, as they have already been placed.\n\n5. Now, everyone who likes kebab and sorbet is satisfied. Those who dislike toast are satisfied since it's in the 'not_orderable' list. Mia and Zac are also satisfied with vegetables in the 'not_orderable' list.\n\n6. Ratatouille must be checked next. It is liked by a few individuals and disliked by others. It cannot be placed in the 'orderable' list as this would dissatisfy Jon, Jim, and Ivy. Placing it in the 'not_orderable' list satisfies Jon and Jim but leaves Shan, Ana, and Hu unsatisfied, since they have no liked items in the 'orderable' list yet. Therefore, ratatouille shouldn't be on any list presently.\n\nSince there are no further conflicts, and this selection satisfies everyone's likes and dislikes, we have our final lists:\n\n- 'orderable' includes kebab and sorbet.\n- 'not_orderable' includes vegetables and toast.\n\nHere is the final output in Python syntax:\n\n```python\norderable=['kebab', 'sorbet'], not_orderable=['vegetables', 'toast']\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 388, 'num_completion_tokens': 791}
