eer, Om, Mina, Rina, Tara, Chao\n- Caramel: May, Gia, Mia, Aarav, Abe, Gus, Tim, Mina, Chao\n\nDislikes:\n- Roti: Lei, Joe, Kit, Nic, Naina, Aarav, Jan, Sol, Pam, Pia, Tim, Ping\n- Sorbet: Ula, Nic, Mia, Aarav, Sol, Eli, Ru, Pam, Cy, Hu, Ping\n- Peach: Ula, Lei, Naina, Jan, Hu, Geet, Pam, Pia\n- Soup: Lei, Veer, Gia, Jan, Om, Abe, Eli, Gus, Geet, Pia\n- Caramel: May, Veer, Nic, Ru, Ping, Chao, Hu, Geet\n\nNow, we need to find a combination of 'orderable' and 'not_orderable' lists so that each person can be satisfied.\n\nWe can start by finding out which items are mutually exclusive—those items that cannot be on the same list to satisfy someone’s like and someone else’s dislike:\n\n- Roti is liked by some and disliked by many (cannot be together on the same list with anything else).\n- Soup is liked and disliked by some (cannot be together on the same list with anything else).\n- Peach is liked by many and disliked by some (cannot be on the same list as roti or soup).\n- Caramel is liked and disliked by some (cannot be together on the same list with anything else).\n- Sorbet is liked by a significant number but disliked by others (cannot be together on the same list with anything else).\n\nIf roti is placed in 'orderable', we exclude those who dislike it; however, five people like roti and must be satisfied (Ula, Naina, Sol, Abe). Placing roti in 'not_orderable' would then require all five of those liking roti to have another like in 'orderable' or a dislike in 'not_orderable'.\n\nOne effective way to start is by looking at people with only one like and ensuring their like is on the 'orderable' list:\n\n- Sol likes only roti and dislikes sorbet and soup. Putting roti on 'orderable' satisfies Sol.\n- Since Sol is satisfied with roti, we don't have to worry about their dislikes.\n- Looking for others who like roti, we find Ula, Naina, and Abe, which overlap.\n- For Ula with only roti liked and dislikes sorbet and peach, we must have roti on 'orderable' as we cannot satisfy Ula otherwise. This automatically satisfies Naina and Abe as well.\n\nSo far, we decide:\n- Roti must be in 'orderable'.\n\nNow, Lei dislikes roti, which is now in 'orderable', so Lei needs to have soup or peach in 'not_orderable' to be satisfied. Lei dislikes both soup and peach. We can satisfy Lei by placing peach, which is heavily liked, into 'not_orderable' to satisfy more of the participants.\n\nMaking these decisions, we have:\n- Roti in 'orderable'\n- Peach in 'not_orderable'\n\nThis choice will satisfy Ula, Naina, Sol, Abe, and Lei, but we must verify that all others can still be satisfied.\n\nAt this point, all individuals who dislike peach are now satisfied because it is in 'not_orderable'. Those who like peach will need to find satisfaction elsewhere.\n\nWe have participants who like sorbet and dislike roti, such as Kit and Nic. If we put sorbet on 'orderable', it immediately satisfies them, as their dislike for roti is not a concern anymore because roti is already in 'orderable'.\n\nVeer likes soup, which is a problem because both his likes would be in 'orderable' then. Therefore, sorbet should be in 'not_orderable' so Veer can be satisfied by soup alone.\n\nRu likes sorbet but dislikes roti and caramel. We could put caramel in 'orderable' to satisfy her so that we can put sorbet in 'not_orderable'.\n\nNow we must consider individuals who dislike soup, because we have not placed it yet.\n\nSince Lei is already satisfied with peach in 'not_orderable', we can place soup in 'orderable' to satisfy others who like soup, such as Joe, Veer, Om, Mina, Rina, Tara, and Chao.\n\nCaramel is still undetermined, but if we put it in 'orderable', we satisfy a number of people like May, Gia, Mia, Aarav, Gus, Tim, Mina, and Chao.\n\nBy placing caramel in 'orderable', anyone with caramel as a like is satisfied, and those with caramel as a dislike will need another item on the 'orderable' list or dislike in 'not_orderable' to be satisfied.\n\nGiven all these conditions