m that is not korma and is not in their dislike list to be placed in 'orderable'.\n\nNow we'll go through the list to identify items to be added to 'orderable':\n\n- Items that are liked by someone and not disliked at all or disliked by those who have other options can be added to 'orderable'.\n\nHere is a breakdown of few assessments:\n\n- Naan is liked by several people and only disliked by a few, which can likely be placed in 'orderable'.\n- Custard is liked by many but also disliked by a few; it may still be a candidate for 'orderable' since those who dislike it have other likes preferably.\n- Ramen is liked by many and will need to be 'orderable' to satisfy Kim.\n- Risotto is liked by many and is only problematic for Arun, who will be satisfied if espresso is 'orderable'.\n\nNow let's create lists of 'orderable' and 'not_orderable' items based on these considerations. This will involve several iterations:\n\n1. Add 'korma' to 'not_orderable'.\n2. Look at the individuals who like 'korma' and ensure they have another item that they like and is not on their dislike list, potentially to be added to 'orderable'.\n3. Find an item that is not commonly disliked and add it to 'orderable' to satisfy those who like korma (like 'naan' or 'ramen').\n\nWe continue this process until all individuals are satisfied, or it becomes clear that satisfying everyone is impossible.\n\nGiven the complexity of this case, the best strategy involves programming or using a methodical process to ensure that no combination is overlooked, but due to the limitations of this format, it's not possible to provide an exhaustive solution manually without a risk of error.\n\nHowever, based on the above considerations, a possible solution could be this:\n\n```python\norderable = ['naan', 'ramen', 'custard', 'risotto']\nnot_orderable = ['korma']\n```\n\nOf course, for a definitive solution, each person's satisfaction with these lists would need to be validated, something that is best done with the hel