 'orderable' list to satisfy everyone. This would be the case if an item is liked by at least one participant and not disliked by any participant.\n\nAfter this step, if there are still unsatisfied participants, we need to juggle the remaining likes and dislikes to see if we can satisfy everyone. If we cannot find a combination that satisfies everyone, we will return empty lists.\n\nNow let's process the given likes and dislikes:\n\n`Dislikes universal:`   \n- tacos: Disliked by Lia, Arun, Gen, Kim, Taj, Ben, Gia, Lan, Eva (Looking at the dislikes, we cannot add 'tacos' to 'not_orderable' as it is liked by Lee, Ivy, and Gia, so we leave it for now.)\n\n`Likes universal:`\n- None (no food item is liked by some without being disliked by others)\n\nNow let's consider the individual satisfaction rules and see if we can find a set of orderable and not_orderable items that satisfies everyone.\n\nWe can see that 'custard' is disliked by many, but liked by Arun, Qi, Lou, and Joy. We cannot place it directly on a list without checking other preferences first.\n\nTo satisfy Ned, who likes 'lasagna' and dislikes 'custard', we would need to have 'lasagna' on the 'orderable' list or 'custard' on the 'not_orderable' list. However, 'lasagna' is disliked by many (Lia, Gen, Tia, Ying, Joy), so having 'lasagna' on the 'orderable' list would not satisfy these people unless we have something they dislike on the 'not_orderable' list.\n\nAs mentioned, 'tacos' are a contentious item with more dislikes than likes, but it is liked by a few, so it cannot be placed directly on the 'not_orderable' list either.\n\nAfter a thorough analysis, it becomes evident that we cannot satisfy all participants simultaneously, as we cannot find a single item that is either universally liked (wholeheartedly by some and not disliked by others) or universally disliked (by all or those not disliking also don't like it). We have many conflicts, such as 'lasagna' liked by some but disliked by others, 'custard' with the same issue, 'tacos' causing divide among the group, etc.\n\nGiven the overlapping and conflicting preferences, there is no satisfactory set of 'orderable' and 'not_orderable' lists that can make every single person satisfied according to the rules.\n\nHence, the solution is:\n```python\norderable = []\nnot_orderable = []\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 556, 'num_completion_tokens': 603}
Data Sample: {'num_vars': 9, 'num_clauses': 36, 'formula': [[7, -9, -4], [5, -1, -9], [4, 6, 5], [2, 9, 3], [1, -3, 6], [3, -6, 7], [-3, 4, 2], [2, 7, 5], [-1, 7, 6], [6, 1, 9], [6, -9, 5], [3, 5, 1], [2, -7, -8], [-3, 2, -6], [8, -2, 4], [5, -4, 3], [-2, 3, -7], [-2, -6, -9], [-3, -2, -1], [-4, 5, -1], [4, 3, -8], [7, 1, 4], [-6, 7, 1], [-5, 8, 2], [-8, -3, -9], [-2, -3, -8], [-9, 3, -1], [-3, 7, 6], [8, 5, -6], [-1, 7, -5], [8, 6, 9], [-2, -9, -5], [9, 6, -3], [-8, 4, -7], [-6, -4, 8], [1, -4, 9]], 'is_sat': False, 'preferences': 'Tao: Likes lobster. Dislikes muffins, bruschetta. Lou: Likes peach. Dislikes pho, muffins. Mia: Likes bruschetta, frittata, peach. Tim: Likes cheesecake, muffins, salmon. Isha: Likes pho, frittata. Dislikes salmon. Shan: Likes salmon, lobster. Dislikes frittata. Neil: Likes bruschetta, cheesecake. Dislikes salmon. Rex: Likes cheesecake, lobster, peach. Lei: Likes lobster, frittata. Dislikes pho. Vic: Likes frittata, pho, muffins. Wei: Likes frittata, peach. Dislikes muffins. Val: Likes salmon, peach, pho. Anil: Likes cheesecake. Dislikes lobster, omelette. Zoe: Likes cheesecake. Dislikes salmon, frittata. Ty: Likes omelette, bruschetta. Dislikes cheesecake. Sky: Likes peach, salmon. Dislikes bruschetta. Ana: Likes salmon. Dislikes cheesecake, lobster. Arun: Dislikes cheesecake, frittata, muffins. Ravi: Dislikes salmon, cheesecake, pho. Ron: Likes peach. Dislikes bruschetta, pho. Sid: Likes bruschetta, salmon. Dislikes omelette. Joy: Likes lobster, pho, bruschetta. Sam: Likes lobster, pho. Dislikes frittata. Gen: Likes omelette, cheesecake. Dislikes peach. Nita: Dislikes omelette, salmon, muffins. Ada: Dislikes cheesecake, salmon, omelette. Feng: Likes salmon. Dislikes muffins, pho. Ula: Likes lobster, frittata. Dislikes salmon. Sol: Likes omelette, peach. Dislikes frittata. Max: Likes lobster. Dislikes pho, peach. Qi: Likes omelette, frittata, muffins. Veer: Dislikes cheesecake, muffins, peach. Gia: Likes muffins, frittata. Dislikes salmon. Tara: Likes bruschetta. Dislikes omelette, lobster. Jay: Likes omelette. Dislikes frittata, bruschetta. Mel: Likes pho, muffins. Dislikes bruschetta. ', 'menu_items': ['pho', 'cheesecake', 'salmon', 'bruschetta', 'peach', 'frittata', 'lobster', 'omelette', 'muffins'], 'gpt_out': 'To solve this problem, let\'s consider the likes and dislikes of each person and try to identify any foods that could satisfy multiple people\'s likes or dislikes without causing conflict with any other person\'s preferences. We should start with the most disliked and liked items to make sure we satisfy the most people\'s dislikes and likes.\n\nItems that are disliked by multiple people could immediately go into `not_orderable`, and items that are liked and not disliked by others could go into `orderable`.\n\nLet\'s begin by tabulating the number of dislikes and likes for each item, since items that are universally disliked can be immediately placed in `not_orderable` and items that are universally liked and not disliked by anyone can be put in `orderable`.\n\nSo, I will start by counting dislikes and likes for each item:\n\n```python\ndislikes = {}\nlikes = {}\n\ndef add_to_dict(dictionary, key):\n    if key in dictionary:\n        dictionary[key] += 1\n    else:\n        dictionary[key] = 1\n\n# Add dislikes\ndislikes_list = [\n    "muffins", "bruschetta", "pho", "salmon", "frittata", "cheesecake", "lobster", "omelette", "peach"\n]\n\nfor food in dislikes_list:\n    add_to_dict(dislikes, food)\n\n# Add likes\nlikes_list = [\n    "lobster", "peach", "bruschetta", "frittata", "cheesecake", "pho", "salmon", "muffins", "omelette"\n]\n\nfor food in likes_list:\n    add_to_dict(likes, food)\n\n# Output the counts (for explanation purposes)\nprint(f"Dislikes counts: {dislikes}")\nprint(f"Likes counts: {likes}")\n```\n\nNow, let\'s list out preferences which seem to influence orderability the most:\n\n# Highly Disliked Foods (Potential for `not_orderable`):\n1. Muffins (8 dislikes)\n2. Pho (7 dislikes)\n3. Bruschetta (6 dislikes)\n4. Salmon (7 dislikes)\n5. Cheesecake (5 dislikes)\n\n# Highly Liked Foods (Potential for `orderable`):\n1. Lobster (7 likes)\n2. Peach (7 likes)\n3. Bruschetta (5 likes)\n4. Frittata (9 likes)\n5. Cheesecake (7 likes)\n6. Pho (5 likes)\n\nNow, we know that if a food is liked by someone, it cannot be in the `not_orderable` list. We can also place a food in the `not_orderable` list if it is disliked by someone and not liked by anyone else. Similarly, a food can be placed in the `orderable` list if it is liked by someone and not disliked by anyone else.\n\nTo decide, we need to ensure we satisfy at least one like or dislike of each person, and no conflicts.\n\nInspecting the likes and dislikes reveals that "muffins" and "pho" are disliked by multiple people and are not indispensable (there are other liked items for the people liking muffins and pho). We could consider these for `not_orderable`.\n\nOn the other hand, "frittata" has a lot of likes and fewer relative dislikes. No one has frittata as their only like, and some of its likers also like other things. \n\nLet\'s now attempt creating the lists:\n\n```python\norderable = []\nnot_orderable = []\n\n# Place "muffins" and "pho" in not_orderable due to widespread dislike\nnot_orderable.append("muffins")\nnot_orderable.append("pho")\n\n# Now, check if there\'s at least one like or dislike satisfied for each person\n# Start by satisfying likes that do not conflict with dislikes\n# Already, since "muffins" is in `not_orderable`, Tim, Vic, Qi, Mel, and Gia are satisfied.\n# We don\'t add "bruschetta" because it is liked as much as it is disliked.\n# We also want to avoid adding "salmon" and "cheesecake" due to their divisiveness.\n\n# Add "lobster" to the orderable as it is liked by many and disliked by only some\n# "Anil" dislikes lobster but likes cheesecake, so if we add cheesecake later, we are covered\norderable.append("lobster")\n\n# Now, adding "peach" to orderable satisfies many, and those who dislike peach, like other items\norderable.append("peach")\n\n# "Frittata" has a high number of likes with some dislikes. It\'s also not anyone\'s only like, but it is necessary to satisfy Isha, Lei, Ula, Qi, and Wei without causing issues for its dislikers. We can\'t add "frittata" to the `orderable` since Sky, Ana, Neil, Zoe, Arun, and Sam dislike it.\n\n# We cannot add "cheesecake", "salmon", or "omelette" due to similar reasoning.\n# We can now review who is not yet satisfied and if we can satisfy them without conflict.\n\n# By now, every individual has at least one like or dislike satisfied except for a few.\n# Arun dislikes "cheesecake", "frittata", and "muffins" - with "muffins" not orderable, he is satisfied.\n# Isha likes "pho" and "frittata" - with "pho" not orderable, she is satisfied.\n# Zoe dislikes "salmon" and "frittata" - since we can\'t order "frittata", "salmon" becomes not orderable to satisfy Zoe.\n# Sky dislikes "bruschetta" - it is not in orderable, so Sky is satisfied.\n# Sam dislikes "frittata" which isn\'t orderable, so he\'s satisfied now.\n# This process is continued until all participants are satisfied by the combination of the two lists or we find an incompatibility.\n\n# "frittata" serves as the crux here - if we add it to orderable, several individuals are unsatisfied, if we do not, we can\'t satisfy Isha, Lei, Ula, Qi, and Wei.\n\n# Let\'s try adding "frittata" to orderable and see if it causes an issue:\n# Isha will be satisfied, Lei satisfied, Ula satisfied, Qi satisfied, and Wei satisfied\n# However, this causes a conflict for Shan, who dislikes frittata and whose only other like is "lobster", which is now in question due to Anil\'s dislike.\n\n# Since "frittata" satisfies multiple people and does not completely satisfy everyone\'s dislikes (due to conflicting situations with lobster), it appears we have reached an impasse and cannot create a list that satisfies all conditions simultaneously.\n\n# Our final solution then, regrettably, is empty lists for both `orderable` and `not_orderable`.\norderable = []\nnot_orderable = []\n\n# Output the solution\nprint(f"orderable={orderable}, not_orderable={not_orderable}")\n```\n\nThe conclusion of this complex problem is that no combination can satisfy all the participants. Thus:\n\n```python\norderable=[], not_orderable=[]\n```', 'num_prompt_tokens': 684, 'num_completion_tokens': 1515}
