 Muffins are disliked by Pat and Pia.\n- Tiramisu is disliked only by Pia.\n- Fries are disliked only by Feng.\n\nSince most of these dislikes overlap, it appears placing \'doughnut\' in the \'not_orderable\' list will satisfy many people, but Pat and Om like doughnuts, which complicates things since placing it in the \'not_orderable\' list wouldn\'t satisfy them.\n\nLooking at the likes, we can try to find items to put in the \'orderable\' list that can satisfy as many people as possible without conflicting with the dislikes:\n\n- Cheese is liked by Mina, Tao, Arun, Ula, and Feng.\n- Tiramisu is liked by Zac and Om.\n- Bread is liked by Kai, Pat, Arun, Qi, and Cal.\n- Fries are liked by Kai, Du, Ula, Cal, and Pia.\n- Udon is liked by Du, Yan, Qi, and Cal.\n- Muffins are liked by Pat, May, and Tim.\n\nCheese is a universally liked item except for Tim, so we could potentially place "cheese" in the \'orderable\' list. Let\'s place "cheese" in the \'orderable\' list and see how we can satisfy the rest, keeping in mind Tim\'s dislike.\n\nNow, let\'s try to arrange the lists so that at least one like or dislike is satisfied for each person:\n\n1. Putting "cheese" in the \'orderable\' list will satisfy Mina, Tao, Arun, Ula, and Feng, but not Tim as he dislikes cheese.\n2. We cannot put "doughnut" in the \'not_orderable\' list since Pat and Om both like it.\n3. We cannot put "bread" in the \'not_orderable\' list because it is liked by many.\n4. We can consider putting "fries" in the \'orderable\' list because it doesn\'t conflict with anyone\'s dislikes, other than Feng.\n5. Considering that, we cannot satisfy Feng by placing "cheese" in the \'orderable\' list. However, we can satisfy him by putting "tart" in the \'not_orderable\' list, as he likes cheese and dislikes fries. This also works for Zac, Kai, Yan, and Om.\n\nSo, our strategy will be to put \'cheese\' and \'fries\' in the \'orderable\' list and \'tart\' in the \'not_orderable\' list. This leaves us to find just a single \'orderable\' item for Tim and a \'not_orderable\' item for Pat.\n\nWe can\'t satisfy Pat with a liked item in \'orderable\' because Pat’s likes (bread, doughnut) conflict with other people\'s dislikes. So, we need to satisfy Pat with a \'not_orderable\' item. Pat dislikes \'muffins\', which no one else has a problem with, so we can add \'muffins\' to the \'not_orderable\' list.\n\nNow we only need to satisfy Tim. Tim likes \'muffins\', which we\'ve put in \'not_orderable\', so Tim is satisfied.\n\nFinal Lists:\n- orderable: \'cheese\', \'fries\'\n- not_orderable: \'tart\', \'muffins\'\n\nLet\'s check these lists against each person\'s preference:\n\n- Mina likes \'cheese\' which is in \'orderable\'.\n- Zac likes \'tiramisu\' which hasn\'t been listed, but dislikes \'tart\' which is in \'not_orderable\'.\n- Kai likes \'bread\' and \'fries\', and \'fries\' is in \'orderable\'.\n- Du likes \'udon\' and \'fries\', and \'fries\' is in \'orderable\'.\n- Tao likes \'cheese\' which is in \'orderable\'.\n- Pat likes \'bread\' and \'doughnut\', but dislikes \'muffins\' which is in \'not_orderable\'.\n- Yan likes \'udon\' which hasn\'t been listed, but dislikes \'tart\' and \'bread\' which are either in \'not_orderable\' or not listed.\n- Arun likes \'bread\' and \'cheese\', and \'cheese\' is in \'orderable\'.\n- Ula likes \'fries\' and \'cheese\', and both are in \'orderable\'.\n- May likes \'muffins\', which is in \'not_orderable\'.\n- Om likes \'doughnut\' and \'tiramisu\', but dislikes \'tart\' which is in \'not_orderable\'.\n- Qi likes \'udon\', \'bread\', and \'tiramisu\', and none of their likes/dislikes conflict with the lists created.\n- Cal likes \'bread\', \'udon\', and \'fries\', and \'fries\' is in \'orderable\'.\n- Feng likes \'cheese\' which is in \'orderable\', but dislikes \'fries\' so is satisfied since he has at least one like in \'orderable\'.\n- Tim dislikes \'doughnut\' and \'cheese\', but likes \'muffins\', which is in \'not_orderable\'.\n- Pia