Data Sample: {'num_vars': 4, 'num_clauses': 16, 'formula': [[2, 1, -4], [-4, 3, 2], [3, -1, 4], [-2, 4, 1], [2, -4, -3], [1, -3, -4], [1, -4, -3], [1, 3, -4], [-3, 2, -1], [4, -3, -1], [-2, 1, -4], [-2, -1, 4], [3, 1, -4], [-4, -3, 2], [-4, 1, 2], [1, 4, -3]], 'is_sat': True, 'preferences': 'Cal: Likes lasagna, caramel. Dislikes naan. Geet: Likes tacos, lasagna. Dislikes naan. Gen: Likes tacos, naan. Dislikes caramel. Rohan: Likes naan, caramel. Dislikes lasagna. Val: Likes lasagna. Dislikes naan, tacos. Vic: Likes caramel. Dislikes tacos, naan. Jon: Likes caramel. Dislikes naan, tacos. Zoe: Likes caramel, tacos. Dislikes naan. Eva: Likes lasagna. Dislikes tacos, caramel. Om: Likes naan. Dislikes tacos, caramel. Gus: Likes caramel. Dislikes lasagna, naan. Tia: Likes naan. Dislikes lasagna, caramel. Veer: Likes tacos, caramel. Dislikes naan. Hal: Likes lasagna. Dislikes naan, tacos. Ping: Likes caramel, lasagna. Dislikes naan. Mina: Likes caramel, naan. Dislikes tacos. ', 'menu_items': ['caramel', 'lasagna', 'tacos', 'naan'], 'gpt_out': 'To create the two lists, I will need to reconcile the various likes and dislikes among the individuals to ensure satisfaction for all based on the criteria provided. Let’s work through the preferences step-by-step to determine what can be ordered (\'orderable\') and what cannot (\'not_orderable\'):\n\nI will start by listing out all the unique food items mentioned by everyone:\n- Lasagna\n- Caramel\n- Naan\n- Tacos\n\nNow, let\'s look at the conditions for each food item to see if they can be in \'orderable\' or \'not_orderable\' list:\n\n1. "Lasagna" is liked by Cal, Geet, Val, Eva, Hal, Ping, and disliked by Rohan, Gus, Tia. Since more people like it than dislike it, and no one who dislikes it only dislikes this item, it\'s a potential candidate for \'orderable\'.\n\n2. "Caramel" is liked by Cal, Gen, Rohan, Vic, Jon, Zoe, Veer, Ping, Mina, Gus. Disliked by Gen, Eva, Om, Tia. Even though this is disliked by some, each of these individuals has another item they dislike, suggesting that "Caramel" can accommodate their preference by putting the other disliked item they have in \'not_orderable\'.\n\n3. "Naan" is liked by Gen, Rohan, Om, Tia, Mina and disliked by Cal, Geet, Val, Vic, Jon, Zoe, Veer, Hal, Ping. More people dislike "Naan" than like it, and all those who like it also like at least one other item, so "Naan" is a potential candidate for \'not_orderable\'.\n\n4. "Tacos" is liked by Geet, Gen, Zoe, Veer and disliked by Val, Vic, Jon, Eva, Om, Hal. More individuals dislike "Tacos" than like it, and all who dislike it like at least one other item that could be put in \'orderable\'. Thus, "Tacos" could also be in \'not_orderable\'.\n\nNow, let\'s evaluate:\n\n- "Lasagna" cannot be \'not_orderable\' because it would leave multiple persons unsatisfied (those who like it and have no other likes).\n- "Naan" fits well in \'not_orderable\' since it satisfies those who dislike it, and doesn\'t affect those who like it as they have other likes.\n- "Tacos" being disliked by several and only liked by a few who have other likes as well, could also be in \'not_orderable\'.\n- "Caramel" can satisfy the majority by being in \'orderable\'. Those who dislike caramel have other dislikes (either tacos or naan) that we can put in \'not_orderable\'. \n\nFrom these points, the most logical categorization would be:\n\n\'orderable\': ["Lasagna", "Caramel"]\n\'not_orderable\': ["Naan", "Tacos"]\n\nThis way, everyone has at least one liked item available or one disliked item that is not available.\n\nTherefore, the final solution is:\n<orderable=["Lasagna", "Caramel"], not_orderable=["Naan", "Tacos"]>.', 'num_prompt_tokens': 397, 'num_completion_tokens': 662}
