, spaghetti, sushi), we can identify the following:\n\n1. Some people dislike spaghetti but everyone else who likes it also likes ravioli or wonton.\n2. Some people dislike sushi but everyone else who likes it also likes wonton or ravioli.\n3. Some people dislike ravioli but everyone else who likes it also likes wonton.\n4. Some people dislike wonton but everyone else who likes it also likes ravioli.\n\nSo, we have nobody who ONLY likes spaghetti or sushi. This means we can satisfy some of the dislikers by putting spaghetti and/or sushi on the not_orderable list without alienating any group. However, some like one and dislike the other.\n\nWe see that Rex, Raj, Kit, and Kai all dislike both sushi and ravioli, meaning that we cannot satisfy them by not ordering one and ordering the other, as they dislike both.\n\nGiven Raj, Rex, Kit, and Kai's mutual dislike for both sushi and ravioli, and taking into account the overlapping likes and dislikes from others:\n\n- If we put sushi on the not_orderable list, Lia and others who like sushi won't be satisfied because they don't like the only remaining item, spaghetti, which others dislike.\n    \n- If we put ravioli on the not_orderable list, Jay, Sid, Jim, Mel, May, Pat and several others who like ravioli won't be satisfied because they don't have sushi to compensate (for those who like it).\n    \n- Putting either ravioli or sushi on the orderable list won't work either, because it will dissatisfy those who dislike them.\n\nTherefore, it's impossible to find a combination that would satisfy everyone's preferences.\n\nOutput:\n```python\norderable=[], not_orderable=[]\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 499, 'num_completion_tokens': 471}
