ences and identifying patterns that might help us classify items as 'orderable' or 'not_orderable'. For each like and dislike mentioned, we should check if there's a conflict (i.e., one person's like is another person's dislike). \n\nGiven that an item cannot appear on both lists, once an item is designated as 'orderable' or 'not_orderable', all subsequent decisions must respect that initial classification to satisfy everyone's preferences.\n\nHere's a summarized list of everyone's likes and dislikes:\n\n**Likes**: \n- Ratatouille: Kim, Ansh, Sal, Diya, Zara, Ned, Du, Oli, Dan, Tara, Om, Hui, Ivy, Xiu.\n- Kebab: Kim, Zara, Feng, Wes, Ivy, Tao, Max, Zan, Jaya, Liz, Hui, Hal, Rob, Jim.\n- Roti: Ty, Bao, Liv, Wei, Ru, Ian, Lei, Rina, Abe, Liz, Hal, Hui.\n- Salmon: Taj, Xan, Gen, Bao, Liv, Ray, Pam, Jun, Sid, Tara, Anil, Max, Tao.\n- Wonton: Sal, Xan, Gen, Liv, Lee, Kai, Arun, Ken, May, Pat, Ravi, Abe, Rina, Hal.\n- Vindaloo: Eva, Mia, Ken, Nita, Eli, Om, Ravi, Anil.\n\n**Dislikes**: \n- Salmon: Kim, Diya, Zara, Li, Lee, Ada, Kai, Arun, Gia, Du, Oli, Xan, Pam, Dan, Pia, Joy, Ravi, Xiu, Pat, Chen.\n- Kebab: Kit, Mina, Lin, Ken, Nita, Ula, Chen, Fay, Ray, Pat, Sid, Chen, Jim.\n- Ratatouille: Kit, Zac, Eva, Ru, Ada, Kai, Arun, Ned, Om, Abe, Rina.\n- Roti: Kit, Ansh, Sal, Ty, Wei, Ru, Li, Zac, Liz, Ian, Eli, Cal, Max, Zan, Hui, Anil.\n- Wonton: Taj, Diya, Lee, Bao, Ty, Wei, Li, Jim, Pam, Dan, Cal, Liz, Jim, Ray, Gia, Chen, Rob.\n- Vindaloo: Taj, Ken, Ansh, Li, Zac, Ada, Ula, Diya, Ian, Xiu, Pat, Chen, Feng, Cal.\n\n### Step-by-Step Analysis:\n\n1. Items that only appear in 'likes' and not in 'dislikes' can be added directly to the 'orderable' list.\n2. Items that only appear in 'dislikes' and not in 'likes' can be added directly to the 'not_orderable' list.\n3. Conflicts arise when an item is both liked and disliked.\n\nFirst, let's see if there are any items that are only liked and not disliked:\n\n- Vindaloo is liked by some, but it is also disliked by multiple individuals, so it cannot be in the orderable list by default.\n\nNow let's see if there are any items that are disliked by some but not liked:\n\n- There are none: each disliked food item is also liked by someone in the group.\n\nSince there are no items that are exclusively liked or disliked without conflict, we start by addressing conflicts by looking at the number of conflicts for each item to decide which item could potentially satisfy more people if it is included or excluded. \n\nI will start by listing the number of likes and dislikes for each food item. Remember, if a person has one of their liked items ordered, or has one of their disliked items not ordered, they will be satisfied. We should aim to exclude foods with the highest number of dislikes when there are conflicting preferences, ensuring the most people are made happy with an order.\n\n- Kebab: Loved by several but also disliked by quite a few.\n- Ratatouille: Same as kebab, loved and disliked by many.\n- Roti: Loved and disliked by many as well.\n- Salmon: A number of people like it but also a considerable number dislike it.\n- Wonton: Liked by some but disliked by others.\n- Vindaloo: Liked by a few but also disliked by several others.\n\nGiven the conflicting preferences, this will be a matter of determining which items can be ordered or left out to maximize overall satisfaction. \n\nLet's find out the item with the minimal negative impact if excluded from the 'orderable' list, which appears to be 'vindaloo' because relatively fewer people like it compared to those who dislike it, and these likers also like other items that fewer people dislike. \n\nIf we include 'vindaloo' in 'not_orderable' and thereby satisfy the dislikes for Ada, Taj, Ansh, Xiu, Ula, Diya, Kai, Chen, Arun, Pat, Ian, Liz, Lin, and Zara, we can satisfy a large number of people straightaway. \n\nNow we must ensure that these individuals also have a liked item in the 'orderable' list.\n\nWe see that 'roti' is liked by many people and only Kit, Sal, Ansh, Ty, Zac, Ru, Wei, Li, Eli, Ian, Liz, Max, Zac, Zan, Hui, and Anil dislike it. Most of these also dislike 'vindaloo', which is already in our 'not_orderable' list, except for Kit, Max, Zan, and Sal, who don't have any liked items yet. \n\n'Roti' is a likely candidate for 'orderable', as it doesn't negatively impact any individuals except for those who dislike 'vindalou' (already chosen as 'not_orderable'), Kit, Max, and Zan. Moreover, putting 'roti' in the 'orderable' list would make Ru, Liv, Bao, Lei, Rina, Abe, Liz, Hal, and Hui happy, as they don't have any items in the 'orderable' list yet. \n\nNext, there could be a contention between 'salmon' and 'wonton', as putting either of these could greatly influence the satisfaction due to the number of individuals concerned. However, looking at numbers, 'wonton' is disliked by more people than 'salmon', and several of those who like 'salmon' do not have other liked foods that aren't highly contested. Thus, 'salmon' should go to the 'orderable' list. This selection of 'salmon' would satisfy Rob, Tao, Max, Jun, Sid, Bao, Taj, Gen, Liv, and Pam, who have no liked items in the 'orderable' list yet. \n\nNow that we have 'roti' and 'salmon' on the 'orderable' list and 'vindalou' on the 'not_orderable' list, we are left with 'kebab', 'ratatouille', and 'wonton'. Adding 'ratatouille' to the 'orderable' list can satisfy a large number of people.\n\nBy adding 'ratatouille', we satisfy Kim, Ansh, Sal, Diya, Zara, Ned, Du, Dan, Oli, Tara, Om, Xiu, and Ivy, who haven't had their liked items added to 'orderable' yet. \n\n'Kebab' is disliked by Mina, Ken, Nita, Ula, Fay, Ray, Sid, Jim, Chen, and Pat but liked by many, and since 'vindalou' is already satisfying most of the 'kebab' dislikers, it's safer to add 'kebab' to the 'orderable' list. \n\nWith 'kebab' in 'orderable', 'wonton' remains, which has several dislikers. Let's place 'wonton' in the 'not_orderable' list.\n\n### Final Tally:\n- Orderable: roti, salmon, ra