o find which food items can appear on the \'orderable\' list (meaning they can be ordered) and which ones must go into the \'not_orderable\' list (meaning they cannot be ordered).\n\nBefore making any decisions, let\'s identify food items that are universally disliked or liked as they can provide an easy starting point.\n\n1. **Identify unanimously disliked foods**: If every single person dislikes an item, that item can immediately be placed on the \'not_orderable\' list.\n2. **Identify foods that, if they are not orderable, would satisfy everyone who dislikes them**: Such items can also be set to \'not_orderable\', because making them orderable would not leave everyone satisfied.\n\nHowever, in this case, there\'s no single food item that is disliked by every participant, and with so many different preferences and intersects, it\'s not immediately clear if there are universal choices. So instead, let’s use a process to determine the \'orderable\' and \'not_orderable\' list:\n\n1. **Identify food items that people like and dislike**: We need to track how many people like and dislike each food item to prioritize the selections.\n\n2. **Check for conflicts**: Certain food items, when ordered, will cause conflicts because some people like them while others dislike them. These are the tricky foods, and we need to be cautious with them.\n\nTo keep track of this, we are going to write out and tally likes and dislikes for each food:\n\nFood items liked (number of people who like it):\n- nachos (16)\n- tandoori (14)\n- frittata (10)\n- pizza (13)\n- curry (8)\n- lobster (15)\n\nFood items disliked (number of people who dislike it):\n- nachos (7)\n- tandoori (12)\n- frittata (15)\n- pizza (11)\n- curry (15)\n- lobster (13)\n\nThere\'s no food item that is unanimously disliked (since the most any item is disliked is 15, and we have more than 15 participants), so let\'s attempt to find items that can be placed in each list while satisfying as many as possible.\n\n**Strategy**:\n- We can immediately place on the \'orderable\' list food items that are liked by some people and not disliked by any. However, there are no such items here.\n- Place on the \'not_orderable\' list items that are disliked by people and not liked by any. However, once again, there are no such items.\n- Look for individuals with unique conditions as their satisfaction may dictate the placement of some foods.\n\nAnalyzing the list, we notice:\n- "Curry" is liked by many, but also a large number (15) of people dislike it, making it less attractive for the \'orderable\' list.\n- "Frittata" is also heavily disliked; it might be a prime candidate for \'not_orderable\'.\n- "Nachos" are heavily liked but have a significant dislike count as well.\n\nLet\'s attempt to start with an item that poses the least conflict:\n- If "frittata" is placed on the \'not_orderable\' list, 15 individuals are satisfied off the bat. It is rarely liked, with only 10 likes, and we stand to satisfy a large number by considering it \'not_orderable\'.\n- "Curry" has a high dislike count, so placing it on the \'not_orderable\' list could also be favorable. However, placing "curry" in the \'not_orderable\' list would mean "lobster" or "tandoori" needs to be \'orderable\' to satisfy some people who like "curry". This creates a conflict because a significant number of people dislike "lobster" and "tandoori".\n\nTo decide on the next step, let\'s consider that "nachos" must be in the \'orderable\' list since they have a high like rate and only pose a conflict for 7 individuals, all of whom have other dislikes that could end up in the \'not_orderable\' list.\n\nGiven these considerations, let\'s move to a potential solution:\n\n**Potential \'not_orderable\':**\n- Frittata (satisfies those who dislike it)\n\n**Potential \'orderable\':**\n- Nachos (satisfies those who like it)\n\nNow let\'s check if anyone is left unsatisfied:\n- People who dislike "nachos" can be satisfied with "frittata", "curry", or other dislikes in \'not_orderable\'.\n- People who like "curry" can be satisfied with "nachos" in \'orderable\'.\n- Anyone who dislikes both "nachos" and "frittata" but likes "lobster" can be satisfied if "lobster" remains in the \'orderable\' list.\n- "Pizza" and "tandoori" are liked by many and disliked by some. They pose a conflict and we need to assess them more carefully.\n\nIt\'s a complex puzzle, and we must ensure that no individual\'s likes are entirely in the \'not_orderable\' list and no individual\'s dislikes are entirely in the \'orderable\' list.\n\nIt seems that we need to satisfy individuals with unique dislikes next. For instance, if someone dislikes "nachos", "frittata", and "curry" - like Taj - there needs to be another food item that they do not like which can be placed on the \'not_orderable\' list or a liked item on the \'orderable\' list. This gets complex because we need to check this for every individual.\n\nHowever, by going through all individuals with unique combinations, we discover there is no food item that satisfies all the criteria mentioned above since there are always overlapping likes and dislikes.\n\nIn conclusion, since there is no combination where at least one liked item is on the \'orderable\' list and one disliked item is on the \'not_orderable\' list, for each individual, the combination that satisfies everyone does not exist.\n\nTherefore, the output will be:\n\n```python\norderable = [], not_orderable = []\n```', 'num_prompt_tokens': 1083, 'num_completion_tokens': 1269}
