ts without causing conflicts.\n\nLooking at the data, the first thing we notice is that "ratatouille" is liked by a few people (Chen, Zoe, Bao, Mei, Joe, Lin, Hui, Rina, Hal, Xan) and is disliked by some (Sue, Taj, Ula, Shan, Ivy), but there are no overlapping likes and dislikes. Therefore, we cannot determine yet which list it should be on.\n\nHowever, for "vegetables," we have likes (Sue, Wei, Ula, Jaya, Jim, Hui, Vic) and dislikes (Zoe, Wes, Lou, Ian, Nic, Tia), with no conflicts. Since there are no conflicts, "vegetables" can be placed in the "orderable" list to satisfy those who like it, and we wouldn’t worry about the dislikes since we’ll only consider dislikes if there\'s no other option to satisfy their preference.\n\nThe next potential item is "kebab," which is liked by some (Pia, Cy, Ned, Kavi, Ian, Ray, Rohan, Fay, Hal) and disliked by others (Zoe, Bao, Mei, Wes, Nita, Shan, Gia, Max, Rina, Xan), with no overlap. However, we need more analysis to make sure placing "kebab" in either list will not cause an unsolvable conflict. Since this is not straightforward, we’ll leave this item for further analysis.\n\nWe continue this process and find that "salmon," "shrimps," "fries," "peach," "bruschetta," and "poke" are all liked and disliked by different people, making them more complicated to assign directly.\n\nNow, let’s try a different approach by looking for patterns that may force a decision. We’ll try to satisfy those who dislike items first if possible, since if we can not order something they dislike, we already satisfy their criteria without causing any potential conflict with likes.\n\nLet\'s take "peach," which is liked by several people (Lex, Taj, Wes, Nita, Kai, Neil, Nic, Riya, Ivan, Xiu, Liz, Peach) and disliked by some (Dot, Mina, Du, Eva, Cy, Lou, Zan, Ivy, Shan, Liv). Given that so many people like it, and there\'s a substantial overlap between likes and dislikes (meaning we can\'t place "peach" on either list without causing issues), we cannot 