a, Pia, Dot, Neil, Yue, Ravi\n- tart: Fay, Pia, Lan, Shan, Geet, Liv, Yue, Rina\n- naan: Fay, Ana, Shan, Neil, Zan, Yue, Ravi, Rina\n- sushi: Ping, Ana, Lan, Shan, Mia, Mina, Kit, Ula, Jon, Feng, Yue, Ravi, Rina\n\nTo begin with, we need to identify which food items cause the most conflict and which could potentially satisfy the highest number of people without making someone unhappy.\n\n1. **Tart** has the highest number of "likes" and "dislikes", so it\'s likely not going to be straightforward to place it in either list without further consideration.\n2. **Espresso** has many likes and dislikes too, but not as many dislikes as tart, which makes it a bit easier to deal with.\n3. **Naan** also has a considerable number of both likes and dislikes, so it\'s going to require careful handling.\n4. **Sushi** seems to be the least controversial since it has several likes and fewer dislikes compared to others.\n\nLooking at individuals who dislike the most universally popular items, the optimal strategy would involve satisfying as many as possible by placing the commonly disliked items in the \'not_orderable\' list. Let\'s review those individuals first and see if we can find a common item that is disliked:\n\n- Ravi dislikes all food items.\n- Yue dislikes espresso, tart, and sushi.\n- Rina dislikes sushi, naan, and tart.\n\nSince all three dislike tart, it is a potential candidate for the \'not_orderable\' list. However, before making such a decision, let\'s make sure there\'s something orderable for everyone else.\n\nIf we were to put tart on the \'not_orderable\' list, we would satisfy Ravi, Yue, and Rina. But that means we need to have sushi, naan, or espresso in the \'orderable\' list because there are people like Gen who like all three and would not be satisfied if all three were in the \'not_orderable\' list. \n\nSince espresso is the next most contentious food item, let\'s see who would be satisfied if it were included in \'orderable\':\n\nAnsh, Gen, Pia, Lei, Hui, Ula, Mina, Ying, Leo like espresso. We need to check if their \'dislikes\' overlap with \'naan\' and \'sushi\' because we want to ensure that we don\'t need to place all items in \'orderable\'.\n\nHowever, there\'s an issue. Let\'s look at individuals who have a strong preference against \'sushi\' and \'espresso\':\n\n- Ping dislikes both sushi and espresso.\n- Dot dislikes both sushi and espresso.\n\nThey would only be satisfied if \'tart\' or \'naan\' were in the \'orderable\' list, but since we put tart in \'not_orderable\', \'naan\' must be on the \'orderable\' list to satisfy them. There seems to be no conflict with placing naan in \'orderable\' at this point, as those who dislike naan will be satisfied with having tart in \'not_orderable\'.\n\nIt might be wise to place sushi in \'not_orderable\' because many people who like espresso also like sushi and will be satisfied with having espresso on \'orderable\'. Similarly, those who dislike both will be satisfied with having either sushi or espresso on \'not_orderable\'. \n\nBut if we do this, we then encounter a problem with Geet, who likes both naan and sushi but dislikes tart. If tart is in \'not_orderable\' and sushi is in \'not_orderable\', to satisfy Geet, naan would have to be included in \'orderable\'. However, since tart is in \'not_orderable\', \'sushi\' cannot be included in \'orderable\' because this would leave individuals like Ping dissatisfied who dislike both espresso and sushi.\n\nTo sum up, the initial attempt to place tart in \'not_orderable\' and espresso in \'orderable\' has failed because several conflicting interests cannot be satisfied simultaneously, particularly the preferences of Geet, Ping, and Dot.\n\nTo find a valid combination, let\'s alter our strategy:\n\n1. Since Ravi dislikes all items, none of them can be in \'orderable\'; therefore, an item that he dislikes that is least liked by others should be in \'orderable\'. So we attempt to place an item Ravi dislikes in \'not_orderable\'. Ravi dislikes sushi, espresso, and naan. Considering \'tart\' has already been identified as the most conflictual item, let\'s see if it would work to put it in the \'orderable\' list and satisfy the rest:\n\nIf tart is \'orderable\', Rina, Yue, and the others who dislike tart get satisfied via \'not_orderable\'.\n\n2. Now check sushi, espresso, and naan, to possibly place in \'not_orderable\':\n\n- Placing sushi in \'orderable\' is problematic because it leaves people like Jon and Neil unsatisfied as they both dislike sushi and espresso. Therefore, it\'s a candidate for \'not_orderable\'.\n- Espresso could then be \'orderable\' since those who dislike it will be satisfied with sushi in \'not_orderable\'.\n- Naan is least conflictual in this context and could also be \'orderable\'.\n\nSo, if tart and naan are \'orderable\' and sushi and espresso are split, with espresso being \'orderable\' and sushi \'not_orderable\', we have to check if this satisfies everyone.\n\nThis seems to satisfy all individuals since everyone who has a dislike now has an item they dislike in the \'not_orderable\' list, and everyone else has at least one item they like in the \'orderable\'. Before finalizing, let\'s ensure no person\'s likes and dislikes completely overlap the \'orderable\' and \'not_orderable\' lists:\n\nNo individual dislikes naan, tart, and espresso simultaneously, and no one likes sushi alone without liking either tart, naan, or espresso. Thus, no other conflict seems to arise, and everyone\'s preference is accounted for with tart and naan in \'orderable\', and sushi in \'not_orderable\'. Espresso can be placed in either list since everyone who likes it also likes naan or tart, and everyone who dislikes it will be satisfied with sushi being in \'not_orderable\'.\n\nTo summarize, the \'orderable\' list will have tart and naan (and optionally espresso), and the \'not_orderable\' list will have sushi. Everyone is satisfied based on the provided criteria.\n\nFinal output:\n\n```python\norderable=[\'tart\', \'naan\', \'espresso\'], not_orderable=[\'sushi\']\n```', 'num_prompt_tokens': 515, 'num_completion_tokens': 1540}
