derable', so Jon is satisfied.\n13. Ben likes falafel and frittata which are in both lists and soup which is 'orderable', so Ben is satisfied.\n14. Ivy also likes frittata which is 'orderable', so Ivy is satisfied.\n15. Arun likes soup and ratatouille ('orderable'), and dislikes kebab which is also 'orderable', so Arun is satisfied.\n16. Zac likes kebab and ratatouille ('orderable') and dislikes soup, so Zac is satisfied.\n17. Dan likes falafel and kebab, but dislikes frittata which is 'orderable', so Dan is satisfied.\n18. Ula likes kebab which will be 'orderable' and dislikes frittata which is also 'orderable', so Ula is satisfied.\n19. Raj likes falafel but it is 'not_orderable' and dislikes frittata which is 'orderable', so Raj is satisfied.\n\nConsidering these steps, it looks like everyone's preferences can be met with the following distribution:\n\n#### Solution:\n- 'orderable': Soup, Ratatouille, Kebab, Frittata\n- 'not_orderable': Falafel\n\nIn python code:\n```python\norderable=['soup', 'ratatouille', 'kebab', 'frittata'], not_orderable=['falafel']\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 581, 'num_completion_tokens': 960}
