),\nJon (dislikes hummus, peach),\nLeo (likes kimchi, dislikes custard),\nNic (likes hummus),\nBo (likes baklava, doughnut).\n\nFor doughnut, let's see who likes it and check if they have another liked item that is not heavily disliked:\n- Kim (also likes peach),\n- Aarav (also likes wonton),\n- Ying (also likes wonton),\n- Eva (also likes baklava),\n- Taj (also likes peach, hummus),\n- Ned (also likes peach),\n- Liv (also likes kimchi),\n- May (also likes custard),\n- Dan (also likes naan, hummus),\n- Bo (also likes baklava, cheesecake).\n\nConsidering these facts, let's create a tentative 'not orderable' list:\n```\nnot_orderable = ['custard', 'cheesecake', 'doughnut']\n```\n\nNow let's try to find food that everyone likes and isn't heavily disliked to make up the 'orderable' list. We can see that 'wonton', 'baklava', 'peach', 'kimchi', and 'hummus' are liked by several and are not universally disliked.\n\nHowever, we need to satisfy all preferences:\n- Sue dislikes wonton which means wonton cannot be orderable if hummus is orderable. The same applies to Tara.\n- Zoe dislikes baklava, so baklava cannot be orderable as well.\n- Cy dislikes wonton, so again hummus and wonton cannot both be orderable.\n\nSo based on the heavy dislikes for doughnut and cheesecake and major likes for hummus and kimchi, let's begin by adding hummus and kimchi to the 'orderable' list along with some items that are not heavily contested.\n\nWe will put peach on the 'not_orderable' list, as Leo is the only person who likes both cheesecake and custard, and dislikes peach. Putting peach on the 'not_orderable' list, satisfies his and everyone else's conditions.\n\nNext, we have to satisfy individuals who like doughnuts, cheesecake, and custard by other means since they might end up being 'not_orderable'. Looking through the list, the only individual whose likes are all ending up in the 'not_orderable' list is Mina. However, Mina dislikes naan, which we can place in the 'orderable' list to satisfy their condition.\n\nNow we have:\n```\norderable = ['hummus', 'kimchi', 'naan']\nnot_orderable = ['custard', 'cheesecake', 'doughnut', 'peach']\n```\n\nWe need to ensure that everyone is satisfied:\n- Individuals who like naan (Jay, Lee, Arun, Du, Ray, Sal, Wei, and others) are satisfied because naan is 'orderable'.\n- People who like hummus and kimchi are satisfied because both are 'orderable'.\n\nLet's check the individuals with unique likes and see if our current list satisfies them:\n- Wes likes baklava, dislikes naan, cheesecake which is taken care of.\n- Ivy likes peach, dislikes cheesecake, custard which is taken care of with hummus or kimchi.\n- Raj likes wonton, and it's not 'orderable' or 'not_orderable' yet but dislikes doughnut which is 'not_orderable' so they are satisfied.\n- Wei is satisfied with kimchi, cheesecake is 'not_orderable'.\n- Tim is also satisfied since baklava can be 'orderable'.\n- Pat, Tom, and Sia like custard but it's 'not_orderable', they are not satisfied yet.\n- Ying likes doughnut, wonton. Doughnut is 'not_orderable', wonton is not decided, but since she dislikes baklava, we cannot put baklava in 'orderable'.\n- May likes custard, doughnut, custard and doughnut are 'not_orderable', but she's unsatisfied because her liked items are 'not_orderable'.\n- Bo likes cheesecake, doughnut, but cheesecake and doughnut are 'not_orderable', and because he dislikes hummus, baklava cannot be 'orderable' either or he'd be unsatisfied.\n\nConsidering the last points, we have a few individuals who like items that are only on the 'not_orderable' list, which means we can't satisfy all participants with this scenario. Therefore, we must output empty lists for both 'orderable' and 'not_orderable'.\n\nThe final python code representing our solution is:\n```python\norderable = []\nnot_orderable = []\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 1183, 'num_completion_tokens': 1368}
