en, Raj, Ying, Lin.\n- burrito is liked by Ken, Ping, Max, Lin but disliked by Gen, Ying.\n- custard is liked by Ping, Max but disliked by Lia, Raj.\n\nStep 2: Given the satisfaction criteria, we need to have at least one liked item or one disliked item in the correct list for each person. Starting with dislikes, since we have many overlaps, we notice that "biscuits" appears as a disliked item the most and is only liked by Vic. To satisfy the most people, we can consider placing "biscuits" in \'not_orderable.\'\n\nStep 3: Given the problem with "kimchi" (liked by many but also disliked), we cannot place it on either list unless we can satisfy Lia and Raj in some other way.\n\nStep 4: We notice that "poke" is liked by many individuals and only disliked by a few. However, satisfying Max (who dislikes poke) will conflict with satisfying the individuals who like poke.\n\nStep 5: We will now try to find a combination that satisfies everyone.\n\nGiven these observations, let\'s put "biscuits" in \'not_orderable\' to satisfy the individuals who dislike biscuits.\n\nnot_orderable = ["biscuits"]\n\nNext, we will see if there is a liked item that can go into \'orderable\' that doesn\'t conflict with dislikes.\n\n- "burrito" can potentially be \'orderable\' because it satisfies Ken, Ping, Max, and Lin. Gen and Ying are unsatisfied, but we can satisfy them potentially with "poke" for Gen and "kimchi" for Ying.\n\nLet\'s tentatively put "burrito" in \'orderable\' and verify if there will be someone unsatisfied:\n\norderable = ["burrito"]\n\nWith "burrito" in \'orderable,\' this satisfies Ken, Ping, Max, Lin. To check if everyone else can be satisfied, we\'ll look at remaining items:\n\n- "poke" is liked by many (Lia, Ken, Vic, Gen) but disliked by a few (Ula, Raj, Max). Since Max is already satisfied with "burrito," we can put "poke" in \'orderable\' to satisfy Lia, Vic, and Gen, while Ula will be satisfied with "biscuits" in \'not_orderable.\'\n\nAdding "poke" to \'orderable\' 