ori, burrito, bread. Fay: Likes tandoori. Dislikes bread, burger. Ying: Likes burrito, macaron, brownies. Wes: Likes tandoori, macaron. Dislikes burger. Kavi: Likes burger. Dislikes bread, macaron. Ru: Likes tandoori, burger, macaron. Tim: Likes tandoori, burger. Dislikes bread. Ray: Likes burger, burrito. Dislikes brownies. Leo: Likes burger, burrito, tandoori. Zac: Likes burger. Dislikes tandoori, brownies. Tao: Likes burrito. Dislikes tandoori, macaron. Joy: Likes macaron. Dislikes bread, tandoori. Du: Likes brownies. Dislikes tandoori, burger. Ada: Likes tandoori. Dislikes brownies, burrito. Lou: Likes tandoori, bread. Dislikes brownies. Max: Likes tandoori, bread, burger. Yue: Likes burrito, burger, brownies. Oli: Likes macaron. Dislikes bread, tandoori. Mel: Likes burrito. Dislikes bread, tandoori. Jan: Likes burger, tandoori. Dislikes macaron. Cal: Likes tandoori, burrito, burger. Jon: Dislikes burger, burrito, tandoori. Ravi: Likes bread, macaron, tandoori. Eli: Likes burrito. Dislikes macaron, tandoori. Amy: Likes burger. Dislikes bread, burrito. Jun: Likes macaron, burrito. Dislikes bread. Lex: Likes tandoori. Dislikes macaron, burrito. Om: Likes tandoori. Dislikes macaron, burrito. Sky: Likes brownies, burger. Dislikes macaron. Ula: Likes burger. Dislikes bread, brownies. Pat: Likes brownies. Dislikes burrito, tandoori. Cy: Likes tandoori, brownies. Dislikes burrito. Wei: Likes tandoori. Dislikes brownies, macaron. Rob: Likes macaron, brownies. Dislikes burger. ', 'menu_items': ['tandoori', 'burrito', 'macaron', 'bread', 'burger', 'brownies'], 'gpt_out': 'To solve this, we need to analyze the preferences of all the individuals and find food items that can satisfy everyone based on their likes and dislikes. Let\'s go through the list and figure out which items fall into \'orderable\' and \'not_orderable\' categories by eliminating conflicts.\n\nWe can follow these steps:\n\n1. Create a list of all unique food items.\n2. Go through preferences and start eliminating conflicts - items that are liked by some and disliked by others.\n3. Deduce the \'orderable\' and \'not_orderable\' list based on process of elimination.\n4. Validate that everyone\'s preference is met by the created lists.\n5. If we encounter an unsolvable conflict, we\'ll return empty lists.\n\nStep 1: List all unique food items.\nFood items: {brownies, bread, burrito, burger, macaron, tandoori}\n\nStep 2: Start eliminating conflicts. \n- If someone likes an item and no one dislikes it, it can potentially be orderable.\n- If someone dislikes an item and no one likes it, it can potentially be not_orderable.\n\nWe immediately notice that every single item is liked by at least one person and disliked by at least another, so we can\'t immediately pick any items for \'orderable\' or \'not_orderable\'.\n\nStep 3: Deduce what can be \'orderable\' and \'not_orderable\'.\nWe have to ensure that everyone is at least satisfied according to the criteria:\n\nPerson likes an \'orderable\' item OR person dislikes a \'not_orderable\' item.\n\nNow, let\'s check whether a solution is possible by analyzing the lists:\n\nIf we take "tandoori" as orderable, person who dislikes "tandoori" (like Ken, Ty, Jon, Wei) must have another item they dislike as not orderable or they must like an item in orderable list. \nFor example, Ken dislikes "bread" and "brownies" as well. However, others like these items.\nThis means we cannot have "tandoori" in orderable list without satisfying Ken\'s requirements unless "bread" or "brownies" become not orderable. \nLet\'s check if there’s someone who dislikes "bread" and "brownies" while not liking "tandoori": there is no such person.\n\nSo, if we add "tandoori" to the orderable list:\n\norderable = ["tandoori"]\n\nNow, we just need to check if we can satisfy everyone else with the other items. No one else dislikes "tandoori" without having another item they also dislike. \n\nFor "bread" and "brownies", since they are liked by some but also disliked by some, we can\'t put them in any list yet. \n\nNext, let\'s consider "macaron" and "burger":\n\nIf we place "burger" in not_orderable list, looking at Rohan who dislikes "burger" will be satisfied. However, "burger" is liked by many people who also like other items, so it could be potentially orderable if those people can be satisfied by other items.\n\nThus, let’s consider "macaron" for not_orderable, since if "macaron" is not orderable, anyone who likes "macaron" also likes another food that hasn’t been assigned to not_orderable.\n\nnot_orderable = ["macaron"]\n\nIf we consider "burger" for orderable, anyone who dislikes "burger" also dislikes "tandoori" or "macaron" or likes "brownies" which have not been assigned:\n\norderable = ["tandoori", "burger"]\n\nThen let\'s look at "burrito" for not_orderable, as anyone who likes "burrito" also likes either "tandoori" or "burger" which are in the orderable list.\n\nnot_orderable = ["macaron", "burrito"]\n\nAt this point, every person who likes "burger” and "tandoori" would be satisfied, and every person who dislikes "macaron” and "burrito" would be satisfied.\n\nLastly, we have two items left: "bread" and "brownies". Since all remaining individuals either like at least one item in the orderable list (which satisfies them) or dislike at least one item in the not_orderable list (which also satisfies them), we do not need to assign "bread" or "brownies" to satisfy everyone.\n\nFor step 4, we validate:\nEveryone who likes "tandoori" and "burger" has at least their one liked item \'orderable\' and those who dislike "macaron" and "burrito" have their disliked items in \'not_orderable\'. All others have at least one like or dislike fulfilled.\n\nAll individuals are satisfied and the final solution is:\n\norderable = ["tandoori", "burger"], not_orderable = ["macaron", "burrito"]', 'num_prompt_tokens': 991, 'num_completion_tokens': 1005}
