ikes kimchi, ratatouille. Dislikes wonton. Jay: Likes wonton, biscuits. Dislikes tacos. Ying: Likes wonton, biscuits, cake. Nic: Likes poke, tacos, kimchi. Ravi: Likes ratatouille. Dislikes kimchi, poke. Bea: Likes tacos. Dislikes cake, poke. Lin: Likes biscuits, kimchi. Dislikes poke. Liv: Likes cake, ratatouille. Dislikes tacos. Wei: Likes tacos, poke. Dislikes cake. Ana: Likes poke, biscuits. Dislikes wonton. Dan: Likes cake, poke. Dislikes biscuits. Rex: Likes tacos, wonton. Dislikes kimchi. Nita: Likes biscuits. Dislikes kimchi, poke. Gen: Likes poke. Dislikes ratatouille, tacos. Ty: Likes kimchi, poke. Dislikes wonton. Lee: Likes biscuits. Dislikes tacos, cake. Pam: Dislikes cake, poke, kimchi. Ken: Likes biscuits, ratatouille. Dislikes poke. Sol: Likes cake. Dislikes tacos, biscuits. ', 'menu_items': ['ratatouille', 'poke', 'tacos', 'wonton', 'cake', 'kimchi', 'biscuits'], 'gpt_out': 'This looks like a complex problem of logical consistency, where we must satisfy all members of a group considering their likes and dislikes. To solve this, we\'ll start identifying food items that must be in one list and then proceed step by step.\n\nBefore starting, it\'s good practice to list all the items and tally the preferences:\n- kimchi\n- tacos\n- cake\n- biscuits\n- poke\n- ratatouille\n- wonton\n\nNow, we need to find items that are either liked by some and not disliked by any (candidates for \'orderable\') or disliked by some and not liked by any (candidates for \'not_orderable\').\n\n1. We quickly realize that there is no food item that is liked by someone and disliked by no one, nor is there any food item that is disliked by someone and liked by no one. This tells us that we can\'t easily assign any food item strictly to \'orderable\' or \'not_orderable\'.\n\n2. Since there are no clear candidates for either list, we will need to look for combinations that satisfy everyone. We will look for a critical dependencies — for example, if a disliked and liked food are such that they satisfy a large number of people.\n\n3. We notice that the key dissatisfaction revolves around "poke", "kimchi", "cake", "tacos", "biscuits", "ratatouille", and "wonton". There\'s an immediate clash with "kimchi" because it\'s liked by many and simultaneously disliked by many—no matter which list it ends up on, it will satisfy some and dissatisfy others. But since more people like "kimchi" than dislike it, let\'s tentatively place it on the \'orderable\' list.\n\nLet\'s make a tentative assignment:\n- Orderable: kimchi\n- Not orderable: -\n\nWe will now see if this creates any conflicts.\n\n4. If "kimchi" is on the \'orderable\' list, then:\n   - Joe, Cy, Mina, Jim, Min, Diya, Om, Oli, Lin, Ty will be satisfied.\n   - Wes, Hu, Leo, Tara, Ravi, Pam, and Nita will be satisfied because at least one of their dislikes is \'not_orderable\'.\n\nNow we need to find at least one like or one dislike for the remaining individuals to satisfy in the context of this assignment. To do this, we need to look at the intersection of likes (for \'orderable\') and dislikes (for \'not_orderable\') among the remaining individuals. \n\n5. If we scan through the remaining preferences, we see that:\n   - "poke" is also disliked by many, and it is not liked by as many people as "kimchi," so we tentatively place "poke" in \'not_orderable\'.\n\nTentative assignment:\n- Orderable: kimchi\n- Not orderable: poke\n\n6. Following this assignment:\n   - Abe, Cy, Eli, Mel, Raj, Gen, and Ken are satisfied because "poke" is \'not_orderable\', and their dislikes are met.\n   - Mina is already satisfied by "kimchi".\n\n7. The food item "cake" is liked by many, but it\'s also disliked by some. However, placing "cake" on the \'orderable\' list will satisfy Joe, Eva, Tia, Rohan, Vic, Hal, Sol, and Dan.\n\nTentative assignment:\n- Orderable: kimchi, cake\n- Not orderable: poke\n\n8. Now let\'s see if there are any conflicts with this latest assignment:\n   - "tacos" is tricky because while it is liked by some (Bao, Zara, Bea, Wei, Rex), it is also disliked by some who are not satisfied by either kimchi or cake (Cy, Eli, Mel).\n   - "biscuits" is liked by many, but also disliked by a significant number (Abe, Leo, Tara, Aarav, Joy, Vic, Lee, Pam, Sol, Nita).\n\n9. At this point, it is evident that we cannot satisfy everyone using this method, as "tacos", "biscuits", "ratatouille", and "wonton" all have conflicting likes and dislikes, and we cannot add them to either \'orderable\' or \'not_orderable\' lists without causing dissatisfaction for some individuals.\n\nThe final conclusion is that there is no way to satisfy everyone with the given combinations of likes and dislikes, and the lists for \'orderable\' and \'not_orderable\' items will both be empty.\n\nFinal output:\n```python\norderable = []\nnot_orderable = []\n```', 'num_prompt_tokens': 823, 'num_completion_tokens': 966}
Data Sample: {'num_vars': 7, 'num_clauses': 35, 'formula': [[2, -1, -6], [6, 1, 2], [-2, -4, -3], [7, 3, 1], [-7, -5, -2], [-6, 2, 5], [5, 6, 1], [5, 4, -7], [2, 6, -1], [-7, 3, 5], [-3, 2, -7], [5, -2, 6], [-6, 4, 3], [-6, 3, -4], [-4, -2, -7], [4, 2, -6], [2, -1, 4], [6, -4, 5], [-3, -4, -7], [3, 4, -6], [-6, -2, -3], [-1, -4, 2], [4, 6, -2], [2, -3, 7], [-2, 3, 7], [-3, 4, 1], [-5, -6, -1], [-2, -7, 6], [-4, 2, -1], [-1, 2, 6], [-4, 3, -2], [4, -6, -1], [-5, -6, 7], [6, -2, 5], [-6, 2, 7]], 'is_sat': False, 'preferences': 'Chen: Likes ravioli. Dislikes brownies, caramel. Cy: Likes caramel, brownies, ravioli. Sky: Dislikes ravioli, muffins, pho. Rex: Likes cake, pho, brownies. Kit: Dislikes cake, roti, ravioli. Cal: Likes ravioli, roti. Dislikes caramel. Bao: Likes roti, caramel, brownies. Gia: Likes roti, muffins. Dislikes cake. Ying: Likes ravioli, caramel. Dislikes brownies. Sid: Likes pho, roti. Dislikes cake. Ava: Likes ravioli. Dislikes pho, cake. Isha: Likes roti, caramel. Dislikes ravioli. Jaya: Likes muffins, pho. Dislikes caramel. Arun: Likes pho. Dislikes caramel, muffins. Ken: Dislikes muffins, ravioli, cake. Veer: Likes muffins, ravioli. Dislikes caramel. Zoe: Likes ravioli, muffins. Dislikes brownies. Ping: Likes caramel, roti. Dislikes muffins. Tom: Dislikes pho, muffins, cake. Lou: Likes pho, muffins. Dislikes caramel. Joy: Dislikes caramel, ravioli, pho. Kavi: Likes ravioli. Dislikes brownies, muffins. Mei: Likes muffins, caramel. Dislikes ravioli. Sal: Likes ravioli, cake. Dislikes pho. Lia: Likes pho, cake. Dislikes ravioli. Om: Likes muffins, brownies. Dislikes pho. Jun: Dislikes roti, caramel, brownies. Anil: Likes caramel. Dislikes ravioli, cake. Ron: Likes ravioli. Dislikes muffins, brownies. Liz: Likes ravioli, caramel. Dislikes brownies. Mina: Likes pho. Dislikes muffins, ravioli. Aarav: Likes muffins. Dislikes caramel, brownies. Ivy: Likes cake. Dislikes roti, caramel. Nita: Likes caramel, roti. Dislikes ravioli. Lee: Likes ravioli, cake. Dislikes caramel. ', 'menu_items': ['brownies', 'ravioli', 'pho', 'muffins', 'roti', 'caramel', 'cake'], 'gpt_out': "To solve this, we must find a solution that respects all likes and dislikes of every individual, ensuring satisfaction according to the criteria given. Let's start by grouping the likes and dislikes to see if we can find common items and patterns:\n\n**Likes Ravioli**: \nChen, Cy, Cal, Ying, Ava, Kavi, Sal, Ron, Liz, Lee\n\n**Dislikes Ravioli**: \nSky, Kit, Isha, Joy, Mei, Mina, Nita\n\n**Likes Caramel**: \nCy, Bao, Ying, Isha, Ping, Mei, Anil, Liz, Nita\n\n**Dislikes Caramel**: \nChen, Cal, Jaya, Arun, Lou, Joy, Tom, Jun, Ivy, Lee\n\n**Likes Cake**: \nRex, Ivy, Lia, Sal, Lee\n\n**Dislikes Cake**:\nKit, Gia, Sid, Ava, Ken, Tom\n\n**Likes Pho**:\nRex, Jaya, Arun, Sid, Mina, Lia\n\n**Dislikes Pho**:\nSky, Joy, Sal, Om, Mei, Tom, Joy\n\n**Likes Brownies**: \nCy, Rex, Bao\n\n**Dislikes Brownies**: \nChen, Ying, Kavi, Jun, Ron, Aarav, Liz\n\nImmediately, we notice that ravioli is liked by many but also disliked by a significant number. This means ravioli cannot be a part of the 'orderable' list as it will dissatisfy those who dislike it. Hence, ravioli must be part of the 'not_orderable' list.\n\nNow, let's look at caramel, which is also commonly liked and disliked. If we could make caramel orderable, those who like caramel and don't have any other likes would be satisfied. However, since it is also commonly disliked, we cannot satisfy those who dislike caramel if it is orderable; we have to make caramel not orderable.\n\nGiven these considerations, our initial lists might look like this:\n\n**Orderable**: (still to be determined)\n**Not Orderable**: ravioli, caramel\n\nNow let's see if we can find a solution that satisfies the remaining preferences.\n\n**Looking for Orderable Item Candidates**:\n- Cake: Liked by 5 people and disliked by 6. It cannot be orderable because it would dissatisfy those who dislike it.\n- Pho: Liked by 6 people and disliked by 7. It cannot be orderable because it would dissatisfy those who dislike it.\n- Brownies: Liked by 3 people and disliked by 7. It cannot be orderable because it would dissatisfy those who dislike it.\n- Roti: Liked by many. Not everyone who likes roti has another like. This seems potentially orderable, so we'll keep it in mind.\n- Muffins: Muffins are liked by several people. Let's examine further.\n\n**Working with Potentially Orderable Items (Roti and Muffins)**:\nIf we make roti 'orderable', it will satisfy Cal, Bao, Sid, Gia, Ping, Isha, and Nita who all like roti and also dislike caramel or ravioli (already not orderable), so they would be satisfied.\n\nIf we make muffins 'orderable', considering that caramel and ravioli are already not orderable, it will satisfy those who like muffins and don't have any other likes except caramel or ravioli, such as Veer, Zoe, and Aarav.\n\nThis means both roti and muffins could potentially be on the 'orderable' list, as there is no immediate conflict. However, we must ensure that nobody who dislikes these items has a dislike solely for items on the 'not_orderable' list.\n\nWe notice that Jun dislikes roti and dislikes caramel and brownies as well, which means Jun would be satisfied if roti is orderable because caramel and brownies will not be ordered. The same logic applies to Ken, who dislikes cake (not issue if cake is not orderable) and dislikes muffins too.\n\nSo, we may have found a satisfactory combination:\n\n**Final Orderable**: roti, muffins\n**Final Not Orderable**: ravioli, caramel\n\nWe need to ensure that everyone's likes and dislikes are covered. Since we have at least one liked item or one disliked item in the corresponding list for all individuals, every person from the group should be satisfied with this combination.\n\nTherefore, the solution would be represented in Python as follows:\n\n```python\norderable=['roti', 'muffins'], not_orderable=['ravioli', 'caramel']\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 673, 'num_complet