art looking for a solution is by trying to find an item that is liked by many and disliked by few, or an item that is disliked by many and liked by a few, to minimize dissatisfaction:\n\n- "omelette" is liked by several people and only a few have it listed as a dislike. This looks like a strong candidate for \'orderable\'.\n- "macaron" is liked by many but is also disliked by multiple people.\n- "gelato" and "lobster" are both highly contested and cannot easily be placed in either list.\n- "salad" and "churros" are both liked and disliked by many.\n\nGiven the high overlap and no clear-cut solution, we shall then have to try and satisfy each person by other means. It appears that due to the high degree of overlap among the likes and dislikes, satisfying all individuals may not be possible.\n\nIn such a case, the criteria state that if no such combination exists that satisfies all, we must output empty lists for both \'orderable\' and \'not_orderable\'.\n\nSince the preferences are highly conflicting and have no clear solutions, the satisfy all criteria are not met for any combination, here is the final output:\n\n```python\norderable = [], not_orderable = []\n```', 'num_prompt_tokens': 1037, 'num_completion_tokens': 742}
