 and contrasting conditions to decide how we can satisfy everyone's preferences.\n\nFirst, we'll collect all the likes and dislikes.\n\nLiked items:\n- Kebab is liked by Hui, Ru, Sia, Hu, Abe, Rob, Veer, Wei, Jon, Nic, and Li.\n- Pie is liked by Hui, Tim, Ru, Kit, Lee, Taj, Bo, Wei, Rex, Ana, and Mina.\n- Naan is liked by Tim, Kit, Val, Li, Ping, Jan, and Joe.\n- Toast is liked by Ru, Jaya, Lou, Taj, Val, Bo, Eli, Xan, Raj, Ana, and Joe.\n\nDisliked items:\n- Kebab is disliked by Tim, Kit, Lee, Jaya, Lou, Taj, Val, Bo, Xiu, Lin, Xan, Raj, Ana, Diya, and Joe.\n- Naan is disliked by Hui, Sia, Lou, Rob, Eli, Lin, Rex, Jon, and Joe.\n- Pie is disliked by Pam, Xiu, Veer, Nic, Lin, Jan, Raj, and Diya.\n- Toast is disliked by Lee, Sia, Hu, Rob, Lin, Rex, Ping, Wei, Jon, Xan, and Diya.\n\nWe can immediately see that 'toast' is the only food item liked by some but not disliked by anyone, making it a safe choice to include in the 'orderable' list. Meanwhile, 'kebab', 'pie', and 'naan' are all liked by some and disliked by others, so we must be careful with these items. \n\nTo satisfy participants:\n- Since 'toast' is liked by some and not disliked by anyone, we can put 'toast' in 'orderable'.\n- If 'kebab', 'pie', or 'naan' is 'orderable', then all who dislike that food must have their disliked food in the 'not_orderable' list to be satisfied.\n- Conversely, if 'kebab', 'pie', or 'naan' is in 'not_orderable', all who like that food must have another liked food or a disliked food in the respective list.\n\nStarting with 'toast' in 'orderable', we cannot put 'kebab', 'pie', or 'naan' in 'orderable' because someone dislikes each of those items, and there isn't any other liked item that could be placed in 'orderable' without conflict. Therefore, 'kebab', 'pie', and 'naan' must all be in 'not_orderable'.\n\nBy placing 'kebab', 'pie', and 'naan' in 'not_orderable' and 'toast' in 'orderable', we satisfy all criterias:\n- People who like 'toast' will be satisfied since it's 'orderable'.\n- People who dislike 'kebab', 'naan', or 'pie' will be satisfied since they are 'not_orderable'.\n- People who like 'kebab', 'naan', or 'pie' are satisfied because they also like 'toast' or have a disliked item in 'not_orderable'.\n- There are no contradictory conditions as no item appears on both lists.\n\nThus, we have a viable solution:\n\n```python\norderable=['toast'], not_orderable=['kebab', 'naan', 'pie']\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 592, 'num_completion_tokens': 746}
