 gelato. Dislikes peach, toast. Qi: Likes peach. Dislikes gelato, kebab. Tim: Likes gelato, espresso. Dislikes peach. Sky: Likes peach, espresso. Dislikes kebab. Aarav: Likes gelato, peach. Dislikes kebab. Arun: Likes kebab. Dislikes espresso, peach. Mei: Likes kebab. Dislikes peach, espresso. Wei: Likes espresso, kebab. Dislikes gelato. Gus: Likes toast. Dislikes espresso, kebab. Jun: Likes peach. Dislikes toast, gelato. Lei: Likes gelato. Dislikes kebab, toast. Kit: Likes kebab. Dislikes espresso, peach. May: Likes toast, gelato, kebab. Raj: Likes toast, kebab. Dislikes gelato. Nita: Likes espresso, toast. Dislikes kebab. Jay: Likes gelato, kebab, peach. Ava: Dislikes peach, gelato, toast. Ana: Likes espresso. Dislikes kebab, peach. Ray: Likes peach. Dislikes gelato, toast. Hui: Likes toast. Dislikes kebab, peach. Yue: Likes toast. Dislikes gelato, peach. Tao: Likes espresso, kebab. Dislikes toast. Rex: Likes toast. Dislikes espresso, peach. Cal: Likes gelato, espresso. Dislikes kebab. Gia: Likes espresso, toast. Dislikes kebab. Diya: Likes toast. Dislikes peach, kebab. ', 'menu_items': ['toast', 'kebab', 'gelato', 'espresso', 'peach'], 'gpt_out': "To solve this problem, we'll iterate through the lists of likes and dislikes to find a combination that satisfies everyone. Let's start by identifying items that appear in some people's likes and others' dislikes.\n\nFirst, list the items that are liked and disliked, and check to see if we have any items that can satisfy a person by being on either list:\n\n1. espresso: Liked by many, disliked by some.\n2. toast: Liked by some, disliked by many.\n3. gelato: Liked by many, disliked by some.\n4. kebab: Liked by many, disliked by many.\n5. peach: Liked by many, disliked by some.\n\nNow, we analyze the lists, paying attention to individual preferences. The goal is to assign items in such a way that, if possible, each person will have at least one liked item in 'orderable' or one disliked item in 'not_orderable'. Since the lists are long, to simplify the solution:\n- If an item is liked by someone and not disliked by anyone, it can safely go to 'orderable'.\n- Conversely, if an item is disliked by someone and not liked by anyone, it can safely go to 'not_orderable'.\n- If an item is liked by some and disliked by others, we have to be more careful, potentially needing to satisfy one group by placing the item in 'orderable' and the other by placing another item in 'not_orderable'.\n\nLet's start by examining the most controversial items, which are disliked by some but liked by many: kebab and gelato. Since both items are liked and disliked by different individuals, they will be key in satisfying the whole group. It's evident that either kebab or gelato must be 'orderable' or 'not_orderable' because if both were on the same list, it would be impossible to satisfy individuals who like one and dislike the other. Since kebab is disputed between many, we can initially position kebab in 'orderable' to satisfy those who like it.\n\nNow we need to consider those who dislike kebab. We need to find an item they all dislike or one they all like that can be placed in the opposite list to their dislike of kebab. A quick review shows that every person who dislikes kebab either likes espresso or gelato or dislikes peach or toast.\n\nTherefore, we have to place gelato in 'not_orderable' because if it's 'orderable', we cannot satisfy those who dislike it.\n\nNow that gelato is in 'not_orderable', Liz dislikes kebab and peach, so we can satisfy her with placing kebab in 'orderable'. It is the same for Liz, Sol, Geet, Tom, Rohan, Mel, Yan, Eli, and Ned who dislike gelato but like kebab.\n\nWe will now place\n- kebab in 'orderable' to satisfy those who like it and dislike gelato, peach, or toast (since satisfying them with any of these will leave others unsatisfied)\n- gelato will be in 'not_orderable' to satisfy those who dislike it (since these people all like kebab or espresso or both).\n\nThat leaves us with espresso, toast, and peach. Since espresso is liked by many and not universally disliked by any, we place espresso in 'orderable'.\n\nNow, we can see that peach cannot be in 'orderable' because that would leave some individuals unsatisfied (those who dislike peach but don't have any liked item in 'orderable'). Therefore, peach must be in 'not_orderable'.\n\nFinally, toast is liked by some and disliked by others, but since those who dislike it are satisfied with espresso or peach on the other lists, toast can be put in either. We'll add toast to 'orderable' since that will satisfy those who like it and not cause any conflicts.\n\nOur final lists are as follows:\n- orderable: kebab, espresso, toast\n- not_orderable: gelato, peach\n\nNow let's convert this to the required Python code format:\n```python\norderable=['kebab', 'espresso', 'toast'], not_orderable=['gelato', 'peach']\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 868, 'num_completion_tokens': 858}
Data Sample: {'num_vars': 5, 'num_clauses': 50, 'formula': [[-1, -5, -2], [1, -5, 2], [-2, 3, 4], [-5, -4, 2], [-5, 3, 2], [-1, 2, 5], [-3, 2, -5], [3, 4, 1], [4, -2, -1], [1, 4, 5], [2, -4, -1], [-2, -5, -4], [-3, -4, -2], [-3, 1, -4], [5, 1, 3], [-4, -2, 1], [4, -3, -2], [-2, -1, 4], [-2, 3, -1], [4, 2, 3], [-5, 1, -4], [1, -4, 3], [2, 4, -3], [2, 3, 4], [-5, -3, 4], [-1, 3, 2], [-1, 4, -3], [4, 2, 5], [2, -4, -3], [-5, 1, 4], [4, 2, -3], [5, 1, -4], [2, -4, -1], [-2, 5, -4], [-1, -5, -3], [-4, -2, -3], [-4, -5, -1], [2, -3, -4], [1, -4, -3], [-2, -1, -5], [3, -4, 1], [-2, -3, -1], [-4, -5, 3], [4, 1, -5], [1, -5, 2], [-3, -5, -2], [2, 3, 4], [-3, -4, 1], [4, -2, 1], [1, -3, -2]], 'is_sat': False, 'preferences': 'Lei: Dislikes falafel, frittata, quiche. Jim: Likes falafel, quiche. Dislikes frittata. Wei: Likes wonton, fries. Dislikes quiche. Diya: Likes quiche. Dislikes frittata, fries. Shan: Likes wonton, quiche. Dislikes frittata. Lin: Likes quiche, frittata. Dislikes falafel. Nic: Likes quiche. Dislikes wonton, frittata. Vic: Likes wonton, fries, falafel. Mina: Likes fries. Dislikes quiche, falafel. Nita: Likes falafel, fries, frittata. Sam: Likes quiche. Dislikes fries, falafel. Qi: Dislikes quiche, frittata, fries. Mel: Dislikes wonton, fries, quiche. Joe: Likes falafel. Dislikes wonton, fries. Min: Likes frittata, falafel, wonton. Geet: Likes falafel. Dislikes fries, quiche. Eli: Likes fries. Dislikes wonton, quiche. Taj: Likes fries. Dislikes quiche, falafel. Pat: Likes wonton. Dislikes quiche, falafel. Sid: Likes fries, quiche, wonton. Oli: Likes falafel. Dislikes frittata, fries. Feng: Likes falafel, wonton. Dislikes fries. Joy: Likes quiche, fries. Dislikes wonton. Neil: Likes quiche, wonton, fries. Ula: Likes fries. Dislikes frittata, wonton. Mia: Likes wonton, quiche. Dislikes falafel. Dot: Likes fries. Dislikes falafel, wonton. Rohan: Likes fries, quiche, frittata. Raj: Likes quiche. Dislikes fries, wonton. Ru: Likes falafel, fries. Dislikes frittata. Naina: Likes fries, quiche. Dislikes wonton. Kai: Likes frittata, falafel. Dislikes fries. Tom: Likes quiche. Dislikes fries, falafel. Ying: Likes frittata. Dislikes quiche, fries. Ravi: Dislikes falafel, frittata, wonton. Du: Dislikes fries, quiche, wonton. Bo: Dislikes fries, frittata, falafel. Gen: Likes quiche. Dislikes wonton, fries. Lia: Likes falafel. Dislikes fries, wonton. Hal: Dislikes quiche, falafel, frittata. Bao: Likes wonton, falafel. Dislikes fries. Eva: Dislikes quiche, wonton, falafel. Riya: Likes wonton. Dislikes fries, frittata. Zoe: Likes fries, falafel. Dislikes frittata. Sal: Likes falafel, quiche. Dislikes frittata. Fay: Dislikes wonton, frittata, quiche. Ada: Likes quiche, wonton, fries. Kim: Likes falafel. Dislikes wonton, fries. Ava: Likes fries, falafel. Dislikes quiche. Tara: Likes falafel. Dislikes wonton, quiche. ', 'menu_items': ['falafel', 'quiche', 'wonton', 'fries', 'frittata'], 'gpt_out': 'To create lists that satisfy everyone\'s tastes, we need to follow the conditions provided, which will require an iterative approach to ensure no conflict arises. Let\'s start by creating a list of all the items liked and disliked.\n\nFirst, let\'s list all the items liked by anyone:\n- Falafel\n- Wonton\n- Fries\n- Quiche\n- Frittata\n\nNow let\'s list all the items disliked by anyone:\n- Falafel\n- Wonton\n- Fries\n- Quiche\n- Frittata\n\nGiven that the disliked list has all the items from the liked list, we are going to have to find an item that is liked by some but not disliked by others, or vice versa, to satisfy everyone.\n\nLet\'s deep dive into each case:\n\n1. If an item is disliked by someone but is not liked by anybody else, it can go straight into the \'not_orderable\' list.\n\n   - However, no such item exists since every disliked item is also liked by someone else.\n\n2. If an item is liked by someone and not disliked by anyone else, it can go into the \'orderable\' list.\n\n   - No item satisfies this condition either, since every liked item is also disliked by at least one person.\n\nFor this scenario, there are no clear-cut items that we can easily place into \'orderable\' or \'not_orderable\' lists without further consideration. This means we need to compare the likes and dislikes in more depth.\n\nUpon closer examination, we can observe the following:\n\n- "Frittata" is disliked by many people, but the only people who like it (Lin, Nita, Ying, Kai, and Rohan) have other likes: Lin likes "quiche," Nita and Rohan like "fries," Ying has no other like but dislikes "fries," and Kai has no other like but dislikes "fries." Therefore, placing "frittata" in \'not_orderable\' might be an option.\n\n- "Falafel" is similar; it\'s disliked by many, but those who like it have other likes: Lei dislikes "falafel" but has no other preferences, Jim likes "quiche," Joe has no other like, Min likes "wonton," Geet has no other like, Oli has no other like, Bao likes "wonton," Lia likes "quiche," Kim has no other like and several others. Even though it\'s complex, we can consider putting "falafel" in the \'not_orderable\' list as most who like it also like something else.\n\n- For "wonton" and "fries," these are liked by many, and many others dislike quiche which overlaps with those who like "wonton" and "fries." Thus "quiche" might be a candidate for the \'orderable\' list because, for most who like "quiche," it is the only item they like, and it\'s also disliked by some.\n\n- We need to be careful with "wonton" and "fries" as they are both liked and disliked by numerous people, and it\'s not as straightforward to satisfy those who like these items with other options.\n\nGiven the overlapping likes and dislikes, and the constraint that every person must be satisfied with either the presence of a liked item or the absence of a disliked item, we can draw the following conclusion:\n- "Frittata" should be in the \'not_orderable\' list since it satisfies a significant number (including those who dislike it and don\'t have it among their likes).\n- "Quiche" should be in the \'orderable\' list because, for most individuals who like it, it\'s their only like, and several dislike it.\n- Leaving "wonton" and "fries" undecided for now, we will look into "falafel." \n\nIf "falafel" is in \'not_orderable\', Lei, Jim, Min, Geet, Oli, and Kim will be left unsatisfied because it is the only like for them and there are no corresponding dislikes to satisfy them. Hence, "falafel" cannot be in \'not_orderable\'. \n\nTherefore, the only solution that satisfies everyone is to have:\n\n- "Quiche" in the \'orderable\' list.\n- "Frittata" in the \'not_orderable\' list.\n\nAnd for the remaining items, we need to satisfy the condition where individuals who dislike the item must have a liked item from the \'orderable\' list or another disliked item in \'not_orderable\'. Since "falafel" cannot be in \'not_orderable\' (otherwise some people would remain unsatisfied), we will consider this:\n\n- Adding "falafel" to \'orderable\' will satisfy Lei, Jim, Min, Geet, Oli, and Kim as it\'s the only item they like.\n\nTo satisfy those who dislike "falafel" but like "quiche" (Mia, Raj, Gen, etc.), having "quiche" in the \'orderable\' list meets their needs. The remaining individuals who dislike "falafel" but have alternative likes (Sam and Tara like "quiche"; Joe, Bao, Lia, and Ava like other items) can be satisfied with their other preferences.\n\n- We will not place "wonton" or "fries" in the \'orderable\' or \'not_orderable\' list as they are both widely liked and disliked, which would make it challenging to satisfy all individuals. So, we avoid these contentious items.\n\nEvery person has at least one item they like in the \'orderable\' or a disapproved item in \'not_orderable\', under the arrangements of "quiche" in \'orderable\', "frittata" in \'not_orderable\', with "wonaton" and "fries" unlisted, and adding "falafel" to \'orderable\' for those who have no other liked items.\n\nLet\'s summarize the final solution:\n\n```python\norderable = [\'quiche\', \'falafel\']\nnot_orderable = [\'frittata\']\n```\n\nThis combination should satisfy everyone\'s preferences according to the criteria: a person is satisfied if at least one liked item is in \'orderable\' or one disliked item is in \'not_orderable\', and each item can only appear in one list, with all participants satisfied with the combination.', 'num_prompt_tokens': 897, 'num_completion_tokens': 1326}
Data Sample: {'num_vars': 6, 'num_clauses': 12, 'formula': [[-4, 5, -6], [4, 6, -5], [5, -3, -4], [-1, -4, 2], [5, -3, -6], [1, 5, -2], [-6, -4, -3], [-4, 2, 3], [-5, -4, -6], [3, -6, 1], [6, -3, 1], [2, 1, -5]], 'is_sat': True, 'preferences': 'Hal: Likes cake. Dislikes tart, pie. Ned: Likes tart, pie. Dislikes cake. Chao: Likes cake. Dislikes burger, tart. Raj: Likes omelette. Dislikes vindaloo, tart. Ada: Likes cake. Dislikes burger, pie. Eva: Likes vindaloo, cake. Dislikes omelette. Mei: Dislikes pie, tart, burger. Kavi: Likes omelette, burger. Dislikes tart. Ansh: Dislikes cake, tart, pie. Riya: Likes burger, vindaloo. Dislikes pie. Bo: Likes pie, vindaloo. Dislikes burger. Kim: Likes omelette, vindaloo. Dislikes cake. ', 'menu_items': ['vindaloo', 'omelette', 'burger', 'tart', 'cake', 'pie'], 'gpt_out': "To find a solution, we will start listing out the preferences of each person first and then we will try to place food items in 'orderable' and 'not_orderable' lists to maintain satisfaction for everyone.\n\nHal: Likes cake. Dislikes tart, pie.\nNed: Likes tart, pie. Dislikes cake.\nChao: Likes cake. Dislikes burger, tart.\nRaj: Likes omelette. Dislikes vindaloo, tart.\nAda: Likes cake. Dislikes burger, pie.\nEva: Likes vindaloo, cake. Dislikes omelette.\nMei: Dislikes pie, tart, burger.\nKavi: Likes omelette, burger. Dislikes tart.\nAnsh: Dislikes cake, tart, pie.\nRiya: Likes burger, vindaloo. Dislikes pie.\nBo: Likes pie, vindaloo. Dislikes burger.\nKim: Likes omelette, vindaloo. Dislikes cake.\n\nNow, let's analyze:\n\n1. Cake is liked by Hal, Chao, Ada, and Eva, but it is disliked by Ned, Ansh, and Kim. If we put cake in 'orderable', Ned, Ansh, and Kim will not be satisfied. If we put cake in 'not_orderable', that doesn’t satisfy Hal, Chao, Ada, and Eva as none of their liked items are in 'orderable' list. Therefore, cake should not be on either list to keep both groups potentially satisfied.\n\n2. Tart is liked by Ned, but disliked by Hal, Chao, Raj, Kavi, Mei, Ansh, and Kim. There are more people disliking tart than liking it, so we can satisfy Ned’s dislike and all the people who dislike tart by placing it in 'not_orderable'.\n\n3. Pie is liked by Ned and Bo, but disliked by Hal, Ada, Mei, Ansh, and Riya. More people dislike pie, so we can place it in 'not_orderable' to satisfy the dislikes of these individuals.\n\n4. Burger is liked by Chao, Kavi, and Riya, but disliked by Eva, Mei, and Bo. Since burger is disliked by individuals who have other likes (Eva likes vindaloo and cake, Bo likes pie and vindaloo, and Mei dislikes multiple items), we can put burger in 'orderable' to satisfy Chao, Kavi, and Riya.\n\n5. Omelette is liked by Raj, Kavi, and Kim, but disliked by Eva. Since there are more people who like omelette and Eva has other likes, we can place omelette in 'orderable'.\n\n6. Vindaloo is liked by Eva, Riya, Bo, and Kim, but disliked by Raj. Vindaloo can be placed in 'orderable' since Raj has other liked items (omelette).\n\nNow, let's write out the lists based on these deductions:\n\norderable: burger, omelette, vindaloo\nnot_orderable: tart, pie\n\nLet's verify that every person's preferences are satisfied with these lists:\n\nHal: cake not in any list (neutral), dislikes tart and pie (satisfied with both in 'not_orderable').\nNed: likes tart and pie (satisfied with dislikes orderable), dislikes cake (neutral).\nChao: likes cake (neutral), dislikes burger (not satisfied because burger is in 'orderable').\nRaj: likes omelette (satisfied), dislikes vindaloo and tart (tart is in 'not_orderable').\nAda: likes cake (neutral), dislikes burger and pie (satisfied with pie in 'not_orderable').\nEva: likes vindaloo and cake (satisfied with vindaloo in 'orderable'), dislikes omelette (conflicting, as omelette is in 'orderable').\nMei: dislikes pie, tart, and burger (satisfied with 'not_orderable'; burger conflict can be ignored as other two dislikes are in 'not_orderable').\nKavi: likes omelette and burger (satisfied), dislikes tart (tart is in 'not_orderable').\nAnsh: dislikes cake, tart, and pie (satisfied with tart and pie in 'not_orderable').\nRiya: likes burger and vindaloo (satisfied), dislikes pie (pie is in 'not_orderable').\nBo: likes pie and vindaloo (satisfied with vindaloo in 'orderable'), dislikes burger (conflict, burger is 'orderable').\nKim: likes omelette and vindalou (satisfied), dislikes cake (neutral).\n\nBased on the issue raised with Chao, Eva, and Bo (due to their conflicts with the 'orderable' list), it seems we've made a mistake in our analysis. Burger conflicts with Chao and Bo's dislikes, and omelette conflicts with Eva's dislike.\n\nTherefore, we have to remove burger and omelette from the 'orderable' list and reconsider the other items based on overall satisfaction.\n\nReevaluation:\n\nIf we remove burger and omelette from 'orderable', we see that our lists do not satisfy Kavi who likes burger and omelette with omelette originally in 'orderable'. We have no way to satisfy Kavi's likes. Similarly, without omelette in 'orderable', Raj is also not satisfied. Since we must satisfy all participants, we've reached a conflict without a valid solution to satisfy everyone's preferences at once given the current deductions.\n\nThe final solution means that no combination of 'orderable' and 'not_orderable' exists to satisfy everyone. Therefore, the final output is:\n\n```python\norderable=[], not_orderable=[]\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 344, 'num_completion_tokens': 1182}
