able\' list would satisfy a subset of individuals.\n\nTo analyze this effectively, let\'s organize the preferences regarding “dislikes” first since including a disliked item in the \'not_orderable\' list could satisfy those who dislike it without contradicting the likes of others:\n\n1. Poke - Disliked by: Isha, May, Neil, Xiu, Du, Sal, Mei, Gus, Kim, Liz, Jon\n2. Wonton - Disliked by: Isha, Diya, Neil, Qi, Du, Kim, Gus, Jon, Ty\n3. Salad - Disliked by: Abe, Mina, Liz\n4. Soup - Disliked by: Meg, May, Ray, Qi, Xiu, Jon, Jim, Li, Ty\n\nFrom the dislikes, we observe that “salad” and “soup” have the fewest dislikes and thus have potential to be in the \'orderable\' list. Placing "poke" or "wonton" in the \'not_orderable\' list would satisfy the individuals who dislike them but, “wonton” has more individuals who like it and none who dislike it alone, making it a strong candidate for the \'orderable\' list.\n\nLet’s start our potential \'orderable\' list with "wonton" since every person besides the ones who dislike it (but also dislike other things) likes it, and it does not seem to immediately exclude anyone if we can place disliked food in the \'not_orderable\' list for those who dislike it:\n\n\'orderable\': [wonton]\n\nGiven this, let\'s see how many individuals\' dislikes can be satisfied with the remaining three items. We\'ll start by placing "poke" on the \'not_orderable\' list as it is the most disliked item:\n\n\'not_orderable\': [poke]\n\nWith "wonton" on the \'orderable\' list and "poke" on the \'not_orderable,\' we need to satisfy the remaining individuals:\n\n- Soup (has several likes and dislikes): Meg, May, Ray, Qi, Xiu, Jon, Jim, Li, Ty are satisfied because they dislike "soup" and it\'s neither orderable nor not_orderable.\n- Salad (has several likes and fewer dislikes): Can’t be placed in \'not_orderable\' because Isha and Ying like both “poke” and “salad."\n\nSince "soup" and "salad" remain and we know that every individual likes one of "wonton," "soup," or "salad," there is no need to include "soup" in the \'orderable\' list to satisfy those who like it because we would upset those who dislike "soup." \n\nThis approach has left everyone satisfied with the current \'orderable\' and \'not_orderable\' setup. Those who like "wonton" are satisfied with it being orderable, those who dislike "poke" are satisfied with it being not orderable, and the remaining individuals are satisfied since at least one of their dislikes is neither explicitly orderable nor not orderable (e.g., "soup" and "salad").\n\nThus, the \'orderable\' and \'not_orderable\' lists that would satisfy everyone are:\n\n\'orderable\': [wonton]\n\'not_orderable\': [poke]\n\nOutput as requested is:\n\norderable=[\'wonton\'], not_orderable=[\'poke\']', 'num_prompt_tokens': 514, 'num_completion_tokens': 789}
