er]\n- Shan: [soup, lobster]\n- Min: [lobster, lasagna]\n- Riya: [pho]\n- Liv: [lobster, paneer]\n- Pat: [pho]\n- Xan: [lasagna, soup]\n- Raj: [nachos, lasagna]\n- Tom: [udon, risotto]\n- Wes: [lasagna]\n- Ned: [lobster, lasagna]\n- Rob: [soup, risotto]\n- Ava: []\n- Jan: [lobster]\n- Val: [soup]\n- Taj: [nachos]\n- Kavi: []\n- Pia: [udon]\n\nWe cannot have any item appearing on both lists, and each individual must have at least one like in 'orderable' or one dislike in 'not_orderable'. Items with unanimous dislikes can immediately go into 'not_orderable', and items with unanimous likes (or items that can satisfy without breaking any of the dislikes) can go into 'orderable'.\n\nUpon reviewing the data, here are the observable safe choices for each list:\n\n'orderable' candidates (if not exclusively disliked by any):\n- risotto (liked by Gus, Pat, Jan; disliked by Tom, Rob - conflict)\n- paneer (liked by Shan; disliked by Gus, Liv - conflict)\n- cake (liked by Min, Pat, Wes, Jan; disliked by none)\n- udon (liked by Riya, Raj, Ned, Ava, Kavi; disliked by Tom, Pia - conflict)\n- pho (liked by Liv, Taj; disliked by Riya, Pat - conflict)\n- nachos (liked by Xan, Rob, Ava, Val, Kavi, Pia; disliked by Raj, Taj - conflict)\n- lasagna (liked by Val, Kavi; disliked by Min, Xan, Wes, Ned - conflict)\n\n'not_orderable' candidates:\n- soup (liked by Gus, Wes, Ava, Taj, Pia; disliked by Shan, Xan, Rob, Val - conflict)\n- lobster (liked by Shan, Riya, Min, Tom; disliked by Liv, Ned - conflict)\n\nNo item is universally disliked or liked without causing a conflict, so there is no obvious choice. We need to determine which items can be ordered without making any individual unsatisfied. Looking at 'not_orderable' first, we see that only one person, Tom, dislikes risotto and would be unsatisfied if it is not orderable, but likes lobster which is liked by others and cannot be in 'not_orderable'. So risotto must be in 'orderable'. Taking risotto as orderable, this satisfies Gus, Pat, and Jan.\n\nWith risotto in 'orderable', Rob is unsatisfied as his likes cannot be accommodated now. Let's consider putting soup in 'not_orderable', as Ava, who likes soup, can be satisfied with nachos, udon, or both.\n\nNow consider lobster — since soup is disliked by several characters and could be 'not_orderable', placing lobster in 'orderable' could work. This would satisfy Riya, Tom, and possibly Shan.\n\nNext, for paneer, since we placed lobster in 'orderable', Shan can be satisfied with lobster, allowing paneer to be in 'not_orderable', satisfying Gus and Liv.\n\nWe focus on nachos — if nachos belong in 'orderable', they could satisfy Rob, Ava, Val, Kavi, and Pia.\n\nNow, udon is liked by several but disliked by a few. Since Pia dislikes udon but likes nachos (in 'orderable'), we can place udon in 'orderable' to satisfy Ava, Raj, Ned, Kavi. Pia will be satisfied with nachos and soup being 'not_orderable'.\n\nSince pho is only disliked by two individuals (Riya, who can rely on lobster, and Pat, who can rely on cake or risotto), we can place it in 'orderable' to satisfy Liv and Taj.\n\nLasagna is the last item to consider; it's disliked by several participants and can be placed in 'not_orderable'.\n\nLet's review the likes and check all are satisfied:\n- Gus is satisfied with risotto being orderable.\n- Shan is satisfied with lobster being orderable.\n- Min is satisfied with cake being orderable.\n- Riya is satisfied with lobster being orderable.\n- Liv is satisfied with pho being orderable.\n- Pat is satisfied with risotto or cake being orderable.\n- Xan is satisfied with nachos or soup being not_orderable.\n- Raj is satisfied with udon being orderable.\n- Tom is satisfied with lobster being orderable.\n- Wes is satisfied with cake or soup being not_orderable.\n- Ned is satisfied with udon being orderable.\n- Rob is satisfied with nachos being orderable.\n- Ava is satisfied with nachos, udon, or soup being not_orderable.\n- Jan is satisfied with risotto or cake being orderable.\n- Val is satisfied with lasagna being not_orderable.\n- Taj is satisfied with soup being not_orderable.\n- Kavi is satisfied with nachos or udon being orderable.\n- Pia is satisfied with nachos being orderable or udon being not_orderable.\n\nOur final lists are thus:\n'orderable': [cake, nachos, lobster, risotto, udon, pho]\n'not_orderable': [paneer, lasagna, soup]\n\nEveryone is satisfied with at least one item on one of the lists according to their preferences, and we've achieved the goal of creating two distinct lists without any overlaps.\n\nTherefore, the output is:\n```\norderable=['cake', 'nachos', 'lobster', 'risotto', 'udon', 'pho'], not_orderable=['paneer', 'lasagna', 'soup']\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 425, 'num_completion_tokens': 1468}
Data Sample: {'num_vars': 9, 'num_clauses': 18, 'formula': [[5, -6, -4], [-6, 7, 4], [8, -6, 7], [-4, 6, -5], [2, 5, -8], [-1, 9, -2], [-4, -7, 3], [6, -2, -5], [-6, 4, -5], [9, 8, -6], [2, 7, -5], [2, 5, 8], [-6, 3, 8], [-2, 6, -8], [6, 9, -8], [2, -4, -6], [-8, 6, -5], [-9, -7, 5]], 'is_sat': True, 'preferences': 'Pat: Likes peach. Dislikes burger, vegetables. Rex: Likes waffles, vegetables. Dislikes burger. Dot: Likes paneer, waffles. Dislikes burger. Jay: Likes burger. Dislikes vegetables, peach. Taj: Likes tiramisu, peach. Dislikes paneer. Lan: Likes cake. Dislikes biscuits, tiramisu. Neil: Likes tart. Dislikes vegetables, waffles. Tia: Likes burger. Dislikes tiramisu, peach. Ian: Likes vegetables. Dislikes burger, peach. Hu: Likes cake, paneer. Dislikes burger. Liz: Likes tiramisu, waffles. Dislikes peach. Veer: Likes tiramisu, peach, paneer. Sid: Likes tart, paneer. Dislikes burger. Sol: Likes burger. Dislikes tiramisu, paneer. Diya: Likes burger, cake. Dislikes paneer. Amy: Likes tiramisu. Dislikes vegetables, burger. Meg: Likes burger. Dislikes paneer, peach. Lin: Likes peach. Dislikes cake, waffles. ', 'menu_items': ['biscuits', 'tiramisu', 'tart', 'vegetables', 'peach', 'burger', 'waffles', 'paneer', 'cake'], 'gpt_out': 'To solve this task, I will create two lists: \'orderable\' and \'not_orderable\'. I will start by finding items that can be immediately placed in \'not_orderable\' due to being disliked by everyone who mentions them, or due to only one person liking them but liking something else that can be ordered instead. This will simplify the number of preferences we have to account for.\n\nFrom the list of dislikes, one can notice that "burger" is disliked by many, but it is also liked by a few individuals. Since satisfying everyone who dislikes "burger" would leave those who like it unsatisfied, we can\'t directly place "burger" in either list without considering other likes and dislikes.\n\nLet\'s analyze further:\n1. "Burger" is the only item liked by Jay, Tia, Meg, Sol, and Diya. Hence it must go to the \'orderable\' list to satisfy them.\n2. Since "burger" goes to \'orderable\', this automatically places "vegetables", "peach", "tiramisu", and "paneer" into the \'not_orderable\' list as they are disliked by at least one person who likes "burger".\n3. "Tart" doesn\'t conflict with any of the current \'not_orderable\' items and is only liked by people who do not have any other likes on the \'not_orderable\' list (Neil and Sid). Therefore, it can go to the \'orderable\' list.\n4. "Cake" can also go to the \'orderable\' list since it\'s liked by Lan (who dislikes biscuits, tiramisu; tiramisu has already been put into the \'not_orderable\' list) and by Hu and Diya (who have other likes on the \'not_orderable\' list).\n5. "Waffles" is liked by Rex, Dot, Liz, and Lin but since it is disliked by Neil and has to be placed in the \'not_orderable\' list, Rex and Liz have to be satisfied some other way.\n6. Rex will be satisfied with "cake" (now in the \'orderable\' list), Liz will be satisfied with "tiramisu" (already in \'not_orderable\'), but Lin is left with no item to satisfy her preference as her only like, "peach", is \'not_orderable\'. Thus, we face a contradiction.\n\nGiven these constraints and the conflict with Lin\'s preferences, it seems that there is no combination possible that would satisfy everyone\'s likes and dislikes. Thus, both \'orderable\' and \'not_orderable\' lists have to be empty.\n\nOur final solution is:\n```python\norderable = [], not_orderable = []\n```', 'num_prompt_tokens': 418, 'num_completion_tokens': 552}
