likes custard. ', 'menu_items': ['soup', 'custard', 'bruschetta'], 'gpt_out': "Let's summarize and analyze the information provided for each individual to determine the 'orderable' and 'not_orderable' lists as follows:\n\nLikes Soup:\n- Kim, Nita, Li, Yue, Gus, Pam, Amy, Mina, Lin, Ravi\n\nDislikes Soup:\n- Ben, Bao, Sal, Diya, Sue, Ron, Kit, Chen, Max, Jan, Ying, Lan, Aarav, Hu, Qi, Naina, May, Ivy, Ru, Abe, Ian, Cy\n\nLikes Custard:\n- Ben, Bao, Diya, Nita, Kit, Chen, Jan, Yue, Gus, Ying, Lan, Hu, Pam, Amy, Ivy, Ru, May, Ian, Cy\n\nDislikes Custard:\n- Kim, Sal, Sue, Ron, Max, Li, Aarav, Mina, Lin, Xan\n\nLikes Bruschetta:\n- Kim, Diya, Sue, Ron, Jan, Yue, Qi, Naina, Ivy, Abe, Ian, Ravi, Cy\n\nDislikes Bruschetta:\n- Ben, Bao, Sal, Nita, Kit, Chen, Max, Li, Aarav, Hu, Lin, May, Ru, Mina, Pam, Amy\n\nBased on overall preferences, the only item that is unanimously disliked is bruschetta. Every other food item—soup and custard—is liked by some and disliked by others. Since there can't be a food item that is both ordered and not ordered and each person must be satisfied, our task is to ensure that every individual either likes something on the 'orderable' list or dislikes something on the 'not_orderable' list.\n\nGiven that bruschetta is the only item that is disliked by several people and not universally liked, it is a candidate for 'not_orderable':\n\nStarting with the 'not_orderable' list with bruschetta -- because it has the most dislikes without any group exclusively liking it:\nnot_orderable = [bruschetta]\n\nNow let's consider soup and custard.\n- For soup and custard, both are liked by at least one person who dislikes the other. This means we cannot place both in the 'orderable' list.\n- If we place soup in the 'orderable' list, all individuals who like soup (e.g., Kim) will be satisfied, and all individuals who dislike custard (e.g., Sal) will be satisfied because custard will not be ordered.\n- If we place custard in the 'orderable' list, all individuals who