Ned\n- Risotto: Vic, Anil, Dan\n\nWe want to maximize satisfaction, by ensuring that each person has at least one liked item in 'orderable' or one disliked item in 'not_orderable'.\n\nGiven the number of dislikes against paneer and udon and only a few people liking them, we could consider placing udon in 'not_orderable' and paneer in 'orderable' This caters to a majority by satisfying people who dislike udon and like paneer. However, Taj likes paneer and dislikes udon which would leave him unsatisfied.\n\nIf we place ravioli in 'not_orderable', we satisfy many dislikes (Cy, Rina, Naina, Jay, Arun, Aarav) and only leave Vic and Val dissatisfied, so we need to check if Vic and Val can still be satisfied with other items.\n\nVal also likes muffins and dislikes omelette, but no one dislikes only muffins, so muffins can be placed in 'orderable' to satisfy Val. Similarly, Vic dislikes muffins and risotto as well but there is no conflict with these and other people's likes/dislikes, so we can satisfy Vic by putting risotto in the 'orderable' list. However, Ned dislikes muffins and Anil dislikes risotto, which would leave one of them unsatisfied in each case.\n\nChecking omelette, only Jaya and Ada have mentioned disliking it, and a significant number of people like it. To keep Ada satisfied, we need to ensure cheesecake or paneer are in 'orderable', so Jaya is the only person who dislikes only omelette. We can put omelette in 'orderable' and meet the criteria for Jaya to be satisfied too.\n\nClearly, it's a complex puzzle with multiple dependencies. We can simplify the problem by prioritizing items that are liked by a few but disliked by none (orderable), and disliked by many but liked by a few (not_orderable). We begin by looking for any such item:\n\n- Cheesecake is liked by many and only disliked by Jaya and Hui, but providing other options could satisfy them both.\n- Muffins are liked by some and only disliked by Ned and Vic, but we can satisfy Vic with risotto, which leaves only Ned, who also likes omelette and can thus be satisfied with that.\n- Risotto is only disliked by Anil, who also dislikes ravioli, but because we have cheesecake, Anil can still be satisfied if cheesecake is available.\n\nWe can now choose to place cheesecake on the 'orderable' list to satisfy a large number of people. This action will satisfy everyone who likes cheesecake, and for those who dislike it (Jaya and Hui), we can ensure they have other liked items or disliked items on the lists:\n\n- Hui can be satisfied with muffins (likes) or cheesecake (dislikes).\n- Jaya is already satisfied with omelette being orderable, despite disliking cheesecake.\n\nSince Hui is taking care of with muffins, we'll place them in 'orderable' too.\n\nBy placing cheesecake on the 'orderable' list, we're also covering Anil who dislikes risotto and ravioli.\n\nNow we can confirm that:\n- ravioli should be in 'not_orderable' (disliked by many, liked by fewer)\n- cheesecake and omelette in 'orderable' (liked by many, including those who dislike ravioli)\n- muffins in 'orderable' (liked by some and can satisfy Hui, and not heavily disliked)\n- risotto can be in 'orderable' (to satisfy Vic and not significantly disliked by others)\n\nGiven this, each person should have at least one item in either list that satisfies the conditions.\n\nLet's test the configuration:\n- Cheesecake is 'orderable', satisfying Taj, Ty, Tao, Rina, Bea, Anil, Naina, and Ada, and acts as a disliked item for Jaya and Hui.\n- Omelette is 'orderable', satisfying Oli, Ned, Bea, Dan, Arun, and also acts as a liked item for Jaya and Hui.\n- Ravioli is 'not_orderable', satisfying Cy, Rina, Naina, Jay, Arun, and Aarav.\n- Muffins are 'orderable', satisfying Hui, Sky, Val.\n- Risotto is 'orderable', satisfying Ned, Bea, and Vic.\n\nSince everyone has at least one liked item on 'orderable', or a disliked item on 'not_orderable', we have a solution. Thus, the final lists are:\n\norderable=['cheesecake', 'omelette', 'muffins', 'risotto']\nnot_orderable=['ravioli']", 'num_prompt_tokens': 510, 'num_completion_tokens': 1233}
