se who only have dislikes and the large number of those dislikes.\n\nSeveral people dislike 'quiche', and 'sorbet' and they're liked by a somewhat smaller group. If we can put 'quiche' and 'sorbet' in 'not_orderable', it tends to satisfy a larger number of people without much conflict:\n\n- Those who like 'quiche' still can be satisfied with 'bruschetta', 'macaron', or 'salad' in the 'orderable' list.\n- Those who like 'sorbet' can be satisfied by 'macaron' or 'bruschetta' in the 'orderable' list.\n\nGiven these observations, let's tentatively mark 'quiche' and 'sorbet' for 'not_orderable'. Now, we have to ensure that every person who likes 'quiche' or 'sorbet' has at least one other like that can be satisfied with the 'orderable' list.\n\nAfter scanning through every person's preferences who likes 'quiche' and 'sorbet', we see that:\n\n- Each of them has at least one other like.\n- 'Macaron' and 'bruschetta' are common likes among them.\n\nThis suggests that we can satisfy them by placing 'macaron' or 'bruschetta' in the 'orderable' list. Since 'macaron' and 'bruschetta' are both liked by some and disliked by others, we can't satisfy everyone with only one of them.\n\nHowever, the combination of 'macaron' and 'bruschetta' being on the 'orderable' list means that everyone who likes 'quiche' or 'sorbet' (since they will be 'not_orderable') can still get something they like. So we'll tentatively place 'macaron' and 'bruschetta' on the 'orderable' list.\n\nNow, we check if any person will become unsatisfied due to this:\n- People who dislike 'macaron' can be satisfied if 'sorbet' is 'not_orderable'.\n- People who dislike 'bruschetta' can be satisfied if 'quiche' is 'not_orderable'.\n\nFrom the current analysis, we have:\n'orderable' = ['macaron', 'bruschetta']\n'not_orderable' = ['quiche', 'sorbet']\n\nBut before finalizing, we need to check the satisfaction condition for 'salad':\n\n- 'Salad' is liked by many. If we don't make it 'orderable', we have to satisfy those individuals with another like.\n\nLet's check:\n- Sam is satisfied with 'quiche' or 'sorbet' being 'not_orderable'.\n- Shan is satisfied since 'macaron' is 'orderable'.\n- Jaya is satisfied since 'bruschetta' is 'orderable'.\n- Om is satisfied since 'macaron' is 'orderable'.\n- Pam is satisfied since 'sorbet' and 'bruschetta' are on the respective 'not_orderable' and 'orderable' lists.\n- Sol has no conflicts as 'quiche' and 'sorbet' are 'not_orderable'.\n- Veer is satisfied with 'quiche' and 'sorbet' being 'not_orderable'.\n- Dot is satisfied with 'quiche' being 'not_orderable'.\n\nEveryone that likes 'salad' has another item they like in the 'orderable' list or a disliked item in the 'not_orderable' list, so they're all satisfied. \n\nThus, the tentative list holds up and satisfies everyone's requirements.\n\nFinal Solution:\n\n```python\norderable = ['macaron', 'bruschetta']\nnot_orderable = ['quiche', 'sorbet']\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 735, 'num_completion_tokens': 1149}
