a)\n\nNow let\'s check for clear inclusions in \'orderable\' and \'not_orderable\' lists:\n\nClear orderable (liked by some, not disliked by any):\n- None, every item liked by someone is also disliked by someone else.\n\nClear not_orderable (disliked by some, not liked by any):\n- None, every item disliked by someone is also liked by someone else.\n\nSince we don\'t have any clear candidates, we\'ll have to look for an item (or set of items) that could satisfy everyone considering their dislikes as well. Checking for dislikes, we can place an item on \'not_orderable\' if someone dislikes it and it\'s not liked by more than who dislike it, thereby leading to satisfaction of more individuals. I\'ll directly include highly disliked foods which are not highly liked in the \'not_orderable\' list. We should also pay attention that preference of multiple "like" should have more weight than multiple "dislike" as a person can only be satisfied with the "likes" not "dislikes".\n\nFollowing that logic:\n- Ravioli is liked by many, so it should be in \'orderable\'.\n- Pizza also has a number of likes, but the presence of ravioli as orderable may suffice for those who like pizza, as they are not overly numerous.\n- Couscous and Udon are both liked and disliked by many. However, couscous has less people disliking it than udon does.\n- Spaghetti, cheese, peach, and mousse are all polarizing.\n\nLet\'s start by choosing ravioli as \'orderable\', as it is liked by many and its inclusion thus satisfies numerous individuals.\n\nNow let’s satisfy people who have dislikes which are not ravioli, cheese, or udon (since these are liked by many), and see if we can include their dislikes in the \'not_orderable\' list without unsatisfying anyone who likes those items:\n\n- Pizza can be \'not_orderable\' because only a few people who like it won\'t be satisfied with ravioli and it\'s disliked by Cy, Jan, Ravi, and Qi who all like ravioli.\n- Waffles are only liked by a few (Bea, Ava, Gus, Joe)