le dislike it and are not compensated by the likes.\n- Tandoori also has many dislikes and can\'t be ordered.\n- Korma also has a lot of dislikes and likes, which is prone to clash.\n- Muffins are liked by many but also disliked by several.\n- Salad is liked by many but disliked by a few.\n\nSince there is no clear item that is only disliked or only liked without opposition, we will now look for an item that can satisfy one group without dissatisfying another.\n\nIf we decide to put \'salad\' in the \'orderable\' list, everyone who likes salad (and nobody else) will be satisfied. Those who dislike salad might still not be unsatisfied if they like muffins, quiche, korma, or tandoori. To ensure this, we cannot include muffins in \'not_orderable\' because that would dissatisfy many. \n\nLet\'s attempt to place "salad" in \'orderable\':\n- This satisfies Max, Gus, Kim, Ron, Ying, Ben, Riya, Dot, Lin, Tim, Val, and Isha.\n\nNext, let\'s place "quiche" and "tandoori" in \'not_orderable\':\n- This will satisfy Max, Kim, Om, Ron, Xan, Raj, Ula, Ru, Lex, Zoe, Taj, Jim, Yan, Isha, Ning, and Ru.\n\nThe remaining issue is with the people who dislike salad and those who like muffins and korma. We cannot put muffins on \'not_orderable\' due to their high likes, so let\'s put "korma" on \'not_orderable\':\n- This will satisfy Om, Ron, Ying, Ben, Ula, Jon, Val, Riya, Bo, Rohan, Tim, Sal.\n\nEveryone who dislikes "quiche", "tandoori", or "korma" has at least one of their dislikes in \'not_orderable\'. Now we need to check the people who have likes in \'orderable\' with salad and see if including korma in \'not_orderable\' has not made anyone unhappy.\n\nUpon reviewing the lists, there are no conflicts as every like and dislike has been considered.\n\nSo our final lists are:\n```python\norderable = [\'salad\']\nnot_orderable = [\'quiche\', \'tandoori\', \'korma\']\n```\n\nThis combination should satisfy all individuals according to their preferences.', 'num_prompt_tokens': 839, 'num_completion_tokens': 932}
