es Udon and Tart.\n13. Lee only likes Tart but dislikes Gelato and Sushi. We look to avoid including Tart on either list, as it is commonly disliked.\n\nBased on the above points, we will definitely add Gelato to \'orderable\' to satisfy several people. We\'re considering adding either Udon or Ratatouille to \'orderable\' next. Tiramisu also is a potential candidate.\n\nNow, let\'s check for any clashes in the consideration:\n- Udon is disliked by multiple people, including Om, Ava, May, Abe, Sia, Yue, and Hu. This makes it difficult to include.\n- Ratatouille is disliked by Gia, Tom, Arun, Li, Anil, and Ken, but not as strongly as Udon, suggesting it\'s a safer option to add to \'orderable\'.\n- Tiramisu is disliked by Jay, Bao, Aarav, Eva, Chen, Ken, and Sia.\n\nIt looks like Ratatouille might be the safest bet for now since it has fewer dislikes than Udon and Tiramisu, so we’ll add it to \'orderable\' along with Gelato. This will already satisfy a lot of individuals on the list:\n\norderable = [Gelato, Ratatouille]\n\nNow that we have Gelato and Ratatouille on the \'orderable\' list let\'s move through the remaining preferences to make sure all individuals can be satisfied. It should be noted that we now need to avoid putting Udon and Tiramisu on either of the lists because there are individuals who\'d be unsatisfied if those were included.\n\nWe will list down the people who have not been considered satisfied yet due to their multiple likes or who have dislikes that we need to account for. We need to ensure that we can satisfy at least one of their likes or their dislikes fall into the \'not_orderable\' category.\n\nIf we find any item universally disliked and not liked by anyone, we can safely place it in \'not_orderable\'. Conversely, if there\'s a liked item by someone who has not been satisfied yet and it\'s not disliked universally, it could be a candidate for \'orderable\'. However, we need to remember to cross-check for any potential overlaps in likes and