 of individuals.\n\nSince pancakes are liked by Wes, Jan, Lin, Cal, and Du, we need to ensure these individuals are satisfied by another item in the \'orderable\' list or another dislike being not orderable.\n\n2. Macaron and paneer also appear as common dislikes. If placed in \'not_orderable\', we would satisfy many individuals. We can consider that in conjunction with the interests as well.\n\nNow, let\'s look at the commonly liked food:\n\n1. Frittata is a common like but is disliked by Kit, Wes, Zan, and Jon, which means it can\'t directly be made \'orderable\' if it doesn\'t conflict with other dislikes for these individuals.\n\n2. Pie is liked by many and only disliked by Wes, Ana, Ying, and Zan. If we place pie in \'orderable\', we will have to ensure that these individuals have another like or dislike satisfied.\n\n3. Doughnut is commonly liked. Since Tom, Ana, Vic, and Jon dislike pie or frittata, including doughnut in \'orderable\' might satisfy them.\n\nLet’s put together a provisional list:\n\n\'Orderable\' – This list should include items liked by many but not disliked by the others. We have to be cautious with doughnut since Ana and Jan dislike it. We can possibly include frittata, as dislikes for it can be covered by pancakes being in \'not_orderable\'.\n\n\'Not_orderable\' – For this list, pancakes are a prime candidate and possibly macaron and paneer, depending on the other likes of the individuals who like macaron and paneer.\n\nLet\'s try to draft a potential list:\n\n- \'Not_orderable\': ["pancakes", "macaron", "paneer"]\n- \'Orderable\': ["pie", "doughnut", "frittata"]\n\nLet\'s verify if this satisfies everyone:\n\n- Pie is liked by those who dislike pancakes, macaron, and paneer. It\'s in \'orderable\' and so is able to satisfy several individuals.\n- Doughnut is liked by quite a number and isn\'t the sole dislike of any individual. Including it in \'orderable\' satisfies some more individuals.\n- Frittata is widely liked, and those who dislike it are satisfied by dislikes in \'not_orderable\'.\n\nNow, let\'s cross-check:\n\nEveryone who dislikes pancakes (included in \'not_orderable\') is satisfied, and those who like pancakes must be compensated by other likes. Wes likes pie which is in \'orderable\', so he\'s satisfied. The same goes for Jan, Lin, Cal, and Du who also have other likes in the \'orderable\' list.\n\nIt looks like our tentative list satisfies every individual, based on the given information. Hence, we can output this as the final solution:\n\n```python\norderable=["pie", "doughnut", "frittata"], not_orderable=["pancakes", "macaron", "paneer"]\n```', 'num_prompt_tokens': 751, 'num_completion_tokens': 989}
