to decide their placement based on satisfying the most people.\n\nObservations:\n\n- Pho is the most liked item, disliked only by Bao, Eva, and Jay.\n- Shawarma is more disliked than liked.\n- Guacamole is in the middle with an almost even split in likes and dislikes.\n\nGiven that pho is the most liked and less disliked, let's consider starting with pho in the 'orderable' list. \n\nShawarma is disliked by many people, more than those who like it. Since we need to satisfy everyone and placing shawarma in the 'orderable' list would dissatisfy many, it should go in the 'not_orderable' list. However, we need to ensure Bao, Neil, Hu, Eva, and Eli still find the selection satisfactory.\n\nGuacamole is liked by enough people that we can't place it in the 'not_orderable' list without dissatisfaction, so it should go in the 'orderable' list.\n\nLet’s create the lists now:\n\n- 'orderable': Pho, Guacamole\n- 'not_orderable': Shawarma\n\nNow check if all individuals are satisfied:\n\n1. Sia likes pho; satisfied.\n2. Bao dislikes shawarma; satisfied.\n3. Naina likes pho; satisfied.\n4. Neil dislikes guacamole; satisfied.\n5. Hu likes pho; satisfied.\n6. Eva dislikes pho; satisfied.\n7. Mia likes guacamole; satisfied.\n8. Eli (likes everything); satisfied.\n9. Tia likes guacamole; satisfied.\n10. Jay dislikes shawarma; satisfied.\n11. Zoe likes pho; satisfied.\n12. Vic likes pho; satisfied.\n\nAll individuals are satisfied by this configuration.\n\nFinal solution:\n```python\norderable=['pho', 'guacamole'], not_orderable=['shawarma']\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 386, 'num_completion_tokens': 683}
Data Sample: {'num_vars': 10, 'num_clauses': 20, 'formula': [[8, -4, 9], [4, 9, -8], [-7, -8, -2], [-4, -1, -6], [-3, -10, -6], [-7, 8, -9], [3, -2, 5], [-3, 2, -8], [-10, 3, 8], [-7, 2, 10], [-5, 6, 7], [3, 4, -2], [-2, 8, -10], [-8, 7, 4], [10, 1, -4], [3, 8, 9], [1, 8, -4], [-10, 8, -7], [-1, -8, 3], [4, -3, -1]], 'is_sat': True, 'preferences': 'Dot: Likes guacamole, macaron. Dislikes shawarma. Kai: Likes shawarma, macaron. Dislikes guacamole. Rob: Dislikes kebab, guacamole, mousse. Leo: Dislikes shawarma, paneer, custard. Ravi: Dislikes tiramisu, salmon, custard. Ava: Likes guacamole. Dislikes kebab, macaron. Yan: Likes tiramisu, gelato. Dislikes mousse. Vic: Likes mousse. Dislikes tiramisu, guacamole. Fay: Likes tiramisu, guacamole. Dislikes salmon. Jon: Likes mousse, salmon. Dislikes kebab. Ansh: Likes custard, kebab. Dislikes gelato. Bea: Likes tiramisu, shawarma. Dislikes mousse. Ken: Likes guacamole. Dislikes mousse, salmon. Du: Likes kebab, shawarma. Dislikes guacamole. Pam: Likes salmon, paneer. Dislikes shawarma. Arun: Likes tiramisu, guacamole, macaron. Ying: Likes paneer, guacamole. Dislikes shawarma. Eva: Likes guacamole. Dislikes salmon, kebab. Bao: Likes tiramisu. Dislikes paneer, guacamole. Sue: Likes shawarma. Dislikes tiramisu, paneer. ', 'menu_items': ['paneer', 'mousse', 'tiramisu', 'shawarma', 'gelato', 'custard', 'kebab', 'guacamole', 'macaron', 'salmon'], 'gpt_out': "To solve this, we need to create two lists: 'orderable' and 'not_orderable'. Each person in the group must be satisfied with these lists according to the given criteria. A person is satisfied if at least one liked item is 'orderable' or one disliked item is 'not_orderable'.\n\nStep 1: Resolve Absolute Constraints\nFirst, let's check if there are any absolute constraints. We can fulfill these requirements by putting all liked items where everybody dislikes it into the 'orderable' list and all disliked items where everybody likes it into the 'not_orderable' list.\n\nIn this case, there is no such item disliked or liked by everyone, so we will proceed to the next step without absolute constraints.\n\nStep 2: Find Conflicting Preferences\nNext, let's note the direct conflicts in preferences where someone likes an item and another dislikes it:\n- Guacamole: Liked by Dot, Ava, Fay, Ken, Arun, Ying, Eva, and Bao (likes it), Disliked by Kai, Rob, Du, and Vic (dislikes it).\n- Shawarma: Liked by Kai, Bea, Du, and Sue (likes it), Disliked by Dot, Leo, Pam, and Ying (dislikes it).\n- Mousse: Liked by Rob, Yan, and Jon (likes it), Disliked by Bea and Ken (dislikes it).\n- And so on...\n\nThere is no way to satisfy everyone because there is a conflict for guacamole, shawarma, and other items where both likes and dislikes exist among different people without a clear separation. We need to find a combination that satisfies everyone if possible.\n\nStep 3: Forming the Lists\nWe need to try and form 'orderable' and 'not_orderable' lists such that we resolve conflicts by keeping in mind that satisfying a person doesn't necessarily require all their likes or dislikes to be catered to, but at least one from their likes should be 'orderable' or one from their dislikes as 'not_orderable'.\n\nAn immediate observation is that tiramisu might be a key item for likes but is also on the dislike list of several people. Let's go person by person to try and satisfy everyone:\n- Dot (needs guacamole or macaron 'orderable', or shawarma 'not_orderable')\n- Kai (needs shawarma or macaron 'orderable', or guacamole 'not_orderable')\n\nGuacamole is a conflicting item, but shawarma only conflicts with one person's like (Kai's). To reduce the likes on guacamole, which is highly conflicted, we could find another item to satisfy Kai's likes and place guacamole in 'not_orderable' to satisfy more people.\n\nLooking through the list again:\n- Mousse can be 'orderable' because doing so satisfies Vic and Jon, and the individuals disliking it either have a like that can be made 'orderable' or another dislike that can be made 'not_orderable'.\n- Similarly, custard cannot be orderable (Leo and Ravi dislike it), but Ansh who likes it can be satisfied with kebab being 'orderable', which is disliked only by Eva and Rob who can be satisfied by salmon and guacamole being 'not_orderable', respectively.\n- Gelato has fewer conflicts than tiramisu and can be 'orderable', satisfying Yan and leaving tiramisu for 'not_orderable'.\n- Paneer has fewer conflicts than shawarma (Robin needs guacamole or macaron 'orderable' and Kai needs shawarma or macaron 'orderable'), so shawarma could potentially be 'not_orderable' to solve the paneer issue for Leo and Ying (Leo dislikes shawarma, paneer, and custard but one of his dislikes, custard, cannot be 'orderable').\n\nBased on this, we start forming the lists with the following assumptions to satisfy as many people as possible:\n- Guacamole 'not_orderable' (satisfying Kai's dislike).\n- Shawarma 'not_orderable' (solving issue for those who dislike it).\n- Mousse 'orderable' (satisfies some likes and no major dislikes are unsolvable).\n- Gelato 'orderable' instead of tiramisu.\n\nNow we need to ensure every other person can be satisfied based on these assumptions:\n- Macaron can be 'orderable' (Dot likes it, the only conflict was with Kai, who is satisfied by guacamole being 'not_orderable').\n- Paneer can be 'orderable' (Leo can't have custard, so his satisfaction comes from disliking shawarma which is 'not_orderable').\n- Kebab can be 'orderable' (satisfying Ansh, Rob dislikes it but is satisfied by guacamole 'not_orderable').\n- Salmon can be 'not_orderable' (satisfying Rob and Pam with their dislikes).\n- Custard must be 'orderable' to satisfy Ansh because gelato is 'orderable'.\n\nAfter picking salmon 'not_orderable' to satisfy Eva, Ying, and Ken, we've exhausted our options without conflicting with the current lists. We assign the remaining positives where possible to the 'orderable' list unless it causes a conflict.\n\nThis creates the following tentative lists:\nOrderable: Mousse, Gelato, Macaron, Paneer, Kebab\nNot_Orderable: Guacamole, Shawarma, Salmon, Tiramisu\n\nChecking against all preferences:\n- Does everyone who likes an item on the 'orderable' list have their dislike avoided, or does everyone who dislikes an item on the 'not_orderable' list have their like fulfilled?\n\nPerforming this check quickly shows that conflicts arise:\n- Bea, who likes tiramisu and shawarma, has no likes in the 'orderable' list after tiramisu is placed in 'not_orderable'.\n- Eva, who likes guacamole, has no likes in the 'orderable' list after guacamole is placed in 'not_orderable'.\n\nTherefore, no combination can be made to satisfy everyone. So the lists remain empty. The correct output then is empty lists:\n\n```python\norderable=[], not_orderable=[]\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 493, 'num_completion_tokens': 1295}
