nflicts and attempt to satisfy as many people as possible.\n\nFrom the contradictory items:\n- "Pie" is liked by many but disliked by 12 people. We cannot exclude it from \'orderable\' as Taj\'s only like is Pie, which means it must be \'orderable\' for Taj to be satisfied.\n- "Toast" is also problematic since it is one of the only two likes for Sol, and "frittata", Sol\'s other like, is disliked by others too.\n- "Cheesecake" is similarly problematic and cannot be comfortably placed in either list without more analysis.\n\nGiven the above conflicts, we need to find an item or a set of items that can be placed on the \'orderable\' list that does not exclude anyone from being satisfied while considering that some items may need to go on the \'not_orderable\' list.\n\nLet\'s now try to find if there is a possible ordering by focusing on problematic items and people with limited likes:\n\n1. "Pie" has to be \'orderable\' for Taj to be satisfied. This means that we need to find a way to satisfy Meg, Aarav, Ada, Kim, Wes, Kit, Ana, Zara, Ravi, Riya, and Ty by having an item they like on the \'orderable\' list or an item they dislike on the \'not_orderable\' list.\n\nAfter scanning through the preferences again:\n- Meg can be satisfied with "frittata" or "churros".\n- Aarav can be satisfied with "toast".\n- Ada can be satisfied with "kimchi" or "hummus".\n- Kim can be satisfied with "kimchi".\n- Wes can be satisfied with "toast".\n- Kit can be satisfied with "caramel".\n- Ana can be satisfied with "kimchi" or "hummus".\n- Zara can\'t be satisfied because all her likes are preferable to others, so they cannot be not orderable.\n- Ravi can be satisfied with "cheesecake".\n- Riya can\'t be satisfied because her only like ("pie") cannot be on the \'not_orderable\' list due to Taj\'s preference.\n- Ty can\'t be satisfied because both items he dislikes ("toast" and "pie") can\'t be put on the \'not_orderable\' list without conflicting with others\' likes.\n\nSince we cannot satisfy Zara, Riya, and Ty simultaneously with the others due to their contradictory preferences, it seems impossible to create a pair of lists that will satisfy everyone in the group. There\'s at least one person who will not be satisfied regardless of the arrangement.\n\nSo, in conclusion, we cannot find a combination of \'orderable\' and \'not_orderable\' foods that satisfies all individuals based on the rules provided. Therefore, the solution is empty lists for both \'orderable\' and \'not_orderable\':\n\n```python\norderable=[], not_orderable=[]\n```', 'num_prompt_tokens': 1110, 'num_completion_tokens': 1052}
