le' list either.\n\nDue to the complex interplay of likes and dislikes, this problem won't be resolved through just pair-wisely exclusion rules. We need to ensure everyone is satisfied by having at least one liked or disliked constraint met. So let's try building a solution by iteratively refining the lists by ensuring at least one satisfactory condition per person is met:\n\n- Start with 'orderable' being empty and adding to it as we find items that don't conflict.\n- At the same time, keep track of people's satisfaction with the choices we make.\n\nStep 1: **korma** can't be in 'orderable'. Too many people dislike it (Hui dislikes korma and likes peach; peach is liked by many others, so it can't be 'not_orderable').\n\nStep 2: **Spaghetti** seems to be liked by many and only a few dislikes, but because of its popularity, we'll tentatively add it to 'orderable'.\n\nSo far, we have:\n'orderable' = [spaghetti]\n'not_orderable' = []\n\nNow let's check if we can satisfy everyone with spaghetti being 'orderable':\n\n- Sia (dislikes peach) → peach can go to 'not_orderable'.\n- Zac, Riya, Hui, Ansh, Ava, Hu, Neil, Aarav, Mia, Pat, Gia, Raj, Lex, Chen (all like peach or spaghetti) → already satisfied by spaghetti on the 'orderable' list.\n- Ula (dislikes nachos) → nachos can go to 'not_orderable'.\n- Kit, Ben, Joe, Ying, Jim, Rina, Rohan (like shrimps, dislike peach or nachos) → satisfied by peach or nachos in 'not_orderable'.\n- Sol, Tara, Cy, Ravi, Yue, Ron (dislikes peach or likes spaghetti) → satisfied by peach in 'not_orderable' or spaghetti in 'orderable'.\n- Wes, Veer, Mei, Zan, Gus (all dislikes overlap with either peach or nachos already in 'not_orderable') → satisfied.\n- Anil (likes peach, dislikes nachos) → satisfied.\n- Du, Jaya, Bea, Jun (dislike peach and/or like spaghetti) → satisfied.\n- Tom, Sal, Yara, Zara, Xan, Joy (dislikes peach or nachos; likes pie or spaghetti) → satisfied.\n- Kit, Kavi, Isha, Ada, Chao, Naina (dislikes peach or likes spaghetti/nach