, salmon. Tia: Likes peach, ravioli. Dislikes macaron. Ray: Likes ravioli, sushi. Dislikes peach. Jaya: Likes toast. Dislikes guacamole, peach. Hu: Likes ravioli, vegetables. Dislikes sushi. Lin: Likes sushi, burrito, vegetables. Zac: Dislikes vegetables, burrito, ravioli. ', 'menu_items': ['salmon', 'guacamole', 'sushi', 'toast', 'peach', 'macaron', 'ravioli', 'vegetables', 'burrito'], 'gpt_out': "To determine which food items can be ordered and which cannot, we will list all unique likes and dislikes, then examine the constraints given by the opinions of the group.\n\nWe begin by listing all likes and dislikes from everyone:\n\n**Likes**:\n- peach (Joe, Kit, Ned, Vic, Sam, May, Val, Ru, Tia, Isha, Xan)\n- salmon (Joe, Rina, Lia, Tim, Ned, Meg, Kim, Rob, Amy, Ru)\n- vegetables (Joe, Qi, Tim, Sid, Taj, Abe, Wes, Mia, Du, Ron, Bea, Hu, Lin)\n- toast (Yue, Ada, Lia, Meg, Veer, Pia, Xan, Mei, Jaya, Jay)\n- macaron (Liz, Sol, Max, Bo, Veer, Jon, Hui)\n- guacamole (Ada, Taj, Aarav, Kavi, Eva, Abe, Sal, Bao, Ken, Jun, Rob, Mei)\n- sushi (Sid, Lee, Aarav, Eva, Pat, Feng, Eli, Li, Mina, Dan, Ray, Lin)\n- burrito (Kit, Wei, Aarav, Hal, Amy, Dan, Lin, Ru)\n- ravioli (Taj, Du, Ivy, Zara, Nita, Hu, Tia, Ray)\n- guacamole (Sal, Bao, Ken, Jun, Mei)\n\n**Dislikes**:\n- salmon (Qi, Yue, Lia, Max, Lee, Sal)\n- macaron (Qi, Lia, Pat, Mel, Ty)\n- vegetables (Rina, Liz, Sol, Cy, Kit, Ned, Tao, Mel, Dot, Wes, Nita, Zac)\n- burrito (Rina, Ada, Sid, Tao, Eva, Abe, Mia, Bo, Dot, Ian, Kim, Hal, Nic, Ken, Jun, Bea, Gen, Ty, Val, Ivy)\n- toast (Liz, Cy, Bao, Ken, Pia, Isha, Ron, Ty)\n- peach (Yue, Max, Lee, Sam, Nic, Jaya, Jay, Ray, Kim)\n- guacamole (Sol, Cy, Tao, Mia, Sam, Nic, Gen, Ty)\n- sushi (Meg, Hal, Dot, Mel, Ivy)\n- ravioli (Tim, Wei, Bo, Ty, Val, Mina, Amy, Eli)\n- macaron (Dot, Gen, Ty, Tia)\n\nBased on the likes and dislikes, no one single food item is universally liked or disliked. However, to satisfy everyone, we need at least one liked item on the 'orderable' list and one disliked item on the 'not_orderable' list for each person.\n\nThere are some conflicting preferences:\n- Qi likes vegetables but dislikes salmon and macaron, while Rina likes salmon but dislikes vegetables.\n- Yue likes toast but dislikes peach and salmon. Liz likes macaron but dislikes vegetables and toast.\n- Wei likes burrito but dislikes toast and ravioli.\n- Cy dislikes guacamole, toast, and vegetables.\n- Taj likes guacamole, ravioli, and vegetables.\n\nTo satisfy everyone, we need to find common ground. The strategy here is to put items that are liked by some and not disliked by others in 'orderable', and items that are disliked by some but not liked by others in 'not_orderable'.\n\nLet's find foods that someone dislikes but nobody likes. Since these items should definitely go in the 'not_orderable' list to satisfy the person disliking them without making anyone unhappy:\n\n**'not_orderable':**\n- macaron (Disliked by Qi, Lia, Pat, Ty, Tia, Mel, and Dot without anyone having it as their sole liked item)\n- burrito (Disliked by Rina, Ada, Sid, Tao, Eva, Abe, Mia, Bo, Ian, Kim, Nic, Ken, Jun, Bea, Dot, Hal, Gen, Ty, and Val without anyone having it as their sole liked item) \n\nNow we need to put some foods on the 'orderable' list. This is trickier, as we must ensure we don't put an item that someone dislikes without having a corresponding disliked item in 'not_orderable'. \n\nLet's find an item that is liked by at least one person and isn't the sole liked item for someone who dislikes it.\n\n**'orderable':**\n- peach (Liked by Joe, Kit, Ned, Isha, Vic, Sam, Ru, May, Tia, Val, Xan liked by many people, and disliked by a few, which have other items they dislike in the 'not_orderable' list or other items they like in the 'orderable' list).\n- toast (Liked by many and the people who dislike it either have other dislikes on the 'not_orderable' list or other likes on the 'orderable' list).\n\nIt seems we have found at least one item for both lists that could potentially satisfy everyone. Let's check to ensure that every individual has at least one liked item or one disliked item in the lists:\n\n1. Joe is satisfied with peach ('orderable').\n2. Qi is satisfied with macaron ('not_orderable').\n3. Rina is satisfied with burrito ('not_orderable') or peach ('orderable').\n4. Yue is satisfied with toast ('orderable').\n5. Liz is satisfied with toast ('orderable').\n6. Sol is satisfied with macaron ('not_orderable').\n... and so on for the remaining individuals.\n\nThe proposed solutions don't seem to violate anyone's likes and dislikes.\n\nThe orderable items are:\n- peach\n- toast\n\nThe not_orderable items are:\n- macaron\n- burrito\n\nThus, the final lists are:\n```\norderable=['peach', 'toast'], not_orderable=['macaron', 'burrito']\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 1116, 'num_completion_tokens': 1230}
Data Sample: {'num_vars': 6, 'num_clauses': 12, 'formula': [[6, 3, 5], [5, 2, 6], [2, -5, 6], [2, -1, -6], [-6, 5, 4], [2, 5, 1], [-6, 5, -1], [-2, 5, 1], [5, -1, -3], [1, -4, 3], [3, -1, 5], [-4, 3, -2]], 'is_sat': True, 'preferences': 'Anil: Likes guacamole, pho, naan. Cy: Likes naan, burger, guacamole. Tara: Likes burger, guacamole. Dislikes naan. Ava: Likes burger. Dislikes sushi, guacamole. Neil: Likes naan, toast. Dislikes guacamole. Gus: Likes burger, naan, sushi. Dot: Likes naan. Dislikes guacamole, sushi. Sid: Likes naan, sushi. Dislikes burger. Li: Likes naan. Dislikes sushi, pho. Zara: Likes sushi, pho. Dislikes toast. Val: Likes pho, naan. Dislikes sushi. Nic: Likes pho. Dislikes toast, burger. ', 'menu_items': ['sushi', 'burger', 'pho', 'toast', 'naan', 'guacamole'], 'gpt_out': 'To solve this problem, we begin by looking for any conflicts in the likes and dislikes. Then we aim to find a solution that satisfies all individuals. I will list out each person’s likes and dislikes, then try to categorize food into \'orderable\', \'not_orderable\', or undecided lists.\n\nLet\'s write down what each person likes and dislikes: \n\nAnil: Likes (guacamole, pho, naan)  \nCy: Likes (naan, burger, guacamole)  \nTara: Likes (burger, guacamole) Dislikes (naan)  \nAva: Likes (burger) Dislikes (sushi, guacamole)  \nNeil: Likes (naan, toast) Dislikes (guacamole)  \nGus: Likes (burger, naan, sushi)  \nDot: Likes (naan) Dislikes (guacamole, sushi)  \nSid: Likes (naan, sushi) Dislikes (burger)  \nLi: Likes (naan) Dislikes (sushi, pho)  \nZara: Likes (sushi, pho) Dislikes (toast)  \nVal: Likes (pho, naan) Dislikes (sushi)  \nNic: Likes (pho) Dislikes (toast, burger)\n\nFirst, we try to find an item that is liked by someone and not disliked by anyone else. This item can immediately go into the \'orderable\' list. \n\nLooking at the lists, "naan" is liked by many people but disliked by Tara and Li. Thus, naan cannot be in \'orderable\'. Similarly, "sushi" is liked by some (Gus, Sid, Zara) but disliked by others (Ava, Dot, Li, Val), so it cannot be in \'orderable\'. "Guacamole" is liked by many but disliked by Ava, Neil, and Dot, and hence cannot be in \'orderable\'. "Pho" is liked by some (Anil, Zara, Val, Nic) but disliked by Li, so it can\'t be in \'orderable\'. "Burger" is liked by many but disliked by Sid and Nic. \n\nSince there\'s a conflict with all other liked items, "toast" stands out. "Toast" is only liked by Neil and disliked by Zara and Nic. This means "toast" can be placed in the \'not_orderable\' list since it satisfies that it is disliked by at least one person and its being \'not_orderable\' satisfies Neil.\n\nNow, since "toast" is in \'not_orderable\', Zara and Nic are both satisfied. We no longer have to worry about their preferences. We will move on to the other people.\n\nNext, since Tara dislikes naan, and she likes burger which conflicts with Sid and Nic\'s dislikes, her satisfaction criterion can be met by placing naan in \'not_orderable\', making her satisfied. This also satisfies Li since she dislikes naan too.\n\nBy placing "naan" in \'not_orderable\', Anil, Cy, Neil, Gus, Dot, and Val are still left unsatisfied. We may look for another item that’s liked by them but not disliked by anyone else. However, every liked item left is now also disliked by someone else according to the remaining unsatisfied individuals\' preferences.\n\nLet\'s focus on the foods that have not been assigned yet: "pho", "naan", "guacamole", "burger", and "sushi".\n\nWe examine these items and their conditions:\n\n- "naan" is now in \'not_orderable\' (satisfying Tara and Li).\n- "pho" can be in \'orderable\' since its only dislike comes from Li, who is already satisfied.\n- "burger" cannot be in \'orderable\' because it is disliked by Sid and Nic (Nic is already satisfied).\n- "sushi" cannot be in \'orderable\' due to multiple dislikes and doesn\'t need to be since those who dislike it are satisfied by the placement of toast and naan.\n- "guacamole" is left and since it\'s liked by Anil, Cy, Tara, and Gus but disliked by Ava, Neil, and Dot who are not satisfied yet, it cannot be put in \'orderable\'.\n\nSince "pho" is the only food preference remaining that isn\'t disputed, it can go into \'orderable\', satisfying Anil and Cy.\n\nNow Anil, Cy, Tara, Li, Zara, and Nic are satisfied. We need to consider the rest:\n\n- Ava will be satisfied if "guacamole" is \'not_orderable\', which it must be since it\'s liked and disliked in conflict with others.\n- Neil, who dislikes "guacamole", will be satisfied by it being in \'not_orderable\' too.\n- Gus likes "burger" and "naan", both of which are in conflict, but "naan" is now in \'not_orderable\', satisfying him.\n- Dot dislikes "guacamole" and "sushi", and with "guacamole" in \'not_orderable\', she is satisfied.\n- Sid dislikes "burger", which won\'t be in \'orderable\', so he requires "sushi" on \'orderable\' to be satisfied, but "sushi" has multiple dislikes, so he can\'t be satisfied this way.\n\nIt seems that Sid, who dislikes "burger" and likes "sushi" and "naan", will stay unsatisfied because "sushi" cannot be made \'orderable\' due to dislikes, and "burger" is also in conflict. No matter what we place in \'orderable\', Sid won\'t be satisfied without conflicting with another person\'s dislikes.\n\nTherefore, we cannot find a combination of \'orderable\' and \'not_orderable\' lists that will satisfy everyone\'s preferences. As a result, both lists will remain empty.\n\nFinal solution:\n```python\norderable=[], not_orderable=[]\n```', 'num_prompt_tokens': 346, 'num_completion_tokens': 1233}
Data Sample: {'num_vars': 4, 'num_clauses': 12, 'formula': [[-3, 1, -2], [2, -1, 3], [-1, -3, -4], [-1, -3, -4], [-1, 3, 2], [4, -2, 1], [-4, -3, 1], [-2, 1, -3], [3, 4, -1], [4, 1, -3], [-2, 3, -1], [-3, -2, 4]], 'is_sat': True, 'preferences': 'Ava: Likes naan. Dislikes risotto, korma. Eli: Likes korma, risotto. Dislikes naan. Sal: Dislikes naan, risotto, pie. Zara: Dislikes naan, risotto, pie. Jon: Likes risotto, korma. Dislikes naan. Riya: Likes pie, naan. Dislikes korma. Ravi: Likes naan. Dislikes pie, risotto. Qi: Likes naan. Dislikes korma, risotto. Rex: Likes risotto, pie. Dislikes naan. Wes: Likes pie, naan. Dislikes risotto. Joe: Likes risotto. Dislikes korma, naan. Val: Likes pie. Dislikes risotto, korma. ', 'menu_items': ['naan', 'korma', 'risotto', 'pie'], 'gpt_out': "To solve this, we need to find a set of food items such that all likes and dislikes are satisfied according to the rules. Let's start by analyzing the preferences of each individual.\n\nHere's the summary of likes and dislikes:\n\n- Ava: Likes naan. Dislikes risotto, korma.\n- Eli: Likes korma, risotto. Dislikes naan.\n- Sal: Dislikes naan, risotto, pie.\n- Zara: Dislikes naan, risotto, pie.\n- Jon: Likes risotto, korma. Dislikes naan.\n- Riya: Likes pie, naan. Dislikes korma.\n- Ravi: Likes naan. Dislikes pie, risotto.\n- Qi: Likes naan. Dislikes korma, risotto.\n- Rex: Likes risotto, pie. Dislikes naan.\n- Wes: Likes pie, naan. Dislikes risotto.\n- Joe: Likes risotto. Dislikes korma, naan.\n- Val: Likes pie. Dislikes risotto, korma.\n\nNaan seems to be a dividing food item. Let's see if it's possible to include it in the 'orderable' list. If we add naan to 'orderable', it would satisfy Ava, Riya, Ravi, Qi, Wes, but Eli, Sal, Zara, Jon, Rex, and Joe would be dissatisfied because they all dislike naan. Therefore, naan will be added to 'not_orderable'.\n\nNext, let's look at risotto. Many individuals either like or dislike it, but no one has it as a sole liked food item. We'll look for someone with risotto as their only liked item. Since no such person exists, and more people have it on their dislike list, it could be advantageous to place risotto on the 'not_orderable' list.\n\nWhen we add risotto to 'not_orderable', Sal and Zara are immediately satisfied, as they dislike risotto along with others. Joe also only likes risotto but dislikes korma and naan, both of which are already in 'not_orderable'. So Joe will be satisfied too.\n\nNext, let's consider pie. For Rex, pie is one of the liked items. Val also only likes pie but dislikes risotto and korma. Thus, adding pie to 'orderable' satisfies Val and Rex. For Riya and Wes, pie is also a liked food item, and since naan is already on 'not_orderable', pie must be on 'orderable' to satisfy them.\n\nKorma, meanwhile, is disliked by many, and liked by Eli, Jon, and Rex. Rex is already satisfied by having pie on 'orderable', so we can ignore his preference for korma. Both Eli and Jon also like risotto, which is not available, so they need korma on 'orderable' to be satisfied.\n\nNow, let's put this all together and satisfy everyone:\n\nGiven the above lists and considering all likes and dislikes, we will put:\n- naan and risotto in 'not_orderable', satisfying Ava, Sal, Zara, Ravi, Qi, and Joe.\n- pie and korma in 'orderable', satisfying Eli, Rex, Jon, Riya, Wes, and Val.\n\nNo other item conflicts with these choices, and every person has at least one liked item on 'orderable' or one disliked item on 'not_orderable'.\n\nSo, the final solution is:\n\n```python\norderable=['pie', 'korma'], not_orderable=['naan', 'risotto']\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 362, 'num_completion_tokens': 757}
Data Sample: {'num_vars': 6, 'num_clauses': 12, 'formula': [[4, -1, -3], [5, 6, 1], [4, 1, 6], [-2, 3, 5], [-2, -6, 4], [4, 2, -3], [6, 3, -2], [3, -5, 2], [-5, -6, 4], [-5, 1, -6], [6, 2, 3], [4, -2, 3]], 'is_sat': True, 'preferences': 'Qi: Likes risotto. Dislikes pie, tacos. Xiu: Likes salad, baklava, pie. Ravi: Likes risotto, pie, baklava. Hui: Likes tacos, salad. Dislikes sorbet. Dot: Likes risotto. Dislikes sorbet, baklava. Kai: Likes risotto, sorbet. Dislikes tacos. Amy: Likes baklava, tacos. Dislikes sorbet. Tao: Likes tacos, sorbet. Dislikes salad. Val: Likes risotto. Dislikes salad, baklava. Mei: Likes pie. Dislikes salad, baklava. Ned: Likes baklava, sorbet, tacos. Ana: Likes risotto, tacos. Dislikes sorbet. ', 'menu_items': ['pie', 'sorbet', 'tacos', 'risotto', 'salad', 'baklava'], 'gpt_out': "Let's start by analyzing the likes and dislikes of each person and find out what combinations might satisfy everyone. We'll look for conflicts where a person's like is another person's dislike, as those are the items that can't be on both lists.\n\n1. Risotto is liked by Qi, Ravi, Dot, Val, and Ana. No one dislikes risotto, so this item can be placed in the 'orderable' list.\n\n2. Pie is liked by Xiu, Ravi, and Mei, but disliked by Qi. Since Qi would be satisfied by pie being on the 'not_orderable' list, we can't put it in 'orderable', but it could go in 'not_orderable'.\n\n3. Baklava is liked by Xiu, Ravi, Amy, and Ned, but disliked by Dot, Val, and Mei. We can’t satisfy the likers and dislikers of baklava simultaneously by placing it in any list without causing dissatisfaction to the other party.\n\n4. Salad is liked by Xiu, and Hui, but disliked by Tao, Val, and Mei. We can place salad in the 'not_orderable' list to satisfy its dislikers.\n\n5. Tacos are liked by Hui, Amy, Tao, Ned, and Ana, but disliked by Qi and Kai. We can place tacos in the 'orderable' list since Qi is already satisfied with risotto being 'orderable' and Kai would be satisfied with sorbet being 'not_orderable'.\n\n6. Sorbet is liked by Kai, Tao, and Ned, but disliked by Hui, Dot, Amy, and Ana. We can place sorbet in the 'not_orderable' list to satisfy its dislikers.\n\nNow we might have a possible combination, so let's review our choices:\n\n- Risotto goes to 'orderable' list.\n- Pie goes to 'not_orderable' list (satisfying Qi).\n- Salad goes to 'not_orderable' list.\n- Tacos go to 'orderable' list.\n- Sorbet goes to 'not_orderable' list.\n\nThis would mean Baklava cannot go into either list as it has both likers and dislikers that are not yet satisfied with another choice. Let’s check if everyone's satisfaction criteria are met:\n\n- Qi is satisfied (risotto in 'orderable', pie in 'not_orderable').\n- Xiu is satisfied (pie or salad in 'not_orderable').\n- Ravi is satisfied (risotto in 'orderable').\n- Hui is satisfied (salad in 'not_orderable', tacos in 'orderable').\n- Dot is satisfied (risotto in 'orderable', sorbet in 'not_orderable').\n- Kai is satisfied (risotto in ‘orderable’, sorbet in 'not_orderable').\n- Amy is satisfied (tacos in 'orderable', sorbet in 'not_orderable').\n- Tao is satisfied (tacos in 'orderable', salad in 'not_orderable').\n- Val is satisfied (risotto in 'orderable', salad in 'not_orderable').\n- Mei is satisfied (pie in 'not_orderable').\n- Ned is satisfied (tacos in 'orderable').\n- Ana is satisfied (risotto in 'orderable', sorbet in 'not_orderable').\n\nIt seems that all individuals will be satisfied with this combination. Baklava can't be added to any list since it would dissatisfy someone either way. \n\nFinally, our lists are:\norderable = ['risotto', 'tacos']\nnot_orderable = ['pie', 'salad', 'sorbet']\n\nNow, we can output this in Python code:\n\n```python\norderable=['risotto', 'tacos'], not_orderable=['pie', 'salad', 'sorbet']\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 348, 'num_completion_tokens': 790}
Data Sample: {'num_vars': 6, 'num_clauses': 24, 'formula': [[-4, -1, -2], [-2, -6, -3], [2, 4, -3], [-4, 5, 6], [2, 3, 4], [3, -1, 5], [-3, 4, 6], [-3, -6, 1], [5, -2, 3], [-6, 1, 4], [4, 1, -3], [3, 4, -1], [-6, 2, -5], [-3, -6, -1], [-1, -3, 4], [-6, -1, 4], [-1, -2, 5], [-6, 2, -1], [-4, -6, 5], [6, 3, -2], [1, 4, -3], [-4, 5, 3], [-3, 6, -2], [-1, 3, 4]], 'is_sat': True, 'preferences': 'Joy: Dislikes poke, baklava, toast. Ray: Dislikes toast, falafel, korma. Kim: Likes toast, poke. Dislikes korma. Aarav: Likes naan, falafel. Dislikes poke. Chao: Likes toast, korma, poke. Jim: Likes korma, naan. Dislikes baklava. Om: Likes poke, falafel. Dislikes korma. Ansh: Likes baklava. Dislikes korma, falafel. Naina: Likes naan, korma. Dislikes toast. Leo: Likes baklava, poke. Dislikes falafel. Zoe: Likes poke, baklava. Dislikes korma. Ty: Likes korma, poke. Dislikes baklava. Pia: Likes toast. Dislikes falafel, naan. Liz: Dislikes korma, falafel, baklava. Meg: Likes poke. Dislikes baklava, korma. Neil: Likes poke. Dislikes falafel, baklava. Tara: Likes naan. Dislikes baklava, toast. Sol: Likes toast. Dislikes falafel, baklava. Zac: Likes naan. Dislikes poke, falafel. Mel: Likes falafel, korma. Dislikes toast. Wei: Likes baklava, poke. Dislikes korma. Liv: Likes naan, korma. Dislikes poke. Wes: Likes falafel. Dislikes korma, toast. Bao: Likes korma, poke. Dislikes baklava. ', 'menu_items': ['baklava', 'toast', 'korma', 'poke', 'naan', 'falafel'], 'gpt_out': "Let's analyze the preferences:\n\nFirst, we need to find food items that are disliked by one or more participants but are not liked by any. These items can go directly into the 'not_orderable' list. Similarly, we'll identify items that are liked by one or more participants and not disliked by any to put in the 'orderable' list.\n\nFrom the dislikes:\n\n1. Toast is disliked by Joy, Ray, Naina, Tara, Mel, Wes. It is liked by Kim, Chao, Pia, Sol.\n- Cannot directly put toast into 'not_orderable' because it is liked by several people.\n\n2. Falafel is disliked by Ray, Aarav, Ansh, Leo, Pia, Liz, Neil, Zac, Sol, Wes.\n- Cannot directly place falafel into 'not_orderable' because it’s liked by some.\n\n3. Korma is disliked by Kim, Ansh, Zoe, Ty, Liz, Meg, Wei, Wes, Liv.\n- Cannot directly put korma in 'not_orderable' because it is liked by many.\n\n4. Baklava is disliked by Joy, Jim, Liz, Ty, Meg, Neil, Tara, Sol.\n- Cannot directly put baklava in 'not_orderable' because it's liked by some.\n\n5. Poke is disliked by Joy, Aarav, Naina, Zac, Liv.\n- Cannot directly put poke in 'not_orderable' due to its likability by others.\n\nFrom the likes:\n\n1. Naan is liked by Aarav, Jim, Naina, Tara, Zac, Liv.\n- Cannot directly put naan in 'orderable' because it may be disliked by others or conflict with other preferences.\n\nConsidering the satisfaction criteria where each person must have at least one liked item on the 'orderable' list or a disliked item on the 'not_orderable' list, we need to carefully choose items for each list. An ideal approach is to look for compromises and conflicts:\n\n- Poke cannot be on 'not_orderable' because it is liked by Kim, Chao, Om, Leo, Zoe, Meg, Neil, Wei, and Bao.\n- Korma cannot be on 'not_orderable' because it is liked by Ray, Chao, Jim, Naina, Ty, Mel, Liv, and Bao. But since it's disliked by many, we should try not to have it on the 'orderable' list unless necessary.\n- Toast cannot be in 'not_orderable' since it's liked by Kim, Chao, Pia, and Sol.\n- Naan can't be in 'orderable' because Pia dislikes it.\n- Falafel can't be in 'not_orderable' because Aarav, Om, and Wes like it.\n- Baklava can't be in 'not_orderable' because it's liked by Ansh, Leo, Wei, and Zoe.\n\nSince picking items for the 'orderable' list doesn't exclude people outright, we can aim to include foods there if we can't place them in 'not_orderable'.\n\nLooking at the likings:\n\n- Naan is liked by Aarav, Jim, Naina, Tara, Zac, and Liv. No one dislikes naan, so it can be placed on the 'orderable' list. This satisfies Aarav, Jim, Naina, Tara, Zac, and Liv.\n- Similarly, since nobody dislikes poke and it is liked by many, we can add it to the 'orderable' list for Kim, Chao, Om, Leo, Zoe, Meg, Neil, Wei, Bao.\n- Toast can be added to the 'orderable' list to satisfy Pia and Sol since it is liked by several and we can't put it in either 'not_orderable' or leave it out.  Chao and Kim are already satisfied with poke.\n\nNow we're left with baklava, falafel, and korma which are all problematic because they are liked and disliked by numerous individuals.\n\nLet's see if we need to put any of the problematic items into the 'orderable' list:\n\n- Ansh, Leo, Wei, and Zoe like baklava. But since they also like poke which is already on 'orderable', they are satisfied.\n- Ty likes baklava and korma but dislikes falafel. Since Ty likes poke, already in 'orderable', Ty is satisfied.\n- Mel dislikes toast but likes falafel and korma. Since we can't put falafel or korma in 'not_orderable', we need to satisfy Mel through falafel or korma being 'orderable'. Let's pick korma for Mel as falafel is liked by more individuals.\n- Ray dislikes toast, falafel, and korma. Since Ray dislikes three items, we can put falafel into 'not_orderable' to satisfy Ray without affecting anyone who likes falafel since they're already satisfied.\n\nThere is no more conflict and we have included an item that each person likes in 'orderable' or dislikes in 'not_orderable'. Therefore, we can proceed with the final lists.\n\nTo summarize, we have:\n\norderable: naan, poke, toast, korma (satisfying everyone who likes at least one of them)\nnot_orderable: falafel (satisfying everyone who dislikes it)\nLet's output this as requested:\n\n```python\norderable=['naan', 'poke', 'toast', 'korma'], not_orderable=['falafel']\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 547, 'num_completion_tokens': 1162}
