nd disliked, so it is contentious too.\n- Shawarma is liked by many but disliked by some, making it contentious.\n- Soup is liked by some and disliked by others, contentious.\n- Muffins are liked by some and disliked by many, contentious.\n- Burger is liked by a few but disliked by many, making it contentious.\n- Shrimps are liked by some but disliked by many, contentious.\n\nGiven the complexity of this list and the individual preferences, there doesn't appear to be any item that is universally liked or disliked by all participants, and thus no clear candidates for either the 'orderable' or 'not_orderable' lists without causing dissatisfaction to some.\n\n### Strategy:\n1. Attempt to put items that are liked by someone but not mentioned (either liked or disliked) by others into the 'orderable' list.\n2. Place items that are disliked by someone and not liked by anyone else into the 'not_orderable' list.\n3. For contentious items, evaluate if they could satisfy more people on one list than the other.\n\nLooking at the preferences, there appears to be no clear items that some individuals like and no one dislikes and vice versa. Therefore, we need a different approach, which will be about accommodating the majority and then exploring exceptions.\n\n1. **Shawarma**: Liked by several and only disliked by Ron, Diya, Kim, and Chen. Since the number of people who like it significantly outweighs those who dislike it, we consider making this 'orderable'.\n   \n2. **Falafel**: Also liked by several but disliked by many as well. It is contentious and hard to place at this stage.\n\n3. **Spaghetti**: It is liked by many and disliked by a comparable number of people, making it hard to allocate.\n\n4. **Brownies, Peach, Soup, Burger, Muffins, Shrimps**: Each of these is mentioned various times in likes and dislikes, making them all contentious items.\n\nSince there are so many conflicting interests and spaghetti, falafel, and shawarma cause the most contention, let's examine if excluding one might make a solution possible. \n\n### Considering Individual Cases to Eliminate Contention:\n- Excluding spaghetti eliminates dislikes from 11 members.\n- Excluding falafel eliminates dislikes from 12 members.\n- Excluding shawarma eliminates dislikes from only 4 members.\n\nIf we were to attempt an elimination strategy, trying to appease the most number of people possible, we could consider eliminating falafel first as it's disliked by the most individuals. This leaves us with spaghetti and shawarma still to consider.\n\nUpon further investigation, it becomes evident that accommodating every person is impossible due to the highly individualized likes and dislikes. Those who like spaghetti or shawarma are relatively well-distributed among those who dislike each item. There's no situation where we can have everyone satisfied because there are people on both sides of the likes and dislikes for each contentious food item, and some only like one specific item, which others dislike.\n\nFor example, if you put spaghetti in 'orderable', you will instantly make Sal, Bao, Lin, Raj, Ron, and several others dissatisfied, as they dislike spaghetti. The same happens if you put brownies or peach in 'orderable' since they are both liked and disliked by different group members.\n\nNo combination exists that can satisfy all group members simultaneously due to the overlapping and conflicting nature of their food preferences. Therefore, based on the satisfaction criteria provided, we have to output empty lists for both 'orderable' and 'not_orderable'.\n\n```python\norderable = []\nnot_orderable = []\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 939, 'num_completion_tokens': 875}
