s korma, macaron; Dislikes custard.\n16. Du: Likes shawarma, custard; Dislikes muffins.\n\nThe strategy here is to allocate as many \'liked\' items as possible to the \'orderable\' list without conflicting with the \'disliked\' items in the \'not_orderable\' list.\n\nFirst, we observe the dislikes:\n- Shawarma is disliked by Meg and Neil.\n- Korma is liked by many and disliked only by Ula and Taj, but Ula also likes tiramisu and Taj likes paneer.\n- Macaron is liked by several but disliked by Ping, Meg, Vic, and Nita.\n- Custard is liked and disliked by many, creating a conflict.\n- Paneer is disliked by Nita and Kai but liked by several others.\n- Tiramisu is disliked by Meg but liked by Ula, Ben, and Nita.\n- Muffins are only liked by Jaya, but disliked by Eli, Neil, Du, and Ivy.\n- Naan is liked by Ivy and disliked by Kit and Ben.\n\nLet\'s try to accommodate all dislikes into the \'not_orderable\' list where possible and likes into the \'orderable\' list with the condition that each individual must have at least one item they like in \'orderable\' or one they dislike in \'not_orderable\'.\n\nWe can start by putting "shawarma" in \'not_orderable\' because it is disliked by two individuals and can be avoided by those who like it since they have other likes. "Muffins" also goes into \'not_orderable\' due to more dislikes than likes.\n\nPossible \'not_orderable\': shawarma, muffins\n\nNext, items like "macaron" and "custard" pose a challenge due to their conflicted status. We notice that everyone who dislikes "custard" also dislikes "shawarma" which is already in \'not_orderable\', satisfying their criteria. Thus, "custard" can be placed in \'orderable\'.\n\nPossible \'orderable\': custard\n\nFor "macaron", Ping and Meg have other likes and dislikes, so we do not need to cater to them. However, Vic dislikes both macaron and custard, but he likes korma. Since we have custard in the \'orderable\', we can satisfy Vic with "korma". "Korma" can also satisfy Ping and Jaya. But we need to ensure that Ula and Taj are satisfied with other items first.\n\nUla likes "tiramisu", which only Meg dislikes, and Meg is already satisfied by having shawarma out. So, we place "tiramisu" in \'orderable\'.\n\nPossible \'orderable\': custard, tiramisu\n\nFor Taj, "paneer" is left, and since Kai, who dislikes "paneer", likes "macaron" which we haven\'t placed yet, we put "paneer" in \'orderable\'. This leaves "korma" for Ping, Vic, and Jaya.\n\nPossible \'orderable\': custard, tiramisu, paneer\n\nNow we can put "korma" in \'orderable\' to satisfy Vic, Ping, and Jaya, since Macaron can be placed in \'not_orderable\' to satisfy Meg and Nita.\n\nSince "macaron" is a complicated item and its placement in \'orderable\' would not satisfy Kit (who likes macaron but dislikes naan), we will place "naan" in \'orderable\', with the understanding that macaron will go to \'not_orderable\' list, satisfying Vic and negating the need for Kit to be satisfied by macaron.\n\nPossible \'orderable\': custard, tiramisu, paneer, korma, naan\n\nFinal \'not_orderable\': shawarma, muffins, macaron\n\nNow, let\'s verify if all are satisfied:\n- Ula is satisfied by tiramisu in \'orderable\'.\n- Ping is satisfied by korma in \'orderable\'.\n- May, though shawarma is in \'not_orderable\', is satisfied by paneer in \'orderable\'.\n- Kit is satisfied by naan in \'orderable\'.\n- Eli is satisfied by either shawarma in \'not_orderable\' or korma in \'orderable\'.\n- Meg is satisfied by shawarma in \'not_orderable\'.\n- Vic is satisfied by korma in \'orderable\'.\n- Taj is satisfied by paneer in \'orderable\'.\n- Ben is satisfied by tiramisu in \'orderable\'.\n- Neil is satisfied by shawarma in \'not_orderable\'.\n- Kai, though paneer is in \'orderable\', is satisfied by custard also being in \'orderable\'. \n- Ivy, though muffins are in \'not_orderable\', is satisfied by custard or naan in \'orderable\'.\n- Jaya is satisfied by korma in \'orderable\'.\n- Nita is satisfied by tiramisu