n\nThere will likely be more mandatory inclusions and exclusions as we go along, but these are the most obvious from the start.\n\nLet\'s start by trying to satisfy those with the least flexible preferences (those with the most dislikes):\n\n- **Ansh** dislikes doughnut, kimchi, ramen and he doesn\'t like anything, so at least one of his dislikes must be in \'not_orderable\'. \n- **Ty** dislikes risotto, naan, cheese, so at least one of his dislikes must be in \'not_orderable\'. We\'ve already established that cheese and naan can\'t be in \'not_orderable\', so risotto will be in \'not_orderable\', confirming our earlier assumption.\n\nWe already decided that naan must be in \'orderable\', and because **Ansh** dislikes ramen, we\'ll put "ramen" in \'not_orderable\' to satisfy Ansh\'s requirements. Now we have:\n\n- Orderable: [\'naan\']\n- Not orderable: [\'risotto\', \'ramen\']\n\nWe\'ll continue to iterate through the list of people, checking likes and dislikes. We already have a dislike for doughnut from Ansh, so everyone who likes doughnut can be satisfied if it\'s in \'orderable\'. It seems many people also like kimchi, and fewer dislike it compared to doughnut, so it may also be a good candidate for \'orderable\'.\n\nAs this process may become extremely complex due to the high number of likes and dislikes, the most algorithmic way to solve it may involve heuristic searching, where we\'d systematically try one option at a time, seeing if it satisfies all people, and backtracking if it doesn\'t. However, for the purposes of this explanation, we are required to think step-by-step and show the work.\n\nWith the constraints established so far and the apparent high number of conflicts, it seems like we need to check the items with the least number of dislikes, or those where the dislikes are already not orderable.\n\nLet\'s consider **kimchi** for \'orderable\', as this item still has relatively fewer dislikes. By putting kimchi in \'orderable\', we satisfy a lot of likes, and those who dislike kimchi also dislike ramen which is in \'not_orderable\', so they are also satisfied.\n\nNow we have:\n- Orderable: [\'naan\', \'kimchi\']\n- Not orderable: [\'risotto\', \'ramen\']\n\nLet\'s then summarize:\n\n- Everyone who likes **naan** or **kimchi** is now satisfied.\n- Everyone who dislikes **risotto** or **ramen** is now satisfied.\n\nWe have several likes of **doughnut** that are not conflicted with its dislikes since all those who dislike **doughnut** either like **naan** or **kimchi**, which are already in \'orderable\'. Therefore, let\'s add **doughnut** to \'orderable\':\n\n- Orderable: [\'naan\', \'kimchi\', \'doughnut\']\n- Not orderable: [\'risotto\', \'ramen\']\n\nNow let\'s summarize again:\n\n- Everyone who likes **naan**, **kimchi**, or **doughnut** is satisfied.\n- Everyone who dislikes **risotto** or **ramen** is satisfied.\n\nNext, we\'ll look for an item that can safely be put in \'not_orderable\':\n\n- **Cheese** is liked by many, but those who dislike it have other dislikes already in \'not_orderable\'.\n- **Pie** is liked but has a similar situation with dislikes as cheese.\n\nLet\'s put **cheese** in \'orderable\', assuming it could satisfy a lot of people, and evaluate:\n\n- Orderable: [\'naan\', \'kimchi\', \'doughnut\', \'cheese\']\n- Not orderable: [\'risotto\', \'ramen\']\n\nNow checking again:\n\n- Everyone who likes **naan**, **kimchi**, **doughnut**, or **cheese** is satisfied.\n- Everyone who dislikes **risotto** or **ramen** is satisfied.\n\nAt this point, we seem to have covered the majority of the group\'s preferences.\n\nThe preferences for **pie** are conflicting, so placing it in \'orderable\' would upset those who dislike it. However, given the logic above, everyone who dislikes pie has an item they like already on the \'orderable\' list or an item they dislike on the \'not_orderable\' list. Therefore, we can add **pie** to \'orderable\':\n\n- Orderable: [\'naan\', \'kimchi\', \'doughnut\', \'cheese\', \'pie\']\n- Not orderable: [\'risotto\', \'ramen\']\n\nFollowing this method, it seems we\'ve satisfied all individuals\' preferences, as everyone can find at least one liked item on the \'orderable\' list, or a disliked item on the \'not_orderable\' list.\n\nFinal lists are:\n`orderable=[\'naan\', \'kimchi\', \'doughnut\', \'cheese\', \'pie\']`\n`not_orderable=[\'risotto\', \'ramen\']`.', 'num_prompt_tokens': 833, 'num_completion_tokens': 1306}
