l require more careful consideration to ensure every person is satisfied.\n\nBased on the given preferences, let\'s start identifying what can be derived from exclusive likes and dislikes.\n\nExclusive likes (suitable for \'orderable\'):\n- None (because for any given food item on the list, there is at least one person who dislikes it)\n\nExclusive dislikes (suitable for \'not_orderable\'):\n- None (because for any given food item on the list, there is at least one person who likes it)\n\nSince there are no items everyone agrees on (either liked by all or disliked by all), we must look for items that might satisfy each person through a combined effort of \'orderable\' and \'not_orderable\' items. Let\'s look at the conditions that could satisfy everyone:\n- If someone likes an item, it cannot be on the \'not_orderable\' list.\n- If someone dislikes an item, it cannot be on the \'orderable\' list.\n- The preference of each person must be respected, meaning we must be able to find at least one liked or one disliked item from their list in the appropriate category.\n\nLet\'s start sifting through this with the objective that, for the most number of people, their likes go into \'orderable\' and their dislikes into \'not_orderable\'.\n\nWe notice that some items like "churros" and "kimchi" are liked by several individuals but also disliked by several. To satisfy the criteria, individuals who like an item must have that item or another they like in the \'orderable\' list, and those who dislike it must have it or another item they dislike in the \'not_orderable\' list.\n\nOne strategy could be to look for patterns where certain items could satisfy large groups of people without conflicting with anyone else\'s dislikes. We group individuals by what they dislike, hoping to find someone who likes that item:\n\n- "churros" are disliked by many, but it\'s also liked by many, so it\'s challenging to place.\n- "kimchi" is also problematic since it\'s liked by many but disliked by