ndividuals are satisfied by having a disliked item on \'not_orderable\', or because another liked item is on \'orderable\'. We need to ensure all members are still satisfied.\n\nLet\'s finalize the lists with lobster added to \'orderable\' to satisfy Joe:\n\n**Final \'orderable\':** paneer, poke, lobster\n\n**Final \'not_orderable\':** couscous, pie\n\nI\'ve gone through the preferences and deduced lists that satisfy all individuals. Participants who liked only items in the \'not_orderable\' list, now have their disliked items on one list or the other, making them satisfied according to the criteria. Here\'s the result in Python code:\n\n`orderable=[\'paneer\', \'poke\', \'lobster\'], not_orderable=[\'couscous\', \'pie\']`', 'num_prompt_tokens': 1015, 'num_completion_tokens': 780}
