y many and liked by one (Shan), should go to 'not_orderable.'\n\nSince Shawarma is disliked by three people and liked by five, to satisfy the maximum number of people, it should go into 'orderable.'\n\nWith 'shawarma' in 'orderable', Rex, Zac, and Raj are now satisfied since one of their disliked items is in 'orderable', even if not in 'not_orderable'.\n\nThe other items are liked by someone and also disliked by someone else, so we cannot directly assign them. We should find an item that is only liked and not disliked by anyone to place in 'orderable'. In this case, muffins can be in 'orderable' as the person who dislikes them, Jon, is already satisfied by having shawarma in 'orderable'.\n\nBy placing 'muffins' in 'orderable', we satisfy Cal, Shan, and Jun (along with Jon who was already satisfied with 'shawarma').\n\nDu and Rohan both like 'kebab', and since Diya dislikes both 'cheesecake' and 'kebab', she will be satisfied with having 'cheesecake' in 'not_orderable'. Raj also dislikes 'kebab', but he's already satisfied by 'shawarma' being 'orderable'. Therefore, 'kebab' can go in the 'orderable' list.\n\n'Lobster' is disliked by multiple people and liked by some. Since the people who like lobster (Rohan, Shan, Jun) are already satisfied by other items (shawarma for Rohan and Jun, muffins for Shan), 'lobster' can go in 'not_orderable' to satisfy those who dislike it.\n\nBased on this analysis, our lists would be:\n\norderable = ['shawarma', 'muffins', 'kebab']\nnot_orderable = ['cheesecake', 'lobster']\n\nLet's verify that this satisfies everyone:\n- Diya, Rex, Zac, and Raj are OK with 'shawarma' being 'orderable' and their dislikes being addressed.\n- Du is satisfied with 'kebab' being 'orderable'.\n- Cal is satisfied with 'muffins' being 'orderable'.\n- Rohan is satisfied with 'kebab' and 'shawarma' being 'orderable'.\n- Shan is satisfied with 'muffins' being 'orderable'.\n- Jon is satisfied with 'shawarma' being 'orderable'.\n- Jun is satisfied with 'shawarma' 