rbet, guacamole. Pam: Likes omelette. Dislikes salad, cheesecake. Min: Likes sorbet. Dislikes churros, roti. May: Likes guacamole, custard. Dislikes roti. Tia: Dislikes guacamole, churros, omelette. Jun: Dislikes sorbet, custard, bread. Qi: Likes bread, omelette. Dislikes custard. Aarav: Likes sorbet. Dislikes churros, risotto. Ravi: Likes guacamole, sorbet. Dislikes roti. Ray: Likes churros, omelette. Dislikes salad. ', 'menu_items': ['cheesecake', 'roti', 'sorbet', 'churros', 'omelette', 'salad', 'risotto', 'custard', 'guacamole', 'bread'], 'gpt_out': 'To solve this task, let\'s begin by identifying the foods that are disliked and liked by the individuals and figure out if we can satisfy everyone\'s preferences by placing some items on the \'orderable\' list and others on the \'not_orderable\' list. We\'ll then incrementally build a solution that satisfies the most critical constraints first.\n\nFirst, we should identify any "dealbreaker" dislikes that can only be in the \'not_orderable\' list to satisfy a person\'s preference, which means no one else must have that item on their \'likes\' list.\n\n1. Eva dislikes bread, omelette, and risotto, but all these are liked by others.\n2. Ron dislikes risotto and custard, but both of these are liked by others.\n3. Rohan dislikes guacamole, bread, and custard, but all of these are liked by others.\n4. Tia dislikes guacamole, churros, and omelette, and all of these are liked by others.\n5. Jun dislikes sorbet, custard, and bread, and all of these are liked by others.\n\nIt seems there are no dealbreaker dislikes that are unique, so we can\'t automatically assign any food items to the \'not_orderable\' list from the get-go. Next, we can try to find "must-have" likes – items that should go into the \'orderable\' list to satisfy someone without causing dissatisfaction to others.\n\n1. Ian likes omelette. but Eva, Lee, Tia and Isha dislike it – cannot add omelette to \'orderable\'.\n2. Sia likes churros, but Max, Zac, Li, Zoe,