ikes:\n- Poke: Ula, Sam, Liv, Max, Anil, Ravi, Dan\n- Kimchi: Dot, Fay, May, Ian, Taj, Kavi, Anil\n- Curry: Fay, Lee, Pam, Zan, Chen, Kit, Taj, Ru\n- Baklava: Om, Lee, Shan, Ansh, Cal, Kavi, Xan\n- Cheesecake: Om, Eli, Oli, Zara\n- Tiramisu: Sam, Kai, Lia, Pia, Cy, Cal, Diya, Sol, Kavi\n- Lobster: Kai, Oli, Ping, Ian, Joe, Taj\n- Cheese: Hu, Shan, Naina, Feng, Chen, Lex, Sol\n- Frittata: Ray, Sam, Mia, Feng, Rina, Wes, Xan\n- Bread: Jan, Lin, Sid, May, Ravi, Lei\n\nDislikes:\n- Frittata, curry, baklava, poke, cheese, kimchi, tiramisu, cheesecake, lobster, bread.\n\nFrom the likes and dislikes, we can determine that there are items exclusively liked and not disliked (hence they can safely be in \'orderable\') and items that are exclusively disliked and not liked (and can be in \'not_orderable\').\n\nBut based on the preferences, the above lists overlap, meaning no item is liked by some without being disliked by others. Therefore, we can\'t use an item in \'orderable\' or \'not_orderable\' without displeasing someone.\n\nTo satisfy everyone, we need to find a compromise where an \'orderable\' item for one person is not a \'disliked\' item for another, and if someone\'s \'liked\' item ends up in \'not_orderable\', then they must have another \'liked\' item in \'orderable\' or their \'disliked\' item in \'not_orderable\'.\n\nLet\'s look at unique likes (i.e., items only one person likes) since those can be used to satisfy particular individuals without conflicting with others:\n\n- For example, "lobster" is only liked by several individuals and is disliked by several others, meaning we cannot place it in \'orderable\' or \'not_orderable\' without making someone dissatisfied.\n\nAs the instructions require all participants to be satisfied, this exercise becomes a matter of balancing the various likes and dislikes to ensure everyone is accounted for.\n\nGiven the complexity of the situation, with no items liked by some without being disliked by others and vice versa, it is clear that no matter what items we put on the \'orderable\' or \'not_orderable\' lists, we will end up with at least one person not being satisfied with the choices. Therefore, based on the given preferences, the task is impossible.\n\nHence, the output will be empty lists for both \'orderable\' and \'not_orderable\':\n\n```python\norderable = []\nnot_orderable = []\n```', 'num_prompt_tokens': 997, 'num_completion_tokens': 693}
Data Sample: {'num_vars': 5, 'num_clauses': 25, 'formula': [[-1, -5, 3], [2, 1, 4], [-4, -2, -5], [-4, -3, 1], [1, -5, -2], [-2, -5, -1], [-3, -2, -4], [-3, -2, -5], [-1, -4, -2], [4, -5, 3], [3, 5, -2], [-2, -1, 3], [-1, 2, -4], [3, -2, 1], [-4, -5, 1], [1, 2, -4], [-4, 1, -3], [-1, 3, 5], [-4, -3, -2], [5, 4, 3], [3, -5, -1], [-5, 3, 2], [-4, -3, 2], [1, -2, 3], [5, 3, -4]], 'is_sat': True, 'preferences': 'Mina: Likes mousse. Dislikes udon, couscous. Isha: Likes naan, udon, vegetables. Nic: Dislikes vegetables, naan, couscous. Pia: Likes udon. Dislikes vegetables, mousse. Meg: Likes udon. Dislikes couscous, naan. Ken: Dislikes naan, couscous, udon. Ivy: Dislikes mousse, naan, vegetables. Ula: Dislikes mousse, naan, couscous. Mia: Dislikes udon, vegetables, naan. Leo: Likes vegetables, mousse. Dislikes couscous. Naina: Likes mousse, couscous. Dislikes naan. Tia: Likes mousse. Dislikes naan, udon. Dan: Likes naan. Dislikes udon, vegetables. Anil: Likes mousse, udon. Dislikes naan. Aarav: Likes udon. Dislikes vegetables, couscous. Ian: Likes udon, naan. Dislikes vegetables. Pat: Likes udon. Dislikes vegetables, mousse. Ru: Likes mousse, couscous. Dislikes udon. Val: Dislikes vegetables, mousse, naan. Ada: Likes couscous, vegetables, mousse. Ron: Likes mousse. Dislikes couscous, udon. Ravi: Likes mousse, naan. Dislikes couscous. Ansh: Likes naan. Dislikes vegetables, mousse. Wei: Likes udon, mousse. Dislikes naan. Eli: Likes couscous, mousse. Dislikes vegetables. ', 'menu_items': ['udon', 'naan', 'mousse', 'vegetables', 'couscous'], 'gpt_out': "Let's start by creating a list of all unique food items liked and disliked by the individuals. Once we have that list, we'll aim to satisfy each individual based on the satisfaction criteria.\n\n**Available items based on likes and dislikes:**\n- mousse\n- udon\n- couscous\n- naan\n- vegetables\n\n**Initial observations:**\n- Every person likes or dislikes mousse, making it a critical item to consider.\n- Similarly, naan is also either liked or disliked by almost everyone.\n- Vegetables are also a common item but less so than mousse or naan.\n\n**Strategy:**\nWe will try to find items that can be placed in 'orderable' or 'not_orderable' to satisfy the most number of individuals and go through this process iteratively to ensure all are satisfied.\n\nLet's analyze the items based on collective preference:\n- **Mousse:**\n    - Liked by: Mina, Leo, Naina, Tia, Anil, Ru, Ada, Ron, Ravi, Wei, Eli.\n    - Disliked by: Pia, Ivy, Ula, Val.\n\nGiven its popularity, mousse seems like a good candidate for 'orderable'. However, it is also disliked by quite a few, putting it into question.\n\n- **Udon:**\n    - Liked by: Isha, Pia, Meg, Anil, Aarav, Ian, Pat, Wei.\n    - Disliked by: Ken, Mia, Tia, Aarav, Ron.\n\nUdon has fewer detractors than mousse, so let's tentatively put it in the 'orderable' list.\n\n- **Couscous:**\n    - Liked by: Naina, Ru, Eli, Ada.\n    - Disliked by: Mina, Nic, Meg, Ken, Leo, Ivy, Ula, Ron, Ravi.\n\nMore people dislike couscous, so it could be placed in 'not_orderable'.\n\n- **Naan:**\n    - Liked by: Isha, Dan, Ian, Ravi, Ansh.\n    - Disliked by: Nic, Meg, Ken, Ivy, Ula, Mia, Naina, Tia, Val, Wei.\n\nNaan is also disliked by more individuals, so it seems suitable for 'not_orderable'.\n\n- **Vegetables:**\n    - Liked by: Isha, Leo, Ada.\n    - Disliked by: Nic, Mia, Ken, Ivy, Ansh, Val, Tia, Aarav, Ian, Pat, Dan, Eli.\n\nVegetables are largely disliked, making them a strong candidate for 'not_orderable'.\n\nNow, let's assign items to lists based on the majority preference, keeping the satisfaction criteria in mind:\n1. If mousse is made orderable, it satisfies the majority but leaves Pia, Ivy, Ula, and Val unsatisfied. However, Ivy, Ula, and Val do not like naan or vegetables, which we will place in 'not_orderable', satisfying them. Pia, though, would still need to be satisfied.\n2. If udon is made orderable, it satisfies fewer people than mousse and has a significant number of people disliking it.\n\nBased on this, here's an initial draft:\n- **Orderable:** mousse\n- **Not_Orderable:** couscous, naan, vegetables\n\nHowever, satisfying Pia becomes an issue here. Pia likes udon but dislikes vegetables and mousse. If udon is in 'orderable,' those who dislike udon would be unsatisfied unless they also dislike naan or vegetables, which we've placed in 'not_orderable'.\n\nLet's satisfy Pia by adding udon to 'orderable'. Now, since Ken, Mia, Tia, and Ron dislike udon, we must ensure that at least one of their dislikes is in 'not_orderable'. Given the lists we've proposed, their dislikes of naan or vegetables are covered in 'not_orderable'. However, Anil likes mousse and udon but dislikes naan, so according to our current list, he will be satisfied too.\n\nNow, our new lists are:\n- **Orderable:** mousse, udon\n- **Not_Orderable:** couscous, naan, vegetables\n\n**Final Step Verification:**\nEvery person either has at least one liked item in 'orderable' or one disliked item in 'not_orderable'. No item appears on both lists. Each individual's preferences are accounted for in one of these lists, achieving a solution where all participants are satisfied.\n\nTherefore, our final solution with the lists filled in with the decided items is as follows:\n```python\norderable=['mousse', 'udon'], not_orderable=['couscous', 'naan', 'vegetables']\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 542, 'num_completion_tokens': 1006}
Data Sample: {'num_vars': 3, 'num_clauses': 24, 'formula': [[3, -2, 1], [3, 1, -2], [-3, -1, 2], [2, -1, -3], [-2, -1, 3], [-2, 1, 3], [3, -1, -2], [2, 1, -3], [1, 3, 2], [-2, 1, -3], [2, 1, 3], [3, -2, -1], [-2, -1, -3], [-2, -1, -3], [1, 3, -2], [-3, -1, 2], [-2, -1, -3], [-2, -1, -3], [-1, -3, 2], [-2, 1, 3], [-3, -2, 1], [-1, -3, 2], [-1, -3, 2], [3, 1, -2]], 'is_sat': True, 'preferences': 'Val: Likes peach, espresso. Dislikes naan. Li: Likes peach, espresso. Dislikes naan. Zara: Likes naan. Dislikes peach, espresso. Anil: Likes naan. Dislikes espresso, peach. Veer: Likes peach. Dislikes naan, espresso. Pam: Likes espresso, peach. Dislikes naan. Mel: Likes peach. Dislikes espresso, naan. Kim: Likes naan, espresso. Dislikes peach. Kavi: Likes espresso, peach, naan. Taj: Likes espresso. Dislikes naan, peach. Yan: Likes naan, espresso, peach. Amy: Likes peach. Dislikes naan, espresso. Ray: Dislikes naan, espresso, peach. Tia: Dislikes naan, espresso, peach. Ned: Likes espresso, peach. Dislikes naan. Jay: Likes naan. Dislikes peach, espresso. Rex: Dislikes naan, espresso, peach. Feng: Dislikes naan, espresso, peach. Ula: Likes naan. Dislikes espresso, peach. Jun: Likes espresso, peach. Dislikes naan. Ru: Likes espresso. Dislikes peach, naan. Ben: Likes naan. Dislikes espresso, peach. Ivy: Likes naan. Dislikes espresso, peach. Jan: Likes peach, espresso. Dislikes naan. ', 'menu_items': ['espresso', 'naan', 'peach'], 'gpt_out': "Let's begin by identifying patterns in the group's preferences to determine which items can be ordered ('orderable') and which cannot ('not_orderable') while satisfying everyone's likes and dislikes.\n\n### Likes and Dislikes Analysis:\nWe have three food items being considered: peach, espresso, and naan.\n\n1. People who like peach: Val, Li, Veer, Pam, Mel, Yan, Amy, Ned, Jun, Ru, Jan\n2. People who like espresso: Val, Li, Pam, Kim, Kavi, Taj, Yan, Ned, Jun, Ru\n3. People who like naan: Zara, Anil, Kim, Kavi, Yan, Jay, Ula, Ben, Ivy\n\nPeople who dislike peach: Zara, Anil, Kim, Taj, Ray, Tia, Rex, Feng, Ula, Ben, Ivy\nPeople who dislike espresso: Val, Li, Mel, Jay, Ray, Tia, Rex, Feng, Ula, Ivy\nPeople who dislike naan: Val, Li, Veer, Pam, Mel, Taj, Amy, Ned, Jun, Ru, Jan\n\n### List Construction:\nGiven the criteria, there are some important points to consider:\n- If any person dislikes all items (like Ray, Tia, Rex, Feng), at least one of their disliked items must be in the 'not_orderable' list to satisfy them.\n- Any item liked by everyone cannot be placed in the 'not_orderable' list, as this would dissatisfy everyone. However, there is no such item in this case.\n- If a person likes only one item and it is the same item disliked by another group of other people, that item must be in the 'orderable' list to satisfy both the liker and dislikers.\n- Because an item cannot be in both lists, if someone dislikes an item that someone else likes, we need to ensure the item is either only in the 'orderable' or the 'not_orderable' list, but not both.\n\nLet's list out the 'must be orderable' and 'must not be orderable' based on the above constraints. Remember that Ray, Tia, Rex, and Feng are critical because they dislike all three items.\n\n#### Must be orderable:\n- peach or espresso (because Val, Veer, Mel, Amy, Ned, Jan like only these two items, so at least one of them must be 'orderable' for these individuals to be satisfied)\n\n#### Must be not orderable:\n- naan (because Ray, Tia, Rex, Feng dislike all items and we must pick one item to go into 'not_orderable' to satisfy them)\n\nNow we have satisfied criteria for the critical members (Ray, Tia, Rex, Feng) by having naan in 'not_orderable'. In order to meet the 'at least one liked item should be orderable' condition for the rest, peach or espresso can be selected as 'orderable'. We must ensure that no individual's likes are entirely in the 'not_orderable' list. Reviewing preferences again, no one likes naan exclusively, so having it as 'not_orderable' is safe.\n\nConsidering that some people only like peach and others only like espresso, and we have no constraints prohibiting us from placing both in 'orderable', we can satisfy both groups by having both peach and espresso as 'orderable'. This decision also satisfies individuals who like both items and do not have specific dislikes that conflict with this choice.\n\nFinal lists:\n- orderable: peach, espresso\n- not_orderable: naan\n\nThis choice ensures that a liked item for each person is 'orderable' and/or a disliked item is 'not_orderable'. Every participant should be satisfied with this arrangement, as those who like naan also like or at least do not dislike both peach and espresso.\n\nLet's represent the solution in Python code:\n\n```python\norderable=['peach', 'espresso']\nnot_orderable=['naan']\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 478, 'num_completion_tokens': 827}
Data Sample: {'num_vars': 8, 'num_clauses': 24, 'formula': [[5, 1, 3], [8, -6, -1], [3, -6, 2], [-3, 7, -8], [2, 8, -5], [2, 7, -5], [1, -8, -5], [6, -3, 4], [2, 5, -1], [3, -4, 6], [2, 8, 5], [4, -2, 1], [-7, 5, 1], [-3, 6, 5], [-5, -1, 7], [-1, -5, -6], [-2, -4, 1], [2, 4, 8], [-5, 7, 4], [-4, -5, 6], [3, -5, -6], [1, 3, 4], [6, 8, -3], [-1, -5, 2]], 'is_sat': True, 'preferences': 'Geet: Likes paneer, salad, soup. Rina: Likes pie. Dislikes pizza, salad. Jon: Likes soup, naan. Dislikes pizza. Tia: Likes roti. Dislikes soup, pie. Hui: Likes naan, pie. Dislikes paneer. Rohan: Likes naan, roti. Dislikes paneer. Leo: Likes salad. Dislikes pie, paneer. Ada: Likes pizza, udon. Dislikes soup. Neil: Likes naan, paneer. Dislikes salad. Hal: Likes soup, pizza. Dislikes udon. Lin: Likes naan, pie, paneer. Cy: Likes udon, salad. Dislikes naan. Zoe: Likes paneer, salad. Dislikes roti. Eli: Likes pizza, paneer. Dislikes soup. Mel: Likes roti. Dislikes paneer, salad. Gus: Dislikes salad, paneer, pizza. Sol: Likes salad. Dislikes naan, udon. Rob: Likes naan, udon, pie. Yan: Likes roti, udon. Dislikes paneer. Bao: Likes pizza. Dislikes udon, paneer. Eva: Likes soup. Dislikes paneer, pizza. Cal: Likes salad, soup, udon. Isha: Likes pizza, pie. Dislikes soup. Ken: Likes naan. Dislikes salad, paneer. ', 'menu_items': ['salad', 'naan', 'soup', 'udon', 'paneer', 'pizza', 'roti', 'pie'], 'gpt_out': "To solve this task, I'll need to ensure that each person's likes and dislikes are accounted for in such a way that nobody's dislikes are in the 'orderable' list and everyone's satisfaction criteria are met.\n\nTo start, we need to identify food items that are universally disliked, since these can be directly placed in the 'not_orderable' list without conflicting with anyone's likes, thus satisfying everyone who dislikes those items. Let's identify any such food items:\n\n- Paneer is disliked by Hui, Rohan, Cy, Gus, Yan, Eva, and Isha, and nobody else has paneer exclusively as a liked item.\n-