kes it likes at least one other item that could be orderable (salmon or churros). So poke could potentially be in the \'not_orderable\' list, as long as salmon or churros is \'orderable\'.\n- Churros is also disliked by many, but each person who dislikes churros either likes poke or salmon. So, for these individuals to be satisfied, either poke or salmon must be in the \'orderable\' list if churros are \'not_orderable\'.\n- Salmon is disliked by fewer people, and all who dislike it like churros or poke or both. So the \'orderable\' list could include either of those, if salmon were \'not_orderable\'.\n\nNow let\'s look at the likes:\n\n- Everyone who likes churros does not dislike poke and salmon simultaneously. So churros could be in the \'orderable\' list and satisfy these people.\n- Everyone who likes salmon also likes either churros or poke or both, so having salmon as \'orderable\' would satisfy these individuals, assuming poke or churros are \'not_orderable\' to cover dislikes.\n- Everyone who likes poke also likes salmon or churros or both, so poke could be \'orderable\' if either of the others is \'not_orderable\'.\n\nFrom this, we can see that churros could be a common item to satisfy most since nobody dislikes churros and dislikes both other items. Therefore, churros is a strong candidate for the \'orderable\' list.\n\nSince churros are a universal like and no one dislikes both other items, we can put churros in the \'orderable\' list.\n\nNow, considering poke is the second one, everyone who dislikes poke likes either churros or salmon. Since we already decided to put churros as \'orderable\', we can put poke in the \'not_orderable\' list which satisfies everybody who dislikes poke.\n\nWe have yet to consider salmon. Since everyone who likes salmon also likes churros and we\'ve already put churros in \'orderable\' list, adding salmon to either list isn\'t necessary for satisfaction but can be added to the \'orderable\' list to maximize options, as long as 