ts (but not both), which will satisfy both individuals. \n\nLooking through the list:\n\n- Bruschetta is liked by many but is also disliked by Abe, Wei, Jim, Tom, Pam, Diya, and Vic. Given its polarizing nature, it fits satisfactorily in either the 'orderable' or 'not_orderable' list to satisfy these individuals.\n- Couscous is liked by many as well but is disliked by Zan, Dot, May, Leo, Wei, Jim, Tom, Du, Rob, Diya, and Dan. It has a similar polarizing effect as Bruschetta and can be handled similarly.\n- Ravioli is also liked and disliked by several individuals, making it another polarizing item.\n\nIt is clear that all three items, bruschetta, couscous, and ravioli, are simultaneously liked and disliked by different people. However, no one dislikes all three items, which means we can satisfy everyone by picking any two items to go into the 'not_orderable' list and the remaining one into the 'orderable' list.\n\nHere's a suitable approach:\n\n- Since Jim dislikes all three items, he will be satisfied as long as at least one of these items is on the 'not_orderable' list.\n- For Naina, Zan, Dot, Liz, Tom, Wei, and Pam, who like at least one item and dislike another, having their disliked items on the 'not_orderable' list and their liked items on the 'orderable' list would satisfy them.\n\nGiven these considerations, we have to balance the likes and dislikes. However, we need to have at least one food item that is liked by each person in the 'orderable' list or one of their disliked items in the 'not_orderable' list. From the lists, we see that:\n\n- Couscous is liked by the highest number of individuals who do not dislike the other two items. So it is a good candidate for the 'orderable' list.\n- Bruschetta and ravioli are each disliked by several individuals. We can put these into the 'not_orderable' list.\n\nBy putting couscous in the 'orderable' list and bruschetta and ravioli in the 'not_orderable' list, it seems we will satisfy all individuals:\n\norderable = [