everal, including Yan, Dot, Arun, Cal, Taj, Hal, Sid, Tom, Ula, and Hui, who only dislike soup. Hence, it goes on the 'not_orderable' list.\n5. Naan is liked by many but is also disliked by a significant number of people, including Cal, Ned, Val, Shan, Ian, Ty, Ru, and Pam. However, all of these individuals have other dislikes.\n6. Since there's no clear consensus on naan and everyone who dislikes it has another dislike, it can go in the 'orderable' list to satisfy those who like it.\n\nWe're putting spaghetti, churros, and naan on the 'orderable' list. Lobster and soup are put on the 'not_orderable' list.\n\nLet's check the final lists for the satisfaction of each group member:\n\n- Everyone who likes 'orderable' items (spaghetti, churros, naan) and dislikes 'not_orderable' items (lobster, soup) is satisfied.\n- Individuals who dislike 'orderable' items have at least one other like or dislike that can be satisfied by other items on the 'orderable' or 'not_orderable' lists.\n\nTherefore, we have our two lists: \n\n'orderable' = [spaghetti, churros, naan]\n\n'not_orderable' = [lobster, soup]\n\nLet's provide these as python code:\n\n```\norderable=['spaghetti', 'churros', 'naan'], not_orderable=['lobster', 'soup']\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 757, 'num_completion_tokens': 643}
