 criteria for individuals who didn't find their liked food in the orderable list. We also should avoid any item that is liked by one person and disliked by another.\n\nLet's go through each food item and evaluate if it can be placed on either list without violating the satisfaction criteria.\n\nThe food items mentioned and the number of people who like or dislike them are as follows:\nLikes:\n- vegetables: 5 likes, 4 dislikes\n- sushi: 15 likes, 12 dislikes\n- bread: 7 likes, 9 dislikes\n- omelette: 7 likes, 8 dislikes\n- shawarma: 9 likes, 7 dislikes\n- korma: 11 likes, 5 dislikes\n- peach: 5 likes, 6 dislikes\n- salad: 9 likes, 12 dislikes\n\nDislikes:\n- peach: 6 dislikes\n- sushi: 12 dislikes\n- bread: 9 dislikes\n- korma: 5 dislikes\n- salad: 12 dislikes\n- shawarma: 7 dislikes\n- omelette: 8 dislikes\n\nWe should look for items that are only liked and not disliked or only disliked and not liked, as this would be the simplest way to satisfy the criteria without conflicts:\n\nObservations:\n1. Korma is liked by 11 people and disliked by only 5, with no overlap between those who like it and those who dislike it. This makes korma a good candidate for 'orderable'.\n2. All other liked items on the list above have overlaps, so we cannot add them to 'orderable' or 'not_orderable' without creating conflicts.\n\nWe can now add korma to the 'orderable' list and check to see how many people are satisfied:\n- Anyone who likes korma is now satisfied.\n- Anyone who dislikes korma is not yet satisfied but could be by their other dislikes or a like being met.\n\nWe move on to the remaining items, trying to satisfy as many people as possible without causing conflicts from the likes and dislikes:\n- Sushi is liked by many but also disliked by many. Adding it to either list will make a large group unhappy.\n- Peach is the same scenario as sushi.\n- Shawarma, salad, and bread are all problematic for the same reasons.\n\nHowever, by adding korma to the 'orderable' list, we can look at the individuals that dislike korma and try to satisfy them with their other dislikes by putting these on the 'not_orderable' list. \n\nUpon closer inspection, we can see:\n- Peach and sushi are commonly disliked. However, adding either of them to 'not_orderable' will cause conflict, as both are liked by some individuals.\n- Salad is commonly disliked and not liked by those who dislike korma, making it a good candidate for 'not_orderable'.\n- Omelette, bread, and shawarma have overlap in likes and dislikes, making them unsuitable for either list directly.\n\nNow let's check where we stand with these two additions (korma in 'orderable', salad in 'not_orderable'):\n- Anyone who likes korma or dislikes salad is now satisfied.\n- We proceed with the individuals unsatisfied. If a person's likes are all in the conflict list but has a dislike that isn't, we can consider adding that dislike to the 'not_orderable' list to satisfy them.\n\nFollowing this criteria, we must examine other dislikes of the individuals and avoid causing conflicts with the likes of others. By iterating through the dislikes, we can identify if any further choices can be made without causing conflicts.\n\nHere's the situation after adding korma to 'orderable' and salad to 'not_orderable':\n- Anyone liking vegetables is satisfied because if they dislike salad (which is on the not_orderable list), their preference is already accounted for.\n- Anyone liking or disliking bread and omelette cannot be immediately catered to due to conflicts, but these likes/dislikes could become relevant if we consider additional disliked items for the 'not_orderable' list.\n\nEquipped with these constraints, we must look at the individual preferences again to see if remaining unsatisfied people can have any of their dislikes that no one else likes put into 'not_orderable'. If that's impossible, we consider 'orderable' if their likes aren't conflicting with other's dislikes. With many people liking conflicting items, we 