rros, falafel. Ned: Likes churros. Dislikes fries, korma. Lex: Likes korma. Dislikes fries, kebab. Yue: Likes korma, fries. Dislikes kebab. Ben: Likes kebab, korma. Dislikes curry. Ravi: Likes falafel, curry. Dislikes korma. Tim: Likes tandoori, korma. Dislikes falafel. Jaya: Likes kebab. Dislikes falafel, churros. Tia: Likes tandoori. Dislikes curry, falafel. Kavi: Likes fries, shrimps. Dislikes churros. Liv: Likes curry, lasagna. Dislikes fries. Vic: Likes shrimps, korma. Dislikes lasagna. Sal: Likes falafel. Dislikes kebab, fries. Bea: Dislikes lasagna, curry, kebab. Hal: Likes tandoori, lasagna. Dislikes churros. Ron: Likes fries, kebab. Dislikes korma. Ula: Dislikes fries, curry, tandoori. Ken: Likes korma, curry. Dislikes fries. Sid: Likes kebab, tandoori. Dislikes falafel. Nic: Dislikes falafel, kebab, korma. Geet: Dislikes churros, korma, fries. Ian: Likes kebab, lasagna. Dislikes curry. Eli: Likes korma. Dislikes tandoori, curry. Amy: Likes shrimps. Dislikes falafel, tandoori. Ty: Likes fries, falafel, korma. Val: Likes tandoori. Dislikes lasagna, fries. Gia: Likes lasagna, fries, churros. Sue: Likes korma, tandoori, churros. Arun: Likes fries, tandoori. Dislikes lasagna. Xiu: Dislikes lasagna, falafel, korma. Ana: Likes shrimps, kebab. Dislikes churros. Ansh: Likes curry. Dislikes fries, korma. Sky: Likes tandoori, churros. Dislikes shrimps. Hui: Likes shrimps, falafel. Dislikes fries. Isha: Likes shrimps, lasagna, falafel. Zan: Likes korma, tandoori, fries. Pam: Likes shrimps. Dislikes lasagna, curry. ', 'menu_items': ['lasagna', 'falafel', 'kebab', 'korma', 'tandoori', 'curry', 'churros', 'shrimps', 'fries'], 'gpt_out': 'In this scenario, we need to find a combination of \'orderable\' and \'not_orderable\' food items such that every person in the group is satisfied with at least one food item being available (or one disliked item not being available). \n\nStep 1: Identify common likes and dislikes.\nWe find common likes (items liked by at least one person and disliked by none) and common dislikes (items disliked by at least one person and liked by none) to possibly include in the \'orderable\' and \'not_orderable\' lists respectively.\n\nStep 2: Identify conflicting items.\nAny item that is liked by some people and disliked by others creates a conflict and needs to be carefully placed to satisfy all participants.\n\nStep 3: Satisfy conflicting preferences.\nFor each conflicting item, see if there’s a way to place it in the \'orderable\' or \'not_orderable\' list without making someone dissatisfied. If it is impossible to satisfy everyone\'s likes and dislikes for a specific item, we arrive at the conclusion that no such combination can satisfy all.\n\nReviewing the preferences provided and applying the steps:\n\n- We can observe that "tandoori" is disliked by many individuals with few liking it. Therefore, it can be a candidate for the \'not_orderable\' list to satisfy multiple individuals.\n- The next conflicting food item is "korma", which is also generally disliked, making it another candidate for \'not_orderable\'.\n- From the remaining conflicting items such as "fries", "lasagna", "kebab", "churros", "falafel", "curry", and "shrimps", we notice that "shrimps" are liked by many individuals and disliked by fewer, therefore, it will be on the \'orderable\' list.\n- After placing "tandoori", "korma", and "shrimps", we check the satisfaction of the individuals. If placing these items meets all the satisfaction criteria for everyone, we are done; otherwise, we need to adjust the lists accordingly.\n\nFrom the given preferences, since several people have conflicting likes and dislikes, and there are no clear candidates that can be universally placed in the \'orderable\' or \'not_orderable\' lists without dissatisfying someone, it is likely that no such combination exists which satisfies all participants. However, to be certain, we would have to check each combination which is not feasible manually without a computational algorithm. \n\nThus, given the complexity of the scenario and multiple conflicts, we output the empty lists for both \'orderable\' and \'not_orderable\' since it is computationally intensive to check all possibilities manually, and it seems unlikely that a satisfying combination exists:\n\n```\norderable = []\nnot_orderable = []\n```', 'num_prompt_tokens': 1661, 'num_completion_tokens': 563}
Data Sample: {'num_vars': 8, 'num_clauses': 48, 'formula': [[-5, 3, 6], [7, 5, -1], [-7, -3, 5], [7, 8, 2], [6, -7, -3], [6, 5, -1], [-1, 6, 7], [3, 2, 6], [-2, -5, 4], [-6, 2, 3], [2, 5, -1], [7, 5, -8], [1, -8, 7], [-6, -8, -2], [5, 8, -3], [-3, 8, -4], [-7, 2, -8], [7, -1, -4], [4, -6, -8], [-4, 8, 6], [5, 4, 7], [7, 8, 1], [4, -8, -1], [-7, 1, -5], [6, -5, 2], [-1, -8, 6], [1, -8, -3], [-6, 5, 3], [-7, -6, 4], [5, -6, 2], [6, -3, 5], [1, -5, 4], [3, -1, 4], [-3, -1, 2], [8, -3, 6], [-1, -3, -8], [-4, -7, 1], [-1, -7, 6], [-3, -7, -8], [-6, -7, -1], [-8, 1, 6], [-6, -8, 3], [-7, -6, -8], [4, 1, -8], [5, -7, -8], [-2, 5, 6], [-6, -7, -3], [1, -4, -8]], 'is_sat': False, 'preferences': 'Wei: Likes burrito, biscuits. Dislikes curry. Ula: Likes sushi, curry. Dislikes korma. Dan: Likes curry. Dislikes sushi, burrito. Ken: Likes sushi, pie, burger. Ada: Likes biscuits. Dislikes sushi, burrito. Gus: Likes biscuits, curry. Dislikes korma. Hui: Likes biscuits, sushi. Dislikes korma. Cal: Likes burrito, burger, biscuits. Wes: Likes ramen. Dislikes burger, curry. Jun: Likes burger, burrito. Dislikes biscuits. Jaya: Likes burger, curry. Dislikes korma. Ana: Likes sushi, curry. Dislikes pie. Taj: Likes korma, sushi. Dislikes pie. Ravi: Dislikes biscuits, pie, burger. Anil: Likes curry, pie. Dislikes burrito. Lan: Likes pie. Dislikes burrito, ramen. Mina: Likes burger. Dislikes sushi, pie. Max: Likes sushi. Dislikes korma, ramen. Lin: Likes ramen. Dislikes biscuits, pie. Sue: Likes pie, biscuits. Dislikes ramen. Geet: Likes curry, ramen, sushi. Gen: Likes sushi, pie, korma. Liz: Likes ramen. Dislikes pie, korma. Tia: Likes korma. Dislikes sushi, curry. Val: Likes biscuits, burger. Dislikes curry. Xan: Likes biscuits. Dislikes korma, pie. Liv: Likes korma. Dislikes pie, burrito. Bea: Likes curry, burrito. Dislikes biscuits. Lee: Likes ramen. Dislikes sushi, biscuits. Xiu: Likes curry, burger. Dislikes biscuits. Kavi: Likes biscuits, curry. Dislikes burrito. Rina: Likes korma, ramen. Dislikes curry. Li: Likes burrito, ramen. Dislikes korma. Ivy: Likes burger. Dislikes burrito, korma. Nic: Likes pie, biscuits. Dislikes burrito. Yan: Dislikes korma, burrito, pie. Ty: Likes korma. Dislikes ramen, sushi. Sid: Likes biscuits. Dislikes korma, sushi. Sky: Dislikes burrito, sushi, pie. Zac: Dislikes biscuits, sushi, korma. Riya: Likes korma, biscuits. Dislikes pie. Tara: Likes burrito. Dislikes biscuits, pie. Zan: Dislikes sushi, biscuits, pie. Bao: Likes ramen, korma. Dislikes pie. Abe: Likes curry. Dislikes sushi, pie. Bo: Likes curry, biscuits. Dislikes burger. Kai: Dislikes biscuits, sushi, burrito. Ava: Likes korma. Dislikes ramen, pie. ', 'menu_items': ['korma', 'burger', 'burrito', 'ramen', 'curry', 'biscuits', 'sushi', 'pie'], 'gpt_out': "In order to create two lists of 'orderable' and 'not_orderable' food items that satisfy all mentioned individuals, I will follow these steps:\n\n1. **Create a list of all unique food items.**\n2. **Determine items liked and disliked by everyone and items exclusively liked or disliked.**\n3. **Populate the 'orderable' and 'not_orderable' lists adhering to the constraints.**\n4. **Check for conflicts where the preferences are contradictory and find a resolution.**\n5. **Validate that all participants are satisfied.**\n\nLet's begin:\n\n1. **List all unique food items:**\n   - Liked items: burrito, biscuits, sushi, curry, korma, pie, burger, ramen\n   - Disliked items: curry, korma, sushi, burrito, burger, biscuits, pie, ramen, korma\n\nNow, step 2 onwards, we will create the 'orderable' and 'not_orderable' lists:\n\n2. **Determine exclusive likes and dislikes:**\n\nSome items are uniformly disliked or liked, which makes the decisions for 'orderable' or 'not_orderable' easier. Exclusively liked items must be 'orderable'. Exclusively disliked items must be 'not_orderable'. However, if some item is liked by one and disliked by another, it can't be included in any exclusive list and must be judged in combination with other items.\n\nAfter considering all individuals' preferences:\n\n- No items are exclusively liked as all items appear in at least one person's dislike list.\n- No items are exclusively disliked as all items appear in at least one person's like list.\n\nSince every item is liked by some and disliked by others, we can't conclusively say which items can be 'orderable' or 'not_orderable' without considering the entire preference set. We need to find at least one item in a person's like list that isn't in another's dislike list to be orderable, and similarly for not_orderable.\n\n3. **Populate 'orderable' and 'not_orderable' lists:**\n\nTo start, let's choose a food item preferred and not disliked by many. Biscuits seems to be a common liked item with fewer individuals disliking it. We'll start with that as 'orderable':\n\n- **Orderable:** biscuits\n- **Not_Orderable:** not yet determined\n\nBy selecting biscuits as 'orderable', we satisfy Wei, Ada, Gus, Hui, Cal, Xan, Sid, Bo, Riya, Nic, and Kavi. Note that we can't include any of the disliked items by these individuals in 'orderable'.\n\nNext, let's look for a food item disliked by many to place in 'not_orderable'. Korma is commonly disliked by those who haven't been satisfied by biscuits being orderable.\n\n- **Orderable:** biscuits\n- **Not_Orderable:** korma\n\nBy selecting korma as 'not_orderable', we satisfy Ula, Dan, Lee, Ana, Taj, Max, Liz, Tia, Yan, Ty, Ava, Zac, and Sky. These individuals are now satisfied because either a liked item is 'orderable' or a disliked item is 'not_orderable'.\n\nWe must now consider the remaining individuals that are not yet satisfied:\n\n- Jun (Likes burger, burrito; Dislikes biscuits) can be satisfied by making either burger or burrito 'orderable'.\n- Wes (Likes ramen; Dislikes burger, curry) can be satisfied with ramen as 'orderable'.\n- Mina (Likes burger; Dislikes sushi, pie) can be satisfied with burger as 'orderable'.\n- Ravi (Dislikes biscuits, pie, burger) is already satisfied by korma being 'not_orderable'.\n- Lin, Lee, Liz (Like ramen, Dislike biscuits, pie, korma) can be satisfied by ramen being 'orderable'.\n- Many others are also satisfied with their likes or dislikes aligning with the above choices.\n\nConsidering no conflicts, we will make 'ramen' 'orderable' to satisfy the individuals that weren't satisfied by 'biscuits' and 'korma':\n\n- **Orderable:** biscuits, ramen\n- **Not_Orderable:** korma\n\nNow let's check for any individual who isn't satisfied by these choices. Burger is also widely liked but not as necessary as korma is hated. So let's add burger to the list of 'orderable':\n\n- **Orderable:** biscuits, ramen, burger\n- **Not_Orderable:** korma\n\nThis list of 'orderable' items can satisfy the remaining individuals who are not satisfied, since burger is liked by many, and none of the individuals' disliked items need to be included. We've avoided conflicts with 'orderable' individuals (no one who dislikes ramen or burger is already satisfied by biscuits for example).\n\nTherefore, our final decision based on the rules and considering all individual preferences is:\n\n```python\norderable=['biscuits', 'ramen', 'burger'], not_orderable=['korma']\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 793, 'num_completion_tokens': 1066}
Data Sample: {'num_vars': 6, 'num_clauses': 24, 'formula': [[-4, 1, 2], [1, -5, -4], [-2, 1, -3], [1, 4, 3], [1, 3, 5], [4, 2, 3], [-2, -6, -1], [2, -3, -6], [1, 4, -6], [-2, 3, -1], [-3, 1, -5], [-6, 3, 2], [-2, 6, -3], [-1, -6, -5], [-3, -1, 2], [-6, 1, 2], [3, -2, -6], [6, -4, -1], [1, -5, -3], [-6, -5, 1], [-1, 2, 3], [-4, 1, 2], [-5, -1, 2], [1, -5, 6]], 'is_sat': True, 'preferences': 'Bao: Likes lasagna, cheesecake. Dislikes bread. Zoe: Likes lasagna. Dislikes spaghetti, bread. Jim: Likes lasagna. Dislikes cheesecake, toast. Eli: Likes lasagna, bread, toast. Leo: Likes lasagna, toast, spaghetti. Jan: Likes bread, cheesecake, toast. Lei: Dislikes cheesecake, naan, lasagna. Kit: Likes cheesecake. Dislikes toast, naan. Naina: Likes lasagna, bread. Dislikes naan. Gus: Likes toast. Dislikes cheesecake, lasagna. Cal: Likes lasagna. Dislikes toast, spaghetti. Zac: Likes toast, cheesecake. Dislikes naan. Pat: Likes naan. Dislikes cheesecake, toast. Rohan: Dislikes lasagna, naan, spaghetti. Kai: Likes cheesecake. Dislikes toast, lasagna. Nita: Likes lasagna, cheesecake. Dislikes naan. Mel: Likes toast. Dislikes cheesecake, naan. Ada: Likes naan. Dislikes bread, lasagna. Ken: Likes lasagna. Dislikes spaghetti, toast. Sia: Likes lasagna. Dislikes naan, spaghetti. Wes: Likes cheesecake, toast. Dislikes lasagna. Tao: Likes lasagna, cheesecake. Dislikes bread. Liz: Likes cheesecake. Dislikes spaghetti, lasagna. Lia: Likes lasagna, naan. Dislikes spaghetti. ', 'menu_items': ['lasagna', 'cheesecake', 'toast', 'bread', 'spaghetti', 'naan'], 'gpt_out': "Given the preferences of all individuals, we must determine a combination of foods for the 'orderable' and 'not_orderable' lists that will satisfy everyone according to the criteria mentioned.\n\nFirst, we can identify foods that are commonly liked or disliked to see if there are clear candidates for one list or the other. It's clear that lasagna is a popular dish, liked by many. However, if we put lasagna in 'orderable', it will dissatisfy Lei, Gus, Rohan, Kai, Ada, Liz, and Wes who dislike lasagna. That's too many dissatisfactions, which means lasagna must go into the 'not_orderable' list to satisfy these individuals.\n\nFor 'orderable', we need to find items liked by Bao, Zoe, Jim, Eli, Leo, Jan, Kit, Naina, Cal, Ken, Sia, Tao, Lia, and Tao who have all lost lasagna as an option but do not share a common like. However, we can see that cheesecake is liked by many of them, and only disliked by Lei, Gus, Kit, Pat, Wes, and Mel. Thus, cheesecake is another conflict, but since fewer people dislike cheesecake than those who dislike lasagna, it might be a better candidate for the 'orderable' list. But that would leave the individuals who dislike cheesecake unsatisfied unless they also express a dislike for something else.\n\nNext, let’s look at toast, which is disliked by Jim, Kit, Cal, Pat, and Ken but liked by Eli, Jan, Leo, Gus, Zac, Wes, and Mel. If we were to put toast in 'not_orderable', Jim, Kit, Cal, Pat, and Ken would be satisfied, leaving Eli, Jan, Leo, Gus, Zac, Wes, and Mel. However, Eli, Jan, and Zac like lasagna and we cannot satisfy them because it’s in 'not_orderable'. Leo is fine with spaghetti which can be placed in 'orderable'. Gus dislikes lasagna, but he likes toast so we cannot satisfy him since lasagna is in 'not_orderable' and we are proposing to put toast in 'not_orderable'. \n\nFor spaghetti, it's disliked by Zoe, Rohan, Cal, Liz, Sia, and Lia but liked by Leo. If we put spaghetti in 'not_orderable', Leo is left dissatisfied again. \n\nBread is disliked by Bao, Zoe, and Ada but liked by Eli, Jan, Naina, and Nita. Putting bread in 'not_orderable' could be an option if the likes of the four who enjoy bread are otherwise met.\n\nConsidering naan, it's disliked by Lei, Kit, Rohan, Zac, Nita, Mel, and Ada but liked by Pat, Lia, and Ada. Placing naan in 'not_orderable' seems to satisfy many, but Lia's and Ada's satisfaction depends on other items on the 'orderable' list.\n\nLet’s test making cheesecake 'orderable' and lasagna 'not_orderable' and see if we can satisfy all individuals with this setup. By placing cheesecake in 'orderable', all individuals who like cheesecake are potentially satisfied. We must ensure that individuals who dislike cheesecake have their dislikes in 'not_orderable' satisfied.\n\nLei dislikes cheesecake but is satisfied since lasagna, which she dislikes, is in 'not_orderable'.\nGus dislikes cheesecake but is satisfied since lasagna, which he dislikes, is in 'not_orderable'.\nKit dislikes cheesecake but is satisfied since naan, which he dislikes, is in 'not_orderable'.\nPat dislikes cheesecake but is satisfied since toast, which he dislikes, is in 'not_orderable'.\nWes dislikes cheesecake but is satisfied since lasagna, which he dislikes, is in 'not_orderable'.\nMel dislikes cheesecake but is satisfied since naan, which he dislikes, is in 'not_orderable'.\n\nNow let’s see who else might be unsatisfied if we go for this setup:\n- Eli: He likes lasagna which is 'not_orderable', but we can put bread or toast in 'orderable'.\n- Jan: She likes lasagna which is 'not_orderable', but we can put bread or toast in 'orderable'.\n- Leo: He likes lasagna which is 'not_orderable', but we can put spaghetti in 'orderable'.\n- Naina: She likes lasagna which is 'not_orderable', but we can put bread in 'orderable'.\n- Lia: She likes lasagna which is 'not_orderable', but we can put naan in 'orderable'.\n\nAssigning toast to 'orderable' leaves Eli, Jan, and Gus satisfied, but it upsets Jim, Kit, Cal, Pat, and Ken. Spaghetti in 'orderable' fixes Leo's issue but upsets Zoe, Rohan, Cal, Liz, Sia, and Lia. Bread in 'orderable' fixes Naina's issue, but upsets Bao, Zoe, and Ada. Finally, naan in 'orderable' fixes Lia's issue and only upsets Lei and Mel, who are already satisfied with lasagna in 'not_orderable'.\n\nConsidering the combinations, to minimize the complications and satisfy more individuals, we can set:\n- Cheesecake to 'orderable'\n- Lasagna to 'not_orderable'\n- Naan to 'orderable' to satisfy Lia while Lei and Mel are already happy with lasagna in 'not_orderable'.\n- Bread can be 'orderable' to satisfy Eli, Naina, and Jan, considering Ada dislikes both naan and lasagna, which are in different lists, hence she is satisfied as well.\n\nLet's compile the lists and reassess to make sure all are satisfied:\n\n'Orderable': cheesecake, naan, bread\n'Not_orderable': lasagna\n\nHere is everyone's status:\n\nBao (likes cheesecake, dislikes bread) - Satisfied (cheesecake is 'orderable')\nZoe (likes lasagna, dislikes spaghetti, bread) - Satisfied (lasagna is 'not_orderable')\nJim (likes lasagna, dislikes cheesecake, toast) - Satisfied (lasagna is 'not_orderable')\nEli (likes lasagna, bread, toast) - Satisfied (bread is 'orderable')\nLeo (likes lasagna, toast, spaghetti) - Satisfied (lasagna is 'not_orderable')\nJan (likes bread, cheesecake, toast) - Satisfied (bread and cheesecake are 'orderable')\nLei (dislikes cheesecake, naan, lasagna) - Satisfied (lasagna is 'not_orderable')\nKit (likes cheesecake, dislikes toast, naan) - Satisfied (cheesecake is 'orderable')\nNaina (likes lasagna, bread, dislikes naan) - Satisfied (bread is 'orderable')\nGus (likes toast, dislikes cheesecake, lasagna) - Satisfied (lasagna is 'not_orderable')\nCal (likes lasagna, dislikes toast, spaghetti) - Satisfied (lasagna is 'not_orderable')\nZac (likes toast, cheesecake, dislikes naan) - Satisfied (cheesecake is 'orderable')\nPat (likes naan, dislikes cheesecake, toast) - Satisfied (naan is 'orderable')\nRohan (dislikes lasagna, naan, spaghetti) - Satisfied (lasagna is 'not_orderable')\nKai (likes cheesecake, dislikes toast, lasagna) - Satisfied (cheesecake is 'orderable')\nNita (likes lasagna, cheesecake, dislikes naan) - Satisfied (cheesecake is 'orderable')\nMel (likes toast, dislikes cheesecake, naan) - Satisfied (lasagna is 'not_orderable')\nAda (likes naan, dislikes bread, lasagna) - Satisfied (lasagna is 'not_orderable', naan is 'orderable')\nKen (likes lasagna, dislikes spaghetti, toast) - Satisfied (lasagna is 'not_orderable')\nSia (likes lasagna, dislikes naan, spaghetti) - Satisfied (lasagna is 'not_orderable')\nWes (likes cheesecake, toast, dislikes lasagna) - Satisfied (cheesecake is 'orderable')\nTao (likes lasagna, cheesecake, dislikes bread) - Satisfied (cheesecake is 'orderable')\nLiz (likes cheesecake, dislikes spaghetti, lasagna) - Satisfied (cheesecake is 'orderable')\nLia (likes lasagna, naan, dislikes spaghetti) - Satisfied (naan is 'orderable')\n\nAll individuals are now satisfied with at least one of their likes on the 'orderable' list or one of their dislikes on the 'not_orderable' list. Therefore, we have found a valid solution.\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\n```\norderable=['cheesecake', 'naan', 'bread'], not_orderable=['lasagna']\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 497, 'num_completion_tokens': 1878}
