ttempt to place the universally disliked items in the \'not_orderable\' list (ones that meet at least one person\'s dislikes and no one\'s likes). However, there are no such items that are disliked by everyone or not liked by anyone.\n\nOn the other hand, if we look for universally liked items (ones that meet at least one person\'s likes and no one\'s dislikes), we will also fail because there are no such items as well—every item is disliked by at least one person.\n\nTo find a solution, we need to combine both likes and dislikes and try to satisfy as many people as possible. Let\'s try to sort this out:\n\nTo maximize satisfaction:\n1. We can place in \'orderable\' those items that are liked by the most people and disliked by the fewest.\n2. We can place in \'not_orderable\' those items that are disliked by the most people and liked by the fewest.\n\nLet\'s analyze the data for such opportunities:\n- Omelette is liked by many but disliked by quite a few too. It\'s a controversial item.\n- Paneer is also controversial but slightly less so than omelette.\n- Pie is generally more disliked than liked, so it\'s a candidate for \'not_orderable\'.\n- Curry, biscuits, and quiche are all controversial as well.\n- Bread seems to be the least controversial item and could potentially find its place in the \'orderable\' list.\n\nLet\'s start with placing "bread" in \'orderable\' since it seems to have the least contention (liked by some, disliked by fewer), and "pie" in \'not_orderable\' since it is disliked by many. Let\'s see if these selections satisfy everyone:\n\n- Bread in \'orderable\': Satisfies Lia, Dot, Zara, Wei, Mei, Ula, and Chao. It does not satisfy those who dislike bread, such as Cal, Feng, Arun, Lex, and May.\n- Pie in \'not_orderable\': Satisfies Ada, Ray, Feng, Meg, Lan, Liv, Chen, Sam, Jaya, Mina, and Mel. \n\nSo far, we have potentially found a satisfactory item for some, but not all. Now, we need to find a complementary set of items.\n\n**Continued analysi