nd disliked by others (e.g., Bea). \n  - Lasagna is liked by many but disliked by a few (e.g., Diya).\n  - Omelette, likewise, has people who like and dislike it.\n  - And so on for the other items.\n\nStep 3 is complex, so let's narrow it down:\n- Since lasagna is liked by many (Joe, Kit, Naina, Wes, Val, Du, Lan, Hui, Eva, Abe, Pat,) and disliked by a few (Diya, Riya, Tao, Jay), can it be placed in 'orderable'? \n  - If we place it in 'not_orderable', Joe, Kit, Val Du, Wes, Naina, and Lan would be unsatisfied because it's their only like. \n  - So, lasagna should be in 'orderable'.\n  \n- Next, let's consider omelette:\n  - It's liked by several people (Naina, Dan, Tara, Rex, Eva, Om, Qi, Bao) and disliked by several (Nic, Veer, Kit, Lan, Sol, Jun, Wes, Val, Mei, Arun, Ravi, Sky, Jay, Liv).\n  - Let's check if putting omelette in 'orderable' makes anyone unsatisfied. Tao dislikes lasagna which is already in 'orderable', so if omelette is also 'orderable', Tao will be unsatisfied. Om also dislikes lasagna which is orderable, so Om needs omelette to be 'not_orderable'.\n\nGiven this conflict, omlette should be 'not_orderable'. This satisfies Tao and Om, and the others who liked omelette are still satisfied because they like at least another item that has been or can be included in 'orderable'.\n\nWith these clues in hand, let's move forward:\n- We've included lasagna in 'orderable' and omelette in 'not_orderable'. Now, we have to ensure that at least one liked item for every individual is in 'orderable' or one disliked item is in 'not_orderable'.\n- By looking briefly, it seems impossible to satisfy Bea, who dislikes couscous, lasagna, and kimchi. If lasagna is orderable, we would need to put either couscous or kimchi in 'not_orderable' to satisfy her, but this causes issues for others. \n\nWe can then conclude that there is no combination that will satisfy everyone based on the given preferences. Therefore, both lists should be empty:\n\n```python\norderable=[], no