re is to consider an orderable item that satisfies the most "likes" without conflicting with "dislikes" from the other members and, similarly, selecting not orderable items that accommodate the most dislikes without conflicting with likes.\n\nLet’s start by tabulating the "likes" and "dislikes" that aren\'t shared among different individuals and see if any clear candidates for \'orderable\' or \'not_orderable\' lists emerge.\n\nIt looks like "shawarma" has mixed responses – some like it and some dislike it, so let\'s set that aside for now. The same applies to "peach," "paneer," "pizza," "spaghetti," "bruschetta," "couscous," "lasagna," and "kebab." These items generate conflict because they are both liked and disliked by different group members.\n\nHowever, we can consider items that have not been mentioned by many or that don\'t conflict. It seems that some individuals have dislikes but don\'t have strong likes or the likes are overlapping with other\'s dislikes. \n\nWe\'ll pick an \'orderable\' item that is liked by at least one person and not disliked by anyone, and a \'not_orderable\' item that is disliked by at least one person and not liked by anyone. If an item is liked by one person and disliked by another, it cannot be cleanly sorted into either list without additional context.\n\nBut unfortunately, out of the items listed, each item is liked by at least one and disliked by at least another, leaving no item conflict-free.\n\nWe cannot conclude on \'orderable\' and \'not_orderable\' lists that will satisfy every individual\'s preferences without conflict, thus we end up with an impossible situation where the criteria cannot be met.\n\nTherefore, the output lists are both empty:\n\n```python\norderable=[], not_orderable=[]\n```', 'num_prompt_tokens': 586, 'num_completion_tokens': 390}
