rong candidate for the \'orderable\' list.\nKimchi is liked by some but disliked by others, making it another conflicting item.\nRavioli is liked by some, but it is also a common dislike.\n\nNow, let\'s attempt to find a pair of lists that satisfies all without conflicts. We\'ll start by identifying items that can be safely placed in the \'orderable\' or \'not_orderable\' lists:\n\nStarting with dislikes:\n- Since "cake" is very commonly disliked and liked by a few, we might look to place it on the \'not_orderable\' list, taking caution with individuals who like cake.\n- "Tart" - Only one person likes tart (Shan), while several dislike it; it might be a good candidate for \'not_orderable\' unless it conflicts with Shan\'s likes. \n\nConsidering likes:\n- "Tacos" could be \'orderable\' as many like them, and few dislike them.\n- "Burrito" is liked by many, but since it\'s also disliked by quite a few, we should avoid adding it to the \'orderable\' list as it would not satisfy all individuals.\n\nIf we place "cake" in \'not_orderable\', all those who like cake must have another liked item on the \'orderable\' list.\n\nNow let\'s make an initial pass based on the most commonly liked and disliked items:\n\nAdding "tacos" to the \'orderable\' list would satisfy Mel, Neil, Om, Pat, and Arun immediately, as it\'s their liked item and not disliked by many.\n\nAdding "tart" to \'not_orderable\' would satisfy all who dislike tart, except Shan, who would then need another liked item of hers on the \'orderable\' list.\n\nNext, considering "cake" for \'not_orderable\' would satisfy those who dislike it, but then we must ensure all those who like it (Neil, Rob, Sam, Jay, Ula, and Joe) have another liked item on the \'orderable\' list to be satisfied.\n\nTo do this, we can examine other liked items by these individuals:\n- Neil has tacos, already in \'orderable\'.\n- Rob, dislike burrito, so burrito can\'t be in \'orderable\'.\n- Sam dislikes bread and tacos, and both cannot be in