 mixed, we now need to find some compromise to satisfy everyone\'s preferences.\n\nGiven the complexity of individual likes and dislikes, we can\'t simply use a linear assignment. We will approach this with an iterative process, resolving conflicts as they arise.\n\nStarting with the \'orderable\' and \'not_orderable\' as empty lists, we will pick an item and see if it can be assigned to one of the lists without causing a conflict. We will try to assign items likely to satisfy multiple people first.\n\nFor example, a starting point could be:\n\n- We notice many people like "shrimps", and it\'s a liked item of Pia\'s but it is also an item many dislike, so we can\'t straight away place it in the \'orderable\'. But if "cake" is in the \'not_orderable\', it satisfies Pia, and this doesn\'t rule out anyone\'s preference, making multiple people satisfied in turn.\n\nLet\'s start by considering "cake" for \'not_orderable\' and see if that meets the criteria for including or excluding other items:\n\n- Pia will be satisfied if "cake" is in the \'not_orderable\' since it\'s on her dislike list, she also likes "shrimps" and "salmon", but those can\'t be put on the \'orderable\' list yet given the dislikes from others.\n- Om dislikes "lasagna", "cake", and "salmon". If "cake" is in \'not_orderable\', Om is satisfied.\n- By having "cake" in \'not_orderable\', many others who dislike "cake" are automatically satisfied, without putting any items in \'orderable\' that are disliked by others.\n- Since "cake" is a common dislike, we can start by setting \'not_orderable\' to include "cake", satisfying a large portion of the group.\n\nNow, we need to look at the rest of the preferences to identify an item to put into \'orderable\' which addresses the likes of the maximum number of people while also considering the dislikes mentioned.\n\nBefore listing out the \'orderable\' let\'s check if any preferences conflict to the point that it\'s not possible to satisfy them:\n\n- We do not seem to have any immediate conflicts that stand out with placing "cake" in \'not_orderable\', but there will likely be conflicts when we start adding à la carte items into \'orderable\'.\n\nBased on the preferences, and in particular the multiple likes for "shrimps", we can consider adding "shrimps" to the \'orderable\' list and check if it conflicts.\n\n- If "shrimps" are in \'orderable\', Pia, Ana, Kit, Ivy, Lin, Tim, Meg, Eli, Naina, all are satisfied with at least one liked food.\n- However, individuals like Sue and Isha who dislike "shrimps" will need an item in the \'not_orderable\' to be satisfied. Since "cake" is already in \'not_orderable\', this might be sufficient. The others who dislike "shrimps" also dislike "cake", which is already catered.\n\nWe will add "shrimps" to \'orderable\'.\n\nNext, let\'s look at preferences for other items and see if we can add more:\n\n- Key preferences are around "lasagna", "salmon", "gelato", "udon" - each of these has conflicts of liking and disliking, making them challenging to allocate without conflict.\n\nAt this point, it seems that every other item left besides "shrimps" and "cake" would be contested by someone\'s preference. Therefore, we must carefully choose items that do not conflict and satisfy the maximum number of people possible without breaking the established \'orderable\' and \'not_orderable\'.\n\nWe are left to try to satisfy remaining people like Om, who dislikes "lasagna", "cake", and "salmon". Since "cake" is not_orderable, his preferences are also satisfied, and we do not need to add anything else specifically for him. This pattern continues for most others, as their dislikes have been covered by "cake" being not orderable or their likings covered by "shrimps" being orderable.\n\nAssessing the preferences of each person, it appears that by having "cake" in \'not_orderable\' and "shrimps" in \'orderable\', we satisfy at least one like or one dislike of all individuals.\n\nTherefore, our solution would b