"orderable".\n- Jan, Joe, Ray, Lex, Wei, Anil, Lin, Chen -> Hummus in not orderable is already satisfied as hummus is "orderable".\n- Ken -> Falafel can go to "not_orderable".\n- Om -> Can be satisfied by having doughnut in "orderable" or fries in "not_orderable".\n- Li -> Satisfied with doughnut in "orderable" or fries in "not_orderable".\n\nConsiderations for Kai, Ula, Tao, Mia, Shan, Kit, Zara, Jaya, Tim, and Fay:\n- Kai -> Satisfied by having hummus in "orderable".\n- Ula -> Likes hummus. Fulfilled.\n- Tao, Zara -> Likes fries and hummus, satisfied with either in "orderable".\n- Mia -> Dislikes doughnut and custard, hummus in "orderable" satisfies.\n- Shan -> Likes brownies, satisfied because it\'s "orderable".\n- Kit -> Dislikes all food items being considered, cannot be satisfied by only disliked ones.\n- Tim -> Falafel could be in "not_orderable" to satisfy him.\n- Fay -> Has dislikes only and no liked items chosen, therefore cannot be satisfied.\n\nUnsatisfiables: Kit, Fay and anyone sharing all their dislikes with these.\n\nBased on the above analysis, we have a conflict of interest for some individuals who cannot be satisfied simultaneously with others. The requirements state that all participants must be satisfied, and given the conflicting likes and dislikes, no such combination exists to meet that target.\n\nThe final solution is: `orderable=[], not_orderable=[]`.', 'num_prompt_tokens': 1101, 'num_completion_tokens': 948}
Data Sample: {'num_vars': 8, 'num_clauses': 32, 'formula': [[5, 4, -7], [-1, 5, 8], [-4, -6, -2], [4, -3, -6], [5, 4, -2], [-4, 2, 8], [-2, 5, 6], [4, 8, -7], [-3, 8, 7], [6, -3, -8], [-5, 3, 6], [4, -3, 5], [-1, 5, 6], [5, -4, -8], [4, -1, 6], [5, 2, 8], [5, 4, -7], [1, 5, 4], [6, 3, 4], [-7, -6, 3], [8, 2, -4], [-7, -8, 2], [-1, -3, 4], [1, 8, 5], [-4, 6, -2], [-7, -6, -5], [-5, 4, -2], [6, 2, -7], [-6, 3, 7], [-6, -8, 2], [-1, 7, -6], [-8, 4, 1]], 'is_sat': False, 'preferences': 'Veer: Likes lasagna, shawarma. Dislikes toast. Pia: Likes lasagna, paneer. Dislikes kebab. Ava: Dislikes shawarma, salmon, pho. Ray: Likes shawarma. Dislikes burger, salmon. Du: Likes lasagna, shawarma. Dislikes pho. Joe: Likes pho, paneer. Dislikes shawarma. Xan: Likes lasagna, salmon. Dislikes pho. Amy: Likes shawarma, paneer. Dislikes toast. Cy: Likes paneer, toast. Dislikes burger. Ravi: Likes salmon. Dislikes burger, paneer. Ned: Likes burger, salmon. Dislikes lasagna. Lia: Likes shawarma, lasagna. Dislikes burger. Joy: Likes lasagna, salmon. Dislikes kebab. Shan: Likes lasagna. Dislikes shawarma, paneer. Arun: Likes shawarma, salmon. Dislikes kebab. Liv: Likes lasagna, pho, paneer. Zac: Likes lasagna, shawarma. Dislikes toast. Jim: Likes kebab, lasagna, shawarma. Pam: Likes salmon, burger, shawarma. Riya: Likes burger. Dislikes toast, salmon. Feng: Likes paneer, pho. Dislikes shawarma. Liz: Likes pho. Dislikes toast, paneer. Rina: Likes shawarma. Dislikes kebab, burger. Leo: Likes kebab, paneer, lasagna. Ru: Likes salmon. Dislikes shawarma, pho. Sid: Dislikes toast, salmon, lasagna. Bao: Likes shawarma. Dislikes lasagna, pho. Bo: Likes salmon, pho. Dislikes toast. Kim: Likes burger, toast. Dislikes salmon. Kai: Likes pho. Dislikes salmon, paneer. Sia: Likes toast. Dislikes kebab, salmon. Dan: Likes shawarma, kebab. Dislikes paneer. ', 'menu_items': ['kebab', 'pho', 'burger', 'shawarma', 'lasagna', 'salmon', 'toast', 'paneer'], 'gpt_out': "Given the food preferences of various individuals, let's first list all the unique food items and then follow the given satisfaction criteria to determine what can be ordered ('orderable') and what cannot be ordered ('not_orderable').\n\nUnique food items:\n1. lasagna\n2. shawarma\n3. toast\n4. paneer\n5. kebab\n6. salmon\n7. pho\n8. burger\n\nWe need to make sure each person is satisfied with at least one liked item in the 'orderable' list or one disliked item in the 'not_orderable' list, while also ensuring no item appears in both lists and that everyone is satisfied. If a person dislikes something, it is less risky to put that in 'not_orderable' since it does not affect their satisfaction if their liked item isn't available. However, for someone to be satisfied with a 'not_orderable' item, that item must be disliked by everyone.\n\nLet's start the sorting process by checking for items that are disliked by everyone who mentioned them. If an item is liked by at least one person, it can't be in the 'not_orderable' list:\n\n- toast (Disliked by Veer, Amy, Riya, Sid; but Liked by Cy, Kim, Sia)\n=> Cannot be 'not_orderable', as there are some individuals who like it.\n\n- salmon (Disliked by Ava, Ray, Ravi, Ru, Sid, Kim; but Liked by Xan, Ravi, Ned, Joy, Arun, Ru, Pam, Bo)\n=> Cannot be 'not_orderable', as there are some individuals who like it.\n\n- pho (Disliked by Ava, Du, Shan, Ru, Bao; but Liked by Joe, Liv, Feng, Liz, Bo, Kai)\n=> Cannot be 'not_orderable', as there are some individuals who like it.\n\n- kebab (Disliked by Pia, Joy, Arun, Rina, Sia; but Liked by Jim, Leo, Dan)\n=> Cannot be 'not_orderable', as there are some individuals who like it.\n\n- burger (Disliked by Ray, Cy, Ravi, Riya, Rina; but Liked by Ned, Kim, Pam, Riya)\n=> Cannot be 'not_orderable', as there are some individuals who like it.\n\nNegative items that can be made 'not_orderable':\n- shawarma (Disliked by Ava, Joe, Shan, Feng, Ru, Bao)\n=> Need to check if at least one liked item for these individuals could be 'orderable'.\n\nNow, we will attempt to find at least one item from the likes of each person that dislikes shawarma to make it 'not_orderable'. If no conflicts arise with other dislikes across individuals, we may proceed:\n\n- Joe likes paneer, which is not disliked universally.\n- Shan dislikes paneer but likes lasagna, which is also not disliked universally.\n- Feng likes pho, which is not disliked by everyone.\n\nAs for others who dislike shawarma (Ava, Ru, Bao), their likes are either also liked by others or not disliked by anyone. If we put shawarma in 'not_orderable', we create a conflict with Veer, Ray, Du, Amy, Zac, Rina, Bao who all like it.\n\nBecause of the conflicts with 'shawarma', and the fact that none of the dislikes are universal, thus can't be put in 'not_orderable' without offending someone who likes them, it is impossible to satisfy all individuals with these preferences. Therefore:\n\norderable = []\nnot_orderable = []", 'num_prompt_tokens': 622, 'num_completion_tokens': 755}
Data Sample: {'num_vars': 6, 'num_clauses': 30, 'formula': [[-1, 2, -4], [1, -4, -2], [-6, -3, -4], [4, -5, 6], [-6, -3, -1], [-5, 3, -1], [-3, -2, 5], [1, 4, -6], [2, -5, -3], [-6, 3, 1], [6, 1, -3], [1, 4, 6], [-4, -3, 5], [-3, 6, 5], [-1, 6, -3], [1, 4, 6], [2, 5, -1], [-3, 4, -5], [5, -2, -4], [-5, -4, 1], [4, -2, 5], [5, -6, -1], [1, 5, -4], [2, -1, -4], [1, -3, 4], [-3, 4, 6], [5, 1, -2], [2, 5, -3], [-3, 5, -4], [1, -2, 4]], 'is_sat': False, 'preferences': 'Tom: Likes shrimps. Dislikes curry, cheese. Lia: Likes curry. Dislikes cheese, shrimps. Gia: Dislikes risotto, pie, cheese. Xan: Likes cheese, risotto. Dislikes churros. Sia: Dislikes risotto, pie, curry. Lex: Likes pie. Dislikes churros, curry. Yue: Likes churros. Dislikes pie, shrimps. Hu: Likes curry, cheese. Dislikes risotto. Jan: Likes shrimps. Dislikes churros, pie. Ivy: Likes pie, curry. Dislikes risotto. Qi: Likes risotto, curry. Dislikes pie. Geet: Likes curry, cheese, risotto. Nita: Likes churros. Dislikes cheese, pie. Chao: Likes risotto, churros. Dislikes pie. Mel: Likes risotto. Dislikes curry, pie. Tim: Likes curry, cheese, risotto. Taj: Likes shrimps, churros. Dislikes curry. Pam: Likes cheese. Dislikes pie, churros. Jaya: Likes churros. Dislikes shrimps, cheese. Fay: Likes curry. Dislikes churros, cheese. Meg: Likes cheese, churros. Dislikes shrimps. Ru: Likes churros. Dislikes risotto, curry. Liv: Likes curry, churros. Dislikes cheese. Ray: Likes shrimps. Dislikes curry, cheese. Oli: Likes curry, cheese. Dislikes pie. Ned: Likes cheese, risotto. Dislikes pie. Tao: Likes churros, curry. Dislikes shrimps. Jay: Likes shrimps, churros. Dislikes pie. Chen: Likes churros. Dislikes pie, cheese. Zac: Likes curry, cheese. Dislikes shrimps. ', 'menu_items': ['curry', 'shrimps', 'pie', 'cheese', 'churros', 'risotto'], 'gpt_out': "To find a combination of 'orderable' and 'not_orderable' lists to satisfy everyone's preferences, let's approach this by listing out what is universally disliked and liked first. Then we can try to determine the items to place on each list by looking for conflicts.\n\nStarting with dislikes, we find out if there's any food item that is disliked by someone yet not liked by anyone else. This item can safely be put in the 'not_orderable' list.\n\nNext, we'll look at the likes and see if there's any food item that is liked by at least one person and not disliked by anyone. That item can be safely put in the 'orderable' list.\n\nFrom the list, we see:\n\n- Cheese is liked by many but also disliked by many, making it a conflict item.\n- Shrimps are liked by some but also disliked by others, another conflict.\n- Pie is a conflict, liked by some and disliked by others.\n- Risotto is also a conflict, liked and disliked by different individuals.\n- Curry is liked by several but disliked by a few.\n- Churros are liked by some and disliked by some.\n\nAlready we see that each food item appears in both likes and dislikes, meaning we will have to look at individual preferences to sort this out.\n\nLet's start categorizing based on universal dislikes and universal likes:\n\n1. We do not have any food item that is universally disliked.\n2. We do not have any food item that is universally liked.\n\nIn the absence of universal likes or dislikes, we need to ensure everyone is at least satisfied by the presence or absence of a certain food. The challenge is finding at least one liked item or one disliked item for each person.\n\nNow, let's attempt to optimize satisfaction:\n\n- Because curry is disliked by several people and liked by several others, we can't put curry in 'orderable' or 'not_orderable' without dissatisfaction of a group. So let's hold off on curry.\n\nThe rest of the strategy is trying to place an item in either list to satisfy as many people as possible while being careful not to dissatisfy others:\n\n- A strategy might be to find an item that only one person likes and no one dislikes or vice versa. However, everything that is liked is also disliked by someone else.\n  \nUnfortunately, due to the criteria, everyone must be satisfied, but any choice we make for either 'orderable' or 'not_orderable' for the items mentioned will result in someone being dissatisfied.\n\nFor example:\n\n- If we place cheese in 'orderable', Xan, Hu, Meg, Pam, Oli, Ned, and Zac will be satisfied, but Tom, Lia, Gia, Nita, Jaya, Fay, Liv, and Chen will not be.\n- Shrimps cannot be placed in 'orderable' without dissatisfying Lia, Yue, and Zac.\n- Pie can't be placed in 'orderable' without dissatisfying Gia, Sia, Jan, Nita, Chao, Pam, Chen, and Jay.\n- Risotto can't be placed in 'orderable' without dissatisfying Gia, Sia, Hu, Ivy, Mel, Ru, and Ru.\n\nNo matter how we try to arrange the items between 'orderable' and 'not_orderable' we cannot satisfy everyone because there is no item that satisfies the first person's criteria without contradicting another's. Therefore, based on the given likes and dislikes, it is impossible to satisfy all participants with the combination of these two lists.\n\nNo such combination exists that satisfies all. The solution is:\n\n```python\norderable=[], not_orderable=[]\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 590, 'num_completion_tokens': 749}
Data Sample: {'num_vars': 5, 'num_clauses': 10, 'formula': [[-5, 2, 3], [1, -3, -5], [4, 3, -2], [2, -3, 5], [-1, 2, 4], [-1, -5, 4], [-4, -3, -1], [-5, 1, 4], [2, -3, 4], [-1, -3, 5]], 'is_sat': True, 'preferences': 'Aarav: Likes couscous, quiche. Dislikes muffins. Min: Likes shrimps. Dislikes quiche, muffins. Bao: Likes salad, quiche. Dislikes couscous. Zan: Likes couscous, muffins. Dislikes quiche. Ava: Likes couscous, salad. Dislikes shrimps. Tara: Likes salad. Dislikes shrimps, muffins. Ben: Dislikes salad, quiche, shrimps. Mia: Likes shrimps, salad. Dislikes muffins. Raj: Likes couscous, salad. Dislikes quiche. Oli: Likes muffins. Dislikes shrimps, quiche. ', 'menu_items': ['shrimps', 'couscous', 'quiche', 'salad', 'muffins'], 'gpt_out': "Let's start by determining which items can be placed on the 'orderable' list and which can go on the 'not_orderable' list by considering everyone's likes and dislikes, following the criteria mentioned above. We will use a process of elimination based on the provided preferences:\n\n1. Determine which items are exclusively liked or disliked across all individuals to see if they can be easily assigned to 'orderable' or 'not_orderable' lists.\n2. Find items that conflict (liked by one person, but disliked by another) and try to resolve these conflicts without violating the satisfaction criteria.\n\nStarting with items that are exclusively liked or disliked:\n\n- Couscous is liked by Aarav, Zan, Ava, and Raj. It is only disliked by Bao. Since Bao also likes quiche, which is not disliked by anyone else, we could satisfy Bao by including quiche in the 'orderable' list and putting couscous in 'orderable' to satisfy the others.\n- Quiche is liked by Aarav, Bao, but is disliked by Min, Zan, Ben, Raj, and Oli. Since quiche has more people disliking it than liking it, and those who like it have other likes too, it can go into the 'not_orderable' list to satisfy the majority.\n- Muffins are liked by Zan and Oli, but disliked by Aarav, Min, Tara, and Mia. Since the dislikes outweigh the likes and those who like it have other likes, muffins can go into the 'not_orderable' list as well.\n- Shrimps are liked by Min and Mia, but disliked by Ava, Tara, Ben, and Oli. Dislikes outweigh the likes, and Min and Mia don't share any other likes, shrimps can not be on either list.\n- Salad is liked by Bao, Ava, Tara, Mia, and Raj, but disliked by Ben. Since Ben also dislikes shrimps and quiche (which is likely to be 'not_orderable'), we could possibly satisfy Ben with shrimps in the 'not_orderable' list. Hence, salad could be put in the 'orderable' list.\n\nNow, let's try to resolve any conflicts and finish composing our lists ensuring all conditions are met:\n\n- Shrimps should be 'not_orderable' to satisfy Ben, but that would leave Min and Mia unsatisfied because they don't have any other item they like, and it conflicts with the satisfaction criteria. Since we cannot satisfy all participants in this case, the task is impossible.\n\nGiven this, we arrive at the conclusion that there's no combination that satisfies all participants. Therefore, we output empty lists for both:\n\n```python\norderable = [], not_orderable = []\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 341, 'num_completion_tokens': 574}
