mmonly disliked into 'not_orderable'. However, we cannot simply do that since some individuals have these items in their 'like' list, creating a conflict. So, let's try to find a balance.\n\nLet's start by analyzing the items that are only liked by one person and disliked by at least one person,\n- pie is liked only by Kit and Shan, but disliked by Eli, Ping, Sol, Mia, and Veer.\n- salmon is liked by Eli, Zoe, Liz, but disliked by more people, making it a candidate for 'not_orderable'.\n- lobster is liked by Zoe, Anil, and Liz but is also disliked by many.\n\nGiven that salmon is disliked by the majority and liked by fewer people than lobster, we start by placing 'salmon' in 'not_orderable'. This decision immediately satisfies Shan, Ping, Anil, Hui, Gus, Mia, and Veer.\n\nFor 'wonton', this is liked by Eli, Anil, Hui, Mia, and Veer, but is disliked by several others too. Since 'pie' is only liked by Kit and Shan (who are 'okay' with salmon in 'not_orderable'), we can consider 'pie' as a candidate for 'orderable', to satisfy Kit and Shan, without making Eli, Ping, Sol, Mia, and Veer unsatisfied.\n\nLobster is liked by several and only disliked by Gus, Sol, and Hui. Since these individuals are already satisfied with 'salmon' in 'not_orderable', we can put 'lobster' in 'orderable'. So now Liz and Zoe are also satisfied.\n\nSo far, we have:\n\n- 'orderable' : lobster, pie\n- 'not_orderable': salmon\n\nNow we only need to satisfy Eli, who likes wonton and dislikes pie. Since pie is already in 'orderable', having wonton in 'orderable' will satisfy Eli. This will not conflict with anyone else's dislikes since all other individuals who disliked wonton are already satisfied.\n\nFinal lists:\n\n- 'orderable' : lobster, pie, wonton\n- 'not_orderable': salmon\n\nEveryone is satisfied with this arrangement:\n\n- Kit: Satisfied by pie (orderable)\n- Eli: Satisfied by wonton (orderable)\n- Shan: Satisfied by pie (orderable)\n- Zoe: Satisfied by lobster (orderable)\n- Ping: Satisfi