at is disliked (to put in the 'not_orderable' list). Moreover, an item can only be present in one of the lists.\n\nConsidering dislikes first, since we have:\n- Tao, Pia, and Oli dislike all three items, they do not influence the choice between 'orderable' and 'not_orderable', as any item in either list would satisfy them.\n- Several individuals dislike spaghetti and hummus, so potentially these items could go into the 'not_orderable' list.\n\nFor 'orderable':\n- If spaghetti were 'orderable', only Eva, Liz, Rob, Dan, Yan, and Xiu are satisfied, because it's the only food they like. For the rest, either spaghetti or hummus is disliked, and ratatouille is liked by some.\n- If hummus were 'orderable', Qi, Feng, Dot, Shan, Jan, Yan, and Mei are satisfied, because it's the only food they like. For the rest, either hummus or spaghetti is disliked, and ratatouille is liked by some.\n- Ratatouille seems to be the most liked item across the board.\n\nSince many people dislike spaghetti and hummus, let's try putting these in the 'not_orderable' list.\n\nThen, placing ratatouille in the 'orderable' list satisfies those who like it, and since spaghetti and hummus are in the 'not_orderable' list, that satisfies those who dislike those items.\n\nOrderable: [Ratatouille]\nNot_orderable: [Spaghetti, Hummus]\n\nSince all individuals like at least one item or dislike at least one of the items, this should satisfy the group.\n\nFinal lists are:\n```python\norderable=['ratatouille'], not_orderable=['spaghetti', 'hummus']\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 466, 'num_completion_tokens': 585}
