ve other food options they like, which can be \'orderable\' or disliked by others.\n- Lasagna is liked by many but also disliked by some. Bea, Nic, Naina, Eli, and Sia like lasagna but have no other likings or dislikes that match with any other food item, so it cannot be placed in either list.\n- Spaghetti is liked by several individuals, but it also has multiple dislikes. All individuals disliking spaghetti have other liked foods or other disliked ones, so spaghetti can be \'orderable\'.\n- Couscous is liked by many, and the only two people who dislike it (Tia and Rex) have other likes (tiramisu and shrimps), so couscous can be \'orderable\'.\n\nNow let\'s try to create the \'orderable\' and \'not_orderable\' lists while respecting these constraints. Since everyone who dislikes nachos has other likes, and no one who likes nachos has other options, we put nachos in \'not_orderable\'. Since everyone who likes gelato has no dislikes or has other likes, we can put gelato in \'orderable\'. Since the people who dislike tiramisu have other likes that are not disliked by someone, and the person who likes tiramisu and dislikes couscous (Tia) can be satisfied with tiramisu which everyone who dislikes can be satisfied with, we put tiramisu in \'orderable\'. Since everyone who dislikes couscous has other likes of things that some people has as dislikes, we put couscous in \'orderable\'. \n\nAll people that likes lasagna have no other likings, but there\'s no dislikes in common in the people that dislikes it, so cannot be added to the lists.\n\nFor the remaining food items (baklava, guacamole, shrimps), we have a more complex set of preferences where individuals have mixed feelings. We\'ll need to find an arrangement that satisfies the conditions for these items. Let\'s check them out:\n\n- No person who dislikes baklava only dislikes baklava, so we can put baklava in \'orderable\' and still satisfy everyone.\n- Guacamole is liked by a few, but all those who like it have other likes that aren\'t negated by a proportional dislike by someone else, so placing guacamole in \'not_orderable\' is feasible because it satisfies the criteria.\n- Everyone who dislikes shrimps has another "like" item (lasagna, baklava, guacamole, or spaghetti), so we can satisfy them by having those in \'orderable\'. And everyone who likes shrimps also likes another item except Arun, and since Arun dislikes tiramisu and Ru dislikes tiramisu but likes guacamole which can be in the \'not_orderable\', we can place also shrimps in \'orderable\'.\n\nBy fitting all preferences within these lists, we reach a point where we have:\n\n\'orderable\' list: [\'gelato\', \'tiramisu\', \'spaghetti\', \'couscous\', \'baklava\', \'shrimps\']\n\'not_orderable\' list: [\'nachos\', \'guacamole\']\n\nIt appears we have found an arrangement that satisfies the preferences. Let\'s formalize this solution in the requested Python list format:\n\norderable=[\'gelato\', \'tiramisu\', \'spaghetti\', \'couscous\', \'baklava\', \'shrimps\'], not_orderable=[\'nachos\', \'guacamole\']', 'num_prompt_tokens': 603, 'num_completion_tokens': 1378}
