acos. Dislikes churros, cake.\n5. Gen: Likes sorbet, burger. Dislikes churros.\n6. Val: Dislikes guacamole, burger, cake.\n7. Bo: Likes tacos. Dislikes burger, salad.\n8. Joy: Likes tiramisu. Dislikes custard, salad.\n9. Kit: Likes custard, churros. Dislikes kebab.\n10. Anil: Likes cake. Dislikes churros, guacamole.\n11. Wei: Likes cake. Dislikes burger, kebab.\n12. Jan: Dislikes kebab, tacos, cake.\n13. Chao: Likes sorbet. Dislikes burger, salad.\n14. Ana: Likes tacos. Dislikes churros, tiramisu.\n15. Ansh: Likes guacamole. Dislikes burger, tiramisu.\n16. Ru: Likes tacos, tiramisu. Dislikes sorbet.\n17. Kim: Likes custard, guacamole. Dislikes sorbet.\n18. Ada: Likes salad. Dislikes sorbet, guacamole.\n19. Zoe: Likes sorbet, churros, kebab.\n20. Sky: Likes guacamole. Dislikes burger, sorbet.\n\nFrom this list, we can find no single item that is liked by some and disliked by others, which would prevent it from being put into either list, thus presenting no direct conflicts. However, we will need to find items that can satisfy everyone by being present in \'orderable\' or in \'not_orderable\'.\n\nTo satisfy everyone, we can attempt to arrange the items as follows:\n\n- "sorbet" is liked by many and only Ru and Kim dislike it. If we put "sorbet" in \'not_orderable\', Ru and Kim will be satisfied but we need to ensure all the others who like it have another item they like in \'orderable\'.\n- "churros" is also problematic because while Yan, Kit, and Zoe like it, Ping, Gen, Ana, and Anil dislike it. To satisfy this, "churros" can go into \'not_orderable\'.\n- "burger" is disliked by many, only Gen and Zoe like it. Since Gen likes "sorbet" and we put "sorbet" in \'not_orderable\', we must put "burger" in \'orderable\' to satisfy Gen. However, "burger" can\'t be \'orderable\' because too many participants dislike it. Hence, "burger" has to go into \'not_orderable\', and Gen can be satisfied by another item.\n- "tacos" are liked by several people and only Xiu and Jan dislike them. Therefore, "tacos" can go into \'orderable\'.\n- "tiramisu" is liked by some and hated by others, and since we have Joy and Ru who like "tiramisu" and no other items from their likes list are in \'orderable\' yet, we can put "tiramisu" in \'orderable\'.\n- "guacamole" is liked by several and disliked by fewer; it can be included in \'orderable\'.\n- "salad" is liked by Ada but disliked by Bo and Chao. Since Ada dislikes "sorbet" and "guacamole" which we considered for \'orderable\', "salad" can be placed in \'orderable\'.\n- "cake" is liked by some and disliked by others, but we have Wei who likes "cake" and its not in \'orderable\' yet. It must go into \'orderable\'.\n- "custard" is liked by a few and disliked by Yan and Joy. It can fit into \'orderable\' to satisfy those who like it, like Kit and Kim.\n\nNow let\'s assemble the lists based on the above reasoning:\n\n\'orderable\' items: \n- tacos: Satisfying Xiu, Ping, Bo, Ana, and Ru.\n- tiramisu: Satisfying Hal and Joy.\n- guacamole: Satisfying Ansh and Sky.\n- salad: Satisfying Ada.\n- cake: Satisfying Anil and Wei.\n- custard: Satisfying Kit and Kim.\n\n\'not_orderable\' items:\n- sorbet: Satisfying Xiu, Gen, Chao, and others who like it.\n- churros: Satisfying Ping, Gen, Ana, and Anil.\n- burger: Satisfying Val, Bo, Chao, Ansh, Sky, and others who dislike it.\n\nWe must check that everyone who likes an item that got placed into \'not_orderable\', has another item they like in \'orderable\', and vice versa.\n\nAfter reviewing the list, it seems everyone can be satisfied:\n\n- Xiu (likes "kebab") and Kim (likes "custard"), both dislike "tacos" and they have their liked items in the \'orderable\' list.\n- Hal and Yan (like "churros", "tiramisu", "churros") are satisfied with "tiramisu" and "cake" or "custard" (for Yan) being \'orderable\'.\n- Gen and Chao (like "sorbet") are satisfied with "burger" being \'not_orderable\'.\n- Val, Bo, Ansh, Sky (dislike "burger") have "guacamole" and "salad" in the \'orderable\' list.\n- Joy (likes "tiramisu") has "tiramisu" in the \'orderable\' list.\n- Kit (likes "custard" and "churros") has "custard" in the \'orderable\' list.\n- Jan (dislikes "tacos", "kebab", "cake") has "kebab" in the \'not_orderable\' list and "cake" in \'orderable\'.\n- Ada (likes "salad") has "salad" in the \'orderable\' list.\n- Zoe (likes "sorbet", "churros", "kebab") has "kebab" in the \'not_orderable\' list, so she requires another item to be satisfied. Since she likes "kebab" and it can\'t be placed in either \'orderable\' or \'not_orderable\' due to Jan\'s dislike), she can\'t be satisfied by any combination.\n\nIt looks like Zoe is not satisfied by any combination, thus we have to declare both lists as empty, since we can\'t fulfill everyone\'s satisfaction criteria.\n\nThe solution is:\n\n```python\norderable=[], not_orderable=[]\n```', 'num_prompt_tokens': 469, 'num_completion_tokens': 1514}
