. If this is not possible, then we need to ensure that for every person, an item they like is in 'orderable' or an item they dislike is in 'not_orderable').\n\nLet's try a different approach since a universally liked item or a universally non-disliked item is not evident.\n\nStep 2: Start by placing the most disliked items in the 'not_orderable' list ensuring at least some satisfaction:\n- 'udon' and 'bruschetta' are common dislikes, so let's consider them for 'not_orderable'.\n\nStep 3: Check satisfaction for each individual if 'udon' and 'bruschetta' are 'not_orderable':\n- Everyone who dislikes 'udon' or 'bruschetta' is at least partially satisfied by these decisions.\n\nStep 4: Identify food items that are liked by those who are not yet satisfied by 'not_orderable' list:\n- 'pizza' is liked by many, and the individuals who dislike 'pizza' (Rob, Sue, Tim, Isha, and Bea) are already satisfied by 'bruschetta' being unavailable.\n- By placing 'pizza' in 'orderable', many of the remaining unsatisfied individuals will be satisfied.\n\nIf we place 'udon' and 'bruschetta' in 'not_orderable' and 'pizza' in 'orderable', we have the following:\n\n'orderable': ['pizza']\n'not_orderable': ['udon', 'bruschetta']\n\nStep 5: Check if all individuals are satisfied with this initial list:\n- Some individuals are not satisfied by 'pizza', 'udon', and 'bruschetta' alone, so we need an additional food item for them.\n\nStep 6: Find additional items for 'orderable' to satisfy those still unsatisfied without causing conflicts:\n- Individuals not satisfied by 'pizza', 'udon', or 'bruschetta' (excluding individuals already satisfied by dislikes) must have at least one of their likes in 'orderable'. For example, Neil dislikes 'bruschetta' and 'udon', so he's already satisfied. Ula likes 'soup' and 'biscuits'. Since 'biscuits' is disliked by some, 'soup' is a better candidate for 'orderable'. We add 'soup' to 'orderable'.\n\nStep 7: Final Check:\n- Now, check satisfaction for individuals 