ither list could lead to satisfaction for a large group while dissatisfying another large group. Therefore, we\'ll leave this item for further consideration once we\'ve made decisions about other food items.\n\n"Paneer" and "gelato" also face a similar kind of conflict as "cheesecake," with multiple likes and dislikes, so we will also set these aside for now.\n\n"Macaron" and "ratatouille" are both liked and disliked by a few, but not as contentious as "cheesecake," "paneer," or "gelato." We are still unsure where to place them due to the presence of both likes and dislikes.\n\n"Soup" is disliked by many but liked by fewer. However, it has to be orderable for those who like it to be satisfied since no one else dislikes it. We must look for a workaround.\n\n"Brownies" are more liked than disliked, but we still need to determine their placement based on satisfaction criteria.\n\n"Tiramisu" is quite liked. Its placement will also need careful consideration to satisfy the maximum number of participants.\n\nLet\'s identify non-contentious items to potentially place on the lists first:\n\n- Items that must be \'orderable\' to satisfy individuals because they are only liked and not disliked by others: None.\n- Items that must be \'not orderable\' because they are disliked but not liked: "tacos."\n\nNow, let\'s start creating potential lists and look at items where we can make educated decisions:\n\n- "Cheesecake," "gelato," "paneer," "macaron," and "ratatouille" are highly contentious and will need more consideration.\n- "Soup" must be on the \'orderable\' list to satisfy those who like it (like Ana, Lin, Zoe, Jay, Wei).\n- "Brownies" and "tiramisu" are liked by many, so it might be beneficial to place them in the \'orderable\' list.\n\nHowever, as we review the potential lists, it becomes clear that no combination will satisfy all individuals because of the conflicting likes and dislikes. It is impossible to put any item in \'orderable\' without dissatisfying someone who 