Data Sample: {'num_vars': 5, 'num_clauses': 10, 'formula': [[2, -1, 3], [4, 1, -5], [-1, -3, 4], [3, -1, 2], [1, -3, -4], [5, -4, -1], [2, -1, 4], [-2, -3, 1], [4, -3, 5], [5, -4, 1]], 'is_sat': True, 'preferences': 'Hal: Likes soup, burrito. Dislikes wonton. Ava: Likes vegetables, wonton. Dislikes spaghetti. Lei: Likes vegetables. Dislikes wonton, burrito. Xan: Likes burrito, soup. Dislikes wonton. Shan: Likes wonton. Dislikes burrito, vegetables. Pam: Likes spaghetti. Dislikes vegetables, wonton. Wei: Likes soup, vegetables. Dislikes wonton. Taj: Likes wonton. Dislikes soup, burrito. Val: Likes vegetables, spaghetti. Dislikes burrito. Ying: Likes spaghetti, wonton. Dislikes vegetables. ', 'menu_items': ['wonton', 'soup', 'burrito', 'vegetables', 'spaghetti'], 'gpt_out': 'Let\'s list out the likes and dislikes of each individual first:\n\nHal:\nLikes: soup, burrito\nDislikes: wonton\n\nAva:\nLikes: vegetables, wonton\nDislikes: spaghetti\n\nLei:\nLikes: vegetables\nDislikes: wonton, burrito\n\nXan:\nLikes: burrito, soup\nDislikes: wonton\n\nShan:\nLikes: wonton\nDislikes: burrito, vegetables\n\nPam:\nLikes: spaghetti\nDislikes: vegetables, wonton\n\nWei:\nLikes: soup, vegetables\nDislikes: wonton\n\nTaj:\nLikes: wonton\nDislikes: soup, burrito\n\nVal:\nLikes: vegetables, spaghetti\nDislikes: burrito\n\nYing:\nLikes: spaghetti, wonton\nDislikes: vegetables\n\nWe need to find at least one item from the \'likes\' list of every person to put into the \'orderable\' list or one item from their \'dislikes\' list to put into \'not_orderable\' list to satisfy everyone\'s preferences.\n\nHere\'s how we can start:\n\n1. We can see that "wonton" is liked by several people (Ava, Shan, Taj, Ying) but also disliked by several (Hal, Lei, Xan, Pam, Wei). Since it is disliked by many, "wonton" cannot be included in \'orderable\' and must be in \'not_orderable\'. This automatically satisfies Hal, Lei, Xan, Pam, and Wei as they are satisfied to see "wonton" in \'not_orderable\'.\n\n2. Because "burrito" is liked by several people (Hal, Xan) but disliked by several (Lei, Shan, Taj, Val), it similarly should be placed in \'not_orderable\' to meet the criteria. This satisfies Lei, Shan, Taj, and Val.\n\n3. "Vegetables" is liked by many (Ava, Lei, Wei, Val), and only Shan and Pam dislike it. If we put "vegetables" in \'orderable\', we would need to include "spaghetti" in \'orderable\' to satisfy Pam and Shan (since they can\'t be satisfied by "vegetables"). Additionally, "soup" could go into \'orderable\' to satisfy Hal, Xan, and Wei.\n\n4. Now, let\'s consider "spaghetti", liked by Ava, Pam, Val, and Ying but disliked by Ava. Ava would be satisfied with "wonton" in \'not_orderable\', so we can place "spaghetti" in \'orderable\' to satisfy Pam, Val, and Ying.\n\nLet\'s see what the lists look like now:\n\n\'orderable\': [vegetables, soup, spaghetti]\n\'not_orderable\': [wonton, burrito]\n\nWith these lists, we can check that each person is satisfied based on the satisfaction criteria:\n- Hal: Satisfied (likes "soup" which is in \'orderable\')\n- Ava: Satisfied ("wonton" is in \'not_orderable\')\n- Lei: Satisfied ("burrito" and "wonton" are in \'not_orderable\')\n- Xan: Satisfied (likes "soup" which is in \'orderable\')\n- Shan: Satisfied ("spaghetti" is in \'orderable\')\n- Pam: Satisfied (likes "spaghetti" which is in \'orderable\')\n- Wei: Satisfied (likes both "soup" and "vegetables" which are in \'orderable\')\n- Taj: Satisfied ("burrito" is in \'not_orderable\')\n- Val: Satisfied (likes "vegetables" and "spaghetti" which are in \'orderable\')\n- Ying: Satisfied (likes "spaghetti" which is in \'orderable\')\n\nEveryone is satisfied with these lists. Thus, the final output should be:\n\n`orderable=[\'vegetables\', \'soup\', \'spaghetti\'], not_orderable=[\'wonton\', \'burrito\']`', 'num_prompt_tokens': 318, 'num_completion_tokens': 824}
