the most inclusive orderable and not_orderable lists.\n\nHere's the criteria to proceed with:\n- To satisfy a person, we must have at least one liked item in the orderable list or one disliked item in the not_orderable list.\n- No item can appear in both lists.\n- All participants must be satisfied.\n\nFirstly, let's identify the foods that are commonly liked and commonly disliked by examining the given preferences:\n\nCommon likes (appear in likes more than in dislikes):\n- Burrito (liked by many, disliked by a few)\n- Ravioli (liked by many, disliked by a few)\n- Kimchi (liked by many, disliked by many)\n\nCommon dislikes (appear in dislikes more than in likes):\n- Caramel (disliked by a few)\n- Naan (disliked by many, liked by a few)\n- Burger (liked and disliked by roughly equal numbers)\n\nFrom this observation alone, it's not immediately clear which foods should be on which list because we have some conflicts for items like kimchi, naan, and burger, which are both liked and disliked by many.\n\nGiven the criteria, we should start by trying to satisfy those with the most restrictive diets (those who dislike many things), as including something they dislike in the not_orderable list will ensure their satisfaction and may create more flexibility elsewhere.\n\nLet's dissect the requirements for Ansh, who dislikes burrito, ravioli, and naan, to cater to the most dislikes.\n\nSince Ansh dislikes three popular items, placing any of these in the not_orderable list can help satisfy many others.\n\nPlacing burrito in the not_orderable list would be problematic since it's liked by many and only disliked by Max and a few others. The same goes for ravioli, which is very popular but only disliked by a few. Naan is a less problematic choice as it is only liked by fewer individuals (Liz, Jan, Rohan, Chao). To reduce the complexity, let's add naan to the not_orderable list.\n\nBy choosing naan for the not_orderable list, we've satisfied Ansh. Now we need to make sure that Liz,