slikes kimchi, we can put burger in the orderable to keep them satisfied.\n\nNow, let's check if it's okay to put burger in the orderable list. We need to make sure nobody who dislikes burger is left unsatisfied:\n\n- Sol (dislikes burger) likes burrito. Placing burrito in the orderable can satisfy Sol.\n- Jan (dislikes burger) is already satisfied by naan being not_orderable.\n- Min (dislikes burger) likes kimchi. Placing kimchi in the orderable can satisfy Min.\n- Dot (dislikes kimchi) is satisfied by burger being in the orderable list.\n\nWith burger in the orderable list, anyone who dislikes burger but likes another item that's in the orderable or dislikes an item that's in the not_orderable list is satisfied.\n\nPia and Ula, who dislike ravioli but like kimchi, will be satisfied by kimchi being in the orderable list.\n\nBy placing ravioli and burger in the orderable list and naan in the not_orderable list, everyone's preferences should be catered to, provided no other conflicts exist. Checking through the list, it seems everyone can be satisfied in this way.\n\nThus, our final solution will be:\n\n```python\norderable=['ravioli', 'burger'], not_orderable=['naan']\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 608, 'num_completion_tokens': 1232}
