ue, Val). If we add `vindaloo` to the `not_orderable` list, we satisfy these people without contradiction, as no one has it as their only like.\n2. Ratatouille is also widely disliked (Jim, Tim, Mel, Yan, Shan, Fay, Zac, Li, Ken, Cy, Meg, Joe, Pia, Ray, Dan, Hal, Ula). If we include `ratatouille` in the `not_orderable` list, we satisfy these individuals.\n\nWe have found two items that can go to the `not_orderable` list without conflicts.\n\nNow, let's see if there are any items that are liked by some but not disliked by anyone. It seems there is no such item because every liked item is also someone's dislike.\n\nGiven the complexity of preferences, a conflict-free universal satisfaction seems challenging.\n\nLet's try to find at least one item for the `orderable` list that satisfies the largest possible group.\n\n1. Muffins are liked by many (Amy, Chao, Jay, Jim, Min, Jay, Mel, May, Wei, Tom, Liz, Mei, Kim, Nic, Jun, Bo, Oli, Zoe), but disliked by some (Mel, Gen, Mina, Li, Diya). To satisfy the maximum number of people, we could include `muffins` in the `orderable` list.\n2. Now, check all the individuals who dislike `muffins` (Mel, Gen, Mina, Li) for their other dislikes to see if those can go into the `not_orderable` list (poke by Mel, ravioli by Gen, biscuits by Mina, vindaloo by Li), but we find that these items are liked by others.\n\nAfter the iteration, we find:\n\n- `vindaloo` and `ratatouille` can be placed in the `not_orderable` list without causing any direct conflict.\n- `muffins` can be in the `orderable` list, satisfying a large group, but some individuals disliking `muffins` have other items they dislike that wouldn't work in the `not_orderable` list because those items are liked by others.\n\nThus, we realize that there is no combination of `orderable` and `not_orderable` food items that will satisfy all individuals based on the criteria provided. We have to output empty lists for both `orderable` and `not_orderable`:\n\n```python\norderable = []\nn