liked by Vic, brownies should be on the \'orderable\' list to satisfy the large majority. This decision will not satisfy Vic, but we will address this later.\n\n2. Gelato is disliked by Nic, Vic, and Tia, but liked by Ru and Xiu. Since more people dislike it, we will put gelato on the \'not_orderable\' list to satisfy those three.\n\n3. Vic now needs an item to be satisfied (since brownies will not satisfy Vic and gelato is already on the \'not_orderable\' list). Since Vic also dislikes shrimps and no one else dislikes them, we cannot immediately decide their placement.\n\n4. Risotto is liked by Nic and Ru, but disliked by Leo, Lee, and Dan. Given that brownies satisfy Nic and Ru, and more people dislike risotto, it should be considered for the \'not_orderable\' list.\n\n5. Shrimps are liked by Rob, Xiu, Shan, and Wei, and disliked by Vic, Leo, Shan, and Dan. They cannot be on the \'not_orderable\' list since that would dissatisfy Rob, Xiu, and Wei. So, we will have to satisfy Vic with another disliked item.\n\n6. Spaghetti is liked by Rob, Lee, Lou, Wei, and Diya, but disliked by Shan and Dan. We cannot put it on the \'not_orderable\' list due to its popularity, and similarly, it cannot be on the \'orderable\' list as Shan dislikes them along with shrimps.\n\n7. Burger is liked by Shan, Ron, and Lou, but disliked by Rob, Shan, and Tia. Shan and Lou would be satisfied by burger since spaghetti and shrimps will not be on the \'not_orderable\' list, and Tan could be satisfied by not having gelato.\n\n8. Burrito is liked by Ron but disliked by Diya and Ru. Since Ron can be satisfied with brownies, and more people dislike burrito, we can put burrito on the \'not_orderable\' list.\n\nWith this analysis, we attempt to satisfy all parties with the following lists:\n\n- Orderable = {brownies, burger}\n- Not_orderable = {gelato, risotto, burrito}\n\nWe then double-check that everyone\'s preferences are met with these lists:\n\n- Nic is satisfied by brownies (likes) being ord