m can appear on both lists. Therefore, in cases where an item is liked and disliked to satisfy everyone, it needs to be present on the 'orderable' list for those who like it and absent from the 'not_orderable' list for those who dislike it (being absent satisfactorily means it is implicitly not ordered).\n\nPaneer is the most disliked item, disliked by 7 people and liked by 4. By default, in order to satisfy the maximum number of people, let's try putting paneer in the 'not_orderable' list. This means the 4 who like paneer will need another food item that they like in the 'orderable' list.\n\nTom dislikes paneer and sorbet, and since paneer is already in the 'not_orderable' list, Tom is satisfied.\n\nKai likes burrito and churros and dislikes sorbet. To satisfy Kai, either burrito or churros should be in the 'orderable' list. Let's choose burrito since churros is less liked.\n\nBen likes tandoori and dislikes burrito and paneer. With paneer in the 'not_orderable' list, we should put tandoori in the 'orderable' list to satisfy Ben.\n\nSince tandoori is in the 'orderable' list, Vic is satisfied but Riya is not. To satisfy Riya, burrito cannot be in the 'orderable' list, contradicting the prior decision to put it there for Kai. Since we cannot satisfy everyone with this contradiction, let's try putting tandoori in the 'not_orderable' list to satisfy Riya and look for other items to satisfy Vic.\n\nFeng likes caramel and dislikes churros and sorbet. If caramel is in the 'orderable' list, Feng will be satisfied.\n\nWith these considerations, we should first place tandoori in the 'not_orderable' list to ensure Riya is satisfied. Now let's identify a second like for Vic and others who like tandoori. They variously like caramel, sorbet, paneer, and vindaloo, but sorbet and paneer have already been eliminated due to dislikes. Vindaloo is also highly disliked. Caramel is liked by a few and Qi dislikes caramel, but since caramel is not already on any list, we can make Qi satisfied by not including poke. Therefore, we can provisionally add caramel to the 'orderable' list.\n\nNow let's review what we have so far and check if everyone is satisfied:\n\n1. Tom dislikes paneer and sorbet. With paneer not on the 'orderable' list, Tom is satisfied.\n2. Ben can no longer have tandoori, but no dislikes are on the 'orderable' list. Ben is tentatively unsatisfied.\n3. Sia likes poke and dislikes kebab and paneer. With paneer not on the 'orderable' list, Sia is satisfied.\n4. Jan dislikes vindaloo, sorbet, and paneer. With paneer not on the 'orderable' list, Jan is satisfied.\n5. Feng likes caramel, which is on the 'orderable' list; therefore, Feng is satisfied.\n6. Vic likes tandoori and paneer but dislikes churros. Tandoori is not orderable, so we'd have to satisfy Vic with a different liked item or by not orderable a disliked one, which we haven't satisfied yet. Vic is tentatively unsatisfied.\n7. Ansh likes paneer and vindaloo but dislikes burrito. With paneer not on the 'orderable' list, we would need to include vindaloo in the 'orderable' list or not include burrito. This requires further examination.\n8. Wei likes caramel, which is on the 'orderable' list, so Wei is satisfied.\n9. Riya likes burrito and dislikes tandoori and poke. With tandoori not on the 'orderable' list, Riya could be satisfied if burrito is orderable. This requires further examination.\n10. Jun likes caramel and dislikes paneer and kebab. Both dislikes are not on the 'orderable' list and caramel is orderable, so Jun is satisfied.\n11. Kai likes burrito and churros and dislikes sorbet. With sorbet not on the 'orderable' list, Kai could be satisfied if burrito or churros is orderable. This requires further examination.\n12. Pam likes tandoori and sorbet but dislikes caramel. With tandoori not on the 'orderable' list and caramel orderable, Pam is unsatisfied, but her liking for sorbet can no longer be satisfied with any choice, as it is highly disliked.\n13. Jim likes paneer and dislikes vindaloo and burrito. With paneer not on the 'orderable' list, Jim could be satisfied if vindaloo is not orderable, but burrito is still in question.\n14. Ru likes vindaloo and kebab and dislikes sorbet. With sorbet not on the 'orderable' list, Ru is tentatively unsatisfied since neither liked item is orderable.\n15. Mina likes tandoori but dislikes paneer and vindaloo. With tandoori not on the 'orderable' list and paneer not orderable, Mina is unsatisfied, but her preference is contradicted by dislikes for paneer and vindaloo.\n16. Lou likes vindaloo but dislikes churros and tandoori. With tandoori not on the 'orderable' list, Lou is tentatively unsatisfied.\n17. Qi likes tandoori and poke but dislikes caramel. With tandoori not on the 'orderable' list and caramel in question due to likes by others, Qi is unsatisfied.\n18. Lee likes caramel and paneer and dislikes sorbet. With sorbet not on the 'orderable' list and caramel orderable, Lee is satisfied.\n\nThe complications arise with caramel, tandoori, and burrito. Caramel was chosen to satisfy several individuals, but it also contributes to the dissatisfaction of others. Tandoori, frequently liked, was initially excluded, leading to potential dissatisfaction.\n\nTo resolve these issues, let's cycle through the conflicted items and try to satisfy the most people without unsatisfying others who have already been satisfied by the current choices:\n\n1. Ben, Vic, and Mina, who like tandoori, could potentially be satisfied by caramel, as long as caramel does not unsatisfy anyone else.\n2. To satisfy Riya, burrito should be orderable, but it is disliked by Ben, Ansh, and Jim.\n3. Ru and Lou, who both like vindaloo, cannot be satisfied because it is disliked by Jan, Jim, and Mina.\n4. Kai would be satisfied by ordering churros, which is only disliked by Feng, Vic, and Lou.\n5. Ansh could also be satisfied if vindaloo is orderable, which contradicts the dislikes of Jim and Mina.\n\nThe mutual exclusivity of these preferences makes it impossible to find an arrangement that satisfies everyone due to the overlapping likes and dislikes.\n\nHowever, an arrangement might yet be possible. Since tandoori is the second most liked item after caramel, and since we put it out of order to satisfy Riya, let's review the situation without considering Riya's dislikes as constraints and try including tandoori again. Since Pam's preferences cannot be met (sorbet and tandoori conflict with multiple others), we will not consider Pam's satisfaction in the following arrangement:\n\n- Putting tandoori back in the 'orderable' list will satisfy Ben, Vic, Mina, Pam, and Qi.\n- We should then move caramel to the 'not_orderable' list to satisfy those who dislike it (Pam and Qi), meaning we need to provide another like for the caramel lovers.\n- Since Riya, Ben, and Ansh dislike burrito, it should go to the 'not_orderable' list, satisfying Ansh; however, this move unsatisfies Riya and Kai. Since Ansh can be satisfied by vindaloo, we can return burrito to the 'orderable' list, satisfy Riya and Kai, and put vindaloo on the 'not_orderable' list to satisfy Ansh.\n- We will add poke to the 'orderable' list to satisfy Sia and Qi since their only other likes are already on the respective 'not_orderable' and 'orderable' lists.\n- Caramel lovers (Feng, Wei, Jun, Lee) are unsatisfied. Wei and Jun have no more likes to satisfy, Feng also likes kebab, and Lee also likes paneer. Putting kebab on the 'orderable' list will satisfy Feng, but not Jun, Wei, or Lee.\n- Lou and Lou, who like vindaloo, would be unsatisfied by its absence, and we can't find another choice to satisfy them without conflict.\n\nWhile we've been able to solve some issues, the remaining contradiction now includes Lee, Kai, who cannot be simultaneously satisfied because Lee's dislike for sorbet and likings of caramel and paneer is entirely conflicting with the rest of the group.\n\nUltimately, it appears impossible to satisfy every participant 