 Lee who like it, but Ula is also satisfied by falafel being orderable.\n\n**Step 4:** Check for any individual who is not satisfied by falafel being orderable or burger being not_orderable. Here, we find that Ula, Lee, Ty, Lan, Dot, Qi, Ray, Sia, Xiu, Gia, Max, and Zan like burger but don't have other likes or dislikes covered. Given that Ula dislikes kimchi, this choice cannot satisfy Ula. \n\nSince we have now reached a conflict where no choice of 'orderable' items can simultaneously satisfy all individuals (specifically Ula), we must declare that no satisfying combination exists.\n\nTherefore, the solution is:\n\n```python\norderable = [], not_orderable = []\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 1209, 'num_completion_tokens': 552}
Data Sample: {'num_vars': 9, 'num_clauses': 18, 'formula': [[-5, 2, -4], [-3, -7, -1], [-8, 9, 5], [4, 2, 5], [-5, -1, 6], [3, -2, 1], [8, 4, -7], [1, 5, 7], [5, -1, 7], [-5, -8, 7], [-9, 2, -8], [1, -6, -4], [3, 7, 8], [2, -6, 1], [1, -7, -8], [-5, -1, 3], [3, -8, 9], [3, -1, 2]], 'is_sat': True, 'preferences': 'Meg: Likes toast. Dislikes lasagna, risotto. Amy: Dislikes soup, kimchi, doughnut. Ana: Likes wonton, lasagna. Dislikes cheese. Abe: Likes risotto, toast, lasagna. Ivy: Likes nachos. Dislikes lasagna, doughnut. Rohan: Likes soup, doughnut. Dislikes toast. Ray: Likes cheese, risotto. Dislikes kimchi. Lei: Likes doughnut, lasagna, kimchi. Nita: Likes lasagna, kimchi. Dislikes doughnut. Eli: Likes kimchi. Dislikes lasagna, cheese. Li: Likes toast. Dislikes wonton, cheese. Rob: Likes doughnut. Dislikes nachos, risotto. Ansh: Likes soup, kimchi, cheese. Mei: Likes toast, doughnut. Dislikes nachos. Tom: Likes doughnut. Dislikes kimchi, cheese. Hui: Likes soup. Dislikes lasagna, doughnut. Cy: Likes soup, wonton. Dislikes cheese. Jim: Likes soup, toast. Dislikes doughnut. ', 'menu_items': ['doughnut', 'toast', 'soup', 'risotto', 'lasagna', 'nachos', 'kimchi', 'cheese', 'wonton'], 'gpt_out': "To solve this, we can approach it methodically by first listing all the likes and dislikes and then attempting to create a list based on the criteria.\n\nFrom the preferences given, we can summarize the likes and dislikes as follows:\n\nLikes:\n\n- toast: Meg, Abe, Li, Mei, Jim\n- wonton: Ana, Cy\n- lasagna: Ana, Abe, Lei, Nita\n- risotto: Abe, Ray\n- nachos: Ivy\n- soup: Rohan, Ansh, Hui, Jim, Cy\n- doughnut: Rohan, Lei, Rob, Tom, Mei\n- kimchi: Lei, Eli, Nita, Ansh\n- cheese: Ray, Ansh\n- kimchi: Eli\n\nDislikes:\n\n- lasagna: Meg, Ivy, Eli, Hui\n- risotto: Meg\n- soup: Amy\n- kimchi: Amy, Ray\n- doughnut: Amy, Ivy, Nita, Jim\n- cheese: Ana, Eli, Tom, Cy\n- wonton: Li\n- toast: Rohan\n- nachos: Rob, Mei\n\nNow, we need to create two lists: 'orderable' and 'not_orderable', with the former including items liked and the latter items disliked, with the condition that each person is satisfied.\n\nLet's create 'orderable' list with items that are liked by those who have exclusive likes:\n- toast is liked by several, but disliked by Rohan, so we cannot include it immediately.\n- wonton is liked by Ana and Cy, with only Li disliking it; we can include it in 'orderable' unless a contradiction arises later.\n- nachos is liked only by Ivy and disliked by Rob and Mei; we cannot include it immediately.\n- soup is liked by many, but disliked by Amy; we cannot include it immediately.\n\nLet's include 'wonton' in the 'orderable' list for now, as it seems to have the least conflict.\n\nNow, let's create 'not_orderable' list with items that are disliked by those who have exclusive dislikes:\n- lasagna is disliked by many, and only liked by a few, we can safely put it in 'not_orderable'.\n- risotto is liked by a few, but Meg is the only one who dislikes it; we cannot include it immediately.\n- cheese is the best candidate for 'not_orderable' because it's disliked by several and only liked by Ray and Ansh. If Ray and Ansh have other foods they like that can be included in the 'orderable' list without conflict, cheese can go into 'not_orderable'.\n\nNow, let's include 'cheese' and 'lasagna' in 'not_orderable'. We need to satisfy Ray and Ansh now since we put one of their liked foods into 'not_orderable'.\n\n- Ray likes risotto, which only Meg dislikes, so we can include 'risotto' in 'orderable' to satisfy Ray and we ensure Meg is satisfied because lasagna which she dislikes is already in 'not_orderable'.\n- Ansh also likes soup and kimchi. Since soup is disliked by Amy and Kimchi is disliked by Amy and Ray, and we have already satisfied Ray with risotto, we can now add 'soup' into 'orderable'.\n\nOur situation is now:\norderable: ['wonton', 'risotto', 'soup']\nnot_orderable: ['lasagna', 'cheese']\n\nEach person on the list must now be satisfied based on one of these two lists. Going through each person:\n\n- Meg: satisfied (dislikes 'lasagna', which is in 'not_orderable')\n- Amy: satisfied (dislikes 'lasagna', which is in 'not_orderable')\n- Ana: satisfied ('wonton' in 'orderable')\n- Abe: satisfied ('risotto' in 'orderable')\n- Ivy: satisfied (dislikes 'lasagna', which is in 'not_orderable')\n- Rohan: satisfied ('soup' in 'orderable')\n- Ray: satisfied ('risotto' in 'orderable')\n- Lei: satisfied ('soup' in 'orderable')\n- Nita: satisfied (dislikes 'lasagna', which is in 'not_orderable')\n- Eli: satisfied (dislikes 'lasagna', which is in 'not_orderable')\n- Li: satisfied (dislikes 'lasagna', which is in 'not_orderable')\n- Rob: satisfied (dislikes 'lasagna', which is in 'not_orderable')\n- Ansh: satisfied ('soup' in 'orderable')\n- Mei: satisfied (dislikes 'lasagna', which is in 'not_orderable')\n- Tom: satisfied (dislikes 'lasagna', which is in 'not_orderable')\n- Hui: satisfied (dislikes 'lasagna', which is in 'not_orderable')\n- Cy: satisfied ('wonton' in 'orderable')\n- Jim: satisfied (dislikes 'lasagna', which is in 'not_orderable')\n\nIt looks like we found a solution that works for everyone's preferences. Each person has at least one liked item on the 'orderable' list or one disliked item on the 'not_orderable' list. Hence, the final lists are:\n\norderable = ['wonton', 'risotto', 'soup']\nnot_orderable = ['lasagna', 'cheese']", 'num_prompt_tokens': 422, 'num_completion_tokens': 1117}
Data Sample: {'num_vars': 7, 'num_clauses': 14, 'formula': [[3, 1, -5], [-7, -3, -5], [4, 6, -3], [4, 1, -7], [4, -7, 1], [7, 3, -1], [3, -4, 6], [-6, -1, -4], [3, -4, -1], [-1, -3, -4], [6, -3, 2], [6, 1, 7], [-1, -7, -3], [-4, -1, 2]], 'is_sat': True, 'preferences': 'Sia: Likes wonton, cheesecake. Dislikes brownies. Liv: Dislikes shawarma, wonton, brownies. Ru: Likes mousse, vegetables. Dislikes wonton. Mia: Likes mousse, cheesecake. Dislikes shawarma. Xiu: Likes mousse, cheesecake. Dislikes shawarma. Jun: Likes shawarma, wonton. Dislikes cheesecake. Kavi: Likes wonton, vegetables. Dislikes mousse. Feng: Dislikes vegetables, cheesecake, mousse. Eli: Likes wonton. Dislikes mousse, cheesecake. Jan: Dislikes cheesecake, wonton, mousse. Min: Likes vegetables, curry. Dislikes wonton. Gia: Likes vegetables, cheesecake, shawarma. Ping: Dislikes cheesecake, shawarma, wonton. Oli: Likes curry. Dislikes mousse, cheesecake. ', 'menu_items': ['cheesecake', 'curry', 'wonton', 'mousse', 'brownies', 'vegetables', 'shawarma'], 'gpt_out': 'We first start by listing each person\'s likes and dislikes:\n\nSia: Likes wonton, cheesecake. Dislikes brownies.\nLiv: Dislikes shawarma, wonton, brownies.\nRu: Likes mousse, vegetables. Dislikes wonton.\nMia: Likes mousse, cheesecake. Dislikes shawarma.\nXiu: Likes mousse, cheesecake. Dislikes shawarma.\nJun: Likes shawarma, wonton. Dislikes cheesecake.\nKavi: Likes wonton, vegetables. Dislikes mousse.\nFeng: Dislikes vegetables, cheesecake, mousse.\nEli: Likes wonton. Dislikes mousse, cheesecake.\nJan: Dislikes cheesecake, wonton, mousse.\nMin: Likes vegetables, curry. Dislikes wonton.\nGia: Likes vegetables, cheesecake, shawarma.\nPing: Dislikes cheesecake, shawarma, wonton.\nOli: Likes curry. Dislikes mousse, cheesecake.\n\nNext, we will create our \'orderable\' and \'not_orderable\' lists based on their preferences, ensuring that both lists hold mutually exclusive items.\n\nFirst, we notice that "wonton" is liked by several people (Sia, Jun, Kavi, and Eli) but disliked by many as well (Liv, Ru, Feng, Jan, Min, Ping). Because it is impossible to put "wonton" on both lists, we need to decide where it should go. Given that "wonton" is disliked by six people and liked by only four, it would be strategic to place "wonton" in the \'not_orderable\' list to satisfy a higher number of people.\n\nNow "shawarma" is disliked by three people (Liv, Mia, Xiu) and liked by two (Jun, Gia). Putting "shawarma" in \'not_orderable\' will satisfy five people (three who dislike it plus Jun and Gia who both have another item they like - vegetables for Gia and wonton for Jun, which is in \'not_orderable\').\n\n"Cheesecake" is liked by four people (Sia, Mia, Xiu, Gia) and disliked by four (Jun, Feng, Eli, Jan). We must ensure that those who like cheesecake have another item they like in the \'orderable\' list. Sia also likes wonton, which is in \'not_orderable\', so she is satisfied. Mia and Xiu both like mousse, so we can satisfy them by putting mousse in \'orderable\'. Gia likes vegetables and shawarma, with shawarma being in \'not_orderable\', she is satisfied too. With this setup, we can put "cheesecake" into \'not_orderable\'.\n\n"Mousse" is liked by three people (Ru, Mia, Xiu) and disliked by four (Kavi, Feng, Eli, Oli). Three out of the four who dislike mousse also dislike cheesecake, so they are already satisfied with cheesecake being in \'not_orderable\'. Thus, placing "mousse" in \'orderable\' would be a good choice - this satisfies Ru, Mia, and Xiu.\n\n"Vegetables" is liked by many (Ru, Kavi, Min, Gia) and only disliked by Feng. Since "mousse" is in \'orderable\', Ru is already satisfied. Kavi likes wonton which is in \'not_orderable\', Min dislikes wonton which is \'not_orderable\', thus they\'re both satisfied. Feng dislikes cheesecake, which is placed in \'not_orderable\', so he is satisfied, leaving "vegetables" to be placed in \'orderable\' to satisfy Gia.\n\n"Brownies" is disliked by two people (Sia, Liv) and hasn\'t been liked by anyone, making it an ideal candidate for \'not_orderable\'.\n\n"Curry" has no dislikes and is liked by two people (Min, Oli), we can put "curry" in \'orderable\'.\n\nReviewing the lists we\'ve built:\n\'orderable\': mousse, vegetables, curry\n\'not_orderable\': wonton, shawarma, cheesecake, brownies\n\nEveryone\'s preferences have been met with this distribution:\n\n- Sia has cheesecake in \'not_orderable\' or wonton in \'not_orderable\'.\n- Liv has brownies and wonton in \'not_orderable\'.\n- Ru has mousse in \'orderable\' and wonton in \'not_orderable\'.\n- Mia has mousse in \'orderable\'.\n- Xiu has mousse in \'orderable\'.\n- Jun has wonton in \'not_orderable\' and is okay since cheesecake which he dislikes is also in \'not_orderable\'.\n- Kavi has wonton in \'not_orderable\' or vegetables in \'orderable\'.\n- Feng has cheesecake in \'not_orderable\'.\n- Eli has wonton in \'not_orderable\'.\n- Jan has cheesecake in \'not_orderable\'.\n- Min has vegetables in \'orderable\' and wonton in \'not_orderable\'.\n- Gia has shawarma in \'not_orderable\' or vegetables in \'orderable\'.\n- Ping has wonton and shawarma in \'not_orderable\'.\n- Oli has curry in \'orderable\'.\n\nEach person is at least satisfied once, so our solution is valid. Our lists are as follows:\n\norderable=[\'mousse\', \'vegetables\', \'curry\'], not_orderable=[\'wonton\', \'shawarma\', \'cheesecake\', \'brownies\']', 'num_prompt_tokens': 386, 'num_completion_tokens': 1159}
