st:\n- ramen: Disliked by many, strong candidate for \'not_orderable\'.\n- cheesecake: Disliked by enough to likely place in \'not_orderable\'.\n- cheese: Disliked by several.\n- doughnut: Mixed reactions, possibly \'not_orderable\'.\n- falafel: Disliked by several participants.\n- korma: Disliked by many, making it likely for \'not_orderable\'.\n- lobster: Many dislikes as well.\n- cake: Too many dislikes, a good candidate for \'not_orderable\'.\n\nGiven the criteria, it seems we have a chance of satisfying all participants with waffles being \'orderable\' and several items potentially being \'not_orderable\' (ramen, falafel, korma, cake, maybe cheesecake). \n\nFirst, let\'s try to satisfy everyone by including waffles in \'orderable\', since it\'s liked by many and has minimal dislikes. Now, let\'s make sure that those who dislike waffles have another item on the \'not_orderable\' list to make them satisfied.\n\nEvery person who dislikes waffles also dislikes either falafel, korma, or cheesecake (and sometimes more than one of these).\n\nThis strategy seems workable. To satisfy all participants:\n- \'orderable\' should include waffles because it\'s not disliked enough to cause a problem, and we don’t have any clear indication that it satisfies no one.\n- \'not_orderable\' should include items that are disliked by many but did not infer a circular requirement with waffles being disliked by anyone who also dislikes these items. Ramen, falafel, cake, and korma seem to be strong candidates for \'not_orderable\'.\n\nNow, there might be conflicts. If persons who dislike waffles only like items that are on the \'not_orderable\' list, we will have an issue. I need to ensure that\'s not the case. Upon checking, each participant that dislikes waffles has other items they like, which aren\'t universally disliked, and none of them solely like ramen, falafel, cake, or korma.\n\nBased on this analysis, a solution that will likely satisfy all participants is:\n- \'orderable\': [waffles]\n- \'not_orderable\': [ramen, falafel, cake, korma]\n\nHowever, if we find someone who only likes the items that are on \'not_orderable\', we would end up not fulfilling all the requirements, and we have to iterate again.\n\nAfter a thorough review, there are no such conflicts, and therefore, the solution provided should satisfy everyone.\n\nTherefore, the final solution is:\n``` python\norderable=["waffles"], not_orderable=["ramen", "falafel", "cake", "korma"]\n```', 'num_prompt_tokens': 1277, 'num_completion_tokens': 849}
