 Tim, Zara, Sue, Yan, Kim, Ying, Ned\n\nLikes for lobster: Sky, Riya, Sia, Tim, Sue, Rex, Leo, Kim, Pam, Dan, Ying, Ned\nDislikes for lobster: Ana, Aarav, Wei, Kavi, Kai, Abe, Yan, Raj, Gen, Fay\n\nLikes for vindaloo: Wei, Sky, Riya, Sia, Tim, Zara, Sue, Abe, Rex, Kim, Kaz, Raj, Gen\nDislikes for vindaloo: Ana, Aarav, Kavi, Meg, Leo, Kai, Yan, Dan, Ying, Ned, Pam, Fay\n\nOne thing to notice:\n- Kai and Yan dislike all three items, and we cannot satisfy them unless we include one of their dislikes in 'not_orderable'.\n- Ned, Ying, and Pam are satisfied if lobster is orderable since it's the only item they like and they each dislike the other two.\n\nGiven that 'lobster' seems to be liked by many, and some have it as their only like, let's try putting 'lobster' in 'orderable' and see if we can still satisfy everyone.\n\norderable: lobster\nnot_orderable: --\n\nNext, since Kai and Yan dislike everything, and there's no overlap in dislikes with anyone who likes lobster, we need to put either cake or vindaloo in the 'not_orderable' list to satisfy Kai and Yan. We'll choose vindaloo because fewer people have it as a dislike, and it's included in the dislike list along with lobster, which is already in 'orderable'.\n\norderable: lobster\nnot_orderable: vindaloo\n\nNow, everyone who likes lobster is satisfied, and everyone who dislikes vindaloo is also satisfied. The remaining issue is with the people who like cake.\n\nWe need to ensure that everyone who likes cake is satisfied without displeasing those who dislike it. Placing cake in the 'orderable' list will satisfy all cake likers.\n\norderable: lobster, cake\nnot_orderable: vindaloo\n\nHowever, this solution won't satisfy the people who dislike cake. Unfortunately, we cannot find a combination that satisfies all because we have people who only like cake and the same amount of people who only dislike cake.\n\nGiven this, satisfying everyone is impossible with the provided preferences. Thus, we must return empty lists for