sts.\n\n**Step 1: Identify items that must be in \'not_orderable\' to satisfy dislikes.**\n\nSeveral people dislike "sorbet" (Shan, Raj, Ray, Joe, Lex, Nic, Sid, Cal, Sue), and since it is also liked by multiple participants, we cannot place it in the \'orderable\' list to satisfy those participants. However, the condition for satisfaction is that either one liked item is on the \'orderable\' list or one disliked item is on the \'not_orderable\' list. \n\nSimilarly, "ravioli" and "shrimps" are disliked by a few people. However, considering the likes and dislikes of others, if we place "ravioli" and "shrimps" in \'not_orderable\', we would end up not satisfying Raj (likes "ravioli"), Ray (likes "shrimps"), Tara (likes "shrimps" and "ravioli"), and Sue (likes "ravioli") unless we find something they all like in \'orderable\'. But there is no such common liked item among them.\n\nSince there is no consensus on "shrimps" and "ravioli" because they are liked by some and disliked by others without any alternate likes to satisfy everyone, we cannot initially determine if "shrimps" or "ravioli" should be in \'not_orderable\'.\n\n**Step 2: Identify items that must be in \'orderable\' to satisfy likes.**\n\nLooking at the likes, "omelette" is liked by several participants (Raj, Ray, Bea, Ken, Bao, Jun, Sol, Cal) and disliked by Xan, Joe, and Isha. Since "omelette" has no alternative like for Xan, Joe, and Isha, and it is liked by many, we should consider placing "omelette" in \'orderable\'.\n\n"Tiramisu" is liked by Bea, Ada, Sid, and Cal and disliked by Nic, Bao, and Abe. Since "tiramisu" is not the only liked item for any participant and it is disliked by a few, it cannot be determined yet whether it should be in \'orderable\'.\n\n**Step 3: Address conflict items and find a solution.**\n\nWe need to identify an item to place in \'orderable\' for those who like "shrimps" and "ravioli" if we decide to put them in \'not_orderable\'. Since no item is commonly liked by Raj, Ray,