ad. Val: Likes curry, korma. Dislikes ravioli. Jim: Likes omelette. Dislikes ravioli, kebab. Shan: Likes omelette, korma. Dislikes bread. Ava: Dislikes omelette, soup, korma. Zara: Likes bread. Dislikes omelette, ravioli. Raj: Likes mousse, soup, korma. Yan: Likes omelette. Dislikes soup, mousse. Ravi: Likes soup, bread, cake. Eva: Likes soup. Dislikes cake, omelette. Ana: Dislikes omelette, cake, curry. Qi: Likes korma. Dislikes ravioli, bread. Ping: Likes soup, curry. Dislikes bread. Gus: Likes soup, kebab. Dislikes bread. Sue: Likes kebab. Dislikes curry, bread. Cal: Dislikes soup, kebab, mousse. Mina: Likes ravioli. Dislikes kebab, cake. Chao: Likes bread, soup. Dislikes omelette. Taj: Likes ravioli. Dislikes mousse, soup. Min: Likes kebab, cake. Dislikes bread. Sol: Likes ravioli, mousse. Dislikes kebab. Chen: Likes korma, ravioli. Dislikes curry. Lei: Likes kebab. Dislikes mousse, cake. Mia: Likes bread, soup, ravioli. Hui: Likes omelette. Dislikes ravioli, soup. ', 'menu_items': ['kebab', 'omelette', 'cake', 'soup', 'korma', 'curry', 'ravioli', 'mousse', 'bread'], 'gpt_out': "To find a combination of `orderable` and `not_orderable` foods that satisfy everyone's likes and dislikes, let's follow a process to determine which items can go on which list.\n\n1. We start with foods that are disliked by one or more individuals but are not liked by anyone. These items can safely be placed in the `not_orderable` list.\n2. Then we look at foods that are liked by one or more individuals and not disliked by anyone. These items can safely be placed in the `orderable` list.\n3. We then continue by looking for items that are liked and disliked but can satisfy the most without conflict.\n4. We have to consider individual requirements and look for potential conflicts. If an item is liked by one person and disliked by another, we cannot place it on the `orderable` list if it makes someone dissatisfied. Conversely, we cannot put an item on the `not_orderable` list if that would make someone who likes it dissatisfied.\n5. For people who only dislike items (e.g., Ansh), we need to ensure that at least one of their disliked items is on the `not_orderable` list.\n6. If we end up with an item that is both liked and disliked by at least one individual and adding it to either list would result in someone being unhappy, we conclude that we cannot satisfy everyone.\n\nLet's sort them out step by step:\n\n**Step 1: Find foods that are only disliked and not liked by anyone.**\n- No food appears to meet this criterion since each food disliked by someone is also liked by someone else.\n\n**Step 2: Find foods that are only liked and not disliked by anyone.**\n- No food appears to meet this criterion either since every liked food is also disliked by at least one person.\n\n**Step 3: Go item by item to try to satisfy the most individuals without conflict.**\nFor example:\n- Ansh dislikes kebab, mousse, and omelette, we can put one of these on the not_orderable list to satisfy their dislike.\n- Ansh dislikes `omelette`, but `omelette` is liked by many and not disliked by many others. Therefore, it would be a candidate for `orderable`, assuming we can satisfy Ansh's dislikes with another item.\n- The same goes for `mousse` and `kebab`, they are both widely liked; hence, we can't place them in `not_orderable` without causing dissatisfaction.\n- `bread` is similar; it is disliked by many, but some (Bo, Zara, and Ava) have it on their liked list.\n\nContinuing this logic, it becomes apparent that many people have conflicting interests over the same items, making it very challenging to satisfy all participants.\n\n**Step 4: Looking for direct conflicts.**\n- For example, `bread` is liked by Bo, Zara, Jaya, Oli, Chao, Mia, and Ravi but is also disliked by many. This is a direct conflict.\n- `ravioli` is liked by several people but is also disliked by many, another conflict.\n- Repeating this for every item will show that each item is both liked and disliked by different participants, causing a conflict.\n\nGiven these conflicting preferences, every item is subject to a conflict, making it impossible to create two lists where each person is satisfied according to the criteria provided.\n\n**Conclusion:**\nSince each food item is liked and disliked by at least one person, there is no way to satisfy everyone with the available foods. Any attempt to include an item in `orderable` or `not_orderable` will inevitably result in at least one unsatisfied individual.\n\n**Solution:**\n```python\norderable = []\nnot_orderable = []\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 924, 'num_completion_tokens': 773}
Data Sample: {'num_vars': 5, 'num_clauses': 35, 'formula': [[-5, 4, 3], [-5, 4, -2], [1, -3, -2], [4, -3, -5], [1, -2, 3], [-1, -3, -5], [4, 2, -3], [2, -4, -5], [-1, 4, -2], [1, 3, 4], [-3, 2, -1], [-4, 3, 1], [-1, -2, 4], [-2, 3, -5], [1, 3, 4], [-5, 3, 4], [-5, -1, 3], [-3, 4, -5], [1, 2, -4], [-1, -5, -2], [-2, -3, -4], [-5, -1, 4], [-5, 3, 4], [-3, -5, 4], [-4, -1, 2], [4, 3, 5], [-4, 2, -1], [-1, -2, 5], [4, 5, -3], [3, 4, -2], [1, 4, -2], [-3, -4, -2], [1, 3, 5], [4, 5, 1], [2, 3, -5]], 'is_sat': False, 'preferences': 'Tara: Likes pizza, churros. Dislikes custard. Rohan: Likes pizza. Dislikes custard, lobster. Dot: Likes roti. Dislikes churros, lobster. Diya: Likes pizza. Dislikes churros, custard. Jim: Likes roti, churros. Dislikes lobster. Ron: Dislikes roti, churros, custard. Ray: Likes pizza, lobster. Dislikes churros. Jun: Likes lobster. Dislikes pizza, custard. Dan: Likes pizza. Dislikes roti, lobster. Tia: Likes roti, churros, pizza. Sia: Likes lobster. Dislikes churros, roti. Raj: Likes churros, roti. Dislikes pizza. Pam: Likes pizza. Dislikes roti, lobster. Pia: Likes churros. Dislikes lobster, custard. Naina: Likes roti, churros, pizza. Rina: Likes churros, pizza. Dislikes custard. Neil: Likes churros. Dislikes custard, roti. Yan: Likes pizza. Dislikes churros, custard. Bo: Likes roti, lobster. Dislikes pizza. Sid: Dislikes roti, custard, lobster. Jan: Dislikes lobster, churros, pizza. Lia: Likes pizza. Dislikes custard, roti. Rob: Likes churros, pizza. Dislikes custard. Liz: Likes pizza. Dislikes churros, custard. Jay: Likes lobster. Dislikes pizza, roti. Liv: Likes pizza, churros, custard. Arun: Likes lobster. Dislikes pizza, roti. Xiu: Likes custard. Dislikes roti, lobster. May: Likes pizza, custard. Dislikes churros. Gus: Likes churros, pizza. Dislikes lobster. Nic: Likes roti, pizza. Dislikes lobster. Zac: Dislikes churros, pizza, lobster. Anil: Likes roti, churros, custard. Rex: Likes pizza, custard, roti. Taj: Likes lobster, churros. Dislikes custard. ', 'menu_items': ['roti', 'lobster', 'churros', 'pizza', 'custard'], 'gpt_out': "To solve this problem, the strategy involves finding common ground among all individuals based on their likes and dislikes. We'll start by listing all the food items and then assess which items can be mutually orderable or not orderable to satisfy the group.\n\nThe food items mentioned are:\n- pizza\n- churros\n- custard\n- lobster\n- roti\n\nHere's the step-by-step plan:\n\n1. Look for items that have to be on the 'not_orderable' list because someone dislikes them but no one specifically likes them, or they are liked and disliked by every person who mentions them. No such items exist because all disliked items are liked by at least one person too.\n\n2. Look for items that have to be on the 'orderable' list because someone likes them but no one specifically dislikes them, or they are liked and disliked by every person who mentions them. No such items exist because all liked items are disliked by at least one person too.\n\n3. Look for a disliked item that can be placed on the 'not_orderable' list to satisfy the maximum number of individuals with dislikes only. Custard is disliked by many individuals who do not have anything else in their 'likes'. This suggests custard should be 'not_orderable'. \n\n4. Confirm that there is no individual who only likes custard with no alternative. Xiu and May only like custard and will therefore not be satisfied if it is not orderable.\n\nWe have found that there are individuals who will only be satisfied with mutually exclusive scenarios, making it impossible to craft an 'orderable' and 'not_orderable' list that will satisfy everyone.\n\nThus, the final output is:\n\n`orderable=[], not_orderable=[]`", 'num_prompt_tokens': 658, 'num_completion_tokens': 350}
Data Sample: {'num_vars': 4, 'num_clauses': 24, 'formula': [[3, -4, 1], [1, 4, 2], [-2, 1, -3], [-4, -2, 3], [3, 1, 4], [-2, 1, -3], [4, 3, -2], [2, 1, -4], [2, 3, -4], [-2, -3, -1], [-2, 1, 4], [-2, -1, 4], [1, -3, -4], [2, 4, -1], [4, 2, 1], [3, 1, 4], [-2, 3, -4], [1, 3, -2], [-4, 2, -1], [1, -3, 4], [-4, -1, 3], [4, 3, 1], [2, 3, 1], [-1, 3, 2]], 'is_sat': False, 'preferences': 'Chao: Likes doughnut, tandoori. Dislikes burrito. Jun: Likes tandoori, burrito, kebab. Veer: Likes tandoori. Dislikes kebab, doughnut. Abe: Likes doughnut. Dislikes burrito, kebab. Ava: Likes doughnut, tandoori, burrito. Vic: Likes tandoori. Dislikes kebab, doughnut. Lou: Likes burrito, doughnut. Dislikes kebab. Om: Likes kebab, tandoori. Dislikes burrito. Cy: Likes kebab, doughnut. Dislikes burrito. May: Dislikes kebab, doughnut, tandoori. Eli: Likes tandoori, burrito. Dislikes kebab. Lan: Likes burrito. Dislikes kebab, tandoori. Lin: Likes tandoori. Dislikes doughnut, burrito. Max: Likes kebab, burrito. Dislikes tandoori. Pam: Likes burrito, kebab, tandoori. Diya: Likes doughnut, tandoori, burrito. Ping: Likes doughnut. Dislikes kebab, burrito. Val: Likes tandoori, doughnut. Dislikes kebab. Jay: Likes kebab. Dislikes burrito, tandoori. Ned: Likes tandoori, burrito. Dislikes doughnut. Geet: Likes doughnut. Dislikes burrito, tandoori. Gia: Likes burrito, doughnut, tandoori. Jim: Likes kebab, doughnut, tandoori. Rex: Likes doughnut, kebab. Dislikes tandoori. ', 'menu_items': ['tandoori', 'kebab', 'doughnut', 'burrito'], 'gpt_out': "Let's start by analysing the likes and dislikes to find any items that must be on one list or the other to satisfy everyone's preferences.\n\n1. **Must be in 'orderable':**\n   - If every person who likes an item does not have any of their disliked items only available in 'orderable', and at least one of their likes is not disliked by anyone else, then that food item must be in 'orderable'.\n\n2. **Must be in 'not_orderable':**\n   - If every person who dislikes an item does not have any of their liked items only available in 'not_orderable', and at least one of their dislikes is not liked by anyone else, then that food item must be in 'not_orderable'.\n\nA quick observation:\n- **Doughnut** is liked by many but also disliked by some. It has to be treated carefully.\n- **Tandoori** is universally liked except by Geet, May, and Lan, who dislike it. These persons do not like anything else. Thus, to satisfy them, tandoori must not be in 'orderable'.\n- **Kebab** is also controversial, liked by many but disliked by many.\n- **Burrito** is liked by many and disliked by only a few.\n\nLet's go through the process with these items in mind:\n\n- **Tandoori**: Because May dislikes tandoori and doesn't like anything else, tandoori cannot be in 'orderable'.\n- **Kebab**, **Doughnut**, and **Burrito**: These items are liked and disliked by multiple people. To avoid conflicts, we'll avoid making assumptions about these items now and revisit them later.\n\nThere is no single food item that meets the criteria for must be in 'orderable'. Because of the conflicting likes and dislikes, we can't satisfy everyone based solely on one universally liked or disliked item.\n\nWe thus have to check combinations, trying to find at least one liked item for every person without placing any of their disliked items in 'orderable', or placing at least one disliked item in 'not_orderable'.\n\nLet's go step by step:\n\n- **Doughnut**: Can't be in 'orderable' because it's disliked by Veer, Vic, May, Ping, Ned, and Geet. To satisfy these people otherwise, at least one of their other likes or dislikes must be accounted for elsewhere.\n- **Veer and Vic's** other dislikes are kebab and doughnut. They only like tandoori. Since tandoori cannot be in 'orderable', at least doughnut or kebab must be in 'not_orderable'.\n- **Ping** dislikes kebab and burrito. Since they only like doughnut, and doughnut can't be in 'orderable', either kebab or burrito must be in 'not_orderable'.\n- **Ned** dislikes doughnut and likes tandoori and burrito. We can't satisfy them with tandoori in 'orderable', so burrito cannot be in 'not_orderable' because that's the only remaining item they like.\n- **Geet** dislikes tandoori and likes doughnut. Doughnut can't be in 'orderable', so we have no way to satisfy Geet directly.\n\nWe're stuck at the last point because there's no way to satisfy Geet. Since his only like is doughnut, and we've determined doughnut can't be in 'orderable', and his dislike, tandoori, also can't be in 'orderable', there's no combination of orderable/not_orderable that satisfies Geet's requirements alongside the requirements of the other individuals like Veer and Vic.\n\nTherefore, there is no combination of 'orderable' and 'not_orderable' lists that can satisfy all participants. Our final lists will be empty:\n\n```python\norderable = []\nnot_orderable = []\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 540, 'num_completion_tokens': 801}
Data Sample: {'num_vars': 8, 'num_clauses': 24, 'formula': [[-7, -2, 5], [-6, 8, 4], [1, 6, 2], [1, 3, 6], [4, -5, 8], [-4, 7, -6], [5, -4, 2], [-2, 1, -8], [-8, 7, 2], [-2, 5, 8], [1, -4, 6], [-7, -3, 2], [-8, -2, 1], [-6, -8, -7], [-7, -8, 6], [8, -5, -6], [-7, -3, 4], [-3, 7, -1], [6, 3, -1], [-7, 5, 1], [-7, 6, -3], [-8, 3, 7], [2, -8, 5], [-5, 1, -2]], 'is_sat': False, 'preferences': 'Dot: Likes sorbet. Dislikes fries, cheese. Lee: Likes vindaloo, roti. Dislikes espresso. Taj: Likes spaghetti, espresso, cheese. Hu: Likes spaghetti, naan, espresso. Ray: Likes roti, vindaloo. Dislikes sorbet. Lou: Likes fries. Dislikes roti, espresso. Chen: Likes sorbet, cheese. Dislikes roti. Rohan: Likes spaghetti. Dislikes cheese, vindaloo. Diya: Likes fries, cheese. Dislikes vindaloo. Xan: Likes sorbet, vindaloo. Dislikes cheese. Ru: Likes spaghetti, espresso. Dislikes roti. Lei: Likes cheese. Dislikes fries, naan. Gus: Likes spaghetti. Dislikes vindaloo, cheese. Ben: Dislikes espresso, vindaloo, fries. Riya: Likes espresso. Dislikes fries, vindaloo. Qi: Likes vindaloo. Dislikes sorbet, espresso. Meg: Likes roti. Dislikes fries, naan. Ian: Likes fries. Dislikes naan, spaghetti. Jan: Likes espresso, naan. Dislikes spaghetti. Ana: Likes sorbet, spaghetti. Dislikes fries. Neil: Likes espresso. Dislikes fries, naan. Ron: Likes naan, fries. Dislikes vindaloo. Lan: Likes cheese, sorbet. Dislikes vindaloo. Om: Likes spaghetti. Dislikes sorbet, cheese. ', 'menu_items': ['spaghetti', 'cheese', 'naan', 'roti', 'sorbet', 'espresso', 'fries', 'vindaloo'], 'gpt_out': "Let's start by listing out the likes and dislikes of every individual and then identify potential conflicts in order to create two separate lists: one for 'orderable' items and one for 'not_orderable' items.\n\n**Likes/Dislikes:**\n\n**Dot:**  \nLikes: sorbet  \nDislikes: fries, cheese\n\n**Lee:**  \nLikes: vindaloo, roti  \nDislikes: espresso\n\n**Taj:**  \nLikes: spaghetti, espresso, cheese  \nDislikes: -\n\n**Hu:**  \nLikes: spaghetti, naan, espresso  \nDislikes: -\n\n**Ray:**  \nLikes: roti, vindaloo  \nDislikes: sorbet\n\n**Lou:**  \nLikes: fries  \nDislikes: roti, espresso\n\n**Chen:**  \nLikes: sorbet, cheese  \nDislikes: roti\n\n**Rohan:**  \nLikes: spaghetti  \nDislikes: cheese, vindaloo\n\n**Diya:**  \nLikes: fries, cheese  \nDislikes: vindaloo\n\n**Xan:**  \nLikes: sorbet, vindaloo\nDislikes: cheese\n\n**Ru:**  \nLikes: spaghetti, espresso\nDislikes: roti\n\n**Lei:**  \nLikes: cheese  \nDislikes: fries, naan\n\n**Gus:**  \nLikes: spaghetti  \nDislikes: vindaloo, cheese\n\n**Ben:**  \nDislikes: espresso, vindaloo, fries\n\n**Riya:**  \nLikes: espresso  \nDislikes: fries, vindaloo\n\n**Qi:**  \nLikes: vindaloo  \nDislikes: sorbet, espresso\n\n**Meg:**  \nLikes: roti  \nDislikes: fries, naan\n\n**Ian:**  \nLikes: fries  \nDislikes: naan, spaghetti\n\n**Jan:**  \nLikes: espresso, naan\nDislikes: spaghetti\n\n**Ana:**  \nLikes: sorbet, spaghetti\nDislikes: fries\n\n**Neil:**  \nLikes: espresso  \nDislikes: fries, naan\n\n**Ron:**  \nLikes: naan, fries  \nDislikes: vindaloo\n\n**Lan:**  \nLikes: cheese, sorbet  \nDislikes: vindaloo\n\n**Om:**  \nLikes: spaghetti  \nDislikes: sorbet, cheese\n\nLooking at the dislikes first might help to identify the items that could be commonly put in the 'not_orderable' list without causing unsatisfaction. In contrast, we will then look at the likes to see what can be safely ordered.\n\n**Dislikes Commonalities:**  \n- Vindaloo: Disliked by Lee, Ray, Rohan, Diya, Ben, Riya, Qi, Gus, Lan\n- Fries: Disliked by Dot, Ben, Ana, Neil, Riya\n- Espresso: Disliked by Lee, Lou, Ben, Qi, Neil\n- Cheese: Disliked by Dot, Xan, Rohan, Om\n- Roti: Disliked by Lou, Chen, Meg, Ru\n- Sorbet: Disliked by Ray, Qi\n- Spaghetti: Disliked by Ian, Jan, Om\n- Naan: Disliked by Lei, Ian, Meg, Neil\n\n**Orderable:**\n- Espresso: Liked by Taj, Hu, Ru, Riya, Jan, Neil; Disliked by Lee, Lou, Ben, Qi, Neil but they can be satisfied by other likes/dislikes.\n- Fries: Liked by Lou, Diya, Ian, Ron; Disliked by Dot, Ben, Ana, Neil, Riya. However, these individuals have other preferences that can be satisfied.\n\n**Not Orderable:**\n- Vindaloo: Disliked by too many participants to be put in 'orderable'.\n\nConsidering these insights, let's attempt a combination:\n\n**Not Orderable:**\n- Vindaloo\n- Cheese (liked by many, but those individuals also like something else, except for Lei)\n- Roti: is liked by many but it is possible to avoid it.\n\n**Orderable:**\n- Sorbet is liked by Dot, Chen, Xan, Lan, Ana (and disliked only by Ray and Qi, but they both like Vindaloo which is already in 'not_orderable')\n- Espresso is liked by many and only a few dislike it, and those could be satisfied otherwise.\n- Fries are disliked by a few, but those who dislike them can be satisfied with other items in the 'orderable' list or by the 'not_orderable' list.\n- Spaghetti, because it's liked by many and only disliked by a few who can be satisfied with other selections.\n\nNow, let's look if we can refine this based on conflicts:\n\n**Conflicts:**\n\n- Lei dislikes fries and naan, but likes cheese which is 'not_orderable'. We need to put naan in 'orderable'.\n- Ian dislikes naan and spaghetti, but we decided to put both in 'orderable'. To satisfy Ian we must put fries in 'orderable', and since others who dislike fries have other options, they will still be satisfied.\n- Meg dislikes fries and naan, but likes roti which is not 'orderable'. Naan is 'orderable', so Meg is satisfied.\n- Ray dislikes sorbet, but likes vindaloo which is 'not orderable'. Ray is satisfied.\n- Qi dislikes espresso and sorbet but likes vindaloo which is 'not orderable'. Qi is satisfied. \n\nAccording to the conditions set above and considering these conflicts, we can derive the final lists:\n\n**Orderable:** sorbet, espresso, fries, spaghetti, naan  \n**Not Orderable:** vindaloo, cheese, roti\n\nNow, the python code for the final solution is:\n\n```python\norderable=['sorbet', 'espresso', 'fries', 'spaghetti', 'naan'], not_orderable=['vindaloo', 'cheese', 'roti']\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 498, 'num_completion_tokens': 1226}
Data Sample: {'num_vars': 8, 'num_clauses': 16, 'formula': [[-7, 1, -8], [1, 4, 3], [-3, 8, -1], [2, 3, 4], [8, -3, -6], [-1, 2, 4], [3, 4, -6], [3, -8, 2], [4, 2, -7], [-5, 4, -2], [7, -3, -4], [5, -1, -7], [2, -7, -3], [-7, 3, 5], [7, 6, 1], [2, -8, 6]], 'is_sat': True, 'preferences': 'Sid: Likes churros. Dislikes burrito, guacamole. Cy: Likes churros, roti, mousse. Mei: Likes guacamole. Dislikes mousse, churros. Tia: Likes muffins, mousse, roti. Rina: Likes guacamole. Dislikes mousse, toast. Ty: Likes muffins, roti. Dislikes churros. Ru: Likes mousse, roti. Dislikes toast. Isha: Likes mousse, muffins. Dislikes guacamole. Ned: Likes roti, muffins. Dislikes burrito. Ravi: Likes roti. Dislikes pancakes, muffins. Ping: Likes burrito. Dislikes mousse, roti. Mel: Likes pancakes. Dislikes churros, burrito. Du: Likes muffins. Dislikes burrito, mousse. Aarav: Likes mousse, pancakes. Dislikes burrito. Tim: Likes burrito, toast, churros. Anil: Likes muffins, toast. Dislikes guacamole. ', 'menu_items': ['churros', 'muffins', 'mousse', 'roti', 'pancakes', 'toast', 'burrito', 'guacamole'], 'gpt_out': "First, let's identify if there are any items that must be on the 'orderable' list or 'not_orderable' list to satisfy everyone's likes and dislikes.\n\nWe search for any foods that are liked by someone and disliked by nobody, which would make them a perfect candidate for the 'orderable' list. Conversely, we search for any foods that are disliked by someone and liked by nobody, which would make them a perfect candidate for the 'not_orderable' list.\n\nUpon inspection, no such item exists that is liked by someone and disliked by nobody, or vice versa. This means we'll need to find a compromise. \n\nNext, lets see if we can satisfy those who have unique likes, as this may reduce the complexity:\n\n- Sid uniquely likes churros, but churros are disliked by Mei and Mel, and we cannot satisfy Sid without making Mei or Mel dissatisfied.\n- Mei uniquely likes guacamole, but guacamole is disliked by Sid, Rina, Isha, and Anil, and we cannot satisfy Mei without making them dissatisfied.\n- Rina uniquely likes guacamole, which faces the same issue as Mei.\n- Mel uniquely likes pancakes, but pancakes are disliked by Ravi, so we cannot satisfy Mel without making Ravi dissatisfied.\n\nSince no unique likes can be satisfied without causing dissatisfaction, we can't immediately place any items on either list based on unique likes.\n\nNext, let's see if we can satisfy people based on their unique dislikes:\n\n- Sid, Ned, and Aarav uniquely dislike burrito, but Ping and Tim like burritos, so we cannot use Sid's, Ned's, or Aarav's dislike to eliminate burritos.\n- Ru uniquely dislikes toast, but Tim and Anil like toast, so we cannot use Ru's dislike to eliminate toast.\n\nAs none of the unique dislikes help us eliminate an item, we need to analyze the group's preferences as a whole and find an overlap by starting with one item and see if we can build a satisfactory combination of 'orderable' and 'not_orderable' lists from there.\n\nLet's start with one item from the likes that seem to be common and can potentially satisfy many individuals: roti. If we place roti on the 'orderable' list, we satisfy Cy, Tia, Ty, Ru, Ned, and Ravi. This choice does not immediately cause dissatisfaction because nobody dislikes roti uniquely.\n\nNow let's see what corresponding 'not_orderable' choice might work. The next most common like is muffins. While muffins are liked by many, they are disliked by Ravi. Given Ravi's roti preference has been satisfied by it being 'orderable', we can put muffins on the 'not_orderable' list to satisfy him.\n\nBy this reasoning, we now have:\norderable: roti\nnot_orderable: muffins\n\nNow let's check if we can keep everyone satisfied.\n\nSid (only likes churros, which is not in conflict with current lists)\nCy (likes roti & mousse; roti is orderable)\nMei (likes guacamole, dislikes mousse and churros; as long as guacamole or mousse aren't on the orderable list and churros isn't on the not_orderable list, satisfied)\nTia (likes mousse and roti; roti is orderable, satisfied)\nRina (likes guacamole, dislikes toast and mousse; similar to Mei)\nTy (likes roti, dislikes churros; roti is orderable, satisfied)\nRu (likes mousse and roti, dislikes toast; roti is orderable, satisfied)\nIsha (likes mousse, dislikes guacamole; none of these items are on the not_orderable)\nNed (likes roti and muffins, dislikes burrito; roti is orderable, satisfied)\nRavi (likes roti, dislikes pancakes, muffins; roti is on the orderable, muffins are on the not_orderable, satisfied)\nPing (likes burrito, dislikes mousse, roti; as long as burrito isn't on the not_orderable list, satisfied)\nMel (likes pancakes, dislikes churros and burrito; satisfied as pancakes aren't on the not_orderable list and churros aren't on the orderable list)\nDu (likes muffins, dislikes burrito and mousse; mousse isn't on the orderable list, satisfied)\nAarav (likes mousse, pancakes, dislikes burrito; as long as mousse isn't on the not_orderable list, satisfied)\nTim (likes burrito, toast, churros; no conflicting items on the current lists)\nAnil (likes muffins, toast; dislikes guacamole; no conflicting items on the current lists)\n\nAs it stands, all individuals can be satisfied with 'roti' as orderable and 'muffins' as not orderable. There are no direct conflicts and all the individuals have at least one like or dislike item on the appropriate list to satisfy their preference. Hence our solution will be:\n\n```python\norderable=['roti'], not_orderable=['muffins']\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 421, 'num_completion_tokens': 1074}
