t' on the 'not_orderable' list to satisfy most individuals. Following that, 'korma' and 'risotto' have a number of people liking each that do not overlap with the people who like 'toast'; so, we can satisfy multiple individuals by allowing these two items to be ordered.\n\nNow we cannot allow 'pizza' and 'baklava' to be ordered as this would make some people who dislike these items and have no liked items ordered unsatisfied. As long as everyone else who dislikes 'toast' has a liked item that will be ordered, we can include 'toast' in the 'not_orderable' list.\n\nHere is a possible solution with 'toast' on the 'not_orderable' list and 'peach', 'korma', and 'risotto' on the 'orderable' list:\n\n```\norderable = ['peach', 'korma', 'risotto']\nnot_orderable = ['toast']\n```\n\nThe following individuals have their likes satisfied with 'orderable': Jay (peach), Xan (korma, baklava), Shan (toast), Arun (risotto, pizza), Lou (peach), Chen (risotto, toast), Lan (korma, peach), Lia (peach), Tara (peach), Hal (korma), Ana (pizza), May (baklava), Nic (korma, peach), Kim, Sol (baklava, toast), Joy (risotto, pizza), Pat (toast), Abe (baklava, risotto, toast), Wei (risotto), Ru (toast), Eva (peach), Kai (risotto, pizza), Ivy (risotto), Sia (risotto), Fay (peach), Dan (peach), Kavi (pizza, peach, baklava), Pam (korma, toast), Vic (risotto, baklava), Ada (korma, risotto), Zara (toast, baklava, korma), Max, Raj (baklava, risotto, toast), Ian (pizza), Cy (baklava), Rob (risotto, peach), Naina (peach, toast), Li (pizza), Qi (baklava, toast), Cal (baklava, toast), Sam (peach), Oli (pizza, risotto), Jan, Mia (toast), Veer (pizza, korma), Lex (baklava), Kit (peach, baklava, pizza), and others who have not had 'toast' as their only like, have their dislikes satisfied with 'not_orderable': Jay (toast), Kit (toast), Shan (baklava), Xan (risotto), Arun (korma), Chen (peach), Tom (toast, baklava), Hal (peach, toast), Lia (toast, pizza), Nic (risotto), Kim (peach, risotto, korma), Joy (toast),