l dislikes, it must be in the 'orderable' list to satisfy those who like it.\n\nGiven this complexity, the next logical step is to iterate: add a liked item to 'orderable' and a disliked one to 'not_orderable', checking each time to see if a person's preferences are met.\n\nSince these lists are complex and we have to ensure everyone is satisfied, let's now iterate over each person's likes alongside their dislikes to adjust the lists:\n\n1. Arun is satisfied with mousse in 'orderable'.\n2. Tao is satisfied since he dislikes vindaloo which is in 'not_orderable'.\n3. Geet likes couscous but dislikes udon, and neither is on the lists yet.\n...\nand so on for each individual.\n\nWe will follow this approach to determine the appropriate items for 'orderable' and 'not_orderable', ensuring all individuals are satisfied according to the satisfaction criteria.\n\nWorking through the list, we realize that it's a complex puzzle, and the right combination of items on these lists needs to ensure that the satisfaction criteria are met for all individuals. This might require several iterations and checks.\n\nThis task might ultimately require computational support due to the complexity and numbers of preferences. A manual approach would be highly time-consuming and prone to error, given the intricate balance of likes and dislikes across a large group with overlapping food preferences.\n\nLet's see the problem from another perspective by trying to add more 'safe' items that are liked by many and disliked by a few. Such items are easier to fit into 'orderable' list.\n\n1. Mousse is already in 'orderable' which satisfies Arun, Ru, Jim, Kavi, Max, Qi, Jon, Val, and Taj.\n2. Nachos satisfy Cal, Zara, Ty, Lou, and Yan, and only two people explicitly dislike it (Meg, Dot).\n\nIterating carefully through the remaining food items and comparing the number of people satisfied by including that food versus the number of people dissatisfied by its absence can help us further refine 'orderable'