em is liked by some and disliked by others.\n\nStep 2: Look for an item that is disliked by many and liked by fewer. Salmon and shrimps appear to be such items, but we need to make sure anyone who likes these items can be satisfied with something else.\n\nHere\'s a systematic approach to figure out a solution:\n\n- We can\'t include salmon in the \'orderable\' list because too many people dislike it (e.g., Anil, Diya, Isha, Mina, Shan, Om, Sue, Jim, Pia, Sid, Dan), but we can\'t include it in the \'not_orderable\' list either because too many people like it (e.g., Sol, Isha, Mina, Kavi, Jan, Jun, Tom, Chao, Sue, Feng, Lei, Dan).\n- Shrimps are liked by many but also disliked by many, and thus they cannot be on the \'orderable\' list. We can actually consider shrimp for the \'not_orderable\' list if every person who likes shrimps can be satisfied by some other food item they also like.\n- Toast is liked by enough number of people (e.g., Abe, Lou, Val, Jan, Jun, Shan, Qi, Amy, Zara, Ava, Lei) but also disliked by several others, thus we cannot safely place it in either list without more analysis.\n- Paneer is liked by several persons (e.g., Isha, Val, Shan, Tom, Gen, Yue, Ava, Gen) but also disliked by many others, so it\'s risky to consider it for \'orderable\'.\n- Vegetables are liked by many and disliked by several, including Oli, Lia, Ty, Sid, Ula, making them not a safe choice for \'orderable\' either.\n\nNow, considering the item "shrimps", we can attempt to place it on the \'not_orderable\' list to satisfy all dislikers of shrimps, but this means we must satisfy all likers of shrimps through other items.\n\nWe need to check if all shrimp-likers have at least another item they like that could be \'orderable\':\n- Anil (likes vegetables, which are not disliked by anyone excessively)\n- Naina (no other likes, but all her dislikes can\'t be \'orderable\')\n- Diya (likes vegetables too)\n- Leo (likes ratatouille, which could potentially be on \'orderable\', as it is