i, Om, Ray, Sal, Meg, Taj, Mei, and Oli (8 dislikes).\n- Spaghetti is liked by Nita, Lei, Chen, Tim, Xan, Feng, Lee, Ron, Wei, Chao, Oli, and Ada (12 likes) and disliked by Kim, Ying, Wes, Sal, Meg, Zoe, Tao, and Shan (8 dislikes).\n- Tiramisu is liked by Kim, May, Sal, Lee, Ron, Zoe, Tao, Diya, and Shan (9 likes) and disliked by Nita, Kai, Ying, Lei, Tim, Ray, Wes, Xan, Meg, Taj, Mei, Oli, and Ada (13 dislikes).\n\nGiven that an item cannot appear on both lists and that there must be at least one liked item on the 'orderable' list or one disliked item on the 'not_orderable' list for everyone to be satisfied, we may proceed as follows:\n\n- There are only three people who dislike all three items (Kai, Om, Ray, Meg, Taj, and Mei). In order to satisfy them, all three items must appear in the 'not_orderable' list, which is logically impossible since it would leave nothing to order.\n- Since nothing can be put on both lists, the best we can do to satisfy everyone is not possible because there isn't a unique item that all of the above-dislike, or like, that could be used to satisfy their condition independently of the other items.\n\nGiven the above observations, it's impossible to create a set of 'orderable' and 'not_orderable' lists that will satisfy everyone. All participants must be satisfied according to the satisfaction criteria, but there's no combination of these items that can achieve it. Therefore, the solution is:\n\n```python\norderable = []\nnot_orderable = []\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 538, 'num_completion_tokens': 445}
