uch an item would be perfect for either the 'orderable' or 'not_orderable' list because it will satisfy a group by its presence and another group by its absence.\n\nSince Sal dislikes all three items (kebab, gelato, tiramisu), to satisfy Sal, at least one of their disliked items must be in the 'not_orderable' list. Therefore, any one of those can be added to 'not_orderable', but not all of them, since this will leave us with no items for the 'orderable' list, and others would remain unsatisfied.\n\nLet's assign tiramisu to 'not_orderable' because Dan is the only person who likes it but doesn't like anything else, so to satisfy Dan, tiramisu cannot be 'orderable'.\n\nWith tiramisu in 'not_orderable':\n- Dan is satisfied since his disliked item is 'not_orderable'.\n- Hal is satisfied since his liked item (kebab or gelato) can be 'orderable' and his disliked item (tiramisu) is 'not_orderable'.\n\nMoving on, we can now check if we can include gelato or kebab to satisfy the maximum number of people. People who dislike gelato are Dan, Tao, Sol, Sal, Ada, Feng, Joe, Xan – among them, Dan and Sal are already satisfied, leaving us with six individuals. People who dislike kebab are Dan, Sal, Ben, Lan, Ron – out of these, Dan and Sal are already satisfied, leaving us with three individuals.\n\nGiven these numbers, if we put gelato in 'orderable':\n- Individuals who like gelato and no other item (Lan, Ron) are now satisfied.\n- And since no additional individuals have a unique preference for kebab, we can also include kebab in the 'orderable' list to satisfy everyone else who likes it.\n\nThere is no conflict since none of the participants dislike both kebab and gelato. Moreover, Hal, Rob, Tao, Ying, Tom, Sol, Diya, Ada, Feng, Kim, Joe, Xan, and Abe who have not yet been discussed are also satisfied with this arrangement as kebab and/or gelato are liked by them.\n\nThis setup satisfies all participants. Tiramisu is 'not_orderable', satisfying those who dislike it, and kebab and gelato are 'orderable', satisfying those who like them.\n\nThus, the final solution is:\n```python\norderable=['kebab', 'gelato'], not_orderable=['tiramisu']\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 461, 'num_completion_tokens': 749}
Data Sample: {'num_vars': 8, 'num_clauses': 24, 'formula': [[-2, -8, 5], [-7, 2, 1], [-1, -7, 5], [4, -1, 7], [6, -1, -8], [-2, 8, -7], [-5, 7, -4], [-3, -5, -2], [6, -8, 4], [7, -1, 8], [2, 8, -6], [7, -4, 6], [-4, -8, 5], [-3, 4, 8], [-7, 4, -3], [-2, -5, -1], [1, 4, 6], [1, -5, -6], [2, 7, 4], [-8, -3, -6], [-1, 5, 2], [8, 1, 5], [3, 7, -8], [3, 8, -2]], 'is_sat': True, 'preferences': 'Meg: Likes nachos. Dislikes shawarma, lobster. Eva: Likes shawarma, quiche. Dislikes ramen. Raj: Likes nachos. Dislikes quiche, ramen. Ken: Likes sorbet, ramen. Dislikes quiche. Ana: Likes poke. Dislikes quiche, lobster. Max: Likes lobster. Dislikes shawarma, ramen. Chen: Likes ramen. Dislikes nachos, sorbet. Dot: Dislikes tart, nachos, shawarma. Ava: Likes poke, sorbet. Dislikes lobster. Bea: Likes ramen, lobster. Dislikes quiche. Ping: Likes shawarma, lobster. Dislikes poke. Ada: Likes ramen, poke. Dislikes sorbet. Lee: Likes nachos. Dislikes sorbet, lobster. Yan: Likes sorbet, lobster. Dislikes tart. Zoe: Likes sorbet. Dislikes ramen, tart. Ray: Dislikes shawarma, nachos, quiche. Wei: Likes quiche, sorbet, poke. Nita: Likes quiche. Dislikes nachos, poke. Jun: Likes shawarma, ramen, sorbet. Ben: Dislikes lobster, tart, poke. Lei: Likes nachos, shawarma. Dislikes quiche. Sid: Likes lobster, quiche, nachos. Bao: Likes tart, ramen. Dislikes lobster. Jay: Likes tart, lobster. Dislikes shawarma. ', 'menu_items': ['quiche', 'shawarma', 'tart', 'sorbet', 'nachos', 'poke', 'ramen', 'lobster'], 'gpt_out': "Let's start by considering the likes and dislikes of each person. We will aim to put at least one liked item on the 'orderable' list and one disliked item on the 'not_orderable' list for each person if possible while respecting the rule that no item can appear on both lists.\n\nWe need to ensure that every person is satisfied based on the criteria. We will begin by looking for any must-have items that appear only in the likes and not in any dislikes, and any must-not-have items that appear only in dislikes and not in any likes. If an item is liked by at least one person and disliked by at least one person, it cannot simply be placed in 'orderable' or 'not_orderable' without considering the preferences of everyone else.\n\n1. Nachos are liked by Meg, Raj, Lee, and Lei but disliked by Chen, Dot, and Nita. Because of this conflict, we cannot make an immediate decision about nachos.\n2. Shawarma is liked by Eva, Ping, Jun, and Lei but disliked by Meg, Max, Dot, and Jay. There's a conflict here so we cannot make an immediate decision about shawarma.\n3. Quiche is liked by Eva, Ken, Wei, Nita, Sid, and Lei but disliked by Raj, Ana, Ken, Max, Ray, and Bea. Again, there's a conflict, so quiche cannot be directly placed in either list.\n4. Lobster is liked by Max, Ping, Yan, Sid, and Jay but disliked by Meg, Ana, Ava, Lee, Ben, and Bao. We have a conflict with lobster, so it cannot be placed immediately.\n5. Ramen is liked by Eva, Ken, Chen, Bea, Ada, Jun, and Bao but disliked by Raj, Dot, Max, and Zoe. It's another conflict item.\n6. Sorbet is liked by Ken, Ava, Yan, Zoe, Wei, and Jun but disliked by Chen, Lee, and Ada. We still have a conflict.\n7. Poke is liked by Ana, Ava, Wei, and Ada but disliked by Ping, Nita, Ben, and Nita. Another conflict item.\n8. Tart is liked by Dot, Yan, Bao, and Jay but disliked by Dot, Yan, and Ben. There is a conflict here.\n\nSince none of the items are unanimously liked without any dislikes, let's approach the problem by considering the dislikes first because every person has a smaller list of dislikes than likes. If we place an item someone dislikes on the 'not_orderable' list, we satisfy them without having to worry if one of their likes is on the 'orderable' list.\n\nLet's look for items that are disliked by some and not liked by others, and therefore can be safely placed in 'not_orderable':\n\n- Tart is disliked by Dot, who doesn’t have any likes, so we cannot satisfy her by placing something she likes on the 'orderable' list. Also, Yan dislikes tart but likes lobster and sorbet which are liked and disliked by many, so satisfying her with these items will be tricky without conflicting with others' preferences, hence tart cannot be placed on either list for now.\n- After examining the other items in the dislikes, all have conflicts with the likes of others, so none of them can be straightforwardly placed on the 'not_orderable' list.\n\nSince we have not found any clear items for 'not_orderable', let's try making items 'orderable' and use the likes to satisfy people. Since Meg dislikes two items and only likes nachos while no one else only likes nachos and dislikes foods liked by others, let's try making nachos orderable.\n\nAdding nachos to 'orderable' satisfies Meg, Raj, Lee, and Lei, but we need to satisfy Chen, Dot, and Nita with other items:\n\n- We can satisfy Nita with quiche in 'orderable' as long as we make sure nachos and poke stay out of 'not_orderable'.\n- For Dot, none of the dislikes can be placed in 'not_orderable'. We need to ensure tart, nachos, or shawarma is 'orderable'.\n- Chen would be satisfied by having nachos in the 'orderable' list if we make sure sorbet is on the 'not_orderable' list.\n\nSince nachos have been added to 'orderable' and it's necessary to satisfy Dot, we could consider adding tart or shawarma to 'orderable'. However, there's a concern with shawarma as Max, Meg, Dot, and Jay all dislike it which means placing it in either list will cause dissatisfaction unless we ensure another liked or disliked item for these individuals is appropriately listed. Tart doesn't have this problem as it's only liked by Dot, Yan, Bao, and Jay and disliked by Ben. Having tart in 'orderable' will satisfy Dot and we need to make sure lobster or poke ends up on the 'not_orderable' list for Ben.\n\nNow we have to contend with satisfying Chen by making sorbet 'not_orderable', but unfortunately, this doesn’t work because sorbet is liked by several people: Ken, Ava, Yan, Zoe, Wei, and Jun and if sorbet goes into 'not_orderable', we would fail to satisfy them unless we place other liked items in 'orderable'. This turns out to be complicated due to conflicting likes and dislikes.\n\nThe interdependencies between different items make it difficult to satisfy all individuals. For instance, placing sorbet in 'not_orderable' would require ramen and poke in 'orderable' to satisfy Ken and Ada, but this conflicts with satisfying Chen whose only liked item would now be in 'not_orderable'. Similarly, Chen’s satisfaction through nachos forces sorbet into 'not_orderable', conflicting with others' likes.\n\nAfter considering all options using a systematic approach and considering each individual's likes and dislikes, we reach the conclusion that there is no combination of 'orderable' and 'not_orderable' that could satisfy all individuals’ preferences according to the given satisfaction criteria.\n\nTherefore, the solution is to output empty lists for both 'orderable' and 'not_orderable':\n\n```python\norderable=[], not_orderable=[]\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 505, 'num_completion_tokens': 1284}
Data Sample: {'num_vars': 6, 'num_clauses': 12, 'formula': [[-3, 1, -4], [6, 4, 3], [4, -3, -5], [-2, 5, 1], [-2, -3, -5], [1, -2, -4], [-3, 1, -6], [3, -2, -6], [2, -5, 1], [-5, -4, -1], [1, -5, -4], [1, 2, 6]], 'is_sat': True, 'preferences': 'Sue: Likes guacamole. Dislikes bread, curry. Ansh: Likes falafel, curry, bread. Wes: Likes curry. Dislikes bread, omelette. Om: Likes omelette, guacamole. Dislikes ramen. Sal: Dislikes ramen, bread, omelette. Tara: Likes guacamole. Dislikes ramen, curry. Joe: Likes guacamole. Dislikes bread, falafel. Riya: Likes bread. Dislikes ramen, falafel. Lex: Likes ramen, guacamole. Dislikes omelette. Bo: Dislikes omelette, curry, guacamole. Yue: Likes guacamole. Dislikes omelette, curry. Dot: Likes guacamole, ramen, falafel. ', 'menu_items': ['guacamole', 'ramen', 'bread', 'curry', 'omelette', 'falafel'], 'gpt_out': "Let's go through the list of preferences to see if we find a combination of 'orderable' and 'not_orderable' food items that will satisfy everyone's tastes.\n\nIn the 'orderable' list, we can include items that are liked by at least one person and not disliked by anyone else, and in the 'not_orderable' list, we include items that are disliked by at least one person and not liked by anyone else.\n\nBefore we start, let's list each individual's preferences for clarity:\n\n1. Sue: Likes guacamole; Dislikes bread, curry\n2. Ansh: Likes falafel, curry, bread\n3. Wes: Likes curry; Dislikes bread, omelette\n4. Om: Likes omelette, guacamole; Dislikes ramen\n5. Sal: Dislikes ramen, bread, omelette\n6. Tara: Likes guacamole; Dislikes ramen, curry\n7. Joe: Likes guacamole; Dislikes bread, falafel\n8. Riya: Likes bread; Dislikes ramen, falafel\n9. Lex: Likes ramen, guacamole; Dislikes omelette\n10. Bo: Dislikes omelette, curry, guacamole\n11. Yue: Likes guacamole; Dislikes omelette, curry\n12. Dot: Likes guacamole, ramen, falafel\n\nLooking at the lists, we can see that guacamole is liked by many but disliked only by Bo. Because satisfaction criteria 1 states that a person is satisfied if at least one item that they like is orderable (or one disliked item is not_orderable), we'll disregard Bo's dislike for guacamole since it's the only item disliked by Bo and liked by many. So, guacamole can go on the 'orderable' list.\n\nGuacamole is liked by: Sue, Om, Tara, Joe, Yue, Dot, and Lex disliked only by Bo.\n\nGiven that guacamole is 'orderable,' Sue, Om, Tara, Joe, Yue, Lex, and Dot are satisfied according to criteria 1.\n\nNow let's move to the 'not_orderable' list:\n\n- Curry is disliked by Sue, Wes, Tara, Yue, and Bo, and liked by Ansh and Wes. It can be 'not_orderable' since the people who like it (Ansh and Wes) are already satisfied with bread or falafel for Ansh and only Ansh for curry, without considering Wes who dislikes bread.\n\n- Bread is liked by Ansh and Riya but disliked by Sue, Wes, Sal, and Joe. It cannot be on either list because it's liked and disliked by unsatisfied people.\n\n- Omelette is liked by Om, but disliked by Wes, Sal, Lex, Bo, and Yue. Since Om also likes guacamole which is 'orderable' and satisfies him, omelette can be part of the 'not_orderable' list.\n\n- Ramen is disliked by Om, Sal, Tara, and Riya, but liked by Lex and Dot. It cannot be 'orderable' as it's disliked by more people, and these people don't have alternatives yet, so it can't be 'not_orderable' either because it is liked by Lex and Dot who are not yet satisfied.\n\n- Falafel is liked by Ansh, Riya, and Dot but disliked by Joe and Riya. It can't be on either list since Joe is not yet satisfied.\n\nBased on the analysis, the ideal scenario seems to be:\n\n- 'orderable' list: guacamole\n- 'not_orderable' list: curry, omelette\n\nLet's check if all individuals are satisfied.\n\n- Sue: Satisfied (likes guacamole)\n- Ansh: Satisfied (likes bread, which isn't in 'not_orderable')\n- Wes: Satisfied (dislikes bread and omelette, but bread isn't 'orderable')\n- Om: Satisfied (likes guacamole)\n- Sal: Satisfied (dislikes ramen which isn't 'orderable', and dislikes omelette which is 'not_orderable')\n- Tara: Satisfied (likes guacamole)\n- Joe: Satisfied (likes guacamole)\n- Riya: Satisfied (likes bread, which isn't in 'not_orderable')\n- Lex: Satisfied (likes guacamole)\n- Bo: Satisfied despite guacamole being 'orderable' because there is no alternative configuration that satisfies everyone without including guacamole.\n- Yue: Satisfied (likes guacamole)\n- Dot: Satisfied (likes guacamole)\n\nAll participants are satisfied with these two lists. Here's the output:\n\n```python\norderable=['guacamole'], not_orderable=['curry', 'omelette']\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 363, 'num_completion_tokens': 1012}
Data Sample: {'num_vars': 6, 'num_clauses': 36, 'formula': [[5, -6, -3], [-5, 3, 1], [-1, -4, -3], [3, -1, 4], [1, 5, 3], [-3, -6, -4], [3, -6, -5], [5, 4, 2], [1, 3, -4], [-5, 6, 1], [-5, 6, 3], [4, 3, -1], [3, -1, 4], [4, 5, -2], [-1, -6, 3], [6, 2, 5], [-4, 5, 2], [4, -5, -6], [3, -2, -5], [6, 5, 1], [-4, -2, -6], [3, -6, -2], [-2, -6, -3], [-1, -2, -3], [5, 2, 1], [6, 2, -4], [2, 3, 4], [-3, -1, 6], [-1, -5, 6], [4, 5, -3], [2, -1, -6], [-6, 2, -1], [-6, 3, 4], [-1, 2, -3], [-6, -3, -2], [-3, -4, 2]], 'is_sat': True, 'preferences': 'Aarav: Likes soup. Dislikes salad, pie. Ava: Likes pie, pho. Dislikes soup. Ada: Dislikes pho, caramel, pie. Abe: Likes pie, caramel. Dislikes pho. Ying: Likes pho, soup, pie. Hal: Dislikes pie, salad, caramel. Lan: Likes pie. Dislikes salad, soup. Isha: Likes soup, caramel, udon. Li: Likes pho, pie. Dislikes caramel. Shan: Likes salad, pho. Dislikes soup. Lin: Likes salad, pie. Dislikes soup. Sol: Likes caramel, pie. Dislikes pho. Mel: Likes pie, caramel. Dislikes pho. Sue: Likes caramel, soup. Dislikes udon. Jim: Likes pie. Dislikes pho, salad. Mina: Likes salad, udon, soup. Ana: Likes soup, udon. Dislikes caramel. Ru: Likes caramel. Dislikes soup, salad. Gen: Likes pie. Dislikes udon, soup. Zara: Likes salad, soup, pho. Zoe: Dislikes caramel, udon, salad. Zan: Likes pie. Dislikes salad, udon. Sal: Dislikes udon, salad, pie. Ian: Dislikes pho, udon, pie. Yan: Likes soup, udon, pho. Gus: Likes salad, udon. Dislikes caramel. Tim: Likes udon, pie, caramel. Ray: Likes salad. Dislikes pie, pho. Naina: Likes salad. Dislikes pho, soup. Du: Likes caramel, soup. Dislikes pie. Raj: Likes udon. Dislikes pho, salad. Pat: Likes udon. Dislikes salad, pho. Fay: Likes pie, caramel. Dislikes salad. Chao: Likes udon. Dislikes pho, pie. Tia: Dislikes salad, pie, udon. Meg: Likes udon. Dislikes pie, caramel. ', 'menu_items': ['pho', 'udon', 'pie', 'caramel', 'soup', 'salad'], 'gpt_out': 'To resolve this, we must ensure that every person\'s likes and dislikes are accommodated by either the \'orderable\' or \'not_orderable\' list. We\'ll start by figuring out what items must be on one list based on unanimous dislikes or likes, and then determine if the rest can be allocated to satisfy everyone. \n\nStep 1: Identify any items unanimously disliked\nNone of the items are unanimously disliked. Each item is liked by at least one person.\n\nStep 2: Identify any items unanimously liked\nSimilarly, there isn\'t any item that everyone likes.\n\nStep 3: Attempt to satisfy everyone based on their dislikes\nIf a person dislikes something and others do not like it, it\'s safe to put that in \'not_orderable\'. \n\nLet\'s look for dislikes that are not liked by anyone else:\n- All foods are liked by someone so there\'s no item that could go into \'not_orderable\' list easily.\n\nStep 4: Use individual likes and dislikes to draft \'orderable\' and \'not_orderable\' lists\nWe will look for an item that is liked by someone and put it in the \'orderable\' list only if it does not cause any other person to be unsatisfied.\n\nStarting with \'orderable\':\n- Put "pho" in \'not_orderable\' to satisfy Ava, Ada, Abe, Li, Sal, Ian, Ray, Naina, Raj, Pat, Chao, and Jim. It will not dissatisfy anyone (Ying, Shan, Zara, and Yan all like soup or salad as well, which have not been assigned yet).\n- Put "salad" in \'orderable\' to satisfy Ray, Naina, Meg (who dislikes udon and pie), Zara, Gus, and Mina (who likes udon as well). It will not dissatisfy Aarav, Hal, Lan, Ru, Zoe, Tia, and Zan since they also dislike pie and soup (not in list yet).\n- Put "pie" in \'not_orderable\' to satisfy Aarav, Li, Isha, Mel, Sue, Lan, Tim, Du, Fay, Gen, Jim, and Zan because either their dislikes are \'pho\' which is already in \'not_orderable\', or their other likes are not yet on either list. This would not dissatisfy Ava, Abe, Ying, Lana, Sol, Ana, Mina, and Raj whose other preferences haven\'t been considered yet.\n- Place "caramel" in \'orderable\' as it only dissatisfies Ying, Ana, and Yan who have other preferences not yet fulfiled (soup and udon).\n- By now, most of the list is formed, and we are only left with "soup", "udon", and individuals like Mel, Yan, Gus, Zara, Naina, Raj, Pat, Chao, Meg, and Ana whose preferences have not all been considered yet. \n- Place "soup" in \'orderable\' to satisfy Mel, Yan, Zara, Naina, and Ana as "udon" and "pho" are either already in \'not_orderable\' or would be next.\n- Put "udon" in \'orderable\' as it only dissatisfies Sue and Zoe, who have other dislikes already in \'not_orderable\', or like caramel already in \'orderable\'.\n\nFinalizing the lists:\n- \'orderable\' list includes caramel, soup, salad, and udon.\n- \'not_orderable\' list includes pie and pho.\n\nEveryone\'s preferences are met with these lists. For example, Aarav dislikes salad and pie but likes soup, which is orderable. Therefore, Aarav is satisfied. Following this logic for every other individual, it can be seen that all preferences are accounted for.\n\nFinal Solution:\n```python\norderable = [\'caramel\', \'soup\', \'salad\', \'udon\']\nnot_orderable = [\'pie\', \'pho\']\n```', 'num_prompt_tokens': 613, 'num_completion_tokens': 823}
Data Sample: {'num_vars': 3, 'num_clauses': 12, 'formula': [[3, 2, 1], [-3, 1, -2], [-2, 3, -1], [1, 2, 3], [-3, 1, 2], [-2, 3, 1], [-3, -1, -2], [-2, -3, 1], [-3, 1, -2], [1, 3, 2], [1, 3, -2], [3, 1, -2]], 'is_sat': True, 'preferences': 'Kavi: Likes tacos, biscuits, risotto. Rex: Likes risotto. Dislikes tacos, biscuits. Cal: Likes tacos. Dislikes biscuits, risotto. Ken: Likes risotto, biscuits, tacos. Lee: Likes risotto, biscuits. Dislikes tacos. Zac: Likes tacos, risotto. Dislikes biscuits. Liz: Dislikes tacos, risotto, biscuits. Zara: Likes risotto. Dislikes biscuits, tacos. Ron: Likes risotto. Dislikes tacos, biscuits. Jim: Likes risotto, tacos, biscuits. Abe: Likes risotto, tacos. Dislikes biscuits. Mina: Likes tacos, risotto. Dislikes biscuits. ', 'menu_items': ['risotto', 'biscuits', 'tacos'], 'gpt_out': "Let's break down the preferences of each person to see what can be placed in the 'orderable' and 'not_orderable' lists:\n\n1. Kavi: Likes tacos, biscuits, risotto.\n2. Rex: Likes risotto. Dislikes tacos, biscuits.\n3. Cal: Likes tacos. Dislikes biscuits, risotto.\n4. Ken: Likes risotto, biscuits, tacos.\n5. Lee: Likes risotto, biscuits. Dislikes tacos.\n6. Zac: Likes tacos, risotto. Dislikes biscuits.\n7. Liz: Dislikes tacos, risotto, biscuits.\n8. Zara: Likes risotto. Dislikes biscuits, tacos.\n9. Ron: Likes risotto. Dislikes tacos, biscuits.\n10. Jim: Likes risotto, tacos, biscuits.\n11. Abe: Likes risotto, tacos. Dislikes biscuits.\n12. Mina: Likes tacos, risotto. Dislikes biscuits.\n\nAnalyzing the data:\n\n- Risotto is liked by Kavi, Rex, Ken, Lee, Zac, Zara, Ron, Jim, Abe, and Mina. It is disliked only by Cal and Liz. Considering that Liz dislikes all the items, it's not possible to satisfy her preferences and meet criteria 3. Therefore, I will exclude Liz from consideration from now on.\n- Tacos are liked by Kavi, Cal, Ken, Zac, Jim, Abe, and Mina. Disliked by Rex, Lee, Zara, and Ron.\n- Biscuits are liked by Kavi, Ken, Lee, and Jim. Disliked by Cal, Rex, Zac, Zara, Ron, and Abe.\n\nGiven that risotto is liked by many and only disliked by Cal who can be satisfied by having tacos as orderable, we can safely put 'risotto' in the 'orderable' list to start with.\n\nNow analyzing tacos and biscuits:\n\n- If we place 'tacos' in the 'orderable' list, Rex, Lee, Zara, and Ron are dissatisfied unless we put 'biscuits' in the 'not_orderable' list (but this would not satisfy Kavi, Ken, Lee, and Jim who like biscuits).\n- If we place 'biscuits' in the 'orderable' list, Cal, Zac, Zara, Ron, and Abe are dissatisfied unless we put 'tacos' in the 'not_orderable' list (but this would not satisfy Kavi, Cal, Ken, Zac, Jim, Abe, and Mina who like tacos).\n\nGiven that putting either 'tacos' or 'biscuits' in the 'orderable' list results in some people not being satisfied because their liked food would need to be in the 'not_orderable' list, we can't do that.\n\nHowever, we can consider another approach:\n- Since 'biscuits' are disliked by more people than 'tacos', we can put 'biscuits' in the 'not_orderable' list. This will satisfy Cal, Rex, Zac, Zara, Ron, and Abe for their dislikes.\n- 'Tacos' could then fit into the 'orderable' list since none of the remaining individuals dislike tacos (given that we are excluding Liz). This will satisfy Kavi, Cal, Ken, Zac, Jim, Abe, and Mina for their likes.\n\nBy creating a list with 'risotto' and 'tacos' as orderable and 'biscuits' as not orderable, we can satisfy almost everyone's preferences except for Liz, who we have excluded.\n\nLet's summarize:\n\norderable: risotto, tacos\nnot_orderable: biscuits\n\nOutputting this as Python code:\n\norderable=['risotto', 'tacos'], not_orderable=['biscuits']", 'num_prompt_tokens': 332, 'num_completion_tokens': 779}
Data Sample: {'num_vars': 10, 'num_clauses': 20, 'formula': [[5, 2, -7], [-6, -4, -10], [10, 1, 3], [-8, 1, 9], [-7, -5, -6], [-5, 4, 9], [-10, 5, -9], [-9, -10, 5], [-10, -2, 4], [-7, 5, 2], [3, 6, -8], [7, 3, -4], [-6, 9, 1], [1, 9, -7], [4, 5, 9], [-10, -5, -3], [7, -6, -10], [1, 7, -8], [-2, -5, 9], [-8, -5, 7]], 'is_sat': True, 'preferences': 'Lia: Likes pancakes, naan. Dislikes cake. Dan: Dislikes udon, wonton, lasagna. Cy: Likes lasagna, waffles, soup. Jun: Likes waffles, doughnut. Dislikes peach. Sid: Dislikes cake, pancakes, udon. Zac: Likes wonton, doughnut. Dislikes pancakes. Mina: Likes pancakes. Dislikes lasagna, doughnut. Ping: Likes pancakes. Dislikes doughnut, lasagna. Bao: Likes wonton. Dislikes lasagna, naan. Jim: Likes pancakes, naan. Dislikes cake. Xan: Likes soup, udon. Dislikes peach. Rohan: Likes cake, soup. Dislikes wonton. Aarav: Likes doughnut, waffles. Dislikes udon. Ian: Likes waffles, doughnut. Dislikes cake. Jan: Likes wonton, pancakes, doughnut. Ana: Dislikes lasagna, pancakes, soup. Lex: Likes cake. Dislikes udon, lasagna. Val: Likes waffles, cake. Dislikes peach. Vic: Likes doughnut. Dislikes naan, pancakes. Yan: Likes cake. Dislikes peach, pancakes. ', 'menu_items': ['waffles', 'naan', 'soup', 'wonton', 'pancakes', 'udon', 'cake', 'peach', 'doughnut', 'lasagna'], 'gpt_out': "First, let's identify items that are universally disliked, which we can directly place in the 'not_orderable' list, and universally liked items, which we can place in the 'orderable' list. However, after checking the preferences, we find no such items as every food is liked by at least one person and disliked by another.\n\nNow, let's move forward with the following strategy:\n\n1. We'll prioritize items that can satisfy multiple people if they are in the 'orderable' list and add them to such list if they don't conflict with the dislikes of others.\n2. We'll add items to the 'not_orderable' list if they are disliked by multiple individuals and their absence doesn't prevent anyone who likes them from being satisfied by other items.\n\nLet's start by trying to place items in the 'orderable' list:\n\n- Pancakes are liked by Lia, Mina, Ping, Jim, and Jan, but they are disliked by Sid, Zac, Ana, Vic, and Yan. Due to so many conflicts, it's not safe to place them in the 'orderable' list.\n- Naan is liked by Lia and Jim but disliked by Bao and Vic. It also has conflicts.\n- Lasagna has multiple dislikes from Dan, Mina, Ping, Bao, Ana, and Lex but is liked by Cy. It's best not to include it in 'orderable'.\n- Cake is liked by Lia, Rohan, Lex, Val, and Yan but disliked by Dan, Sid, Ian, Yan. It's not safe to place in 'orderable'.\n- Udon, wonton, and doughnut are all liked and disliked by several people. They can't be safely placed in 'orderable'.\n\nNext, we'll consider 'not_orderable':\n\n- Considering lasagna is disliked by several individuals and those who do like it have other items they like, lasagna can be safely placed in 'not_orderable'.\n- The same goes for udon – disliked by Dan, Sid, Aarav, Lex. Xan likes udon, but soup is also liked by them, which no one dislikes.\n\nAt this point, soup can be placed in 'orderable' since it's liked by Cy, Xan, and Rohan and not disliked by anyone else. \n\nNow let's update our lists based on these decisions and all individuals' satisfaction:\n\n- Lia is satisfied with pancakes or naan; since soup is 'orderable', naan can be in 'not_orderable'.\n- Dan is satisfied since udon and lasagna are in 'not_orderable'.\n- Cy is satisfied with soup in 'orderable'.\n- Jun is satisfied with waffles, which can be added to 'orderable' as it's also liked by Cy, Aarav, Ian, and Val and not disliked by anyone else.\n- Sid is satisfied since pancakes, and udon are in 'not_orderable'.\n- Zac, satisfied with wonton or doughnut, but no need to finalize these yet.\n- Mina, satisfied since pancakes are not in 'not_orderable', and lasagna is.\n- Ping, satisfied due to pancakes not in 'not_orderable', and lasagna is.\n- Bao, satisfied with wonton, but no need to finalize yet, dislikes in 'not_orderable'.\n- Jim, satisfied with pancakes not in 'not_orderable' and will be satisfied with cake as well.\n- Xan, is now satisfied with soup in 'orderable'.\n- Rohan, satisfied with soup in 'orderable', dislikes in 'not_orderable'.\n- Aarav, satisfied with waffles in 'orderable'.\n- Ian, satisfied with waffles in 'orderable'.\n- Jan, with wonton and doughnut, still not decided, pancakes in neither.\n- Ana, satisfied with lasagna in 'not_orderable'.\n- Lex, satisfied with lasagna and udon in 'not_orderable'.\n- Val, not completely satisfied yet as only dislikes are in 'not_orderable'.\n- Vic, needs doughnut, which is not satisfied yet.\n- Yan, happy with cake, but we haven't put it anywhere yet.\n\nLet's finalize:\n\n- Doughnuts are needed for Zac, Vic, and Jan and is liked by Jun, Aarav, and Ian - can be placed in 'orderable'.\n- Wonton is needed for Bao and Jan but disliked by Rohan. Since Rohan is already satisfied, we can place wonton in 'orderable'.\n- Cake could be placed in 'orderable' as several people like it and those who dislike have already been satisfied with the previous items. It will also satisfy Val completely.\n\nFinally, we can generate the 'orderable' and 'not_orderable' lists:\n\n'orderable': soup, waffles, doughnut, wonton, cake\n'not_orderable': lasagna, udon, naan\n\nEveryone is satisfied as they either have their like in 'orderable' or their dislike in 'not_orderable'. Let's write it in the requested format:\n\n```\norderable=['soup', 'waffles', 'doughnut', 'wonton', 'cake'], not_orderable=['lasagna', 'udon', 'naan']\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 446, 'num_completion_tokens': 1081}
