n1. Some people like 'guacamole' while several others dislike it. If we placed it in 'orderable', it would not satisfy the dislikes of Geet, Pam, Isha, Abe, Kim, Rohan, Qi, Ron, Gen, and Neil. Putting it in 'not_orderable' would satisfy dislikes but would conflict with the likes of Taj, Sue, Mei, Riya, Leo, Cy, Nic, May, Qi, Ron, Om, and Wes. So, we can put 'guacamole' directly in 'not_orderable'.\n\n2. Next, let's look at 'tandoori'. People who like it include Sue, Mei, Riya, Leo, Nic, May, Qi, Yue, Gen, Neil. Those who dislike it are Tara, Pam, Kim, Ron, Wes. By placing 'tandoori' on the 'orderable' list, we can satisfy both the likes of some and the dislikes of others who dislike 'hummus' or 'guacamole' which are already addressed (for example Tara is satisfied because 'burrito' is not on the 'orderable' list and Ron is satisfied because 'guacamole' is on 'not_orderable').\n\n3. Many people dislike 'hummus', so it's good to place 'hummus' on the 'not_orderable' list.\n\n4. A number of people dislike 'pizza', but others like it. Taj, Tara, Ken, Nic, Mia, Om, and Yue like it, whereas Abe, Kim, Rohan, Qi, Yue, Neil dislike it. By placing 'pizza' on the 'orderable' list, we satisfy the likes of many while the dislikes are either already satisfied (Abe, Kim, Qi, and Neil by 'guacamole' or 'hummus' being 'not_orderable') or by having 'tandoori' or 'guacamole' in the other list (Rohan and Yue).\n\n5. 'Burrito' is liked by Taj, Mia, Riya, and Yue, while it is disliked by Geet, Tara, Isha, Ken, Cy, Neil, and Wes. All the disliking individuals are already satisfied by something on the 'not_orderable' list except for Cy, but Cy is satisfied because 'pizza' is on the 'orderable' list. Therefore, we can place 'burrito' on the 'orderable' list.\n\nGiven these considerations, we can propose the following lists:\n\n'orderable' list: 'tandoori', 'pizza', 'burrito'\n'not_orderable' list: 'guacamole', 'hummus'\n\nLet's verify if all participants are satisfied:\n\n- Anyone who likes