 one person with a conflicting opinion.\n\nGiven the complexity of the task, we will consider the strategy of looking for \'orderable\' items that are liked by some and not disliked by others or \'not_orderable\' items that are disliked by some and not liked by others. Let\'s start by looking for an item that is liked by someone and not disliked by anyone else.\n\n1. "Curry" is liked by many but also disliked by "May" and "Ned" so it can\'t be \'orderable\'.\n2. "Burger" is liked by many but disliked by "Leo" and "Ty", so it can\'t be \'orderable\' either.\n3. "Sushi" is liked by some but disliked by many, which makes it\'s a candidate for \'not_orderable\'.\n4. "Cheesecake" is liked by many but disliked by "Jaya", "Shan", "Sal", "Ansh", and "Min", so it can\'t be \'orderable\'.\n5. "Couscous" and "Lasagna" both have strong opinions on both sides as well.\n6. "Paneer" is liked by several but disliked by "Liv", "Ned", "Zara", "Hu", and "Ying".\n7. "Brownies" are liked by many, and the only person who dislikes it is "Zan".\n\nFrom these observations, no food item can be classified as orderable without displeasing someone. However, in terms of \'not_orderable\', "Sushi" remains the most widely disliked and less commonly liked food, which suggests we could satisfy the criterion for those who dislike "Sushi" by putting it on the \'not_orderable\' list, under the assumption that none of the \'Sushi\' dislikers only likes \'Sushi\'.\n\nWe will first attempt to make "Sushi" \'not_orderable\':\n\nMost dislikers of sushi have other likes except "Leo". Therefore, "Sushi" can\'t be \'not_orderable\' alone without displeasing "Leo". However, if we can find an \'orderable\' item for "Leo", we can place "Sushi" on the \'not_orderable\' list.\n\nSince "Leo" likes "curry" and "curry" is not disliked by anyone who likes "sushi", let\'s try making "curry" \'orderable\'. This would meet "Leo\'s" requirements and would not cause any direct conflicts. However, "curry" is disliked by "May