ates for 'not_orderable'.\n\nMina likes tandoori, couscous, and churros. This conflicts heavily with the dislikes we’ve seen, and since Mina likes all three items Ken dislikes, at least one needs to be 'orderable' to satisfy both, which is currently impossible (creating a potential point of failure for finding a solution).\n\nHal, Ravi, Leo, and Hui dislike tandoori and/or churros but like cake. We can consider cake 'orderable' to potentially satisfy these individuals without conflicting with anyone's dislikes.\n\nTaj likes couscous, muffins, and churros, providing no new information about 'orderable' items yet, but muffins and churros are possible 'not_orderable' due to other's dislikes.\n\nSid likes cake and tandoori but dislikes paneer. We can't make a decision about tandoori yet, but paneer could be 'not_orderable' based on Sid and Ru's dislikes (Ru also dislikes muffins and paneer).\n\nConsidering the information above, let's try to find a list that can satisfy everyone:\n\nWe put cake on the 'orderable' list to satisfy Hal, Ravi, Leo, Hui, Sid (and potentially others if they don't dislike it).\n\nNext, considering the widespread dislike for muffins (Bo and Ru), we can tentatively place muffins on the 'not_orderable' list. \n\nHowever, we cannot put churros on the 'not_orderable' list because Taj likes them. Thus, we must revisit our decision if we find no other way to satisfy all individuals.\n\nCouscous is liked by Mina, Qi, Bo, and Taj, and disliked by Ula, Ken, Leo, and Hui. The likes and dislikes are in conflict.\n\nPaneer is liked by Ula, and Qi but disliked by Ru and Sid. Putting paneer as 'not_orderable' could potentially be a solution.\n\nTandoori is liked by Mina, Ru, and Sid, but disliked by Ken, Hal, Ravi, and Leo. The widespread dislike makes it a candidate for 'not_orderable'.\n\nFinding an ordering that satisfies everyone is becoming complex, as putting tandoori, couscous, or paneer on either 'orderable' or 'not_orderable' appears to conflict with someone's preference. At this analysis stage, it seems the conflict is centering around couscous and tandoori. We know cake has to be 'orderable' so far with no conflicts arising.\n\n1. To satisfy Ula, we leave paneer 'orderable'. Qi will also be satisfied.\n2. To satisfy Bo, we do not put muffins or churros on the 'orderable' list.\n3. To satisfy Ken, we must put tandoori, couscous, or churros on the 'not_orderable' list. We can put churros and couscous as 'not_orderable', but considering Mina can't be satisfied with those choices, we must reconsider.\n4. For Sid, paneer must be 'not_orderable'.\n\nA possible solution now looks like:\n- Cake is 'orderable' to satisfy Hal, Ravi, Leo, Hui, and Sid.\n- Muffins are 'not_orderable' to satisfy Bo and Ru.\n- Paneer is 'not_orderable' to satisfy Sid and Ru, while Ula will be satisfied with cake as 'orderable'.\n- Churros cannot be decided yet.\n\nThis situation leaves us with the following conclusions:\n- Tandoori can't be 'orderable', as it is disliked by many.\n- Couscous is a challenge because it's liked and disliked with equal intensity.\n\nLet's check if this satisfies everyone:\n\n- Ula: Cake 'orderable' satisfies her.\n- Qi: Cake 'orderable' satisfies her.\n- Bo: Couscous 'orderable' and muffins 'not_orderable' satisfies him.\n- Ken: Couscous 'not_orderable' satisfies him.\n- Mina: Cake 'orderable' satisfies her (won't be satisfied with couscous 'not_orderable', but with another liked item 'orderable', she is satisfied).\n- Hal: Cake 'orderable' satisfies him.\n- Ravi: Cake 'orderable' satisfies him.\n- Leo: Cake 'orderable' satisfies him.\n- Hui: Cake 'orderable' satisfies her.\n- Taj: Couscous 'not_orderable' won't satisfy him, and he isn't satisfied by cake, so this is a problem.\n- Sid: Cake 'orderable' and paneer 'not_orderable' satisfy him.\n- Ru: Tandoori 'not_orderable' satisfies her.\n\nWe can't satisfy Taj because couscous and churros can't be 'orderable' due to others' dislikes. Since we don’t have an a