ikes and dislikes to ascertain its place.\n\nSince no food item is unequivocally liked or disliked by all, we need to negotiate between likes and dislikes, ensuring that everyone is satisfied.\n\nDue to the complexity of the constraints, we can't rely solely on the most obvious observations. Instead, we must try to identify items that satisfy the largest number of people without causing dissatisfaction. Let's see if there are any items that are liked by individuals who dislike only one item. For example:\n\n- Bruschetta is liked by those who dislike fewer items, so we might consider placing it in `orderable`. However, this would only make Gus dissatisfied if we don't place an item they dislike in `not_orderable`. We can satisfy Gus by placing brownies (disliked only by a few) in `not_orderable`. This satisfies a larger number of individuals, such as Wes, Liz, Lex, May, Arun, and others.\n\nNow let's switch to items more commonly disliked, like salad and baklava:\n- Salad is not so controversial and is mostly liked, hence potentially considered `orderable`. However, it is the only item that Eva likes, so if salad is `orderable`, we need another item Eva dislikes to be `not_orderable`.\n\n- Baklava is disliked by numerous people. Since those who like baklava like other items too, we put baklava in `not_orderable` to satisfy those who dislike it.\n\nGiven the above, let's see if we can make a preliminary list and check for satisfaction:\n\n`orderable` candidates: salad, bruschetta\n`not_orderable` candidates: baklava, brownies\n\nWe place salad and bruschetta in `orderable` because the people who like these items either have no other options or many other likes.  We put brownies and baklava in `not_orderable` because this would satisfy individuals who dislike these items and there are other items liked by people who like brownies and baklava.\n\nNow let's check if everyone is satisfied:\n\n- Salad is `orderable`, satisfying Jay, Vic, Sky, Rob, Isha, Naina, Oli, Du, Ty,