 can satisfy everyone by placing "fries" in \'orderable\':\n\n- Individuals who like fries and don\'t dislike any other orderable items: satisfied.\n- Individuals who dislike fries: would need another disliked item in \'not_orderable\' or another liked item in \'orderable\'.\n\nNow, let\'s examine placing "caramel" in \'not_orderable\':\n\n- Individuals who dislike caramel: satisfied.\n- Individuals who like caramel: would be satisfied as long as fries are \'orderable\'.\n\nSo, these two decisions seem to satisfy a significant number of preferences from both likes and dislikes. All other items, ratatouille, tacos, sorbet, and waffles, appear to be liked and disliked to some degree, but these preferences are less universal than those for fries and caramel.\n\nNow, let\'s verify if we can satisfy everyone with "fries" in \'orderable\' and "caramel" in \'not_orderable\':\n\n- Those who like only fries and/or caramel will be satisfied since fries are \'orderable\' and they\'re already satisfied with caramel being \'not_orderable\'.\n- Those who dislike only fries and/or caramel will be satisfied since their disliked item caramel is in \'not_orderable\'.\n- For individuals with preferences including other items, as long as they like fries or dislike caramel, they will be satisfied.\n\nNote that no individual only dislikes "fries" without disliking "caramel" or liking at least one other item. Similarly, no individual only likes "caramel" without liking fries or another item that isn\'t universally disliked. This means we are covered and don\'t have additional constraints for selecting items for \'orderable\' and \'not_orderable\' lists.\n\nFrom the remaining items, we must decide without conflict:\n\n- Ratatouille is liked by many but disliked by a few.\n- Tacos are liked by many but disliked by a few.\n- Sorbet and waffles are liked by some but disliked by others.\n\nBy placing "caramel" in \'not_orderable\', all individuals who dislike it are satisfied. Now, every other