s:\n\nLikes:\n\n- tart: Naina, Chao, Ivy, Ansh, Abe, Lei, Tom, Ying, Feng, Cy, Min\n- frittata: Naina, Chao, Mia, Ru, Ying, Mina, Cal, Min\n- shrimps: Geet, Hal, Lan, Eva\n- bruschetta: Geet, Ivy, Ansh, Ravi, Tom, Sid, Feng, Joe, Bo, Oli, Min\n- cheese: Geet, Ben, Shan, Ru, Hal, Mina, Cy, Lan, Neil\n- udon: Ansh, Sky, Hal, Sid, Feng, Oli\n- None (likes not affecting choices): Gus, Pam\n\nDislikes:\n\n- shrimps: Naina, Pam, Ravi, Ru, Cal, Bo, Eva\n- bruschetta: Chao, Gus, Sky, Mina, Neil, Abe\n- udon: Gus, Abe, Lei, Joe\n- tart: Ben, Shan, Mia, Gus, Lei\n- frittata: Ivy, Pam, Shan, Lei, Joe, Bo, Neil, Eva, Lan\n- cheese: Sid, Sky, Eva, Oli\n\nThe goal here is to find food items that some people like and no one dislikes to populate the \'orderable\' list and those that sufficient people dislike so we can move them to \'not_orderable\' list.\n\nFrom the dislikes, we notice that "tart" and "frittata" can\'t be on our orderable list because they have strong dislikes from multiple people.\n\nLet\'s move "tart" and "frittata" to \'not_orderable\'; this will satisfy all the individuals who dislike those items (Gus, Ben, Shan, Mia, Ivy, Pam, Lei, Joe, Bo, Neil, Eva, Lan).\n\nSince Gus dislikes three items \'udon\', \'tart\', and \'bruschetta\' and is satisfied by \'tart\' being in \'not_orderable\', we do not have to worry about his preferences anymore.\n\nNext, let\'s look at "shrimps" since there are a number of likes and dislikes. We cannot satisfy both of those who like and dislike this item. But, because we can satisfy all those who dislike "shrimps" by other means, we opt not to put it in either list just yet.\n\nWe\'re left with "cheese", "bruschetta", and "udon" to consider:\n\n- "cheese" has several likes and only dislikes from Sid, Sky, Eva, and Oli who are all satisfied with "frittata" in the not_orderable list.\n\n- "bruschetta" can\'t be in the \'orderable\' because there is a considerable number of individuals who dislike it. However, some individuals like "bruschetta" and have no other likes, so it should not be in the \'not_orderable\' either.\n\n- "udon" is preferred by a few, but a few also dislike it, so we cannot put it on either list without making some people unhappy.\n\nWith this in mind, we can add "cheese" to the \'orderable\' list to satisfy those who like it (Shan, Ru, Hal, Mina, Cy, Lan, Neil).\n\nNow everyone who likes "shrimps", "bruschetta", and "udon" also likes "cheese" or dislikes "tart" or "frittata", so they are satisfied. Anyone else who is left must dislike "shrimps", "bruschetta", or "udon" or like "cheese", so they are also satisfied.\n\nTherefore, our final solution is:\n\n- orderable: "cheese"\n- not_orderable: "tart", "frittata"\n\nIn Python code format:\n\n```python\norderable=["cheese"], not_orderable=["tart", "frittata"]\n```', 'num_prompt_tokens': 625, 'num_completion_tokens': 843}
Data Sample: {'num_vars': 7, 'num_clauses': 14, 'formula': [[-5, 4, -7], [5, 7, 1], [-2, 6, 7], [-6, -7, -4], [6, 2, 5], [2, -5, 3], [7, -3, -6], [7, -5, -2], [-4, -1, 3], [-4, -3, -5], [-4, 2, 6], [-4, 2, -7], [3, 6, 5], [-3, 4, -5]], 'is_sat': True, 'preferences': 'Rina: Likes cheese. Dislikes bread, fries. Joy: Likes bread, fries, toast. Pia: Likes roti, fries. Dislikes poke. Tom: Dislikes roti, fries, cheese. Mel: Likes roti, poke, bread. Lia: Likes poke, korma. Dislikes bread. Ula: Likes fries. Dislikes korma, roti. Kim: Likes fries. Dislikes bread, poke. Sky: Likes korma. Dislikes cheese, toast. Bo: Dislikes cheese, korma, bread. Xiu: Likes poke, roti. Dislikes cheese. Arun: Likes poke. Dislikes cheese, fries. Abe: Likes korma, roti, bread. Kai: Likes cheese. Dislikes korma, bread. ', 'menu_items': ['toast', 'poke', 'korma', 'cheese', 'bread', 'roti', 'fries'], 'gpt_out': "Let's approach the task by creating two lists: one for 'orderable' items that participants like and one for 'not_orderable' items that participants dislike. We will then exclude any conflicts where an item is both liked and disliked. Here's the list of likes and dislikes:\n\nLikes:\n- Rina: cheese\n- Joy: bread, fries, toast\n- Pia: roti, fries\n- Mel: roti, poke, bread\n- Lia: poke, korma\n- Ula: fries\n- Kim: fries\n- Sky: korma\n- Xiu: poke, roti\n- Arun: poke\n- Abe: korma, roti, bread\n- Kai: cheese\n\nDislikes:\n- Rina: bread, fries\n- Pia: poke\n- Tom: roti, fries, cheese\n- Lia: bread\n- Ula: korma, roti\n- Kim: bread, poke\n- Sky: cheese, toast\n- Bo: cheese, korma, bread\n- Xiu: cheese\n- Arun: cheese, fries\n- Abe: korma, roti, bread\n- Kai: korma, bread\n\nNext, we find items that are exclusively in either like or dislike list without conflicts with other participants.\n\nConflicting items (liked by some and disliked by others):\n- cheese (liked by Rina, Kai; disliked by Tom, Sky, Bo, Xiu, Arun)\n- bread (liked by Joy, Mel, Abe; disliked by Rina, Lia, Kim, Bo, Kai)\n- fries (liked by Joy, Pia, Ula, Kim; disliked by Rina, Tom, Arun)\n- roti (liked by Pia, Mel, Xiu, Abe; disliked by Tom, Ula, Abe)\n- poke (liked by Mel, Lia, Xiu, Arun; disliked by Pia, Kim)\n- korma (liked by Lia, Sky, Abe; disliked by Ula, Bo, Kai)\n\nNon-conflicting items:\n- toast (liked by Joy; disliked by nobody)\n- cheese (liked only by Rina and Kai)\n\nLet's first satisfy the non-conflicting like (toast). This will satisfy Joy and does not cause any conflicts.\n\nThe item cheese is a conflicting item but it's liked by multiple people. By placing it on the 'orderable' list, it satisfies several participants. Those who dislike cheese only dislike cheese (Sky, Bo, Xiu, Tom, Arun) must then have their disliked items on the 'not_orderable' list. We proceed with this.\n\nNow, we place the disliked items of those who dislike cheese but like other conflicting items into the 'not_orderable.' This gives us:\n- not_orderable: bread, fries, roti, poke, korma\n\nBy doing so, the following people will be satisfied since at least one of their disliked items is not orderable:\n- Sky (toast on orderable, cheese and toast not on not_orderable)\n- Bo (cheese, korma, bread on not_orderable)\n- Xiu (bread, fries, roti, poke, korma on not_orderable)\n- Tom (roti, fries, cheese on not_orderable)\n- Arun (fries, cheese on not_orderable)\n\nNow we need to check if we can satisfy those who have their likes in the conflicting list with the current 'not_orderable' items.\n\n- Rina: likes cheese (orderable) and dislikes bread, fries (not_orderable)\n- Joy: likes toast (orderable) and dislikes none\n- Pia: likes fries (not_orderable), so we cannot directly satisfy Pia by likes, but none of dislikes are on the orderable list\n- Mel: likes bread (not_orderable), poke (not_orderable), so we cannot directly satisfy Mel by likes, but none of dislikes are on the orderable list\n- Lia: likes poke (not_orderable), satisfied by dislikes\n- Ula: likes fries (not_orderable), but none of dislikes are on the orderable list\n- Kim: likes fries (not_orderable), but dislikes poke (not_orderable) satisfies her\n- Abe: likes roti (not_orderable), korma (not_orderable), but none of dislikes are on the orderable list\n\nChecking for remaining items:\n- There is no item left that is liked by everyone remaining without a satisfying condition and not disliked by someone else. Therefore, their satisfaction has to come from dislikes category rather than likes. \n\nAll participants are either satisfied by having at least one liked item in the 'orderable' list or by having at least one disliked item in the 'not_orderable' list. Therefore, the final lists are:\n\n- orderable: [toast, cheese]\n- not_orderable: [bread, fries, roti, poke, korma]\n\nExpressed in the requested Python format:\n\n```python\norderable=['toast', 'cheese'], not_orderable=['bread', 'fries', 'roti', 'poke', 'korma']\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 361, 'num_completion_tokens': 1073}
