able\' that can satisfy the unsatisfied people.\n\nLet\'s look for those with unique dislikes that no one else likes:\n- Yue dislikes \'shrimps\', \'caramel\', and \'shawarma\', all liked by others, so she can\'t be satisfied via \'not_orderable\'.\n- Gus dislikes \'cake\', which is liked by others, so he can\'t be satisfied via \'not_orderable\'.\n- Geet dislikes \'caramel\', \'churros\', and \'pancakes\', all liked by others, so she can\'t be satisfied via \'not_orderable\'.\n\nThere\'s no clear path to satisfy everyone. We have several conflicts:\n- \'cake\' is liked by many but is a dislike for only one person who also dislikes another item.\n- The same applies to \'caramel\', \'shrimps\', and the other items.\n\nWithout a clear candidate that can be disliked and placed in \'not_orderable\' without causing dissatisfaction to anyone, and because at least one like for every individual is already in a conflict with another\'s dislike, we are unable to create a list that will satisfy all participants.\n\nTherefore, per the rules, we must output empty lists for both \'orderable\' and \'not_orderable\'.\n\nThe solution is: `orderable=[], not_orderable=[]`.', 'num_prompt_tokens': 651, 'num_completion_tokens': 1131}
