m a deadlock because a person likes one item and dislikes another item that someone else has opposite feelings for:\n\n1. If we put "pho" in the \'not_orderable\' list, we satisfy Hu, Mei, Ty, Wei, Ray, Jon, Dan, Mina, Ru, Sal, Jim, Hui, Hal. However, if we do this, Lex, Rina, Eva, Ula, Taj, and Ula will not be satisfied (a large group).\n\nThis is not a good start because "pho" is liked by many individuals whose only like is "pho" and cannot be compensated by any disliked item in \'not_orderable\'. Hence, "pho" should be in \'orderable\'.\n\n2. When we consider "salad", placing it in \'not_orderable\' will satisfy Rob, May, Ray, Ty, Wei, Ru, Sal, Joy, and Gus. However, Qi, Sid, Joe, Ken, Jon, Eva, and Rina will be dissatisfied. In addition, "salad" is the only item liked by Ken and Eva; thus, "salad" should be in \'orderable\'.\n\n3. "Macaron" being in the \'not_orderable\' list would satisfy Yue, Abe, Rina, Gus, Lou, Taj, Sid, Xan, and Hal. But, Vic, Xiu, Joe, Mina, Jay, and Gus would be dissatisfied. Given that we cannot satisfy Xan\'s likes, and "macaron" is his only dislike, we will have to put "macaron" in the \'not_orderable\' list and satisfy him.\n\n4. For "kebab", if we put it in \'not_orderable\', we satisfy Lee, Mia, Mei, Jay, Wei, Lou, and Xiu. However, Hal, Raj, Xan, Abe, and Ada will be dissatisfied. We can\'t place it in \'orderable\' either, as Jay\'s only like is "pho" and we have to satisfy him by putting "kebab" in \'not_orderable\', since we have already decided on "macaron" being in \'not_orderable\'.\n\nThus, by these moves, we\'re already seeing a list form where "pho" and "salad" would be \'orderable\', and "macaron" and "kebab" would have to be \'not_orderable\' to satisfy a majority. But, by placing "kebab" in \'not_orderable\', we are failing to satisfy Hal, Raj, Abe, and Ada, as their only liked items are in \'not_orderable\'.\n\nContinuing this line of reasoning, we can see that no matter how we proceed, we will have some individuals who will be unsatisfied due to the interlocking likes and dislikes that cannot be resolved in a way that satisfies everyone. Therefore, we must conclude that there is no combination of \'orderable\' and \'not_orderable\' that can satisfy everyone\'s requirements based on the given criteria. \n\nConsequently, the output should be:\n\n```python\norderable=[], not_orderable=[]\n```', 'num_prompt_tokens': 722, 'num_completion_tokens': 961}
