flict.\n\nFor example, if "vindaloo" is liked by Ravi and disliked by Wei, we continue to check for other preferences. Since "vindaloo" is liked by multiple people and disliked by multiple people, it would go into the conflicted set:\n```python\nconflicted.add("vindaloo")\n```\n\nWe would need to do this for all items mentioned by the group of individuals. However, given the large number of individuals and their preferences, this task would be laborious to perform manually. Ideally, we would use a program to automate this process of finding conflicts and assigning foods to \'orderable\', \'not_orderable\', or \'conflicted\' lists.\n\nFor now, to provide a manual approach to this task, I\'ll use logical reasoning to resolve conflicts and try to find at least one combination that satisfies everyone. If no such combination can be found, then the result will be empty lists as stipulated in criteria 4.\n\nLet\'s analyze the foods that are central to most conflicts: "vindaloo", "cheese", "omelette", "roti", "gelato", "nachos", "tandoori" and "baklava". It\'s possible that none of these can go into either \'orderable\' or \'not_orderable\' because of the conflicts.\n\nThis means we need to identify foods from the \'likes\' and \'dislikes\' that aren\'t as widely conflicted. "kebab" seems like a candidate for an \'orderable\' item because it is not widely disliked (Min, Shan, and Mel are the only ones who dislike it, but we could satisfy them with other foods they dislike). If everyone who likes "kebab" also likes or dislikes something that is not conflicted, we can add "kebab" to the \'orderable\' list.\n\nWithout a specific algorithmic approach and the whole dataset to process this information, I cannot provide an exact answer. This problem requires a considerable amount of computation that is beyond manual methods to assure that all individuals\' preferences are respected while also not violating any of the given criteria.\n\nIn a typical programming scenario, one would 