oori is liked by Ansh, Vic, and Isha but disliked by Lin and Aarav.\n\nWe can attempt to satisfy the dislikes by making toast, lasagna, and tandoori 'not_orderable':\n- Toast disliked by 5 participants and liked by only Vic.\n- Lasagna disliked by Lin, Vic, and Jun and liked by several others.\n- Tandoori disliked by Lin and Aarav and liked by several as well.\n\nThis would satisfy the dislikes of everyone who dislikes toast, lasagna, and tandoori and doesn't directly conflict with anyone's likes because we can satisfy the other likes with alternative options. However, to solidify this list, we must ensure that there's at least one item from the likes of each person in the 'orderable' list. Let's check that:\n\n- Shan (likes lasagna) will not be satisfied because lasagna is 'not_orderable'.\n- Ana (likes pho) can be satisfied with pho being 'orderable'.\n- Lan (likes roti, kebab) can be satisfied with roti being 'orderable'.\n- Tia (likes roti) can be satisfied with roti being 'orderable'.\n- Ravi (likes kebab) will not be satisfied because kebab is 'not_orderable'.\n- Ansh (likes tandoori, pho) can be satisfied with pho being 'orderable'.\n- Naina (likes lasagna, pho) can be satisfied with pho being 'orderable'.\n- Vic (likes toast, tandoori) will not be satisfied because both are 'not_orderable'.\n- Aarav (likes soup, toast) will not be satisfied because toast is 'not_orderable' and soup conflicts with Ravi's dislike.\n- Jun (likes roti) can be satisfied with roti being 'orderable'.\n- Isha (likes roti, tandoori) can be satisfied with roti being 'orderable'.\n- Sam (likes kebab) will not be satisfied because kebab is 'not_orderable'.\n- Min (likes roti) can be satisfied with roti being 'orderable'.\n\nSince Shan, Ravi, Vic, Aarav, and Sam cannot be satisfied with the current arrangement, the list needs to be adjusted. However, these adjustments would inevitably lead to someone else being dissatisfied because of the opposing likes and dislikes.\n\nIt's clear that with the given preferences, there's no combination of food items that can ensure all participants are satisfied. As a result, the lists should be empty.\n\nThus, the output would be empty lists for both 'orderable' and 'not_orderable':\n\n```python\norderable = []\nnot_orderable = []\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 389, 'num_completion_tokens': 854}
