o, Ray (+13)\n\nNow, we will look for food items that are disliked by many but not liked by anyone, or liked by at least one person and not disliked by anyone else, to satisfy all participants.\n\nBased on the tallies, we see:\n- frittata and quiche are both disliked by 19 people, but they are also liked by some, so they cannot be on the 'not_orderable' list.\n- fries are liked by the most (15 people), and only 8 people dislike it.\n- guacamole is liked by the second-most (14 people) and has 11 dislikes.\n- burger has 11 likes and 13 dislikes.\n\nFrom the dislikes column, we pick the most disliked item that isn't liked by anyone, which would be ideal for 'not_orderable'. However, all disliked items here are liked by at least one person.\n\nSo, let's approach it differently and attempt to satisfy as many people as possible with the 'orderable' list. We need an item from the likes column not matched by dislikes. However, all liked items have dislikes.\n\nGiven the constraints, it's clear that someone will not be satisfied no matter how we divide the items into 'orderable' and 'not_orderable', because every liked item is also disliked by someone else.\n\nTherefore, it is impossible to create a list that satisfies everyone, and the only solution is to output empty lists:\n\n```python\norderable = []\nnot_orderable = []\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 1203, 'num_completion_tokens': 898}
Data Sample: {'num_vars': 9, 'num_clauses': 18, 'formula': [[2, 4, -3], [-5, -6, -2], [-7, -2, 3], [-7, 4, 6], [9, 5, 4], [-5, -2, -6], [-3, -9, 8], [6, 5, 8], [-1, 4, 5], [-2, -5, -3], [5, 2, 9], [-4, -6, 1], [3, 8, 4], [-8, 9, 4], [-9, 8, 4], [6, 7, 3], [-3, -5, -8], [7, 9, 2]], 'is_sat': True, 'preferences': 'Kim: Likes risotto, paneer. Dislikes kimchi. Ken: Dislikes spaghetti, sorbet, risotto. Ping: Likes kimchi. Dislikes fries, risotto. Min: Likes paneer, sorbet. Dislikes fries. Cal: Likes salad, spaghetti, paneer. Mei: Dislikes spaghetti, risotto, sorbet. Anil: Likes biscuits. Dislikes kimchi, salad. Ray: Likes sorbet, spaghetti, biscuits. Rohan: Likes paneer, spaghetti. Dislikes sushi. Ana: Dislikes risotto, spaghetti, kimchi. Ava: Likes spaghetti, risotto, salad. Ron: Likes sushi. Dislikes paneer, sorbet. Kavi: Likes kimchi, biscuits, paneer. Hal: Likes salad, paneer. Dislikes biscuits. Gus: Likes biscuits, paneer. Dislikes salad. Liz: Likes sorbet, fries, kimchi. Nita: Dislikes kimchi, spaghetti, biscuits. Chen: Likes fries, salad, risotto. ', 'menu_items': ['sushi', 'risotto', 'kimchi', 'paneer', 'spaghetti', 'sorbet', 'fries', 'biscuits', 'salad'], 'gpt_out': "To satisfy these preferences, we must ensure that each person has at least one liked item on the 'orderable' list or one disliked item on the 'not_orderable' list. Here's the process step by step:\n\n1. Identify any universally disliked items.\n2. Check for items liked by some but disliked by others.\n3. Deduce a combination that satisfies all participants.\n\nFirst, let's look for any universally disliked items. If an item is disliked by everyone who mentioned it, it can safely go into the 'not_orderable' list.\n\nLooking at the dislikes:\n- Spaghetti is disliked by Ken, Mei, Ana, and Nita but liked by Cal, Ray, Ava, Rohan.\n- Sorbet is disliked by Ken, Mei, Ron but liked by Min, Ray, Liz.\n- Risotto is disliked by Ken, Ping, Mei, Ana but liked by Kim, Ava, Chen.\n- Kimchi is disliked by Kim, Anil, Ana, Nita but liked by Ping, Kavi, Liz.\n- Fries are disliked by Ping, Min but liked by Liz, Chen.\n- Salad is disliked by Anil, Gus but liked by Cal, Hal, Ava, Chen.\n- Biscuits are disliked by Hal, Nita but liked by Anil, Ray, Kavi, Gus.\n- Sushi is only disliked by Rohan but liked by Ron.\n\nSince there is no item universally disliked, we'll move to the next step.\n\nNow, let's find an item or items that can satisfy everyone:\n- Kim can be satisfied with risotto (liked) or no kimchi (disliked), etc.\n  \nTo approach the actual allocation, let's spot potential conflicts and items that could be on the 'orderable' without controversy:\n\n- Paneer is liked by Kim, Min, Cal, Rohan, Kavi, Hal, Gus and not disliked by anyone, so it can be on the 'orderable' list safely.\n- Biscuits are only disliked by Hal and Nita but liked by Anil, Ray, Kavi, and Gus. Given there is more like than dislike, we could consider it for the 'orderable' list.\n- Salad is liked by several but disliked by a couple, so we should not consider it for 'orderable' without more analysis.\n\nLet's start with what we know:\n\n1. To satisfy Kim, we can have risotto in 'orderable' and ensure kimchi is not there. Since Ping likes kimchi, we cannot put kimchi in 'not_orderable', so we have to keep risotto on the 'orderable'.\n2. Ken dislikes risotto, which conflicts with Kim's preference, so we can satisfy Ken with 'not_orderable' to have spaghetti, sorbet, or both.\n3. Ping likes kimchi, disliking fries and risotto. Ken has already made fries safe to include in 'orderable', satisfying Ping with fries on 'not_orderable'.\n4. Min likes paneer and sorbet, disliking fries. Since we've got paneer in 'orderable' and fries in 'not_orderable', Min is satisfied.\n5. Cal likes salad, spaghetti, paneer. Paneer is already 'orderable', satisfying Cal.\n6. Mei dislikes spaghetti, risotto, sorbet; we can add spaghetti to 'not_orderable' to satisfy Mei.\n7. Anil likes biscuits but dislikes kimchi, salad. We can't place kimchi or salad in 'not_orderable', so we could include biscuits in 'orderable'.\n8. Ray likes sorbet, spaghetti, biscuits, with no conflicting dislikes, so we can work with the previous decisions.\n9. Rohan likes paneer, spaghetti, and we've got paneer in 'orderable', satisfying Rohan.\n10. Ana dislikes risotto, spaghetti, kimchi; we already plan to have spaghetti in 'not_orderable', satisfying Ana.\n11. Ava likes spaghetti, risotto, salad, with no conflicts based on previous deductions.\n12. Ron likes sushi, disliking paneer and sorbet. We've got paneer in 'orderable', but that doesn't affect Ron as long as sushi is untouched.\n13. Kavi likes kimchi, biscuits, paneer. Paneer is in 'orderable' and based on previous decisions, satisfies Kavi.\n14. Hal likes salad, paneer, and biscuits in 'orderable' satisfies Hal.\n\nGiven the above preferences, here are the lists:\n\n`orderable`: paneer, biscuits, risotto\n`not_orderable`: spaghetti, fries\n\nNow let's check each person against these lists to ensure every individual is satisfied:\n\nKim: Likes risotto in 'orderable'\nKen: Dislikes spaghetti in 'not_orderable'\nPing: Dislikes fries in 'not_orderable'\nMin: Likes paneer in 'orderable'\nCal: Likes paneer in 'orderable'\nMei: Dislikes spaghetti in 'not_orderable'\nAnil: Likes biscuits in 'orderable'\nRay: At least one liked item is 'orderable'\nRohan: Likes paneer in 'orderable'\nAna: Dislikes spaghetti in 'not_orderable'\nAva: Likes risotto in 'orderable'\nRon: No conflict with lists\nKavi: Likes paneer in 'orderable'\nHal: Likes biscuits in 'orderable'\n\nThe only individuals not mentioned are Liz, Gus, Nita, and Chen:\n- Liz likes sorbet, which is not on either list. She also likes kimchi and fries, but dislikes don't affect liking items on neither list. She's satisfied as long as one like is not on the 'not_orderable' list.\n- Gus likes biscuits (orderable) and dislikes salad which is on neither list, so he's satisfied.\n- Nita dislikes kimchi and spaghetti (not_orderable) and biscuits which are on 'orderable' but just need one dislike on 'not_orderable' so she's satisfied.\n- Chen likes fries (but they are not_orderable) though Chen isn't explicitly satisfied by the 'orderable' items; however, it doesn't automatically mean Chen is unsatisfied, as there are no conflicts with dislikes.\n\nSolution:\n\n```\norderable=['paneer', 'biscuits', 'risotto'], not_orderable=['spaghetti', 'fries']\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 406, 'num_completion_tokens': 1314}
Data Sample: {'num_vars': 7, 'num_clauses': 14, 'formula': [[1, -6, 5], [-4, 1, -7], [5, 4, 7], [1, -2, 3], [5, -1, -2], [7, -5, -1], [-2, 4, -7], [-3, 6, -1], [-3, 6, -7], [-2, 1, -3], [-6, 5, -3], [-2, 6, -4], [4, 1, 7], [1, 3, -7]], 'is_sat': True, 'preferences': 'Bo: Likes falafel, couscous. Dislikes macaron. Li: Likes falafel. Dislikes quiche, ramen. Ivy: Likes couscous, quiche, ramen. Hal: Likes falafel, omelette. Dislikes spaghetti. Sid: Likes couscous. Dislikes falafel, spaghetti. Mei: Likes ramen. Dislikes couscous, falafel. Tom: Likes quiche. Dislikes spaghetti, ramen. Amy: Likes macaron. Dislikes omelette, falafel. Rob: Likes macaron. Dislikes omelette, ramen. Taj: Likes falafel. Dislikes spaghetti, omelette. Ben: Likes couscous. Dislikes macaron, omelette. Ying: Likes macaron. Dislikes spaghetti, quiche. Nita: Likes quiche, falafel, ramen. Zac: Likes falafel, omelette. Dislikes ramen. ', 'menu_items': ['falafel', 'spaghetti', 'omelette', 'quiche', 'couscous', 'macaron', 'ramen'], 'gpt_out': "To find the correct ordering of the food items, we need to make sure the rules for satisfaction are met for each person: at least one liked item is in the 'orderable' list or one disliked item is in the 'not_orderable' list. However, the liked item of a person must not be the disliked item of another person for it to be satisfactorily 'orderable.' Similarly, the disliked item for a 'not_orderable' list must not be a liked item by another person.\n\nLet's start by listing all the likes and dislikes and find possibilities for each group:\n\nLikes:\n- Falafel: Bo, Li, Hal, Taj, Nita, Zac\n- Couscous: Bo, Ivy, Sid, Ben\n- Quiche: Ivy, Tom, Nita\n- Ramen: Ivy, Mei, Nita\n- Omelette: Hal, Zac\n- Macaron: Amy, Rob, Ying\n- Spaghetti: (nobody likes spaghetti)\n\nDislikes:\n- Macaron: Bo, Ben\n- Quiche: Li, Ying\n- Ramen: Li, Tom, Rob, Zac\n- Falafel: Sid, Amy, Mei\n- Spaghetti: Hal, Sid, Taj, Ying\n- Omelette: Amy, Rob, Ben, Taj\n\nSince nobody likes spaghetti, it's a perfect candidate for the 'not_orderable' list, as it will satisfy Hal, Sid, Taj, and Ying (they dislike spaghetti).\n\nNext, we have to find liked items that are not disliked by anyone else or disliked items that are not liked by anyone else. Omelette is liked by two (Hal, Zac) and disliked by four (Amy, Rob, Ben, Taj), so it is better suited for the 'not_orderable' list.\n\nAt this stage, we have 'spaghetti' and 'omelette' in our 'not_orderable' list. That will satisfy Hal, Sid, Taj, Ying, Amy, Rob, and Ben. We need to satisfy the remaining individuals.\n\nNext, 'macaron' is liked by Amy, Rob, and Ying but it is disliked by Bo and Ben. Since Ben is already satisfied, if we put 'macaron' in the 'orderable' list, we need to take care of Bo's preference.\n\nWe cannot include 'falafel' in the 'orderable' list because it's disliked by Sid, Amy, and Mei, so let's look for other options. 'Couscous' is liked by Bo, Ivy, Sid, and Ben, but since Ben is already satisfied, and Sid and Bo would be satisfied by remarking 'couscous' as orderable, 'couscous' is a good candidate for 'orderable.'\n\nPerhaps we can satisfy Mei by including 'ramen' as something that is not orderable, even though this will affect Nita's satisfaction. This action would satisfy Mei but not Nita. However, since 'quiche' is liked by Ivy, Tom, and Nita, we will satisfy Nita by considering 'quiche' in the 'orderable' list.\n\nConsidering the remaining dislikes and likes, given all the already satisfied preferences, those who like 'falafel' (Bo, Li, Hal, Taj, Nita, Zac) will already be satisfied because they will either have their dish in the 'orderable' list or their disliked dish in the 'not_orderable' list.\n\nLet's summarize our selections so far:\n- 'orderable': couscous, macaron, quiche\n- 'not_orderable': spaghetti, omelette, ramen\n\nThis selection satisfies everyone:\n- Bo will be happy with couscous.\n- Li, Hal, Taj, and Zac are satisfied via disliked items in 'not_orderable' (omelette, ramen) or liked items in 'orderable' (quiche).\n- Ivy is satisfied with couscous.\n- Sid is satisfied because falafel is not in 'orderable', couscous is in 'orderable', and spaghetti is in 'not_orderable'.\n- Mei is happy with ramen being in 'not_orderable'.\n- Tom is satisfied with quiche in 'orderable'.\n- Amy is satisfied with macaron in 'orderable'.\n- Rob is happy with macaron in 'orderable' and ramen in 'not_orderable'.\n- Ben was already satisfied by omelette being 'not_orderable'.\n- Ying is satisfied with macaron in 'orderable'.\n- Nita is satisfied with quiche in 'orderable'.\n\nTherefore, the final lists are:\n\norderable = ['couscous', 'macaron', 'quiche']\nnot_orderable = ['spaghetti', 'omelette', 'ramen']", 'num_prompt_tokens': 400, 'num_completion_tokens': 988}
Data Sample: {'num_vars': 3, 'num_clauses': 15, 'formula': [[1, -2, 3], [3, -1, 2], [1, -2, -3], [2, -3, -1], [1, -2, -3], [-3, -2, -1], [1, -3, -2], [1, 2, 3], [1, 2, -3], [2, -3, 1], [-3, 2, -1], [1, 3, -2], [3, -1, 2], [-3, -1, 2], [3, -2, 1]], 'is_sat': True, 'preferences': 'Lan: Likes spaghetti, ravioli. Dislikes korma. Hui: Likes ravioli, korma. Dislikes spaghetti. Max: Likes spaghetti. Dislikes korma, ravioli. Cy: Likes korma. Dislikes ravioli, spaghetti. Tia: Likes spaghetti. Dislikes korma, ravioli. Hal: Dislikes ravioli, korma, spaghetti. Bo: Likes spaghetti. Dislikes ravioli, korma. Qi: Likes spaghetti, korma, ravioli. Xan: Likes spaghetti, korma. Dislikes ravioli. Abe: Likes korma, spaghetti. Dislikes ravioli. Zac: Likes korma. Dislikes ravioli, spaghetti. Gus: Likes spaghetti, ravioli. Dislikes korma. May: Likes ravioli, korma. Dislikes spaghetti. Lee: Likes korma. Dislikes ravioli, spaghetti. Fay: Likes ravioli, spaghetti. Dislikes korma. ', 'menu_items': ['spaghetti', 'korma', 'ravioli'], 'gpt_out': "To find the items that can be ordered (`orderable`) and those that cannot (`not_orderable`) to meet the preferences of a group of individuals, let's examine their likes and dislikes to identify any conflicts and possibilities.\n\nGiven the people and their likes/dislikes:\n\n1. Lan: Likes spaghetti, ravioli. Dislikes korma.\n2. Hui: Likes ravioli, korma. Dislikes spaghetti.\n3. Max: Likes spaghetti. Dislikes korma, ravioli.\n4. Cy: Likes korma. Dislikes ravioli, spaghetti.\n5. Tia: Likes spaghetti. Dislikes korma, ravioli.\n6. Hal: Dislikes ravioli, korma, spaghetti.\n7. Bo: Likes spaghetti. Dislikes ravioli, korma.\n8. Qi: Likes spaghetti, korma, ravioli.\n9. Xan: Likes spaghetti, korma. Dislikes ravioli.\n10. Abe: Likes korma, spaghetti. Dislikes ravioli.\n11. Zac: Likes korma. Dislikes ravioli, spaghetti.\n12. Gus: Likes spaghetti, ravioli. Dislikes korma.\n13. May: Likes ravioli, korma. Dislikes spaghetti.\n14. Lee: Likes korma. Dislikes ravioli, spaghetti.\n15. Fay: Likes ravioli, spaghetti. Dislikes korma.\n\nBased on the preferences above, an item can be placed in the `orderable` list if it is liked by at least one person and not disliked by anyone, or it can be placed in the `not_orderable` list if it is disliked by at least one person and not liked by anyone else.\n\nHere is the conflict and possibility analysis:\n- Spaghetti is liked by many (Lan, Max, Tia, Bo, Qi, Xan, Abe, Gus, Fay) but is disliked by Hui, Cy, Zac, and Lee. This would mean it cannot satisfy everyone if placed in either list.\n- Ravioli is liked by many (Lan, Hui, Qi, Gus, May, Fay) but is also disliked by many (Max, Cy, Tia, Hal, Bo, Xan, Abe, Zac, Lee). This too has conflicts and cannot satisfy everyone if placed in either list.\n- Korma is liked by some (Hui, Cy, Qi, May, Abe, Zac, Lee) and disliked by many (Lan, Max, Tia, Hal, Bo, Gus, Fay). Similar to spaghetti and ravioli, it also cannot satisfy everyone if placed in either list.\n\nConsidering Hal who dislikes all items, we can't put any item in `orderable` list which Hal likes, since there's none and putting any item in `not_orderable` that Hal dislikes would satisfy Hal. Other people's preferences need to be accommodated as well. Let's consider the dislikes:\n\n- If we put ravioli in `not_orderable` list, it would satisfy Lan, Max, Cy, Tia, Hal, Bo, Xan, Abe, Zac, and Lee, since it's an item they all dislike.\n- To satisfy Hui, who dislikes spaghetti (already liked by others so we cannot place it in `not_orderable`), we must ensure korma or ravioli is in `not_orderable`, and with ravioli in `not_orderable`, Hui is satisfied.\n- Gus and Fay are already satisfied with ravioli in `not_orderable`.\n- The remaining individuals (Qi, May) would be satisfied as long as ravioli or korma is in `not_orderable`.\n- Since everyone who dislikes ravioli is already satisfied by its inclusion in `not_orderable`, we can select an item liked by as many people as possible for `orderable`. \n- Spaghetti is the most liked item remaining, so we can place it in `orderable`, which does not conflict with any other preferences after ravioli is placed in `not_orderable`.\n\nThus, we can satisfy everyone by placing ravioli in the `not_orderable` list and spaghetti in the `orderable` list. The `not_orderable` list does not need to contain korma because placing ravioli there already satisfies everyone who dislikes korma (as it is a disliked item by at least one person who does not like anything else).\n\nTherefore, the final solution will be:\n```\norderable=['spaghetti'], not_orderable=['ravioli']\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 418, 'num_completion_tokens': 960}
