ider making "soup" orderable.\n- Nita dislikes kebab but likes caramel and salad; if either is orderable, she is satisfied.\n- Xiu dislikes kebab but likes tandoori, which is already in not_orderable, so Xiu is satisfied.\n- Gus dislikes kebab but likes soup, possibly making soup orderable satisfies Gus.\n\nNow, let\'s consider "salad":\n\n- Ana and Wes like salad and dislike both soup and kebab, so we cannot make salad orderable because it excludes other items they both dislike.\n\nFinally, let\'s check caramel and pho:\n\n- Lia likes salad and soup and dislikes pho, putting pho on the not_orderable list will satisfy her.\n- Tim likes caramel and tandoori and dislikes kebab, with the plan above he\'s satisfied.\n- Ron likes soup and dislikes caramel and pho, making caramel not_orderable satisfies Ron.\n\nTo summarize, we have the following potential plan:\n\n- Kebab is orderable to satisfy a large group.\n- Tandoori is not orderable to satisfy another group.\n- Since Sue and others are satisfied with kebab, we no longer need to make pho orderable.\n\nLet\'s finalize by ensuring all are satisfied with this arrangement:\n\n- Those who like \'kebab\' are automatically satisfied.\n- Those who dislike \'kebab\' but like \'pho\' or \'soup\' are satisfied.\n- Those who dislike \'kebab\' but dislike \'tandoori\' more are also satisfied.\n- For those who like \'salad\' and dislike \'kebab\', \'soup\' or \'pho\' being orderable satisfies them (e.g., Ty, Ana, Lia)\n- Tim is satisfied with \'tandoori\' being not_orderable.\n\nThis leaves us with:\norderable = [\'kebab\']\nnot_orderable = [\'tandoori\'] \n\nLet\'s check if anyone would be unsatisfied:\n\n- Ty likes salad but now has kebab orderable, which he dislikes. However, making \'salad\' orderable would unsatisfy Riya and Isha. This is a conflict.\n- Sue likes pho, dislikes kebab but kebab is orderable. If we make \'pho\' orderable, then Rohan would be unsatisfied.\n- Rohan likes soup and dislikes kebab, if we add \'soup\