tisfy the most individuals who haven\'t been satisfied by "tacos" being \'not_orderable\'.\n\nThus, as potential solution, we get:\n- orderable: salad\n- not_orderable: tacos\n\nNow, we need to check if every individual is satisfied:\n\n```plaintext\nMei: salad (orderable) satisfies.\nJaya: salad (orderable) satisfies.\nZac: Dislikes salad, so not satisfied by these lists.\nYue: Is not satisfied (nothing in dislikes).\nMina: dislikes tacos (not_orderable) satisfies.\nWes: dislikes tacos (not_orderable) satisfies.\nDiya: Is not satisfied (nothing in dislikes).\nArun: dislikes tacos (not_orderable) satisfies.\nMeg: Is not satisfied (nothing in dislikes).\nMia: Is not satisfied (nothing in dislikes).\nTia: dislikes tacos (not_orderable) satisfies.\nVic: dislikes tiramisu, but neither tiramisu nor poke are settled yet, not satisfied by these lists.\nRay: likes tacos, but tacos are \'not_orderable\', so not satisfied by these lists.\nZoe: Orderable contains dislikes (salad), not satisfied.\nAarav: dislikes tacos, satisfied with \'not_orderable\'.\nDot: likes tacos, but tacos are \'not_orderable\', so not satisfied by these lists.\nKavi: Not satisfied by current \'orderable\' or \'not_orderable\' items.\nJay: Like \'salad\', so \'orderable\' satisfies.\nTara: Not satisfied by current \'orderable\' or \'not_orderable\' items.\nGus: Not satisfied by current \'orderable\' or \'not_orderable\' items.\nLiz: Nothing in current \'orderable\' or \'not_orderable\' satisfies.\nChao: Not satisfied by current \'orderable\' or \'not_orderable\' items.\nYan: dislikes tacos, satisfied with \'not_orderable\'.\nRiya: Nothing in current \'orderable\' or \'not_orderable\' satisfies.\nMel: dislikes tacos (not_orderable) satisfies.\nMay: Not satisfied by current \'orderable\' or \'not_orderable\' items.\nSal: likes salad (orderable) satisfies.\nLou: Not satisfied by current \'orderable\' or \'not_orderable\' items.\nShan: likes shrimps, but shrimps are not on either list yet, so not satisfied