ts within the given preferences and try to find a combination of food items for the \'orderable\' list that meets the satisfaction criteria for as many individuals as possible, and determine what\'s left for the \'not_orderable\' list.\n\nLet\'s first identify if there are items that cannot be ordered or not ordered based on the collective preferences.\n\nWe will begin by identifying if there is any item that everybody either likes or dislikes:\n\n- Likes by everyone: None.\n- Dislikes by everyone: Tandoori.\n\nSo, tandoori must be placed in the \'not_orderable\' list.\n\nNext, we can categorize each individual based on whether:\n\n1. They have specific dislikes that must not be ordered.\n2. They have specific likes that must be ordered.\n\nFollowing this logic, let\'s examine who dislikes what and if these dislikes are liked by anyone else or not:\n\n- Roti is disliked by Kit, Pat, and Arun, but liked by several people. Roti cannot be placed in \'not_orderable\' as it\'s also liked by many, so it is a candidate for \'orderable\'.\n- Guacamole is disliked by Ned, Pat, Cal, and Yan, but liked by Lee, Ru, May, and Bo. It cannot be placed in \'not_orderable\' due to the likes.\n- Gelato is disliked by Ned, Hui, Cal, Jun, Yan, and Min but liked by Gia, Jim, and Eva. Gelato cannot be part of \'orderable\' due to the number of dislikes.\n- Burrito is disliked by Neil, Lee, Sky, and Arun, but liked by Neil, Du, Xiu, and Eva. It cannot be placed in \'not_orderable\' because it is also liked.\n- Lasagna is liked by Gia, Ron, Ru, Jun, Meg, and Zan, but disliked by Rohan, Qi, Xiu, Mel, and Max. Lasagna cannot enter \'not_orderable\'.\n- Pie is liked by Sky, Xiu, Bo, Mel, and Sal but disliked by Ron, Kit, Arun, and Meg. It cannot be placed in \'not_orderable\' due to the likes.\n- Brownies are liked by Lee, Yan, Min, and Max but disliked by Jim, Qi, Du, Mel, and Rohan. Brownies cannot be placed in "not_orderable".\n- Quiche is liked only by Qi but disliked by Neil, Ru, Hui, and Cal. Quiche can potentially be in \'not_orderable\'.\n\nSince \'gelato\' and \'tandoori\' have multiple dislikes and less conflict with the likes, we can consider them for the \'not_orderable\' list. Then, we look at \'roti,\' which is liked by several individuals, for the \'orderable\' list to satisfy a large group of individuals.\n\nFrom here, we can proceed to iteratively select items for the \'orderable\' and \'not_orderable\' lists to satisfy as many individuals as possible without causing conflicts.\n\nLet\'s begin by adding non-conflicting items to satisfy as many individuals as we can:\n\n1. Add \'tandoori\' to \'not_orderable\' list since it is disliked by everyone.\n2. Add \'roti\' to \'orderable\' list as it is liked by several and only a few dislike it. This will satisfy many people.\n3. Add \'gelato\' to \'not_orderable\' since it has multiple dislikes.\n\nNow, we will iterate through the rest of the dislikes to see if any can be placed in the \'not_orderable\':\n\n- Quiche is only liked by Qi and disliked by many, so we add \'quiche\' to \'not_orderable\'.\n\nNow, let\'s review the items and satisfy the remaining preferences where possible.\n\nNotice that \'pie,\' \'brownies,\' \'burrito,\' \'lasagna,\' and \'guacamole\' are liked and disliked by several individuals, so they should not be placed in \'not_orderable\'. However, we can consider whether any can be placed in \'orderable\' without causing any conflicts.\n\nConsidering the likes, \'pie,\' \'brownies,\' and \'guacamole\' are liked by some who don\'t have other likes in their list, so it might be necessary to add them to \'orderable\' to satisfy those individuals:\n\n- May likes \'guacamole\' and \'lasagna\' (lasagna is not in \'not_orderable\' and liked by others, so no conflict there). \'Guacamole\' can be placed in \'orderable\' to satisfy May and others.\n- Mel likes \'pie\' and it\'s the only like for Mel that is not disliked by others. \'Pie\' can go to \'orderable\'.\n- Max likes \'brownies\' and both of the other items on his list (\'burrito\' and \'lasagna\') are not in \'not_orderable\' and liked by others, so we can add \'brownies\' to \'orderable\'.\n\nWith these considerations, we are satisfying as many individuals as we can based on the complex conditions and avoiding conflicts. Here are our final lists based on the choices meeting the criteria:\n\norderable=[\'roti\', \'guacamole\', \'pie\', \'brownies\'], \nnot_orderable=[\'tandoori\', \'gelato\', \'quiche\']', 'num_prompt_tokens': 620, 'num_completion_tokens': 1107}
Data Sample: {'num_vars': 4, 'num_clauses': 20, 'formula': [[-4, -1, -2], [-2, 3, -1], [-3, -2, -1], [-4, -1, -3], [2, -4, 1], [-1, -3, 2], [4, -3, 1], [-3, -1, 2], [3, -2, 4], [1, -3, 4], [1, -3, 4], [-4, -3, 2], [-2, 4, 3], [1, 3, 4], [4, 3, 2], [3, -2, -4], [-4, 3, -1], [-1, 4, -3], [4, -1, -3], [-3, 1, -4]], 'is_sat': False, 'preferences': 'Wei: Dislikes cheesecake, risotto, lobster. Amy: Likes churros. Dislikes lobster, risotto. Du: Dislikes churros, lobster, risotto. Riya: Dislikes cheesecake, risotto, churros. Hal: Likes lobster, risotto. Dislikes cheesecake. Gus: Likes lobster. Dislikes risotto, churros. Ivy: Likes cheesecake, risotto. Dislikes churros. Ru: Likes lobster. Dislikes churros, risotto. Kai: Likes churros, cheesecake. Dislikes lobster. Pia: Likes risotto, cheesecake. Dislikes churros. Sia: Likes risotto, cheesecake. Dislikes churros. Ula: Likes lobster. Dislikes cheesecake, churros. Ken: Likes cheesecake, churros. Dislikes lobster. Bea: Likes risotto, churros, cheesecake. Mia: Likes cheesecake, churros, lobster. Wes: Likes churros. Dislikes lobster, cheesecake. Raj: Likes churros. Dislikes cheesecake, risotto. Nita: Likes cheesecake. Dislikes risotto, churros. Oli: Likes cheesecake. Dislikes risotto, churros. Rob: Likes risotto. Dislikes churros, cheesecake. ', 'menu_items': ['risotto', 'lobster', 'churros', 'cheesecake'], 'gpt_out': 'From the given preferences, we can deduce that:\n\n1. Lobster is disliked by a majority but liked by Hal, Gus, Ru, Ula, and Mia. Since there are more likes than dislikes for lobster, it cannot simply be discarded into the \'not_orderable\' list without considering other preferences.\n2. Cheesecake is liked by many but also disliked by some. However, the likes outweigh the dislikes.\n3. Risotto is liked by many but also disliked by several. It appears that it is liked by a sufficient number to consider it for the \'orderable\' list.\n4. Churros are liked by many and disliked by fewer people. It seems more favorable to include them in the \'orderable\' list if possible.\n\nWe need to find a combination that satisfies the largest number of preferences without conflict.\n\nTo begin tackling this systematically, let\'s start by listing items that are uniquely liked or disliked by individuals:\n\n- Amy, Du, Riya, and Wes all like churros and dislike lobster and risotto.\n- Hal likes both lobster and risotto but dislikes cheesecake, items that are contentious among others.\n- Gus, Ru, and Ula like lobster and dislike risotto and churros. Gus\'s and Ru\'s preferences are particularly strong since they only like lobster.\n\nLooking at these patterns, it seems that we can satisfy a maximum number of people by putting "churros" in the \'orderable\' list and "risotto" in the \'not_orderable\' list. This will satisfy Amy, Du, Riya, and Wes immediately because they would have a liked item in the \'orderable\' list or a disliked item in the \'not_orderable\' list. It would also satisfy Gus, Ru, and Ula through their dislikes and likes.\n\nThe contentious items are cheesecake and lobster. Lobster is liked by several but disliked by more, and cheesecake is the opposite. We have to see if we can satisfy the cheesecake/lobster likes/dislikes without causing anyone to be dissatisfied by the presence/absence of the other contested items (churros and risotto).\n\nIf we put "lobster" in \'orderable\', it dissatisfies anyone who only dislikes lobster. Similarly, putting "cheesecake" in \'not_orderable\' would dissatisfy anyone who only likes cheesecake. Our goal is to avoid dissatisfaction.\n\nWe will tentatively place "churros" in \'orderable\', as their inclusion satisfies many people. Now let\'s try to put "cheesecake" in \'orderable\', which would satisfy Ivy, Pia, Sia, Ken, Bea, Mia, Nita, Oli, and potentially others.\n\nGiven this setup, let\'s see if anyone would be dissatisfied:\n\n- Anyone who dislikes "churros" and doesn\'t have their dislikes in \'not_orderable\' would be dissatisfied.\n- Anyone who likes "lobster" and doesn\'t have their likes in \'orderable\' would also be dissatisfied.\n\nLet\'s check against each person\'s preferences:\n\n- Hal, Ivy, and Pia like risotto, so they are not satisfied because risotto is \'not_orderable\'.\n- Gus, Ru, and Ula like lobster, which hasn\'t been decided yet, and they are satisfied because their other dislikes (risotto, churros) are catered by the lists.\n- Wei dislikes all three, so she is satisfied as long as lobster or cheesecake is \'not_orderable\'.\n- Amy, Du, Riya, and Wes will be satisfied with churros being \'orderable\'.\n- Kai, Ken, Raj, and Nita will be satisfied with cheesecake being \'orderable\'.\n- Mia will be satisfied as she likes all three.\n\nNow we need a decision on "lobster" to see if it\'s possible to satisfy everyone. If we include "lobster" in \'orderable\', we\'ll satisfy the rest (Gus, Ru, Ula, and anyone else who likes lobster). But this will make individuals who only dislike lobster (Wei, Amy, Du, Riya) dissatisfied as none of their dislikes are in \'not_orderable\'. On the other hand, excluding "lobster" from both lists should still satisfy everyone who likes lobster since they have "cheesecake" and/or "churros" in \'orderable\', and it will satisfy those who dislike lobster, as they won\'t have to encounter it.\n\nThus, our tentative lists are:\n\n- \'orderable\': ["churros", "cheesecake"]\n- \'not_orderable\': ["risotto"]\n\nChecking every individual\'s satisfaction:\n\n1. Wei is satisfied (dislikes "risotto" which is \'not_orderable\').\n2. Amy is satisfied ("churros" are \'orderable\').\n3. Du is satisfied ("churros" are \'orderable\').\n4. Riya is satisfied ("churros" are \'orderable\').\n5. Hal is not satisfied (likes "lobster" and "risotto", but both are not in \'orderable\').\n6. Gus is satisfied ("churros" are \'orderable\').\n7. Ivy is satisfied ("cheesecake" is \'orderable\').\n8. Ru is satisfied ("cheesecake" is \'orderable\').\n9. Kai is satisfied ("cheesecake" is \'orderable\').\n10. Pia is not satisfied (likes "risotto" which is \'not_orderable\').\n11. Sia is not satisfied (likes "risotto" which is \'not_orderable\').\n12. Ula is satisfied ("lobster" is not in \'not_orderable\').\n13. Ken is satisfied ("cheesecake" is \'orderable\').\n14. Bea is satisfied ("cheesecake" is \'orderable\').\n15. Mia is satisfied ("cheesecake" is \'orderable\').\n16. Wes is satisfied ("churros" are \'orderable\').\n17. Raj is satisfied ("churros" are \'orderable\').\n18. Nita is satisfied ("cheesecake" is \'orderable\').\n19. Oli is satisfied ("cheesecake" is \'orderable\').\n20. Rob is not satisfied (likes "risotto" which is \'not_orderable\').\n\nSince Hal, Pia, Sia, and Rob are not satisfied with these lists, the solution doesn\'t satisfy all participants. It seems that we have an impasse because we can\'t have "lobster" and "risotto" on both \'orderable\' and \'not_orderable\' lists to satisfy everyone due to the conflicting likes and dislikes. No such combination exists that satisfies all, so we must return empty lists for both.\n\nFinal solution:\n\n```python\norderable = []\nnot_orderable = []\n```', 'num_prompt_tokens': 478, 'num_completion_tokens': 1397}
Data Sample: {'num_vars': 7, 'num_clauses': 63, 'formula': [[7, 1, -4], [-2, -5, 1], [5, 1, -3], [2, 6, 3], [-2, -7, -4], [4, -5, 3], [-6, 2, -7], [4, -7, 1], [-6, -4, -5], [-1, -5, -7], [-6, -3, -7], [-7, -2, 5], [5, -1, 3], [-4, 6, -3], [-1, -5, 3], [1, 7, -2], [3, -7, -6], [1, -4, -6], [7, -1, 2], [-3, 7, -6], [6, -4, -5], [-3, 5, -4], [-2, -5, -6], [-3, -4, -6], [-5, 6, 2], [1, 3, 4], [-2, 6, 7], [1, 3, -6], [-7, -3, -4], [-1, 5, 6], [-3, -5, 2], [5, -2, -7], [-3, 4, 7], [3, -6, 2], [4, 1, -5], [-7, -5, 4], [-6, 7, 5], [-4, 7, 6], [-5, 7, 2], [-6, 3, -4], [1, -5, -3], [6, 3, -7], [6, 1, 2], [4, -5, 7], [-2, -5, -6], [5, -3, -1], [5, 4, -2], [6, -1, -2], [3, 6, 1], [-1, -4, -7], [6, -1, -4], [4, 2, 1], [-6, 4, 7], [-1, 4, -6], [7, -1, 6], [5, 1, -7], [1, 2, -5], [-7, -4, -5], [-1, 7, 3], [-7, -2, -3], [-4, 5, 2], [6, -3, -2], [-4, 7, -2]], 'is_sat': False, 'preferences': 'Jon: Likes fries, salad. Dislikes risotto. Kit: Likes salad. Dislikes udon, roti. Om: Likes roti, salad. Dislikes kimchi. Sid: Likes udon, falafel, kimchi. Isha: Dislikes udon, fries, risotto. Lia: Likes risotto, kimchi. Dislikes roti. Bo: Likes udon. Dislikes falafel, fries. Chen: Likes risotto, salad. Dislikes fries. Cal: Dislikes falafel, risotto, roti. Ned: Dislikes salad, roti, fries. Nic: Dislikes falafel, kimchi, fries. Chao: Likes roti. Dislikes fries, udon. Ian: Likes roti, kimchi. Dislikes salad. Sam: Likes falafel. Dislikes risotto, kimchi. Rina: Likes kimchi. Dislikes salad, roti. Eli: Likes salad, fries. Dislikes udon. Lan: Likes kimchi. Dislikes fries, falafel. Jun: Likes salad. Dislikes risotto, falafel. Aarav: Likes fries, udon. Dislikes salad. Ken: Likes fries. Dislikes kimchi, falafel. Nita: Likes falafel. Dislikes risotto, roti. Veer: Likes roti. Dislikes kimchi, risotto. Hui: Dislikes udon, roti, falafel. Sal: Dislikes kimchi, risotto, falafel. Dan: Likes falafel, udon. Dislikes roti. Zara: Likes salad, kimchi, risotto. Qi: Likes falafel, fries. Dislikes udon. Tao: Likes salad, kimchi. Dislikes falafel. Ava: Dislikes fries, kimchi, risotto. Min: Likes roti, falafel. Dislikes salad. Tom: Likes udon. Dislikes kimchi, roti. Ray: Likes roti. Dislikes udon, fries. Rob: Likes risotto, fries. Dislikes kimchi. Yan: Likes kimchi, udon. Dislikes falafel. Leo: Likes risotto, salad. Dislikes roti. Geet: Likes risotto. Dislikes fries, roti. Kim: Likes fries, roti. Dislikes falafel. Wei: Likes fries, falafel. Dislikes risotto. Kavi: Likes fries, udon. Dislikes roti. Ula: Likes kimchi. Dislikes falafel, risotto. Sue: Likes salad. Dislikes roti, kimchi. Pia: Likes falafel, kimchi. Dislikes fries. Pat: Likes falafel, salad, udon. Fay: Likes risotto, fries. Dislikes roti. Jay: Dislikes udon, roti, falafel. Arun: Likes roti. Dislikes kimchi, salad. Dot: Likes roti, risotto. Dislikes udon. Gus: Likes falafel. Dislikes salad, udon. Cy: Likes kimchi, falafel, salad. Liv: Dislikes salad, risotto, fries. Vic: Likes falafel. Dislikes salad, risotto. Anil: Likes risotto, udon, salad. Eva: Likes risotto, fries. Dislikes falafel. Max: Likes risotto. Dislikes salad, falafel. Riya: Likes fries, falafel. Dislikes salad. Li: Likes roti, salad. Dislikes fries. Tim: Likes salad, udon. Dislikes roti. Hal: Dislikes fries, risotto, roti. Jan: Likes fries, kimchi. Dislikes salad. Bea: Dislikes fries, udon, kimchi. Naina: Likes roti, udon. Dislikes risotto. Val: Likes falafel. Dislikes kimchi, udon. Lee: Likes fries. Dislikes risotto, udon. ', 'menu_items': ['salad', 'udon', 'kimchi', 'risotto', 'roti', 'falafel', 'fries'], 'gpt_out': "Let's begin by compiling all the likes and dislikes of each individual and determine which items can be put in the 'orderable' list and which can be in the 'not_orderable' list.\n\n**Step 1: Identify universally liked and disliked items**\n\nItems that everyone likes could be put in 'orderable', items everyone dislikes could be put in 'not_orderable'. With the diverse preferences provided, it's unlikely we have such items, but let's ensure.\n\nFrom a quick scan, no single food item is liked by everyone, and no single food item is disliked by everyone. \n\n**Step 2: Identify individual blocker items**\n\nThese are items that, if put in one list, would prevent at least one person from being satisfied.\n\nFrom the lists, it’s clear that every item is liked by some and disliked by others. Thus we don't have a straightforward item that satisfies or dissatisfies all.\n\n**Step 3: Start with mandatory placements due to dislikes**\n\nSome individuals dislike certain items so much that they have no likes to compensate, so these items must go into the 'not_orderable' list for them to be satisfied.\n\nFor instance, Sal dislikes kimchi, risotto, and falafel, and has no likes, which means at least one of those must be in 'not_orderable'. \nNext, let's see if there's a common item that satisfies all individuals who don't have likes, or has likes that are already in someone else's dislike list. \n\nThere are multiple individuals like Sal with no likes, so let's find items that are commonly disliked among them:\n- Falafel: Disliked by Sal, Jay\n- Kimchi: Disliked by Sal, Nic, Om, Isha, etc.\n- Risotto: Disliked by Sal, Isha, Ava, etc.\n- Roti: Disliked by Kit, Cal, etc.\n\nSince every item is disliked by multiple people but also liked by others, we need to find at least one item for these individuals that is not liked by anyone else. However, this will not be possible as all food items are indeed liked by at least one person. This means that there will be a conflict for any item that we try to place into 'not_orderable' based on dislikes alone.\n\n**Step 4: Pair likes with dislikes**\n\nFor every person who likes an item, we need to find if there’s someone who dislikes that item. If there is and they don't like any other item, we cannot place it in 'orderable'. \n\n**Finding a path to satisfy all: An Item-by-Item Approach**\n\nTo satisfy Jon, we can place fries in 'orderable', because at least one person dislikes each of Jon's liked items (fries, salad), but fries are disliked by fewer individuals than salad.\n\nBefore adding fries, let's check if there's a conflict:\n- Ken, who dislikes fries, likes nothing else, so fries must not be 'orderable'.\n- Lee dislikes fries and likes nothing else, so fries must not be 'orderable'.\n- Aarav dislikes salad, likes fries and udon, so fries being 'orderable' works for Aarav as udon is disliked by others who don't have alternative likes.\n- Nic dislikes fries but likes falafel, so we could potentially satisfy Nic with falafel in 'orderable', provided there's no issue with other people's preferences.\n\nSince Ken and Lee block fries from being orderable, we cannot satisfy Jon with fries. Jon also likes salad, which is also liked by many and disliked by some, but since there is no strict blocker like with fries, we can tentatively put salad in 'orderable'.\n\nNow let's ensure that placing salad in 'orderable' satisfies everyone who likes salad and doesn't cause issues with those who dislike it. \n\nNed dislikes salad and doesn't like anything else, so salad cannot be 'orderable'.\n\nWe've encountered an impasse: Jon cannot be satisfied with fries or salad, and without Jon being satisfied, we can't satisfy everyone.\n\nGiven these conflicts, it appears that no set of items can be placed in the 'orderable' and 'not_orderable' lists to ensure everyone’s satisfaction. Hence, the solution is:\n\norderable=[]\nnot_orderable=[]\n\nLet's output this in the specified Python format:\n\n```python\norderable=[], not_orderable=[]\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 1043, 'num_completion_tokens': 901}
