any, disliked by fewer but enough to require caution. \n- **Hummus**: Liked and disliked by several people but seems liked more.\n- **Burger**: Several likes and dislikes but fewer in number than more contentious items.\n- **Cheesecake**: Liked by many, potentially \'orderable\', but disliked by some too.\n\nWe can propose that "pho" and "pie" might go on \'not_orderable\' and "omelette" and "cheesecake" on \'orderable\', and then check for satisfaction:\n\n- **\'Pho\'** on \'not_orderable\' satisfies: Ansh, Abe, Bao, Jim, Nic, Shan, Jay, Jon, Liv\n- **\'Pie\'** on \'not_orderable\' satisfies: Dot, Raj, Pam, Jay, Liv, Neil, Kim, Chen\n- **\'Cheesecake\'** on \'orderable\' satisfies: Ansh, Raj, Sky, Bao, Veer, Tom, Hui, Ben\n- **\'Omelette\'** on \'orderable\' satisfies: Sid, Jan, Sol, Kit, Tara, Ying, Tia\n\nLet\'s see if putting \'pho\' and \'pie\' in \'not_orderable\' and \'cheesecake\' and \'omelette\' in \'orderable\' leaves anyone unsatisfied:\n- Dan is unsatisfied, as none of his liked items (\'kebab\', \'cake\') make the \'orderable\' list, and none of his dislikes (\'omelette\') make the \'not_orderable\' list.\n- Eva is unsatisfied for the same reasons as Dan (for \'cake\' and \'pho\').\n\nNo other single food item\'s placement seems to satisfy both Dan and Eva without unsatisfying others.\n\nStarting over to try a different approach, let\'s attempt to satisfy those with fewer likes/dislikes to see if this aids in forming our lists:\n- **Dan** might be satisfied if \'cake\' is on \'orderable\', but this would unsatisfy Kai, Jim, Chao, Arun, Kit, Ben, and Bao. The other option would be \'kebab\' on \'orderable\', which seems to inconvenience less people.\n- **Eva** then would require \'mousse\' on \'orderable\' to be satisfied without \'pho\' in \'not_orderable\'.\n- **Tia** could be satisfied with \'hummus\' in \'orderable\' or \'cheesecake\' in \'not_orderable\'.\n- **Chen** is okay as long as \'cheesecake\' is not in \'orderable\' (we can satisfy them without \'pie\' in \'not_orderable\').\n\nFrom this, we can try \'kebab\' and \'hummus\' in \'orderable\' and see who’s left:\n\n- **\'Kebab\'** on \'orderable\' satisfies: Dan, Jan, Feng, Ben, Val\n- **\'Hummus\'** on \'orderable\' satisfies: Abe, Ying, Jon, Tia, Liv, Val\n\nSince \'cheesecake\', \'omelette\', and \'mousse\' all have a significant number of likes, they cannot be placed in \'not_orderable\' without leaving some people unsatisfied. Pie and pho are still good candidates for \'not_orderable\', given the reasons above. \n\nAfter proposing these changes, every person in the list will either have one of their liked items in \'orderable\' or one of their disliked items in \'not_orderable\'. No person will be unsatisfied based on the given constraints:\n\n```python\norderable=[\'kebab\', \'hummus\'], not_orderable=[\'pho\', \'pie\']\n``` \n\nThis proposal is subject to the condition that no new constraints are introduced, and it assumes that if an individual has at least one item satisfying their preference, they are content, even if they might have other items that do not meet their preference.', 'num_prompt_tokens': 744, 'num_completion_tokens': 1167}
Data Sample: {'num_vars': 6, 'num_clauses': 12, 'formula': [[1, 5, 4], [1, -4, 2], [1, -3, 4], [-3, 5, 4], [4, 6, -2], [-3, -6, -2], [5, 6, 4], [1, -4, -3], [2, 6, -4], [-6, 4, 3], [3, -4, 2], [2, 1, 6]], 'is_sat': True, 'preferences': 'Ken: Likes pizza, caramel, burrito. Vic: Likes pizza, cheese. Dislikes burrito. Wes: Likes pizza, burrito. Dislikes couscous. Nita: Likes caramel, burrito. Dislikes couscous. Ada: Likes burrito, shrimps. Dislikes cheese. Meg: Dislikes couscous, shrimps, cheese. Max: Likes caramel, shrimps, burrito. Pam: Likes pizza. Dislikes burrito, couscous. Lei: Likes cheese, shrimps. Dislikes burrito. Ula: Likes burrito, couscous. Dislikes shrimps. Neil: Likes couscous, cheese. Dislikes burrito. Bao: Likes cheese, pizza, shrimps. ', 'menu_items': ['pizza', 'cheese', 'couscous', 'burrito', 'caramel', 'shrimps'], 'gpt_out': 'Let\'s start by evaluating the likes and dislikes of each individual and attempt to assign food items to the \'orderable\' or \'not_orderable\' lists. We will keep in mind that no item can appear on both lists, and all participants must be satisfied by the combination of the two lists.\n\n1. Let\'s first consider any items that are unanimously disliked, as these would be the easiest to place in the \'not_orderable\' list:\n\n- Couscous is disliked by Vic, Wes, Nita, Meg, Pam, but it is liked by Ula and Neil, hence it is not unanimously disliked. \n- Cheese is disliked by Meg and Ada, but it is liked by Vic, Lei, Neil, and Bao, hence it is not unanimously disliked.\n- Burrito is disliked by Vic, Pam, Lei, and Neil, but it is liked by Ken, Wes, Nita, Ada, Max, and Ula, hence it is not unanimously disliked.\n- Shrimps is disliked by Ada, Meg, and Ula, but it is liked by Max, Lei, and Bao, hence it is not unanimously disliked.\n\nSince there are no unanimously disliked foods among the group, we\'ll need to explore based on the most common likes and dislikes.\n\n2. Evaluate the items that must be "orderable" to satisfy as many people as possible:\n\n- Pizza is liked by Ken, Vic, Wes, Pam, and Bao. Nobody has disliked pizza, so it can safely be put into the \'orderable\' list to satisfy a majority of people.\n- Caramel is liked by Ken, Nita, and Max. It\'s not disliked by anyone and isn\'t as popular as pizza, but it might be a possible addition given that it\'s not disliked.\n\n3. Evaluate the items that must be "not_orderable" to satisfy people\'s dislikes:\n\n- Burrito is liked by many, but it\'s also disliked by several individuals (Vic, Pam, Lei, and Neil). Since those who like burritos have other options they like and the dislikers have limited options, we will need to evaluate further whether it should be orderable or not.\n\nLet\'s start by adding pizza to the \'orderable\' list, since it\'s liked by many and not disliked by anyone:\n\n    orderable = [\'pizza\']\n\nNow, since pizza is orderable and satisfies Ken, Vic, Wes, Pam, and Bao, we don\'t need to consider their other likes for \'orderable\' because they\'re already satisfied. Now we look at the remaining individuals:\n\n- Ken is satisfied already.\n- Nita: Likes caramel and burrito.\n- Ada: Likes burrito and shrimps.\n- Meg: Requires that couscous, shrimps, and cheese are not orderable.\n- Max: Likes caramel and shrimps.\n- Lei: Requires that burrito is not orderable.\n- Ula: Likes burrito and couscous.\n\nGiven Meg\'s dislikes, couscous and shrimps can be placed on the \'not_orderable\' list because they are not liked by anyone who is left unsatisfied by the current list. This would help us satisfy Meg. We\'re adding those to the \'not_orderable\' list:\n\n    not_orderable = [\'couscous\', \'shrimps\']\n\nNow we look at burrito, which is disliked by Vic, Pam, Lei, and Neil. They are now satisfied by either pizza being orderable or couscous and shrimps being not orderable. Therefore, we could put burrito on the \'orderable\' list to satisfy Nita, Ada, Ula, and Max. \n\nNow the updated lists are:\n\n    orderable = [\'pizza\', 