es, and Kim that can be placed in the \'not_orderable\' list to start off:\n\n- Ava dislikes: biscuits, bruschetta, sushi\n- Wes dislikes: bruschetta, mousse, salad\n- Kim dislikes: mousse, gelato, biscuits\n\nAll three of them dislike biscuits.\n\nLet\'s add "biscuits" to \'not_orderable\':\n\nnot_orderable = [biscuits]\n\nNow, let\'s satisfy the people who have biscuits as a liked item (Tara and Chen) by placing their other liked items in \'orderable\':\n\n- Tara likes: mousse, sushi\n- Chen likes: salad\n\nWe need to avoid sushi for Ava\'s dislike, and salad is disliked by Raj and Wes. Thus, "mousse" ends up being the only viable option to go to \'orderable\' to satisfy Tara:\n\norderable = [mousse]\n\nBut this causes a problem: "mousse" is disliked by Lia, Meg, and Kim, so it cannot be in \'orderable\'.\n\nLet\'s backtrack and find another combination. We cannot satisfy Ava by placing "sushi" or "bruschetta" in \'not_orderable\' because that would dissatisfy Zan, Ru, and others. However, if we decide to satisfy others and ignore Ava\'s preferences, we would still get stuck because of overlapping likes and dislikes. \n\nFor instance, even if we placed "salad" in \'orderable\' to satisfy Ru, Chen, and Jan, Wes and Raj wouldn\'t be satisfied. Alternatively, placing "bruschetta" in \'orderable\' to satisfy Lia, Raj, Meg, and Jan would cause Zan and Lin to become unsatisfied since they dislike "bruschetta."\n\nWith every attempt to satisfy all participants, we end up infringing on someone else\'s dislikes. Therefore, it\'s clear that there is no combination possible where at least one liked food of a person is in \'orderable\' or one disliked food is in \'not_orderable\', without causing conflict with someone else\'s dislikes.\n\nSince we cannot find such a combination that satisfies everyone, we conclude with the following output:\n\norderable = []\nnot_orderable = []\n\nHere is the final output in Python code format:\n\n```python\norderable = [], not_orderable = []