those who dislike sorbet. For them, either their other likes need to be in 'orderable' or their dislikes in 'not_orderable'.\n\nChecking for anyone who dislikes both kimchi and sorbet, we find:\n- Qi, Ravi, Meg\n\nMeg also dislikes doughnut, so we can add doughnut to 'not_orderable'.\n\norderable = [kimchi, sorbet]\nnot_orderable = [bread, doughnut]\n\nNow this setup should satisfy everyone except potentially Qi and Ravi, who dislike sorbet and doughnut (both on 'orderable' and 'not_orderable', respectively).\n\nQi likes kimchi (which is in 'orderable'), so Qi is satisfied. \nRavi dislikes ravioli and kimchi (which are not in 'not_orderable') but doesn't have a like to counter these dislikes. Therefore, we cannot find a list combination that will satisfy Ravi unless we remove an item from 'orderable' or 'not_orderable' that he dislikes, or add an item to 'orderable' that he likes.\n\nRavi likes nothing that isn't already countered by a dislike (ravioli and sorbet are out due to other dislikes), so we are at an impasse.\n\nGiven this, there is no combination of 'orderable' and 'not_orderable' items that satisfies everyone in the list.\n\nOur final solution is:\norderable = []\nnot_orderable = []", 'num_prompt_tokens': 1128, 'num_completion_tokens': 1491}
