arting point.\n\nLet\'s try to place "peach" in \'not_orderable\' and "ravioli" in \'orderable\'. Now, we need to check if everyone can be satisfied with this choice.\n\n1. Persons who are satisfied with "ravioli" in \'orderable\' list (liking it and not disliking "peach"):\n   - Oli, Sal, Rob, Tao, Shan, Liz, Tim, Ravi, Lou, Kavi, Sid, Arun, Isha, Nic, Jun.\n\n2. Persons who are satisfied with "peach" in \'not_orderable\' list (disliking it):\n   - Max, Sal, Kit, Tao, Lia, Rob, Pia, Veer, Lee, Bao, Dan, Ava, Rex, Xan, Bo, Sid, Sol.\n\nA person like "Max" who likes "lasagna" and dislikes "burgers" and "peach" can be satisfied with "peach" in \'not_orderable\' and is neutral to "ravioli". We see that everyone who dislikes "peach" is now satisfied because they have at least one dislike in \'not_orderable\'.\n\nNow let\'s analyze the people who neither dislike "peach" nor like "ravioli":\n\n- Ada: Likes lasagna, dislikes pie, burger (can be satisfied with "lasagna").\n- Kit: Likes lasagna, pie, dislikes peach (already satisfied because dislikes "peach").\n- Pia: Likes lasagna, dislikes pie, burger (can be satisfied with "lasagna").\n- Sam: Likes lasagna, dislikes ravioli, pie (can be satisfied with "lasagna").\n- Jun: Likes lasagna, burger, dislikes ravioli (can be satisfied with "lasagna").\n- Dan: Likes lasagna, dislikes peach, burger (already satisfied because dislikes "peach").\n- Chao: Likes pie, peach, lasagna (already satisfied because dislikes "peach").\n- Ty: Likes burger, lasagna, dislikes ravioli (can be satisfied with "lasagna").\n\nNow, we observe that everyone who is neither satisfied with "peach" in \'not_orderable\' nor likes "ravioli" likes "lasagna". Therefore, we can put "lasagna" in \'orderable\'.\n\nLet\'s finalize the lists:\n\n\'orderable\' list: "ravioli", "lasagna"\n\'not_orderable\' list: "peach"\n\nNow let\'s verify if we have met the satisfaction criteria for all individuals with these lists:\n\n- All individuals who like "ravioli" and "lasa