y all individuals who have an opinion about them.\n\nNotice:\n- Nobody only likes poke; those who like it, like something else as well.\n- Nobody only likes mousse; those who like it, like something else as well.\n\nTherefore, 'poke' and 'mousse' can be placed in the 'not_orderable' list because doing so will not prevent anyone who likes these items from being satisfied, as they also like other items. Let's put them in the 'not_orderable' list and then see if we can satisfy everyone else:\n\nnot_orderable = ['poke', 'mousse']\n\nEveryone who likes poke or mousse also likes other items or dislikes something, so we are still okay.\n\nNext step is to check if we can satisfy the rest of people with udon, couscous, brownies, and paneer.\n\nWe'll start by placing udon in the 'orderable' list because it's liked by many, and the individuals who dislike udon either like paneer, couscous, or brownies or dislike poke or mousse, which are already on the 'not_orderable' list.\n\norderable = ['udon']\n\nNow let's check if we can satisfy everyone with the remaining items:\n\nDisliking udon is not an issue anymore, since udon is in the 'orderable' list.\n\nWe need to satisfy people who like or dislike couscous, brownies, and paneer.\n\nChecking for paneer, we see that it's possible to put paneer in the 'orderable' list because every person who dislikes paneer either likes an item already on 'orderable' list or dislikes an item on 'not_orderable' list. Thus, adding paneer to 'orderable' will satisfy those who like it without affecting those who dislike it.\n\norderable = ['udon', 'paneer']\n\nBrownies and couscous remain. Now we'll add brownies to the 'orderable' list because every person who dislikes brownies either likes udon or paneer (which are already in the 'orderable' list) or dislikes poke or mousse (which are in the 'not_orderable' list).\n\norderable = ['udon', 'paneer', 'brownies']\n\nLastly, we have couscous. We cannot place couscous in the 'orderable' list because some 