ice.\n* **Lobster** - This item is liked by some and disliked by others, which means it cannot be on any of the two lists.\n* **Omelette** - Several people like it and only a few dislike it, so there\'s potential here.\n* **Doughnut** - It has mixed preferences.\n* **Brownies** - Liked by several, but also disliked by multiple others.\n\nBased on the above, we can clearly see that "poke", "baklava", "lobster", "doughnut", and "brownies" cannot be confidently placed in either list without further analysis because they are liked and disliked by several individuals.\n\nLet\'s examine if “omelette” can be placed in the \'orderable\' list since it\'s primarily liked with only a few disliking it:\n1. Individuals who dislike "omelette" are Mel, Bao, Tim, Isha, Yan, and Eva. But if these individuals have an item they like on the \'orderable\' list or an item they dislike on the \'not_orderable\' list, they can still be satisfied.\n2. "Omelette" needs to be on the \'orderable\' list because it will satisfy Rohan, Chen, Du, Shan, Ada, Lex, Yue, Tia, and potentially others who neither like nor dislike it but may not have any other liked items on the \'orderable\' list.\n\nApplying these observations, let\'s attempt to build the \'orderable\' and ‘not_orderable’ lists. We have several items liked by just one or two individuals, and these items are not disliked by anyone else; these are prime candidates for the \'orderable\' list due to their uncontroversial nature.\n\nWe’ll mark candidates for the \'orderable\' list, taking care not to include items disliked by anyone:\n- "Omelette" is liked by several and disliked by a few, but it looks like it might be necessary in \'orderable\'.\n  \nCandidates for \'not_orderable\' are items that are only disliked:\n- "lobster" is disliked by several individuals, and not placing it on the \'orderable\' list could satisfy these individuals without upsetting those who like "lobster".\n\nConsidering candidates for \'not_orderable\' and lack of