n the 'not_orderable' list because there are people who like one but dislike the other, for example, Qi likes sushi and dislikes tacos, and Jay likes tacos and dislikes sushi. So, we cannot satisfy both Qi and Jay if we put both items on the 'not_orderable' list. Let's select 'tacos' for the 'not_orderable' list as it is disliked by more individuals (20) than 'sushi' (19). This approach will ensure that all individuals disliking tacos are already satisfied.\n\nNext, let's find an item for the 'orderable' list. 'Omelette' and 'churros' are the most liked (both by 16 individuals) and also have a reasonable amount of dislikes which we can use to satisfy the individuals who don't have their liked items on the 'orderable' list. Between 'omelette' and 'churros,' we will pick 'omelette' for the 'orderable' list because fewer people dislike it (10 dislikes against 11 for 'churros'), which reduces the risk of dissatisfaction.\n\nNow, let's see if there is any individual who does not like 'omelette' and also likes 'tacos'. Such individuals would not be satisfied by our current selection. By scanning the list, we find Raj, Ty, Neil, Anil, Li, Liv, and Jan. They all have other likes or dislikes that we can use to satisfy them. For example, Raj dislikes 'shrimps', so we can add 'shrimps' to the 'not_orderable' list to satisfy Raj. However, by adding 'shrimps' to the 'not_orderable' list, we need to make sure it doesn't conflict with anyone else's preferences. \n\nUpon checking the list, we find that 'shrimps' is disliked by 16 individuals and liked by only 8. Adding 'shrimps' to the 'not_orderable' list seems safe since for the individuals who liked 'shrimps', they also like 'omelette' or other items.\n\nOnce we've added 'shrimps' to the 'not_orderable' list, now we need to check each person again to ensure they each have at least one like in 'orderable' or one dislike in 'not_orderable'.\n\nTo summarize, our 'orderable' list starts with 'omelette', and our 'not_orderable' list 