 Hu, Fay, Naina, Eli, Kit, Om, Du, Qi, Hal, Max, Zan, Pia, Vic, Kavi\nTart Dislikes: Meg, Riya, Ru, Gus, Jun, Ivy, Nita, Leo, Abe, Ned\n\nWonton Likes: Fay, Riya, Ben, Om, Pia, Lin, Max, Ivy\nWonton Dislikes: Hu, Eli, Meg, Kit, Ru, Kavi, Jun, Nita, Leo, Chen, Vic, Zan, Abe, Ned\n\nRavioli Likes: Naina, Meg, Kit, Ru, Gus, Qi, Hal, Vic, Ivy, Lin\nRavioli Dislikes: Hu, Fay, Eli, Om, Du, Jun, Pia, Ben, Chen, Abe, Ned\n\nHummus Likes: Naina, Ru, Du, Qi, Hal, Isha, Nita, Lin, Leo, Max, Kavi\nHummus Dislikes: Eli, Meg, Kit, Gus, Jun, Zan, Chen, Ben, Abe, Ned\n\nBased on the dislikes of Jun, Chen, Abe and Ned, it appears these four dislike all four items; therefore, to satisfy their satisfaction criteria, their disliked items must be in the 'not_orderable' list. This would require placing all items in the 'not_orderable'. However, this can't work because every other person has at least one like, so their satisfaction criteria would not be met.\n\nAs there is no item that is liked by someone and not disliked by someone else, it is impossible to satisfy everyone. Therefore, the 'orderable' and 'not_orderable' lists must be empty to reflect that we cannot place an order that satisfies all people in the group. \n\nThe solution is to output both lists as empty:\n\n```python\norderable=[], not_orderable=[]\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 576, 'num_completion_tokens': 479}
