kes nachos. Dislikes falafel, kimchi. Ty: Likes kimchi, falafel, mousse. Jim: Likes muffins, shawarma. Dislikes nachos. Zara: Likes mousse, kimchi. Dislikes shawarma. Shan: Likes shawarma. Dislikes kimchi, nachos. Tao: Likes shawarma, muffins, mousse. Lex: Likes nachos, mousse. Dislikes muffins. Rohan: Likes muffins, shawarma. Dislikes falafel. Ravi: Likes nachos, kimchi, muffins. Eva: Likes falafel, nachos. Dislikes muffins. Wes: Likes kimchi. Dislikes shawarma, nachos. Gus: Likes mousse, muffins. Dislikes nachos. Ping: Likes muffins, mousse. Dislikes shawarma. Mei: Likes mousse. Dislikes muffins, kimchi. Geet: Likes falafel. Dislikes mousse, muffins. Kit: Likes mousse, shawarma, kimchi. Tim: Dislikes kimchi, mousse, nachos. Leo: Dislikes shawarma, kimchi, muffins. Rina: Likes kimchi. Dislikes muffins, falafel. Sid: Likes mousse, muffins, kimchi. Du: Likes nachos, kimchi. Dislikes mousse. Raj: Likes shawarma. Dislikes nachos, mousse. Tara: Likes mousse, nachos, shawarma. Yan: Likes muffins, kimchi. Dislikes shawarma. Kai: Likes muffins, mousse. Dislikes nachos. Vic: Likes falafel. Dislikes mousse, nachos. Isha: Likes muffins. Dislikes shawarma, nachos. Ying: Likes muffins. Dislikes falafel, mousse. Lia: Likes nachos, kimchi, muffins. Rob: Likes nachos, falafel. Dislikes mousse. Ansh: Likes muffins. Dislikes kimchi, mousse. Sol: Likes falafel, nachos. Dislikes muffins. Pia: Likes muffins, nachos. Dislikes falafel. Lin: Likes kimchi. Dislikes shawarma, falafel. ', 'menu_items': ['muffins', 'nachos', 'falafel', 'shawarma', 'kimchi', 'mousse'], 'gpt_out': 'To address this complex preference puzzle, we need to find a combination of food items such that:\n\n- At least one liked item is orderable OR one disliked item is not orderable for each individual.\n- No item can appear on both lists, "orderable" and "not_orderable".\n- All participants must be satisfied by the combination of the two lists.\n\nTo tackle this task, I will first examine the commonly disliked items, since putting a commonly disliked item in the "not_orderable" list can satisfy multiple people at once. Similarly, a commonly liked item that is not commonly disliked can go in the "orderable" list.\n\nFirst, we need to identify food items and their like-dislike count. However, we must first look for deal-breakers – items that are universally disliked by at least one person and liked by others. If such an item exists, there will be no possible solution, as satisfying one person would dissatisfy the other.\n\nLooking at the dislikes first:\n\n1. "shawarma" is disliked by Yue, Tia, Pam, Max, Jaya, Arun, Wes, Shan, Leo, Raj, Isha, Tim, Yan, and Lin (14 people).\n2. "kimchi" is disliked by Yue, Bo, Xan, Mei, Rina, Tim, Leo, Ansh, Lin (9 people).\n3. "falafel" is disliked by Mel, Kavi, Yue, Max, Hui, Jim, Xan, Ru, Ada, Ty, Leo, Ying, Lin, Sol, Pia (15 people).\n4. "mousse" is disliked by Feng, Veer, Ken, Zac, Mei, Geet, Tim, Ansh, Ying (9 people).\n5. "nachos" is disliked by Taj, Mel, Eli, Zac, Gus, Kai, Isha, Vic, Tim (9 people).\n6. "muffins" is disliked by Tia, Yue, Jaya, Xan, Mei, Geet, Leo, Sol (8 people).\n\nAnd looking at the likes:\n\n1. Everyone who dislikes "shawarma" has other items to consume.\n2. Everyone who dislikes "kimchi" has other options except Yue.\n3. Everyone who dislikes "falafel" has other options.\n4. Everyone who dislikes "mousse" has other options.\n5. Everyone who dislikes "nachos" has other options except Arun and Tim.\n6. Everyone who dislikes "muffins" has other options.\n\nFrom this, we can note that Yue, Arun, and Tim are special cases.\n\nYue dislikes kimchi, falafel, and shawarma, and only has a muffin option. To satisfy Yue, we must include "muffins" in the orderable list because muffins are not universally disliked by anyone. Arun dislikes kimchi, falafel, and nachos, and only has the muffin option left, which is fine because it\'s orderable. Tim dislikes kimchi, mousse, and nachos, and only has the options of falafel and shawarma.\n\nThus, "muffins" appears to be a key item to include in the "orderable" list, as it\'s required to satisfy crucial pivot people like Yue and Arun, and it\'s not disliked enough to cause widespread dissatisfaction.\n\nIf "muffins" goes into the "orderable" list, then to satisfy Tim, we must put one of "falafel" or "shawarma" in the "orderable" list or the other in the "not_orderable" list. However, putting "falafel" in the "orderable" list would dissatisfy Mel, Kavi, Max, Hui, Jim, Ru, Ada, Ty, Leo, Ying, Lin, Sol, Pia, and several others, similar to "shawarma". And "shawarma" cannot be in "not_orderable" because that would dissatisfy Mel who doesn\'t have any other likes.\n\nTherefore, given these constraints and the intersecting dislikes and likes, it seems we have run into a condition where no combination will satisfy every individual based on the set rules. Consequently, the result is empty lists for both \'orderable\' and \'not_orderable\'.\n\nHere is the final solution representation in Python code:\n\n```python\norderable = []\nnot_orderable = []\n```', 'num_prompt_tokens': 1082, 'num_completion_tokens': 910}
Data Sample: {'num_vars': 8, 'num_clauses': 24, 'formula': [[6, -4, 7], [5, 4, 2], [7, 3, -2], [-6, -2, -7], [-3, -5, -6], [-3, 1, -2], [-2, -3, 7], [6, 2, 3], [3, -4, -7], [8, 6, 5], [6, -1, 5], [-2, 5, -7], [6, -2, -3], [2, 6, 4], [2, 5, 6], [7, -8, -4], [3, -6, 8], [-7, 8, -5], [7, 8, 1], [2, -3, -1], [-1, 2, 4], [5, -1, 6], [-3, 1, 4], [7, -1, 3]], 'is_sat': True, 'preferences': 'Kai: Likes biscuits, frittata. Dislikes wonton. Jaya: Likes salmon, wonton, vindaloo. Dot: Likes frittata, soup. Dislikes vindaloo. Lin: Dislikes biscuits, vindaloo, frittata. Aarav: Dislikes soup, salmon, biscuits. Lex: Likes mousse. Dislikes soup, vindaloo. Bao: Likes frittata. Dislikes vindaloo, soup. Kim: Likes biscuits, vindaloo, soup. Ken: Likes soup. Dislikes wonton, frittata. Max: Likes vegetables, biscuits, salmon. Oli: Likes biscuits, salmon. Dislikes mousse. Xan: Likes salmon. Dislikes vindaloo, frittata. Sid: Likes biscuits. Dislikes vindaloo, soup. Min: Likes vindaloo, biscuits, wonton. Pia: Likes vindaloo, salmon, biscuits. Jon: Likes frittata. Dislikes vegetables, wonton. Dan: Likes soup, vegetables. Dislikes biscuits. Rina: Likes vegetables. Dislikes frittata, salmon. Veer: Likes frittata, vegetables, mousse. Zan: Likes vindaloo. Dislikes soup, mousse. Bea: Likes vindaloo, wonton. Dislikes mousse. Zoe: Likes salmon, biscuits. Dislikes mousse. Val: Likes mousse, wonton. Dislikes soup. Tim: Likes frittata, soup. Dislikes mousse. ', 'menu_items': ['mousse', 'vindaloo', 'soup', 'wonton', 'salmon', 'biscuits', 'frittata', 'vegetables'], 'gpt_out': "To determine the 'orderable' and 'not_orderable' lists, let's go step-by-step through the preferences of each individual, identifying common likes and dislikes where possible to satisfy the most individuals.\n\n1. Identify items with exclusive likes or dislikes.\n- Mousse is only liked by Lex, Veer, and Val and disliked by Oli, Zan, Zoe, and Tim. Since more people dislike it than like it, we can consider placing mousse in 'not_orderable'.\n- Vegetables are liked by Dan, Rina, Veer and disliked by Jon. We should consider placing vegetables in 'orderable'.\n\n2. Identify items that are universally disliked or not disliked by anyone (ignoring the previous step where exclusivity was found):\n- There are no items that are universally liked or disliked.\n\n3. Check for conflicts (based on step 1 and 2) and look for a common ground:\n- Biscuits are liked by Kai, Max, Oli, Sid, Min, Pia, and Zoe but disliked by Lin and Aarav. To maximize satisfaction, we'd consider biscuits as 'orderable'.\n- Salmon is liked by Jaya, Oli, Max, Pia, Xan, and Zoe but disliked by Aarav and Rina. To maximize satisfaction, we should also consider salmon as 'orderable'.\n- Frittata is liked by Kai, Dot, Bao, Jon, Tim, Veer, and Zan, but disliked by Lin and Ken. Since more people like it than dislike it, we should consider frittata as 'orderable'.\n- Soup is liked by Dot, Kim, Ken, and Dan but disliked by Dot, Bao, Sid, and Zan. Since it's equally liked and disliked, we must look deeper.\n- Vindaloo is liked by Jaya, Kim, Min, Pia, and Bea but disliked by Dot, Lin, Lex, Bao, Xan, Sid, and Zan. Since more people dislike it than like it, we should consider vindaloo as 'not_orderable'.\n- Wonton is liked by Jaya, Min, Bea, and Val but disliked by Kai, Ken, and Jon. Since less people dislike it than like it, we should keep it 'orderable' unless it is needed in 'not_orderable' to satisfy someone.\n\n4. Check whether every individual can be satisfied:\n- For Kai to be satisfied, we must ensure biscuits or frittata are 'orderable' or wonton is 'not_orderable'.\n- Jaya will be satisfied if salmon, wonton, or vindaloo are 'orderable'. We've already considered vindaloo as 'not_orderable', so we need to keep either salmon or wonton 'orderable' to satisfy Jaya.\n- And so on for each individual. This balancing act requires ensuring we don't place an item that someone likes in 'not_orderable' if they don't have another liked item in 'orderable' or vice versa.\n\n5. Resolve conflicts:\n- The issue we have is around soup. Both liked and disliked by four individuals. The placeholders we have are mousse in 'not_orderable' and vegetables in 'orderable'. This gives us a starting point. Since soup is contentious, we will focus on satisfying other preferences first.\n\nBy following the logic above and trying to satisfy the most individuals possible, we can draft initial lists:\n\nOrderable: vegetables, biscuits, salmon, frittata\nNot_orderable: mousse, vindaloo\n\nNow we should iterate through the list to make sure everyone is satisfied:\n\n- Kai is satisfied (frittata is orderable).\n- Jaya is satisfied (salmon is orderable).\n- Dot is satisfied (frittata is orderable, vindaloo is not_orderable).\n- Lin is satisfied (biscuits, vindaloo, frittata are not in the same list and at least one dislike is in 'not_orderable').\n- Aarav is satisfied (biscuits are orderable, but salmon and soup are not in 'not_orderable').\n- Lex is satisfied (mousse is not_orderable).\n- Bao is satisfied (frittata is orderable, vindaloo and soup are not together).\n- And so on, checking each individual’s satisfaction.\n\nKim and Ken present an issue where we currently make neither of them happy (as Kim likes soup, which is currently neither 'orderable' nor 'not_orderable', and Ken dislikes wonton but currently has no likes in 'orderable'). This suggests that we should put wonton in 'not_orderable' to satisfy Ken, as we have an alternative for Jaya (salmon) in the 'orderable' list.\n\nOur adjusted lists then are:\n\nOrderable: vegetables, biscuits, salmon, frittata\nNot_orderable: mousse, vindaloo, wonton\n\nNow we need to check if anyone is left unsatisfied with this arrangement. Running through the list again and checking each individual, it seems that everyone has at least one like in 'orderable' or one dislike in 'not_orderable', which means everyone is satisfied.\n\nTherefore, our final lists are:\n\norderable = ['vegetables', 'biscuits', 'salmon', 'frittata']\nnot_orderable = ['mousse', 'vindaloo', 'wonton']", 'num_prompt_tokens': 512, 'num_completion_tokens': 1116}
