nd burrito.\n\nLet's see if we can put burrito on the 'orderable' list:\n- Everyone who likes burrito (Hal, Joy, Abe, May, Chen, Ravi, Fay, Liz, Min, Zoe, Wei, Jan, Geet, Sam, Ansh, Sal, Tara, Val, Meg) doesn't have a critical dislike for it.\n- Neil, who dislikes burrito, likes custard. Since both shawarma and churros are heavily disliked, custard may be placed on the 'not_orderable' list to satisfy Neil.\n\nNow let's consider wonton:\n- Everyone who likes wonton (Joy, Ravi, Xan, Ian, Min, Zoe, Sid, Lei, Lan, Kim, Ivy, Li) has no critical dislikes.\n- Those who dislike wonton like at least one item from the 'orderable' list (burrito).\n\nThis analysis leads us to the following preliminary decision:\n- Burrito can be on the 'orderable' list.\n- Wonton can also be on the 'orderable' list.\n- Shawarma and churros, which are commonly disliked, should be on the 'not_orderable' list.\n- Custard should be on the 'not_orderable' list because Neil, who dislikes burrito, can remain satisfied as long as custard remains unavailable.\n\nThe lists would be as follows:\n- Orderable: burrito, wonton\n- Not_Orderable: shawarma, churros, custard\n\nTo ensure these lists work, we need to confirm that everyone is satisfied according to the given rules.\n\nVerification:\n\n1. Everyone who likes burrito or wonton and doesn't dislike the other will be satisfied with the 'orderable' list.\n2. Everyone who dislikes shawarma, churros, or custard will be satisfied with the 'not_orderable' list.\n3. Neil, who dislikes burrito, will be satisfied with custard on the 'not_orderable' list.\n\nSince each person will be satisfied with at least one item they like on 'orderable' or dislike on 'not_orderable', the above lists fulfill all the conditions.\n\nThe final output is:\n\n```python\norderable=['burrito', 'wonton'], not_orderable=['shawarma', 'churros', 'custard']\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 875, 'num_completion_tokens': 775}
