ne found that meet this strict criteria.\n\nNext, we\'ll find the items that are either liked or disliked but not both by many which will help to split the lists such that we can satisfy the most number of people:\n\nFor example, if an item is liked by many and disliked by few, we might consider adding it to \'orderable\', and vice versa for \'not_orderable\'.\n\nTracking likes vs dislikes:\n- Tacos are liked by Xan, Arun, Ping, Sky, Hal, Chen, Ty but disliked by Vic, May, Jan, Ava, Ada, Bea, Rex, Ana, Max, Hui, Kavi, Tim which makes it more controversial and might not be easy to place in either list at this stage.\n- Shawarma is liked by several people but disliked by a notable amount as well. Same for falafel.\n\nBy now, it\'s clear that we need a strategic approach to satisfy everyone\'s restrictions since there are no easy winners for the \'orderable\' list. "Greedy" selections into either list (simply putting items liked by many into \'orderable\' and those disliked by many into \'not_orderable\') won\'t work because there are complicated interdependencies.\n\nThe next step is an iterative one, where we must start by placing a food item in one list and then adjust based on the satisfaction of each individual.\n\nLet\'s start with the \'not_orderable\' list by adding brownies, since it\'s the most commonly disliked item and no one likes it. We\'ll also track who\'s satisfied with this choice:\n- Adding brownies to \'not_orderable\' satisfies Cy, Jan, Wes, Chao, Ravi, Dot, Sid, Chen.\n\nThen we continue by looking at items with the most dislikes against the fewest likes, to add to \'not_orderable\', and those with most likes against fewest dislikes, to add to \'orderable\':\n\nLooking at the list, we can see that vegetables are disliked by May, Jay, Mia, Eva, Oli, Wei, and Chao, but liked by some, so we cannot easily place it in either list.\n\nSoup is liked by many but also disliked by Zara, Jay, Wes, Min, Isha, Joy, Kim. It\'s risky to put it into \'orderable\'