usly disliked or liked since these are good candidates for \'not_orderable\' or \'orderable\' respectively.\n\n1. No food item is liked by everyone.\n2. No food item is disliked by everyone.\n\nNext, we need to consider options to satisfy everyone. We look for items that are:\n\n- Liked by some people and not disliked by anyone – these can be placed in \'orderable\' straight away.\n- Disliked by some people and not liked by anyone – these can be placed in \'not_orderable\' straight away.\n\nHowever, after reviewing the list, it\'s clear there\'s no such unanimously liked or disliked item that could instantly be assigned to \'orderable\' or \'not_orderable\'. \n\nTherefore, we’ll start working with the satisfaction criteria, trying to satisfy as many people as possible with the least controversial choices:\n\n- Looking at "churros", it\'s liked by several people and only disliked by a few. Since the satisfaction criteria states that a person is satisfied if at least one liked item is \'orderable\' or one disliked item is \'not_orderable\', we will choose "churros" for \'orderable\'. The few people who dislike "churros" might be satisfied by other items in their \'like\' list being \'orderable\' or other items in their \'dislike\' list being \'not_orderable\'.\n- We cannot add any item to \'orderable\' if it\'s disliked by someone unless it is absolutely necessary to satisfy someone who likes the item and cannot be satisfied by any other means.\n\nLet\'s iterate through people\'s preferences to fill \'orderable\' and \'not_orderable\' lists:\n\n1. Start with empty \'orderable\' and \'not_orderable\' lists.\n2. Add "churros" to the \'orderable\' list, satisfying Lia, Tim, Jay, Ava, Ray, Ivy, Nita, Lex, Diya, Oli, and others who like "churros".\n3. To satisfy people who like "toast" and those who dislike "quiche", add "toast" to \'orderable\'.\n4. Everyone who likes "burger" (not universally liked or disliked) also likes at least one of "toast" or "churros", so we don\'