(disliked by Ned, Wei, Ula): Tao likes caramel but dislikes ravioli which is already ‘not_orderable’. Om likes caramel but dislikes nachos so he has no other options if caramel is not orderable. Hence, Caramel can\'t be ‘not_orderable’.\n- Lobster (disliked by Kai, Tim): No conflicts, so it can be \'not_orderable\'. Both are satisfied.\n  \nNext, let\'s look at \'orderable\':\n\n- Risotto is liked by Jim, Rina, Nita, Kai, Chao but is disliked by Ada and Val who both like nachos, which are not on either list yet. So, risotto can be \'orderable\' to satisfy their preferences.\n- Nachos liked by Rina and Chao; is not disliked by anybody left so it can be \'orderable\'.\n- Biscuits cannot be \'orderable\' because it\'s disliked by Zara and Nita whom have no alternatives they like better. \n- Bruschetta is liked by Raj, Sky, Wei; Sky and Wei dislike fries already ‘not_orderable’. Raj also likes biscuits which have no conflict so far, so bruschetta can be \'orderable\'.\n\nAfter adding risotto, nachos and bruschetta into the \'orderable\' list, let\'s check if all participants are satisfied:\n\nJim: likes risotto (orderable), satisfied.\nZara: dislikes biscuits, fries (both not_orderable), satisfied.\nRaj: likes fries, biscuits (not_orderable), bruschetta (orderable), satisfied.\nOm: Dislikes nachos but likes caramel, which has no disliked conflict, so caramel can be \'orderable\', satisfied.\nAda: Dislikes risotto (orderable), satisfied.\nNed: Dislikes caramel (not on either list but Ned likes ravioli which is \'not_orderable\'), satisfied.\nVal: Dislikes risotto (orderable), satisfied.\nWei: Likes bruschetta (orderable), satisfied.\nTao: Likes biscuits (neither list), dislikes ravioli (not_orderable), could be satisfied if biscuits are \'orderable\', as caramel not possible.\nSky: Likes bruschetta (orderable), biscuits (not on either list), satisfied.\nGeet: Likes biscuits, nachos (orderable), satisfied.\nRohan: Dislikes poke (not_orderable), likes lobster (not_orderable) which is not a problem because he also likes ravioli (not_orderable), satisfied.\nRina: Likes risotto (orderable), satisfied.\nNita: Likes fries (not_orderable), satisfied.\nKai: Dislikes lobster (not_orderable) but likes risotto which is \'orderable\', satisfied.\nMax: Dislikes brownies, fries (both not_orderable), satisfied.\nTim: Likes biscuits (not on either list), can be satisfied if biscuits are \'orderable\'.\nUla: Dislikes caramel (not on either list), she likes fries which are \'not_orderable\', satisfied.\nTy: Dislikes ravioli (not_orderable), no conflicts, satisfied.\nChao: Likes risotto (orderable), satisfied.\n\nIt looks like if we add biscuits to \'orderable\', everyone will be satisfied. Tao and Tim need this to be satisfied, and it does not conflict with any current items (since the people disliking biscuits are already satisfied by other items).\n\nThis results in both lists being:\norderable = [risotto, nachos, bruschetta, caramel, biscuits]\nnot_orderable = [poke, brownies, ravioli, fries, lobster]\n\nSo the final output is:\norderable = [\'risotto\', \'nachos\', \'bruschetta\', \'caramel\', \'biscuits\'],\nnot_orderable = [\'poke\', \'brownies\', \'ravioli\', \'fries\', \'lobster\']', 'num_prompt_tokens': 448, 'num_completion_tokens': 1515}
