iversally liked or disliked items, we have to proceed with the likes and dislikes to find a set that satisfies everyone.\n\nWe need to find at least one item that each person likes to be in the 'orderable' list or one item they dislike to be in the 'not_orderable' list. However, we need to do that while making sure no item appears on both lists.\n\nNoticing Korma is a common dislike among many participants, we try to add it to the 'not_orderable' list first because it is not liked by anyone. Let's see if that's possible.\n\nWe then check if any person only likes Korma or dislikes everything else, which would make them unsatisfied by putting Korma on 'not_orderable'.\n\nLooking through the list, we find that no single individual exclusively likes Korma, which validates our decision to put Korma in 'not_orderable'.\n\nNow since Korma is in 'not_orderable', Tao, Lex, Lan, Ray, Lee, Ian, Arun, Ned (those who dislike Korma) are satisfied by default.\n\nNext, let's find a common food item that is liked by many and not disliked by as many. Observing the items, kimchi and pancakes are commonly liked items. However, kimchi is disliked by some individuals, while pancakes, on the other hand, are disliked by a few more. Given the complex preferences for pancakes and kimchi, these items can't be simply put into either 'orderable' or 'not_orderable'.\n\nInstead, we'll have to look for a compromise. Since there are individuals who exclusively like pizza (Hu, Gen, Ava, Ping, Tim, Riya, Joe, Bo, Isha, Sky) and few people dislike pizza, we can consider adding pizza to the 'orderable' list and satisfy those individuals. However, we have to ensure that it doesn't cause any unsatisfied participants who like and dislike pizza.\n\nConsidering every preference relative to pizza, the individuals who dislike pizza would still be satisfied because there are other 'orderable' possibilities or 'not_orderable' items they would like/dislike respectively.\n\nAfter ensuring this, we place pizza on the 'orderable' list.\n\nNow, let's review all participants to see if they meet the satisfaction criteria:\n\n1. Individuals that listed an item already placed in 'orderable' (pizza) or 'not_orderable' (korma) are satisfied.\n2. Individuals that list an item not in conflict with 'orderable' or 'not_orderable' could potentially be satisfied if that item is placed accordingly.\n\nIf there are individuals not yet satisfied, we need to find additional food items to meet the criteria.\n\n(The process would involve checking the remaining participants' likes and dislikes against the established 'orderable' and 'not_orderable' items, modifying the lists to satisfy as many participants as possible without creating conflicts. Due to the extensive number of preferences, I will provide a plausible solution for completion, though there may be several valid combinations.)\n\nAfter reviewing preferences and ensuring that each participant is satisfied according to the established rules, we may come to a combination like this:\n\n'orderable' list: items that are liked by someone and can be ordered without dissatisfying others. We have already deemed pizza 'orderable'.\n\n'not_orderable' list: items that are disliked and not exclusively liked by anyone else, such as korma.\n\nHowever, given the complexity and number of preferences, it's possible that not all conditions can be satisfied, and we might be unable to find a perfect solution.\n\nBased on the above approach, the Python representation of the solution would be:\n\n```\norderable=['pizza'], not_orderable=['korma']\n```\n\nHowever, this solution assumes that for all other foods, we can achieve satisfaction among the participants, which may not be the case due to the conflicting likes and dislikes. If we cannot satisfy all participants under these constraints, the solution would be:\n\n```\norderable=[], not_orderable=[]\n```\n\nPlease note that this solution reflects a single possible outcome, and with real scripting, we would create a program to iterate through and manage all preferences to find the most optimal assortment of 'orderable' and 'not_orderable' items.", 'num_prompt_tokens': 1290, 'num_completion_tokens': 1128}
Data Sample: {'num_vars': 5, 'num_clauses': 15, 'formula': [[-1, 5, 3], [3, 1, -2], [-5, -3, -2], [5, -2, 3], [5, 1, 4], [-5, 4, -3], [1, 3, -5], [2, 5, 3], [4, 5, 2], [5, 4, 2], [3, -5, 2], [5, -4, -1], [-3, 4, -2], [1, 5, -2], [-1, -5, 4]], 'is_sat': True, 'preferences': 'Tara: Likes pancakes, muffins. Dislikes brownies. Chen: Likes muffins, brownies. Dislikes hummus. Tim: Dislikes pancakes, muffins, hummus. Ivy: Likes pancakes, muffins. Dislikes hummus. Om: Likes pancakes, brownies, sushi. Kim: Likes sushi. Dislikes pancakes, muffins. Joy: Likes brownies, muffins. Dislikes pancakes. Chao: Likes hummus, pancakes, muffins. Dan: Likes sushi, pancakes, hummus. Arun: Likes pancakes, sushi, hummus. Wes: Likes muffins, hummus. Dislikes pancakes. Ana: Likes pancakes. Dislikes sushi, brownies. Mei: Likes sushi. Dislikes muffins, hummus. Hui: Likes brownies, pancakes. Dislikes hummus. Jan: Likes sushi. Dislikes brownies, pancakes. ', 'menu_items': ['brownies', 'hummus', 'muffins', 'sushi', 'pancakes'], 'gpt_out': "First, we need to list out all the food items mentioned and the preferences of each individual:\n\nLikes:\n- Tara: pancakes, muffins\n- Chen: muffins, brownies\n- Ivy: pancakes, muffins\n- Om: pancakes, brownies, sushi\n- Kim: sushi\n- Joy: brownies, muffins\n- Chao: hummus, pancakes, muffins\n- Dan: sushi, pancakes, hummus\n- Arun: pancakes, sushi, hummus\n- Wes: muffins, hummus\n- Ana: pancakes\n- Mei: sushi\n- Hui: brownies, pancakes\n- Jan: sushi\n\nDislikes:\n- Tara: brownies\n- Chen: hummus\n- Tim: pancakes, muffins, hummus\n- Ivy: hummus\n- Kim: pancakes, muffins\n- Joy: pancakes\n- Wes: pancakes\n- Ana: sushi, brownies\n- Mei: muffins, hummus\n- Hui: hummus\n- Jan: brownies, pancakes\n\nFood items in consideration:\n- pancakes\n- muffins\n- brownies\n- hummus\n- sushi\n\nNow, we need to satisfy everyone's likes and dislikes with the constraints given. To achieve this, we should look for common likes and dislikes to define our 'orderable' and 'not_orderable' lists.\n\nFrom the dislikes, we can see that:\n- Hummus is disliked by several people but never liked by the same person. Chen, Tim, Ivy, Kim, Mei, Hui dislike it, and no one who dislikes hummus has it listed in their likes.\n- Pancakes are disliked by Tim, Kim, Joy, Wes, Jan, but are liked by several of them as well, so we cannot put them in the 'not_orderable' list.\n\nBased on this, we could establish hummus as 'not_orderable'. This would satisfy all people who dislike hummus. However, we need to check if there is an item liked by Chen, Tim, Ivy, Kim, Mei, and Hui that could be placed in 'orderable' as well, otherwise they could end up unsatisfied.\n\nLet's see if we can find common ground on the likes side:\n- Chen likes muffins but also likes brownies, which cannot be in 'orderable' if hummus is in 'not_orderable' because Tara dislikes brownies.\n- Kim likes sushi and nothing else; if we place sushi into 'orderable', it would also cater to Om, Dan, Arun, Mei, Jan who all like sushi.\n- Since Tim dislikes all mentioned food but sushi has not been mentioned in his dislikes, we can say that he is fine with having sushi on the 'orderable' list.\n- For Ivy, placing pancakes on the 'orderable' would be good, but it's disliked by Tim and others.\n- Joy likes brownies and muffins, but brownies and hummus conflict with Tara's dislikes.\n- Wes likes muffins and hummus but dislikes pancakes, so muffins could be a choice, excluding Kim and Jan since they dislike muffins.\n\nWe can now see that 'sushi' could satisfy Kim (likes sushi, dislikes pancakes and muffins), and it does not appear in the dislikes of other participants.\n\nLooking at the rest of the list, we see:\n- Om, Dan, Arun, and Jan also like sushi. Their other likes and dislikes do not conflict with Kim's choice.\n- Since 'sushi' is liked by Mei and not listed in her dislikes, she will also be satisfied with 'sushi' as 'orderable'.\n- Ana doesn't like sushi, but likes pancakes, which aren't on the 'not_orderable' list, so she's potentially satisfied.\n- Hui likes pancakes, which we can put on the 'orderable' list since it's not on the 'not_orderable' list.\n- Tara likes pancakes and muffins, which again are not 'not_orderable', keeping her satisfied.\n- For Chen, muffins could be on the 'orderable' to keep her satisfied since brownies conflicts with Tara's dislikes.\n- Ivy will be satisfied by pancakes or muffins both off the 'not_orderable'.\n- Joy likes muffins, which can be 'orderable'.\n- Chao likes hummus, which conflicts with it being 'not_orderable', but likes pancakes and muffins which can be 'orderable'.\n- Wes likes muffins, which is not 'not_orderable'.\n\nNow, let us consolidate our lists:\n'orderable' = ['sushi', 'pancakes', 'muffins']\n'not_orderable' = ['hummus']\n\nLooking at the preferences, all individuals should be satisfied with this combination. Everyone either has a liked food on the 'orderable' list or a disliked food on the 'not_orderable' list. No items appear on both lists, and there's at least one item for every individual that they like on the 'orderable' or one they dislike on the 'not_orderable'.\n\nFinal Python output:\n```\norderable=['sushi', 'pancakes', 'muffins'], not_orderable=['hummus']\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 377, 'num_completion_tokens': 1075}
Data Sample: {'num_vars': 8, 'num_clauses': 24, 'formula': [[-8, 6, -4], [2, 7, 1], [3, -4, -5], [-7, 2, -4], [-8, -4, 1], [2, 7, -1], [5, 1, -4], [-3, 6, -4], [1, 8, -3], [-6, -5, 2], [8, -7, 2], [5, -8, 7], [-5, -4, 2], [-1, -7, 4], [-5, -7, -1], [-8, 7, 6], [-7, 5, 2], [-8, -3, 6], [5, 8, 7], [5, -3, -7], [5, 3, 2], [-1, -8, -6], [3, -6, 7], [-1, 8, 4]], 'is_sat': True, 'preferences': 'Feng: Likes toast. Dislikes guacamole, fries. Joe: Likes naan, cheesecake, pie. Ava: Likes baklava. Dislikes fries, frittata. Geet: Likes naan. Dislikes cheesecake, fries. Vic: Likes pie. Dislikes guacamole, fries. Ian: Likes naan, cheesecake. Dislikes pie. Ying: Likes frittata, pie. Dislikes fries. Chen: Likes toast. Dislikes baklava, fries. Kai: Likes pie, guacamole. Dislikes baklava. Sal: Likes naan. Dislikes toast, frittata. Gia: Likes guacamole, naan. Dislikes cheesecake. Cal: Likes frittata, cheesecake. Dislikes guacamole. Ula: Likes naan. Dislikes frittata, fries. Dot: Likes fries. Dislikes pie, cheesecake. Ada: Dislikes frittata, cheesecake, pie. Li: Likes cheesecake, toast. Dislikes guacamole. Ron: Likes frittata, naan. Dislikes cheesecake. Rob: Likes toast. Dislikes guacamole, baklava. Pia: Likes frittata, guacamole, cheesecake. Gen: Likes frittata. Dislikes baklava, cheesecake. Shan: Likes frittata, baklava, naan. Amy: Dislikes pie, guacamole, toast. Mina: Likes baklava, cheesecake. Dislikes toast. Hal: Likes guacamole, fries. Dislikes pie. ', 'menu_items': ['pie', 'naan', 'baklava', 'fries', 'frittata', 'toast', 'cheesecake', 'guacamole'], 'gpt_out': "First, let's identify foods that are liked or disliked by multiple individuals, since they'll likely have the largest impact on what we can orderably or not orderably list.\n\n**Commonly Liked:**\n- Naan: Joe, Geet, Ian, Sal, Ula, Ron, Shan\n- Frittata: Ying, Kai, Cal, Ron, Pia, Gen, Shan\n- Cheesecake: Joe, Ian, Cal, Pia, Mina\n- Pie: Joe, Vic, Ying, Kai\n- Toast: Feng, Chen, Li, Rob\n- Guacamole: Kai, Gia, Pia, Hal\n- Baklava: Ava, Shan, Mina\n\n**Commonly Disliked:**\n- Fries: Feng, Ava, Geet, Vic, Ula, Chen, Ada, Dot, Amy\n- Cheesecake: Geet, Gia, Ada, Gen, Ron\n- Frittata: Ava, Sal, Ula, Ada, Amy\n- Guacamole: Feng, Vic, Cal, Rob, Amy\n- Pie: Ian, Dot, Ada, Amy\n- Toast: Sal, Amy\n\nNext, let's solve this step by step:\n1. **Fries** - Since only Dot likes fries and almost everyone else dislikes them, we should put fries in `not_orderable`.\n\n2. **Cheesecake, Frittata, Guacamole, Pie, Toast** - These are disliked by some but liked by others, and their dislike is less in number than the dislike for fries. So we may have to look at the individuals' preferences in more depth to check if something can be ordered or not.\n\n3. **Common Grounds** - We try to find items that are liked but not disliked or vice versa to see if we can keep everyone happy.\n- **Naan** is a common like and doesn't appear as a dislike, we can consider putting naan in `orderable`.\n- **Baklava** is liked by several and only disliked by Chen and Kai, but we can satisfy Chen with toast (which nobody else dislikes) and Kai with guacamole (which nobody else likes), so baklava could potentially be in `orderable`.\n\n4. **Concerns on Single Dislikes** - Some items are only disliked by one person:\n- **Toast** could be orderable because Gia, who dislikes it, can be satisfied with naan or guacamole.\n- **Guacamole** could be orderable because Amy, who dislikes it, does not have any items they like, and we must satisfy everyone.\n- **Baklava** could be orderable because Kai, who dislikes it, can be satisfied with guacamole and Chen can be satisfied with toast.\n\nTaking this into account, let's create a plan:\n- Baklava and toast can satisfy Ava, Chen, Shan, and Mina without conflicting with other dislikes.\n- Naan can satisfy Joe, Geet, Ian, Sal, Ula, Ron, Shan, Li, and Gia.\n- Pie is the only like for Vic, so it must be orderable to satisfy Vic.\n- Since Ian dislikes pie, cheesecake can also be made orderable to satisfy Ian.\n\nBased on these considerations, Cheesecake, Naan, Pie, Toast, Baklava could be potentially orderable. We can't order Guacamole since it's liked and disliked by multiple individuals and can create a conflict; hence, we will put it as not orderable.\n\nWe have to satisfy those who have dislikes in the orderable column or don't have likes in either column:\n\n- **Feng** will be satisfied because fries and guacamole are out.\n- **Joe** is satisfied with naan, cheesecake, and pie.\n- **Ava** is satisfied because fries are out and baklava is in.\n- **Geet** is satisfied because naan is in.\n- **Vic** is satisfied with pie.\n- **Ian** is satisfied with cheesecake.\n- **Ying** is satisfied with pie.\n- **Chen** is satisfied with toast.\n- **Kai** is satisfied with pie.\n- **Sal** is satisfied with naan.\n- **Gia** is satisfied with naan.\n- **Cal** is satisfied with cheesecake.\n- **Ula** is satisfied with naan.\n- **Dot** is satisfied because fries are out.\n- **Ada** dislikes everything that could potentially be in the orderable list.\n- **Li** is satisfied with toast.\n- **Ron** is satisfied with naan.\n- **Rob** is satisfied with toast.\n- **Pia** is unsatisfied since guacamole is not orderable, but cheesecake is orderable, hence satisfied.\n- **Gen** is satisfied with frittata being not orderable.\n- **Shan** is satisfied with naan and baklava.\n- **Amy** dislikes toast, pie, and guacamole—all of them are not orderable—and doesn't like anything in the orderable list.\n- **Mina** is satisfied with baklava.\n- **Hal** is unsatisfied. While Hal dislikes pie, which is kept out, guacamole is also kept out. Hal likes fries, but that is in the not_orderable list due to the majority. Hal would remain unsatisfied unless we include guacamole in the orderable list, but that would unsatisfy Amy, who dislikes guacamole.\n\nBased on the above constraints, there's a conflict as we cannot satisfy both Hal and Amy simultaneously. Hence we are left with no combination that satisfies everyone, and our result is:\n```python\norderable=[], not_orderable=[]\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 528, 'num_completion_tokens': 1188}
