ants (and no strong opposition as dislikes aren\'t unanimous)\n- Custard: Liked by 6 participants (and no strong opposition as dislikes aren\'t unanimous)\n\nCandidates for \'not_orderable\':\n- Pancakes: Disliked by the majority (8 participants)\n- Tacos: Disliked by the majority (11 participants)\n\nWe now look for combinations that would satisfy all individuals:\n\nStep 1:\n- If we place "Tacos" in the \'not_orderable\' list, it automatically satisfies those who dislike tacos and those who like it will still need an item they like in the \'orderable\' list. Considering the widespread dislike for tacos, it seems like a good candidate for \'not_orderable\'.\n\nStep 2:\n- Pizza has a high amount of likes but also has several dislikes, and the satisfaction criterion is that at least one item should be liked in the \'orderable\' list or disliked in the \'not_orderable\' list. Thus, placing pizza in either list won\'t work as it will not satisfy all participants. So, pizza must not be on either list.\n\nStep 3:\n- "Pancakes" could potentially be placed in the \'not_orderable\' list, satisfying a large number of participants. But placing it in the \'not_orderable\' would not satisfy Mel, so this option is not viable if selecting it alone without any other liked food for Mel.\n\nStep 4:\n- "Custard" seems like a good candidate for the \'orderable\' list because, although it\'s disliked by some participants, it is liked by six participants, some of which have no other likes, such as Bea and Ying.\n\nStep 5:\n- That leaves "Shrimps" as a possible \'orderable\' item. Most shrimp dislikers are either satisfied with \'orderable\' custard or \'not_orderable\' tacos (or pancakes). Also, those who like shrimps and nothing else will require it to be on the \'orderable\' list to be satisfied.\n\nCombination:\n\nAn optimal solution seems like:\n\n- \'orderable\': Custard, Shrimps\n- \'not_orderable\': Tacos, Pancakes\n\nNow, let\'s check if every individual is satisfied:\n\n- Meg: 