e need to check if this satisfies all:\n\n- Meg: Dislikes Tacos (\'not_orderable\').\n- Mel: Likes Custard and Pancakes (\'orderable\').\n- Jun: Dislikes Tacos (\'not_orderable\').\n- Hu: Likes Custard (\'orderable\').\n- Jaya: Is satisfied because she dislikes Tacos (\'not_orderable\').\n- Ben: Is not satisfied because Pizza can\'t be on \'orderable\' and he dislikes Custard (\'orderable\').\n- Qi: Likes Shrimps, which is no longer in any list but dislikes Tacos (\'not_orderable\').\n- Sky: Is not satisfied because he likes Pizza and dislikes Custard and Shrimps, with none of his likes in \'orderable\'.\n- Zan: Likes Shrimps, which is not in any list, but dislikes Tacos (\'not_orderable\').\n- Riya: Likes Shrimps and Pancakes (\'orderable\').\n- Mei: Dislikes Tacos (\'not_orderable\').\n- Jim: Likes Shrimps, which is not in any list but dislikes Tacos (\'not_orderable\').\n- Joe: Likes Custard (\'orderable\').\n- Ned: Likes Custard (\'orderable\').\n- Bea: Likes Custard (\'orderable\').\n- Ying: Likes Custard (\'orderable\').\n- Lou: Likes Pancakes (\'orderable\').\n- Kit: Dislikes Custard (\'orderable\'), but is satisfied because Tacos (\'not_orderable\') is disliked.\n- Jon: Likes Custard (\'orderable\').\n\nBased on the constraints, Ben and Sky are not satisfied with this combination as none of their likes appear on the \'orderable\' list, and their dislikes don\'t account for it.\n\nWith the given preferences, constructing a combination that satisfies all participants seems impossible, as we cannot find an \'orderable\' item that includes at least one liked item for every individual, and \'not_orderable\' that includes at least one disliked item for every individual. We encounter an unsolvable conflict with pizza being widely liked, yet enough individuals have a dislike for it that it cannot be on either list unanimously.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that there\'s no combination that satisfies all criteria, and the output should be two empty lists:\n\n`orderable=[