t, Ru, Val, Jim, Ava, Xan, and Mina, and disliked by Max, Kavi, Ada, Ivy, Qi, Ansh, Ken, Wei, May, Hal, Ty, Chao, Taj, Tia, Jan, Tao, Dan. Another conflicting item with many on both sides of like/dislike. Leave it aside.\n- **Custard**: Liked by Max, Jay, Ada, Leo, Geet, Wei, Liv, Vic, Rina, Chao, and disliked by Sal, Mia, Ivy, Ken, Ty, Jim, Taj, Mina, Oli, Xan. Another conflict.\n- **Caramel**: Liked by Sal, Jay, Kavi, Ada, Leo, Mia, Veer, Ken, Tom, Ru, Ava, Vic, Kit, Oli, and disqualified by Ivy, Qi, Ansh, Wei, Lan, Taj, Chao, Val, Gus, Ty, Jan. Another item with a contentious status.\n- **Doughnut**: Liked by Sal, Leo, Qi, May, Hal, Gen, Sia, Taj, Tia, and disliked by Jay, Geet, Ivy, Ru, Val, Liv, Kai, Tao. Doughnut also has likes and dislikes, but it\'s less controversial than tandoori or caramel.\n\nSince all the liked items also have dislikes, there is no straightforward solution. We must find a way to place items in a way that meets the conditions where every individual is satisfied.\n\nWe can start by observing that certain people dislike items that are not liked by others, making them candidates for \'not_orderable\':\n\n- **Lan** dislikes tandoori, caramel, and salad. All three items are liked by others, so Lan does not provide an easy decision.\n- **Jan** dislikes doughnut, salad, and custard, but all these items are liked by others.\n\nWithout individuals who conclusively point us toward a specific item that can be placed in \'not_orderable\' without consequence, we have to employ another strategy. Since every single food item is problematic, let\'s prioritize the items based on the number of conflicts.\n\nFirst, we will count the number of individuals satisfied by placing an item in \'orderable\' versus \'not_orderable\':\n\n- **Tandoori**: 14 like it, 11 dislike it - placing it in orderable has a net satisfaction of +3.\n- **Salad**: 11 like it, 17 dislike it - placing it in not_orderable has a net satisfaction of +6.\n- **Custard**: 11 like it, 10 dislike it - placing it in orderable has a net satisfaction of +1.\n- **Caramel**: 15 like it, 11 dislike it - placing it in orderable has a net satisfaction of +4.\n- **Doughnut**: 10 like it, 8 dislike it - placing it in orderable has a net satisfaction of +2.\n\nFrom this, the clearest decision is to place **Salad** in \'not_orderable\', as many more dislike it than like it. This satisfies individuals who dislike salad and are unhappy if it is orderable.\n\nNow we\'ll check the satisfaction balance again, considering that everyone who dislikes salad is already satisfied by it being not_orderable:\n\n- **Tandoori**: 6 previously unsatisfied individuals now satisfied, so it has effectively 8 likes with 0 dislikes from the previously dissatisfied group.\n- **Custard**: 2 previously unsatisfied individuals now satisfied (Xan, Mina), so it has effectively 9 likes with 0 dislikes from the previously dissatisfied group.\n- **Caramel**: 3 previously unsatisfied individuals now satisfied (Lan, Ken, Ty), so it has effectively 12 likes with 0 dislikes from the previously dissatisfied group.\n- **Doughnut**: now has 5 likes with 0 dislikes from the previously dissatisfied group (Sal, Leo, Qi, May, Gen, Taj, Tia), since Geet, Ivy, and Ru are satisfied with Salad being not_orderable.\n\n**Doughnut** now appears to be the least controversial to put in \'orderable\', as fewer individuals dislike it, and all individuals who dislike it are no longer concerned due to salad being in \'not_orderable\'.\n\nPlacing **Doughnut** in \'orderable\' now satisfies Sal, Leo, Qi, May, Gen, Taj, and Tia. The remaining population that was concerned about the controversial items can be broken down as follows:\n\n- Those who like Tandoori are all satisfied except for Ty, since Caramel is not settled (as he dislikes Caramel).\n- Custard becomes the item with the least contention since it effectively now has 9 likes with 0 previously dissatisfied dislikes.\n- Those who like Caramel are all satisfied except for Veer, since Salad is placed in \'not_orderable\' (as he dislikes both Doughnut and Salad).\n\nRemaining to satisfy: Ty and Veer, who are concerned about Caramel and Tandoori. Let\'s try placing one of them:\n\n- If we put Caramel in \'orderable\', we would satisfy Veer but need to consider Ty\'s preference. Ty dislikes both Salad (which is already \'not_orderable\') and Caramel, so including Caramel would dissatisfy him. However, Ty likes Tandoori.\n- If we put Tandoori in \'orderable\', we would satisfy Ty, but since Tandoori is liked by so many and disliked by many, we might create dissatisfaction elsewhere.\n\nGiven that Tandoori is liked by a significant portion of our group, placing it in \'orderable\' might prove to be the best strategy, fitting within the remaining constraints, since the number of likes outweighs the dislikes, especially after all the dislikes of salad have been taken care of.\n\nThus we can place **Tandoori** in \'orderable\', satisfying Ty and the majority that also likes it.\n\nAt this point, the list would look like this:\n\n- orderable: Doughnut, Tandoori\n- not_orderable: Salad\n\nNow, we review if anyone remains unsatisfied:\n- For Caramel: Veer would be unsatisfied, but every other caramel lover is now satisfied due to doughnut and tandoori being orderable, or salad not being orderable.\n- For Custard: No one remains unsatisfied because those who dislike custard dislike salad more. Thus, they are satisfied with salad being not_orderable.\n\nEveryone seems satisfied with the current arrangement.\n\nFinal solution:\n```python\norderable=["doughnut", "tandoori"], not_orderable=["salad"]\n```', 'num_prompt_tokens': 794, 'num_completion_tokens': 1672}
