 some individuals like items that others dislike. Therefore, we need to find items that can be ordered that are not disliked by anyone and items that can be avoided that are not liked by anyone.\n\nFirst, let\'s identify commonly disliked and liked foods across all people\'s preferences:\n\nCommonly liked:\n- Custard: Liked by many, but disliked by some.\n- Burrito: Liked by many, but disliked by some.\n- Curry: Liked by many, but disliked by some.\n- Espresso: Liked by some, but disliked by many.\n- Nachos: Liked by some, but disliked by many.\n- Pie: Liked by some, but disliked by many.\n\nFrom the initial observation, it is evident that "pie" and "nachos" are disliked by several individuals, and thus they are strong candidates for the \'not_orderable\' list since including them in the \'orderable\' list would leave many people unsatisfied.\n\nBy placing "pie" and "nachos" in the \'not_orderable\' list, we ensure that all individuals who dislike these items will be satisfied by their absence.\n\nNow, we need to find items for the \'orderable\' list that are liked by at least one person and not disliked by any person. Based on the preferences given:\n\n- Custard is liked by many but is also disliked by some, so it cannot be on any list.\n- Burrito and Curry also receive conflicted opinions, so we cannot include them confidently on the \'orderable\' list.\n- Espresso is also controversial, with a significant number of people disliking it.\n\nHowever, the prompt does not list any commonly liked item that is not disliked by anyone. Given the high number of participants with varying dislikes, it is quite challenging to find an item for the \'orderable\' list that satisfies the condition of being disliked by none.\n\nDue to the conflicted nature of the likes and dislikes, we cannot build an \'orderable\' list that will satisfy everyone according to the provided criteria, and every person should be satisfied as per the rules. Therefore, the only solution here is to provide an empty \'orderable\' list and at least a \'not_orderable\' list containing the items that are disliked by several people and likely to satisfy the many who dislike them.\n\nThe finalized lists:\n\n- orderable: []\n- not_orderable: ["pie", "nachos"]\n\nWith these lists, we might satisfy each individual by the absence of "pie" and "nachos", because at least one disliked item from their list will be on the \'not_orderable\' list. This solution assumes there are no like-only individuals for items not on the list, a common ground since no food item is liked by someone without being disliked by someone else.\n\nFinal solution:\n```python\norderable = [], not_orderable = ["pie", "nachos"]\n```', 'num_prompt_tokens': 1008, 'num_completion_tokens': 624}
