y different people—and try to satisfy the most people by deciding which list to place these foods in.\n\nLet's review the preferences:\n\nFoods that are liked by some but disliked by no one can go straight into the 'orderable' list. However, after reviewing the preferences, no food item is universally liked without being disliked by somebody else.\n\nFoods that are disliked by people but not liked by anyone should be placed in the 'not_orderable' list. There are no such items here.\n\nNext, we will look at individual items and make logical deductions based on preferences.\n\n- Kebab is liked by many but also disliked by Ying, Mia, Amy, Rina, and Ken. However, since it is the only like of Diya, Wes, Bo, and Anil, it must be on the 'orderable' list to satisfy them; we have to count on satisfying Ying, Mia, Amy, Rina, and Ken with other items.\n\n- Fries are liked by Mel, Mia, Amy, Rina, Ken, Yan, and Max, but disliked by many others. Since it is the only like of Mia, Amy, Rina, and Ken, it must be ordered to satisfy them; we have to count on satisfying others with other items.\n\n- Lobster is liked by many but disliked by Diya, Dan, Naina, Sky, Ying, Mia, Rina, Ken, and Anil. Since there are no individuals for whom lobster is the only liked item, it can be placed in the 'not_orderable' list.\n\n- Cheese is liked by several but disliked by an equal number. Since it is the only liked item for Dan and Zac, it must be on the 'orderable' list; this means we'll have to satisfy Lei, Wes, Bo, Naina, Sky, Ying, and Mina with other items. However, satisfying Naina becomes impossible because both items she dislikes (lobster and cheese) cannot be in the 'not_orderable' list simultaneously, and there's no like to place in the 'orderable' list.\n\nFrom this analysis, we identify that it is impossible to construct an 'orderable' and 'not_orderable' list combination that satisfies everyone because fulfilling the requirements for one person precludes satisfying another. Naina and Dan/