e at least one item on the 'not_orderable' list.\n2. No item can appear on both lists.\n3. All participants must be satisfied by the combination of the two lists.\n\nLet's start by considering the items with the most dislikes first, as we might be able to place these in the 'not_orderable' list without conflicts.\n\nAfter a quick observation, we can see some items are more problematic than others because their inclusion or exclusion directly affects many participants. We need to address these items carefully to find a workable solution:\n\n- **Couscous** is disliked by many but also liked by many, which makes it difficult to place in either list without causing dissatisfaction.\n- **Roti** is liked by many but also is disliked by a significant number of people, so this also can't be easily placed on either list.\n- **Paneer** and **kebab** face a similar predicament.\n- **Korma**, **burger**, **salmon**, and **risotto** have their issues as well due to conflicting likes and dislikes.\n\nSince there are also no items liked by just one person and not disliked by others, we can't include an item in the 'orderable' list guaranteed to satisfy only one person without affecting others.\n\nGiven the complex and contradictory preferences, it doesn't seem possible to create two distinct lists that would satisfy everyone's likes and dislikes accordingly. No matter what item we choose to put on the 'orderable' list, someone who dislikes that item will not be satisfied. Similarly, if we put an item on the 'not_orderable' list, someone who likes it will not be satisfied.\n\nTherefore, based on the satisfaction criteria provided and the constraint that all participants must be satisfied, the only solution is to have empty lists for both 'orderable' and 'not_orderable':\n\n```python\norderable = []\nnot_orderable = []\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 957, 'num_completion_tokens': 562}
