em on the \'not_orderable\' list can potentially satisfy someone without affecting others negatively, while an \'orderable\' food item must be liked by all who have expressed a dislike toward a specific food.\n\nFrom the given data:\n\n- Wes dislikes \'burrito\', \'couscous\', and \'poke\', immediately meaning these items cannot be orderable as we don\'t have anything Wes likes to counterbalance this (there is no food item Wes likes that we can put in the \'orderable\' list to satisfy Wes).\n- Ravi dislikes \'poke\', \'burrito\', and \'couscous\', which aligns with Wes\'s dislikes and reinforces that these items should not be on the \'orderable\' list.\n\nGiven this information, let\'s now consider \'ravioli\' since it\'s the only remaining item that some people dislike:\n\n- Ray dislikes \'ravioli\', but likes \'poke\'. Since we can\'t place \'poke\' in the \'orderable\' list due to Wes\'s and Ravi\'s dislikes, we must put \'ravioli\' in the \'not_orderable\' list to satisfy Ray.\n- Riya dislikes \'couscous\' and \'burrito\', but we can\'t place either in the \'orderable\' list, so placing \'ravioli\' in the \'not_orderable\' list satisfies Riya.\n- Sue dislikes \'couscous\' and \'ravioli\'. Since \'couscous\' is off the table for \'orderable\', \'ravioli\' must go to \'not_orderable\' to satisfy Sue.\n- Nita dislikes \'ravioli\' and has other likes that can\'t be satisfied by \'orderable\', so \'ravioli\' should be \'not_orderable\' for Nita\'s sake.\n- Pam dislikes \'couscous\' and \'burrito\', so \'ravioli\' going to \'not_orderable\' is necessary to satisfy Pam.\n- Hui dislikes \'ravioli\' and \'poke\', meaning \'ravioli\' goes to \'not_orderable\' so Hui can be satisfied given \'poke\' can\'t be \'orderable\'.\n- Aarav dislikes \'poke\' and \'ravioli\', solidifying \'ravioli\'s placement in \'not_orderable\'.\n- Mia dislikes \'ravioli\', fitting the pattern of \'ravioli\' being necessary on \'not_orderable\'.\n- Naina dislikes \'ravioli\', again aligning with the necessity of \'ravioli\' being \'not_orderable\'.\n- Xiu dislikes \'ravioli\', supporting it\'s not orderable.\n- Ivy dislikes \'burrito\', with like \'couscous\' or \'ravioli\'. Since \'couscous\' is not going to be orderable due to Wes and Ravi, \'ravioli\' must be \'not_orderable\'.\n- Ada dislikes \'ravioli\' ensuring it remains \'not_orderable\'.\n- Zara dislikes \'ravioli\', consistent with other inputs for \'not_orderable\'.\n- Leo dislikes \'ravioli\'. Since \'poke\' cannot be ordered and \'burrito\' is disliked by others, \'ravioli\' is \'not_orderable\' to satisfy Leo.\n\nAt this point, we\'ve established that \'ravioli\' must be \'not_orderable\' to satisfy a majority of individuals who either dislike it directly or cannot be satisfied with their likes being on the \'orderable\' list due to Wes\'s and Ravi\'s dislikes.\n\nNext, let\'s look at the \'orderable\' list:\n\nEveryone who likes \'poke\', \'couscous\', or \'burrito\' is ruled out for the \'orderable\' list because of the dislikes of Wes and Ravi. Since dislikes take priority and we need to satisfy everyone, the \'orderable\' list cannot have \'poke\', \'couscous\', or \'burrito\'. The individuals who have likes outside of these foods cannot be satisfied with the \'orderable\' list and must be satisfied with the \'not_orderable\' list, which is why \'ravioli\' is included there.\n\nNo other food items are mentioned as liked items that aren\'t disliked by others. Therefore, our only option is to put \'ravioli\' on the \'not_orderable\' list and keep the \'orderable\' list empty to satisfy everyone according to the criteria. \n\nThus, the output lists are:\n```\norderable = []\nnot_orderable = ["ravioli"]\n```', 'num_prompt_tokens': 607, 'num_completion_tokens': 975}
