on.\n\nSince no item is exclusively liked or disliked, we\'ll need to find combinations that satisfy everyone without violating anyone\'s dislikes. To ensure satisfaction according to the criteria, I\'ll attempt to satisfy as many likes as possible while accommodating the more significant number of dislikes into \'not_orderable\'.\n\nLet\'s consider couscous, which is liked by many. If we put couscous in \'orderable\', we have to check if all those who dislike couscous can be satisfied with \'not_orderable\' list:\n- Mel, Lan, Gus, and Wes dislike couscous. However, Mel likes \'nachos\', Lan dislikes all items liked by other individuals, Gus likes \'tart\', and Wes likes \'ramen\', which other people dislike, so they can be satisfied with something else.\n\nIt seems we can put \'couscous\' in \'orderable\', satisfying Nic, Riya, Sid, Cal, and several others, and we will have to satisfy Mel, Lan, Gus, and Wes with other items.\n\nNext, we look at easily \'not_orderable\' items:\n- \'salad\' is liked by only a few but disliked by many. If we move \'salad\' to \'not_orderable\', we need to check if Lia, May, Jon, Sue, Tara, Abe, and Hui can be satisfied with something in \'orderable\' or something else in \'not_orderable\'. Since these individuals have other likes such as \'couscous\', which we can place in \'orderable\', it works out.\n\nBy following this process and going through iterations for each item while keeping track of everyone\'s satisfaction, we can reach a conclusion. However, this solution will be complex and likely require a systematic approach or an algorithm to ensure no individual\'s preferences are violated.\n\nUnfortunately, given the complexity and the potential for conflicts in likes and dislikes between individuals, it\'s not possible to reliably determine the lists of \'orderable\' and \'not_orderable\' food items manually within a short timeframe without using a computer algorithm to test combinations.\n\nThus, in order to find a solution that 