 brownies, roti. Jim: Likes quiche. Dislikes spaghetti, lobster. Cal: Likes roti, lobster. Dislikes frittata. Liz: Likes korma. Dislikes risotto, frittata. Lex: Likes spaghetti, brownies, roti. Feng: Likes roti. Dislikes cheesecake, frittata. Amy: Likes spaghetti, risotto. Dislikes korma. Eli: Likes frittata, quiche. Dislikes spaghetti. Ava: Likes frittata, korma. Dislikes cheesecake. Tim: Likes spaghetti. Dislikes frittata, brownies. ', 'menu_items': ['quiche', 'roti', 'frittata', 'naan', 'brownies', 'lobster', 'risotto', 'spaghetti', 'korma', 'cheesecake'], 'gpt_out': "To start, we'll sort out all the preferences to figure out items that must go in 'orderable' and 'not_orderable' to satisfy as many people as possible.\n\nWe're going to make lists of all likes and dislikes, and notice if there are items that everyone who likes them dislikes another specific item, we can then put the liked item in 'orderable' and the other in 'not_orderable'. Also, if there's any item that everyone dislikes, it can go straight into 'not_orderable'.\n\nLikes and dislikes for all participants should be sorted to see if there are clear decisions to be made based on items liked by some and disliked by others. \n\nThe process begins by looking for items that can be straightforwardly classified:\n\n1. If an item is liked by at least one person and not disliked by anyone, it should go to 'orderable'.\n2. If an item is disliked by at least one person and not liked by anyone, it should go to 'not_orderable'.\n\nLet's categorize the items:\n\nLiked by somebody and not disliked by anybody:\n- naan (liked by Ada, Ian, Gia, Kit; not disliked by non-likers)\n- korma (liked by Ivy, Kavi, Ansh, Gus, Liz; not disliked by non-likers)\n\nDisliked by somebody and not liked by anybody:\n(None)\n\nNow given the complicated interconnections of likes and dislikes, we must take careful note of each food preference. Since there are no items that are universally disliked, to make sure we satisfy all participants, we have to find at least one item that each participant likes to go into the 'orderable' list unless we can satisfy them with the 'not_orderable' list. Also, a disliked item for any individual cannot go into 'orderable' if there's no counter item for that person in 'not_orderable'.\n\nHowever, no person is satisfied by only 'not_orderable' from the start, which means that all people must get at least one liked item in 'orderable'. \n\nThe two straightforward choices we have so far are naan and korma for the 'orderable' list. Because they do not have any exclusive dislikes, they can be ordered without any conflicts. Everyone who likes naan or korma is satisfied as per the satisfaction criteria #1.\n\nNow let's look at other items, focusing on individuals who haven't been satisfied yet. We'll look for an item or a combination of items that could satisfy multiple people without dissatisfying anyone else.\n\nConsidering the individual preferences, balancing likes and dislikes, trying to find another dish that is either:\n- liked by someone who doesn't have their preference met yet and not disliked by anyone, or\n- a dish that is a liked preference which doesn't conflict with the 'orderable' items we have identified so far (naan and korma).\n\nAfter a thorough review of the list, we find that there's no clear next choice that doesn't create a conflict for another participant. The multitude of interdependencies between the likes and dislikes of all the participants makes satisfying everyone with a selection of 'orderable' and 'not_orderable' items impossible without conflict.\n\nUnfortunately, we cannot find a combination that satisfies all participants based on the criteria provided, as there will always be someone left unsatisfied whatever combination we come up with. Therefore, both lists should be empty.\n\nOutputting the final solution in python code:\n```python\norderable=[], not_orderable=[]\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 748, 'num_completion_tokens': 716}
