 Likes burrito: Kim, Arun, Bao, Pam, Lex, Gen, Sal, Eva, Tara, Sky, Du, Yan, Sue, Taj, Lia, Pat, Ying, Jon\n- Dislikes burrito: May, Lee, Gia, Yue, Zoe, Mia\n- Likes macaron: Arun, Bao, Lex, Hal, Gia, Eva, Tara, Amy, Sky, Du, Sue, Yue, Ying, Jon\n- Dislikes macaron: Kim, May, Bao, Pam, Lex, Gen, Hal, Yan, Taj\n- Likes pie: Arun, May, Lee, Sal, Lin, Zoe, Mia, Amy, Ying, Jon\n- Dislikes pie: Kim, Bao, Pam, Lex, Gen, Hal, Eva, Sky, Du, Yan, Taj, Yue\n\nNext, we create a strategy to find an item that satisfies as many people's likes as possible without hitting anyone's dislikes.\n\nObservations:\n1. `burrito` is liked by the most people and is only disliked by 6 people, making it a potential candidate for the 'orderable' list.\n2. `macaron` has a smaller group of likers and a more substantial set of dislikers, which makes it less suitable to be on the 'orderable' list without checking the combinations.\n3. `pie` is liked by fewer people than `burrito` but more than `macaron`, and it is disliked by a large group as well. Therefore, it is also less suitable to be on the 'orderable' list without checking the combinations.\n\nLet's see who will not be satisfied if we add `burrito` to the 'orderable' list.\n- Adding `burrito` to 'orderable' will not satisfy: May, Lee, Gia, Yue, Zoe, Mia since they dislike `burrito`.\n\nTo satisfy those who dislike `burrito`, we can consider adding `macaron` or `pie` to the 'not_orderable' list, as this is the only way to satisfy them due to them disliking `burrito`.\n\nBetween `macaron` and `pie`:\n- If we add `macaron` to 'not_orderable', we will dissatisfy Kim, Bao, Lex, Gen, Hal, Yan, Taj because they all like `macaron` and dislike the remaining options.\n- If we add `pie` to 'not_orderable', we will dissatisfy Arun, Lia (because they like `pie` and `burrito` is already orderable, thus they are not affected by their dislike), Lin, Zoe, Mia, Amy, Ying, Jon since these people only like `pie` and the remaining option is disliked by them.\n\nConsidering that `burrito` is liked by the most participants and disliked by the least, and if we include `pie` in the not_orderable list, we satisfy more than if we put `macaron` there. Therefore, we can come up with the following lists:\n\n- orderable: `burrito`\n- not_orderable: `pie`\n\nNow every individual is satisfied by at least one of their likes being orderable or one of their dislikes being not orderable, except for Arun, Lia, Lin, Zoe, Mia, Amy, Ying, and Jon who we will check now if they would be satisfied.\n\n- Arun is satisfied because they like `burrito`.\n- Lia is satisfied because they like `burrito`.\n- Lin is dissatisfied because neither of their likes are orderable and neither of their dislikes are not orderable.\n- Zoe is dissatisfied for the same reason as Lin.\n- Mia is dissatisfied for the same reason as Lin.\n- Amy is satisfied because they like `macaron` which has not been listed as not_orderable.\n- Ying is satisfied because they like `burrito`.\n- Jon is satisfied because they like `burrito`.\n\nLooking at Lin, Zoe, and Mia, we find that there's no way to include either of their likes in the 'orderable' list without including someone else's dislikes since `burrito` (which they dislike) is already there and `pie` (which they do like) would satisfy them, but it's already in the 'not_orderable' list, and adding `macaron` to 'orderable' would upset those that dislike `macaron`.\n\nEven though we've reached a decent level of satisfaction for many participants, Lin, Zoe, and Mia can't be satisfied with the constraints we are given.\n\nGiven this situation, we must output empty lists for both 'orderable' and 'not_orderable' as it is impossible to satisfy all participants:\n\n```python\norderable=[], not_orderable=[]\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 536, 'num_completion_tokens': 1065}
