es. Lou: Dislikes waffles, quiche, korma. Yue: Likes waffles. Dislikes salad, korma. Xan: Likes quiche. Dislikes korma, salad. Xiu: Likes waffles. Dislikes salad, quiche. Om: Likes waffles. Dislikes quiche, korma. Dan: Likes waffles, salad. Dislikes korma. Tara: Dislikes korma, salad, waffles. ', 'menu_items': ['salad', 'korma', 'waffles', 'quiche'], 'gpt_out': 'To satisfy everyone\'s preferences, let\'s begin by finding any common dislikes and any unique likes. If everyone dislikes an item, it can go directly into the \'not_orderable\' list. If only one person likes a specific item, that item can go in the \'orderable\' list to ensure their satisfaction.\n\nFirst, let\'s find common dislikes (if any):\n\n1. Korma is disliked by: Kit, Sam, Ivy, Jon, Ron, Qi, Hui, Ken, Joy, Diya, Feng, Abe, Veer, Nic, Rob, Neil, Lou, Yue, Xan, Tara.\n2. Quiche is disliked by: Kit, Sam, Ava, Jon, Fay, Nita, Liz, Sky, Tao, Veer, Pat, Vic.\n3. Waffles are disliked by: Ava, Jon, Ada, Gia, Ken, May, Rob, Feng, Veer, Nic, Lou, Neil, Tara.\n4. Salad is disliked by: Taj, Ava, Jon, Ken, Zoe, Mina, Feng, Yue, Xan, Xiu, Tara.\n\nNo single food item is included in everyone\'s dislikes, so no item can be immediately placed into the \'not_orderable\' list.\n\nNext, let\'s find any items that have unique likes (liked by only one person):\n\n1. Salad is liked uniquely only by Rob, Nic, Abe, Arun, Dan which is not unique.\n2. Waffles liked by many so they are not unique.\n3. Quiche liked by many so they are not unique.\n4. Korma liked by many so they are not unique.\n\nNo food item is exclusively liked by only one person. Therefore, no item can be immediately placed into the \'orderable\' list.\n\nGiven that we have no unique likes or common dislikes, our next step is to create a combination that satisfies as many people as possible while not violating anyone\'s strong dislikes.\n\nOne way to approach this is by placing one of the most liked items into the \'orderable\' list as long as it does not conflict with the dislikes of others.\n\nLet\'s consider "waffles" since it is liked by many:\n\nWaffles are liked by: Sam, Taj, Ivy, Nita, Liz, Sky, Mina, Xiu, Om, Yue, Vic, Pat, Dan, Xan, Lia, Abe, Diya, Joy, Rex, Gia, Ava, Quinn, and Fay.\n\nDisliked by: Ava, Jon, Ada, Gia, Ken, May, Rob, Feng, Veer, Nic, Lou, Neil, Tara.\n\nSince waffles are liked by many but also disliked by many, if they are to be orderable, all individuals who dislike waffles should have another like or dislike in the opposite list to be satisfied.\n\nFor everyone who dislikes waffles:\n\n- Ava, Ada, Gia, May, Zoe, Tao like korma and dislike salad (korma can\'t be orderable due to Ava, Ada, Gia, May, Zoe, Tao and others who dislike it; salad cannot be orderable because they dislike it and it would dissatisfy them).\n- Jon, Ken, Veer, Feng, Lou, Tara dislike all options, so they cannot find satisfaction with waffles being orderable (since they dislike it and all their other dislikes would then have to be orderable to satisfy them).\n- Rob, Nic dislike korma and waffles, like salad (but like salad), making it impossible to satisfy them if waffles are orderable as they share the dislike for waffles and korma.\n- Neil dislikes waffles and salad, likes quiche (quiche can\'t be orderable as then Neil\'s dislikes would be violated)\n\nFrom this, we know waffles being orderable conflicts with the satisfaction of Jon, Ken, Veer, Feng, Lou, Tara, Rob, Nic, and Neil. Therefore, waffles cannot be orderable.\n\nUsing the same process, we\'d discover that no single food item can be \'orderable\' without leaving some individuals unsatisfied. Thus, the only way to meet the criteria where each person is happy with at least one item on the \'orderable\' list or \'not_orderable\' list is not attainable.\n\nSince no combination of \'orderable\' and \'not_orderable\' foods can satisfy everyone, based on their likes and dislikes, the solution would be empty lists for both:\n\n```python\norderable=[], not_orderable=[