ked by various people.\n- **Nachos** are liked and disliked by various people.\n- **Bruschetta** is liked and disliked by various people.\n- **Salad** is liked and disliked by various people.\n- **Kebab** is liked and disliked by various people.\n\nSince every single item is liked by some and disliked by others, we will have to find a compromise where at least one liked item or one disliked item from everyone's list ends up in our 'orderable' or 'not_orderable' list, respectively.\n\nThe strategy will be to satisfy people with the most restrictive preferences first. \n1. We will look for people who only like or only dislike one type of food.\n2. We will also focus on foods with the highest number of likes and the lowest number of dislikes to satisfy the most people.\n\nUpon reviewing the lists, it is clear that there is no item that is not liked by at least one person or no item that is not disliked by at least one person. This means that there is no quick way to assign items to 'orderable' or 'not_orderable' based on unanimous dislikes or likes.\n \nTherefore, let's try satisfy preferences with the following steps:\n\n1.  Firstly, try to satisfy individuals with unique preferences or individuals with extreme preferences (only items liked or only items disliked). There are no individuals with unique preferences or individuals who only like or dislike one item.\n\n2. Secondly, address the more common preferences. Ratatouille and churros are the most frequently mentioned items. Since churros are liked by some and disliked by others, placing churros in either list would unsatisfy another group. However, ratatouille is liked by many and disliked by fewer individuals, which means if we can place it in one of the lists, it can potentially satisfy a large number of people. \n\nUpon scanning the list, we identify that every single person who dislikes ratatouille likes at least one other item that isn't universally disliked. This means we can attempt to place ratatouille in 