ems.\n\n2. Identify food items that must be on the \'orderable\' list because they are liked by someone and not disliked by anyone:\n   - No such food items exist as all liked items are also disliked by someone else.\n\n3. Create a table or a map of likes and dislikes to see if there\'s a food item that can satisfy multiple people. Try to order food that is liked by people who have fewer other likes. Avoid food items that would prevent a large number of people from being satisfied due to their dislikes.\n\nThrough reviewing the preferences, we find that "falafel" and "tacos" are critical because they are liked and disliked by many. However, there\'s a conflict because some individuals like one and dislike the other:\n\n- Jim dislikes "tacos".\n- Nita likes "tacos".\n- Taj dislikes both "falafel" and "tacos".\n- Cal dislikes "tacos".\n\nLet\'s attempt to build a solution:\n\n- Since Taj dislikes both "tacos" and "falafel", and we have to satisfy Taj, at least one of these must be on the \'not_orderable\' list.\n- Several people dislike "naan", but it is essential to some people\'s satisfaction. If we place "falafel" on the \'not_orderable\' list to satisfy Taj (who is one participant with strong preferences), "naan" could be a candidate for \'orderable\' because it would satisfy Nita and others without making anyone unhappy.\n- However, we have to ensure that everyone who likes "falafel" and dislikes "naan," like Jim, can still be satisfied by something else.\n\nTo attempt satisfying everyone:\n- Since "falafel" is liked by many but is one of the only disliked items for Taj who has no other likes, we place "falafel" on the \'not_orderable\' list to ensure Taj is satisfied.\n- To satisfy Jim, we cannot have "vegetables" on the \'not_orderable\' list because then he\'d have nothing he likes available. Therefore, let\'s consider "vegetables" for the \'orderable\' list.\n- To satisfy Nita, we place "tacos" on the \'orderable\' list since she likes "tacos" and dislikes "v