ctly into \'orderable\' or \'not_orderable\' without conflicting with the aforementioned rules.\n\nFrom analyzing the dislikes, it is evident that "vegetables", "pizza", "risotto", "lobster", "paneer", "vindaloo", and "hummus" each have at least one person who dislikes them. Thus, they cannot go directly into \'orderable\'.\n\n"Bruschetta" is disliked by Hu, Hal, Nita, Lee, Geet, and Bea, however, "bruschetta" is liked and not disliked by enough people (Dan, Ty, Sia, Rohan, and Kai), which means it cannot be concluded without a doubt that it belongs in \'not_orderable\' directly.\n\nNow let\'s look for the foods that must be in \'orderable\' to satisfy someone\'s like when no one else has a corresponding dislike. This occurs when a person dislikes something that is exclusive to others:\n- Jaya: Likes lobster. No other exclusive likes, but dislikes vegetables and hummus, which others like. So lobster can\'t be put in \'orderable\', but this doesn\'t help with vegetables or hummus.\n- Hu: Likes pizza and vindaloo, but both are liked and disliked by others.\n- Pam: Likes vegetables and risotto. Both are controversial items.\n- ...\n- Ula: Dislikes vindaloo, risotto, and hummus. \n- Ana: Likes hummus, paneer; dislikes risotto, but others like all these items.\n- ...\n- Gen: Dislikes risotto, vegetables, and hummus. But since others like them, we cannot make a decision based on these alone.\n\nNo one person\'s preference forces any single item into either \'orderable\' or \'not_orderable\' due to every food item being liked by multiple people and disliked by others. \n\nTherefore, the problem becomes more complex, as we must find a combination of likes and dislikes.\n\nConsider pizza - many people like and dislike it. However, people who dislike it also have other dislikes (e.g., Ula dislikes vindaloo, risotto, and hummus as well). If pizza is placed in \'orderable\', Ula can still be satisfied if vindaloo, risotto, or hummus is in \'not_orderable\'. So, pizza looks like