 if even one person likes that item, it should not go into the 'not_orderable' list.\n\nStep 2: List the foods that are unanimously liked or are only disliked by those whose dislikes can be otherwise accounted for\nAny food item that is liked by at least one person must not go into the 'not_orderable' list. Those items that are liked by individuals who have other dislikes that can be accounted for might still be considered orderable.\n\nStep 3: Look for unique likes and dislikes\nAny item that is uniquely liked by someone and doesn't conflict with someone else's dislikes will go into the 'orderable' list. Similarly, any item uniquely disliked (and not liked by anyone else) can go into the 'not_orderable' list.\n\nStep 4: Consider the mutual dislikes\nIf there is an item that is disliked by many but not liked by equally as many or more, it may need to go into the 'not_orderable' list to satisfy the majority.\n\nStep 5: Validate and update the list iteratively\nAfter each step, we need to make sure all the criteria are still met. If we place an item into one list, we need to reconsider the implications for the other items and people's preferences.\n\nStarting with the list of unique dislikes (those disliked by just one person or disliked by many but liked by fewer):\n- Pho is liked by many but disliked by some. We can't place it in either list yet, as it's neither uniquely liked nor uniquely disliked.\n- Curry is liked and disliked by many. We can't place it in either list yet.\n- Udon is liked by many and disliked by many. We can't place it in either list yet.\n- Kebab is liked by many and disliked by many. We can't place it in either list yet.\n- Kimchi is liked by many but disliked by some. We can't place it in either list yet.\n- Pie is liked by some but is the unique dislike of only Oli, so it can't go into the 'not_orderable' list.\n- Burger is liked by some and uniquely disliked by Chen, so it can't go into the 'not_orderable' list.\n- Korma is liked by many and not uniquely disliked.\n- Caramel is liked by several people and no one uniquely dislikes it.\n- Kebab, as mentioned, cannot be placed in either list.\n\nAt this stage, we can't find any unanimous decisions without risking the satisfaction of at least one person for each food item.\n\nBecause there's no apparent food item that can satisfy all individuals by being on one list without dissatisfying others in the other list, it seems that no solution that satisfies all criteria is possible. Every person has at least one preferred dish that is disliked by another individual in the group. Consequently, we cannot construct a combination of 'orderable' and 'not_orderable' foods satisfying every individual based on the provided criteria.\n\nGiven these restrictions and the group's divergent likes and dislikes, the correct output would be:\n\n```python\norderable=[], not_orderable=[]\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 991, 'num_completion_tokens': 773}
