ust place "waffles" in the "not_orderable" list to satisfy people like Tara, Aarav, and Max.\n\nWith "cheesecake" in the "orderable" list and "waffles" in the "not_orderable" list:\n- The people who like "waffles" but also like "cheesecake" or "udon" will be satisfied by either "cheesecake" or "udon."\n- The people who dislike "waffles" will be satisfied by it being "not_orderable."\n\nLet\'s check if anyone is left unsatisfied:\n- Everyone who dislikes "waffles" is satisfied because waffles are "not_orderable."\n- Everyone who dislikes "udon" but likes "cheesecake" is satisfied because cheesecake is "orderable."\n- Everyone who dislikes "cheesecake" but likes either "waffles" or "udon" is satisfied because "udon" remained neither "orderable" nor "not_orderable."\n\nTherefore, let\'s see our lists:\n- orderable: ["cheesecake"]\n- not_orderable: ["waffles"]\n\nWe did not include "udon" in either list, which means it remains neutral, and anyone who likes "udon" will not be dissatisfied, provided they do not exclusively dislike both other items. Checking through the list, there\'s no such case that would make them unsatisfied.\n\nHere is the final python code form:\n```\norderable=["cheesecake"], not_orderable=["waffles"]\n```', 'num_prompt_tokens': 652, 'num_completion_tokens': 896}
