t disliked by Min, Shan, and Zara. It's a candidate for 'orderable' but depends on satisfaction criteria for those who dislike it.\n5. Cheesecake is liked by some but disliked by multiple, so it's a candidate for 'not_orderable'.\n6. Nachos are liked by many but also disliked by many, so we cannot decide without further analysis.\n7. Lasagna is liked by many but also disliked by Eli, Kit, Diya, Pam, Feng, Cal, Ula, and Ping. This makes it a viable option for 'orderable', especially since we can't make risotto 'orderable' to satisfy Eli, and Pam can be satisfied if lobster is 'not_orderable'.\n8. We must also note that Gia likes risotto, lasagna, and cheesecake. Since cheesecake will likely be 'not_orderable', either risotto or lasagna must be 'orderable' to satisfy her.\n\nBased on the above considerations, to satisfy the most individuals with conflicting preferences, the lists should look like this:\n\n- Peach must be `orderable` because it is the only liked item for Abe, who dislikes both lobster and cheesecake. This would also satisfy Sol, Sam, Sia, and Pam.\n- Lobster must be `not_orderable` because this satisfies Ron, Ada, Tom, Sol, Pam, Shan, Ula, Zara, and other lobster dislikers while not conflicting with those who like it since they have other likes.\n- This means risotto cannot be `orderable` since both Eli and Diya dislike it and don't have another like that can be `orderable`, but it can still be `not_orderable` to satisfy them.\n- Cheesecake must be `not_orderable` to satisfy Gen, Wei, Mia, Tom, Pia, Sia, Sam, Feng, Sal, Abe, and Jan because they either dislike cheesecake or have another liked item that can be made `orderable`.\n- Lasagna can be `orderable` to satisfy Gia, and since there are dissenters of risotto, kimchi, and lasagna, those who dislike lasagna like Ty, Kit, May, Eli, and Ron can be satisfied with lobster as `not_orderable` and others like peach.\n- Kimchi will be `orderable` because it's only disliked by Min, Shan, and Zara. Since Shan