 liked by many and disliked by a few. However, Jan dislikes both, and since even a single disliked item on the 'not_orderable' list can satisfy a person, we need to include either shawarma or gelato (or both) in the 'not_orderable' to satisfy Jan.\n- Bread is highly contested and cannot satisfy everyone if placed in either list on its own.\n- Naan is liked by fewer people than shawarma or gelato, and disliked by many.\n\nLooking at the preferences, we find that Arun, Aarav, and Jan dislike the most liked items (shawarma and/or gelato), so we must at least put one of these on the 'not_orderable' list to satisfy them. Moreover, Ian dislikes gelato and naan, but he likes shawarma, so he gives us a direction that gelato should be in 'not_orderable' to satisfy his preference.\n\nGiven the analysis, let's attempt to satisfy everyone with the following tentative categorization:\n\n'not_orderable':\n- Gelato (Disliked by Arun, Aarav, and Jan, which makes them satisfied.)\n\n'orderable':\n- Shawarma (Liked by many, which will satisfy a large number of people if it's orderable.)\n\nThis choice leaves us with the problem of satisfying people who dislike shawarma but like gelato (Val, Kai), and those who like naan (Ada, Ping, Ula, Oli, Gus, Arun). Since we can't place any disliked items in the 'orderable' list, we can try placing naan in the 'orderable' list to satisfy those who like naan. This would immediately rule out including bread because it's disliked by too many people.\n\nSo our updated lists are:\n\n'orderable': \n- Shawarma\n- Naan\n\n'not_orderable': \n- Gelato \n\nCheck if all are satisfied:\n\n- Tara (Likes shawarma or gelato, dislikes naan) ✔\n- Val (Likes gelato, dislikes shawarma) ❌ - Not satisfied because likes gelato and it's not in the 'orderable' list\n- Jan (Dislikes naan, shawarma, gelato) ✔\n- Du (Likes gelato, dislikes naan, shawarma) ❌ - Not satisfied because likes gelato and it's not in the 'orderable' list\n- Nic (Likes shawarma, dislikes bread, naan