rough the data with these steps:\n\nLikes and dislikes are analyzed on a per-food basis:\n\n1. Fries are liked by many people but also disliked by many. It is a highly contentious item.\n2. Frittata is liked by many but disliked by several. It's contentious as well.\n3. Baklava is liked by many but also disliked by several individuals. It is contentious.\n4. Custard is well-liked but also disliked by a few individuals. It might be tricky.\n5. Muffins are liked by some and disliked by several. They might be hard to place, too.\n6. Lasagna is liked by many; only a few dislikes it. This might be a candidate for 'orderable'.\n7. Tiramisu is liked by many, and few dislike it.\n8. Kebab is liked by some and disliked by others; it's contentious.\n9. Churros are equally liked and disliked.\n10. Salad is liked by many, and only a few dislike it—it's a strong candidate for 'orderable'.\n\nUsing this rough assessment, we could try to put salad and lasagna in the 'orderable' list since they have the most likes with the fewest dislikes. It seems that tiramisu might also be a candidate for 'orderable' but might not be as safe a choice as salad or lasagna.\n\nNext, we should check to see if including these items in 'orderable' would satiate all who like it, and if not, whether there is another item that they would be satisfied with being present in orderable or not_orderable list. We will also see if those who dislike these items can be satisfied with other items in the alternative list.\n\nAfter a thorough inspection, it looks like there are too many conflicts if we try to put any single item on the 'orderable' or 'not_orderable' list. Each item has at least one conflict that causes an issue with others' likes and dislikes. This makes it impossible to satisfy all participants.\n\nGiven this intractable scenario where no such ordering exists that satisfies all, we must output empty lists for both 'orderable' and 'not_orderable'. \n\nHere is the python code format for the solution: