lso a significant conflict.\n\nStrategy:\nTo minimize conflicts, let's attempt to satisfy the maximum number of people by putting their like in 'orderable' or dislike in 'not_orderable'.\n\nWe have to ensure:\n- Every person must be satisfied.\n- No item may appear on both lists.\n\nLets see if we can come up with solution by following these steps:\n\nItems that must be in 'not_orderable' to satisfy the majority:\n- Cheesecake is disliked by 9 and liked by 3, so it goes to 'not_orderable'.\n- Pho is disliked by 10 and liked by 7, so it goes to 'not_orderable'.\n\nTentative lists:\n- orderable: []\n- not_orderable: [Cheesecake, Pho]\n\nHowever, we can't simply place Pho in 'not_orderable' because many people who like Pho dislike other items such as Salmon and Guacamole, which would be difficult to satisfy with the remaining items. Let's skip this decision for now and move on to sorbet and paneer, which have fewer conflicts and consider some individuals with unique dislikes:\n\n- Sorbet has more likes than dislikes. If we place Sorbet in 'orderable', it also satisfies Min, Xan, Joe, Raj, and Jon.\n- Paneer has more likes than dislikes and is part of many satisfying conditions. \n\nTrying to minimize conflicts:\n- Sorbet can be 'orderable' to satisfy Tara, Liv, Min, Xan, Joe, Raj, Jon, and avoid any direct conflicts.\n- Paneer can also be 'orderable' to satisfy Tim, Tara, Jun, Zan, Cy, Om, Mina, Yan, and Geet.\n\nOur tentative lists now would be:\n- orderable: [Sorbet, Paneer]\n- not_orderable: [Cheesecake]\n\nThis approach satisfies a number of individuals who either like Sorbet and Paneer or dislike Cheesecake. Let's continue resolving:\n\n- Placing Guacamole in 'orderable' helps satisfy individuals like Tim, Liv, Max, Joe, Jim, Fay, and Raj.\n- Salmon also has a significant number of likes. Whoever likes Salmon, also likes at least another item we have already placed in 'orderable' (except for Vic).\n\nWe can go ahead and place Salmon in 'orderable' as well. \n\nNow