not_orderable' list.\n\n- tart is disliked by Nic, Amy, Jan, Lei, and Gus, and although liked by Neil, Min, Fay, Anil, Dot, and Zac, placing tart in the 'not_orderable' list would satisfy more people than it would dissatisfy.\n- pancakes is disliked by Neil, Min, Arun, Veer, Cy, Sue, Gus, Pat, Jay, with lesser likes, indicating it is also a candidate for 'not_orderable'.\n- fries is disliked by Amy, Ansh, Jay, and Tia, and despite being liked by Shan, Max, and Ben, it may not be imperative for the 'orderable' list because it is less critical for satisfaction compared to their dislikes.\n- tiramisu is disliked by Jan, Nita, Arun, Max, and Anil, but it's liked by Joe, Veer, Ben, and Dot which makes it controversial.\n\nNow we need to sift through the list to ensure at least one liked item for each individual is on the 'orderable' list or one disliked item is on the 'not_orderable' list.\n\nConsidering this:\n1. We can add tart to 'not_orderable' to satisfy multiple individuals who dislike tart.\n2. We can also add pancakes to 'not_orderable' because it is disliked by many and liked by fewer. This will satisfy all those who dislike pancakes.\n3. Fries can potentially be placed in 'not_orderable' as well since more people dislike than like them.\n\nLet's put tart, pancakes, and fries in the 'not_orderable' list and review the satisfaction criteria for each person. If we find a person not satisfied with these items in the 'not_orderable' list, that indicates we might need to place one of their likes in the 'orderable' list instead.\n\nBy placing tart, pancakes, and fries in 'not_orderable', we see the following:\n- Every person who dislikes tart is now satisfied as it is in 'not_orderable'.\n- Every person who dislikes pancakes is now satisfied with it in 'not_orderable'.\n- Every person who dislikes fries is now satisfied with it in 'not_orderable'.\n\nNow we must ensure that each person has at least one liked item in the 'orderable' list since tart, pancakes, and fries