 list."\n\nStep 1: Begin with individuals with specific single likes or dislikes to see if there is any immediate conflict. We find that:\n\n- Nobody has a unique like that only they like and no one else dislikes.\n- Nobody has a unique dislike that only they dislike and no one else likes.\n\nStep 2: Address individuals with many likes and dislikes since they may have items that others dislike and we need to satisfy everyone. Here\'s what we find:\n\n- If "poke" is ordered, then it would not satisfy "Ben," "Xan," "Raj," and "Ravi," who all dislike it. \n- If "curry" is ordered, it will not satisfy "Lia," "Aarav," "Ben," "Ned," "Raj," "Tia," and "Ravi," who all dislike it. But since more people like curry than poke, let\'s consider it for the \'orderable\' and not add poke.\n- If "pho" is ordered, it will not satisfy "Lia," "Rohan," "Shan," "Ben," "Xan," "Val," "Ava," "Yue," "Li," "Ned," and "Gus," who all dislike it. Pho is disliked by more than any other food, let\'s add it to \'not_orderable\'.\n- If "cake" is not ordered, it will satisfy "Lia," "Aarav," "Rohan," "Shan," "Xan," "Val," "Ava," "Yue," "Gus," "Raj," and "Ula," who all dislike it. Since "cake" is liked by several people but disliked by more people, let\'s put "cake" in \'not_orderable\'.\n\nHaving cake and pho on the \'not_orderable\' list satisfies a large number of people: \n\nSatisfied by not ordering pho: Lia, Rohan, Shan, Ben, Xan, Yue, Val, Ava, Li, Du, Ned, Gus, Ravi (13)\nSatisfied by not ordering cake: Lia, Aarav, Rohan, Shan, Xan, Yue, Val, Ava, Raj, Tia, Ned, Ula, Gus (13)\n\nNow, let\'s try to order "curry":\n\nSatisfied by ordering curry: Rohan, Tara, Ava, Yue, Dan, Du, Tao (7)\n\nThis does not cause any new individual to become unsatisfied because everyone who dislikes "curry" is already satisfied by not ordering "cake" or "pho".\n\nRemaining people who need satisfaction from the \'orderable\' list: \n- Aarav (likes pho)\n- Shan (likes cake) - already satisfied by not ordering cake.\n- Tara (likes cake, curry, poke) - satisfied by curry.\n- Dan (likes curry, cake, pho) - satisfied by curry.\n- Du (likes cake, curry, poke) - satisfied by curry.\n- Gus (likes pho, poke) - needs poke.\n- Li (likes poke) - needs poke.\n- Ned (likes poke) - needs poke.\n- Ravi (likes pho) - not satisfied yet.\n- Tia (likes cake) - already satisfied by not ordering \'cake\'.\n- Tao (likes pho, poke, curry) - satisfied by curry.\n- Ula (likes poke) - needs poke.\n- Val (likes poke) - needs poke.\n\nLet\'s see if we can satisfy "Ravi" and "Aarav" (who like "pho") without creating conflict. It appears that we cannot add "pho" to \'orderable\', since more people dislike "pho" than any other item, so that means "Ravi" and "Aarav" need to be satisfied by not ordering something else they dislike. Since "Ravi" dislikes "poke" and "curry," and "Aarav" dislikes "curry," we could not satisfy them by ordering "poke" or "curry." Since we have already decided to not order "cake," the only way "Ravi" and "Aarav" can be satisfied is by not ordering "curry."\n\nHowever, not ordering "curry" would leave Rohan, Tara, Ava, Yue, Dan, Du, and Tao unsatisfied since they like "curry." Therefore, the only option left is to not have poke on the \'orderable\' list, but since both "Ravi" and "Aarav" will still be unsatisfied because their only remaining \'likes\' cannot be ordered and there is nothing they \'dislike\' that can be put in \'not_orderable\'. Thus, it is impossible to satisfy everyone with the given constraints.\n\nOur final output with empty lists will be:\n\n```python\norderable=[], not_orderable=[]\n```', 'num_prompt_tokens': 423, 'num_completion_tokens': 1215}
