ked by someone and not disliked by anyone else, it can go in the 'orderable' list.\n\nFrom the lists, we see that Ben uniquely dislikes cheese, korma, and tart, but since these items are liked by other individuals, we cannot put them in the 'not_orderable' list without causing dissatisfaction.\n\nTo satisfy the maximum number of preferences, we'll have to do a balance between the likes and dislikes. Notice that tart is liked by Du, Zara, and Anil, but only disliked by Ying, Ken, and Ben. This makes tart a potential candidate for the 'orderable' list, as we only need to satisfy Ying, Ken, and Ben with other items.\n\n- By placing tart in 'orderable', we satisfy Du, Zara, and Anil.\n- Cy, Pat, Pam, Ula, and Riya all like cheese and korma but dislike tandoori, they would be satisfied as long as tandoori is not in 'orderable'.\n- Ken also likes cheese but dislikes tart and korma, so by having tart in 'orderable' and not having korma or cheese on the 'not_orderable' list, Ken is also satisfied.\n- Ty likes tandoori but dislikes korma and tart, since tart is in the 'orderable', Ty would need tandoori or an alternative to be satisfied. But we cannot have both tandoori and tart on the same list since they are both liked items and one is disliked by Zara and the other by Ty.\n- Ben dislikes everything, and hence is irrelevant to the 'orderable' list, as he won't be satisfied by adding an item there.\n\nAt this point, to satisfy Ying and Ken, cheese could be placed in 'orderable', because they are the only ones disliking tart, which has been placed in 'orderable', and thus Ying and Ken could be satisfied by having cheese, which they like.\n\nNow, since Ty likes tandoori and cannot be satisfied with tart or anything else, and we cannot satisfy Ty, the criteria cannot be fulfilled for all individuals.\n\nIt seems no such combination exists that satisfies all participants because of the conflicting likes and dislikes specifically around items such as tandoori, tart, and cheese.\