 dislikes, so it will also be polarizing.\n- Salad has a reasonable number of likes and dislikes, again making it challenging.\n- Gelato is mostly liked but has some dislikes.\n\nGiven these observations, we should look for opportunities to satisfy dislikes without alienating any likes. However, there's a complexity here: the lists of likes and dislikes conflict with each other for each item.\n\nWe can't avoid putting an item both groups of people like in the not_orderable list because:\n\n- Everyone who dislikes an item is also countered by someone who likes that item, which would make either party unsatisfied should that item be placed exclusively on either list.\n- Vice versa is also true; every liked item is also disliked by at least one other person.\n\nGiven this deadlock, we start to see that we can't make everybody happy - no matter what we choose, we'll end up with at least one person unhappy with the selection.\n\nFor each food item, there is at least one person who likes it and at least one person who dislikes it, which means no food item can be straightforwardly classified into 'orderable' or 'not_orderable' without leaving someone unsatisfied.\n\nGiven that the criteria mandate that everybody must be satisfied, but we have conflicting likes and dislikes for each and every item, it becomes clear there is no possible combination that could satisfy everybody. Therefore, both lists must be empty to reflect that we cannot fulfill the satisfaction conditions for all individuals.\n\nSo, the final output is:\n\n```python\norderable = [], not_orderable = []\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 812, 'num_completion_tokens': 677}
