, Qi, Ping, Ian, Veer, Naina (likes tiramisu, not ravioli) Arun\n- Salad: Cy, Diya, Bao, Lei, Geet, Qi, Ian\n\nCommon Likes:\n- Tiramisu: Cy, Ana, Raj, Tara, Cal, Hu, Veer, Naina, Arun, Anil\n- Ravioli: Cy, Ty, Raj, Tara, Cal, Hu, Liz\n- Salad: Ty, Ana, Raj, Tara, Cal, Ping, Hu, Veer, Liz\n\n2. Second, we make sure that no item appears on both lists. Since everyone who dislikes a certain food item must have at least one item they dislike on the 'not_orderable' list and everyone who likes a certain food item must have at least one item they like on the 'orderable' list, we cannot have an item on both lists.\n\nStarting with Tiramisu, we see that multiple people like it (e.g., Cy, Ana, Raj, Tara, Cal, Hu, Veer, Naina, Arun, Anil) and several dislike it (e.g., Diya, Ty, Lei, Bao, Chao, Geet, Ping, Ian). If we included Tiramisu on the 'not_orderable' list, Ana and Anil who dislike Ravioli will not have any item they like on the 'orderable'. Since Tiramisu is the only item Ana likes, it must be on the 'orderable' list to satisfy Ana. Therefore, Tiramisu must be on the 'orderable' list. This also satisfies Naina and Arun, who only like Tiramisu.\n\nRavioli is disliked by enough individuals (e.g., Diya, Ana, Lei, Bao, Chao, Geet, Qi, Ping, Ian, Veer, Naina, Arun) that it should be on the 'not_orderable' list. Notice that for all individuals who only like Ravioli, there is another food item they also like which is not universally disliked (e.g., Ty, Liz like Salad which is not universally disliked).\n\nSalad is disliked by several individuals (e.g., Cy, Diya, Bao, Lei, Geet, Qi, Ian) and liked by many as well (e.g., Ty, Ana, Raj, Tara, Cal, Ping, Hu, Veer, Liz), but every person who likes salad either likes at least one additional item that is not universally disliked or dislikes an item that is expected to be in the 'not_orderable' list. Therefore, we don't have to make a decision about Salad yet.\n\nBased on these analyses:\n- Tiramisu must be added to the 'orderable' list to satisfy Ana and others.\n- Ravioli must be added to the 'not_orderable' list because it is disliked by many and those who like it have other options.\n\n3. Now, considering the remaining item, Salad, since we have satisfied the condition for individuals who like/dislike Ravioli and Tiramisu, we can now place Salad on the 'orderable' list if it doesn't conflict with our previous choices. Since Salad is not universally disliked, and all the individuals who dislike salad either like an item already on the 'orderable' list (tiramisu) or dislike an item on the 'not_orderable' list (ravioli), we can safely add Salad to the 'orderable' list.\n\nHere are the final lists that should satisfy everyone:\n- 'orderable': Tiramisu, Salad\n- 'not_orderable': Ravioli\n\nNow let's output the result in the requested Python format:\n\n```python\norderable=['tiramisu', 'salad'], not_orderable=['ravioli']\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 501, 'num_completion_tokens': 965}
