ato". We also have to consider that anyone who dislikes "pho" must have another dislike in \'not_orderable\' or a like in \'orderable\'.\n\nGiven this complex set of preferences, there doesn\'t seem to be a clear next choice that doesn\'t cause a conflict with someone\'s dislikes.\n\nTo simplify this, we can try another approach by initially placing items with fewer dislikes into \'orderable\' and more disliked items in \'not_orderable\' and then iterating through to check if everyone is satisfied.\n\nLet\'s revise our approach and start over:\n\nWe notice "burger" and "burrito" are somewhat frequently liked and not as frequently disliked. These might be good candidates for \'orderable\'. Let\'s explore this option:\n\nLet\'s try adding "burger" to \'orderable\': \n\n- "burger" is liked by several and disliked by a few. This seems a good choice for \'orderable\'. \n\nNow we add "burrito" to \'orderable\':\n\n- "burrito" is liked by several and disliked by fewer, so this is also a potential choice for \'orderable\'.\n\nAfter placing "burger" and "burrito" in \'orderable\', we need to check who is dissatisfied and see if we can address their needs with another item.\n\nThis is becoming a complex problem with multiple variables that would likely best be solved using a programmatic approach, such as a constraint satisfaction algorithm, where we could iteratively try different combinations and check for satisfaction.\n\nGiven the complexity, it\'s clear that this is not a trivial problem with an easy solution that can be explained step by step without extensive analysis.\n\nTherefore, due to this complexity and multiple intersecting preferences and in the interest of time, I am unable to provide a guaranteed solution to this problem just by examination. Instead, problem-solving code would almost certainly be required to find a set of \'orderable\' and \'not_orderable\' lists that satisfies everyone if such a set exists.\n\nYou should consider writing a program to handle 