e" list. The items with dislikes and no likes or very few likes compared to dislikes are candidates for the "not_orderable" list.\n\nHere\'s a strategy:\n- First, find an item that is only disliked and not liked by anyone, if any, and put it in the "not_orderable" list (there doesn\'t seem to be such an item in this case).\n- Then, find items that are liked by at least one person and not disliked by anyone, which would go into the "orderable" list.\n- Finally, try to satisfy the majority of individuals while avoiding conflicts (i.e., an item being in both lists).\n\nFrom the lists above, we can see that there are no items that are only disliked by everyone or liked by everyone without conflict. So, the next step is to choose items that are liked by many and disliked by fewer people while making sure that every individual has at least one of their liked items on the "orderable" list or one of their disliked items on the "not_orderable" list.\n\nLet’s start by making an attempt:\n\n1. We could place "tiramisu" and "risotto" in the "orderable" list since they have more likes than dislikes and are not universally disliked by anyone. However, Zac dislikes both, so this would not satisfy him. Therefore, we cannot place both in the "orderable" list without conflicting with someone\'s dislike. This means we need to be careful with these items.\n\n2. "Burrito" and "peach" are strongly disliked, so we could consider putting one of them in the "not_orderable" list. However, there\'s a problem: they are also liked by several people. If we place "burrito" in the "not_orderable" list, which is mostly disliked, we would satisfy many people\'s dislikes, including Zac\'s, but we would not meet the satisfaction criterion for those who like "burrito". A similar issue arises with "peach."\n\n3. We could place "poke" in the "orderable" list since it is liked by some and disliked by fewer. Rohan, for example, likes "poke" and dislikes "gelato" and "muffins". If we put "poke" in the "orderable" list, we satisfy Rohan. But the problem is that putting "poke" in the "orderable" list would not satisfy Zac, Diya, and Jun, as their dislikes won\'t be fulfilled and their likes won\'t be in the "orderable" list.\n\n4. Considering all of the individuals\' likes and dislikes, it becomes apparent that it is not possible to find a combination of "orderable" and "not_orderable" lists that satisfy everyone since the preferences conflict significantly.\n\nTherefore, the result will be:\n```\norderable = []\nnot_orderable = []\n```', 'num_prompt_tokens': 717, 'num_completion_tokens': 1111}
