iversally liked items with no dislikes:\n- Similarly, no such item exists which is liked and is not disliked by anyone in the group.\n\nSince we don't have any definitive items for our lists, we need to analyze further. We will now proceed to try to construct our lists based on the group's mixed preferences.\n\nWe start by analyzing items that have the fewest dislikes, as they may be easier to place on the 'orderable' list. Simultaneously, we'll look for items with the fewest likes to consider for the 'not_orderable' list.\n\nHere are the items that seem potentially least disputed:\n- Vindaloo: liked by many but disliked by several.\n- Pho: liked by many but disliked by some.\n- Cheesecake: liked by many but disliked by some.\n- Muffins: liked by many but disliked by some.\n- Korma: liked by several but disliked by several.\n- Kimchi: liked by a few but disliked by many.\n- Fries: liked by some but disliked by many.\n- Pancakes: liked by several but disliked by several.\n- Cake: liked by several but disliked by several.\n- Shrimps: liked by several but disliked by a couple.\n\nWe see a lot of conflict, but let's try to satisfy the criteria for every individual:\n\n1. Start by satisfying those with the least flexible preferences. For example, Sam dislikes kimchi, muffins, and pho but doesn't have any likes to balance these, meaning we cannot put any of his dislikes on the 'orderable' list.\n2. Items like kimchi and fries are disliked by many and could potentially be in 'not_orderable' to satisfy multiple people.\n3. Vindaloo and cake are both liked and disliked by many; we need to navigate these preferences carefully.\n\nTo satisfy the requirements, we need to ensure that if a participant's liked item is not in 'orderable', one of their disliked items must be in 'not_orderable' and vice versa. With the conflicting likes and dislikes, it seems challenging to satisfy everyone as the dislikes seem to overlap with someone else's likes.\n\nAfter examining all preferences,