being on any list exclusively.\n- Tandoori is liked by Bo, May, Anil, Ty, Vic, Eli, Leo and disliked by Zoe, Neil, Ivy, Jan, Raj, Max, Hui, Tia, Mel, Liv, Arun. Therefore, tandoori also cannot satisfy the condition of being on any list exclusively.\n\nLet's look at individual combinations and crosses based on the satisfaction criteria:\n\n1. Zoe will be satisfied if either risotto or brownies is orderable, or tandoori is not orderable.\n2. Neil will be satisfied if risotto is orderable, or tandoori and brownies are not orderable.\n3. Bo will be satisfied with any of the items being orderable.\n4. May, Ty, and Vic will be satisfied if either risotto or tandoori is orderable, or brownies are not orderable.\n5. Ivy, Jan, Max, Tia will be satisfied if brownies are orderable or both tandoori and risotto are not orderable.\n6. Raj, Anil, and Eli will be satisfied if tandoori is orderable or both risotto and brownies are not orderable.\n7. Arun and Mel will be satisfied if either risotto or brownies is orderable, or tandoori is not orderable.\n8. Leo will be satisfied if either tandoori or brownies is orderable or risotto is not orderable.\n9. Hui will be satisfied only if all items he dislikes are not orderable.\n10. Liv will also be satisfied only if all items she dislikes are not orderable.\n\nGiven that Hui and Liv dislike all three items and will only be satisfied if all of those are not orderable, we immediately face a conflict, as this would mean no items could be orderable, which would make it impossible to satisfy the others who need at least one item orderable to be satisfied.\n\nConsidering these constraints, it seems impossible to find a combination of 'orderable' and 'not_orderable' lists that satisfies everyone in the group. No matter what we choose, Hui and Liv's complete dislikes conflict with other people's likes, preventing us from being able to satisfy all participants.\n\nSince we cannot meet the satisfaction criteria for all individuals, we will output