s tally:\n\n- Udon: Likes 9, Dislikes 10\n- Korma: Likes 10, Dislikes 9\n- Cheesecake: Likes 17, Dislikes 4\n- Tart: Likes 13, Dislikes 9\n- Cake: Likes 21, Dislikes 9\n- Baklava: Likes 11, Dislikes 11\n- Macaron: Likes 11, Dislikes 13\n- Tacos: Likes 17, Dislikes 15\n- Lobster: Likes 13, Dislikes 9\n- Bread: Likes 14, Dislikes 5\n\nNow, based on the satisfaction criteria, for a food item to be 'orderable', it should be disliked by nobody. For a food item to be 'not_orderable', it must be liked by at least one person. Looking at the tallies, we can see the following:\n\n- No food item is disliked by nobody, so we cannot directly add any item to the 'orderable' list.\n- The following foods are liked by someone and, based on criteria 2, could be potential candidates for the 'not_orderable' list since they are liked and therefore would satisfy those who like them:\n  - Udon\n  - Korma\n  - Cheesecake\n  - Tart\n  - Cake\n  - Baklava\n  - Macaron\n  - Tacos\n  - Lobster\n  - Bread\n\nNow, we must carefully choose items for the 'not_orderable' list to make sure we satisfy everyone based on their dislikes as well, without directly contradicting someone else's likes. To maximize satisfaction, we take items that the most people dislike while ensuring no one who likes those items is left without an option.\n\nThe following item is liked by the most people and should be our primary consideration for the 'orderable' list to satisfy the majority of preferences:\n\n- Cake (liked by 21 people and disliked by 9)\n\nSimilarly, we can add cheesecake to the list of 'not_orderable' items to satisfy the most number of people who do not like it while ensuring that those who do like it have other options among their likes:\n\n- Cheesecake (likes 17, dislikes 4)\n\nAs we add items to the 'not_orderable' list, we must ensure every person who likes that item has another item they like that is not 'not_orderable'. We also need to ensure that every person who dislikes that item is satisfied by having an item they dislike in the 'not_orderable' list or an item they like in the 'orderable' list.\n\nCake, being liked by many and with fewer dislikes, could potentially be in the 'orderable' list to satisfy a large group of people, but we must cross-check with the people who dislike cake to ensure that they will be satisfied by the 'not_orderable' list or have other likes in the 'orderable' list.\n\nThis process requires careful addition and checks for both lists. Given the complexity of balancing everyone's likes and dislikes, it may not be possible to satisfy all individuals' criteria perfectly without further constraints or additional information.\n\nSince no food item meets the criteria for being universally orderable (no dislikes), we can't satisfy everybody with the food selection, and we must output empty lists for 'orderable' and 'not_orderable':\n\n```python\norderable = []\nnot_orderable = []\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 1483, 'num_completion_tokens': 800}
