e hummus, which can be orderable).\n\nGiven the nature of these preferences and the satisfaction criteria, we propose the following solution:\n\n'Orderable' list:\n- hummus (neutral, satisfies several likes)\n- cheesecake (neutral, satisfies likes and no forced dislikes)\n\n'Not_orderable' list:\n- pho (satisfies all dislikes, no exclusive likes)\n- biscuits (satisfies dislikes, no exclusive likes)\n- baklava (satisfies dislikes, no exclusive likes)\n- burger (satisfies dislikes, no forced likes since Fay and Mel like hummus which is orderable)\n\nBy choosing these items for the 'not_orderable' list, we ensure that people who dislike them are satisfied without making disliked items orderable. The rest of the items are either neutral or result in conflicts if placed on either list, and therefore we avoid listing them entirely.\n\nTherefore, the solution would be:\n\norderable=['hummus', 'cheesecake'], not_orderable=['pho', 'biscuits', 'baklava', 'burger']", 'num_prompt_tokens': 1032, 'num_completion_tokens': 896}
