ep by step and identify if there are any contradictions that make it impossible to create such lists.\n\nHere are the high-level steps I will follow:\n1. Identify the food items that are liked by at least one person and disliked by at least one person. These items are potential candidates for conflicts.\n2. Identify if there are any foods that everyone who likes them, has a dislike that could be put into the 'not_orderable' list to satisfy their conditions.\n3. Check for any items that are universally disliked or liked only by people who have a satisfying disliked item. These can be safely added to the 'not_orderable' list.\n4. For the remaining items that are liked, ensure that adding them to the 'orderable' list does not cause a conflict for individuals who dislike them.\n\nLet’s identify the conflicts and see if it’s possible to create the 'orderable' and 'not_orderable' lists that meet everyone's criteria.\n\nFirst, let’s summarize preferences:\n\nLikes:\n- Tiramisu: liked by many, disliked by some (cannot be placed in neither list without further analysis).\n- Pie: liked by many, disliked by some (cannot be placed in neither list without further analysis).\n- Ravioli: liked by many, disliked by some (cannot be placed in neither list without further analysis).\n- Mousse: liked by many, disliked by some (cannot be placed in neither list without further analysis).\n- Bruschetta: liked by many, disliked by many (cannot be placed in neither list without further analysis).\n- Waffles: liked by many, disliked by some (cannot be placed in neither list without further analysis).\n\nDislikes:\n- Waffles: disliked by many, liked by some.\n- Bruschetta: disliked by many, liked by some.\n- Tiramisu: disliked by some, liked by many.\n- Ravioli: disliked by many, liked by some.\n- Pie: disliked by some, liked by many.\n- Mousse: disliked by some, liked by many.\n\nWe see that every food item is liked by someone and disliked by someone else. \n\nGiven that, we need to look for