x, Liv\n\nNext, let\'s establish what we know:\n\n1. An item that someone likes can not be on the \'not_orderable\' list if it is also liked by another person. Similarly, an item that someone dislikes cannot be on the \'orderable\' list if it is also disliked by another person.\n2. An individual will be satisfied if either one of their liked items is on the \'orderable\' list or one of their disliked items is on the \'not_orderable\' list.\n\nGiven that \'couscous\' is disliked by many but not liked by anyone, we can safely put it on the \'not_orderable\' list to satisfy Min, Wei, Tao, Pia. Let\'s start with that:\n\n`not_orderable = ["couscous"]`\n`orderable = []`\n\nNow let\'s look for other items that can be placed in either list without violating the satisfaction criteria.\n\n- \'Burrito\' is disliked by many, and only Val and Fay like it. Since Val dislikes both \'doughnut\' and \'muffins\', which are liked by others, and Fay likes \'nachos\' and \'vindaloo\', which no one dislikes, we can safely place \'burrito\' on the \'not_orderable\' list to satisfy both Val through her dislikes and Fay through her other likes.\n\n`not_orderable = ["couscous", "burrito"]`\n`orderable = []`\n\nNext, we need to look for more items that can be clearly categorized without causing issues:\n\n- \'Vindaloo\' is liked by quite a few people and only disliked by Wes and Chao. Wes also likes \'nachos\', and Chao dislikes \'nachos\' and \'burrito\' (already not_orderable), so \'vindaloo\' can be on the \'orderable\' list.\n\n`not_orderable = ["couscous", "burrito"]`\n`orderable = ["vindaloo"]`\n\nLet\'s move forward:\n\n- \'Muffins\' are liked by many, but disliked by many as well. So it’s tricky to place it in either list without more information.\n- \'Salmon\' is disliked by several people, and only Vic and Gen like it. Gen also likes \'muffins\', which are controversial, but Vic dislikes \'burrito\' (already not_orderable), so we cannot satisfy Gen through her like of \'salmon\' without having other information on \'muffins\'. We should pause on \'salmon\'.\n\nChecking the least controversial items at this point:\n\n- \'Nachos\' is liked by many and only disliked by Chao, who is already satisfied through \'burrito\'. It’s a clear candidate for \'orderable\'.\n\n`not_orderable = ["couscous", "burrito"]`\n`orderable = ["vindaloo", "nachos"]`\n\nNow let\'s look at \'muffins\' again:\n\n- The people who dislike \'muffins\' (Val, Vic, May, Ansh, Max, Liv) are already satisfied either by \'burrito\' on the \'not_orderable\' list or by other likes on the orderable list (\'nachos\' for Sky and Wes, \'vindaloo\' for Ron), except for Liv, who also likes \'doughnut\'.\n- Since \'doughnut\' cannot be on the \'not_orderable\' list to satisfy Liv through a like, \'doughnut\' must be on the \'orderable\' lest, and this settle \'muffins\' to be on the \'not_orderable\' list.\n\n`not_orderable = ["couscous", "burrito", "muffins"]`\n`orderable = ["vindaloo", "nachos", "doughnut"]`\n\nSo we have a solution that seems to satisfy everyone:\n\n- Sid is satisfied by \'doughnut\' (like).\n- Min is satisfied by \'nachos\' (like) and \'couscous\' (dislike).\n- Val is satisfied by \'burrito\' (dislike).\n- Sky is satisfied by \'doughnut\' and \'nachos\' (likes).\n- Vic is satisfied by \'burrito\' (dislike).\n- Oli is satisfied by \'muffins\' (dislike).\n- Wei is satisfied by \'muffins\' (dislike).\n- Wes is satisfied by \'nachos\' (like).\n- May is satisfied by \'burrito\' (dislike).\n- Ansh is satisfied by \'burrito\' (dislike).\n- Tao is satisfied by \'vindaloo\' (like).\n- Ron is satisfied by \'vindaloo\' (like).\n- Max is satisfied by \'burrito\' (dislike).\n- Liv is satisfied by \'doughnut\' (like).\n- Pia is satisfied by \'nachos\' (like).\n- Chao is satisfied by \'burrito\' (dislike).\n- Gen is satisfied by \'muffins\' (dislike).\n- Fay is satisfied by \'nachos\' and \'vindaloo\' (likes).\n- Meg is satisfied by \'muffins\' (dislike).\n- Feng is satisfied by \'do