 some logic to narrow down what can be on the 'orderable' and 'not_orderable' lists based on the minimum satisfaction criteria:\n\n1. An item that no one dislikes can be automatically ordered. In this case, no such item exists.\n2. An item that everyone who likes it also dislikes something else can be 'not_orderable' if that something else is 'orderable'. For instance, all people who like naan also dislike at least one other item. This means that naan can be 'not_orderable' as long as one of the items they dislike is 'orderable'.\n3. If an item is disliked by more people than it is liked, it makes more sense to put it in the 'not_orderable' list to satisfy the larger group.\n\nFrom the dislike section, gelato has the highest number of dislikes (11) with only 4 likes. This suggests that gelato should be 'not_orderable'.\n\nWe can satisfy many people by putting gelato on the 'not_orderable' list, leaving custard, which almost everyone likes and only 3 dislike, as 'orderable'. This takes care of many people.\n\nLet's start with these two items and iterate:\n\n- Gelato is 'not_orderable'.\n- Custard is 'orderable'.\n\n**After these assignments, everyone's satisfaction criteria that involve custard and gelato are met.**\n\nNow, for naan and fries, since they are liked and disliked by some, we have to make sure each person who likes these items would still be satisfied through their other likes or dislikes if these were 'not_orderable'. We can ensure everyone who likes naan is satisfied because they all have some other dislike which can be served or another like which can be ordered. This cannot be said for fries as Gen likes only fries and dislikes only salmon and custard; custard is already ordered, so if fries are not orderable, Gen won't be satisfied.\n\nTherefore, naan will be 'not_orderable', and fries will be 'orderable'.\n\n- Naan is 'not_orderable'.\n- Fries is 'orderable'.\n\nNow we have to check for salmon and udon. Salmon is difficult since it's liked almost as much as it is disliked. Udon can be 'not_orderable' because individuals who like udon have another food they like which could be 'orderable.' \n\n- Udon is 'not_orderable'.\n\nWith custard already 'orderable', individuals who like salmon will be satisfied. However, we have to ensure that everyone who dislikes salmon can still be satisfied. This is already true since gelato, which is disliked alongside salmon by some, is 'not_orderable', and custard, which is liked, is 'orderable'. Thus, salmon can be 'not_orderable'.\n\n- Salmon is 'not_orderable'.\n\nHaving done this process, each individual has at least one like that is 'orderable' or a dislike that is 'not_orderable', as follows:\n\n- Orderable: [custard, fries]\n- Not_Orderable: [gelato, naan, udon, salmon]\n\nThis satisfies everyone according to the given criteria. \n\nHere is the python representation of our solution:\n\n`orderable=['custard', 'fries'], not_orderable=['gelato', 'naan', 'udon', 'salmon']`", 'num_prompt_tokens': 498, 'num_completion_tokens': 850}
