r instance, if someone likes "X" and dislikes "Y" where "Y" is liked by many others, "X" should likely be \'orderable\' (to satisfy that person).\n\nGiven the complexity of the task with many individuals and their varied preferences, a brute-force approach to consider every possible combination could be very time-consuming and unlikely to be done manually. We should look for a combination that satisfies the satisfaction criteria for as many people as possible and then finesse our list by including or excluding certain items.\n\nSince Sorbet and Risotto are the most contentious items, let\'s consider the implications of choosing each as \'orderable\' or \'not_orderable\':\n\nOption 1: Put Sorbet in \'orderable\' and Risotto in \'not_orderable\':\n- This potentially satisfies Liv, Tim, Veer, Li, Kavi, Mina, Bao, Lei, Jun, Ravi, Sorbet-likers, and Hal, Eva, Om, Yan, Risotto-dislikers.\n\nComparatively, if we put Risotto in \'orderable\', given the number of people who dislike it, it might be harder to satisfy the group.\n\nNow, let\'s see if we can satisfy everyone by this arrangement:\n\n- We can also place Tart in \'not_orderable\' because the people who dislike Tart never like Risotto. This satisfies people with a dislike for Tart.\n- We now have to place items in orderable that each person who had Tart or Sorbet as their only like has in their like list, without conflicting with those who dislike those items. From the list, we see that the people who like Sorbet and Tart also like other items.\n\nNow, let\'s start fulfilling the likes of participants who dislike both Sorbet and Risotto:\n- Ivy likes Tandoori and dislikes Sorbet and Tart, so we can consider Tandoori for \'orderable\'. This also satisfies Ana, Mia, Ping, Riya, Nita, and Yan (without considering their dislikes yet).\n- Tom likes Omelette and dislikes Sorbet and Caramel. Since we already have Tandoori, and Ana dislikes Toast, putting Omelette in \'orderable\' is a good choice. This satisfies Tom, Liv, 