 risotto, tacos, pie\nLeo: Likes pie, tacos, risotto\nLiz: Likes vegetables, churros. Dislikes tacos\nZoe: Likes risotto, vegetables, salmon\n\nPie is disliked by several, but also liked by a few, so its placement will determine many other items.\n\nLet's first note the items that must be in the 'orderable' list to satisfy those who only like specific items:\n- Liv, Riya, Leo, and Zoe need salmon or risotto to be satisfied.\n\nBased on that, salmon or risotto must be in the 'orderable' list. However, salmon is disliked by several people and risotto by fewer people. Let's put risotto into the 'orderable' list to satisfy Liv, Riya, Zoe, Meg, Rex, and Ty, and Leo.\n\n'orderable' = [risotto]\n\nNow, for the 'not_orderable' list:\n\n- Since risotto is in the 'orderable' list, Ivy and Mina, who dislike risotto, are already satisfied.\n- Dot and Rina need muffins to be in the 'not_orderable' list to be satisfied (since pancakes and vegetables are neutral).\n- Mel and Liz are satisfied with muffins being in the 'not_orderable' list as they both like tacos and vegetables, which have not been placed yet and no dislikes were placed in 'orderable'.\n\n'not_orderable' = [muffins]\n\nNow we've satisfied everyone whose major preferences depend on items that have been placed.\n\nWith risotto placed in 'orderable' (satisfying Riya, Meg, Rex, Ty, and Leo), and muffins placed in 'not_orderable' (satisfying Dot, Rina, Mel, and Liz), we already satisfied quite a few people. Now let's continue:\n\n- We cannot place both salmon and pie due to Sam and Nic's preferences, and we know that we already satisfied those who like risotto.\n- Since Ty and Riya like pie, and Riya is already satisfied with risotto, we need to satisfy Ty, but we cannot put pie in 'orderable' because Sam, Nic, Liv, Ivy, Zara, and Mina dislike pie.\n- However, since Ty also likes risotto which is orderable, he doesn't need pie to be orderable, only not in not_orderable.\n- Sam, Nic, Liv, Zara, Mina, and Ivy are now all satisfied as they have a disliked food in 'not_orderable' or a liked food in 'orderable'.\n- Since salmon cannot be placed in either list due to conflicts, everyone who likes it is satisfied by risotto instead.\n- Pancakes and churros have no conflicts and can satisfy Zara, Dot, Lan, and Liz.\n- Custard can also satisfy Zara and Rex since it has no major dislikes.\n\nNow let's check the last few people:\n\n- Zara is now satisfied with either pancakes or custard being 'orderable').\n- Lan likes churros, salmon, and muffins. Muffins are not 'orderable', so Lan is satisfied.\n- Li is satisfied as pancakes and risotto are not 'not_orderable'.\n- Meg is satisfied with risotto being 'orderable'.\n- Mel likes tacos, and they can be 'orderable' since her dislikes are not in the 'orderable' list.\n\nThis seems to satisfy everyone. Now, let's finalize the 'orderable' list:\n\n'orderable' = [risotto, pancakes, churros, custard, tacos]\n\nNothing else needs to be added to 'not_orderable', as doing so may risk dissatisfaction to someone.\n\n'orderable' = [risotto, pancakes, churros, custard, tacos]\n'not_orderable' = [muffins]\n\nFinal solution:\n```\norderable=['risotto', 'pancakes', 'churros', 'custard', 'tacos'], not_orderable=['muffins']\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 410, 'num_completion_tokens': 1058}
