sed on the remaining unsatisfied preferences. Let\'s try to satisfy as many people as possible with these preliminaries:\n\n- \'orderable\': [muffins]\n- \'not_orderable\': [ravioli]\n\nNow we\'ll check if we can satisfy everyone with "bruschetta" and "sushi." We have some people who dislike "bruschetta" (Veer, Sal, and Jan), and some who dislike "sushi" (Lex, Hui, and Wes). We\'ll have to find a balance. Looking at the lists:\n\n- If "bruschetta" is \'not_orderable\', Veer, Sal, and Jan are satisfied, but Du, Jon, Zoe, Feng, Ivy, Isha, Wes, Rohan, and Wes would not be satisfied.\n- If "bruschetta" is \'orderable\', Du, Jon, Zoe, Feng, Ivy, Isha, Wes, and Rohan would be satisfied, but Veer, Sal, and Jan would not.\n\n"Sushi" is a bit easier since it is liked by fewer people and those that dislike "ravioli" would already be satisfied by it being \'not_orderable\'. We\'ll check how "sushi" affects satisfaction:\n\n- If "sushi" is \'orderable\', Jaya, Bea, Kai, and Rohan would be satisfied.\n- If "sushi" is \'not_orderable\', Lex, Hui, and Wes would be satisfied.\n\nSince "muffins" are already \'orderable\' and satisfy Lex, Hui, and Wes, we can consider placing "sushi" in \'orderable\' to satisfy Jaya, Bea, Kai, and Rohan without negatively affecting the others.\n\nNow, we need to determine the placement of "bruschetta":\n\n- Those who dislike "bruschetta" are Veer, Sal, and Jan. However, all of them like "muffins", which are \'orderable\', so they\'re already satisfied.\n- Those who like "bruschetta" require it to be \'orderable\' to be satisfied (unless they also like "muffins"). Ivy, who only likes "bruschetta", will not be satisfied unless "bruschetta" is on the \'orderable\' list.\n\nBy placing "bruschetta" in the \'orderable\' list, everyone who likes "bruschetta" will be satisfied while those who dislike it will be satisfied by other items on the \'orderable\' list. This way, we can satisfy everyone with the combination:\n\n- \'orderable\': [muffins, bruschetta,