amental constraints: \n1. If someone likes an item and no one dislikes it, it can be 'orderable'. \n2. If someone dislikes an item and no one likes it, it can be 'not_orderable'.\n3. If there is a conflict where some like and some dislike an item, we need to look deeper to see if satisfying one would still allow satisfying the other through another item.\n\nThrough examination, I realized that these steps alone won't suffice and it's highly likely that we'll need an iterative approach to check for different combinations. Since the instructions demand a step-by-step process with all the work shown, we must proceed more organically, looking at common likes and dislikes, and move forward inductively.\n\nLet's begin by finding conflicts to understand what cannot be straightforwardly categorized as 'orderable' or 'not_orderable':\n\nConflicts are found where at least one person likes an item and at least one person dislikes the same item:\n- sushi: liked by many, disliked by many\n- bruschetta: liked by many, disliked by many\n- tandoori: liked by many, disliked by many\n- churros: liked by many, disliked by a few\n- quiche: liked by many, disliked by a few\n- pho: liked by many, disliked by many\n- sorbet: liked by many, disliked by many\n- ravioli: liked by many, disliked by a few\n- udon: liked by many, disliked by a few\n- tart: liked by many, disliked by many\n\nNone of the items can be straightforwardly categorized without conflict. Since satisfying all participants is a requirement, we need to look for items that are liked by some but not disliked by any or disliked by some but not liked by any to make them 'orderable' or 'not_orderable'. But such a scenario does not exist here due to the conflicts.\n\nTherefore, we are left with the dynamic situation where we must satisfy each person through a careful selection. Let's try creating a combination that satisfies everyone. We need to consider the following:\n- Every person should have at least one liked item in 'orderable' or one disliked item in 'not_orderable'.\n- Trying to satisfy people with the most restrictions first may lead us to a solution that fits others as well because it requires more specific conditions to be met.\n\nReviewing the list, we can see that every food item is liked by some participants and disliked by others. This makes it impossible to satisfy all participants with a combination of 'orderable' and 'not_orderable' lists since we would need at least one item to be exclusively liked or disliked, which is not the case.\n\nConsequently, we cannot find a set of 'orderable' and 'not_orderable' items that will satisfy everyone's preferences according to the rules. Thus, the lists must be empty.\n\nFinal solution:\n```python\norderable = []\nnot_orderable = []\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 1645, 'num_completion_tokens': 680}
