r the 'not_orderable' list. If an item is both liked and disliked, it requires further consideration to see if it can be placed in one of the lists without leaving anyone unsatisfied.\n\nLet’s start by reviewing items that can be directly placed into 'orderable' because they are liked by at least one person and not disliked by any, and items that can be directly placed into 'not_orderable' because they are disliked by at least one person and not liked by any:\n\nAfter a quick survey, we see that no item is universally liked without any dislikes. Similarly, no item is universally disliked without being liked. Therefore, we need to choose items that can satisfy the highest number of people without causing dissatisfaction to others.\n\nTo maximize satisfaction, we should prefer to include items that are liked by most and are the least disliked. For example, 'shawarma' is liked by many but also disliked by several. Items like 'ravioli', 'salmon', 'custard', 'frittata', 'pancakes', and 'kimchi' also follow this pattern.\n\nLet's try to satisfy as many people as possible, one by one, while fulfilling the rest of the criteria. We should start by including items on the 'orderable' list that are uniquely liked by someone but not disliked and then including items on the 'not_orderable' list that are uniquely disliked by someone but not liked. However, given the complexity, we should take an item that is liked by many and not disliked by a greater number of people and put it in the 'orderable' list. Then we can counterbalance this by placing an item that is disliked by many of these same people in the 'not_orderable' list to satisfy them.\n\nTo find if there's a combination that could potentially satisfy everyone, we need an algorithmic approach that likely includes backtracking or constraint satisfaction. We're seeking a satisfactory mix of items for the 'orderable' and 'not_orderable' lists where:\n\n- Orderable list contains at least one liked item OR\n- Not_orderable list 