sial, with a large number of likes and equivalent dislikes.\n\nOur next step is to find an item or items that everyone who dislikes them would find something they like in the \'orderable list.\'\n\nLet\'s attempt to find a solution:\n\n- If we put "Omelette" on the \'not_orderable\' list, it will satisfy those who dislike it, and every one of those individuals has at least one other item they like that is not Omelette.\n  \n- "Bruschetta" only has two dislikes, and both individuals disliking it (Tao & Bea) like Wonton, which is not that controversial. This suggests that we might be able to place Bruschetta on the \'orderable\' list.\n\n- "Guacamole" and "Ramen" are the most controversial items; with almost equal likes and dislikes, placing them on either list will likely leave several individuals unsatisfied.\n\n- "Baklava" has many likes, but also a significant number of dislikes, making it challenging to please both groups. However, every individual disliking Baklava has at least one liked food item that is not Baklava.\n\n- "Wonton" and "Curry" are equally controversial, though less so than Ramen and Guacamole.\n\nBased on this analysis, we could consider placing \'Omelette\' in the \'not_orderable\' list (since everyone who dislikes Omelette has at least one other food they like), and \'Bruschetta\' in the \'orderable\' list (since the ones that dislike it, like other items that could be made orderable). \n\nGiven this is a complicated problem and might have conflicts, let’s see what we’re left with:\n- Baklava has many likes and several dislikes, but every person disliking Baklava likes something else not yet categorized, so we could probably put Baklava in the \'orderable\' list.\n- "Guacamole", "Ramen", "Wonton", and "Curry" are still problematic, given their split opinions. We\'ll avoid placing any of these on either list just yet.\n\nGiven this, let\'s see if we can satisfy everyone:\n\nBy placing \'Omelette\' on the \'not_orderable\' list, we\'re satisfying 7 people who dislike it, provided the \'orderable\' list contains at least one item they all like.\n\nBy placing \'Bruschetta\' and \'Baklava\' on the \'orderable\' list, we\'re satisfying at least 10 and 17 people who like these items, respectively.\n\nNow, the remaining individuals who either like or dislike \'Guacamole\', \'Wonton\', \'Ramen\', and \'Curry\' must be checked to see if they are satisfied with \'Bruschetta\' and \'Baklava\' as orderable items.\n\nWe need to ensure that individuals who dislike \'Guacamole\', \'Wonton\', \'Ramen\', and \'Curry\' also like either \'Bruschetta\' or \'Baklava\'. If not, they must have a liked item in the \'orderable\' list, which we have not yet defined, to make them satisfied. Those who like "Guacamole", "Wonton", "Ramen", and "Curry" are already satisfied since they have nothing in \'not_orderable\' that they dislike.\n\nFor those who dislike \'Guacamole\', \'Wonton\', \'Ramen\', and \'Curry\', and their liked item isn’t \'Bruschetta\' or \'Baklava\', we need an additional item from their like list to add to the \'orderable\' list, or we will hit a contradiction.\n\nLet\'s take a specific case:\n- Hui: Dislikes \'Guacamole\' and \'Wonton\', Likes \'Baklava\' (Satisfied)\n- Pam: Dislikes \'Baklava\', Likes \'Curry\' and \'Ramen\' (Not Satisfied yet; needs \'Curry\' or \'Ramen\' in \'orderable\')\n- ... and similar logic applies to others.\n\nCompiling the likes and dislikes we see that there is no remaining food item that is liked by someone who has a disliked item in \'orderable\', but is not disliked by someone who has nothing that they liked in \'orderable\'. Thus, we cannot satisfy the criteria for all individuals with the food items left.\n\nThe conclusion is that no combination of \'orderable\' and \'not_orderable\' lists exists that would satisfy all participants according to the given criteria. Therefore, both lists should be empty:\n\n```python\norderable=[], not_orderable=[]\n```', 'num_prompt_tokens': 1362,