 step by step:\n\n1. Identify food items that are universally disliked or cannot be in 'orderable' to satisfy everyone's preferences. If someone dislikes a food item and no one exclusively likes that same food item (meaning it’s not their only like), it can be safely put into 'not_orderable'.\n\n2. Identify food items that are universally liked or must be in 'orderable' to satisfy someone's preferences. If someone likes a food item and no one dislikes that same food item, it can be safely put into 'orderable'.\n\n3. Solve for items that are liked and disliked by the group to satisfy the most number of preferences without conflict. A conflict occurs when an item is liked by one and disliked by another.\n\nLet's begin:\n\n- Shawarma is disliked by many, and although liked by some, it can be put into 'not_orderable'. This will satisfy those who dislike shawarma without conflicting with those who like it because they have other likes as well.\n- Espresso is liked by many and would need to be 'orderable', but Bao, Ana, Du, and Sue dislike it and would not be satisfied if it were simply in the 'orderable' list. For each of them, they have an alternative like (pizza for Bao, Ana, and Sue, roti for Du) that can be in 'orderable', except for Bao and Ana, who also dislike shawarma, which is already in 'not_orderable', satisfying their preference. However, Du must have roti in 'orderable' to be satisfied.\n- Pizza is liked by many. However, Bao and Ana have been satisfied with shawarma in 'not_orderable'. Others disliking Pizza have other likes. So pizza can be safely put in 'orderable'.\n- Korma is disliked by a few who have other options liked. It can go either way, and since more people dislike it, we'll initially leave it out of 'orderable' and see if there's a need to adjust later.\n- Salad is disliked by a few. It can be put into 'orderable' for Chen, who dislikes both pizza and espresso, which cannot be done since one of those must be orderable.\n- Roti is liked by many but disliked by a few, such as Isha, Lia, and some others who have other options liked. For now, we keep it out of 'orderable' until we see all the preferences.\n- Omelette is also in a similar situation as roti. We keep it out of 'orderable' for now.\n- Waffles have been disliked by some, meaning they must be placed in 'not_orderable' to satisfy the preferences of those individuals.\n\nNow we will list out the items based on our analysis:\n\n'not_orderable':\n- Shawarma (disliked by many)\n- Waffles (disliked by some, and those liking it have other options)\n\n'orderable':\n- Pizza (liked by many without strong conflicting dislikes)\n- Salad (liked by many, and the disliking ones have alternatives or are satisfied with the 'not_orderable' list)\n- Espresso (liked by many and necessary for some, the ones that dislike have been taken care of)\n\nGiven this preliminary classification, let's recheck whether this distribution satisfies each individual's preference. Each one should have at least one liked item in 'orderable' or one disliked item in 'not_orderable':\n\n- Ken, Jaya, Sky, Pam, and many others who like the items in 'orderable' are satisfied.\n- Bao, Ana, Du have shawarma in 'not_orderable' and are thus satisfied. \n- Abe dislikes espresso but has korma as a like; we may have to put korma in 'orderable' to satisfy Abe.\n- Du is also satisfied with roti in 'orderable'.\n- Abe, Tim, Min, Ty, and a few who dislike korma are the problem now. Tim can be satisfied with shawarma in 'not_orderable', Min and Ty dislike all three items of 'orderable' so far and no dislikes in 'not_orderable'. Similarly, Abe's likes do not align with 'orderable', and his dislikes are not all in 'not_orderable'.\n- Since Min and Ty dislike all of our current 'orderable' items and their dislikes aren't all in 'not_orderable', they cannot be satisfied. This means there's no distribution that satisfies everyone's preferences given the current 'orderable' and 'not_orderable' lists.\n\nAfter