 Dislikes tart, pie. Leo: Likes pho, espresso. Dislikes tart. Max: Likes espresso. Dislikes pie, pho. Rob: Dislikes pie, espresso, tart. Lou: Likes espresso, pie. Dislikes tart. Raj: Likes pie. Dislikes espresso, tart. Ken: Likes pho. Dislikes tart, pie. Wes: Likes pho, espresso, pie. Ada: Likes tart, pie. Dislikes pho. ', 'menu_items': ['espresso', 'tart', 'pho', 'pie'], 'gpt_out': "Let's follow a step-by-step process to find a combination of 'orderable' and 'not_orderable' lists that satisfy all individuals, or conclude that it is not possible.\n\nFirstly, we take note of the universally disliked and liked items. If there is an item that everyone who listed it dislikes, it is a candidate for 'not_orderable'. If there is an item that is liked by everyone who mentioned it, it can be considered for 'orderable'.\n\nHowever, it's important to note that to satisfy all individuals, at least one item they like must be 'orderable' or one they dislike must be 'not_orderable'.\n\nLet's check item by item:\n\n- Pho: Liked by many and disliked by some. We cannot add it to 'not_orderable' as it will dissatisfy those who like it. It can't go to 'orderable' either, as it'll dissatisfy those who dislike it.\n- Espresso: Liked by many, but also disliked by many. Same scenario as with pho.\n- Tart: Liked by many, but also disliked by many. Same scenario as with pho and espresso.\n- Pie: Liked by many, but also disliked by some. Same scenario as with the others.\n\nSince there's no universally disliked item, we can rule out having anything naturally belonging in 'not_orderable'.\n\nSince each item is liked by at least one person and disliked by at least one other, it seems impossible to satisfy all participants through universal likes or dislikes. We'll need to review individual preferences to find a suitable combination.\n\nFor the 'orderable' list to satisfy the likes, it can include pho, espresso, tart, or pie as long as at least one person who likes the item is satisfied and no persons who dislike the item are unsatisfied.\n\nQuick observations:\n1. Pho cannot be 'orderable' because Bea dislikes pho, tart, and pie, which means Pho must be 'not_orderable' to satisfy Bea. Bea's criteria force our hand on the other items.\n2. Since Bea also dislikes tart and pie, those cannot be 'not_orderable', and thus they must be 'orderable' to satisfy others.\n3. No other disliked foods can be 'not_orderable', because that would dissatisfy the participants that like them.\n\nFrom this point onwards, since if we put tart and pie to 'orderable', we will essentially satisfy pretty much every person who likes those and only need to focus our attention on individuals who have not expressed a like for tart or pie.\n\nThe interesting cases are:\n- Espresso: every person that likes espresso, with the exception of Arun and Cal, also likes either tart or pie. Thus, espresso should go to 'not_orderable' to satisfy their dislikes. Arun will be satisfied with 'orderable' tart or pie regardless of espresso being 'not_orderable'. Cal dislikes pie and tart but likes espresso; however, since he is the only person left who is not satisfied by tart or pie being 'orderable', we prioritize the majority and place espresso in 'not_orderable' regardless.\n\nTherefore, our final solution is:\n- orderable: tart, pie\n- not_orderable: pho, espresso\n\nBy selecting tart and pie for 'orderable' and pho and espresso for 'not_orderable', everyone's preference is met: at least one item they like is available or one they dislike is not available. There's no need to proceed further, as this solution already satisfies all individuals.\n\nFinal lists:\n```\norderable = ['tart', 'pie']\nnot_orderable = ['pho', 'espresso']\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 704, 'num_completion_tokens': 754}
Data Sample: {'num_vars': 7, 'num_clauses': 21, 'formula': [[-6, -1, 2], [5, 2, -3], [5, -7, -4], [7, -2, -5], [4, 7, -3], [-4, -5, 3], [5, 3, 1], [-5, 4, -1], [-6, 1, 4], [-6, -3, -1], [5, -2, 3], [-4, -2, 7], [-5, -2, -4], [7, 4, 5], [-2, -4, 1], [3, 2, 4], [6, -1, -5], [6, -7, -3], [1, -3, 7], [6, -5, 2], [4, -7, -1]], 'is_sat': True, 'preferences': 'Mei: Likes muffins. Dislikes waffles, sushi. Hui: Likes wonton, muffins. Dislikes pie. Kim: Likes wonton. Dislikes lobster, doughnut. Dan: Likes lobster. Dislikes muffins, wonton. Lan: Likes doughnut, lobster. Dislikes pie. Bo: Likes pie. Dislikes doughnut, wonton. Joy: Likes wonton, pie, sushi. Qi: Likes doughnut. Dislikes wonton, sushi. Rob: Likes sushi, doughnut. Dislikes waffles. Mia: Dislikes waffles, pie, sushi. Aarav: Likes wonton, pie. Dislikes muffins. Nic: Likes lobster. Dislikes doughnut, muffins. Kavi: Dislikes wonton, muffins, doughnut. Tia: Likes lobster, doughnut, wonton. Eli: Likes sushi. Dislikes muffins, doughnut. Pam: Likes pie, muffins, doughnut. Wei: Likes waffles. Dislikes sushi, wonton. Li: Likes waffles. Dislikes lobster, pie. Mina: Likes sushi, lobster. Dislikes pie. Ping: Likes waffles, muffins. Dislikes wonton. Tom: Likes doughnut. Dislikes lobster, sushi. ', 'menu_items': ['sushi', 'muffins', 'pie', 'doughnut', 'wonton', 'waffles', 'lobster'], 'gpt_out': "Let's tackle this step-by-step by considering each person's preferences and creating a satisfying combination of 'orderable' and 'not_orderable' lists. To begin, we can list all unique food items mentioned:\n\nLikes: muffins, wonton, lobster, doughnut, pie, sushi, waffles\nDislikes: waffles, sushi, pie, lobster, doughnut, wonton, muffins\n\nFirst, we look for contradictions. If a person likes an item that another person dislikes and vice versa, that item cannot be simultaneously orderable and not orderable, and we need to see if there is a workaround or if it's a deal-breaker.\n\n1. Mei dislikes waffles and sushi, but Eli and Mina like sushi, and Wei and Li like waffles. So, sushi and waffles can’t be on the 'orderable' list.\n2. Dan and Nic dislike muffins, but Mei likes muffins and must have it on the 'orderable' list or not on the 'not_orderable' list.\n3. Bo dislikes doughnut, but Lan, Qi, Rob, and Tom like doughnut, so doughnut can't be on the 'not_orderable' list.\n4. Bo, Mei, and Mia dislike wonton, but Hui, Kim, Joy, Aarav, Tia, and Pam like wonton, so wonton can't be on the 'not_orderable' list.\n5. Dan and Li dislike wonton, but it's liked by many others; however, since Dan likes lobster and Li likes waffles, and both waffles and lobster are liked by at least one other person, we might be able to leave wonton off the lists to satisfy them.\n6. Dan likes lobster, and Lan, Nic, Mina, and Tia also like lobster, while Li dislikes it. But since Li likes waffles, which is not generally disliked (and since we've left sushi out due to others' preferences), we can keep lobster on the 'orderable' list.\n7. Kavi dislikes doughnut, muffins, and wonton, but since there are people who like these items and dislike others, we might not be able to put these on the 'not_orderable' list to keep Kavi satisfied.\n\nWe can resolve some of these contradictions by avoiding listing items on either list if not necessary. The challenge is to satisfy everyone without putting the disputed items on any list if possible.\n\nLet’s now look for items that are collectively disliked by most, if not all:\n- Pie is disliked by Hui, Lan, Kavi, and Mina but liked by Bo, Joy, Aarav, Pam, and Li. We can’t put pie on either list as it’s liked and disliked by too many.\n- Waffles are disliked by Mei, Mia, and Wei but liked by Wei, Li, and Ping. Wei's only like is waffles, so waffles can't be on the 'not_orderable' list.\n\nNow, let's try to create lists satisfying everyone using the remaining items:\n- Muffins are liked by Mei, Hui, and Ping. It is disliked by Dan, Nic, and Kavi. Since Dan is satisfied with lobster, and Nic can also be satisfied with lobster, we can ignore Kavi and place muffins on the 'orderable' list to satisfy Mei, Hui, and Ping.\n- Wonton is liked by Hui, Kim, Joy, Aarav, and Tia and disliked only by Kavi and Bo, but since it was earlier concluded that we can keep wonton off the lists to satisfy Dan, we will not include it on any list.\n- Lobster is liked by Dan, Lan, Nic, Mina, and Tia. Li dislikes lobster but can be satisfied with waffles. Therefore, lobster should be on the 'orderable' list.\n- Doughnut is liked by Lan, Qi, Rob, Tom, and Tia but disliked by Bo and Kavi. We need to keep doughnut off the 'not_orderable' list, but since nobody else dislikes doughnut, we can place it on the 'orderable' list.\n\nNow, let’s summarize:\n\n'orderable': lobster, muffins, doughnut\n'not_orderable': sushi, waffles\n\nNote that some of the disliked items like waffles and sushi are not placed on the 'orderable' list, but since the people who like waffles (Wei and Li) don't dislike any of the 'orderable' options, they are still satisfied. Similarly, those who dislike the 'orderable' options (e.g., Kavi) do not have the items they like on the 'not_orderable' list, so they can also be considered satisfied. All other individuals find at least one of the items they like on the 'orderable' list.\n\nLet's double-check to ensure every person is satisfied with at least one aspect:\nMei (likes muffins, included in 'orderable')\nHui (likes muffins, included in 'orderable')\nKim (likes wonton, not included, but dislikes not on 'orderable')\nDan (likes lobster, included in 'orderable')\nLan (likes doughnut, included in 'orderable')\nBo (likes pie, not included, dislikes doughnut, not on 'orderable')\nJoy (likes pie, not included, dislikes not on 'orderable')\nQi (likes doughnut, included in 'orderable')\nRob (likes doughnut, included in 'orderable')\nMia (dislikes sushi, included in 'not_orderable')\nAarav (likes pie, not included, dislikes not on 'orderable')\nNic (likes lobster, included in 'orderable')\nKavi (is the challenging one, dislikes all 'orderable', but none of their likes are on 'not_orderable', thus unsatisfied with the current combination)\nTia (likes lobster and doughnut, included in 'orderable')\nEli (likes sushi, not included, dislikes not on 'orderable')\nPam (likes muffins, included in 'orderable')\nWei (likes waffles, not included, dislikes not on 'orderable')\nLi (likes waffles, not included, dislikes not on 'orderable')\nMina (likes lobster, included in 'orderable')\nPing (likes muffins, included in 'orderable')\nTom (likes doughnut, included in 'orderable')\n\nUnfortunately, Kavi is left unsatisfied since all their disliked items are in the 'orderable' list and there's no workaround to avoid it. Since all participants must be satisfied by the combination of the two lists, we cannot come up with a solution to satisfy everyone's likes and dislikes.\n\nThus, the output is:\n\n```python\norderable=[], not_orderable=[]\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 459, 'num_completion_tokens': 1408}
Data Sample: {'num_vars': 7, 'num_clauses': 14, 'formula': [[7, 1, -5], [7, 3, 2], [-7, -1, -3], [5, 6, -2], [-1, -2, -4], [4, -3, -7], [3, 2, 1], [6, 1, -2], [-3, 1, -4], [-7, 5, -3], [5, -4, 1], [-6, -2, 4], [5, -7, 1], [-4, -3, 1]], 'is_sat': True, 'preferences': 'Naina: Likes korma, bread. Dislikes pizza. Zac: Likes korma, bruschetta, wonton. Pam: Dislikes korma, bread, bruschetta. Jon: Likes pizza, custard. Dislikes wonton. Pia: Dislikes bread, wonton, omelette. Kim: Likes omelette. Dislikes bruschetta, korma. Neil: Likes bruschetta, wonton, bread. Bo: Likes custard, bread. Dislikes wonton. Wes: Likes bread. Dislikes bruschetta, omelette. Riya: Likes pizza. Dislikes korma, bruschetta. Lou: Likes pizza, bread. Dislikes omelette. Abe: Likes omelette. Dislikes custard, wonton. Lex: Likes pizza, bread. Dislikes korma. May: Likes bread. Dislikes omelette, bruschetta. ', 'menu_items': ['bread', 'wonton', 'bruschetta', 'omelette', 'pizza', 'custard', 'korma'], 'gpt_out': "Let's analyze this step by step, considering each individual's preferences and eliminating options in an attempt to categorize the food items into 'orderable' and 'not_orderable' lists.\n\nStarting with 'dislikes', as placing a disliked item in 'not_orderable' potentially satisfies an individual without risking dissatisfaction:\n\nPam dislikes korma, bread, bruschetta.\nPia dislikes bread, wonton, omelette.\nKim dislikes bruschetta, korma.\nWes dislikes bruschetta, omelette.\nRiya dislikes korma, bruschetta.\nMay dislikes omelette, bruschetta.\nNeil dislikes nothing explicitly.\nAbe dislikes custard, wonton.\nJon dislikes wonton.\nLou dislikes omelette.\nBo dislikes wonton.\n\nFrom the dislikes, we see several patterns that can lead us towards a solution:\n\n1. Korma is disliked by Pam, Kim, and Riya, none of whom dislike pizza, so we can consider korma for 'not_orderable', as it satisfies multiple people without conflict.\n2. Bruschetta is disliked by Kim, Wes, Riya, and May, and none of them dislikes korma.\n3. Wonton is disliked by Jon, Pia, Bo, and Abe, and none of them dislikes either korma or bruschetta.\n4. Omelette is disliked by Lou, Pia, Wes, and May, and none of them dislikes korma, bruschetta, or wonton.\n5. Bread is only disliked by Pam, who also dislikes korma and bruschetta. Since placing korma and bruschetta in 'not_orderable' satisfies Pam, bread doesn't need to be there.\n6. Custard is disliked only by Abe, and because others like it, we would prefer not to place it in 'not_orderable'.\n\nGiven these observations, placing korma, bruschetta, wonton, and omelette in 'not_orderable' would satisfy all individuals who have expressed specific dislikes for these items without causing any conflicts. \n\nNow let's look at 'likes' to determine how placing items in 'orderable' can satisfy individuals:\n\nNaina likes korma, bread.\nZac likes korma, bruschetta, wonton.\nJon likes pizza, custard.\nPia has no 'likes' that conflict with the proposed 'not_orderable' list.\nNeil likes bruschetta, wonton, bread.\nBo likes custard, bread.\nWes likes bread.\nRiya likes pizza.\nLou likes pizza, bread.\nAbe likes omelette.\nLex likes pizza, bread.\nKim likes omelette.\nMay likes bread.\n\nFrom the likes, we see that bread and pizza can go on the 'orderable' list without causing any conflicts, as these items have not been placed in the 'not_orderable' list. Neil, Bo, Wes, Lou, Lex, and May will be satisfied by having bread in 'orderable', while Riya, Jon, Lex, and Lou will be satisfied by having pizza in 'orderable'. Since neither Abe nor Kim dislikes pizza or bread, they will also be satisfied (Kim with bread, Abe is already satisfied due to custard not being 'not_orderable').\n\nFinal 'orderable' list: [pizza, bread].\nFinal 'not_orderable' list: [korma, bruschetta, wonton, omelette].\n\nNow we check if this satisfies everyone:\n\n- Naina: Satisfied (bread is orderable).\n- Zac: Satisfied (his likes are in 'not_orderable' but not a deal breaker).\n- Pam: Satisfied (korma, bruschetta in 'not_orderable').\n- Jon: Satisfied (pizza is orderable).\n- Pia: Satisfied (dislikes are in 'not_orderable').\n- Kim: Satisfied (omelette is 'not_orderable', bread is orderable).\n- Neil: Satisfied (bread is orderable).\n- Bo: Satisfied (bread is orderable).\n- Wes: Satisfied (bread is orderable).\n- Riya: Satisfied (pizza is orderable).\n- Lou: Satisfied (pizza, bread are orderable).\n- Abe: Satisfied (omelette is 'not_orderable').\n- Lex: Satisfied (pizza, bread are orderable).\n- May: Satisfied (bread is orderable).\n\nAll individuals are satisfied with this arrangement, so the final lists are:\n\n```python\norderable=['pizza', 'bread'], not_orderable=['korma', 'bruschetta', 'wonton', 'omelette']\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 390, 'num_completion_tokens': 966}
Data Sample: {'num_vars': 4, 'num_clauses': 36, 'formula': [[-3, -4, 1], [-1, 4, -2], [1, 4, -2], [3, 2, -1], [4, -1, 2], [-4, -2, -1], [-4, -2, 3], [-2, 3, -4], [-2, 3, -4], [3, -1, 2], [-3, 2, 4], [-4, -3, 2], [-2, -3, 1], [1, -4, -2], [3, 2, 1], [1, 3, -4], [2, 3, 4], [-1, 3, 4], [3, -4, 1], [-3, -1, 2], [-1, 3, 4], [1, 2, -3], [1, -4, 3], [-4, 3, -2], [4, -3, 2], [-4, -1, -2], [2, 4, 3], [2, 3, 4], [-4, -2, 1], [-4, -1, 2], [-1, 2, 4], [2, -4, 1], [-2, -1, 4], [-1, 3, -2], [3, -4, -2], [1, -4, 2]], 'is_sat': False, 'preferences': 'Sal: Likes salad. Dislikes roti, biscuits. Ada: Likes biscuits. Dislikes salad, frittata. Tara: Likes salad, biscuits. Dislikes frittata. Feng: Likes roti, frittata. Dislikes salad. Gia: Likes biscuits, frittata. Dislikes salad. Lin: Dislikes biscuits, frittata, salad. Ian: Likes roti. Dislikes biscuits, frittata. Ty: Likes roti. Dislikes frittata, biscuits. Jay: Likes roti. Dislikes frittata, biscuits. Hu: Likes roti, frittata. Dislikes salad. Val: Likes frittata, biscuits. Dislikes roti. Oli: Likes frittata. Dislikes biscuits, roti. Ben: Likes salad. Dislikes frittata, roti. Kai: Likes salad. Dislikes biscuits, frittata. Sky: Likes roti, frittata, salad. Taj: Likes salad, roti. Dislikes biscuits. Ava: Likes frittata, roti, biscuits. Pat: Likes roti, biscuits. Dislikes salad. Jaya: Likes roti, salad. Dislikes biscuits. Mel: Likes frittata. Dislikes roti, salad. Anil: Likes roti, biscuits. Dislikes salad. Rina: Likes salad, frittata. Dislikes roti. Shan: Likes salad, roti. Dislikes biscuits. Hal: Likes roti. Dislikes biscuits, frittata. Dot: Likes biscuits, frittata. Dislikes roti. Lan: Dislikes biscuits, salad, frittata. Arun: Likes frittata, biscuits, roti. Cy: Likes frittata, roti, biscuits. Ansh: Likes salad. Dislikes biscuits, frittata. Tim: Likes frittata. Dislikes biscuits, salad. Lia: Likes frittata, biscuits. Dislikes salad. Aarav: Likes frittata, salad. Dislikes biscuits. Dan: Likes biscuits. Dislikes frittata, salad. Ray: Likes roti. Dislikes salad, frittata. Sid: Likes roti. Dislikes biscuits, frittata. Ned: Likes salad, frittata. Dislikes biscuits. ', 'menu_items': ['salad', 'frittata', 'roti', 'biscuits'], 'gpt_out': "Let's figure out which foods can be ordered ('orderable') and which cannot ('not_orderable') by satisfying everyone's preferences. We will go through the foods item-by-item and determine which list they could belong to without breaking one of the four satisfaction criteria.\n\nThe foods we have are salad, roti, biscuits, and frittata.\n\n1. **Salad** is liked by several individuals and disliked by some. If we put salad in the 'orderable' list, some people will be dissatisfied because they dislike it and they won’t have any of their disliked food in the 'not_orderable' list. Let's tentatively move salad to the 'not_orderable' list.\n\n2. **Roti** also has a mix of likes and dislikes. However, for all individuals who dislike roti, they dislike biscuits and/or frittata. Since we have no firm decision yet on roti, we will keep roti undecided for now.\n\n3. **Biscuits** are liked by many and disliked by quite a few as well. However, if biscuits go into the 'not_orderable' list, Ada - who only likes biscuits - would be unsatisfied. Therefore, biscuits must go into the 'orderable' list to satisfy Ada's condition.\n\n4. **Frittata** is liked by many individuals, but also disliked by a few. However, putting frittata in either list won't fully satisfy all preferences by itself because for those who dislike it, their other dislikes include either salad, which is already in 'not_orderable', or biscuits, which is in 'orderable'. Frittata remains undecided for now.\n\nNow that we've placed biscuits in 'orderable', let's see how this impacts our decision for salad (already in 'not_orderable'), roti, and frittata:\n\n- **Salad** remains in 'not_orderable', since placing it in 'orderable' would dissatisfy those who dislike it without a disliked item in 'not_orderable'.\n- **Roti** should be in 'orderable', because anyone who dislikes roti also dislikes either biscuits (orderable) or salad (not_orderable), so they will always be satisfied with one of their dislikes being in the correct list.\n- **Frittata** can now go to 'orderable'. With biscuits and roti already in 'orderable', all those who dislike frittata have a disliked food in 'orderable' or 'not_orderable'.\n\nLet's review if every individual is satisfied with this setup:\n- Everyone who likes biscuits, roti, or frittata will find something they like in 'orderable'.\n- Everyone who dislikes salad will be satisfied with salad being 'not_orderable'.\n- No one who dislikes biscuits, roti, or frittata will be left without a disliked item in 'not_orderable', because we've placed salad there, and everyone who dislikes frittata also dislikes either salad or biscuits, and everyone who dislikes roti dislikes either salad or biscuits as well.\n\nThe final lists will be:\n- orderable: biscuits, roti, frittata\n- not_orderable: salad\n\nNow, let's output this solution:\n\n```python\norderable=['biscuits', 'roti', 'frittata'], not_orderable=['salad']\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 671, 'num_completion_tokens': 684}
Data Sample: {'num_vars': 6, 'num_clauses': 36, 'formula': [[-3, 6, 5], [-4, 5, -3], [1, 2, 4], [-3, -2, 6], [2, -4, -6], [2, 5, 1], [-4, 5, -2], [-1, -4, -5], [-6, -5, 4], [4, 2, -1], [1, 6, 3], [6, 2, -1], [1, -4, -2], [-1, -6, -5], [4, 5, -3], [-6, 2, -1], [5, 3, 2], [-4, 6, -2], [-2, 5, -6], [-3, 5, 2], [-3, -4, -5], [-5, -2, 1], [6, -2, 5], [6, -1, 3], [2, 3, 4], [-5, 4, 2], [-1, -2, -6], [-5, -2, -1], [-6, 5, 2], [2, -3, -5], [1, -2, 5], [1, -3, 2], [-2, -5, -6], [1, -2, -6], [1, -6, 4], [2, -3, -5]], 'is_sat': False, 'preferences': 'Diya: Likes guacamole, falafel. Dislikes espresso. Tom: Likes falafel. Dislikes couscous, espresso. Ru: Likes fries, doughnut, couscous. May: Likes guacamole. Dislikes espresso, doughnut. Leo: Likes doughnut. Dislikes couscous, guacamole. Gia: Likes doughnut, falafel, fries. Cy: Likes falafel. Dislikes couscous, doughnut. Ron: Dislikes fries, couscous, falafel. Sky: Likes couscous. Dislikes guacamole, falafel. Jim: Likes couscous, doughnut. Dislikes fries. Ansh: Likes fries, guacamole, espresso. Lei: Likes guacamole, doughnut. Dislikes fries. Yan: Likes fries. Dislikes couscous, doughnut. Rina: Dislikes fries, guacamole, falafel. Fay: Likes couscous, falafel. Dislikes espresso. Sal: Likes doughnut. Dislikes guacamole, fries. Hui: Likes falafel, espresso, doughnut. Feng: Likes guacamole. Dislikes couscous, doughnut. Nic: Likes falafel. Dislikes doughnut, guacamole. Aarav: Likes falafel, doughnut. Dislikes espresso. Sid: Dislikes espresso, couscous, falafel. Lee: Likes fries. Dislikes falafel, doughnut. Abe: Likes guacamole, falafel. Dislikes doughnut. Gen: Likes guacamole, espresso. Dislikes fries. Ned: Likes doughnut, espresso, couscous. Tao: Likes couscous, doughnut. Dislikes falafel. Sia: Dislikes fries, doughnut, guacamole. Min: Dislikes falafel, doughnut, fries. Lia: Likes falafel, doughnut. Dislikes guacamole. Jay: Likes doughnut. Dislikes espresso, falafel. Rob: Likes fries, falafel. Dislikes doughnut. Mel: Likes fries, doughnut. Dislikes espresso. Lan: Dislikes doughnut, falafel, guacamole. Ken: Likes fries. Dislikes doughnut, guacamole. Sue: Likes fries, couscous. Dislikes guacamole. Chen: Likes doughnut. Dislikes espresso, falafel. ', 'menu_items': ['fries', 'doughnut', 'espresso', 'couscous', 'falafel', 'guacamole'], 'gpt_out': "First, let's identify the set of all unique food items mentioned:\n\n- guacamole\n- falafel\n- espresso\n- couscous\n- fries\n- doughnut\n\nNext, let's see if there are any items disliked by everyone who mentioned them. If so, these items can be immediately placed on the 'not_orderable' list, as they would satisfy everyone's dislikes without affecting likes.\n\nUpon scanning the list, it seems espresso is universally disliked by everyone who mentions it. Thus, we can safely add espresso to the 'not_orderable' list.\n\nHowever, upon further inspection, Ansh, Hui, Gen, Ned, and Feng like espresso, so it cannot be put directly in 'not_orderable'.\n\nNow, let's find an item that is liked by at least one person and not disliked by anyone. This item can be placed on the 'orderable' list.\n\nThere is no such item — every food item that someone likes is disliked by another person. Therefore, we have to look for a compromise where we can satisfy the criteria of everyone being either satisfied with having what they like in 'orderable' or what they dislike in 'not_orderable', without any conflict.\n\nWe will build the 'orderable' list by looking for an item that is liked by people who have their dislikes already in the 'not_orderable' list. There are a couple of strategies we could try. Let's start with falafel since it is liked by many.\n\nIf falafel is in 'orderable', it satisfies Diya, Tom, Gia, Cy, Fay, Hui, Aarav, Abe, Lia, and Rob, but Ron, Sky, Sid, Min, and Jay dislike it, that means falafel cannot be in 'not_orderable'. We need to ensure Ron, Sky, Sid, Min, and Jay have something they dislike in 'not_orderable'.\n\nRon dislikes couscous and fries in addition to falafel, thus we have a chance to satisfy Ron with fries or couscous in 'not_orderable'.\nSky dislikes guacamole; we can't use falafel, so guacamole could be in 'not_orderable'.\nSid dislikes couscous and can be satisfied like Ron with it in 'not_orderable'.\nMin dislikes doughnut in addition to falafel, so we could use doughnut to satisfy Min if it's in 'not_orderable'.\nJay dislikes espresso in addition to falafel; thus we need espresso in 'not_orderable'.\n\nIf we manage to have espresso in 'not_orderable', Diya, May, Tom, Fay, Aarav, and many others who dislike espresso will be satisfied.\n\nLet's decide on 'not_orderable'. Since espresso is disliked by people who don't have alternative dislikes, espresso should go into 'not_orderable' to satisfy those who dislike it.\n\nGiven this, people who like espresso must have their likes in 'orderable' instead. Ansh likes guacamole, fries, and espresso, with espresso out, either guacamole or fries needs to be orderable. Gen likes guacamole and Ned likes doughnut and couscous.\n\nNow let's synthesize the information:\n\n- Espresso should be 'not_orderable'.\n- Guacamole satisfies those who like it and dislike espresso.\n- Not everyone who dislikes doughnut can use it to be 'not_orderable' (Ansh, Lan like guacamole, so fry should also be 'orderable').\n\nLet's place items considering no one's likes end up in 'not_orderable' and no one's dislikes in 'orderable':\n\n- 'orderable': guacamole, fries, doughnut (satisfies guacamole and fries likers without offending those who dislikes, also Ned, Leo dislike guacamole; Jim, Ru, May, Lei, Sal, Sue dislikes fries; Ansh, Hui, Tao, Min, Ken, Leo, Mel, Chen, Jay, Lee dislikes doughnut)\n- 'not_orderable': espresso (satisfies everyone who dislikes espresso)\n\nEveryone else's preferences can be met through 'orderable'. Note, though, the complexity of the problem and the tradeoffs necessary to satisfy all participants. \n\nFinal lists:\n\norderable = ['guacamole', 'fries', 'doughnut']\nnot_orderable = ['espresso']", 'num_prompt_tokens': 710, 'num_completion_tokens': 903}
Data Sample: {'num_vars': 5, 'num_clauses': 10, 'formula': [[-5, -4, -2], [-2, 3, 4], [-5, -4, -2], [-1, -3, 2], [1, -3, -2], [5, 1, -2], [-2, -3, -4], [5, 4, -3], [2, 4, -3], [-5, -2, -4]], 'is_sat': True, 'preferences': 'Joe: Dislikes kimchi, lasagna, ravioli. Anil: Likes sorbet, lasagna. Dislikes ravioli. Jaya: Dislikes kimchi, lasagna, ravioli. Amy: Likes ravioli. Dislikes tacos, sorbet. Wei: Likes tacos. Dislikes sorbet, ravioli. Tim: Likes kimchi, tacos. Dislikes ravioli. Kai: Dislikes ravioli, sorbet, lasagna. Ron: Likes kimchi, lasagna. Dislikes sorbet. Zan: Likes ravioli, lasagna. Dislikes sorbet. Kit: Dislikes kimchi, ravioli, lasagna. ', 'menu_items': ['tacos', 'ravioli', 'sorbet', 'lasagna', 'kimchi'], 'gpt_out': "To begin, let's list the likes and dislikes for each person:\n\n- Joe: \n  - Likes: (None stated)\n  - Dislikes: kimchi, lasagna, ravioli\n- Anil: \n  - Likes: sorbet, lasagna\n  - Dislikes: ravioli\n- Jaya: \n  - Likes: (None stated)\n  - Dislikes: kimchi, lasagna, ravioli\n- Amy: \n  - Likes: ravioli\n  - Dislikes: tacos, sorbet\n- Wei: \n  - Likes: tacos\n  - Dislikes: sorbet, ravioli\n- Tim: \n  - Likes: kimchi, tacos\n  - Dislikes: ravioli\n- Kai: \n  - Likes: (None stated)\n  - Dislikes: ravioli, sorbet, lasagna\n- Ron: \n  - Likes: kimchi, lasagna\n  - Dislikes: sorbet\n- Zan: \n  - Likes: ravioli, lasagna\n  - Dislikes: sorbet\n- Kit: \n  - Likes: (None stated)\n  - Dislikes: kimchi, ravioli, lasagna\n\nSince ravioli is disliked by everyone who mentioned it and is only liked by Amy and Zan, it is a contentious item. If ravioli is on the 'orderable' list, Amy and Zan will be satisfied. Still, Joe, Anil, Jaya, Wei, Tim, and Kai will not be, as they all dislike it. Hence, ravioli must be on the 'not_orderable' list to satisfy the majority.\n\nLet's put ravioli in 'not_orderable':\n\n- not_orderable: ravioli\n\nAmy and Zan need an item on the 'orderable' list to be satisfied since their liked item (ravioli) is on the 'not_orderable' list. Zan likes lasagna, but it's disliked by several people, including Joe, Jaya, Kai, and Kit. So, lasagna cannot be on the 'orderable' list. Thus, we must find another solution for Zan. \n\nSorbet is liked by Anil, but disliked by Amy, Wei, Kai, Ron, and Zan, making it also a non-viable option for the 'orderable' list.\n\nKimchi is liked by Tim, but disliked by Joe, Jaya, and Kit. It cannot be on the 'orderable' list.\n\nTacos are liked by Wei and Tim but disliked by Amy. Tacos might be a viable option for the 'orderable' list since it's only disliked by one person.\n\nLet's try adding tacos to the 'orderable' list and see if that works for everyone:\n\n- orderable: tacos\n\nNow, let's check each person's satisfaction:\n\n- Joe: Dislikes kimchi, lasagna, ravioli - Satisfied (ravioli not orderable)\n- Anil: Likes sorbet, lasagna; Dislikes ravioli - Satisfied (ravioli not orderable)\n- Jaya: Dislikes kimchi, lasagna, ravioli - Satisfied (ravioli not orderable)\n- Amy: Likes ravioli; Dislikes tacos, sorbet - Not satisfied yet (tacos on orderable, but none of liked items)\n- Wei: Likes tacos; Dislikes sorbet, ravioli - Satisfied (likes tacos on orderable)\n- Tim: Likes kimchi, tacos; Dislikes ravioli - Satisfied (likes tacos on orderable)\n- Kai: Dislikes ravioli, sorbet, lasagna - Satisfied (ravioli not orderable)\n- Ron: Likes kimchi, lasagna; Dislikes sorbet - Satisfied (dislikes on not orderable or likes not on orderable)\n- Zan: Likes ravioli, lasagna; Dislikes sorbet - Not satisfied yet (ravioli not orderable, lasagna not mentioned)\n- Kit: Dislikes kimchi, ravioli, lasagna - Satisfied (ravioli not orderable)\n\nTo satisfy Amy and Zan without conflicting with the dislikes of others, we have to find at least one food item that they both like and that is not disliked by any other person. Unfortunately, their only common liked item is ravioli, which has already been placed in 'not_orderable'.\n\nGiven this impossibility, there are no items left that can be added to 'orderable' to satisfy both Amy and Zan without also including the dislikes of others (since they dislike each other's other liked foods), and thus we have to conclude that it's not possible to satisfy all participants with the given constraints.\n\nHence, the solution is that there is no combination that satisfies everyone, and we output empty lists for both 'orderable' and 'not_orderable':\n\n```python\norderable=[], not_orderable=[]\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 337, 'num_completion_tokens': 1040}
