e drawn from this alone.\n\nConsidering all the preferences, we try to satisfy as many people as we can while still respecting dislikes. Let's consider the following scenario: \n\n- If we put caramel in 'orderable', Rina, Gus, Ping, Kit, Meg, Lex, Chen, and Jay will be satisfied because they all like caramel.\n- Fay, Tia, Jun, Xiu, Diya, Rob, Ken, Isha, Liv, Dot, Om, and Taj will be satisfied by putting caramel in 'orderable' because they don't have soup or shrimps to account for their satisfaction.\n- Naina, Tia, Ken, Taj, Liv, Dot, and Rob would not be satisfied by that because they dislike caramel.\n\nTo satisfy Gus, Ping, Kit, Meg, Lex, Chen, and Jay, we have only one choice, and it is to put caramel into the 'orderable' list. Sue will be satisfied if we put either caramel or shrimps on the 'orderable' list. If we put caramel into the 'orderable' list, the dislikes of Fay, Tia, Jun, Xiu, Diya, Rob, Ken, Isha, Liv, Dot, Om, and Taj for caramel do not impact since they have soup to be satisfied when caramel is put in the 'orderable' list.\n\nWe almost got the decision done. Now, consider shrimps and soup:\n- If shrimps are on the 'not_orderable' list, Rina, Fay, Jun, Xiu, Diya, Rob, (Liv, Dot satisfied by caramel), Isha, Kit, Meg, Taj (satisfied by soup), Lex, Om, and Sue (satisfied by caramel) will be satisfied.\n- If soup is on the 'not_orderable' list, Gus (satisfied by caramel), Ping (satisfied by caramel), Zan, Tia, Ken (satisfied by caramel), Naina, Liv (satisfied by caramel), Dot (satisfied by caramel), Rob (satisfied by caramel), Jun (satisfied by caramel), Meg (satisfied by caramel), Chen (satisfied by caramel), Jay (satisfied by caramel), and Diya (satisfied by caramel) will be satisfied.\n\nHere, we can put caramel on the 'orderable' list and shrimps on the 'not_orderable' list. This will satisfy all conditions for all individuals. No individual’s like and dislike conflict with this combination.\n\nTherefore, the final solution is:\n```python\norderable