follow these steps:\n\n1. Tally likes and dislikes for each food item.\n2. Identify any items that are unanimously disliked, which can go directly into the not_orderable list.\n3. Identify any items that are unanimously liked, which can go directly into the orderable list.\n4. Consider items with mixed feedback to find a compromise that satisfies everyone.\n\nLet's start by tallying the preferences for each of the food items:\n\n### Likes:\n- Spaghetti: Val, Jon, Max, Jaya, Chen, Mel, Jay, Gen\n- Burrito: Val, Zan, Jon, Max, Isha, Lan, Zara, Rina, Eva, Hu, Bao, Jay\n- Vegetables: Jon, Raj, Sid, Jaya, Lan, Min, Chen, Rina, Sue, Gen\n- Churros: Zan, Raj, Zara, Om, Rina, Zoe, Yan, Mel, Bao, Bea\n\n### Dislikes:\n- Spaghetti: Zan, Raj, Sid, Lan, Zara, Pat, Kavi, Leo, Lin, Yan, Min\n- Burrito: Raj, Jaya, Pat, Zoe, Bea, Lin\n- Vegetables: Isha, Om, Pat, Kavi, Eva, Hu, Leo, Lin\n- Churros: Val, Max, Sid, Isha, Pat, Sue, Jay, Kavi, Eva\n\nNow, let's apply the satisfaction criteria to the tallies:\n\n1. **Unanimously disliked:** There seems to be no food item that is disliked by everyone, so nothing goes into the not_orderable list from this criterion.\n\n2. **Unanimously liked:** Similarly, there is no food item that is liked by everyone, so nothing goes into the orderable list from this criterion.\n\nNext, looking at the likes and dislikes, we have to ensure each person has something they like or dislike:\n\nSince there is no single food item that satisfies the whole group, we need to find a compromise:\n\n- Although spaghetti is liked by several people, it is also disliked by several others. Including spaghetti in the orderable list and not including it in the not_orderable list could satisfy Val, Jon, Max, Jaya, Chen, Mel, Jay, and Gen but would dissatisfy Zan, Raj, Sid, Lan, Zara, Pat, Kavi, Leo, Lin, Yan, and Min. Spaghetti will remain a maybe until we consider more items.\n- Burrito is liked by the most people and is only disliked by six individuals. If burrito is in 