see:\n- Ratatouille is disliked by Om, Rex, Meg, Lei, and Jun, but liked by Ned, Amy, and Eva.\n- Curry is disliked by Om, Riya, and Jun, but liked by Meg, Amy, and Eva.\n- Sorbet is disliked by Meg, Amy, Lei, Shan, Sia, and Jun, but liked by Riya and Rex.\n- Guacamole is disliked by Ned, but liked by Anil, Lei, Shan, and Sia.\n- Ravioli is disliked by Jun but liked by Anil, Ned, Riya, Shan, and Sia.\n- Spaghetti is disliked by Eva, but liked by Anil, Om, and Rex.\n\nFrom this analysis:\n- Spaghetti can be in the 'orderable' list, satisfying Anil, Om, and Rex, as it's only disliked by Eva who can be satisfied with Curry or Ratatouille.\n- Sorbet is the only item disliked by many (6 people) and liked by only 2 (Riya and Rex), who have other likes that can be put in the 'orderable' list (ravioli and spaghetti, respectively). This makes sorbet a good candidate for the 'not_orderable' list.\n\nNow, let's create a draft of the lists based on the above analysis:\n\nDraft orderable list: [spaghetti]\nDraft not_orderable list: [sorbet]\n\nIf we follow our draft, we satisfy the following people:\n- Anil (likes spaghetti)\n- Om (likes spaghetti, dislikes sorbet)\n- Rex (likes spaghetti, dislikes sorbet)\n- Meg (dislikes sorbet)\n- Amy (dislikes sorbet)\n- Lei (dislikes sorbet)\n- Shan (dislikes sorbet)\n- Sia (dislikes sorbet)\n- Jun (dislikes sorbet)\n\nNow we need to make sure the remaining individuals (Ned, Riya, Eva) can be satisfied:\n- Ned is satisfied with ravioli (likes it and nobody dislikes it)\n- Riya can be satisfied with ravioli too\n- Eva can be satisfied with either curry or ratatouille\n\nLooking at the remaining likes:\n- Curry is liked by Eva and Meg, but disliked by Jun, Om, and Riya. However, all three have other items to be satisfied. Hence, curry can be put in the 'orderable' list.\n- Ratatouille cannot be in the 'orderable' list since it's liked and disliked by too many, and those who like it have no alternative in the 'likes'. Therefore, it must be in 