ikes of several people, it cannot be put directly into the \'not_orderable\' list without further consideration since doing so would make those who like it not satisfied.\n\nStep 2: Common Likes\nNext, we examine commonly liked items which could be placed on the \'orderable\' list as long as they\'re not disliked by someone else.\n\n- Likes naan: May, Tara, Eva, Nic, Ray, Meg, Lee\n- Likes cheesecake: Tara, Rina, Hui, Meg, Ping, Isha\n- Likes pancakes: Nic, Ray, Hu, Lin, Ava, Ping, Hui, Isha\n- Likes espresso: Stated above.\n\nStep 3: Attempt to Satisfy Everyone\nTo satisfy everyone:\n- At least one item from the "likes" of a person should be on the \'orderable\' list, or\n- At least one item from the "dislikes" should be on the \'not_orderable\' list.\n\nConsidering the combinations:\n- Lin and Lee have opposing likes and dislikes for "naan" and "pancakes," but they both dislike "cheesecake."\n- Joy dislikes "cheesecake" and "pancakes," but likes "espresso."\n- Ava and Hu, like "espresso" and "pancakes," but dislike "cheesecake."\n\nBased on these considerations, we can satisfy the likes of May, Tara, Eva, Nic, Ray, Meg, and Lee by placing "naan" on the \'orderable\' list while leaving out those who dislike it. The rest can be satisfied if "cheesecake" is on the \'not_orderable\' list because they all dislike it, even if it\'s someone else\'s like.\n\nStep 4: Finalizing the Lists\nPlace "naan" in the \'orderable\' list to satisfy those who like it.\nPlace "cheesecake" in the \'not_orderable\' list to satisfy those who dislike it.\nBy doing this, everyone who likes "espresso" or "pancakes" will also be satisfied because their dislikes are covered by having "cheesecake" on the \'not_orderable\' list.\n\nFinal Solution:\n- orderable: ["naan"]\n- not_orderable: ["cheesecake"]\n\nWe\'ll output the solution in Python code format:\n\n```python\norderable=["naan"], not_orderable=["cheesecake"]\n```', 'num_prompt_tokens': 386, 'num_completion_tokens': 699}
