ch person, to identify patterns that might help us create the two lists:\n\n- The disliked items are: roti, paneer, and lobster.\n- If we satisfy anyone who dislikes an item by placing it in the 'not_orderable' list, we will dissatisfy others who like that item.\n- We must find at least one item that is liked by some and not disliked by others to place in the 'orderable' list.\n\nAnalyzing the likes: \n- Lei, Hu, Dan, Arun, Sid, Ada, Isha, Ned, Kim, Diya like lobster but it is disliked by many others.\n- Rohan, Rex, Rob, Arun, Zara, May, Tia, Bao, Jaya, Diya like roti but it is also disliked by many others.\n- Paner is liked by Lei, Rob, Bo, Hu, Lan, Isha, Ned, Cal, Riya, Kit, Kim, Eva, Tia, May, Vic, Diya but it as well is disliked by many.\n\nGiven the preferences, there seems to be no single food item that is liked by some and not disliked by others. This implies that we cannot satisfy all participants by placing any one food item in the 'orderable' list without having at least one person dissatisfied.\n\nExamining Wes, Ying, Tara, Sky, Vic, Liz, who all dislike paneer, roti, and lobster. They will not be satisfied unless all three items are on the 'not_orderable' list. However, this would dissatisfy everyone who likes at least one of these food items.\n\nSince no such combination exists that satisfies all of the group members, we must return empty lists for both 'orderable' and 'not_orderable.'\n\nTherefore, the solution is:\n\n```python\norderable = []\nnot_orderable = []\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 623, 'num_completion_tokens': 391}
