le' lists following the rules. As we go, we will keep track of which person has been satisfied by either liking something that is 'orderable' or disliking something that is 'not_orderable'.\n\nEvery person with unique disliked food items becomes a pivoting decision for the solution. Anyone who dislikes all three items (for instance, Tim, Liv, Oli, and Jaya), cannot be directly satisfied by their dislikes because placing all three items into the 'not_orderable' list is impossible. Instead, they must be indirectly satisfied by including at least one of the dishes they do not dislike on the 'orderable' list, which for these persons are null. This means we cannot include their opinions in the decisive process as it would lead to a contradiction (since they dislike all food items, adding any to 'not_orderable' would satisfy them, but upset everyone else who likes that same dish).\n\nNow let's list out the likes and dislikes of everyone else, and see if we can satisfy the group.\n\nLikes: \n- Korma: Yue, Hu, Leo, Zoe, Yan, Gen, Ty, Lei, Dan, Mia, Sia, Nic, Gus\n- Spaghetti: Hu, Ben, Joe, Lei, Mia, Mel, Sia, Cal, Dan\n- Nachos: Du, Zara, Hu, Xan, Zan, Nic, Gus, Sam, Vic, Ana, Ron, Ken\n\nDislikes: \n- Korma: Du, Tim, Liv, Oli, Zara, Joe, Jaya, Cal, Vic, Ana, Sam, Ken, Ron\n- Spaghetti: Yue, Du, Tim, Liv, Oli, Zara, Gus, Xan, Nic, Vic, Ana, Ken, Yan, Ron, Ty, Gen, Leo, Zoe\n- Nachos: Yue, Du, Tim, Liv, Oli, Ben, Joe, Mia, Dan, Lei, Mel, Cal, Jaya, Sia, Gen, Yan, Ty, Zoe\n\nFrom the likes and dislikes, we see that:\n1. Korma is liked by 13 people and disliked by 13 people.\n2. Spaghetti is liked by 9 people and disliked by 19 people.\n3. Nachos is liked by 12 people and disliked by 18 people.\n\nThe most disliked item is spaghetti, and the least disliked is korma. To satisfy the highest number of people, we should consider putting spaghetti on the 'not_orderable' list. This will satisfy 19 people who dislike spaghetti, if there is no contradiction later. We must then satisfy the people who like spaghetti by another mean. These people are Ben, Joe, Lei, Mia, Mel, Sia, Cal, Dan, and Ana.\n\nOn the other hand, Korma is liked by almost as many people as those who dislike it. There is no clear decision on korma, so let's consider nachos next.\n\nNachos are liked by 12 people and disliked by 18 people. However, unlike spaghetti, which is disliked by everyone who dislikes korma, nachos are liked by some people who also like korma. This makes a case for including nachos on the 'orderable' list, as it does not conflict with the people who like korma; this would satisfy 12 people.\n\nAfter putting spaghetti on the 'not_orderable' list and nachos on the 'orderable' list, we can analyze the situation:\n- Everyone who dislikes spaghetti is now satisfied because it is on the 'not_orderable' list.\n- Everyone who likes nachos is now satisfied because nachos are on the 'orderable' list.\n\nThis leads us to a scenario where:\n\n- Korma can be put on the 'orderable' list, and it won't affect anyone who is already satisfied by nachos being orderable.\n- For individuals who like either spaghetti or korma and are not satisfied by nachos, placing korma on the 'orderable' list would satisfy them.\n- The people who are satisfied by disliking spaghetti no longer impact the decision on the other food items.\n\nGiven this arrangement and considering no direct conflicts, the final lists would be:\n\norderable: nachos, korma\nnot_orderable: spaghetti\n\nNow let's output the lists in the required Python code format:\n\n```python\norderable=['nachos', 'korma'], not_orderable=['spaghetti']\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 609, 'num_completion_tokens': 957}
