he \'orderable\' list first and \'pho\' and \'burrito\' on the \'not_orderable\' list to check for conflicts:\n\n- \'cake\' in \'orderable\' satisfies: Wei, May, Ivy, Ned, Hu, Tara, Val, Ben, Riya, Om, Kavi, Zara, Jaya, Ray, Gen, Yan, Pam, Neil, Ken, Rohan\n- \'lobster\' in \'orderable\' satisfies: Lan, Dan, Tao, Yue, Lin, Kai, Aarav, Ula, Amy, Ada, Mina, Raj, Mel\n- \'pho\' in \'not_orderable\' satisfies: Wei, Sid, Rob, Sue, Ava, Geet, Liz, Mia, Sam, Zac, Shan, Jay, Lia\n- \'burrito\' in \'not_orderable\' satisfies: Zan, Lei, Hal, Bea, Veer, Jan\n\nWe must check that adding \'cake\' and \'lobster\' to \'orderable\', and \'pho\' and \'burrito\' to \'not_orderable\' does not cause any individual to be dissatisfied. From the preferences provided, no conflicts arise with these selections. All individuals either have a like in \'orderable\' or a dislike in \'not_orderable\'. This means that we\'ve found a suitable combination.\n\nTherefore, the final output is:\n\norderable = [\'cake\', \'lobster\']\nnot_orderable = [\'pho\', \'burrito\']', 'num_prompt_tokens': 1191, 'num_completion_tokens': 1121}
