e are both likes and dislikes across the list of preferences.\n\nTo decide what goes into the \'orderable\' list, we must ensure at least one of every person\'s likes is there, or one of their dislikes is in \'not_orderable\'. Let\'s make the following observations:\n\n1. "ramen" is the most commonly disliked item, but several people like it. However, we can likely put "ramen" in the \'not_orderable\' list because those who like it have other likes that may be orderable. Placing "ramen" in \'not_orderable\' satisfies many people who have it as a dislike.\n   \n   Before finalizing this decision, we should ensure that everyone liking "ramen" also has another food they like not in conflict with someone else\'s dislikes. For instance, Lan likes "kebab" as well, and since there is no apparent conflict (i.e., a person who only dislikes "kebab"), this is a supporting argument for including "ramen" in \'not_orderable\'.\n\n   Performing this check for every individual who likes "ramen", we find that they all have at least one other like not in conflict with other dislikes, confirming we can move "ramen" to \'not_orderable\'.\n\n2. We\'ll now repeat this process for other universally disliked food items.\n\n   "macaron" is also widely disliked. People who like "macaron" have other likes which are not universally disliked and hence could be placed in \'orderable\'. This suggests "macaron" could also be placed in \'not_orderable\'.\n\nAt this point, we have "ramen" and "macaron" in \'not_orderable\', satisfying a large number of people.\n\nNow we must deal with the other food items to see if we can satisfy everyone. Since "ramen" and "macaron" are in \'not_orderable\', let\'s look at other dislikes, considering what is liked by those who dislike each item, and ensuring no conflict interferes with their satisfaction.\n\nIt seems that there is no single combination of items that can satisfy everyone\'s preferences due to conflicting likes and dislikes. The problem is that for virtually every food item, there is at least one person who likes it and one person who dislikes it. \n\nTherefore, there is no combination of \'orderable\' and \'not_orderable\' lists that can simultaneously satisfy all participants according to the given criteria. The final solution would be empty lists for both:\n\n```python\norderable = [], not_orderable = []\n```', 'num_prompt_tokens': 1130, 'num_completion_tokens': 936}
