ral but also disliked by several.\n- "caramel" is often liked or disliked, creating a dividing issue.\n- "gelato" and "fries" show a similar conflict as above.\n\nBecause of the conflicts, there is no clear item to start with. We need to consider one person at a time due to the lack of a unanimous decision and proceed iteratively to resolve the satisfaction criteria. \n\nWe will start by considering the items that appear most frequently in dislikes and likes. If an item is disliked by many, it can be a good candidate for \'not_orderable\', and if it\'s liked by many, it can be a candidate for \'orderable\', as long as including it doesn\'t conflict with other people\'s preferences.\n\nLet\'s consider "lasagna" because it appears many times in dislikes. Including "lasagna" in the \'not_orderable\' list will satisfy those who dislike it. But we must also ensure that people who like lasagna have something else they like in the \'orderable\' list.\n\nStarting with "lasagna" in \'not_orderable\':\n\n\'not_orderable\'=["lasagna"]\n\nNow, we must satisfy people who like lasagna by including something else in \'orderable\'. "roti", "wonton", "fries", "naan", and "gelato" are liked by some of those who like lasagna. We cannot include "naan" in \'orderable\' because it is disliked by others not satisfied by \'lasagna\' being \'not_orderable\'. A similar issue arises with "fries" and "gelato".\n\nHowever, looking at those who like "roti", none of them have it as a disliked item. We can then add "roti" to \'orderable\':\n\n\'orderable\'=["roti"]\n\nSo far, we have:\n\n\'orderable\'=["roti"] \'not_orderable\'=["lasagna"]\n\nNext, we must check that all other people are satisfied with these choices, item by item. This will help us identify the items that must be in the \'orderable\' or \'not_orderable\' lists to satisfy everyone.\n\nLet\'s have a look at "doughnut":\n- Zac, Mia, Jan, Taj, Hal, Jaya (dislike "doughnut") can be satisfied with having "doughnut" in \'not_orderable\' since none of their likes are contradicted.\n- Ian, Geet, Nita, Shan, Dot, Sky, Xan (like "doughnut") can be satisfied with "roti" already in \'orderable\'.\n\nIf we add "doughnut" to \'not_orderable\' then:\n\n\'orderable\'=["roti"] \'not_orderable\'=["lasagna", "doughnut"]\n\nWe will proceed systematically with each remaining food item, checking that every person\'s satisfaction criteria are met.\n\nLet\'s examine the remaining items one by one:\n\n- "caramel": Adding "caramel" to \'not_orderable\' satisfies Zac, Mel, Cy, Lia, and Zoe, who dislike "caramel". Those who like "caramel" are satisfied with "roti" in \'orderable\'.\n- "sushi": Adding "sushi" to \'not_orderable\' is not possible as it is liked by many and disliked by some, and adding it to \'orderable\' could also result in dissatisfaction because it\'s also highly disliked. It remains unassigned.\n- "gelato" is liked by several and has a strong dislike, but Anil, who dislikes caramel, has gelato as a liked item, so gelato will have to be \'orderable\'.\n- "wonton": It is liked by many, with fewer dislikers, and those who like it would not be satisfied with just "roti" as it was disliked by some who liked "lasagna". So, we put off assigning it for the moment.\n- "fries": It\'s liked by some but those who dislike "lasagna" don\'t like "fries", so it is still complicated to decide.\n- "naan": Many people dislike "naan", so it can\'t be in \'orderable\'. It must be in \'not_orderable\', but we have to satisfy those who like it separately.\n\nConsidering the rest of the items and our rules, our lists could look like this:\n\n\'orderable\'=["roti", "gelato"]\n\'not_orderable\'=["lasagna", "doughnut", "naan", "caramel"]\n\nNow, this is a proposed solution, but we have to confirm that all participants are satisfied:\n\n- Anyone who likes "lasagna", "doughnut", "naan", or "caramel" is satisfied by "roti" or "gelato"\n- Anyone who dislikes "lasagna", "doughnut", "naan", or "caramel" is satisfied by its exclusion 