aya: Likes fries, vegetables. Dislikes falafel. Oli: Likes vegetables, fries. Dislikes spaghetti. Chen: Likes churros, fries. Dislikes peach. Gus: Likes churros, pizza, salmon. Gen: Likes pizza. Dislikes vegetables, peach. Amy: Likes peach, salmon, pizza. Kai: Likes spaghetti, pizza. Dislikes peach. Ula: Likes vegetables, peach. Dislikes salmon. Tao: Likes churros. Dislikes wonton, pizza. Jim: Dislikes fries, falafel, spaghetti. Mina: Likes churros, wonton. Dislikes salmon. Wei: Likes falafel, peach. Dislikes churros. Bao: Likes vegetables, salmon. Dislikes pizza. Mel: Likes vegetables. Dislikes peach, spaghetti. Hal: Likes fries, peach. Dislikes salmon. ', 'menu_items': ['falafel', 'pizza', 'churros', 'salmon', 'fries', 'spaghetti', 'wonton', 'peach', 'vegetables'], 'gpt_out': "First, we will look for any direct conflicts where a disliked item by one person is liked by another. In such cases, we would have no way to satisfy both individuals with our two lists.\n\nNext, we should see if there is any item that is universally disliked or liked by everyone who has an opinion on them. Such items can initially define our 'orderable' and 'not_orderable' lists, making it easier to satisfy participants. \n\nFinally, we will look for items that can satisfy the most number of people, being careful that adding an item to one list doesn't cause dissatisfaction due to conflicting preferences.\n\nLet's begin by examining the likes and dislikes of each individual:\n\n- Churros are disliked by Lin, Dan, Ada, Zoe, Wei, Tao and liked by Lee, Meg, Tara, Eva, May, Chen, Tao, Mina.\n- Fries are disliked by Lee, Lin, Ned, Veer, Sam, Raj, Geet, Jim, Zoe and liked by Lin, Veer, Zoe, Chen, Oli, Jaya, Hal.\n- Wonton is disliked by Lin, Sid, Dan, Liz, Val, Pat and liked by Lee, Lei, Tara, Chao, Neil, Ned, Rina, Sal, Mina.\n- Pizza is disliked by Lei, Lia, Zoe, Bao, Tao, Gen and liked by Lei, Meg, Lan, Liz, Ned, Rina, Raj, Gus, Gen, Kai, Amy.\n- Falafel is disliked by Ivy, Sid, Lia, Sam, Ping,