iked by many, including Lia who only likes vindaloo and burger, so burger must be in 'orderable')\n  - vindaloo (liked by many, disliked by many)\n  - cheese (liked by many, disliked by several)\n  - hummus (liked by many, disliked by several including Qi who only dislikes foods)\n\nGiven these observations and the fact that we can't satisfy everyone's dislikes, we will prioritize satisfying likes since some people only have dislikes.\n\nSince burger is the only liked item by Lia and disliked by many, it must go in the 'orderable' list. Since burger is on the 'orderable' list, anyone who dislikes burger will already be satisfied, so we don't need to worry about their dislikes anymore.\n\nWe must then ensure that everyone else has at least one liked item on the 'orderable' list while avoiding placing any other disliked items on it. For people who both like and dislike the same item, we cannot place either on the lists, as they could not be satisfied.\n\nLet's start by placing burger on the 'orderable' list because Lia can only be satisfied by it:\n- orderable: burger\n- not_orderable: []\n\nNow we look for all people who are unsatisfied because they don't like burgers and don't have a disliked item on the 'not_orderable' list. For these people, we can potentially satisfy them with their likes because their dislikes are not relevant anymore unless they only dislike items, in which case we must not order an item they dislike.\n\nIf we look at Neil, who dislikes vindaloo, burger, and cheese, since we can't satisfy his dislikes (burger is already ordered, and cheese is liked by many), we focus on others' likes. For Zum, Meg, Du, Ben, Sue and Bea liking hummus, and nobody dislikes it enough for it to be their only disliked item (hummus can't be ordered for Qi, Ying since they only have dislikes), therefore we can order hummus.\n\n- orderable: burger, hummus\n- not_orderable: []\n\nNext, let's look at likes and dislikes of other people:\n\n- People who exclusively like nachos have their dislikes already satisfied (Qi and Fay).\n- People who exclusively like burrito have their dislikes already satisfied.\n- People who exclusively like doughnut have their dislikes already satisfied (Ava and Lee dislikes burrito; Leo dislikes vindaloo; Ned dislikes guacamole; Zac dislikes hummus and burrito). Therefore, we can add doughnut to the 'orderable' list.\n- Zoe likes nachos and vindaloo, but we want to avoid vindaloo because it is disliked often and likes nachos which have not created conflicts so far, so we add nachos.\n- Cheese has not been disliked by people who only have dislikes (Geet, Rina, Mei), so we can add cheese to the 'orderable' list.\n- Roti is only disliked by people who have some likes, so we can add roti.\n\nNow we can construct the final lists:\n\n- orderable: burger, hummus, doughnut, nachos, cheese, roti\n- not_orderable: []\n\nLet's confirm that this satisfies everyone's conditions:\n\n- Everyone who disliked burger is satisfied because it's in the 'orderable' list (e.g., Jun, Bea, Jim, Ping, Lin, Ray, Neil, Rina, Taj, Vic, Arun, Meg, Qi, Sky, May, Jaya, Mel, Ying, Ru).\n- Everyone else has at least one liked food in the orderable list (e.g., Zan, Veer, Eli, Lou, Anil, Mina, Naina, Ned, Mei, Arun, Geet, Sid, Zac, Chao, Lee, Wes, Liz, Lia, Cy, Ben, Rob, Sue, Fay, Lia, Ru).\n\nEvery person is satisfied by this configuration. We can now write the output in the required format:\n\n```python\norderable=['burger', 'hummus', 'doughnut', 'nachos', 'cheese', 'roti'], not_orderable=[]\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 932, 'num_completion_tokens': 1026}
