s its dislikes, hence cannot be 'orderable'.\n- `Salad` is liked by some but disliked by others, hence cannot be 'orderable'.\n- `Pho` is also both liked and disliked, hence cannot be 'orderable'.\n\nAnalyzing the Dislikes:\n- `Tiramisu` is widely disliked; it can't be ordered unless there is no one liking it, but since some do, we cannot place it in 'not_orderable'.\n- `Couscous`, `Hummus`, `Peach`, `Pho`, `Roti`, `Salad`, and `Wonton` all have both likes and dislikes, so none of them can be placed in 'not_orderable' solely on the basis of dislikes.\n\nThis means we must find a balanced combination where at least one liked item of each individual must be 'orderable' or one disliked item must be 'not_orderable', and no individual dislikes any of the 'orderable' items nor likes any of the 'not_orderable' items.\n\nHowever, a quick scan of the list shows that items either liked or disliked are overlapping significantly, such that there's no clear item to be safely placed in either 'orderable' or 'not_orderable' without further conflicts. Every food item on the list is liked by at least one person and disliked by at least another, rendering a satisfactory combination for the entire group impossible under the given rules.\n\nTherefore, since there is no combination of items that can satisfy all participants at once without breaking the rules, the output for both 'orderable' and 'not_orderable' lists should be empty.\n\nThe final answer is:\n\n```python\norderable = [], not_orderable = []\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 1329, 'num_completion_tokens': 685}
