ultiple people (Lee, Min, Dan, Anil, Fay, Gia) and not liked by anyone, meaning it is a good candidate for the not_orderable list.\n- Salmon is liked by Anil but disliked by Sid, Min, and Ula. Like lasagna, it is a good candidate for the not_orderable list.\n\nIf we put lasagna and salmon on the not_orderable list:\n- Lee, Min, Dan, Anil, Fay, Sid all would be satisfied because their disliked items are on the not_orderable list.\n- We then need to satisfy Kim, Ula, Feng, and Gia by putting at least one of their liked items on the orderable list.\n- Ula only likes wonton and dislikes all other items, so wonton must be on the orderable list to satisfy Ula.\n- By adding wonton to the orderable list, we automatically satisfy Kim, Feng, and Gia.\n\nNow let's see if any conflicts arise:\n- By having wonton orderable, we have a conflict with Dan, who dislikes wonton. However, since Dan likes quiche, we can satisfy Dan by placing quiche on the orderable list as well.\n\nThis satisfies everyone's requirements:\n- All those who dislike lasagna and salmon are satisfied by having them on the not_orderable list.\n- Kim, Ula, Feng, and Gia are satisfied by having wonton orderable.\n- Dan and Fay are satisfied by having quiche orderable.\n- The likes of Lee, Sid, and Min are implicitly satisfied by the existing orderable items.\n\nBased on the above deductions:\n```\norderable = ['wonton', 'quiche']\nnot_orderable = ['lasagna', 'salmon']\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 339, 'num_completion_tokens': 707}
