: Likes naan. Dislikes tart, pie. Cal: Likes burger. Dislikes naan, tart. Dot: Likes naan. Dislikes burger, tart. Zan: Likes naan, burger. Dislikes tart. Kai: Likes pie. Dislikes burger, tart. Jaya: Likes naan. Dislikes tart, pie. Rohan: Likes tart, naan. Dislikes pie. Tia: Likes tart, naan. Dislikes pie. ', 'menu_items': ['pie', 'burger', 'naan', 'tart'], 'gpt_out': "To solve this, we'll need to analyze the likes and dislikes of each individual and find a pattern that satisfies the criteria: at least one liked item in the 'orderable' list or one disliked item in the 'not_orderable' list, with the constraint that no item can appear on both lists. Additionally, all participants must be satisfied.\n\nGiven the data, let's first establish the list of all possible food items:\n\n- Pie\n- Tart\n- Burger\n- Naan\n\nLet's start by identifying if there are any food items that are disliked by everyone who has an opinion on them, as these would be ideal candidates for 'not_orderable'. However, in this case, every food item is liked by at least one person, so we cannot simply place a food item in 'not_orderable' just based on universal dislike.\n\nThe next step is to identify if there's a food item that is liked by some but not disliked directly by others, which could be a candidate for 'orderable'. We can see that:\n\n- Pie is disliked by Zoe, Sue, Ian, Fay, Gen, Ada, Ray, Du, and Tia.\n- Tart is disliked by Nic, Sal, Riya, Lia, Jim, Ansh, Cal, Dot, Zan, and Jaya.\n- Burger is disliked by Zoe, Liz, Tara, Sia, Yue, Pat, Ian, Lia, Jim, Ray, and Tia.\n- Naan is disliked by Nic, Abe, Lee, Ian, Kai, Ray, Ana, and Cal.\n\nIt looks challenging to place any single food item in 'not_orderable' given the diversity of dislikes, as every item is liked by at least one person who dislikes it.\n\nOne strategy is to look for an item that is liked by those who dislike that same item, for example, Pie is liked by everyone who dislikes Pie, this means that if Pie is orderable everyone that dislikes it will be fine because they also like it making Pie a candidate to be orderable:\n\nZoe dislikes Pie also likes Burger, Naan, and Tart\nSue dislikes Pie also likes Burger and Naan\nIan dislikes Pie also likes nothing, which doesn't help\nFay dislikes Pie also likes nothing, which doesn't help\nGen dislikes Pie also likes Naan\nAda dislikes Pie also likes Naan\nRay dislikes Pie also likes nothing, which doesn't help\nDu dislikes Pie also likes Naan\nTia dislikes Pie also likes Naan and Tart\n\nNow since everyone that dislikes Pie likes Naan, we can try to make Naan not_orderable:\n\nAbe dislikes Naan also likes Pie and Tart\nCal dislikes Naan also likes Burger\nKai dislikes Naan also likes Pie\nLee dislikes Naan also likes Pie and Tart\nRay dislikes Naan also likes nothing, which doesn't help, but since he dislikes all items.\nAna dislikes Naan also likes Tart\n\nSo Tart becomes orderable, and the people that dislike it are satisfied:\n\nJim dislikes Tart also likes nothing, which doesn't help, but since he dislikes all items.\nRohan dislikes Tart also likes Naan and Pie\nRiya dislikes Tart also likes Burger\nZan dislikes Tart also likes Burger and Naan\nDot dislikes Tart also likes Naan\nCal dislikes Tart also likes Burger\nAnsh dislikes Tart also likes Naan\nSal dislikes Tart also likes Burger and Pie\nPat dislikes Tart also likes Naan and Pie\nJoy dislikes Tart also likes Burger and Naan\nLia dislikes Tart also likes Naan\nHal dislikes Tart also likes Naan and Pie\nTia dislikes Tart also likes Naan and Pie\n\nAnd the people that like Tart will also like Pie or Naan:\n\nLiz, Tara, Lee, May, Yue, Pat, Hal, Ian, Veer, Hui, Jun, Arun, Eli, Rex, Abe, Ana\n\nNow we have:\n\n- Pie (orderable)\n- Naan (not_orderable)\n- Tart (orderable)\n- Burger (needs to be determined)\n\nFor Burger, we can see that individuals who dislike Burger have their likes already covered by either Pie or Tart:\n\nZoe dislikes Burger but likes Tart.\nLiz dislikes Burger but likes Pie and Tart.\nTara dislikes Burger but likes Pie.\nSia dislikes Burger but likes Pie.\nYue dislikes Burger but likes Pie.\nPat dislikes Burger but likes Naan and Tart, but since Naan is not_orderable, Tart satisfies Pat.\nIan dislikes everything and therefore will not be satisfied by any orderable item anyway.\nLia dislikes Burger but likes Naan.\nJim dislikes everything and therefore will not be satisfied by any orderable item anyway.\nRay dislikes Burger but dislikes everything, so he can't be satisfied by an 'orderable' item anyway.\nTia dislikes Burger but likes Naan and Tart, but since Naan is not_orderable, Tart satisfies Tia.\nSue dislikes Pie but likes Burger.\n\nWith the given information and the established satisfaction of most individuals, we can include Burger in the 'orderable' list:\n\n- Burger (orderable)\n\nWe managed to satisfy all individuals except Ian, Ray, and Jim who are not satisfied by any combination due to their universal dislikes. Since they cannot be satisfied and the criteria is that all participants must be by the combination, we must return the empty list for both 'orderable' and 'not_orderable'.\n\nSo the final solution would be:\n\n```python\norderable = []\nnot_orderable = []\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 712, 'num_completion_tokens': 1121}
Data Sample: {'num_vars': 4, 'num_clauses': 28, 'formula': [[-4, 3, 1], [-2, -1, -4], [1, -2, -4], [4, 3, -1], [-3, 1, 4], [2, -3, 1], [3, 1, 4], [1, 2, -3], [-4, 2, 1], [2, -4, 3], [-4, -1, 3], [-1, 2, 4], [3, 1, 4], [-3, 1, 2], [2, -3, 4], [2, 4, -3], [-3, 4, 2], [-3, 1, -2], [3, 4, -2], [2, -3, 1], [3, 2, 1], [-3, 4, 1], [2, -1, 4], [1, -3, -4], [-3, -2, 4], [-3, 1, 2], [2, -4, -3], [-3, 1, 2]], 'is_sat': False, 'preferences': 'Jan: Likes doughnut, pizza. Dislikes couscous. Tom: Dislikes kimchi, pizza, couscous. Cy: Likes pizza. Dislikes kimchi, couscous. Wes: Likes couscous, doughnut. Dislikes pizza. Wei: Likes pizza, couscous. Dislikes doughnut. Jun: Likes kimchi, pizza. Dislikes doughnut. Arun: Likes doughnut, pizza, couscous. Pat: Likes pizza, kimchi. Dislikes doughnut. Diya: Likes kimchi, pizza. Dislikes couscous. Chao: Likes kimchi, doughnut. Dislikes couscous. Xan: Likes doughnut. Dislikes couscous, pizza. Amy: Likes kimchi, couscous. Dislikes pizza. Ru: Likes doughnut, pizza, couscous. Yue: Likes pizza, kimchi. Dislikes doughnut. Lan: Likes kimchi, couscous. Dislikes doughnut. Sia: Likes kimchi, couscous. Dislikes doughnut. Dan: Likes couscous, kimchi. Dislikes doughnut. Rex: Likes pizza. Dislikes doughnut, kimchi. Zara: Likes doughnut, couscous. Dislikes kimchi. Chen: Likes kimchi, pizza. Dislikes doughnut. Kit: Likes doughnut, kimchi, pizza. Ken: Likes couscous, pizza. Dislikes doughnut. Eva: Likes kimchi, couscous. Dislikes pizza. Ava: Likes pizza. Dislikes doughnut, couscous. Tia: Likes couscous. Dislikes doughnut, kimchi. Fay: Likes pizza, kimchi. Dislikes doughnut. Abe: Likes kimchi. Dislikes couscous, doughnut. Taj: Likes pizza, kimchi. Dislikes doughnut. ', 'menu_items': ['pizza', 'kimchi', 'doughnut', 'couscous'], 'gpt_out': 'To satisfy everyone\'s preferences and separate the food items into "orderable" and "not_orderable" lists, we need to analyze the likes and dislikes of each individual. Let us list all the preferences and see if we can find a solution that satisfies all conditions.\n\n### **Likes:**\n- Doughnut: Jan, Wes, Arun, Xan, Chao, Ru, Zara, Kit\n- Pizza: Jan, Cy, Wei, Jun, Pat, Diya, Yue, Rex, Ken, Ava, Taj, Fay, Abe, Chen\n- Kimchi: Jun, Pat, Diya, Chao, Amy, Lan, Sia, Dan, Eva, Fay, Taj, Kit, Abe, Yue, Chen\n- Couscous: Wes, Wei, Arun, Amy, Lan, Sia, Dan, Tia, Zara, Ken, Eva\n\n### **Dislikes:**\n- Couscous: Jan, Tom, Cy, Diya, Chao, Xan, Ava, Abe\n- Kimchi: Tom, Cy, Rex, Tia\n- Doughnut: Tom, Wei, Jun, Pat, Yue, Lan, Sia, Dan, Rex, Ken, Ava, Taj, Fay, Abe\n- Pizza: Tom, Wes, Amy, Eva\n\nFrom the likes and dislikes, we see:\n\n- Pizza is liked by many but disliked by a few, so if we place it on the "orderable" list, we will satisfy many but not all.\n- Doughnuts are liked by several people but disliked by many, so it is not a good candidate for the "orderable" list.\n- Kimchi is liked by many and disliked by few, but placing it on the "orderable" list would not satisfy the criteria for some individuals (like Cy and Rex).\n- Couscous is somewhat liked and disliked by different individuals, but no one who likes pizza dislikes couscous.\n\nBased on the satisfaction criteria, an item can only be "orderable" if it is not explicitly disliked by any participant. Otherwise, someone will be unsatisfied. Likewise, an item can be "not_orderable" if it is disliked by someone who does not have an item they like on the "orderable" list.\n\nLet\'s first look at the dislikes because they are absolute; if someone dislikes an item, it cannot be in the "orderable" list. We immediately note that pizza is disliked by some participants, so if it goes on the "orderable" list, those participants will not be satisfied. On the other hand, couscous can be "not_orderable" because everyone who likes couscous also likes pizza. This will satisfy all participants who like couscous since they also like pizza (which will be on the "orderable" list).\n\nTherefore, we can satisfy the condition for the participants who dislike couscous by putting it in the "not_orderable" list. This will not dissatisfy anyone who likes couscous because all couscous likers also like pizza, which we plan to place in the "orderable" list.\n\nNow, considering pizza and kimchi as "orderable," we can satisfy nearly everyone. However, we must also consider that some participants dislike doughnut, which should not be on the "orderable" list then.\n\nIf pizza and kimchi are "orderable," we can satisfy all participants except those who dislike pizza or kimchi. Putting kimchi on the "orderable" list resolves this because those who dislike kimchi do not dislike pizza. However, kimchi must also be "orderable" to satisfy everyone that dislikes doughnuts since they have no common likes with those who dislike pizza.\n\nSo, this gives us a solution:\n- "Orderable": Pizza, Kimchi\n- "Not_orderable": Doughnut, Couscous\n\nBecause:\n- Everyone who likes pizza will be satisfied.\n- Everyone who likes kimchi will be satisfied.\n- Everyone who dislikes kimchi has at least pizza to satisfy them.\n- Everyone who dislikes doughnut has at least pizza or kimchi to satisfy them.\n- Everyone who dislikes couscous has at least pizza or kimchi to satisfy them.\n\nThis setup satisfies all participants\' conditions, so we have:\n\n```python\norderable=[\'pizza\', \'kimchi\'], not_orderable=[\'doughnut\', \'couscous\']\n```', 'num_prompt_tokens': 583, 'num_completion_tokens': 902}
Data Sample: {'num_vars': 4, 'num_clauses': 8, 'formula': [[1, 4, 3], [-1, 4, -3], [4, 2, 1], [1, -4, 3], [2, -3, -4], [-3, 4, -1], [2, -3, -1], [-3, 1, -4]], 'is_sat': True, 'preferences': 'Hu: Likes bruschetta, espresso, hummus. Amy: Likes espresso. Dislikes bruschetta, hummus. Ty: Likes espresso, toast, bruschetta. Jay: Likes bruschetta, hummus. Dislikes espresso. Bao: Likes toast. Dislikes hummus, espresso. Ansh: Likes espresso. Dislikes hummus, bruschetta. Joe: Likes toast. Dislikes hummus, bruschetta. Ada: Likes bruschetta. Dislikes hummus, espresso. ', 'menu_items': ['bruschetta', 'toast', 'hummus', 'espresso'], 'gpt_out': "Let's analyze this step by step, considering each person's likes and dislikes and finding a common solution that satisfies everyone's preferences.\n\n**Hu**:\n- Likes: bruschetta, espresso, hummus\n- There's no dislike, so we can put any of the likes in 'orderable'.\n\n**Amy**:\n- Likes: espresso\n- Dislikes: bruschetta, hummus\n- Espresso can be orderable to satisfy her, bruschetta and hummus should go into 'not_orderable'.\n\n**Ty**:\n- Likes: espresso, toast, bruschetta\n- No dislikes, so any of the likes can go into 'orderable'.\n\n**Jay**:\n- Likes: bruschetta, hummus\n- Dislikes: espresso\n- Since espresso is a dislike, it can't go into 'orderable'. We can satisfy Jay by putting bruschetta or hummus into 'orderable'.\n\n**Bao**:\n- Likes: toast\n- Dislikes: hummus, espresso\n- Toast can go into 'orderable', hummus, and espresso should be in 'not_orderable'.\n\n**Ansh**:\n- Likes: espresso\n- Dislikes: hummus, bruschetta\n- Espresso can be 'orderable' to satisfy Ansh, and bruschetta and hummus must be 'not_orderable'.\n\n**Joe**:\n- Likes: toast\n- Dislikes: hummus, bruschetta\n- Toast must go into 'orderable', and hummus and bruschetta into 'not_orderable'.\n\n**Ada**:\n- Likes: bruschetta\n- Dislikes: hummus, espresso\n- Bruschetta must go into 'orderable', and hummus and espresso into 'not_orderable'.\n\nNow, let's find a common configuration that satisfies all of the above. \n\n- Espresso can't go into 'orderable' because Jay and Ada dislike it, and it should be 'not_orderable'.\n- Hummus can't go into 'orderable' because Amy, Bao, Ansh, Joe, and Ada dislike it, it should be 'not_orderable'.\n- Bruschetta can't go into 'orderable' because Amy, Ansh, and Joe dislike it, it should be 'not_orderable'.\n- Toast hasn't been disliked by anyone, so it can safely go into 'orderable'.\n\nConsidering all this, we can satisfy everyone by putting toast in 'orderable', and espresso, hummus, and bruschetta in 'not_orderable'. \n\nHu is satisfied by toast (liked) in 'orderable'.\nAmy is satisfied by bruschetta and hummus (disliked) in 'not_orderable'.\nTy is satisfied by toast (liked) in 'orderable'.\nJay is satisfied by bruschetta and hummus (disliked) in 'not_orderable'.\nBao is satisfied by toast (liked) in 'orderable'.\nAnsh is satisfied by bruschetta and hummus (disliked) in 'not_orderable'.\nJoe is satisfied by toast (liked) in 'orderable'.\nAda is satisfied by bruschetta and hummus (disliked) in 'not_orderable'.\n\nHere are the final lists:\n\n```python\norderable=['toast'], not_orderable=['espresso', 'hummus', 'bruschetta']\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 299, 'num_completion_tokens': 688}
Data Sample: {'num_vars': 8, 'num_clauses': 40, 'formula': [[-4, 5, -8], [-3, 5, -1], [2, 4, -8], [-6, 8, 7], [1, -2, 8], [-8, 6, 2], [1, -2, 7], [-8, -4, 6], [-6, 3, 1], [-5, 4, -3], [-7, -4, 2], [3, -6, 4], [-1, 3, 8], [-2, -4, -7], [8, -6, 2], [-1, -4, 5], [4, -8, -5], [-8, 3, -4], [8, 7, 1], [3, 6, 8], [4, 8, 6], [-5, 7, 4], [4, 7, 3], [5, -1, 4], [3, 4, -2], [4, 3, 6], [-5, -1, 2], [-8, 3, -2], [5, -6, 1], [-4, 5, 6], [7, -5, -4], [4, 8, 2], [8, -6, -7], [4, 6, 5], [8, 2, 6], [6, -2, 7], [-8, -6, 7], [8, -4, -7], [-8, -1, 7], [1, -3, 8]], 'is_sat': False, 'preferences': 'Lin: Likes custard. Dislikes waffles, cheesecake. Sam: Likes custard. Dislikes brownies, espresso. Kai: Likes paneer, waffles. Dislikes cheesecake. Min: Likes cheesecake, wonton. Dislikes fries. Yan: Likes espresso, cheesecake. Dislikes paneer. Ana: Likes fries, paneer. Dislikes cheesecake. Ying: Likes espresso, wonton. Dislikes paneer. Mei: Likes fries. Dislikes cheesecake, waffles. Max: Likes brownies, espresso. Dislikes fries. Hu: Likes waffles. Dislikes custard, brownies. Veer: Likes paneer. Dislikes wonton, waffles. Ian: Likes brownies, waffles. Dislikes fries. Li: Likes brownies, cheesecake. Dislikes espresso. Liv: Dislikes paneer, waffles, wonton. Raj: Likes cheesecake, paneer. Dislikes fries. Arun: Likes custard. Dislikes espresso, waffles. Amy: Likes waffles. Dislikes cheesecake, custard. Val: Likes brownies. Dislikes cheesecake, waffles. Xan: Likes cheesecake, wonton, espresso. Nic: Likes brownies, fries, cheesecake. Ada: Likes waffles, cheesecake, fries. Rob: Likes wonton, waffles. Dislikes custard. Jaya: Likes waffles, wonton, brownies. Chao: Likes custard, waffles. Dislikes espresso. May: Likes brownies, waffles. Dislikes paneer. Dot: Likes waffles, brownies, fries. Zan: Likes paneer. Dislikes custard, espresso. Ned: Likes brownies. Dislikes cheesecake, paneer. Taj: Likes custard, espresso. Dislikes fries. Jim: Likes custard, fries. Dislikes waffles. Eva: Likes wonton. Dislikes custard, waffles. Cal: Likes waffles, cheesecake, paneer. Anil: Likes cheesecake. Dislikes fries, wonton. Wei: Likes waffles, fries, custard. Jan: Likes cheesecake, paneer, fries. Sia: Likes fries, wonton. Dislikes paneer. Sid: Likes wonton. Dislikes cheesecake, fries. Mina: Likes cheesecake. Dislikes waffles, wonton. Pat: Likes wonton. Dislikes cheesecake, espresso. Gus: Likes espresso, cheesecake. Dislikes brownies. ', 'menu_items': ['espresso', 'paneer', 'brownies', 'waffles', 'custard', 'fries', 'wonton', 'cheesecake'], 'gpt_out': "To satisfy as many people as possible with their food preferences, we need to look for patterns and conflicts between likes and dislikes. Let's first create a list of all the food items mentioned:\n\n`custard, waffles, cheesecake, brownies, espresso, paneer, wonton, fries`\n\nNow, let's analyze the preferences to find a common ground while making sure every person has at least one liked item in the 'orderable' list or one disliked item in the 'not_orderable' list.\n\nWe start by identifying any items that must be in one of the lists due to an irreconcilable conflict where one person likes an item that another dislikes. If multiple people like an item and nobody dislikes it, it can safely go on the 'orderable' list. If multiple people dislike an item and nobody likes it, it can go on the 'not_orderable' list.\n\nCheesecake is disliked by many but also liked by many. We will check if it can be put in the orderable list notwithstanding that some people dislike it.\n\n**Likes Cheesecake**: Lin, Min, Yan, Li, Raj, Xan, Nic, Ada, Cal, Anil, Jan, Mina, Gus\n**Dislikes Cheesecake**: Kai, Ana, Mei, Liv, Amy, Val, Sid, Pat\n\nNow, we need to check if for those who dislike cheesecake, there is at least one liked item they can have in the orderable list or one disliked in the not_orderable list.\n\nFor instance:\n- Kai likes paneer, which is not disliked by anyone; hence, paneer can go in the 'orderable' list.\n- Ana likes fries, which is also not disliked by anyone; therefore, fries go to 'orderable'.\n- Mei dislikes waffles; this can't inform us yet, as others like waffles.\n- And so on… We need to go through each person who dislikes cheesecake and ensure they can be satisfied otherwise.\n\nNow let's find items that are uniformly disliked or liked by those who have a strong opinion about cheesecake.\n\n**Disliked by Cheesecake Dislikers**:\n- Paneer: disliked by Yan, Ying, Liv, Zan; liked by Kai, Ana, Veer, Raj, Cal, Jan, Zan => conflicted.\n- Waffles: disliked by Lin, Mei, Hu, Amy, Arun, Jim, Eva, Liv, Sid, Val, Nic; liked by others => conflicted.\n- Wonton: disliked by Veer, Liv, Anil, Sid, Mina => conflicted.\n- Espresso: disliked by Sam, Liv, Zan, Pat => conflicted.\n- Fries: disliked by Min, Jim, Mei, Raj, Taj, Sid => conflicted.\n- Brownies: no dislikes found! => can be 'orderable'.\n\n**Liked by Cheesecake Likers**:\nSince brownies have no dislikes, they can go in the 'orderable' list. And we stop here for now because brownies being 'orderable' will need further analysis if there's someone who both likes cheesecake and dislikes brownies, but that's not the case.\n\nSo we have:\n`orderable = [brownies]`\n`not_orderable = []`\n\nNow let's go step by step to add items to the 'orderable' or 'not_orderable' list, always ensuring everyone's satisfaction criteria are met.\n\n**Paneer** is liked by several and disliked by several. Since those who dislike it also have other items they like (such as espresso for Ying, fries for Yan, cheesecake for Liv), conflicts arise here. We cannot make a decision on paneer yet.\n\n**Waffles** are liked by many but disliked by many who like brownies (which are already on 'orderable'). So we can't add waffles yet.\n\n**Espresso** is liked by some (e.g., Yan) who dislike other 'orderable' or must-have items. This could potentially be 'orderable', but we need to see whether accommodating espresso affects others. Max, Sam, Arun, & Jim are the ones who dislike espresso; all of them like custard which is not disliked by anyone, meaning custard can be 'orderable'.\n\nSo far:\n`orderable = [brownies, custard]`\n`not_orderable = []`\n\nLet's see what we have left that could fit into 'not_orderable':\n\n**Cheesecake** still has mixed preferences. Now let's see about the other items:\n\n**Wonton** is a critical item with several likes and dislikes. Since Sid dislikes both cheesecake and wonton, we cannot make it not_orderable. Liv dislikes paneer, waffles, and wonton, so if we can satisfy her with the orderable, wonton could be not_orderable. Let's now check Liv on the updated orderable list. She dislikes everything currently on the orderable list (brownies and custard).\n\n**Fries** have a number of fans but also are disliked by some including Min, Sid, and Raj, who all have other items available in 'orderable'.\n\nSo, we put wonton in 'not_orderable' and since it is liked by Yan and Sid who both like fries, we need to add fries to 'orderable'.\n\n`orderable = [brownies, custard, fries]`\n`not_orderable = [wonton]`\n\nWith the lists updated, let's check back with Liv, who dislikes paneer, espresso, and waffles, and now she can be satisfied because wonton is not_orderable. Sid, who dislikes cheesecake and fries, now can be satisfied with brownies as it’s 'orderable’.\n\nContinue rechecking everyone's dislikes and likes until all can be satisfied:\n\n**Waffles** is now the only item not in any list. People who dislike waffles are Lin, Mei, Hu, Amy, Arun, Jim, Eva, Liv, Sid, Val, Nic all have items in the 'orderable' list to satisfy them. So we can put waffles into 'orderable' since it doesn't conflict there.\n\nFinally:\n`orderable = [brownies, custard, fries, waffles]`\n`not_orderable = [wonton]`\n\nNow we must ensure that every individual is satisfied. We recheck:\n\n- Everybody who dislikes 'not_orderable' (wonton) has at least one item they like in 'orderable'.\n- Everybody who dislikes any 'orderable' items has alternative likes or dislikes that are satisfied by the lists.\n\nAs every individual's criteria seem to be met by these lists, we've found a satisfactory combination.\n\nFinal lists:\n```python\norderable = ['brownies', 'custard', 'fries', 'waffles']\nnot_orderable = ['wonton']\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 716, 'num_completion_tokens': 1419}
