i and wonton but dislikes cheesecake. This directly conflicts with multiple others who dislike roti or wonton and like cheesecake.\n\nRight away, we can note that cheesecake is liked by Ravi, Max, Chen, Ken, and Jan, and it's not disliked by anyone. Therefore, cheesecake can be placed in the 'orderable' list to satisfy these five individuals. Now we need to consider the others.\n\nChao and Fay like guacamole, and Max dislikes it; since Max will be satisfied with cheesecake, we can put guacamole on the 'orderable' list, thus satisfying Chao and Fay without concerning Max.\n\nLastly, May's preferences are the most problematic as she likes roti and wonton but dislikes cheesecake. However, cheesecake is already in the 'orderable' list which she dislikes, and that alone makes her unsatisfied with the order, rendering it impossible to satisfy everyone's preferences. May's dissatisfaction means there is no combination possible that satisfies all participants.\n\nConclusion: Since all it takes is one person (in this case, May) to not have their preferences met for the solution to fail, no satisfactory combination exists.\n\nOutputting empty lists as per point 4 of the satisfaction criteria:\n\n```python\norderable=[], not_orderable=[]\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 309, 'num_completion_tokens': 588}
