t everyone dislikes, to quickly add them to the \'not_orderable\' list but from the preferences stated it seems there\'s no such item because all food items are liked by at least one person.\n\nAlternatively, we look if there\'s an item liked by at least one person and disliked by none, to add them to \'orderable\'. Again, this is not the case as every item is disliked by at least one person.\n\nSince we cannot find a universal item for either list, we\'ll have to go through the preferences one by one to find a combination that can satisfy everyone\'s likes and dislikes. We will use a process of elimination and logical deduction to satisfy the largest number of requirements possible. \n\n1. An item that appears as liked by someone and not disliked by anyone else is a good candidate for \'orderable\'.\n2. An item that is disliked by someone and not liked by anyone else is a good candidate for \'not_orderable\'.\n\nWe can then perform more nuanced analysis by identifying if there are some dislikes that could be moved to \'not_orderable\', which will not affect others\' satisfaction because they have other likes available.\n\nLet\'s analyze the requirements further:\n\n- Kebab is disliked by many and liked by only a few, so could be a candidate for \'not_orderable\'. We need to ensure that Lex, Eli, Geet, and Wes have other items that they like that can be made \'orderable\'.\n- Naan is liked by many and also disliked by many, so we need to be careful.\n- Spaghetti is liked by many, and only a few dislike it, making it a potential candidate for \'orderable\'.\n- Fries are similar to spaghetti, liked by many, disliked by only a few, so could also be considered for \'orderable\'.\n\nGiven the complexity of multiple overlapping likes and dislikes, it\'s plausible that this may take several iterations to come to a set that satisfies everyone. However, with the information provided and the approach of listing everyone\'s preferences, we should be able to start piecing together a solution.\n\nLet\'s run through a set of items and try to allocate them to either the \'orderable\' or \'not_orderable\' based on the above strategy. \n\n- Kebab, as it is liked by Lex, Eli, Geet, and Wes. However, all of them also like other items which gives leeway to put kebab into \'not_orderable\'.\n\nSo, we can start with:\nnot_orderable = [\'kebab\']\n\nThen we look at the individuals who liked kebab to ensure they have other options:\n- Lex dislikes peach and vegetables which rules those two out.\n- Eli dislikes naan.\n- Geet dislikes toast and naan.\n- Wes dislikes peach.\n\nNow we need to find orderable items for these individuals which aren\'t universally disliked by others, which becomes challenging as most foods are disliked by someone in the group.\n\nNext, we need to identify the most "popular" items in terms of likes and ensure these are not in the \'not_orderable\' for any individual to be satisfied.\n- Spaghetti is liked by many and could be a potential \'orderable\' but disliked by Zara, Lin, Ava, and Zac.\n- Toast is liked by many but disliked by Nic, Gen, Jun, Kim, Hui, and Geet.\n- Peach is also liked by many but is disliked by Rina, Yue, Ty, Max, Xiu, Ben, Ned, Sal, and Wei.\n\nConsidering the complexity, it\'s becoming apparent that finding a perfect match might not be feasible as adding any one item to \'orderable\' or \'not_orderable\' seems to result in at least one person not being satisfied. The dislikes list seems to overlap with someone else\'s likes, which indicates that satisfying everyone may not be possible.\n\nTherefore, after trying to balance the preferences, it becomes clear that we are unable to construct a list that can satisfy all conditions dictated by all liking at least one item in \'orderable\' or disliking at least one in \'not_orderable\'. Hence, our output should be two empty lists.\n\nHere is the solution in Python:\n\n```python\norderable=[], not_orderable=[]\n```', 'num_prompt_tokens': 966, 'num_completion_to