aring likes and dislikes. Let's start by attempting to satisfy preferences involving these two items.\n\n- Putting tandoori in 'orderable' can satisfy Pat, Abe, Rob, Oli, Neil, Chen, Veer, Eva, Sky, Sid, Tom, Raj, Ana, Qi, Ty, and Raj, but Shan, Ula, Ivy, Lex, Tim, Gus, Dot, Sal, Ada, Bo, Jon, Mel will be dissatisfied.\n- Putting bruschetta in 'orderable' will satisfy everyone who likes it (Sam, Abe, Lex, Tim, Neil, Chen, Cal, Lin, Veer, Eva, Gus, Tom, Anil, Bo, Jon, and Mel), however, bruschetta is disliked by Yue, Rina, Pat, Ula, Ivy, Rob, Zoe, Raj, Qi, and Sid, so they will be dissatisfied unless shawarma or bread are in 'not_orderable'.\n\nSince bread and shawarma are generally the most conflicted items, it is clear that no matter which item we place in 'orderable', and which in 'not_orderable', we will not be able to satisfy everyone due to the cross-conflicts. Each of the four food items is liked by at least one person who dislikes all the other three items. This makes it impossible to create a set of 'orderable' and 'not_orderable' lists that will satisfy all participants. \n\nTherefore, we conclude that there is no combination of 'orderable' and 'not_orderable' lists that can satisfy everyone and we must output empty lists:\n\n```python\norderable = []\nnot_orderable = []\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 743, 'num_completion_tokens': 826}
