uld potentially be on the 'orderable' list.\n\n- 'Salad' and 'ramen' could potentially be 'orderable'. We need to ensure that individuals who dislike 'salad' and 'ramen' have other items to satisfy them on the 'not_orderable' list.\n    - Raj dislikes 'korma', 'ratatouille', and 'salad'. If either 'korma' or 'ratatouille' is 'not_orderable', Raj can be satisfied.\n    - Sal, Mia, Qi, Kai, and Du dislike 'ramen' but also dislike some other items that could potentially be 'not_orderable'.\n\nNext, let's check conflicts for those who like 'salad' and 'ramen':\n\n- 'Salad': It's liked by Ivy, Meg, Lee, Kai, Li and Lee who dislike espresso, which is already disliked by many and can be placed on not_orderable.\n- 'Ramen': It's liked by several people but some people who dislike it like Abe and Max dislike naan as well which has possibility to be placed on not_orderable list.\n\nGiven the preferences, it seems challenging to make all of them satisfied with only two lists without conflicts. However, one possible solution could be to set 'salad' and 'ramen' on the 'orderable' list and decide other items based on further dependencies.\n\nLet's try to arrange the items such that 'salad' and 'ramen' are 'orderable', and check if we can satisfy everyone:\n\n- 'Naan' is liked by many, and those who dislike it also dislike other items, so potential orderable.\n- 'Paneer' is liked by many but disliked by some; its placement depends on satisfying those who dislike it.\n- 'Korma' is contentious as it's liked and disliked by many—it's challenging to place.\n- 'Ratatouille' also has mixed opinions; its placement will need careful consideration.\n- 'Espresso' is liked by many but disliked by those who also dislike other items, so potential not_orderable.\n- 'Omelette' has mixed emotions as well; its placement depends on satisfying all.\n- 'Kimchi' is disliked by some and all of them have alternative likes, so potential not_orderable.\n\nNow, let's decide the placement:\n- 'Naan' can be orderable because those who dislike naan dislikes other items that can be made not orderable like ratatouille.\n- 'Paneer' is liked by some and disliked by some. The ones who dislike it have other items they dislike, like espresso.\n- 'Korma' is liked by many who don't have any other likes that could be on the orderable list, so korma must be orderable.\n- 'Ratatouille' is the favorite of many. But those who dislike it can be satisfied with other dislikes on the not_orderable list, so ratatouille can be orderable.\n- 'Espresso' is liked by many. But those who like it have other items they like that could be orderable and those who dislike it, dislike it only. So, espresso can be not_orderable.\n- 'Omelette' is liked by some and disliked by many. All who dislike omelette dislike something else that can be put on the not_orderable list, making omelette orderable.\n- 'Kimchi' has several dislikes, and they all have other items they like/dislike which can be adjusted. So, kimchi can be not_orderable.\n\nGiven the above analysis, a potential solution would be to have 'salad', 'ramen', 'naan', 'korma', 'ratatouille', and 'omelette' on the 'orderable' list, and 'espresso' and 'kimchi' on the 'not_orderable' list.\n\nLet's present this solution:\n\n`orderable=['salad', 'ramen', 'naan', 'korma', 'ratatouille', 'omelette'], not_orderable=['espresso', 'kimchi']`\n\nThis solution seems to satisfy everyone's requirement, though ensuring every person is satisfied with these lists would require a detailed check that there aren't any individual conflicts missed in this overview.", 'num_prompt_tokens': 1127, 'num_completion_tokens': 1415}
