ed by many and not disliked by anyone (orderable).\n2. Custard is liked by many but disliked by some.\n3. Omelette is liked by many but disliked by some.\n4. Caramel is liked by many and not disliked by any (orderable).\n5. Cheesecake is liked by many but is disliked by some.\n\nFrom the list above, tiramisu and caramel can be put on the orderable list. This immediately satisfies individuals who exclusively like those items.\n\nIf we place sushi on the not_orderable list, it would satisfy the people who dislike it without upsetting those who like it since more liked items are still available to meet their needs. \n\nNext, let\'s place "waffles" on the orderable list, as it\'s liked by many and those who dislike it are already satisfied by caramel or tiramisu.\n\nDoing this process systematically, we create two lists while watching out for conflicts:\n\n1. Add tiramisu to the orderable list. This satisfies Vic, Jon, Shan, Raj, Liv, Jun, Lei, Fay, Pam, Ravi, Li, Bea, and Mel (none of these individuals dislike tiramisu).\n2. Add caramel to the orderable list. This satisfies Vic, Sia, Zoe, Feng, Oli, Ying, Ron, Min, Kai, Ken, Ru, and Ben. None of the individuals who like caramel dislike tiramisu, and vice versa.\n3. Add sushi to the not_orderable list because everyone who dislikes it has not yet been satisfied (e.g., Max, Vic, Joy, Ula, Cy, Jim, Sol, Gen, Arun, Liz, Gia, Abe, Kim, Hu, Meg, Ada, Xan, and Naina). On the orderable list, we already have tiramisu and caramel which are liked by some who like sushi (e.g., Min, Hu, Leo).\n\nAfter placing these items, we have to check that every individual has at least one like on the orderable list or one dislike on the not_orderable list. If not, we try to add an item (or more) from their likes to the orderable list or from their dislikes to the not_orderable list.\n\nNow let\'s check this with the remaining individuals:\n\n- Dislikes that aren\'t on the not_orderable list: custard, vindaloo, cheesecake, risotto, etc.\n- Likes