avioli: Kai, Tia, Zara, Zan, Hu, Feng\nDislikes ravioli: Amy, Cal, Sue, Ada, Ping, Gia\nLikes poke: Sid, Hui, Ping\nDislikes poke: Veer, Kai, Zara, Gia\nLikes guacamole: Eli, Tao, Xan, Sue, Pat, Diya, Feng\nDislikes guacamole: Liz, Kai, Zara\n\nSince everyone must be satisfied, let's follow the criteria:\n\n1. For each individual who has a dislike, it is safe to put one of their disliked items on the 'not_orderable' list if it does not conflict with another's like. This is because doing so automatically satisfies the individual, regardless of what is on the 'orderable' list.\n\n2. For the individuals who only have likes, at least one of their liked items must be on the 'orderable' list.\n\nLet's start by looking for items that are unanimously disliked:\n\n- Burger is disliked by Veer, Amy, and Lia and liked by many. It cannot be on 'not_orderable'.\n- Sushi is disliked by Mel, Kavi, Eli, and Tao but liked by many. It cannot be on 'not_orderable'.\n- Burrito is disliked only by Sid and Tia, suggesting it could be a candidate for 'not_orderable'.\n- Ravioli is disliked by Amy, Cal, Sue, Ada, Ping, and Gia but liked by many. It cannot be on 'not_orderable'.\n- Korma is disliked by Lia, Hui, and Tia, suggesting it could also be a candidate for 'not_orderable'.\n- Poke is disliked by Veer, Kai, Zara, and Gia but liked by some. It cannot be on 'not_orderable'.\n- Guacamole is disliked only by Liz, Kai, and Zara. It cannot be 'not_orderable' due to multiple people liking it.\n- Mousse is disliked by Lia, Eli, and Ping. However, Gia and Cal like it, so it can't be 'not_orderable'.\n\nSince no food item is disliked by everyone who dislikes something, and liked by no one else, we cannot automatically assign any item to 'not_orderable'.\n\nNext step, we should start making a possible 'orderable' list that can satisfy most people considering their likes and dislikes:\n\nSince burger is liked by many individuals and only disliked by 3, it is a strong candidate for the 'orderable' list. However, if burger is on the 'orderable' list, we need to ensure Veer, Amy, and Lia can be satisfied with other items.\n- Veer likes korma, so that could be orderable, but Lia and Tia dislike it. Korma is therefore not a good candidate.\n- Amy likes sushi, and since sushi is only disliked by 4 individuals who have other likes, it's a candidate for 'orderable'.\n- Lia dislikes all three items she's mentioned with, so she must be satisfied with the 'not_orderable' list.\n\nTesting burger and sushi as 'orderable':\n- Mel (burger), Liz (sushi and burrito), Cal (burrito), Sue (burrito), Xan (sushi and burger), Zan (sushi and burrito), Pat (burger), Diya (burger), Ada (burrito), Feng (burger), Zara (ravioli), Kai (ravioli), Tia (ravioli), and Hu (burger) would be content with burger and/or sushi being orderable.\n\nNow we have to consider whether every person who has a dislike for either burger or sushi can still be satisfied. Lia will be satisfied as she dislikes korma, so it must be placed on the 'not_orderable' list. But Hui and Tia also dislike korma, and Hui likes burger, so there is a conflict if korma is 'not_orderable' because Hui would not be satisfied with anything 'orderable'.\n\nWithout a clear solution to satisfy everyone with the orderable/not_orderable setup, we should conclude that no satisfactory combination exists.\n\nThus we output empty lists:\n\n```python\norderable=[], not_orderable=[]\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 530, 'num_completion_tokens': 1022}
