ist, so we can instantly put the items that everyone dislikes into the 'not_orderable' list. If there's an item that is disliked by someone but not liked by anyone else, it's safe to put it on the 'not_orderable' list. Conversely, if there's an item that is liked by someone but disliked by no one, it can go on the 'orderable' list.\n\nJim dislikes churros, paneer, and mousse, so to satisfy Jim we can’t have any of these items on the 'orderable' list. However, we have people in the group who like these items. Thus, we must approach the problem by finding common likes that do not conflict with someone's dislikes and finding common dislikes that are not liked by anyone.\n\nHowever, when looking at the lists, we find several items are both liked and disliked by different people in the group:\n\n- Paneer is liked by many but disliked by Ty, Eli, Jim, and Gen.\n- Churros are liked by some but disliked by Eli, Ana, Aarav, Jon, Dan, and Mei.\n- Sorbet cannot be ordered because it is liked by some but disliked by Ray, Li, and Lin.\n- Mousse also cannot be ordered because it is liked by some but disliked by Rob, Ana, Dot, Jim, Ray, and Li.\n- Risotto cannot be ordered because it is liked by some but disliked by Joy, Geet, Aarav, Lin, and Tim.\n\nGiven these conflicts and the criteria that all participants must be satisfied, there seems to be no possible combination of 'orderable' and 'not_orderable' lists that can satisfy all the individuals, as everyone's likes conflict with someone else's dislikes. The common items that could potentially satisfy each person conflicts with someone else's dislikes, and vice versa.\n\nConsidering the above analysis, the output would be two empty lists, as there's no way to satisfy all the given criteria:\n\n```python\norderable=[], not_orderable=[]\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 480, 'num_completion_tokens': 646}
