't be orderable for Pia, Arun, Sam, Aarav, Cal, Chen;\n- Baklava is liked by Pam, Arun, Feng, Ava, Jim, Chen, Ana, Riya and disliked only by Aarav, Geet, Cy, Raj, Ron, Mei - can be 'orderable';\n- Tart is liked by Aarav, Vic, Tara and disliked by Cal, Ava, Ana, Jim - can be put in 'not.orderable' to satisfy these individuals;\n- Ravioli is liked by Geet, Kit, Cy, and disliked by Pam, Ru, Yan, Lia, Riya - can be put in 'not_orderable'.\n\nNow we have covered all individuals and their preferences, either by placing at least one liked item in 'orderable' or one disliked item in 'not_orderable', let's summarise our distribution:\n\n'orderable' = [caramel, falafel, baklava, bruschetta]\n'not_orderable' = [tiramisu, tart, ravioli]\n\nWe need to check that all individual requirements are met with these lists, and since it satisfies the criteria for each person on the list (as every individual either has at least one like in 'orderable' or one dislike in 'not_orderable'), the following will be our output:\n\norderable=['caramel', 'falafel', 'baklava', 'bruschetta'], not_orderable=['tiramisu', 'tart', 'ravioli']", 'num_prompt_tokens': 625, 'num_completion_tokens': 835}
