, Yan, Kit, Liz, Shan) and disliked by several, making it contentious.\n- Nachos are liked by several (Qi, Mel, Joy, Tao, Feng, Ansh, Liz, Shan, Ray, Zoe, Aarav) without a strong majority disliking them.\n\n**Step 4**: Attempt to create a solution that can satisfy the satisfaction criteria for all participants while minimizing conflicts.\n\nFirst, we look for an item that is only disliked (and not liked by anyone), or only liked (and not disliked by anyone); however, it seems each item is both liked and disliked by different individuals, so no such item exists.\n\nWe notice that risotto is a highly contentious item, disliked by the majority but liked by some. It's impossible to satisfy the likings of individuals who like risotto without dissatisfying those who dislike it; hence, we attempt to place it on a list that minimizes dissatisfaction.\n\nSince Nachos, Pie, and Custard also have both likers and dislikers, we must strike a balance. Let's attempt by placing the most contentious items, starting with the most disliked one, in 'not_orderable' to see if we can satisfy the requirement of at least one liked item in 'orderable' or one disliked item in 'not_orderable'.\n\nNoting the following people who have likes that would allow for an opposite of their likes to be in 'not_orderable':\n- Joy (likes nachos and risotto) – allows paneer and pie in 'orderable'.\n- Pam (likes paneer and risotto) – allows pie and custard in 'orderable'.\n- Tom (likes risotto and custard) – allows nachos and pie in 'orderable'.\n- Lin (likes risotto and pie) – allows paneer and custard in 'orderable'.\n- Ivy (likes risotto) – allows paneer, pie and custard in 'orderable'.\n- Naina (likes risotto) – allows paneer, pie and custard in 'orderable'.\n- Bo (likes risotto) – allows paneer, pie and custard in 'orderable'.\n- Ray (likes nachos and risotto) – allows paneer and pie in 'orderable'.\n- Shan (likes risotto, nachos, and pie) – allows paneer and custard in 'orderable'.\n\nLet's consider 'orderable: nachos, pie' and 'not_orderable: risotto', as nachos and pie are liked by several people but not the most disliked items, and look to see if all participants can be satisfied:\n\n- Individuals like Joy, Pam, Tom, Lin, Ivy, Naina, Bo, Ray, and Shan can be satisfied by having risotto in 'not_orderable'.\n- Individuals like Ru, Ada, Veer, Qi, Mel, Gen, Sal, and Leo can be satisfied with risotto in 'not_orderable'.\n- Paneer cannot be ordered because it's liked by those disliking pie or nachos, and vice versa.\n\nHowever, custard presents a problem because it is liked by Sam, Zac, Vic, Jan, Sue, Sol, Fay, Sid, and Rob, who all dislike pie, meaning pie can't be orderable to satisfy those individuals, which means custard must be 'orderable' to satisfy these individuals.\n\nSo, if we put custard in 'orderable', we cannot have pie there due to conflicts. But if we put nachos in 'orderable', no individual dislikes nachos and none of their liked items are threatened; hence nachos is unanimous and can be 'orderable'.\n\nGiven custard and nachos in 'orderable', those satisfied include: Sam, Zac, Vic, Jan, Sue, Sol, Fay, Sid, Rob, and all individuals who like nachos. Everyone left, who dislikes risotto, can be satisfied with it being 'not_orderable', and by ensuring paneer is also 'not_orderable', we cater to all others who disliked paneer.\n\nThe lists look like this:\n- orderable: custard, nachos\n- not_orderable: risotto, paneer\n\nNow we check for each individual:\n- Custard satisfies Sam, Zac, Vic, Jan, Sue, Sol, Fay, Sid, Rob.\n- Nachos satisfies Qi, Mel, Joy, Tao, Feng, Liz, Shan, Ray, Zoe, Aarav, Joy, Aarav.\n- Not having risotto satisfies Ru, Ada, Veer, Gen, Leo, Jon, Neil, Sal, Geet, etc.\n- Not having paneer satisfies most of the others, given the presence of custard or nachos in 'orderable' for the likes of individuals whose dislikes include paneer.\n\nThis configuration seems to satisfy every individual's preferences according to the given criteria, and no individual is left unsatisfied, as either an item they like is 'orderable' or an item they dislike is 'not_orderable'.\n\nHence, the final solution for the lists is:\n\n`orderable = ['custard', 'nachos']`\n`not_orderable = ['risotto', 'paneer']`", 'num_prompt_tokens': 785, 'num_completion_tokens': 1388}
Data Sample: {'num_vars': 7, 'num_clauses': 28, 'formula': [[5, 2, -3], [-7, -1, 5], [6, -3, 7], [-3, -4, -2], [-5, -3, 7], [5, -6, 1], [-3, -4, -7], [-7, 5, 1], [-1, 5, -7], [-6, 1, 5], [6, 4, -7], [-5, 2, 3], [-6, -5, 3], [-7, -4, -3], [-5, 3, 4], [5, -7, 4], [7, 6, -2], [4, 7, 6], [7, -3, -4], [7, -6, 5], [1, -2, -3], [6, -1, -3], [5, -3, 2], [-6, 7, 4], [-5, 6, -3], [2, 6, 4], [-3, -5, -4], [-5, -3, 1]], 'is_sat': True, 'preferences': 'Neil: Likes omelette, macaron. Dislikes curry. Pam: Likes omelette. Dislikes hummus, doughnut. Nic: Likes waffles, hummus. Dislikes curry. Zoe: Dislikes curry, burger, macaron. Meg: Likes hummus. Dislikes omelette, curry. Lei: Likes omelette, doughnut. Dislikes waffles. Ray: Dislikes curry, burger, hummus. Veer: Likes omelette, doughnut. Dislikes hummus. Kit: Likes omelette. Dislikes doughnut, hummus. Qi: Likes doughnut, omelette. Dislikes waffles. Zan: Likes waffles, burger. Dislikes hummus. Tom: Likes macaron, curry. Dislikes omelette. Shan: Likes curry. Dislikes waffles, omelette. Ben: Dislikes hummus, burger, curry. Gen: Likes curry, burger. Dislikes omelette. Amy: Likes omelette, burger. Dislikes hummus. Bo: Likes hummus, waffles. Dislikes macaron. Gus: Likes burger, hummus, waffles. Min: Likes hummus. Dislikes curry, burger. Jun: Likes hummus, omelette. Dislikes waffles. May: Likes doughnut. Dislikes macaron, curry. Tara: Likes waffles. Dislikes doughnut, curry. Zara: Likes omelette, macaron. Dislikes curry. Dan: Likes hummus, burger. Dislikes waffles. Oli: Likes waffles. Dislikes omelette, curry. Sol: Likes macaron, waffles, burger. Yan: Dislikes curry, omelette, burger. Riya: Likes doughnut. Dislikes omelette, curry. ', 'menu_items': ['doughnut', 'macaron', 'curry', 'burger', 'omelette', 'waffles', 'hummus'], 'gpt_out': 'To begin, let\'s first find the items that should be in the \'not_orderable\' list because they are disliked by multiple people. For an item to be "safe" to put on this list, it must be disliked by at least one person and not liked by anyone else. Next, we\'ll look for items to put in the \'orderable\' list. These would be the items liked by several people but disliked by none. We have to ensure that each person is satisfied by either list, according to our satisfaction criteria.\n\nLet\'s start by listing out the likes and dislikes, tallying each item:\n\nLikes:\n- Omelette: Neil, Pam, Kit, Lei, Veer, Amy, Zara, Qi, Jun\n- Macaron: Neil, Zara, Tom, Sol\n- Waffles: Nic, Zan, Tara, Gus, Sol, Bo, Oli\n- Hummus: Meg, Nic, Bo, Dan, Gus, Jun, Min\n- Doughnut: Lei, Veer, May, Riya, Qi\n- Burger: Zan, Amy, Sol, Gen, Dan, Gus\n- Curry: Tom, Shan, Gen\n\nDislikes:\n- Curry: Neil, Nic, Meg, Ben, Yan, Riya, May, Zara, Oli, Sol\n- Hummus: Pam, Ray, Veer, Kit, Zan, Ben, Amy\n- Doughnut: Pam, Kit\n- Burger: Zoe, Ray, Min, Yan\n- Macaron: Zoe, May, Bo\n- Omelette: Meg, Tom, Shan, Yan, Riya\n- Waffles: Lei, Shan, Jun, Dan, Qi, Tara\n\nNext, let\'s identify the items to put on the \'not_orderable\' list by looking for items that are disliked by multiple people and are not liked by anyone. Curry seems like a good candidate to be \'not_orderable\'; every person who likes curry is outnumbered by people who dislike it. No one likes curry who exclusively likes curry, so we can satisfy these people with other likes. Let\'s mark curry as \'not_orderable\'.\n\nNow, let\'s consider the \'orderable\' list. To make this task simpler, if one person exclusively likes one food (no one else likes it or dislikes it), that food goes into the \'orderable\' list. Here, no such food item exists; thus, we cannot automatically place any food item into the \'orderable\' list.\n\nWe need to ensure that at least one liked item for each person (who hasn’t been satisfied by the \'not_orderable\' list with curry) is on the \'orderable\' list or ensure that least one disliked food is on the \'not_orderable\' list.\n\nHere is a safer approach:\n\n1. Add Curry to the not_orderable list because it is disliked by the most number of people and some who like curry also like something else except Shan and Gen, but there were fewer such and we can satisfy them with other liked items. This satisfies: Neil, Nic, Meg, Ben, Yan, Riya, May, Zara, Oli, and Sol. (Curry is a no-go for 10 of our people)\n\nWe should next look for items to add to the \'orderable\' list, paying attention to ensure everyone who was not satisfied by curry being \'not_orderable\' is satisfied:\n\n2. Omelette is liked by most people, and it doesn\'t appear as a disliked item as many times as Hummus or Waffles. We will add Omelette to the \'orderable\' list unless someone exclusively dislikes Omelette and nothing else, which will make this person not satisfied by any combination. Shan likes curry and dislikes Omelette, but he dislikes waffles as well, which is liked by some other people, so we can still consider Omelette for \'orderable\'. Tom is satisfied already because he likes Macaron. Meg is not satisfied because she dislikes Omelette, but she can be satisfied if we add Hummus to \'orderable\', which she likes. Yan dislikes Omelette, but he dislikes Burger too, which is liked by others. Riya and Oli exclusively dislike Omelette without any other dislikes, but they like doughnut and waffles, respectively, so they can potentially be satisfied if these items are added to the \'orderable\' list. So adding Omelette to \'orderable\' is so far possible.\n\n3. Hummus seems to be a good candidate for \'orderable\' too, as it is liked by a number of people, while those who dislike it mostly have other dislikes too, except Ray. However, Ray can be satisfied by not having Burger or Omelette in \'orderable\', and since Omelette is already added, he is satisfied. Meg, who dislikes Omelette, will also be satisfied if we add Hummus to \'orderable\'. Nic, Jun, Gus, Bo, and the rest of the individuals who like Hummus are now satisfied since nobody is exclusively liking Hummus only. \n\nNow we have to ensure everyone is still satisfied, and especially the ones who don\'t like the items we\'ve just put in \'orderable\':\n\n- Shan needed curry, but since we\'ve added Omelette to \'orderable\', Shan is satisfied because of not having Waffles in \'orderable\'.\n\n- Meg is now satisfied by having Hummus \'orderable\' and is not bothered by Omelette being \'orderable\'.\n\n- Ray is already satisfied with curry being \'not_orderable\'. \n\n- Riya and Oli are satisfied by having Doughnut and Waffles, respectively, as their exclusive likes. We need to make sure that one of these two items (Doughnut or Waffles) goes into the \'orderable\' list next.\n\n4. Upon reviewing Waffles and Doughnut for our next \'orderable\' candidate, we see that Waffles is preferred by more people who are exclusively connected to it. Doughnut is liked by some people but is also disliked by some who do not have it on their \'dislike\' list and like something else (like Pam and Kit who like Omelette). \n\n5. Adding Waffles to \'orderable\' now ensures Oli and those who exclusively like Waffles are satisfied, and anyone who dislikes Waffles was already satisfied either by \'not_orderable\' or \'orderable\' items already on one of the lists.\n\nLet us double-check for any conflicts:\n\n- Anyone who likes Doughnut (Lei, Veer, Qi, May, Riya) must not dislike both Omelette and Waffles; they don\'t.\n- Anyone who likes Burger must not dislike both Omelette and Waffles; they don\'t. Additionally, Burger is disliked by Yan, and excluding it from \'orderable\' satisfies him.\n\nUpon review, everyone is now satisfied according to the criteria. Therefore, we can confidently declare the finalized lists:\n\nOrderable: Omelette, Hummus, Waffles\nNot Orderable: Curry\n\nAny other items not listed can be ignored because they are not needed in order to meet the satisfaction criteria for each person.\n\nSo our final output is:\n\n```python\norderable=[\'Omelette\', \'Hummus\', \'Waffles\'], not_orderable=[\'Curry\']\n```', 'num_prompt_tokens': 570, 'num_completion_tokens': 1510}
