n 'orderable', then hummus, pie, espresso, and ravioli could potentially be in 'not_orderable', as all individuals that dislike poke dislike at least one of these. Let's verify this:\n\n- Yan dislikes hummus and ravioli - we could put one of them in 'not_orderable'.\n- Taj dislikes hummus - which can go in 'not_orderable'.\n- Ula dislikes espresso - which can go in 'not_orderable'.\n- Ru dislikes poke - hummus is liked, so no help there.\n- Kit dislikes hummus - which can go in 'not_orderable'.\n- Ron dislikes hummus - which can go in 'not_orderable'.\n- Cy dislikes ravioli - which can go in 'not_orderable'.\n- Mel likes all three of ravioli, espresso, and poke, so we can't satisfy Mel if poke is 'orderable'.\n- Ansh dislikes poke - hummus is liked, so can't put that in 'not_orderable'.\n\nAlready, we are unable to satisfy Mel if poke is 'orderable', as they like all other items. This means poke can't be on the 'orderable' list.\n\nSince poke was the most liked item and can't satisfy everyone, we must check if pie can be on the 'orderable' list:\n\n- If pie is in 'orderable', let's check the individuals who dislike pie:\n\n- Rohan dislikes ravioli and hummus apart from pie, so we could put one of these in 'not_orderable'.\n- Taj dislikes hummus - which can go in 'not_orderable'.\n- Ula dislikes espresso - which can go in 'not_orderable'.\n- Vic dislikes ravioli and espresso - which can go in 'not_orderable'.\n- Ping dislikes hummus - which can go in 'not_orderable'.\n- Tia dislikes hummus - which can go in 'not_orderable'.\n- Ron dislikes hummus - which can go in 'not_orderable'.\n- Bo dislikes espresso - which can go in 'not_orderable'.\n- Val dislikes hummus - which can go in 'not_orderable'.\n\nSince putting pie in 'orderable' does not conflict with any individual's preferences (as they all have a distinct dislike from the remainders), we can satisfy everyone by placing it there.\n\nTherefore, let's place pie in 'orderable' and determine others:\n\n- For those who dislike pie, we can place one of their dislikes in 'not_orderable'. Espresso and hummus appear as common dislikes, so we can choose either.\n\nSince we have fewer people disliking espresso than hummus, we will put espresso in 'not_orderable' and verify once more:\n\n- All individuals who dislike pie dislike espresso, so they are satisfied.\n- Individuals who like pie are satisfied by it being in 'orderable'.\n\nNow we have:\norderable = [pie]\nnot_orderable = [espresso]\n\nLet's check if everyone is satisfied with such lists:\n- Anyone who likes pie (and there are many) is satisfied with it in 'orderable'.\n- Anyone who dislikes pie is satisfied by espresso being in 'not_orderable'.\n- Those who have not been mentioned yet either like pie or dislike espresso or have their dislikes not conflicting with the 'orderable' item, so they are satisfied.\n\nEveryone can be satisfied by pie being 'orderable' and espresso being 'not_orderable'. Thus, our lists will be:\n\norderable = [pie]\nnot_orderable = [espresso]", 'num_prompt_tokens': 762, 'num_completion_tokens': 1073}
