ndoori (since it's commonly disliked) and do not have any other likes that would conflict with this arrangement.\n\nSo, our initial solution could be:\n- 'orderable': hummus\n- 'not_orderable': tandoori\n\nThis would satisfy every person except Bo, Ying, Raj, Ana, Lei, Val, Zoe, and Cy, who like tandoori. However, looking at their preferences:\n- Bo, Raj, Zoe, and Cy would be satisfied with hummus being orderable.\n- Lei likes all three, so Lei is satisfied with hummus being orderable.\n- Ana also likes caramel, so she would be satisfied with hummus on the orderable list.\n- Ying is the only one who likes tandoori and dislikes the other two, so Ying would not be satisfied with this condition.\n\nSince Ying cannot be satisfied with the current lists since her liked item is on the 'not_orderable' list and she dislikes the 'orderable' item, we won't be able to satisfy everyone according to the posted rules.\n\nThus, our output will be two empty lists:\n\n```python\norderable = [], not_orderable = []\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 483, 'num_completion_tokens': 883}
