es tacos. Dislikes tiramisu, bruschetta. ', 'menu_items': ['pancakes', 'tacos', 'bruschetta', 'tiramisu'], 'gpt_out': "Let's compile the information given about each person to understand the overall group preferences for the food items tacos, bruschetta, tiramisu, and pancakes.\n\n### Tacos\n- Likes: Eva, Ron, Oli, Ivy, Raj, Xiu, Geet, Lan, Ping, Geet\n- Dislikes: Hui, Du, Tara, Vic, Isha, Sue, Rohan, Wei, Gus, Wes\n\n### Bruschetta\n- Likes: Jon, Tara, Vic, Isha, Oli, Chao, Ana, Joy, Zara, Wei, Ying, Li\n- Dislikes: Eva, Kit, Hui, Ron, Ivy, Raj, Xiu, Taj, Hal, Hu, Gus, Ping, Sue, Wes\n\n### Tiramisu\n- Likes: Hui, Jon, Dan, Chao, Taj, Hal, Rohan, Zara, Lan\n- Dislikes: Eva, Kit, Du, Tara, Vic, Sue, Jim, Hu, Wei, Ping\n\n### Pancakes\n- Likes: Dan, Ivy, Raj, Ana, Jim, Joy, Rohan, Li, Lan\n- Dislikes: Kit, Du, Jon, Chao, Isha, Sue, Taj, Hal, Hu, Xiu, Gus, Wes, Ying\n\nFor an item to be orderable, it must at least be liked by one and not disliked by any; similarly, for an item to be not_orderable, it must be disliked by at least one and not liked by any individual.\n\nHowever, since the individual preferences are complex, and each food item is both liked and disliked by different people, we cannot use the above simple filter. Instead, we must satisfy the criterion that each person is okay with at least one orderable item or at least one item on the not_orderable list.\n\nGiven that more people dislike pancakes than any other single item, let's consider pancakes for the not_orderable list and see if a combination exists that satisfies everyone. \n\n- No person only likes pancakes and dislikes every other item, so it is safe to move pancakes to not_orderable.\n- Bruschetta seems to be the most liked, with a number of people only disliking pancakes, so let's move it to orderable.\n\nNow let's check if everyone is satisfied with pancakes on not_orderable and bruschetta as orderable:\n\n- Eva, Hui, Jon, Kit, Du, Oli, Ron, Tara, Vic, Ping prefer either bruschetta or dislike