nd tandoori can be not_orderable.\n\n6. Jon likes frittata, ravioli and dislikes naan.\n   We cannot put frittata or naan on the not orderable list because they are liked by Jon or others.\n\n7. Pia likes frittata and dislikes bruschetta, ravioli.\n   This reinforces frittata for orderable, and tandoori or ravioli for not_orderable.\n\n8. Om likes frittata, tandoori and dislikes fries.\n   This reinforces frittata for orderable but leaves room for tandoori for not_orderable since he likes it, and fries for not_orderable. \n\n9. Ron likes shrimps, bruschetta and dislikes naan.\n   Shrimps can be orderable, and naan cannot be on the not_orderable list.\n\n10. Zara likes shrimps and dislikes bruschetta, ravioli.\n    This reinforces shrimps for orderable, and bruschetta or ravioli for not_orderable.\n\n11. Bea likes bruschetta, fries and dislikes tandoori.\n    This rules out tandoori and bruschetta from orderable but brings in fries as a potential orderable item since she likes it.\n\n12. Leo likes tandoori and dislikes frittata, shrimps.\n    Tandoori could be orderable, as Leo likes it, but it is disliked by multiple people.\n\n13. Vic likes naan, frittata and dislikes bruschetta.\n    Naan and frittata already are potential orderable items.\n\n14. Val dislikes naan, bruschetta, tandoori.\n    This confirms naan and frittata for orderable, as Val disliking naan makes it unsuitable for the not_orderable list.\n\n15. Ula likes bruschetta and dislikes tandoori, shrimps.\n    This rules out bruschetta, tandoori, and shrimps from our orderable list since she dislikes some of the items that others like. \n\nSummary so far:\n- Potential orderable items: frittata, shrimps, naan, fries.\n- Potential not_orderable items: bruschetta, tandoori, ravioli.\n\nLet's verify once more if the potential orderable items would satisfy everyone:\n- frittata is liked by Shan, Hal, Jon, Pia, Om, Vic. No one dislikes frittata.\n- shrimps is liked by Ron, Zara, but disliked by Leo and Ula. So, shrimps cannot be orderable.\n- naan is liked by Eli, Ron, Vic, but disliked by Jon, Ron, Val. So, naan cannot be orderable.\n- fries is liked by Bea, but disliked by Dan and Om. So, fries also cannot be orderable.\n\nSo far, we have that frittata is our only confirmed orderable item.\n\nNext, let's check if we can find suitable not_orderable items that would satisfy everyone:\n- bruschetta is liked by Dan, Hal, Ron, Bea, Ula, but disliked by Eli, Pia, Vic, Zara, Val. \n- tandoori is liked by Hal, Om, Leo, but disliked by Shan, Eli, Bea, Ula, Val. \n- ravioli is liked by Dan, Jon, but disliked by Shan, Pia, Zara.\n\nSince there are no dislikes for frittata, there's no conflict, and it must be orderable to satisfy Shan, Hal, Jon, Pia, Om, and Vic.\n\nTo include an item on the not_orderable list, we cannot select an item that any person likes without that person also having another item that person likes on the orderable list or a disliked item on the not_orderable list.\n\n- ravioli cannot be on the orderable list as Shan and Pia dislike it, but Dan and Jon like it. However, Dan and Jon would be satisfied by bruschetta being orderable, so ravioli can be on the not_orderable list.\n- tandoori is liked by Hal, Om, Leo but disliked by multiple people, and since those who like it are already satisfied by frittata being orderable, we can have tandoori on the not_orderable list.\n\nThus, we can satisfy all participants by having:\n\n- frittata on the orderable list.\n- ravioli and tandoori on the not_orderable list.\n\nHere are the final lists:\n\norderable = ['frittata']\nnot_orderable = ['ravioli', 'tandoori']\n\nOutputting the result in the Python code format:\n```\norderable=['frittata'], not_orderable=['ravioli', 'tandoori']\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 420, 'num_completion_tokens': 1231}
