ed item on the 'orderable' list or one disliked item on the 'not_orderable' list without having the same item in both.\n\nLet's categorize these items into three groups:\n\n- Items that are liked by some and not disliked by any: These can go into 'orderable'.\n- Items that are disliked by some and not liked by any: These can go into 'not_orderable'.\n- Items that are both liked and disliked by different members: These require careful consideration to ensure that all are satisfied.\n\nNow, let's analyze the preferences:\n- No one dislikes falafel and it is liked by Pam, Jun, Bea, Abe, and Joe, so we can add falafel to 'orderable'.\n- No one likes paneer and it is disliked by Jun, Abe, and Ned, so we can add paneer to 'not_orderable'.\n- Everyone who likes guacamole also likes another item already orderable (falafel). Guacamole is the most disliked item, so we can safely add guacamole to 'not_orderable'. Taj, who likes salad and dislikes guacamole, has another disliked item in the 'not_orderable'.\n- Salad is only liked by Taj but disliked by Tia, Zoe, Geet, Ron, and Ned. Tah's preferences are covered, so we can add salad to the 'not_orderable'.\n- Baklava is liked by Bea and disliked by Max, Geet, and Ned; Bea's preferences are covered by falafel in the 'orderable' list so we can add baklava to 'not_orderable'.\n- Biscuits are liked by Tia, Joe, and Sue but disliked by Bea, Zoe, and Li. However, everyone who likes biscuits has another liked item already available (falafel for Joe, guacamole for Sue), therefore biscuits can be in 'not_orderable'.\n- Bruschetta is liked by Jun, Zoe, Max, Sue, and Li but disliked by Pam, Taj, and Ron. Everyone who likes bruschetta also has a different liked item available (falafel for Jun, guacamole for Sue), so we can add bruschetta to 'not_orderable'.\n\nBased on the analysis above, we can deduce the following 'orderable' and 'not_orderable' lists:\n\norderable = [falafel]\nnot_orderable = [paneer, guacamole, salad, baklava, biscuits,