er, this means we need to also satisfy Zoe, Dan, and Yan in other ways.\n\nTo satisfy Zoe, we cannot use nachos or couscous since other people like them, and Zoe does not like “spaghetti” or “pancakes,” which we\'ve added to the \'orderable\' list. We need to find a dislike to put in the \'not_orderable\' list.\n\nSince Zoe also dislikes "couscous”, we can consider putting it in the \'not_orderable\' list. But doing this would leave Sia, Rina, Dan, and Hui unsatisfied because it is their only like mentioned that could be orderable.\n\nLooking at the remaining likes and dislikes, we cannot resolve the preferences of everyone without conflict, as any additional items placed into either list will create a situation where someone\'s likes and dislikes are both in the same list, or they are left without any of their liked items in the \'orderable\' list.\n\nThis means that there is no combination that satisfies all without conflict, and therefore, both \'orderable\' and \'not_orderable\' lists should be empty.\n\nOutput:\n```\norderable=[], not_orderable=[]\n```', 'num_prompt_tokens': 432, 'num_completion_tokens': 993}
