nies on the 'not_orderable' list and lobster on the 'orderable' list, and then proceed from there. Now let's see if we can add cheese and tart. Cheese is liked by many, but a few people are satisfied only by not having cheese; similarly for tart.\n\nNow, I will move to the next step by making a decision on other items, such as cheese, wonton, tart, and poke, which are discussed heavily:\n\n- Choose cheese to be placed on 'orderable' because many like cheese, and only a few dislike it as their sole dislike.\n- Wonton should also be 'orderable' because Joy, who exclusively dislikes it, has another like which can be satisfied by pie.\n- Tart will be 'not_orderable' because multiple people exclusively dislike it as the only alternative, like Min, who can be satisfied with lobster.\n\nFinally, we need to make sure that someone who likes poke can also be satisfied since we've identified that it has to go on 'not_orderable' due to the high number of dislikes. Most poke likers have alternative likes:\n\n- Mina's other likes are already covered or are 'not_orderable'.\n- Kit and Lou both like tart, but tart is 'not_orderable'. So we can check one of their other likes: ramen. Since Ansh is the only one whose satisfaction is based only on not having ramen, and he has other dislikes (lobster and tart), we can consider ramen to be 'orderable'.\n- Lin also likes poke but dislikes cheese and brownies. Since cheese is 'orderable', this satisfies Lin's requirements.\n\nAfter considering all these options, we now construct the lists:\n\n'not_orderable': brownies, tart, poke\n'orderable': lobster, cheese, wonton, ramen\n\nSome cases might be overlooked, but for now, here is the conclusion:\n\n```python\norderable=['lobster', 'cheese', 'wonton', 'ramen'], not_orderable=['brownies', 'tart', 'poke']\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 772, 'num_completion_tokens': 1151}
