e' list, but not both.\n\n5. If a conflict arises that cannot be resolved to satisfy everyone, output empty lists.\n\nLet's analyze the individuals' preferences:\n\n1. No one likes and dislikes the same food, so there's no obvious initial assignment.\n\n2. Dislikes that are common amongst all who dislike a particular food:\n   - Quiche is disliked by everyone who has mentioned it except Diya, Meg, Jim, Xiu, Ben, Yan, and Oli who like it. We cannot directly place it in 'not_orderable', as it would dissatisfy these individuals.\n   - Shawarma is disliked by everyone who has mentioned it except Sue, Tao, Vic, Liv, Zoe, Isha, Tara, Ada, and Val who like it. So it cannot directly be in 'not_orderable'.\n   - Roti and sushi similarly have people who both like and dislike them, so we can’t place them directly either.\n   - However, lobster is only liked by individuals who have something else they like. For instance, Hui dislikes lobster but likes sushi. This is true for everyone who likes lobster. So, 'lobster' can be placed in the 'not_orderable' list, satisfying everyone who dislikes it, and the people who like it will be satisfied by other foods they like.\n\n3. Based on the findings above, let's propose 'lobster' for 'not_orderable':\n\nSo far we have:\nnot_orderable = ['lobster']\n\n4. To determine the 'orderable' list, let's check for any food items that are liked by individuals but not disliked by others. We can see that 'burrito' is liked by at least one individual who dislikes everything else on their list. Therefore, it is a necessary item to include in 'orderable':\n\nCurrent proposal:\norderable = ['burrito']\n\n5. Finally, confirm that for each person, at least one liked item is in 'orderable', or a disliked item is in 'not_orderable'. \n\nSince 'burrito' is in 'orderable', it will satisfy:\n- Aarav, Ava, Joe, Lan, Rohan, Ken, Nic, Diya, Eli, Ron, Ula, Ray, Mel, Pia, Ben, Sky, Ansh, Xiu, Meg, and Yan.\n\n'Lobster' is in 'not_orderable', satisfying:\n- Hui, Du,