ot_orderable\':\n\n- Liz, Ansh, Dan, Li, Eva, Jay, Pat, Mel, Nita, Hu, Ping, Leo, Kai, and Mel are satisfied by "cheesecake" being \'orderable\'.\n- Vic, Sky, Lee, Ula, Hal, Tao, Veer, Ru, Gen, Liv, Feng, Jon, Lan, Jim, Sia, Zan, Sam, Amy, and any others who said they dislike "wonton" are satisfied by it being \'not_orderable\'.\n- Since "pie" and "espresso" are not placed in any list yet, those who like these items are not satisfied.\n- People like Raj, who likes "espresso" and dislikes both "wonton" and "cheesecake", are not satisfied by the current combination.\n\nTherefore, the "cheesecake" \'orderable\' and "wonton" \'not_orderable\' combo does not satisfy everyone, particularly those who like "pie" or "espresso" but dislike "wonton" and "cheesecake".\n\nTrying to place "espresso" or "pie" on any lists is impossible because it is disliked by someone who does not dislike "wonton" or like "cheesecake".\n\nGiven these constraints, it is impossible to create a set of \'orderable\' and \'not_orderable\' items that leaves all participants satisfied based on the provided likes and dislikes. There are conflicting preferences making any combination that satisfies one person dissatisfies another. Therefore, we must return an empty list for both \'orderable\' and \'not_orderable\':\n\n```python\norderable = []\nnot_orderable = []\n```', 'num_prompt_tokens': 895, 'num_completion_tokens': 1008}
