: Veer, Lan, Jaya, Hu, Jon, Eli\n- Roti: Jim, Chao, Eli, Yan\n- Macaron: Jun, Hu\n\nItems that are disliked by at least one person and not liked by anyone else can directly go into `not_orderable`. Items that are liked but not disliked by anyone can directly go into `orderable`.\n\n**Directly 'not_orderable':**\n- Roti (disliked by Jim, Chao, Eli, Yan and liked by nobody else).\n\n**Directly 'orderable':**\n- Churros (liked by Sue, Tim, Rina and disliked by nobody else).\n\nNow that we have items that can be directly categorized, let's check the remaining items against each participant's preferences to see if they can be categorized into `orderable` and `not_orderable` lists.\n\n**Potential 'orderable':**\n- Doughnut – can be orderable if people with dislike have other items to satisfy them.\n- Baklava – can be orderable if people with dislike have other items to satisfy them.\n- Fries – can be orderable if people with dislike have other items to satisfy them.\n- Kimchi – can be orderable if people with dislike have other items to satisfy them.\n- Ratatouille – can be orderable if people with dislike have other items to satisfy them.\n- Macaron – can be orderable if people with dislike have other items to satisfy them.\n\n**Potential 'not_orderable':**\n- Pho – can be not_orderable if people with like have other items to satisfy them.\n- Hummus – can be not_orderable if people with like have other items to satisfy them.\n\nWe categorize doughnut, baklava, fries, kimchi, ratatouille, and macaron based on whether satisfying the people who dislike them is possible through other 'orderable' or 'not_orderable' items.\n\nFor example, Cy dislikes baklava, fries, and kimchi. If any of these items are placed in the 'not_orderable' list, Cy will be satisfied. Let's start by satisfying as many individuals with only one like if possible:\n\n- Pho is liked by several people; let's go with the assumption that it will end up in the 'orderable' list and see if everyone who dislikes