on: Liked by Bao, Kit, Eli, Gen\n- Nachos: Liked by Ian, Jun\n- Sushi: Liked by Xiu, Zara\n- Fries: Liked by Bao, Cy, Eli, Yan, Gen\n\nDislikes:\n- Salmon: Disliked by Lex, Taj, Jun, Sal, Kit, Chao, Fay\n- Sushi: Disliked by Taj, Liz, Tom, Sue, Jon, Sal, Anil, Chao, Fay, Zara, Cy\n- Soup: Disliked by Liz, Pat, Jaya, Zara\n- Nachos: Disliked by Liz, Cy, Chao\n- Gelato: Disliked by Ian, Fay, Yan, Gen\n- Pancakes: Disliked by Sue, Jaya, Max, Yan\n- Vindaloo: Disliked by Pat, Tom, Xiu, Zara, Kit\n- Fries: Disliked by Xiu\n- Gelato: Disliked by Ian, Fay, Yan, Gen\n\nFrom the given preferences, we can identify certain trends:\n\n1. Sushi is disliked by the most number of people (11). No one's satisfaction depends on sushi being orderable, so it definitely should be in the 'not_orderable' list to satisfy those 11 people.\n   \n2. Soup is liked by 5 people but disliked by 4. We can't place soup in either list without making someone dissatisfied since for any person who likes soup, there's another who dislikes it.\n   \n3. Salmon is liked by 4 and disliked by 7. Similarly to sushi, more people dislike it than like it, and no one's satisfaction depends on it being available, so we put salmon in 'not_orderable'.\n   \n4. Pancakes, vindaloo, and gelato each have their likes and dislikes, but placing them in 'orderable' or 'not_orderable' might affect multiple persons.\n   \n5. Fries are liked by many (5) and only disliked by 1 (Xiu). Since Xiu also dislikes salmon which is already in 'not_orderable', we can satisfy Xiu without considering fries. Thus, we can put fries in the 'orderable' list to satisfy those 5 people who like it.\n\nLet's proceed to find an optimal list based on these observations.\n\nFirst, we add sushi and salmon to 'not_orderable', and fries to 'orderable':\n\nnot_orderable = [sushi, salmon]\norderable = [fries]\n\nNow, let's check the remaining items:\n\n- Vindaloo is liked by 4 people. Disliking vindaloo would be satisfied either by Pat (who likes gelato, 