d \'not_orderable\' without any direct overlap, we should consider the following pattern to potentially satisfy everyone\'s conditions:\n\n- The most frequently liked items are Gelato, Biscuits, Muffins, and Bread. Since they are also disliked by a few, we must ensure their presence or absence doesn\'t conflict.\n- We can observe that people who dislike Gelato, Biscuits, and Muffins always seem to like at least one of Bread, Poke, or Quiche and vice versa, which gives us options to satisfy them.\n- To complicate matters, nobody dislikes both Bread and Poke, so we can\'t put both of these on the same list.\n\nTrying different combinations, we find it difficult to list any food item that will satisfy everyone due to conflicts in likes and dislikes. For example, placing "Gelato" in \'orderable\' will satisfy many but will also dissatisfy many who dislike it and don\'t have their other dislikes to rely on. The conflicts continue as such with every item.\n\nAfter trying several combinations and patterns, it looks like it’s not possible to create \'orderable\' and \'not_orderable\' lists that will satisfy everyone, since there are individuals with conflicting absolute needs, which means that this puzzle has no solution.\n\nTherefore, our outcome is:\n```python\norderable=[], not_orderable=[]\n```', 'num_prompt_tokens': 1013, 'num_completion_tokens': 733}
