st, spaghetti. Dislikes paneer. Yan: Dislikes soup, paneer, macaron. Mia: Likes paneer, spaghetti. Dislikes soup. Li: Likes toast, paneer, falafel. Tao: Likes soup. Dislikes toast, falafel. Nic: Likes paneer, macaron, toast. Neil: Likes falafel. Dislikes toast, soup. Zara: Likes paneer. Dislikes macaron, soup. Ken: Likes spaghetti, paneer. Dislikes falafel. Kavi: Likes falafel. Dislikes spaghetti, macaron. Ping: Likes macaron. Dislikes soup, paneer. Val: Likes paneer, spaghetti, toast. Cal: Likes toast. Dislikes macaron, soup. Mel: Likes falafel, paneer. Dislikes macaron. Riya: Dislikes toast, macaron, paneer. Yue: Likes spaghetti. Dislikes toast, paneer. Zoe: Likes soup. Dislikes paneer, spaghetti. Ada: Likes falafel, toast. Dislikes spaghetti. Eli: Likes soup. Dislikes spaghetti, macaron. Lia: Likes soup, falafel. Dislikes spaghetti. Ian: Dislikes paneer, toast, falafel. Naina: Likes soup, paneer. Dislikes spaghetti. Ansh: Likes falafel, toast. Dislikes spaghetti. Liz: Dislikes spaghetti, macaron, toast. Joy: Likes falafel. Dislikes spaghetti, macaron. ', 'menu_items': ['falafel', 'soup', 'paneer', 'toast', 'spaghetti', 'macaron'], 'gpt_out': "First, let's compile the likes and dislikes of every individual into two sets for easier visualization and examination.\n\nLiked foods:\n- paneer: Ying, Ivy, Gus, Chao, Max, Ty, Ron, Li, Nic, Zara, Ken, Mel, Val, Naina\n- toast: Ying, Tim, Gus, Lin, Mina, Kim, Ron, Diya, Li, Nic, Cal, Ada, Ansh\n- spaghetti: Ying, Rohan, Kai, Ned, Mia, Yue, Val\n- soup: Rohan, Mei, Sol, Max, Xan, Tao, Geet, Ava, Zoe, Lia, Naina\n- macaron: Rohan, Shan, Chao, Zac, Veer, Ty, Ava, Nic, Ping\n- falafel: Tim, Ivy, Gus, Mina, Veer, Sol, Kavi, Mel, Ada, Ansh, Joy\n\nDisliked foods:\n- macaron: Tim, Ben, Mina, Max, Mei, Kavi, Mel, Riya, Eli, Joy, Liz\n- spaghetti: Shan, Lin, Jay, Zac, Ty, Ned, Zoe, Lia, Naina, Ansh, Liz\n- paneer: Shan, Taj, Mei, Jim, Yan, Ping, Riya, Yue, Zoe, Ian\n- toast: Taj, Mei, Jay, Zac, Jim, Ava, Hal, Riya, Tao, Liz\n- falafel: Taj, Lin, Jay, Ben, Xan, Hal, Ian, Ken, Kavi, Neil\n- soup: Ivy, Chao, Ben, Jim, Yan, Ron, Val, Nic, Cal, Zara, Ken\n\nNow let's identify mandatory items for 'not_orderable' list based on dislikes. Here are the mandatory 'not_orderable' items based on individuals who only have dislikes:\n\n1. Ava dislikes toast - toast must be 'not_orderable'.\n2. Ben dislikes falafel - falafel must be 'not_orderable'.\n3. Jim dislikes soup - soup must be 'not_orderable'.\n4. Liz dislikes spaghetti - spaghetti must be 'not_orderable'.\n5. Riya dislikes paneer - paneer must be 'not_orderable'.\n\nNext, we need to check whether placing these items on 'not_orderable' list makes everyone satisfied or forces any unsolvable conflicts.\n\nUpon placing toast, falafel, soup, spaghetti, and paneer in the 'not_orderable' list, we have to check if any individual is left unsatisfied:\n\n- Individuals who like toast may be unsatisfied since it's 'not_orderable'. We need to ensure at least one of their other likes is 'orderable' or have another disliked item on the 'not_orderable' list. Same logic goes for other items.\n- Individuals who dislike toast, falafel, soup, spaghetti, and paneer are already satisfied by the chosen 'not_orderable' list.\n\nExamining the lists, we hit a point of conflict.\n- Tao likes soup (now not_orderable), dislikes toast and falafel (both now also not_orderable). Impossible to satisfy since all liked and disliked foods are not orderable.\n- Those exclusively liking toast will also be unsatisfied due to toast being 'not_orderable', while soup and falafel are also 'not_orderable', and those are items commonly liked by the same individuals liking toast.\n\nDue to these conflicts, no combination of 'orderable' and 'not_orderable' items can satisfy all participants based on the rules provided. Therefore, both lists must be empty.\n\nSo the solution is:\n```python\norderable = [], not_orderable = []\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 879, 'num_completion_tokens': 766}
Data Sample: {'num_vars': 4, 'num_clauses': 20, 'formula': [[4, 1, 3], [3, 1, -4], [3, -1, 4], [-1, -4, 3], [2, 3, 4], [1, -2, -4], [3, -4, 1], [2, -4, 1], [1, -2, 4], [-1, -3, -2], [1, -3, 2], [-4, 1, -3], [-1, 2, -3], [1, -4, -2], [-2, 3, 4], [-3, -2, 4], [1, -2, -3], [-3, 2, -1], [-2, 1, -3], [-3, -2, 1]], 'is_sat': False, 'preferences': 'Taj: Likes hummus, ramen, lasagna. Lei: Likes lasagna, ramen. Dislikes hummus. Ula: Likes lasagna, hummus. Dislikes ramen. Ben: Likes lasagna. Dislikes ramen, hummus. Ping: Likes cheese, lasagna, hummus. Jaya: Likes ramen. Dislikes cheese, hummus. Ana: Likes lasagna, ramen. Dislikes hummus. Naina: Likes cheese, ramen. Dislikes hummus. Lin: Likes ramen, hummus. Dislikes cheese. Joy: Dislikes ramen, lasagna, cheese. Abe: Likes ramen, cheese. Dislikes lasagna. Om: Likes ramen. Dislikes hummus, lasagna. Dot: Likes cheese. Dislikes ramen, lasagna. Fay: Likes ramen. Dislikes hummus, cheese. Xiu: Likes lasagna, hummus. Dislikes cheese. Veer: Likes hummus. Dislikes lasagna, cheese. Lia: Likes ramen. Dislikes cheese, lasagna. Xan: Likes cheese. Dislikes lasagna, ramen. Jon: Likes ramen. Dislikes cheese, lasagna. Kavi: Likes ramen. Dislikes lasagna, cheese. ', 'menu_items': ['ramen', 'cheese', 'lasagna', 'hummus'], 'gpt_out': 'To find a combination that satisfies all participants, we must consider each person\'s likes and dislikes. We will compile the likes and dislikes and analyze them to create \'orderable\' and \'not_orderable\' lists. Let\'s start by listing out likes and dislikes that are common:\n\nCommon likes:\n- Lasagna: Liked by Taj, Lei, Ula, Ben, Ping, Ana, Xiu.\n- Ramen: Liked by Taj, Lei, Jaya, Ana, Naina, Lin, Abe, Om, Fay, Lia, Jon, Kavi.\n- Hummus: Liked by Taj, Ula, Ping, Lin, Veer.\n- Cheese: Liked by Ping, Naina, Abe, Dot, Xan.\n\nCommon dislikes:\n- Ramen: Disliked by Ula, Ben, Joy, Dot, Fay.\n- Lasagna: Disliked by Joy, Abe, Om, Xan, Jon, Kavi.\n- Hummus: Disliked by Lei, Om, Fay.\n- Cheese: Disliked by Jaya, Lin, Joy, Veer, Lia.\n\nWe will consider the following rules to create our lists: \n1. A person must have at least one liked item in \'orderable\' or one disliked item in \'not_orderable\'.\n2. No item can appear on both lists.\n3. All participants must be satisfied.\n\nStep 1: Immediate Disqualifications\n- "Cheese" is disliked by persons who don\'t dislike any other item.\n- "Ramen" is liked by persons who don\'t like any other item.\n\nSo, "Cheese" must go to \'not_orderable\', and "Ramen" must go to \'orderable\'.\n\nStep 2: Addressing Conflicts\n- "Lasagna" is liked by many and only disliked by those who have other dislikes that could be satisfied. However, because "Ramen" is included in \'orderable\', we must add "Lasagna" to \'orderable\' to satisfy Ben.\n\nStep 3: Considering "Hummus" \n- Some participants like "Hummus" and others dislike it. All who dislike "Hummus" are satisfied with "Ramen" in \'orderable\' except for Joy who dislikes everything but is indifferent to "Hummus". We can satisfy Joy by putting "Hummus" in \'not_orderable\'.\n\nBy adding "Lasagna" to \'orderable\' and having "Hummus" in \'not_orderable\', we can satisfy everyone because:\n- Those who dislike "Hummus" will be satisfied by its absence from \'orderable\'.\n- Those who like "Lasagna" will be satisfied by its presence in \'orderable\'.\n- "Cheese" is in \'not_orderable\' satisfying those who dislike it.\n\nPresenting the final lists:\n\norderable = [\'Ramen\', \'Lasagna\'], not_orderable = [\'Cheese\', \'Hummus\']', 'num_prompt_tokens': 461, 'num_completion_tokens': 607}
Data Sample: {'num_vars': 5, 'num_clauses': 10, 'formula': [[-5, 2, 3], [1, -4, -3], [-5, 2, 4], [1, -4, -3], [-2, 3, -4], [-2, -3, 1], [-1, -2, 5], [4, -2, -3], [5, -1, -3], [1, -4, 3]], 'is_sat': True, 'preferences': 'Val: Likes paneer, spaghetti. Dislikes guacamole. Naina: Likes toast. Dislikes brownies, spaghetti. Dot: Likes paneer, brownies. Dislikes guacamole. Lin: Likes toast. Dislikes brownies, spaghetti. Veer: Likes spaghetti. Dislikes paneer, brownies. Jay: Likes toast. Dislikes paneer, spaghetti. Rob: Likes guacamole. Dislikes toast, paneer. Hu: Likes brownies. Dislikes paneer, spaghetti. Bao: Likes guacamole. Dislikes toast, spaghetti. Yue: Likes toast, spaghetti. Dislikes brownies. ', 'menu_items': ['toast', 'paneer', 'spaghetti', 'brownies', 'guacamole'], 'gpt_out': "To satisfy everyone's preferences, we must create two distinct lists: `orderable` and `not_orderable`. Let's go step by step, starting by noting everyone's preferences:\n\n1. Val likes paneer, spaghetti. Dislikes guacamole.\n2. Naina likes toast. Dislikes brownies, spaghetti.\n3. Dot likes paneer, brownies. Dislikes guacamole.\n4. Lin likes toast. Dislikes brownies, spaghetti.\n5. Veer likes spaghetti. Dislikes paneer, brownies.\n6. Jay likes toast. Dislikes paneer, spaghetti.\n7. Rob likes guacamole. Dislikes toast, paneer.\n8. Hu likes brownies. Dislikes paneer, spaghetti.\n9. Bao likes guacamole. Dislikes toast, spaghetti.\n10. Yue likes toast, spaghetti. Dislikes brownies.\n\nTo ensure a liked item for each person on the `orderable` list or a disliked item on the `not_orderable` list without contradictions, let's analyze:\n\n- Paneer is liked by Val and Dot, but disliked by Veer, Jay, Rob, and Hu. Due to the number of dislikes, paneer should be on the `not_orderable` list.\n- Spaghetti is liked by Val, Veer, and Yue, but disliked by Naina, Lin, Jay, Hu, and Bao. Despite being liked by several people, the dislikes once again suggest spaghetti should also go on the `not_orderable` list.\n- Guacamole is liked by Rob and Bao, but disliked by Val and Dot. Here, the likes and dislikes are balanced, we’ll postpone deciding where guacamole should go.\n- Toast is liked by Naina, Lin, Jay, and Yue but disliked only by Rob and Bao. Since toast is liked by more people and there aren't strong opposing dislikes, toast should be on the `orderable` list.\n- Brownies are liked by Dot and Hu, but disliked by Naina, Lin, Veer, and Yue. Therefore, brownies should end up on the `not_orderable` list.\n\nWe still need to determine where to put guacamole since it is the only item not yet classified. Both Rob and Bao dislike toast and spaghetti, both of which are already settled (spaghetti is `not_orderable`, toast is `orderable`). As toast is their only disliked food on the `orderable` list, they need their liked food guacamole to be `orderable` to be satisfied. Therefore, guacamole should go on the `orderable` list.\n\nLet’s revisit our lists to ensure everyone is satisfied:\n\n- `orderable`: toast, guacamole\n- `not_orderable`: paneer, spaghetti, brownies\n\nNow, let's double-check against participant satisfaction:\n\n- Val: Is satisfied since paneer (liked) is not orderable.\n- Naina: Is satisfied since toast (liked) is orderable.\n- Dot: Is satisfied since brownies (liked) are not orderable.\n- Lin: Is satisfied since toast (liked) is orderable.\n- Veer: Is satisfied since paneer (disliked) is not orderable.\n- Jay: Is satisfied since toast (liked) is orderable.\n- Rob: Is satisfied since guacamole (liked) is orderable.\n- Hu: Is satisfied since brownies (liked) are not orderable.\n- Bao: Is satisfied since guacamole (liked) is orderable.\n- Yue: Is satisfied since toast (liked) is orderable.\n\nEvery person is satisfied, so the above `orderable` and `not_orderable` lists are valid. Here is the Python code representing the final solution:\n\n```python\norderable=['toast', 'guacamole'], not_orderable=['paneer', 'spaghetti', 'brownies']\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 325, 'num_completion_tokens': 803}
