iked by Li, Lex, Anil, Sol, Lan, Shan, Fay, and Veer, but liked by Sal, Raj, Ivy, Qi, Mina, Joe, Wes and Du, so it's not universally disliked.\n- Poke is disliked by Li, Pat, Lex, Fay, and Lan, but liked by Jim, Tao, Hui, Ivy, Mei, Wes and Veer, so it's not universally disliked.\n- Baklava is disliked by Li, Jim, Nic, Lex, Anil, Sol, Hui, Sid, Fay, Lan and Du, but liked by Sal, Pat, Ying, Lee, Joe and Veer, so it's not universally disliked.\n- Lasagna is disliked by Sal, Pat, Ying, Nic, Raj, Sid, Anil, Sol and Qi, but liked by Raj, Tao, Sid, Ivy, Mei, Shan, Fay, Du and Wes, so it's not universally disliked.\n- Salad is disliked by Jim, Ying, Raj, Tao, Hui, Joe and Qi, but liked by Nic, Sid, Lee, Mina and Lan, so it's not universally disliked.\n\nNo item is universally disliked, so we can’t put anything directly into the 'not_orderable' list on this basis.\n\nStep 2: Identify any universally liked items\nIf an item is liked by everyone who has mentioned it, it can be safely added to the 'orderable' list.\n\nThere does not seem to be any item liked by everyone who mentioned it, so nothing can be added to the 'orderable' list on this basis.\n\nStep 3: Attempt to satisfy the most people possible with a combination\nHere, we look to satisfy everyone with a combination of items, following the satisfaction criteria.\n\nHere we can use a process of elimination and logical deduction:\n\n- Lee, Nic, Joe and Mina all dislike kimchi or baklava but like salad. No one dislikes salad without liking a food that could be ordered. Thus, salad must be on the 'orderable' list.\n- Now that salad is 'orderable', Raj, Tao, Ivy, Qi, Wes have their dislikes satisfied, further more Ying, Pat, Jim, and Hui are all now satisfied due to salad being 'orderable'.\n- From those that remain, lasagna is liked by multiple people (Raj, Tao, Sid, Ivy, Mei, Shan, Fay, Du, and Wes) and only disliked by people who are already satisfied. Therefore, we can add lasagna to the 'orderable' list. \n- With lasagna now 'orderable', all participants are satisfied except for Li, Lex, Lan, and Sol.\n- We can satisfy Li by making poke 'not_orderable'.\n- We can then satisfy Lex and Lan by making baklava 'not_orderable'. Finally, we satisfy Sol since both kimchi and baklava are not orderable.\n\nSince all participants are now satisfied, we can list the items as follows:\n\norderable = ['salad', 'lasagna']\nnot_orderable = ['poke', 'baklava']\n\nSo the Python code representation of the solution will be:\n\n```python\norderable=['salad', 'lasagna'], not_orderable=['poke', 'baklava']\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 531, 'num_completion_tokens': 772}
