reate 'orderable' and 'not_orderable' lists that satisfy everyone's requirements. Let's try to create the lists by placing ratatouille on the 'not_orderable' list and tiramisu on the 'orderable' list first, since that seems to be satisfied by the most people.\n\n'not_orderable': ratatouille\n'orderable': tiramisu\n\nNow, let's see if we can satisfy everyone with these items.\n- Eva, Lan, Sam, Bea, Lou, Dan, Geet, Val, Kim, and Eli like tiramisu, so they are satisfied.\n- Ty, Yue, Hal, and Ava don't like ratatouille, so they're satisfied. \n\nNext, let's go through others and try to satisfy them without causing a contradiction.\n- For those who like curry, like Ken and Xiu, we don't have to put it on 'orderable' because they dislike pho, which is already on the 'not_orderable' list, so they are satisfied.\n- Taj, Liz, and Jan like tandoori; they have other dislikes that are not on the 'orderable' list (pho, cheesecake), so we don't have to add tandoori to 'orderable'.\n- For Tara, who likes tandoori, curry, and udon, we see curry and tandoori cannot be on 'orderable', but we can consider putting udon there since only Dot and Dan dislike it and we can check if we can satisfy them differently.\n- Jim, Hui, and Dot like tandoori but dislike ratatouille, which is already on 'not_orderable', so they are satisfied.\n- Feng likes pho but dislikes tandoori and cheesecake; those haven't been placed on either list yet. We can leave pho off the 'orderable' list since Feng can be satisfied with the non-inclusion of items she dislikes.\n- May, who likes cheesecake, can be left unsatisfied with cheesecake not being on 'orderable' because she dislikes curry and tandoori.\n- Ava likes pho and udon; dislikes ratatouille (already 'not_orderable'). We can satisfy Ava by either adding pho or udon to 'orderable' if we can do this without causing a problem for others.\n- Vic likes pho, but dislikes curry, and ratatouille is already 'not_orderable', so Vic is satisfied.\n\nSince only Dot and Dan dislike udon and they dislike other items as well (Dot dislikes udon and Dan dislikes ratatouille, which is already not orderable), we can add udon to the 'orderable' list and thus satisfy Tara and Ava, without causing any contradictions.\n\n'orderable': tiramisu, udon\n'not_orderable': ratatouille\n\nNow let's see if this list satisfies everyone:\nEva, Lan, Sam, Kim, Bea, Eli, Lou, Dan, Geet, and Val are satisfied by tiramisu (liked) being orderable.\nTy, Yue, Hal, Tia, Jim, Liz, and Hui are satisfied by ratatouille (disliked) not being orderable.\nTaj, Feng, Ken, May, Vic, Xiu, Tim, and Jan don't like an item on the 'orderable' list and have at least one disliked item not on the 'orderable' list, so they are satisfied.\nTara and Ava are satisfied by udon being on the 'orderable' list.\n\nFinally, we can confirm that our 'orderable' list contains tiramisu and udon, and our 'not_orderable' list contains ratatouille, and this satisfies all individuals according to the rules provided.\n\nTherefore, the output is:\norderable=['tiramisu','udon'], not_orderable=['ratatouille']", 'num_prompt_tokens': 620, 'num_completion_tokens': 1392}
