i: Likes udon, tart. Dislikes vegetables. Raj: Likes wonton, udon. Dislikes tart. ', 'menu_items': ['wonton', 'tart', 'udon', 'vegetables'], 'gpt_out': 'Given the information provided, we can see that there are four types of food items involved:\n\n1. Vegetables\n2. Wonton\n3. Udon\n4. Tart\n\nEach person has their likes and dislikes among these items. To find out what can be ordered (\'orderable\') and what cannot (\'not_orderable\'), we must ensure that at least one of each person\'s likes are in the \'orderable\' list, or one of their dislikes is in the \'not_orderable\' list, without any overlap between the two lists.\n\nLet\'s start by identifying if any food item is universally liked or disliked by everyone, or if certain items are dependably in the opposite list across all individuals:\n\n- Vegetables are disliked by Rohan, Ken, Bea, Tao, Gen, Lee, and Kai, but liked by many others. So, they cannot be universally assigned to any list.\n- Wonton is liked by many individuals but disliked by Rohan, Joe, Anil, Fay, Gen, Mia, and Lee, so it also cannot be universally assigned.\n- Udon is liked by Aarav, Ping, Wei, Nic, Ava, Zan, Arun, Kai, and Raj, but disliked by several including Rohan, Aarav, Liv, Max, Sid, Zara, Wei, Fay, Gus, Gen, Tao, Nic, Rob, Lee, and Dot, so it also cannot be universally assigned.\n- Tart is liked by many individuals but disliked by Joe, Du, Wes, Anil, Ping, Gus, Tao, Nic, Eva, Lee, Zan, Ned, and Arun. It cannot be universally assigned either.\n\nThere is no single item that everyone agrees upon, either universally liked or universally disliked.\n\nNow, we need to look at overlaps. Since everyone needs to be satisfied, we can check for any food item that someone likes and no one dislikes or universally disliked and liked by no one.\n\n- No item is universally liked or universally disliked by all.\n- Tart is disliked by at least one person who likes every other item (Tao dislikes tart, vegetables, and udon but has no comment on wonton), so