rable\':\n\n   - "muffins" cannot be in \'orderable\' because some people (like Xan, Ravi, Shan, Feng, Lin, Sid, Kit, Zac) dislike them, and they will not be satisfied unless "shrimps" or "tandoori" are in \'orderable\', which is not possible. Similarly, "muffins" cannot be in \'not_orderable\' either because it is liked by many (e.g., Riya, Kai, Pat, Chao, Rex, Lou, Nita, Gia, Liz).\n   - The same logic applies to all other items which are both liked and disliked by different people, such as "lobster", "naan", "omelette", "sorbet", "tiramisu". Hence, none of these can clearly be placed in either \'orderable\' or \'not_orderable\' without further analysis.\n\n5. It seems we have a conflict as all the items except for "shrimps" and "tandoori" are both liked and disliked by various individuals, and "shrimps" and "tandoori" have already been placed in \'not_orderable\'.\n\n6. Since we cannot find items that all individuals collectively agree on, no configuration of \'orderable\' and \'not_orderable\' will satisfy the criteria.\n\nThus, the output lists for \'orderable\' and \'not_orderable\' should both be empty:\n\n   orderable = []\n   not_orderable = []\n\nHere\'s the final solution in the requested Python format:\n\n   orderable=[], not_orderable=[]', 'num_prompt_tokens': 1382, 'num_completion_tokens': 589}
