ryone's likes. We need to fulfill some dislikes to satisfy them.\n\nFrom the dislikes, we can see:\n\n1. Pie is commonly disliked by many, but it is also liked by some.\n2. Brownies are commonly disliked by fewer participants than pie, but it is also liked by several people.\n3. Pizza has fewer dislikes and is liked by many.\n4. Sorbet has a mixed reaction but has fewer dislikes than likes.\n\nOur strategy should be to satisfy the dislikes of the person whose likes cannot be met due to conflicting interests with others' dislikes. We'll start with an item that is disliked by someone but not liked by anyone who has no other likes (for example, if a person only likes one food item and another person dislikes it, we can't use it because the person who likes it wouldn't be satisfied). \n\nFrom the list, we see that the following is true:\n\n- Pizza is not the sole like of any person (Tim likes pizza but dislikes sorbet, Qi dislikes pizza), so putting pizza in the 'not_orderable' list satisfies Qi without making it impossible to satisfy Tim and everyone else who likes pizza.\n\nThis allows us to satisfy people who dislike pizza, and we still have an option to satisfy those who like pizza by placing something else they like in the 'orderable' list. Now we proceed with that mindset to find a solution:\n\nWe know:\n- Qi will be satisfied with 'not_orderable: pizza'.\n- Ken needs pie, brownies, or pizza in the 'orderable' list; since pizza is out, we need pie or brownies for him.\n- Geet can be satisfied with pie in 'orderable' or sorbet in 'not_orderable'.\n- Aarav can be satisfied with pie or brownies in 'orderable'.\n- Ava will be satisfied if sorbet, pie, or brownies are in 'not_orderable'.\n- Ula will be satisfied if pie is in 'orderable' or brownies in 'not_orderable'.\n\nSince we identified pizza for the 'not_orderable' list and we cannot satisfy Ken, Aarav, and Ula's 'like' for pizza, we need to satisfy them with something else they like. Ken, Aarav, and Ula also like