Likes cheesecake. Dislikes macaron, baklava. Zac: Likes cheesecake. Dislikes baklava, hummus. Jaya: Likes macaron, cheesecake. Dislikes baklava. Zoe: Likes salad, cheesecake, macaron. Tara: Likes baklava, salad. Dislikes cheesecake. Ula: Likes salad. Dislikes macaron, cheesecake. Jay: Likes macaron, baklava. Dislikes salad. Ru: Dislikes cheesecake, macaron, hummus. Bea: Likes macaron. Dislikes baklava, cheesecake. Amy: Likes cheesecake, hummus, macaron. Ansh: Likes salad. Dislikes hummus, baklava. Nic: Likes macaron. Dislikes hummus, baklava. Sam: Likes salad, macaron. Dislikes baklava. Liz: Likes macaron. Dislikes salad, hummus. Sol: Likes hummus, baklava. Dislikes cheesecake. Pat: Likes cheesecake. Dislikes salad, baklava. Jon: Likes hummus, macaron. Dislikes baklava. Sky: Likes hummus, cheesecake, macaron. Ned: Likes hummus. Dislikes salad, baklava. Ana: Likes macaron, baklava. Dislikes cheesecake. Joy: Likes macaron, salad. Dislikes cheesecake. Dot: Likes cheesecake, hummus. Dislikes baklava. ', 'menu_items': ['baklava', 'cheesecake', 'salad', 'macaron', 'hummus'], 'gpt_out': 'Let’s start by listing all food items, and then categorizing them according to how many people like or dislike them. This might help us decide which food items can be ordered (orderable) and which should not (not_orderable) to satisfy everyone\'s preferences. If an item is liked by someone and disliked by another, it becomes a contentious item, and we will have to balance these to fulfill the satisfaction criteria.\n\nFood items:\n- Salad\n- Macaron\n- Hummus\n- Baklava\n- Cheesecake\n\nNow let\'s count how many people like/dislike each item:\n\nLikes/Dislikes:\n- Salad: Likes (10), Dislikes (4)\n- Macaron: Likes (12), Dislikes (6)\n- Hummus: Likes (11), Dislikes (11)\n- Baklava: Likes (10), Dislikes (9)\n- Cheesecake: Likes (12), Dislikes (12)\n\nNext, we will determine which food items must be in which list to satisfy everyone. We will prioritize the items with the most "likes" and least "dislikes" to optimize the number of people satisfied. We also must note that we can only place an item in one list or the other, not both.\n\nSalad can satisfy the most individuals if it is orderable as 10 people like it and only 4 dislike it.\nSalad: orderable\n\nMacaron is liked by some who dislike salad and liked by many, so ideally, it should also be orderable, but let\'s keep looking for conflicts.\nMacaron: potentially orderable\n\nHummus is divisive: it is both liked and disliked by 11 people. Since it is a contentious item, we\'ll have to see if we can place it in a list without conflicts.\nHummus: contentious\n\nBaklava, too, is contentious but has slightly fewer likes than dislikes, which might make it easier to place in the not_orderable list.\nBaklava: potentially not_orderable\n\nCheesecake is the most contentious, with equal likes and dislikes. Its placement will rely heavily on other items\' placement.\nCheesecake: contentious\n\nNow, we must check for conflicts and try to make a viable combination. We notice individuals who dislike hummus are quite often satisfied with other items they like. For example, Kit, who dislikes hummus, likes salad – if salad is orderable, Kit is satisfied.\n\nIf we assume Macaron is orderable, it would satisfy many, but it is also disliked by a significant number. We might place it in the orderable list but let\'s hold off on finalizing that.\n\nLet\'s find a non-contentious item for the not_orderable list. Since more people dislike Baklava than they do Salad, and it is not liked by anyone who dislikes all other items, it seems a safe choice to put Baklava to not_orderable list to satisfy those who dislike it.\nBaklava: not_orderable\n\nLet\'s check Cheesecake. With Cheesecake being liked and disliked intensely, it\'s impractical to make everyone happy using this item. People\'s satisfaction will rely on their other likes and dislikes.\n\nWe notice all people who have dislikes have at least one like outside their dislikes. This is key because we can use what they like to satisfy them. As such, Hummus is also contentious but it might be better off in the not_orderable list to satisfy those who dislike it; they typically have other likes that are not Hummus.\n\nNow, we have to handle the Macaron situation. We can\'t fully satisfy people who like Macaron and dislike everything else or vice versa. However, our goal is to satisfy everyone. Given that Macaron has a good amount of likes and dislikes both, we can attempt to put it in the not_orderable list and satisfy dislikers with this. But people who like Macaron typically like other things except for Chen and Jun. So in order to satisfy Chen and Jun, we need to have Macaron as orderable.\n\nFinally, let\'s look at Cheesecake. We\'ve noticed that people who dislike Cheesecake often have other likes (e.g., Salad, Macaron, Hummus). Therefore, it makes sense to put Cheesecake in the orderable list since those who dislike it will mostly be satisfied with either Salad or Macaron being orderable, and those who like Cheesecake will be satisfied too.\n\nThe final lists that should satisfy everyone would be:\n\norderable:\n- Salad (satisfying 10 people who like it, and only 4 who dislike it can satisfy with Hummus or Cheesecake)\n- Macaron (satisfying 12 people who like it and those who dislike it, Chen and Jun, will be satisfied as they do not have other items liked which are not already in disliked by others)\n- Cheesecake (satisfying 12 people who like it and those who dislike it can be satisfied with Salad or Macaron)\n\nnot_orderable:\n- Baklava (satisfying 9 people who dislike it, and the likes can be satisfied by Salad, Macaron or Cheesecake)\n- Hummus (satisfying 11 people who dislike it, and the likes can be satisfied by Salad, Macaron or Cheesecake because there is no person that only likes Hummus and dislike other items without having at least one liked item not being disliked by others)\n\nThe final solution is:\n\n```python\norderable=[\'salad\', \'macaron\', \'cheesecake\'], not_orderable=[\'baklava\', \'hummus\']\n```', 'num_prompt_tokens': 888, 'num_completion_tokens': 1153}
