g, Gen, Pat, and May without direct conflict with others\' likes, since Gen also dislikes "burger", allowing it to go on the \'orderable\' list to satisfy them.\n\nLet\'s add "burger" to the \'orderable\' list, satisfying Aarav, Min, Lan, and Lou.\n\nAfter these additions, let\'s check who is not satisfied yet and try to satisfy them with further additions:\n\n- Yan is not satisfied. Yan likes poke which conflicts with many dislikes, thus we can choose to not order "muffins", another dislike of Yan\'s.\n- Vic is not satisfied and dislikes "kebab", which also doesn\'t conflict after adding "burger" to \'orderable\', so we can add "kebab" to \'not_orderable\'.\n- Max is not satisfied but he also likes “biscuits” which no others dislike. We can add "biscuits" to \'orderable\'.\n- Eva is now satisfied because "biscuits" are \'orderable\'.\n- Val is now satisfied because "muffins" are \'not_orderable\'.\n- Rex is now satisfied because "poke" is not \'orderable\'.\n- Joe is not satisfied. He dislikes "naan", but we can\'t make "naan" \'not_orderable\' due to many who like it, so we could add "espresso" to \'orderable\' since other dislikers (Ian, Anil) already have their dislikes in \'not_orderable\'.\n- Zara is now satisfied with “naan” in \'orderable\'.\n- Jaya, Rohan, Mia, Pat, Sid, and Diya are now satisfied due to either “naan” or “ramen” being orderable.\n- Jon is now satisfied as "baklava" is \'not_orderable\'.\n- Ian is satisfied with "bread" in \'orderable\'.\n- Ava is satisfied as "poke" is \'orderable\'.\n- Meg and Joe are satisfied with "kebab" in \'orderable\'.\n- Shan and Diya are satisfied with "ramen" in \'orderable\'.\n- Cal is satisfied now because "kebab" is \'not_orderable\' and likes “biscuits”.\n\nThis should satisfy everyone. Let\'s output our solution:\n\n```python\norderable=[\'burger\', \'biscuits\', \'kebab\', \'naan\', \'ramen\', \'espresso\'], not_orderable=[\'baklava\']\n```', 'num_prompt_tokens': 581, 'num_completion_tokens': 1076}
