ast. Ana: Likes pho, espresso, toast. Ava: Likes pho, toast. Dislikes tandoori. Sia: Likes tandoori, espresso. Dislikes toast. Fay: Likes espresso, toast, pho. Xan: Likes espresso. Dislikes roti, pho. Pia: Dislikes espresso, roti, tandoori. Nic: Likes espresso. Dislikes roti, pho. May: Likes toast. Dislikes pho, espresso. Bea: Likes roti. Dislikes pho, tandoori. Zac: Likes pho, tandoori. Dislikes roti. Aarav: Likes espresso. Dislikes roti, tandoori. Du: Likes toast, roti. Dislikes pho. Ben: Dislikes tandoori, toast, roti. Eva: Likes toast, pho. Dislikes tandoori. Kim: Likes tandoori. Dislikes roti, pho. Ula: Likes roti, pho. Dislikes toast. Dot: Likes roti. Dislikes toast, tandoori. Hu: Dislikes tandoori, espresso, toast. Li: Likes tandoori, pho. Dislikes roti. ', 'menu_items': ['roti', 'tandoori', 'toast', 'espresso', 'pho'], 'gpt_out': "To solve this, we first need to consider items that are universally liked or disliked, as these will be the easiest to classify. If an item is liked by some people and disliked by others, it is much trickier to place in a list without conflicting with someone's preference.\n\nHere's how we approach the requirements:\n- If an item is disliked by everyone who mentioned it, it can go into the 'not_orderable' list.\n- If an item is liked by everyone who mentioned it, it can go into the 'orderable' list.\n- If an item is liked by some and disliked by others, we need to see if placing it in either list meets the first criterion (every person is satisfied as long as at least one liked item is 'orderable' or one disliked item is 'not_orderable').\n\nBy reading through all the likes and dislikes, we determine how to categorize each of the four items: espresso, roti, pho, toast, and tandoori.\n\n1. Espresso:\n   - Liked by many.\n   - Disliked by some (Lei, Wes, May, Pia, Hu).\n   - Since only 5 people dislike espresso and they each have other dislikes which aren't universally liked, espresso can be placed in 'orderable'. This would satisfy 