e for the \'not_orderable\' list. A quick scan shows that "frittata" is disliked by Ping, Rob, May, Feng, Joe, Sol, Tia, Lan, Lei, Lee, Zoe, Taj, and Sue. No one has mentioned "frittata" as a liked food, so we can confidently add it to the \'not_orderable\' list.\n\nNext, let\'s find an item that everyone who likes it doesn\'t dislike anything that someone else likes – this can be added to the \'orderable\' list. After perusing the lists, "lobster" seems to be a common like without being problematic (it\'s disliked but won\'t conflict with any liked items when placed in the orderable list).\n\nNow let’s revise the lists with "frittata" in \'not_orderable\' and "lobster" in \'orderable\':\n\n- Lobster is liked by Lou, Pat, Lex, Feng, Pam, Mia, Ana, Aarav, Ula, Diya, Lee, Aarav, Veer, Gen and Lee, and it\'s not disliked by anyone without other options.\n- Frittata is disliked by several and liked only by May, Chen, and Lei, who all have other likes as well. \n\nSo let\'s note down:\n\norderable = [\'lobster\']\nnot_orderable = [\'frittata\']\n\nNow, let\'s review our lists and see who is not yet satisfied. We need to find at least one liked item for each person or place one of their disliked items on the \'not_orderable\' list, without conflicts. \n\nFrom the information, we can identify that "cake" is widely disliked and liked by a few, but these people also like other items that no one dislikes, so we can confidently add it to \'not_orderable\'. The "pie" is liked by some people; however, it is also widely disliked. Since some people only like the "pie" and not anything else, we can\'t place it in either list without further review. "Peach" appears the same way as "pie."\n\nNow let\'s look at the remaining items – "poke", "falafel", and "vegetables" – these are liked by some and disliked by others, but the critical part is who likes them exclusively or who exclusively dislikes them. \n\nSince "poke" is the exclusive like of Yue, Eva, and Tia, it needs to be present 