uals who have a minimal number of likes (to ensure their satisfaction) and including items in \'not_orderable\' that are disliked by individuals who have a minimal number of dislikes (to ensure their dissatisfaction is not an issue).\n\nFor example, Yan likes ramen and sorbet and dislikes shrimps. Since Yan\'s likes are not unique to her and her dislike is commonly disliked, it\'s better not to include shrimps in \'orderable\'. This logic will apply to all individuals with similar patterns.\n\nWe will now iterate through the list to find which items can be categorized as orderable or not_orderable without disappointing anyone.\n\nUnfortunately, upon further inspection, it appears that due to the varying likes and dislikes for each item and no clear unanimous decisions for any item, it is impossible to satisfy everyone\'s preferences with an \'orderable\' and \'not_orderable\' list according to the given rules.\n\nSince no food item is universally liked without being disliked by someone else and vice versa, it\'s impossible to make both an \'orderable\' and a \'not_orderable\' list that would keep everyone satisfied. Hence, both lists would be empty.\n\nThe correct output based on the analysis is:\n\n```python\norderable=[], not_orderable=[]\n```', 'num_prompt_tokens': 1377, 'num_completion_tokens': 957}
