o dislike it. So, let\'s tentatively place it in `orderable`.\n- Lobster: Similarly, let\'s tentatively place lobster in `orderable`, as its liked by several and placing it in `orderable` won\'t directly make anyone unsatisfied since we already plan to have "salad" in `not_orderable`.\n\nAfter these considerations, let\'s see if we can satisfy all individuals.\n- Everyone who dislikes salad is satisfied by `not_orderable`.\n- Ping\'s dislikes are all in on the lists, so they are satisfied.\n- Ping, Mina, and Ravi all dislike naan and lobster, but salad will satisfy them as a disliked item in the `not_orderable` list.\n- Jon likes salad and dislikes couscous; but since salad is in `not_orderable`, we satisfy Jon.\n- "Zac" dislikes tiramisu but likes naan and couscous, both of which are in `orderable`.\n\n## Conclusion:\nWe can propose the following lists:\n\norderable = ["tiramisu", "couscous", "naan", "lobster"]\nnot_orderable = ["salad"]\n\nThese lists ensure that each individual is satisfied according to the given criteria. No individual dislikes all items in `orderable` nor likes any in `not_orderable`. \n\nLet\'s output the lists in the given format:\n\n```python\norderable=[\'tiramisu\', \'couscous\', \'naan\', \'lobster\'], not_orderable=[\'salad\']\n```\n', 'num_prompt_tokens': 540, 'num_completion_tokens': 957}
