o disliked by some.\n- Udon: Liked by many but also disliked by some.\n- Waffles: Liked by many but also disliked by some.\n- Roti: Liked by many but also disliked by some.\n- Bread: Liked by many but also disliked by some.\n\nDislikes:\n- Curry: Disliked by many.\n- Gelato: Disliked by many.\n- Udon: Disliked by many.\n- Waffles: Disliked by many.\n- Roti: Disliked by many.\n- Bread: Disliked by many.\n\nNone of the items are universally liked or disliked.\n\nFrom here, let's move to find a combination that satisfies at least one like or one dislike for each person. Let’s pick an item that is liked by someone and see if adding it to 'orderable' or 'not_orderable' will satisfy the most people. Since 'curry' and 'gelato' are heavily polarizing, we should avoid them initially in selection.\n\nLet’s start with 'udon', which is liked by a number of people and disliked by some. We can try adding 'udon' to 'orderable' and see if we can satisfy everyone's preferences by doing so. Remember, if 'udon' is in 'orderable', no one who dislikes 'udon' can have all their dislikes in 'not_orderable', and they must have a liked food in 'orderable'.\n\nIf 'udon' is 'orderable': Jim, Mia, Zan, Ken, Eva, Wes, Xiu, Ava, Ivy, Jun, Ravi, and Tim will be satisfied because they like 'udon'. \n\nHowever, Lin and Taj dislike 'udon' and their other dislikes are 'gelato' (not universally liked) and 'roti' (which cannot be placed in 'not_orderable' because it is liked by others without an alternative like). Val also dislikes 'udon' among other items, and since he dislikes everything, we cannot satisfy him by adding 'udon' to 'orderable'. \n\nBy similar logic, 'waffles', 'roti', and 'bread' also cannot satisfy everyone since they are liked by some and disliked by others without an alternative for the dislikes.\n\nIt becomes evident that we are in a position where every food item is either liked or disliked by different individuals without a clear opportunity for a universal solution that meets th