tial to be 'orderable' because placing it there would immediately satisfy those who like it and does not go against too many dislikes.\n\nNow, let's consider the doughnut. It is liked by many people and only 10 people dislike it. If we put doughnut in the 'orderable' list, we need to make sure that there is at least one item that each of those 10 individuals likes in 'orderable' or one item they dislike in 'not_orderable'.\n\nAmong the 10 doughnut dislikers, five dislike pancakes (Sky, Wes, Tao, Pia, Ron), and five dislike shrimps (Lee, Neil, Wei, Ivy, Sky). All of them dislike multiple items. This could potentially lead to a situation where doughnut is 'orderable' while both pancakes and shrimps are 'not_orderable'.\n\nBy making pancakes and shrimps 'not_orderable,' all individuals who dislike doughnut would be satisfied. We then need to check with individuals who like pancakes and shrimps to ensure that they still have something they like 'orderable' or another disliked item 'not_orderable.'\n\nFor people who dislike pancakes: \n- Udon can be 'orderable', because it is liked by many who dislike pancakes (Abe, Rina, Xan, Ying, Hu, Zac).\n- Those who dislike pancakes and like shrimps (Zan, Pam, Tara) would be satisfied as shrimps are selected 'not_orderable'.\n\nUdon still remains a suitable choice for 'orderable'. Since udon is disliked by a number of participants, we must ensure their satisfaction as well:\n\n- Tia dislikes all three and does not have any likes, so her preferences do not factor in the decision of 'orderable' items.\n- Some participants dislike udon but like doughnut (Chao, Kit, Mei, Taj, Lex, Ava, Mia, Yue, Zoe, Shan), so they are satisfied because doughnut is 'orderable'.\n- Others who dislike udon but like pancakes (Ron, Ivy, Neil, Liz) will not be satisfied with udon 'orderable' and pancakes 'not_orderable', which is problematic.\n\nAt this point, with the proposed solution, individuals like Ron, Ivy, Neil, and Liz would still not be satisfied.