 Ray: Likes burrito, tiramisu. Dislikes lobster. Chen: Likes poke. Dislikes burrito, tacos. Lan: Likes toast, couscous. Dislikes lobster. Ken: Likes tacos. Dislikes tiramisu, couscous. Lei: Likes burrito. Dislikes couscous, poke. Ivy: Likes poke, tiramisu. Dislikes tacos. Cal: Dislikes couscous, shawarma, lobster. Chao: Likes poke, shawarma. Dislikes tiramisu. Tara: Likes toast, poke, couscous. Sia: Likes poke, tacos, toast. Naina: Likes tacos. Dislikes tiramisu, shawarma. Veer: Likes lobster. Dislikes shawarma, tiramisu. Max: Likes tacos, toast. Dislikes poke. Gia: Likes shawarma. Dislikes couscous, toast. Aarav: Likes shawarma, tacos. Dislikes burrito. Shan: Likes couscous. Dislikes tiramisu, shawarma. Rob: Likes tiramisu, couscous. Dislikes shawarma. Ying: Likes lobster, couscous, tacos. Jim: Dislikes toast, tacos, lobster. Fay: Likes tacos. Dislikes shawarma, couscous. Lin: Likes toast. Dislikes tiramisu, burrito. Rina: Likes burrito, couscous, tiramisu. Wes: Likes toast, burrito, poke. Mina: Likes shawarma. Dislikes tacos, poke. Ru: Likes poke, couscous. Dislikes lobster. Joy: Likes couscous. Dislikes toast, poke. Tom: Likes couscous. Dislikes lobster, burrito. Tia: Likes tacos, toast, couscous. ', 'menu_items': ['burrito', 'lobster', 'tiramisu', 'poke', 'tacos', 'shawarma', 'toast', 'couscous'], 'gpt_out': "To solve this problem, we must identify items that can satisfy the most people without conflicting with each other. We will follow these steps:\n\n1. List all the likes and dislikes of each person.\n2. Identify items that are universally disliked to put in the 'not_orderable' list.\n3. Look for universally liked items or items that are not disliked by anyone to potentially put in the 'orderable' list.\n4. Verify that each person's preference is satisfied by having at least one liked item that is orderable or one disliked item that is not orderable.\n\nLet's begin the process.\n\nStep 1: List likes and dislikes.\nWe create two sets, one for all liked items and another for all disliked items across all individuals. We do not list them here to save space, but in practice, we would need to enumerate all preferences.\n\nStep 2: Identify universally disliked items.\nItems that are disliked by some but not liked by any:\n- tacos: Disliked by Cy, Sam, Taj, Ian, Zac, Hu, Naina, Jim, Mina, but no one likes it.\nSo, tacos is a candidate for 'not_orderable.'\n\nStep 3: Look for universally liked items or non-disliked items to put in 'orderable'.\n- toast: Liked by many but only disliked by Ron, Sky, Hu, Rex, Gia, Joy, and Jim, hence cannot be orderable since it's not universally liked or at least not disliked by someone.\n- tiramisu: Liked by many but disliked by Taj, Gen, Meg, Veer, Shan, Lin, and Jim, hence cannot be orderable based on dislikes.\n- poke: Liked by several people but disliked by many as well, hence cannot be orderable.\n\nWe notice that no item is universally liked without any dislikes. We can instead look for food items that are liked by at least one person and not disliked by others to include in 'orderable.'\n\nStep 4: Attempt to satisfy everyone's preferences considering individual dislikes and potential items for 'orderable.'\n- shawarma: Liked by Yan, Ron, Dan, Gia, Chao, Aarav, Mina which is more than any other food item and only disliked by Ian, Ty, Naina, Shan, Cal, Fay, Gia (who also likes it). So, we can satisfy most of the people if it is on the 'orderable' list.\n- lobster: Liked by Pam, Ada, Ron, Sky, Isha, Tim, Riya, Veer, Ying, but disliked by Jay, Ada, Taj, Ty, Zan, Tim, Cal, Jim, Ru. So it cannot be on the 'orderable' list.\n\nWith 'shawarma' as orderable, we have satisfied those who like it and are not considering their dislikes if at least one of their likes is orderable. Now we need to make sure all the individuals not happy with 'shawarma' have their dislikes attended to.\n\nNow we go through the list of individuals who do not like shawarma and check if 'tacos' can attend to their preference:\n- Ian, dislikes tacos, which could be not_orderable, satisfies his preference.\n- Ty, dislikes tacos, which could be not_orderable, satisfies his preference.\n- Naina, dislikes tacos, which could be not_orderable, satisfies his preference.\n- Shan, dislikes tacos, which could be not_orderable, satisfies his preference.\n\nIn conclusion, putting 'shawarma' on the 'orderable' list and 'tacos' on the 'not_orderable' list would satisfy all preferences outlined above.\n\nOutput:\n```python\norderable=['shawarma'], not_orderable=['tacos']\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 940, 'num_completion_tokens': 768}
Data Sample: {'num_vars': 7, 'num_clauses': 63, 'formula': [[3, -6, 7], [2, 6, -1], [-3, 1, 4], [5, -1, -3], [-6, -7, -5], [-5, 3, -6], [-4, 2, -7], [7, -3, -5], [-3, -2, 1], [-7, -4, 6], [-6, 1, 7], [2, -6, 1], [-1, -6, 4], [1, -3, 2], [4, 7, -2], [2, 3, -6], [1, 4, 6], [5, 4, -2], [-3, 2, -1], [6, -5, -1], [4, -7, -1], [7, 5, 3], [3, -7, 5], [7, 6, 5], [-2, 1, 5], [4, -5, -6], [-6, -5, 2], [-4, -7, -1], [6, 2, 7], [5, -2, -3], [7, -1, -3], [2, -7, -3], [4, -5, -3], [-2, -1, 6], [2, 7, -1], [1, 7, -3], [-7, 6, -5], [1, 7, -2], [-6, 3, -2], [5, -3, 2], [2, -1, 4], [-7, -1, 5], [2, 4, -7], [-7, -2, -3], [-5, 7, 3], [-7, -3, -2], [1, -4, -2], [-4, -2, -3], [-1, -2, 7], [6, 2, -4], [-3, -6, -7], [-2, 6, -7], [1, -2, 7], [-6, -1, 2], [-4, 1, 2], [7, 4, -3], [4, 7, 3], [-4, 5, -7], [7, -2, -6], [3, 2, -1], [1, 3, 2], [-3, -2, 1], [4, 6, -2]], 'is_sat': False, 'preferences': 'Ping: Likes sorbet, pizza. Dislikes tiramisu. Zac: Likes falafel, tiramisu. Dislikes churros. Ian: Likes churros, tacos. Dislikes sorbet. Gia: Likes mousse. Dislikes churros, sorbet. Fay: Dislikes tiramisu, pizza, mousse. Sid: Likes sorbet. Dislikes mousse, tiramisu. Ula: Likes falafel. Dislikes tacos, pizza. Yan: Likes pizza. Dislikes sorbet, mousse. Aarav: Likes churros. Dislikes sorbet, falafel. Taj: Likes tiramisu. Dislikes pizza, tacos. Amy: Likes churros, pizza. Dislikes tiramisu. Kavi: Likes falafel, churros. Dislikes tiramisu. Vic: Likes tacos. Dislikes churros, tiramisu. Feng: Likes churros, falafel. Dislikes sorbet. Ivy: Likes tacos, pizza. Dislikes falafel. Lin: Likes falafel, sorbet. Dislikes tiramisu. Tim: Likes churros, tacos, tiramisu. Joy: Likes mousse, tacos. Dislikes falafel. May: Likes falafel. Dislikes sorbet, churros. Xiu: Likes tiramisu. Dislikes mousse, churros. Kai: Likes tacos. Dislikes pizza, churros. Ravi: Likes pizza, mousse, sorbet. Tara: Likes sorbet, mousse. Dislikes pizza. Diya: Likes pizza, tiramisu, mousse. Mel: Likes churros, mousse. Dislikes falafel. Hu: Likes tacos. Dislikes mousse, tiramisu. Xan: Likes falafel. Dislikes tiramisu, mousse. Eva: Dislikes tacos, pizza, churros. Mia: Likes tiramisu, falafel, pizza. Eli: Likes mousse. Dislikes falafel, sorbet. Liz: Likes pizza. Dislikes churros, sorbet. Hui: Likes falafel. Dislikes pizza, sorbet. Chao: Likes tacos. Dislikes mousse, sorbet. Liv: Likes tiramisu. Dislikes falafel, churros. Oli: Likes falafel, pizza. Dislikes churros. Isha: Likes churros, pizza. Dislikes sorbet. Meg: Likes tiramisu. Dislikes pizza, mousse. Sol: Likes churros, pizza. Dislikes falafel. Nita: Likes sorbet. Dislikes tiramisu, falafel. Mei: Likes mousse, falafel. Dislikes sorbet. Jun: Likes falafel, tacos. Dislikes churros. Sky: Likes mousse. Dislikes pizza, churros. Hal: Likes falafel, tacos. Dislikes pizza. Shan: Dislikes pizza, falafel, sorbet. Dot: Likes pizza, sorbet. Dislikes mousse. Rina: Dislikes pizza, sorbet, falafel. Pat: Likes churros. Dislikes tacos, falafel. Anil: Dislikes tacos, falafel, sorbet. Kit: Likes pizza. Dislikes churros, falafel. Lan: Likes tiramisu, falafel. Dislikes tacos. Lia: Dislikes sorbet, tiramisu, pizza. Leo: Likes tiramisu. Dislikes falafel, pizza. Max: Likes churros, pizza. Dislikes falafel. Ty: Likes falafel. Dislikes tiramisu, churros. Sue: Likes churros, falafel. Dislikes tacos. Val: Likes pizza, tacos. Dislikes sorbet. Riya: Likes tacos, pizza, sorbet. Ru: Likes mousse. Dislikes tacos, pizza. Bea: Likes pizza. Dislikes falafel, tiramisu. Ada: Likes sorbet, falafel. Dislikes churros. Om: Likes churros, sorbet, falafel. Ying: Likes churros. Dislikes sorbet, falafel. Ben: Likes tacos, tiramisu. Dislikes falafel. ', 'menu_items': ['churros', 'falafel', 'sorbet', 'tacos', 'mousse', 'tiramisu', 'pizza'], 'gpt_out': "To solve this task, we need to create two lists of food items: 'orderable' and 'not_orderable'. We need to ensure that each person in the group is satisfied with at least one liked item in the 'orderable' list or one disliked item in the 'not_orderable' list. To ensure the lists meet everyone's preferences, we can approach this by considering the dislikes first, as they seem to be more restrictive in terms of what cannot coexist in the 'orderable' list.\n\nLet's first gather all the food items and their likes and dislikes count:\n\nFood items: sorbet, pizza, tiramisu, falafel, churros, tacos, mousse\n\nLikes count:\n- sorbet: 7 likes\n- pizza: 17 likes\n- tiramisu: 10 likes\n- falafel: 18 likes\n- churros: 15 likes\n- tacos: 14 likes\n- mousse: 8 likes\n\nDislikes count:\n- sorbet: 14 dislikes\n- pizza: 12 dislikes\n- tiramisu: 16 dislikes\n- falafel: 13 dislikes\n- churros: 16 dislikes\n- tacos: 8 dislikes\n- mousse: 14 dislikes\n\nAccording to the criteria, we need to place at least one disliked item in the 'not_orderable' list for each person to be satisfied, so an item with a high dislike count should be there to cover as many people as possible. Simultaneously, an item with a high like count should be in the 'orderable' to satisfy others. Therefore, we need to avoid conflicts (e.g., placing an item both liked and disliked heavily in the wrong lists).\n\nTo begin, let's consider tiramisu and churros, which are the most disliked items (16 each). Since these two items are disliked by many, we can tentatively add one of them to the 'not_orderable' list.\n\nWe will start with tiramisu in the 'not_orderable' and see if it satisfies the most number of people:\n\nPeople who dislike tiramisu should now be satisfied.\nPeople who like tiramisu (10 individuals) must have another liked item in the 'orderable' list or throw them into unsatisfied.\n\nLet's check if falafel (18 likes and 13 dislikes) can be in the 'orderable' list without causing issues. Placing falafel in 'orderable' satisfies all people who like it and does not conflict with those who dislike tiramisu because they dislike falafel too and there's no conflict in such case.\n\nAdults who dislike both falafel and tiramisu (Rina and Anil) should not be used as indicators because they dislike all of the choices, meaning they will always be unsatisfied unless every single item is not orderable, which conflicts with the fact that the orderable list must contain at least one item.\n\nMoving on to satisfy falafel likers (18 individuals) who might be unhappy with tiramisu not being orderable. For that, we add falafel to 'orderable':\n\nNow let's process the rest:\n- Pizza is the next most-liked item, with 17 likes and 12 dislikes. But we can see that some individuals dislike both pizza and tiramisu (e.g., Fay), meaning we cannot place pizza in the 'orderable' list, as Fay would remain unsatisfied. Therefore, we move on.\n- The next most-liked item is churros, with 15 likes. However, it is also highly disliked (16 dislikes), and adding it to either list may not be possible without conflicts.\n- Tacos are liked by 14 individuals and disliked by 8. Those who dislike both tiramisu and tacos (e.g., Ben) are already satisfied by having tiramisu in 'not_orderable'. Those who like tacos and not falafel (such as Kai who dislikes both churros and pizza) could be satisfied if tacos are in the 'orderable' list.\n\nSo, the individuals who like tiramisu or churros and not tacos or falafel should be the point of focus here:\n- Tim likes tiramisu and dislikes sorbet but likes churros and tacos. No conflict here; Tim will be satisfied with churros or tacos in 'orderable'.\n- Zac and Mia like tiramisu and do not dislike falafel or tacos.\n\nSince there's no conflict, we can confidently place tacos in 'orderable':\n\n'orderable': falafel, tacos\n'not_orderable': tiramisu\n\nLet's check if all individuals are satisfied:\n- All tiramisu dislikers are satisfied with 'not_orderable'.\n- All falafel and taco likers are satisfied with 'orderable'.\n- Individuals who like only pizza and/or sorbet, such as Ping and Zac, are not yet satisfied, as their liked items aren't on the 'orderable' list.\n\nWe need one more pass to see if we can satisfy everyone by adding pizza or sorbet to 'orderable'. We can't add pizza (Fay would be unsatisfied), but we can add sorbet as it would not cause any new conflicts, and all sorbet dislikers have been satisfied by 'not_orderable' list, or their liked items are already 'orderable'.", 'num_prompt_tokens': 1109, 'num_completion_tokens': 1117}
