vi, Yue, Lia, Riya, Yan, Ravi, Sia, Vic, Liz\n\nGiven these likes and dislikes:\n\n- Lex, Vic, and Liz dislike everything, so one of their dislikes must be on the 'not_orderable' list to satisfy them.\n- Bruschetta is liked by many and only disliked by five people. It should go on the 'orderable' list to satisfy a large number of people, provided there's an alternative for the people who dislike it.\n- Caramel is liked by many and disliked by seven, so it's not as clear-cut as bruschetta. It cannot be on the same list as bruschetta due to the rule that no item can appear on both lists.\n- Couscous is also polarizing, liked by fewer and disliked by several.\n\nSince the people who dislike bruschetta also dislike caramel and couscous, caramel or couscous could go on the 'not_orderable' list to satisfy Lex, Vic, and Liz. This leaves bruschetta as a strong candidate for the 'orderable' list, as it satisfies the majority and there's a choice that will satisfy those who dislike it.\n\nNow we test:\n\nOption 1: Put bruschetta on 'orderable', caramel on 'not_orderable'.\n- This will satisfy everyone who likes bruschetta and also those who dislike caramel. However, there's an issue for the people who like caramel and don't have an alternative liked option available. Also, the ones who dislike couscous do not have their dislike on the 'not_orderable' list, leaving them unsatisfied.\n\nOption 2: Put bruschetta on 'orderable', couscous on 'not_orderable'.\n- This satisfies everyone who likes bruschetta and dislikes couscous. It also satisfies those who dislike bruschetta, caramel, and couscous (Lex, Vic, and Liz), because they are satisfied with couscous being on the 'not_orderable' list. People who like caramel are also satisfied since bruschetta is available for them.\n\nBased on this analysis, the best option is:\n'orderable': Bruschetta, because it is liked by many and those who dislike it have alternatives.\n'not_orderable': Couscous, because less people like it, and it sa