ied. Thus, "muffins" must go on the not_orderable list.\n\nWith "muffins" now on the not_orderable list, we need to find at least one liked food item to be orderable for those who like muffins.\n\nAll those who liked muffins also like something else, except Ava and Anil:\n\n- Ava (Likes muffins, Dislikes korma, vegetables) is now satisfied by having muffins on the not_orderable list.\n- Anil (Likes muffins, Dislikes burger, korma) is now satisfied by having muffins on the not_orderable list.\n\nSince "burger" is liked by many and is the only other item liked by Lia besides muffins (which have been put on the not_orderable list), "burger" should be on the orderable list.\n\nWith "burger" now on the orderable list, all those who liked burger will be satisfied, but we need to check whether adding it to the orderable list will satisfy others and not dissatisfy the ones who dislike burger.\n\n- By placing "burger" on the orderable list and "muffins" on the not_orderable list, everyone who has liked either will be satisfied.\n- Everyone who dislikes burger is satisfied by other items that they like, which are not exclusive (like vegetables, korma), or they are already satisfied because something they dislike is on the not_orderable list (muffins).\n\nNow that we have "muffins" on the not_orderable list and "burger" on the orderable list, we\'ve taken into account everyone\'s key likes and dislikes. This combination should satisfy all individuals based on the problem criteria:\n\n1. Everyone who likes muffins or burger has at least one other item satisfied.\n2. Cy, who dislikes all, is satisfied because muffins are on the not_orderable list.\n3. All other dislikes are covered since they are not unanimously disliked.\n\nThe final lists, therefore, stand as:\norderable = ["burger"]\nnot_orderable = ["muffins"]\n\nPeople who disliked both muffins and burger are satisfied because muffins are not being ordered. Moreover, those who only disliked burger are also satisfied because