akes, cheesecake. Ian: Likes burrito, waffles. Dislikes pancakes. Sol: Likes pancakes, waffles. Dislikes cheesecake. Zac: Likes vindaloo, udon. Dislikes pancakes. Pat: Likes burrito, vindaloo, naan. Sue: Likes vindaloo. Dislikes burrito, salmon. Hui: Likes naan. Dislikes pancakes, cheesecake. Leo: Likes vindaloo, pancakes. Dislikes udon. ', 'menu_items': ['waffles', 'naan', 'salmon', 'burrito', 'cheesecake', 'vindaloo', 'pancakes', 'udon'], 'gpt_out': "To solve this, we need to ensure that every individual in the group is satisfied based on the criteria provided. Let's list the likes and dislikes together with the additional satisfaction criteria (one liked item must be orderable or one disliked item must be not orderable, no item appearing in both lists, and all must be satisfied) to guide our process.\n\nTo begin, let's consider the following strategies:\n- If a food item is liked by someone and not disliked by anyone, it can safely be put in the 'orderable' list.\n- If a food item is disliked by someone and not liked by anyone, it can safely be put in the 'not_orderable' list.\n- If a food item is liked and disliked by different individuals, we need to decide which list it should go in based on other foods liked or disliked by the involved individuals to satisfy everyone.\n\nWe will have to carefully iterate through the preferences without violating the satisfaction criteria.\n\nLet's start by considering the people that have exclusive likes (likes not shared with dislikes by others):\n- Exclusive likes (and their likers): udon (Cy, Lia, Mei, Neil, Zac), cheesecake (Val, Ken, Wes), naan (Lou, Ula, Qi, Jon, Gia, Fay, Rina, Hui), pancakes (Mel, Sid, Sol), salmon (Lee, Mel, Amy, Qi, Wes, Cal), burrito (Lee, Lou, Ada, Amy, Mei, Ian, Pat), vindaloo (Cy, Lee, Zan, Val, Ada, Zac, Sue), waffles (Ula, Xan, Ian, Sol).\n\nNow, consider exclusive dislikes (dislikes not shared with likes by others):\n- Exclusive dislikes (and their dislikers): pancakes (Ava, Zan, Qi, Om, Ray, Hui), udon (Ava, Bao, Leo), cheesecake (Ada, Hu, Om, Kavi, Ray, Hui), waffles (Lou, Lia, Ken, Sal, Xan).\n\nLet's also look at universal dislikes (if such exist), which everyone or almost everyone dislikes and can be immediately placed in the 'not_orderable' list:\n- There is no universal dislike.\n\nNext, we need to look at the conflicts (items both liked and disliked by different people) and see if we can satisfy both sides:\n- udon: liked by some, disliked by Ava, Bao, Leo, and Hu. However, Ava dislikes pancakes and waffles, which others like, so we can put pancakes and waffles in 'orderable' to satisfy her. Therefore, Ava is satisfied, and we can consider udon for 'orderable'. Leo dislikes udon but likes vindaloo and pancakes, which we may place in 'orderable'. Bao dislikes udon but likes naan, which is exclusively liked and could be in 'orderable'. Hu dislikes udon but also dislikes cheesecake, thus one of his dislikes can go to 'not_orderable' to satisfy him.\n- vindaloo: liked by many but disliked by Sal, Nic, Fay, Sue, and Cal. Since it is liked by many and there is an alternative dislike for Sal (waffles), Nic (cheesecake), Fay (burrito), and Sue (salmon), they could be satisfied without vindaloo. Cal dislikes vindaloo but likes pancakes, which may be placed in 'orderable'. Thus, it is possible to satisfy everyone without having vindaloo on either list.\n\nGiven these criteria, no single item can satisfy everyone, so we must try combinations where every person has at least one like in 'orderable' or one dislike in 'not_orderable'.\n\nLet us now begin by placing food items that are liked by some and not disliked by others in the 'orderable' list. Items such as naan, cheesecake, and pancakes are good candidates for 'orderable' since their likes are not contradicted by universal dislikes.\n- Naan can be orderable (liked by many and solves for Bao).\n- Pancakes and waffles can be orderable since they satisfy multiple individuals and are only disliked by those who have alternatives they like.\n\nNow, let's see if the remaining individuals can be satisfied:\n- Since Naan is orderable, we satisfy Bao, Ula, Qi, Jon, Gia, Fay, Rina, Hui.\n- Since Waffles is orderable, we satisfy Ula, Xan, Ian, Sol.\n- Since Pancakes is orderable, we satisfy Mel, Sid, Sol.\n- Hu dislikes udon, waffles, cheesecake. With pancakes and waffles orderable, he's not satisfied yet. We could place cheesecake in 'not_orderable' to satisfy Hu.\n\nBased on the current selections:\n- Burrito conflicts arise with Lee, Lou, Ada, Amy, Mei, Ian, Pat liking it, and Cy, Lia, Ula, Jan, Kim, Fay, Rina, Bao, Sue, Sid disliking it. Since Ula, Ian, Pat, and Sid are now satisfied with either naan or pancakes/waffles being orderable, they aren't a concern. We can consider putting burrito in 'orderable' to satisfy Lee, Lou, Ada, Amy, Mei, which satisfies a larger group and deals with fewer conflicts since many of the dislikers are already satisfied.\n- Cheesecake is only liked by Ken and Wes now and disliked by Ada, Hu, Om, Kavi, Ray, Hui. Since Ken and Wes have their other likes (naan for Ken and salmon for Wes) already in 'orderable', we can put cheesecake in 'not_orderable' to satisfy the dislikes of many individuals including Hu.\n- Salmon is liked by several with only Zan, Jan, Sue, Cal, Om, Ray, Sue disliking it. Most of the dislikers are dealt with via other items, so we can make salmon 'orderable' for a similar reason.\n- Udon remains with Cy, Lia, Mei, Neil, Zac liking it, dislikers are Ava (satisfied by waffles being orderable), Bao (satisfied by naan being orderable), Leo (can be satisfied by vindaloo or pancakes, which are both candidates for 'orderable'), Hu (satisfied with cheesecake in 'not_orderable'). Thus, udon can be 'orderable'.\n- Vindaloo has a lot of people liking it (Cy, Lee, Zan, Val, Ada, Zac, Sue), and a few dislikers (Sal, Nic, Fay, Sue, Cal) who can be solved without it (Naan for Fay, cheesecake for Nic). We need to ensure Zan, Val, and Sue can be satisfied. With Naan and pancakes/waffles orderable, they don't immediately require vindaloo. Considering this, vindaloo doesn't have to be on either list but could be 'orderable' if other preferences dictate.\n  \nBased on the current combination of preferences, we can establish the following:\n- 'Orderable': naan, pancakes, waffles, burrito, salmon, udon, potentially vindaloo.\n- 'Not_orderable': cheesecake, potentially other items disliked by individuals whose likes are now in 'orderable'.\n\nGiven that each person now has at least one satisfied preference, we have found a solution that satisfies all group members without the need for further iterations:\n\n```\norderable=['naan', 'pancakes', 'waffles', 'burrito', 'salmon', 'udon'],\nnot_orderable=['cheesecake']\n```\n", 'num_prompt_tokens': 721, 'num_completion_tokens': 1585}
Data Sample: {'num_vars': 7, 'num_clauses': 21, 'formula': [[-5, 7, -6], [-1, -4, 2], [6, 1, 3], [-2, 5, -3], [-4, 1, -5], [3, 4, -2], [2, 5, -6], [1, -3, 4], [-5, 2, -6], [-1, 4, 7], [6, 3, -4], [-3, 5, -1], [7, 1, 5], [5, 2, 1], [-2, 3, -7], [7, -2, 3], [4, 6, -3], [-7, 3, 4], [1, -2, -5], [-2, 3, 5], [-6, -4, 1]], 'is_sat': True, 'preferences': 'Hu: Likes tandoori. Dislikes custard, cake. Jim: Likes biscuits. Dislikes naan, spaghetti. Ben: Likes cake, naan, bruschetta. Bea: Likes custard. Dislikes biscuits, bruschetta. Meg: Likes naan. Dislikes spaghetti, custard. Mei: Likes bruschetta, spaghetti. Dislikes biscuits. Liv: Likes biscuits, custard. Dislikes cake. Arun: Likes naan, spaghetti. Dislikes bruschetta. Anil: Likes biscuits. Dislikes custard, cake. Rex: Likes spaghetti, tandoori. Dislikes naan. Ken: Likes cake, bruschetta. Dislikes spaghetti. Ron: Likes custard. Dislikes bruschetta, naan. Ivy: Likes tandoori, naan, custard. Xiu: Likes custard, biscuits, naan. Yan: Likes bruschetta. Dislikes biscuits, tandoori. Shan: Likes tandoori, bruschetta. Dislikes biscuits. Fay: Likes spaghetti, cake. Dislikes bruschetta. Du: Likes bruschetta, spaghetti. Dislikes tandoori. Mel: Likes naan. Dislikes biscuits, custard. Jan: Likes bruschetta, custard. Dislikes biscuits. Wei: Likes naan. Dislikes cake, spaghetti. ', 'menu_items': ['naan', 'biscuits', 'bruschetta', 'spaghetti', 'custard', 'cake', 'tandoori'], 'gpt_out': "To determine the 'orderable' and 'not_orderable' lists, we need to respect the preferences of each individual. The constraints given need to be met for everyone to be satisfied.\n\nLet's look at the dislikes first to see if there's an item that is universally disliked, as that would be a good candidate for the 'not_orderable' list:\n\n- Custard is disliked by Hu, Jim, Meg, Anil, and Mel, but liked by Bea, Liv, Ron, Ivy, Xiu, Jan, and we can not put it in 'not_orderable'.\n- Biscuits are disliked by Bea, Mei, Liv, and Jan, but liked by Jim, Anil, Liv, Xiu. So we can't put biscuits in 'not_orderable' either.\n- Bruschetta is disliked by Hu, Bea, Arun, Ron, Fay, and Du, but liked by Ben, Mei, Yan, Shan, Jan. Again, we can't put bruschetta in 'not_orderable'.\n- Cake is disliked by Hu, Liv, Anil, and Ron, but liked by Ben, Ken, and Fay. We can't put cake in 'not_orderable'.\n- Naan is disliked by Jim, Meg, Ron, and Rex, but liked by Ben, Meg, Arun, Ivy, Wei, Xiu, and Mel. Hence, we can't put naan in 'not_orderable'.\n- Spaghetti is disliked by Jim, Meg, Ken, and Wei, but liked by Mei, Rex, Arun, Fay, and Du. We can not put it in 'not_orderable'.\n- Tandoori is disliked by Yan and Du, but liked by Hu, Rex, Ivy, Shan. We can not put it in 'not_orderable'.\n\nSince no food item is universally disliked, we cannot easily fill our 'not_orderable' list initially. \nAs a result, we must look at the likes and work our way towards creating an 'orderable' list first, being careful not to list an item that is liked by someone and disliked by someone else at the same time.\n\nFrom the likes, we can try to find an item that only one person likes and nobody else dislikes. For example:\n- Tandoori is liked by Hu, Rex, Ivy, and Shan. Only Yan and Du dislike it, and their likes (bruschetta for Yan and spaghetti, bruschetta for Du) are liked by other people too. Thus, putting tandoori in the 'orderable' list may be a safe choice.\n- Biscuits are liked by Jim, Anil, Liv, and Xiu. Disliked by Bea, Mei, and Jan. Xiu, Liv, and Anil can still be satisfied with their likes and dislikes for custard and cake, respectively. So it's likely safe to put biscuits in the 'orderable' list.\n\nLet's place tandoori and biscuits in the 'orderable' list:\n\norderable: ['tandoori', 'biscuits']\n\nWe now need to ensure that all individuals who have tandoori and biscuits as their dislikes can be satisfied by making sure at least one item they do like is on the 'orderable' list or an item they dislike is on 'not_orderable' list.\n\n- Yan dislikes tandoori but likes bruschetta. We cannot decide on bruschetta yet since others dislike it.\n- Du dislikes tandoori but likes spaghetti and bruschetta. We cannot decide yet on spaghetti or bruschetta since others dislike them.\n\nNow we have to iterate over the preferences again, looking for options to satisfy everyone with the list we have started.\n\n- Naan: Is liked by many and only disliked by a few. If we put naan in the 'orderable', we satisfy Ben, Meg, Arun, Ivy, Xiu, and Mel. Jim, Meg, Ron, and Rex dislike naan. We check if these can be satisfied with another item or by 'not_orderable' entries. Ron likes custard, which is liked by many and dislikes naan and bruschetta; we cannot satisfy him with other likes. Rex likes spaghetti and dislikes naan, but there's a conflict with spaghetti (liked by others).\n- Custard is liked by several people but also disliked by several. It's not safe to choose this yet.\n- Spaghetti: We cannot choose yet, given that it is liked by some and disliked by others.\n- Bruschetta has the same issue as spaghetti, with conflicts on both sides.\n\nTo satisfy the most people, we may try adding 'naan' to 'orderable', as it has the most likes minus dislikes ratio, and check if there's a way to satisfy those who dislike it:\n\norderable: ['tandoori', 'biscuits', 'naan']\n\nNow let's satisfy the ones who dislike naan (Jim, Ron, Rex):\n\n- Jim: Dislikes naan but likes biscuits, which are already in 'orderable'. Thus, Jim is satisfied.\n- Ron: Dislikes naan, but we cannot satisfy him because his like, custard, cannot be placed in either list due to the number of people who like/dislike it.\n- Rex: Dislikes naan but likes spaghetti and tandoori. Tandoori is already in 'orderable', so Rex is satisfied.\n\nAt this point, Ron is the only individual we have not satisfied, and he dislikes both naan and bruschetta, which others like. And his only 'like' is custard which we cannot place in 'orderable' as Bea and Liv are not satisfied yet. We will attempt to satisfy Ron by putting 'cake' to 'not_orderable' since it's his dislike and Bea and Liv likes:\n\nnot_orderable: ['cake']\n\nNow, let's see if this arrangement works:\n- Hu can be satisfied with tandoori (likes) being orderable.\n- Jim can be satisfied with biscuits (likes) being orderable.\n- Ben would require naan (likes) to be orderable, which it is.\n- Bea can be satisfied with custard (likes) being neither list, but her dislike cake is in 'not_orderable'.\n- Meg would require naan (likes) to be orderable, which it is.\n- Mei would require bruschetta or spaghetti (likes), and we don’t have those in any list. So we can’t yet satisfy Mei.\n- Liv can be satisfied with biscuits (likes) being orderable.\n- Arun would require naan or spaghetti (likes) to be orderable, naan is on the list.\n- Anil can’t be satisfied with biscuits (likes) being orderable.\n- Rex can be satisfied with tandoori (likes) being orderable.\n- Ken won’t be satisfied, none of his like is in the 'orderable'. We fail here.\n- Ron can be satisfied since cake (dislikes) is in 'not_orderable'.\n- Ivy would be satisfied with naan or custard (likes) being orderable and tandoori is also in 'orderable'.\n- Xiu is okay with biscuits (likes) being orderable.\n- Yan requires bruschetta (likes), which isn't catered for yet. We fail here.\n- Shan would need bruschetta or tandoori, tandoori is in the list.\n- Fay can't be satisfied since cake (likes) is in 'not_orderable'. We fail here.\n- Du requires bruschetta or spaghetti (likes), which aren't catered for yet. We fail here.\n- Mel would be satisfied with naan (likes) being orderable.\n- Jan would need bruschetta or custard (likes), which isn't catered to yet. We fail here.\n- Wei can be satisfied with naan (likes) being orderable.\n\nHowever, since this arrangement fails to satisfy all participants, we must conclude that it is not possible to create two distinct lists of 'orderable' and 'not_orderable' based on their preferences while making everyone satisfied.\n\nTherefore, the final lists are empty:\n\n```python\norderable = [], not_orderable = []\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 474, 'num_completion_tokens': 1690}
