 by a few. Placing "nachos" in \'orderable\' will satisfy a majority of the participants.\n2. "Frittata" is often disliked, and not as many people specifically like it. If we can place it in \'not_orderable\', it will satisfy those who dislike it but we must check if this conflicts with those who like it.\n3. "Vegetables" are liked by many, so putting them in \'not_orderable\' would displease a lot of participants. Conversely, several people also dislike vegetables; however, they are in the minority.\n4. "Toast" and "macaron" are somewhat mixed, making them more difficult to categorize without considering individual cases.\n\nGiven the satisfaction criteria and to ensure cover for everyone\'s preferences, we will select "nachos" for \'orderable\', since they are liked by a very large number of participants and only disliked by a few. We\'ll keep an eye on those who dislike nachos to make sure they have another item in the \'orderable\' list they like or have something they dislike in \'not_orderable\'.\n\nLet\'s begin listing "nachos" as \'orderable\' and then work on the others. We\'ll also list "frittata" as \'not_orderable\' since a significant subset dislikes it and fewer people have it as a liked item. As we do this, we must ensure that no one is left unsatisfied (each one must like at least one item in \'orderable\' or dislike one in \'not_orderable\').\n\nPutting "nachos" in \'orderable\' satisfies: Taj, Kai, Ansh, Ava, Tim, Bao, Bo, Sky, Hui, Geet, Tara, Cal, Dan, Diya, Rina, Naina, Jun, Meg, Sal, and possibly others who like nachos.\n\nPutting "frittata" in \'not_orderable\' satisfies: Taj, Ray, Isha, Chen, Joe, Ava, Rob, Tom, Mei, Jay, Om, Gus, Tara, Liv, Wei, Max, and Ravi.\n\nNow let\'s move to "vegetables", which are liked by many. However, since "frittata" is already in \'not_orderable\', those who like "vegetables" (Kim, Isha, Ansh, Rob, Bo, Sky, Gen, Ned, Feng, Mia, Om, Tara, Veer, Diya, Nita, and Ivy) will be satisfied because "frittata" is on their dislike list. Therefore, we do not need to place "vegetables" in \'orderable\'.\n\nFor "toast", it is disliked by several participants and only absolutely needed by Joe and perhaps a few others who dislike both other items but like toast to be satisfied.\n\nLastly, "macaron" is liked by some and disliked by others, making it a tricky choice.\n\nLet\'s try to satisfy those who haven\'t been covered yet by placing "toast" in \'not_orderable\' since it\'s disliked by most (and therefore likely to satisfy a few remaining people who haven\'t been satisfied yet), and "macaron" in \'orderable\' to satisfy the macaron lovers because there seem to be fewer conflicts.\n\nUpon a complete analysis, we can see that:\n- "nachos" being \'orderable\' satisfy a large group.\n- "frittata" being \'not_orderable\' also satisfies those who dislike it.\n- "vegetables" can be placed in any list since everyone who likes them is already satisfied by "frittata" being \'not_orderable\'.\n- "toast" and "macaron" can satisfy remaining individuals by being in \'not_orderable\' and \'orderable\' respectively.\n\nNow let\'s check individuals who might still not be satisfied:\n- Since "nachos" are \'orderable\', anyone who dislikes nachos must have another dislike in \'not_orderable\' such as "frittata" or a like in \'orderable\'.\n- Everyone who has "toast" as a dislike is covered because it\'s in \'not_orderable\'.\n- People who dislike "macaron" should have another dislike or a like covered elsewhere.\n\nGiven that every individual has at least one item they like in \'orderable\' or dislike in \'not_orderable\', we can establish our final lists without violating any of the satisfaction criteria.\n\nThe final solution is:\n```python\norderable=[\'nachos\', \'macaron\'], not_orderable=[\'frittata\', \'toast\']\n```\n\nThis solution satisfies all participants since everyone has at least one liked item in \'orderable\' or one disliked item in \'not_orderable\', fulfilling the criteria outlined initially.', 'num_prompt_tokens': 862, 'num_completion_tokens': 1147}
Data Sample: {'num_vars': 5, 'num_clauses': 10, 'formula': [[5, 3, -2], [-1, 2, 3], [4, -3, -1], [3, 1, -4], [1, -5, 2], [-5, -2, 1], [1, -4, 3], [-3, -1, -5], [-2, -1, 4], [-1, -3, 2]], 'is_sat': True, 'preferences': 'Ru: Likes tacos, bruschetta. Dislikes pie. Hu: Likes pie, bruschetta. Dislikes burger. Tara: Likes poke. Dislikes bruschetta, burger. Mia: Likes bruschetta, burger. Dislikes poke. Leo: Likes burger, pie. Dislikes tacos. Bo: Likes burger. Dislikes tacos, pie. Diya: Likes burger, bruschetta. Dislikes poke. Kit: Dislikes bruschetta, burger, tacos. Lex: Likes poke. Dislikes pie, burger. Sue: Likes pie. Dislikes burger, bruschetta. ', 'menu_items': ['burger', 'pie', 'bruschetta', 'poke', 'tacos'], 'gpt_out': "Let's consider each individual's preferences and determine a combination that could satisfy them all. Our goal is to have at least one liked food in the 'orderable' list or one disliked food in the 'not_orderable' list for each person.\n\n1. Ru likes tacos and bruschetta, dislikes pie.\n   - Pie can go into 'not_orderable' to satisfy Ru since they dislike it.\n   \n2. Hu likes pie and bruschetta, dislikes burger.\n   - Burger can go into 'not_orderable' to satisfy Hu since they dislike it.\n   \n3. Tara likes poke, dislikes bruschetta and burger.\n   - Poke must go into 'orderable' to satisfy Tara since their dislikes are already chosen for 'not_orderable'.\n\n4. Mia likes bruschetta and burger, dislikes poke.\n   - Bruschetta can go into 'orderable' to satisfy Mia since poke is already in 'orderable'.\n\n5. Leo likes burger and pie, dislikes tacos.\n   - Pie can go into 'orderable' to satisfy Leo since burger is not an option (it's in 'not_orderable') and his dislike, tacos, is not yet decided.\n\n6. Bo likes burger, dislikes tacos and pie.\n   - We can't add burger to 'orderable' because it's already in 'not_orderable', but we can add tacos to 'not_orderable' since Bo dislikes it.\n\n7. Diya likes burger and bruschetta, dislikes poke.\n   - Bruschetta is in 'orderable'; Diya is satisfied.\n\n8. Kit dislikes bruschetta, burger, and tacos.\n   - Since Kit only has dislikes and bruschetta, burger, and tacos are all already placed appropriately, Kit is satisfied.\n\n9. Lex likes poke, dislikes pie and burger.\n   - Poke is in 'orderable'; Lex is satisfied.\n\n10. Sue likes pie, dislikes burger and bruschetta.\n    - Pie is in 'orderable' and burger is in 'not_orderable'; Sue is satisfied.\n\nLet's summarize and confirm:\n\nOrderable: poke, bruschetta, pie\nNot_orderable: burger, tacos\n\nEvery person now has either an item they like in 'orderable' or an item they dislike in 'not_orderable' and no item appears on both lists. Therefore, we have a solution that satisfies all participants.\n\nOutput in Python list format:\n\n```python\norderable=['poke', 'bruschetta', 'pie'], not_orderable=['burger', 'tacos']\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 316, 'num_completion_tokens': 520}
Data Sample: {'num_vars': 4, 'num_clauses': 12, 'formula': [[2, -4, 3], [-3, -1, -4], [-2, 4, 3], [3, -2, 1], [1, -3, -4], [1, 4, 2], [-2, 4, -1], [1, 2, -3], [3, 4, 2], [4, -3, 2], [3, -2, -4], [-1, -2, 3]], 'is_sat': True, 'preferences': 'Riya: Likes tart, tandoori. Dislikes brownies. Zoe: Dislikes tandoori, nachos, brownies. Pia: Likes brownies, tandoori. Dislikes tart. Kavi: Likes tandoori, nachos. Dislikes tart. Hui: Likes nachos. Dislikes tandoori, brownies. Ula: Likes nachos, brownies, tart. Lin: Likes brownies. Dislikes tart, nachos. Oli: Likes nachos, tart. Dislikes tandoori. Rina: Likes tandoori, brownies, tart. Kim: Likes brownies, tart. Dislikes tandoori. Cal: Likes tandoori. Dislikes tart, brownies. Isha: Likes tandoori. Dislikes nachos, tart. ', 'menu_items': ['nachos', 'tart', 'tandoori', 'brownies'], 'gpt_out': "Step 1: Compile the likes and dislikes for each person to understand the conditions for their satisfaction.\n- Riya: Likes tart, tandoori. Dislikes brownies.\n- Zoe: Dislikes tandoori, nachos, brownies.\n- Pia: Likes brownies, tandoori. Dislikes tart.\n- Kavi: Likes tandoori, nachos. Dislikes tart.\n- Hui: Likes nachos. Dislikes tandoori, brownies.\n- Ula: Likes nachos, brownies, tart.\n- Lin: Likes brownies. Dislikes tart, nachos.\n- Oli: Likes nachos, tart. Dislikes tandoori.\n- Rina: Likes tandoori, brownies, tart.\n- Kim: Likes brownies, tart. Dislikes tandoori.\n- Cal: Likes tandoori. Dislikes tart, brownies.\n- Isha: Likes tandoori. Dislikes nachos, tart.\n\nStep 2: Identify conflicting likes and dislikes.\n- Tandoori is liked by many but is disliked by some (Zoe, Hui, Oli, Kim).\n- Brownies are also liked by many but disliked by Riya and Hui.\n- Tart is liked by several but disliked by others (Pia, Kavi, Lin, Cal, Isha).\n- Nachos are liked by some and disliked by others (Zoe, Hui, Lin, Isha).\n\nStep 3: Figure out the possible 'orderable' and 'not_orderable' items respecting the rules.\n- We cannot have an item that is both liked and disliked by different people in 'orderable' list since that would not satisfy all.\n- Similarly, we cannot have an item that is both liked and disliked by different people in 'not_orderable' list.\n\nStep 4: The key is to identify at least one item for each person that satisfies them, either something they like that can be ordered or something they dislike that cannot be ordered.\n\nStep 5: Find something that satisfies all.\n- Tandoori cannot be on the 'orderable' list because it's disliked by Zoe, Hui, Oli, and Kim, but no other likes are in conflict, and thus it can be on the 'not_orderable' list to satisfy them.\n- Brownies are liked by Pia, Ula, Lin, Rina, and Kim, and also disliked by Hui and Riya. However, since we have other likes for these respective people that could go on the 'orderable' list, we can leave brownies out of both lists to avoid conflicts.\n- Tart is liked by Riya, Ula, Rina, Kim, and Oli but disliked by Pia, Kavi, Lin, Cal, and Isha. Similar to brownies, if we satisfy those who dislike tart with something else, we can leave it out of both lists.\n- Nachos is liked by Kavi, Hui, Ula, and Oli but disliked by Zoe, Lin, and Isha. Since there are no conflicts with what Hui and Ola like, we can place nachos in the 'orderable' list to satisfy them.\n\nConclusion:\nWe will place tandoori on the 'not_orderable' list to satisfy Zoe, Hui, Oli, and Kim. We will place nachos on the 'orderable' list to satisfy Kavi, Hui, Ula, and Oli. For everyone else, we can omit their likes and dislikes from the lists because they have at least one other item that satisfies them.\nTherefore, the solution is:\n\norderable = ['nachos']\nnot_orderable = ['tandoori']\n\nLet's output it in Python code as requested:\n\norderable=['nachos'], not_orderable=['tandoori']", 'num_prompt_tokens': 363, 'num_completion_tokens': 794}
Data Sample: {'num_vars': 4, 'num_clauses': 32, 'formula': [[-1, 3, 2], [-3, -1, 4], [3, -4, 2], [-3, -4, 2], [4, -2, 3], [-1, 2, -3], [-4, 2, -1], [2, 1, -4], [2, 4, 3], [-4, -3, 1], [4, -1, -3], [1, 4, -3], [1, -2, -3], [1, 4, 3], [2, -4, 1], [2, 1, -3], [-3, -4, 2], [4, 1, 2], [3, -1, -2], [3, -1, -2], [-4, 3, 2], [2, 1, 3], [4, 1, 2], [3, -1, 2], [1, 3, 4], [1, 2, -4], [-4, -3, 1], [1, -2, 4], [4, 1, -2], [1, -3, -4], [1, 4, -2], [4, 2, 1]], 'is_sat': True, 'preferences': 'Jun: Likes ramen, doughnut. Dislikes korma. Eli: Likes spaghetti. Dislikes ramen, korma. Du: Likes ramen, doughnut. Dislikes spaghetti. Hui: Likes doughnut. Dislikes ramen, spaghetti. Ty: Likes spaghetti, ramen. Dislikes doughnut. Jan: Likes doughnut. Dislikes korma, ramen. Lan: Likes doughnut. Dislikes spaghetti, korma. Sam: Likes doughnut, korma. Dislikes spaghetti. Jim: Likes doughnut, spaghetti, ramen. Bo: Likes korma. Dislikes spaghetti, ramen. Arun: Likes spaghetti. Dislikes korma, ramen. Sky: Likes korma, spaghetti. Dislikes ramen. Pam: Likes korma. Dislikes doughnut, ramen. Dan: Likes korma, spaghetti, ramen. Ava: Likes doughnut, korma. Dislikes spaghetti. Raj: Likes doughnut, korma. Dislikes ramen. Ray: Likes doughnut. Dislikes ramen, spaghetti. Riya: Likes spaghetti, korma, doughnut. Chen: Likes ramen. Dislikes korma, doughnut. Shan: Likes ramen. Dislikes korma, doughnut. Kavi: Likes ramen, doughnut. Dislikes spaghetti. Eva: Likes doughnut, korma, ramen. Kai: Likes spaghetti, korma, doughnut. Sal: Likes ramen, doughnut. Dislikes korma. Joe: Likes korma, ramen, spaghetti. May: Likes korma, doughnut. Dislikes spaghetti. Sol: Likes korma. Dislikes spaghetti, ramen. Rina: Likes korma, spaghetti. Dislikes doughnut. Pia: Likes spaghetti, korma. Dislikes doughnut. Rohan: Likes korma. Dislikes ramen, spaghetti. Mel: Likes korma, spaghetti. Dislikes doughnut. Xan: Likes spaghetti, doughnut, korma. ', 'menu_items': ['korma', 'doughnut', 'ramen', 'spaghetti'], 'gpt_out': "To solve this task, I will evaluate the likes and dislikes of each individual and iteratively come up with a combination of 'orderable' and 'not_orderable' lists to try to satisfy everyone's preferences based on the provided criteria. \n\nLet's begin by counting how many people like and dislike each food item:\n\n- Ramen: Likes (Jun, Du, Ty, Jim, Chen, Shan, Kavi, Sal, Eva, Joe) = 10 ; Dislikes (Eli, Hui, Jan, Bo, Arun, Sky, Pam, Raj, Ray, Rohan, Sol) = 11\n- Doughnut: Likes (Jun, Du, Hui, Jan, Lan, Sam, Jim, Ava, Raj, Ray, Kavi, Eva, Kai, May, Xan) = 15 ; Dislikes (Ty, Pam, Rina, Pia, Mel) = 5\n- Spaghetti: Likes (Eli, Ty, Jim, Arun, Sky, Kai, Xan, Joe, Rina, Pia, Mel) = 11 ; Dislikes (Du, Hui, Sam, Bo, Ava, Ray, Dan, May, Rohan, Sol) = 10\n- Korma: Likes (Bo, Sam, Sky, Pam, Dan, Ava, Raj, Ema, Kai, Joe, May, Rina, Pia, Rohan, Mel, Xan) = 16 ; Dislikes (Jun, Eli, Jan, Lan, Sal, Shan, Chen) = 7\n\nNext, we try to include at least one liked item in the 'orderable' list or one disliked item in the 'not_orderable' list for each person.\n\n1. Any common dislikes that we can put into 'not_orderable'?\n   - Ramen is disliked by the most individuals, so we'll place it into 'not_orderable'.\n\n2. Is there any food that only has likes and no dislikes or vice versa, such that it can easily fit into one of the lists without causing dissatisfaction?\n   - No, all foods have both likes and dislikes.\n\n3. Now, we need to include an item liked by everyone who dislikes ramen into the 'orderable' list or into 'not_orderable' if it is their disliked item.\n   - The people who dislike ramen like either spaghetti, korma, doughnut, or a combination of these.\n   - Raj and Pam like both doughnut and korma, so we could consider one of these for 'orderable' to satisfy them along with Ray who also dislikes ramen but likes doughnuts. \n   \n4. If doughnut is in the 'orderable' list, will everyone be satisfied?\n   - Yes, because doughnut is liked by more people than it's disliked.\n\nGiven these notes, we can tentatively put 'ramen' in 'not_orderable', and 'doughnut' in 'orderable'. Now we need to double-check if this satisfies everyone's preferences:\n\n- 'not_orderable': [ramen]\n- 'orderable': [doughnut]\n\nEvery individual who dislikes ramen would be satisfied because ramen is not orderable. Likewise, every individual who likes doughnuts would be satisfied. Since 'ramen' is the only item with no overlapping likes, placing it in 'not_orderable' should not cause any dissatisfaction.\n\nNow, if we organize this into two lists, we would have:\n\n- 'orderable': [doughnut]\n- 'not_orderable': [ramen]\n\nWith these two choices, let's ensure that all individuals' conditions are met:\n- All those who dislike ramen are satisfied by having it on the 'not_orderable' list.\n- All those who like doughnuts are satisfied by having it on the 'orderable' list.\n- All other items (spaghetti and korma) are not involved, so we don't worry about satisfying them yet.\n\nSo far, every individual is satisfied. We should now check if there's a remaining unsatisfied individual because they do not have a liked item on the 'orderable' list or a disliked item on the 'not_orderable' list:\n\nConsidering spaghetti and korma:\n- All individuals who dislike spaghetti have either doughnuts or korma that they like (or ramen on the not orderable list).\n- All individuals who like spaghetti are either okay with doughnuts or okay with ramen being off the table.\n\nWe do not need to include spaghetti on any list, as its absence does not cause dissatisfaction for anyone.\n\nFor korma:\n- All individuals who dislike korma (Jun, Eli, Jan, Lan, Sal, Shan, Chen) are satisfied with doughnuts being available.\n\nFinally, we check if placing spaghetti and korma on the 'neutral' list, not being ordered but also not being explicitly not orderable, will satisfy everyone. Since all individuals are satisfied with the currently 'orderable' and 'not_orderable' foods, we can confidently declare that everyone's criteria are met without needing to include spaghetti or korma on either list.\n\nTherefore, we have come up with the following lists:\n\n```python\norderable=['doughnut'], not_orderable=['ramen']\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 636, 'num_completion_tokens': 1089}
