duals.\n\n2. I\'ll go through each item and see if there\'s a clear decision for it:\n   - "bruschetta" can be placed in \'not_orderable\' because everyone who likes it (Li, Shan, Jun, Veer, Bao, Ian, Ru, Qi) dislikes something else.\n   - "peach" cannot be placed in \'orderable\' or \'not_orderable\' because some people who like it dislike nothing that could be orderable and some who dislike it like nothing that could be not_orderable.\n\nGiven that, I\'ll start assigning the clear-cut items and see if we can meet all criteria.\n\nLet\'s start by putting "bruschetta" in \'not_orderable\':\n   \n   not_orderable = [\'bruschetta\']\n\nNow, no one who likes "bruschetta" will be unsatisfied, so we don\'t need to worry about putting something they like in \'orderable\'. Similarly, anyone who dislikes "bruschetta" is already satisfied. We will now check the next item considering "bruschetta" is not orderable.\n\nNext, we can consider "waffles" since there is at least one person (Zoe, dislikes caramel) who dislikes it and does not have an alternative like. Therefore, we cannot fully satisfy everyone\'s dislikes with "waffles", so we should try to satisfy the likes instead.\n\n"tacos" and "macaron" are liked by some who dislike nothing else (Cy dislikes only orderable items), so they must be in \'orderable\' to satisfy those individuals.\n\n   orderable = [\'tacos\', \'macaron\']\n\nThis takes care of the people whose only likes are tacos and macaron.\n\nSince "caramel" is liked by some who dislike only "peach", which has not been placed in either list, we cannot make a decision for "caramel" yet.\n\nFor "curry" and "peach", we face a similar problem, they are liked and disliked by individuals who do not have any other preferences for the items that could be in \'not_orderable\'.\n\nSo, let\'s look at "waffles" again now that we know "tacos" and "macaron" are orderable. We see that everyone who likes "waffles" has something else they like that can be ordered or dislikes something that can be not orderable (for example, Ula likes curry, and Veer dislikes caramel). Thus, we can put "waffles" in \'not_orderable\':\n\n   not_orderable = [\'bruschetta\', \'waffles\']\n\nNow let\'s process the last item "caramel". Every individual who dislikes "caramel" satisfies one of our conditions (they either like "macaron" or "tacos"). So, we can put “caramel” in \'orderable\':\n\n   orderable = [\'tacos\', \'macaron\', \'caramel\']\n\nWith "caramel" now in \'orderable\', we have placed all items except "peach" and "curry". Returning to "peach", we see that Ray dislikes "bruschetta", which is in \'not_orderable\', so Ray is satisfied. This fact also satisfies others who like "peach" since their dislikes are covered in \'not_orderable\'. No one likes "peach" exclusively now; thus, we can safely place it in \'not_orderable\':\n\n   not_orderable = [\'bruschetta\', \'waffles\', \'peach\']\n\nFor "curry", everyone who likes "curry", dislikes "tacos", "waffles", "bruschetta", or "peach", which have already been accounted for in \'orderable\' or \'not_orderable\'. That means "curry" can be placed in \'orderable\':\n\n   orderable = [\'tacos\', \'macaron\', \'caramel\', \'curry\']\n\nNow that all items are accounted for, every person should be satisfied. Here\'s the final solution in Python code:\n\n```python\norderable=[\'tacos\', \'macaron\', \'caramel\', \'curry\'], not_orderable=[\'bruschetta\', \'waffles\', \'peach\']\n```', 'num_prompt_tokens': 809, 'num_completion_tokens': 1094}
