iked by many, which makes it a conflict item.\n- Vindaloo is also widely disliked by the group but liked by some.\n- Burrito, salmon, risotto, and curry are liked and disliked by several people.\n- Lasagna is liked by a few but disliked by many - this could be a potential candidate for 'not_orderable'.\n- Bruschetta is also a candidate for 'not_orderable' since it's disliked by some and liked by fewer than lasagna.\n\nIt becomes clear that with the numerous overlaps in likes and dislikes, putting a disliked item in the 'not_orderable' list might displease someone else who likes it.\n\nGiven the complexity with the conflicting preferences, the only way to satisfy everyone is to resort to a logical resolution based on trial and error.\n\nTo resolve this systematically, we ensure the following:\n\n1. An item can go to 'orderable' if it's liked by at least one person and not disliked by anyone.\n2. An item can go to 'not_orderable' if it's disliked by at least one person and not liked by anyone.\n\nUnfortunately, such straightforward candidates do not exist in this case.\n\nHere are the sequential steps we can take:\n- Consider first the items disliked by many and liked by few.\n- Start with one such item, place it in 'not_orderable', and try to satisfy others with their likes.\n- If we encounter a necessity to place an item that is both liked and disliked into 'orderable', we must not have it in 'not_orderable' already.\n\nUpon reviewing the list multiple times and considering the extensive overlaps, we find that no combination of the two lists can be made without violating someone's preferences. \n\nThis is confirmed by the fact that widespread dislikes such as roti, vindaloo, burrito, salmon, risotto, curry, and pie are also among the widely liked items. The conflicts are too many to resolve while satisfying all conditions.\n\nTherefore, we output empty lists for both 'orderable' and 'not_orderable':\n\n```python\norderable = []\nnot_orderable = []\n```", 'num_prompt