g the criteria.\n- Spaghetti is liked by some (Zac, Ying, Gen, Rex, Eli, Neil, Liv), and all who dislike it (Du, Zara, Hal, Chen, Ava) dislike another item that could potentially not be ordered. Since cake is potentially not orderable, those who like spaghetti and cake will be satisfied.\n- Quiche is liked by those who dislike spaghetti or cake, which potentially can be on the opposite list, making them satisfied. Therefore, quiche could also be \'orderable\'.\n- Macaron is disliked by some and liked by others, but there\'s no person who only likes macaron and dislikes everything else, so there is flexibility with this being \'orderable\' or \'not_orderable\'.\n- Tandoori is liked by many, and those who dislike it either have an alternative like or dislike. Hence, it can be \'orderable\'.\n\nNow we can try to form our lists while avoiding any conflicts. Here\'s one possible solution where we ensure at least one like or one dislike for each person:\n\norderable:\n- Brownies\n- Quiche\n- Tandoori\n- Kebab\n- Spaghetti\n\nnot_orderable:\n- Cake\n- Macaron\n\nThis list will leave those who like "Brownies," "Quiche," "Tandoori," "Kebab," and "Spaghetti" satisfied with their likes being \'orderable\', and the people who dislike "Cake" and "Macaron" will be satisfied because they are \'not_orderable\'.\n\nLet\'s output these lists in the required format:\n\n```python\norderable=["Brownies", "Quiche", "Tandoori", "Kebab", "Spaghetti"], not_orderable=["Cake", "Macaron"]\n```', 'num_prompt_tokens': 656, 'num_completion_tokens': 758}
