sider for \'not_orderable\'.\n6. "cheesecake" has much fewer dislikes – likely \'orderable\'.\n7. "vindaloo" has strong dislikes – consider for \'not_orderable\'.\n8. "nachos", "waffles", "poke" have less conflict but require further consideration.\n\nLet’s consider individual conflicts and resolve them:\n\n- For individuals liking "doughnut", "ramen" can satisfy Ula, Tim, and Raj. "cheesecake" can satisfy Gen, Veer, and Sal.\n- For individuals liking "naan", other likes might be satisfied by items like "cheesecake", "curry", "vindaloo".\n- Individuals liking "ramen" who cannot be satisfied due to their dislikes will determine if "ramen" goes to \'orderable\' or \'not_orderable\'.\n\nLet\'s make "curry", "cheesecake", "vindaloo" as \'orderable\' to satisfy most individuals:\n\n\'orderable\': ["curry", "cheesecake", "vindaloo"]\n\nLet\'s now consider more candidates for \'not_orderable\':\n\n- Place "doughnut" in \'not_orderable\' to satisfy its many dislikes.\n- "naan" and "hummus" also have significant dislikes, add them to \'not_orderable\'.\n\n\'not_orderable\': ["doughnut", "naan", "hummus"]\n\nNow, check to ensure all are satisfied:\n- Everyone who liked "doughnut" has an alternative in "curry", "cheesecake", or "vindaloo".\n- Those who liked "naan" have alternatives too.\n- "hummus" dislikes are taken care of and likers have alternatives.\n- "ramen" should be \'orderable\' to address the likers since their dislikes are covered.\n- Add "ramen" to \'orderable\'.\n\n\'orderable\': ["curry", "cheesecake", "vindaloo", "ramen"]\n\nNow, look for any individual not satisfied by the current \'orderable\' list or whose dislikes are not in \'not_orderable\'. Since all likes have been checked for conflicts with the dislikes, and there seems to be no conflict with our current lists, we can assume that this configuration will satisfy everyone.\n\nFinal solution:\n\n```python\norderable=["curry", "cheesecake", "vindaloo", "ramen"], not_orderable=["doughnut", "naan", "hummus"