e alternative likes which should be considered for the "orderable".\n- For everyone liking salmon, Vic, Veer, Yan, Joy, Lei, Joe, Lex, Jaya, Mel, and Neil, we must ensure one of their likes are in the "orderable" list or another of their dislikes is on the "not_orderable".\n\nLet\'s select kimchi and fries for the "orderable" list because they\'re liked by many and have fewer dislikes. We\'ll try to satisfy the rest with these choices.\n\n`orderable = [\'kimchi\', \'fries\']`\n\nNow we\'ll go through the entire list and see if everyone is at least satisfied with one item from either "orderable" or "not_orderable". If someone\'s preferences cannot be accommodated, we\'ll have to make adjustments.\n\nAfter reviewing the preferences, we see that all individuals who like salmon or caramel have other likes (e.g., Veer likes burrito and kimchi, Rohan likes quiche, Rex likes quiche) that can be placed on the "orderable" list. We will also place burrito in the "orderable" list to satisfy the individuals who like it and because it does not have as many dislikes as some other options such as pancakes, soup, or quiche which debate a lot among many individuals.\n\nWith the addition of burrito, we will double-check that everyone who dislikes burrito will be satisfied with either fries or kimchi in the "orderable" list. After checking, it can be seen that everyone satisfied by burrito also likes fries or kimchi. This should cover everyone\'s dislikes as well with salmon and caramel in the "not_orderable" list.\n\nFinal "orderable" and "not_orderable" lists would therefore be:\n`orderable = [\'kimchi\', \'fries\', \'burrito\']`\n`not_orderable = [\'salmon\', \'caramel\']`\n\nThis should satisfy all participants:\n\n1. Those who like items in "orderable" have at least one item they like.\n2. Those who dislike items in "not_orderable" have at least one item they dislike.\n\nTherefore, we can conclude the resulting lists as:\n\n```python\norderable=[\'kimchi\', \'fries\', \'burrito\'