d to ensure their satisfaction with other items.\n- frittata: Multiple likes and dislikes. Putting frittata in 'orderable' or 'not_orderable' list needs careful consideration to not dissatisfy others.\n- kimchi: Liked by several individuals, but disliked by many as well. Cannot singularly decide its placement yet.\n- korma: Liked by several individuals and only a few dislike it. It could potentially be an 'orderable' item.\n- kebab: Liked by many, and only a few dislike it. Similar to korma, could potentially be an 'orderable' item.\n- falafel: Liked and disliked by several individuals. Placement depends on the combination of other items.\n- churros: Liked by several individuals and disliked by some as well. We need to ensure that persons who dislike churros find satisfaction in other items if we place it in 'orderable'.\n\nWe should try placing korma and kebab in 'orderable' as only a few people dislike them and they are liked by several individuals. If we put korma and kebab in 'orderable', all individuals who like them (Hu, Dan, Mel, Zoe, Hui, Tom, Ben, Diya, Mia, Sue, Ken, Dot, Ada, Mina, Chao, Taj) will be satisfied.\n\nNow we must ensure that the rest of the individuals are satisfied by placing at least one disliked item in 'not_orderable' or another liked item in 'orderable'. Let's go through individuals who disliked korma or kebab and ensure they have a liked item in 'orderable' or a disliked item in 'not_orderable':\n\n- Ben dislikes korma, but kebab is liked, so satisfied.\n- Cy dislikes korma, but no other likes are satisfied, so we cannot put korma on the 'orderable' list.\n- Diya dislikes kebab, but likes salmon, so if we added salmon to 'orderable', Diya would be satisfied. However, this would anger Joy and Kai who dislike salmon, and we must ensure they have a liked item on 'orderable' or disliked on 'not_orderable'.\n- Gen dislikes kebab, but likes salmon, putting salmon on 'orderable' satisfies Gen.\n- Lee dislikes kimchi, but likes kebab, so satisfied.\n- Li dislikes kebab, but likes frittata. To satisfy Li, frittata would need to go on 'orderable', but that would be an issue for individuals like Joy, Kai and others who dislike frittata.\n- Mel dislikes salmon, likes frittata, but we cannot place frittata on 'orderable'.\n- Mei dislikes kebab, but likes korma, so satisfied.\n- Mia dislikes kebab but has no other likes, so Mia cannot be satisfied if kebab is 'orderable'.\n- Mina dislikes kimchi, but likes korma, so satisfied.\n- Neil dislikes salmon, but likes churros. We need to consider placing churros on 'orderable' to satisfy Neil, but we must ensure others who dislike churros find some satisfaction.\n\nThere are conflicts that prevent us from having korma and kebab both on the 'orderable' list, as it would not satisfy Cy and Mia. We have a situation where no matter which items we place in 'orderable' or 'not_orderable', there's always at least one individual who will not be satisfied, based on the conflicting likes and dislikes.\n\nSince no such combination exists that satisfies all participants, the final solution is empty lists for both 'orderable' and 'not_orderable'.\n\nOutput:\n```python\norderable=[], not_orderable=[]\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 830, 'num_completion_tokens': 1085}
