ittata. Dislikes burrito. Ivy: Likes risotto. Dislikes poke, frittata. Cy: Likes lobster. Dislikes frittata, poke. Aarav: Likes tandoori, poke, burrito. Gia: Likes burrito, quiche, tandoori. Tara: Likes tandoori. Dislikes salad, frittata. Pam: Likes poke. Dislikes waffles, quiche. Qi: Likes poke. Dislikes lobster, quiche. Mei: Likes poke, burrito. Dislikes quiche. Hal: Likes risotto, burrito. Dislikes salad. Ansh: Dislikes poke, quiche, salad. Leo: Likes frittata. Dislikes waffles, burrito. Ava: Likes lobster. Dislikes risotto, frittata. Isha: Likes lobster, burrito. Dislikes quiche. Gus: Likes risotto. Dislikes tandoori, salad. Ty: Likes tandoori. Dislikes risotto, poke. Ray: Likes quiche. Dislikes salad, poke. Nic: Likes poke, lobster, tandoori. Ian: Likes quiche, tandoori, burrito. Chao: Dislikes poke, quiche, risotto. Ravi: Likes waffles, tandoori, salad. Sam: Likes quiche. Dislikes burrito, frittata. Tia: Dislikes burrito, poke, salad. Bao: Likes risotto. Dislikes waffles, frittata. Tom: Likes burrito, risotto, salad. Veer: Likes burrito, tandoori. Dislikes salad. Amy: Likes tandoori, salad. Dislikes waffles. Rohan: Likes burrito, quiche, tandoori. ', 'menu_items': ['tandoori', 'burrito', 'waffles', 'frittata', 'poke', 'quiche', 'lobster', 'salad', 'risotto'], 'gpt_out': 'To solve this, I\'ll first list out all the likes and dislikes and attempt to find a combination that satisfies everyone. Let\'s start with the food items that are universally disliked, since they can be safely placed in the \'not_orderable\' list without preventing anyone from being satisfied. Conversely, we\'ll look for any items that are universally liked by those who have a preference for them, as these can go in the \'orderable\' list.\n\nFirst, let\'s make a tally of how many people like and dislike each food:\n\n- Burrito: Likes: 13, Dislikes: 6\n- Poke: Likes: 9, Dislikes: 5\n- Salad: Likes: 6, Dislikes: 9\n- Tandoori: Likes: 21, Dislikes: 8\n- Frittata: Likes: 6, Dislikes: 8\n- Waffles: Likes: 11, Dislikes: 5\n- Quiche: Likes: 10, Dislikes: 7\n- Risotto: Likes: 12, Dislikes: 10\n- Lobster: Likes: 9, Dislikes: 8\n\nNo food item is universally disliked, so we\'ll need to find a more complex solution that satisfies all parties based on their likes and dislikes.\n\nThe strategy will be to identify the foods that, if placed in the \'orderable\' list, will satisfy the most people and leave no one unsatisfied. We will also try to place foods in the \'not_orderable\' list to satisfy those who dislike them, provided it does not conflict with someone who likes them.\n\nWe can immediately notice that "tandoori" is liked by the most people and it is the only like for some (Geet, Lan, Lee, Riya, Chen, Max, Tara, and Rose). Thus, we must include "tandoori" in the \'orderable\' list to satisfy them.\n\nSince "tandoori" is now in the \'orderable\' list, anyone who dislikes "tandoori" must have another like in \'orderable\' or another dislike in \'not_orderable\'.\n\nLet\'s move \'tandoori\' to \'orderable\' and look for the next steps:\n\n- Shan is now unsatisfied, but dislikes burrito. If burrito were in \'not_orderable,\' Shan would be satisfied, but this conflicts with many people who like burrito and have no other like in \'orderable.\'\n- Looking at frittata, more people dislike it than like it, and those who like it have other options.\n\nNext:\n\n- If we move \'frittata\' to \'not_orderable,\' we satisfy Sky, Val, Lin, Max, and many others. Now, those who liked \'frittata\' must be checked if they have another liking in \'orderable,\' such as Om, who also likes \'waffles.\' So \'waffles\' can be considered for \'orderable\'.\n\nWe continue with this process, shifting items to \'orderable\' and \'not_orderable\' carefully to cover as many likes and dislikes without conflicts. However, this is a highly iterative process and requires checking each person\'s preferences after adding any item to a list. Since this can be complex, it\'s common to use a recursive backtracking algorithm or constraint satisfaction techniques to solve such problems programmatically.\n\nFor practical purposes and due to the complexity and length of the list, we may not be able to find a complete solution manually here. Instead, I\'ll demonstrate the beginning of the process:\n\n- \'tandoori\' --  orderable \n- \'frittata\' --  not_orderable \n\nWe would then continue to iterate, looking at which items would satisfy the most people without causing any conflicts, and which items left could logically be placed in each list. However, this solution may require computer assistance to complete within a reasonable timeframe due to the intricate balancing of likes and dislikes.\n\nSince it\'s difficult to proceed manually due to the scale of preferences, we would need computational assistance to find a solution that satisfies all individuals. If it were determined that no such combination can satisfy all, the final output would be empty lists:\n\n```\norderable = [], not_orderable = []\n``` \n\nBut, from what we\'ve discussed, here\'s a starting point for a potential (partial) solution:\n\n```\norderable = [\'tandoori\'], not_orderable = [\'frittata\']\n``` \n\nDue to complexity, I cannot provide a full solution manually within this format. A proper solution would likely require implementing a constraint satisfaction problem (CSP) solver.', 'num_prompt_tokens': 1419, 'num_completion_tokens': 944}
Data Sample: {'num_vars': 7, 'num_clauses': 14, 'formula': [[-4, 7, -5], [-5, 4, 2], [7, -2, 1], [-5, 2, 3], [-2, 3, 4], [5, 1, -2], [-2, 7, 6], [2, 4, -5], [-5, -7, -1], [4, 5, -3], [-3, 2, 5], [7, 1, 2], [5, -7, 3], [7, 3, -2]], 'is_sat': True, 'preferences': 'Zan: Likes spaghetti. Dislikes nachos, pie. Lex: Likes nachos, shawarma. Dislikes pie. Eli: Likes spaghetti, ramen. Dislikes shawarma. Lin: Likes shawarma, gelato. Dislikes pie. Abe: Likes gelato, nachos. Dislikes shawarma. Xiu: Likes pie, ramen. Dislikes shawarma. Meg: Likes spaghetti, biscuits. Dislikes shawarma. Chao: Likes shawarma, nachos. Dislikes pie. Bo: Dislikes pie, spaghetti, ramen. Lei: Likes nachos, pie. Dislikes gelato. Li: Likes shawarma, pie. Dislikes gelato. Tia: Likes spaghetti, ramen, shawarma. Ru: Likes pie, gelato. Dislikes spaghetti. Anil: Likes spaghetti, gelato. Dislikes shawarma. ', 'menu_items': ['ramen', 'shawarma', 'gelato', 'nachos', 'pie', 'biscuits', 'spaghetti'], 'gpt_out': "To solve this task, we will need to find a combination of 'orderable' and 'not_orderable' food items that meet all the given preferences. We start by creating two lists and adding items in a way that we maintain rule 2 (no item can appear on both lists), and everyone's satisfaction is guaranteed (rule 1), with the goal of satisfying all participants (rule 3).\n\nLet's break down each person's preferences to deduce what must be in 'orderable' or 'not_orderable' and what we can deduce from the information given:\n\n1. Zan: Likes spaghetti, which means spaghetti can be 'orderable'. Dislikes nachos and pie, can be 'not_orderable'.\n2. Lex: Likes nachos, which means nachos can be 'orderable'. Dislikes pie, can be 'not_orderable'.\n3. Eli: Likes spaghetti and ramen, one of which can be 'orderable'. Dislikes shawarma, can be 'not_orderable'.\n4. Lin: Likes shawarma and gelato, one of which can be 'orderable'. Dislikes pie, supporting it being 'not_orderable'.\n5. Abe: Likes gelato and nachos, which supports nachos being 'orderable'. Dislikes shawarma, supporting it being 'not_orderable'.\n6. Xiu: Likes pie and ramen but dislikes shawarma, supporting shawarma being 'not_orderable'.\n7. Meg: Likes spaghetti and biscuits, supporting spaghetti being 'orderable'. Dislikes shawarma.\n8. Chao: Likes shawarma and nachos, which supports nachos being 'orderable'. Dislikes pie.\n9. Bo: Dislikes pie, spaghetti, and ramen. We need to make sure none of his dislikes are in 'orderable'.\n10. Lei: Likes nachos and pie supporting nachos being 'orderable'. Dislikes gelato.\n11. Li: Likes shawarma and pie but dislikes gelato.\n12. Tia: Likes spaghetti, ramen, and shawarma. Her dislikes are not listed, but one of her likes should be 'orderable'.\n13. Ru: Likes pie and gelato, against pie being 'not_orderable'. Dislikes spaghetti, however, spaghetti may be in conflict since some participants like it.\n14. Anil: Likes spaghetti and gelato, supporting spaghetti being 'orderable'. Dislikes shawarma.\n\nFrom the initial analysis, these observations can be made:\n\n- Spaghetti is liked by many and only Ru dislikes it – it is a good candidate for 'orderable'.\n- Shawarma is disliked by many and only Lex, Lin, Chao, Li, and Tia like it – it is a good candidate for 'not_orderable'.\n- However, we have a problem with pie, as it is liked by some (Lei, Xiu, Li, Ru) but disliked by many others. Including or excluding pie would result in dissatisfaction for a significant subset of individuals.\n- Nachos is liked by many and only Zan dislikes it. If we consider nachos for 'orderable', Zan still would be satisfied because his dislike (nachos) would also be on 'not_orderable'.\n- Gelato is liked by some and disliked by others (Lei, Li). We should consider this carefully.\n\nNext step, we did find conflicting items: pie and gelato. Let's break into scenarios:\n\n**Scenario 1: Include pie in 'orderable'.**\n   - This would dissatisfy Zan, Lex, Lin, Chao, and Bo. To satisfy them another way, we would need spaghetti and nachos to be 'orderable' (which satisfies Zan) or shawarma to be 'not_orderable'. \n\n**Scenario 2: Include pie in 'not_orderable'.**\n   - This would dissatisfy Ru, Lei, Xiu, and Li. Ru could be satisfied with gelato being 'orderable'. Xiu requires ramen to be 'orderable' to be satisfied. Lei can be satisfied if nachos are 'orderable'. Li would be satisfied with shawarma being 'orderable', but it conflicts with many others' preferences.\n\n**Scenario 3: Include gelato in 'orderable'.**\n   - This would dissatisfy Lei and Li. They could both be satisfied if nachos and pie are 'orderable', but this contradicts Zan, Lex, Lin, and Chao's preferences.\n\n**Scenario 4: Include gelato in 'not_orderable'.**\n   - This would dissatisfy Ru and Anil. Ru also dislikes spaghetti. Anil can be otherwise satisfied if spaghetti is 'orderable'. We need to consider an option for Ru to be satisfied if gelato is 'not_orderable'.\n\nThe only way to satisfy all without conflicts is to have shawarma in 'not_orderable' because it is disliked by many, and to have spaghetti in 'orderable', as it is liked by many and disliked by only one person who also dislikes an 'orderable' item (spaghetti by Ru, nachos by Zan). Nachos could be also 'orderable' with Zan being ok because it's also 'not_orderable'. The remaining problem is pie which cannot be orderable because it is disliked by many, but cannot be not_orderable because it is also liked by many. As spaghetti, we cannot include gelato in neither list. Ramen is disliked by Bo, but can be included in 'orderable' since there are no direct likes. \n\nGiven this analysis, the most promising approach seems to be:\n\n1. Include spaghetti and nachos in 'orderable'.\n2. Include shawarma, pie, and gelato in 'not_orderable'.\n3. Spaghetti satisfies Zan, Eli, Meg, Tia, Anil and ramen satisfies Xiu. Nachos satisfies Lex, Abe, Chao, and Lei.\n4. Ru poses a problem because including gelato (which satisfies her) in the 'orderable' would mean displeasing