ed by someone else\'s dislike. Thus "Frittata" is a candidate for \'orderable\' but also opposed by several.\n- Many individuals dislike "Toast", but it\'s also liked by many, so it cannot be solely in \'not_orderable\'.\n- "Fries" is liked by enough individuals that it can\'t be only in \'not_orderable\'.\n\nAttempting manual resolution with these items seems to reach a conflict, as those who dislike a popular option are often countered by others who like it.\n\nAt this point, it appears that there is no combination of \'orderable\' and \'not_orderable\' lists that will satisfy all participants based on the preferences given, as their preferences are highly conflicting. Therefore, the solution to this problem, based on the manual analysis, is to output empty lists for both \'orderable\' and \'not_orderable\':\n\n```python\norderable = [], not_orderable = []\n```', 'num_prompt_tokens': 781, 'num_completion_tokens': 1014}
