e liked by people who have diverse dislikes:\n1. Place sushi in \'orderable\'. This satisfies people who like sushi and have diverse dislikes.\n\nReview and adjust:\n- This displeases those who dislike sushi. To satisfy them, we need to ensure an item they like is \'orderable\' or an item they dislike is \'not_orderable\'.\n\n2. Next, we can place custard in \'orderable\' since no one who dislikes custard likes sushi without liking something else. We avoid placing an item in \'orderable\' that someone dislikes without also having another dislike that can go into \'not_orderable\'.\n\nContinue reviewing and adjusting:\n- This displeases those who dislike custard. We must ensure, again, that there\'s a satisfaction condition for them.\n\n3. Now let\'s place falafel in \'not_orderable\'. Many people dislike falafel, and it\'s often paired with a dislike for sushi or custard, making it a good fit for \'not_orderable\'.\n\n4. We can place sorbet in \'orderable\' since those who dislike it have an item in \'not_orderable\' or another liked item could still be \'orderable\'.\n\n5. Place biscuits in \'orderable\', as those who dislike biscuits either like custard, sushi, or sorbet (which are \'orderable\'), or dislike falafel (which is \'not_orderable\').\n\nNow review who\'s left:\n- Most of those who like naan dislike sushi or falafel, so naan can\'t be \'orderable\' because sushi is. Therefore, naan must go into \'not_orderable\'.\n\nWe\'re left with bruschetta. This item is trickier because it is liked by some who dislike other \'orderable\' items and disliked by some who like \'orderable\' items:\n- Place bruschetta in \'not_orderable\'. Those who dislike bruschetta like sushi, custard, sorbet, or biscuits which are \'orderable\'.\n\nWith these assignments, check if all individuals are satisfied:\n- Everyone who likes an \'orderable\' item does not dislike all \'orderable\' items.\n- Everyone who dislikes a \'not_orderable\' item has at least one liked item that\'s \'o