rable' list. But ratatouille cannot be 'orderable' as Om, Rohan, Ivy, and Ula disliked it. Hence, frittata cannot be 'orderable'.\n\nAnalyzing with ratatouille as 'orderable', this satisfies Ravi, Jay, Meg, Diya, Joe, Du, Li, and Pia. The remaining parties, Dot, Om, Rohan, Lee, Rob, and Ula, who dislike ratatouille, require spaghetti to be 'not_orderable'. Thus, with ratatouille as 'orderable', we need spaghetti to be in 'not_orderable' to satisfy groups other than Ivy.\n\nHowever, this creates a problem for Veer, Eli, Bea, Neil, and Diya, who like spaghetti. Falafel can be 'orderable' to satisfy Ben, Lee, Rob, Vic, and Pia, and does not conflict with those who dislike it (because Dot and Zara could be satisfied with frittata being 'not_orderable'). Additionally, since spaghetti is 'not_orderable', Eli, Bea, and Neil can be satisfied with falafel being 'orderable'.\n\nThe only remaining person with a conflict is Ivy, who dislikes all options. To satisfy Ivy, any of the food items she dislikes should be in the 'not_orderable' list. Since spaghetti is already there, Ivy is also satisfied.\n\nTherefore, the combination where ratatouille and falafel are 'orderable', while spaghetti is 'not_orderable' satisfies everyone. Frittata will also be 'not_orderable' to satisfy those who dislike it, as the needs of those who like frittata can be met with other items on the 'orderable' list. \n\nThus, the final lists are:\n\norderable = ['ratatouille', 'falafel']\nnot_orderable = ['spaghetti', 'frittata']", 'num_prompt_tokens': 587, 'num_completion_tokens': 1010}
