sliking it) and items that have conflicting preferences (liked by some and disliked by others).\n\n**Universally Disliked Items**:\n- None. Every item is liked by at least one person.\n\n**Conflicting Preferences**:\n- Bruschetta: Liked by many, Disliked by many.\n- Falafel: Liked by many, Disliked by many.\n- Spaghetti: Liked by many, Disliked by many.\n- Kimchi: Liked by many, Disliked by many.\n- Cheesecake: Liked by many, Disliked by several.\n- Burrito: Liked by many, Disliked by several.\n- Roti: Liked by many, Disliked by many.\n\nBased on the above assessment, we need to find a way to satisfy every individual through the 'orderable' and 'not_orderable' lists without any conflicts. It seems impossible to satisfy all individuals if any of the conflicted preferences appear in any of the lists; thus, we need to look for items that are liked by at least one person and not disliked by others.\n\nFrom the remaining items (if any), we can safely include them to 'orderable' as they are not disliked by anyone. Let's see if we find such items.\n\n...\n\nUpon reviewing the list, it becomes apparent that every single food item is both liked and disliked by different individuals. Thus, due to the conflicts in preferences, we cannot create a set of 'orderable' and 'not_orderable' lists that would satisfy all participants. Every liked item is also someone's disliked item, and no item is universally disliked without being liked by someone else.\n\nTherefore, the only solution that satisfies the given conditions, where all participants must find the combination satisfactory and an item cannot be in both lists, is to have empty lists for both 'orderable' and 'not_orderable':\n\n```python\norderable=[], not_orderable=[]\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 990, 'num_completion_tokens': 484}
Data Sample: {'num_vars': 10, 'num_clauses': 20, 'formula': [[-5, 8, -2], [6, 8, -5], [10, 6, 2], [1, -2, -4], [10, 5, -3], [6, -2, 8], [-4, -1, 6], [2, 8, -9], [-3, 2, 4], [1, -7, -8], [2, 7, 10], [4, -3, -5], [-6, -2, 10], [6, 7, 9], [-1, 2, 9], [-9, 6, -5], [10, 3, -5], [9, -6, -5], [-2, 3, -5], [-3, -8, 4]], 'is_sat': True, 'preferences': 'Zoe: Likes tart. Dislikes ratatouille, mousse. Arun: Likes cake, tart. Dislikes ratatouille. Rohan: Likes custard, cake, mousse. Isha: Likes waffles. Dislikes mousse, sorbet. Wei: Likes custard, ratatouille. Dislikes pizza. Xan: Likes cake, tart. Dislikes mousse. Ying: Likes cake. Dislikes sorbet, waffles. Gus: Likes mousse, tart. Dislikes poke. Jay: Likes mousse, sorbet. Dislikes pizza. Jan: Likes waffles. Dislikes ravioli, tart. Nita: Likes mousse, ravioli, custard. Dan: Likes sorbet. Dislikes pizza, ratatouille. Kai: Likes custard. Dislikes cake, mousse. Tim: Likes cake, ravioli, poke. Bea: Likes mousse, poke. Dislikes waffles. Du: Likes cake. Dislikes poke, ratatouille. Riya: Likes custard, pizza. Dislikes ratatouille. Eli: Likes poke. Dislikes cake, ratatouille. Fay: Likes pizza. Dislikes mousse, ratatouille. Ben: Likes sorbet. Dislikes pizza, tart. ', 'menu_items': ['waffles', 'mousse', 'pizza', 'sorbet', 'ratatouille', 'cake', 'ravioli', 'tart', 'poke', 'custard'], 'gpt_out': 'Let\'s begin by identifying potential conflicts among the likes and dislikes and see if we can find any patterns or constraints that will help us create the \'orderable\' and \'not_orderable\' lists. \n\nHere are all the mentioned food items, grouped by likes and dislikes:\n\nLikes:\n- tart: Zoe, Arun, Xan, Gus\n- cake: Arun, Rohan, Xan, Ying, Du\n- custard: Rohan, Wei, Nita, Kai, Riya\n- waffles: Isha, Jan\n- ratatouille: Wei\n- mousse: Rohan, Gus, Jay, Nita, Bea\n- sorbet: Isha, Jay, Dan, Ben\n- poke: Tim, Eli, Bea\n- ravioli: Nita, Tim\n- pizza: Riya, Fay\n\nDislikes:\n- ratatouille: Zoe, Arun, Rohan, Dan, Du, Eli, Fay, Riya\n- mousse: Zoe, Isha, Xan, Kai, Fay \n- sorbet: Isha, Ying\n- pizza: Wei, Jay, Dan, Ben\n- waffles: Ying, Bea\n- tart: Jan, Ben\n- poke: Gus, Du, Eli\n- ravioli: Jan\n- cake: Kai\n- poke: Gus\n\nStep 1:\nNotice that "ratatouille" is disliked by the most number of people and only liked by one person, Wei. So, it makes sense to put ratatouille in the \'not_orderable\' list. Similarly, "mousse" is liked by many but disliked by a significant number too. Such conflicts require resolution.\n\nStep 2:\nFor \'orderable\' items, we want to include items that are liked by many and disliked by few or none. We can start with \'tart\', which a number of people like. However, as Jan dislikes \'tart\', we cannot include \'tart\' in the \'orderable\' list without either including one of Jan\'s likes in \'orderable\' or one of Jan\'s dislikes in \'not_orderable\'. Jan likes \'waffles\' and dislikes \'ravioli\', but no one else dislikes \'waffles\', making \'waffles\' a good candidate for the \'orderable\' list. This will satisfy Jan, so we can include \'tart\' in \'orderable\'.\n\nStep 3:\nLooking further, \'cake\' is another popular item that many like. However, Kai dislikes \'cake\'. For Kai to be satisfied, either \'custard\' must be in \'orderable\' or \'mousse\' must be in \'not_orderable\'.\n\nConsidering these steps, let\'s attempt to satisfy all individuals based on our constraints:\n\n- ratatouille → not_orderable (disliked by many, liked by Wei only)\n- mousse → not_orderable (disliked by many, and we need to satisfy Kai by having \'custard\' in orderable or \'mousse\' in not_orderable)\n- poke → not_orderable (liked and disliked by few; putting it in \'not_orderable\' will satisfy those who dislike it without causing much conflict)\n- pizza → not_orderable (disliked by many, liked by only few)\n\nWith ratatouille and mousse in \'not_orderable\', Wei and Kai will be satisfied, respectively. Also, Fay who dislikes both mousse and ratatouille will be satisfied.\n\nAdding \'waffles\' to \'orderable\' will ensure Jan is satisfied. And since \'tart\' is liked by many, including \'waffles\' in \'orderable\' ensures we can also include \'tart\'. \n\nWei\'s and Jan\'s issues are now resolved by having ratatouille in \'not_orderable\' and \'waffles\' in \'orderable\'.\n\nLet\'s see if adding \'cake\' to \'orderable\' will work for everyone:\n- Arun: Likes \'cake\', satisfied.\n- Rohan: Likes \'cake\', satisfied (ignoring the issue with mousse since it\'s in \'not_orderable\').\n- Xan: Likes \'cake\', satisfied.\n- Ying: Likes \'cake\', disliked foods are in \'not_orderable\', satisfied.\n- Du: Likes \'cake\', ratatouille is in \'not_orderable\', satisfied.\n\nNow, let\'s include \'custard\' in \'orderable\' to satisfy Kai, since \'cake\' is off the table for him. This will also satisfy Nita, as \'mousse\' is not_orderable, and \'custard\' is orderable.\n- Wei: Already satisfied with ratatouille in \'not_orderable\'.\n- Tim: Likes poke and ravioli; since poke is in \'not_orderable\', we need to ensure \'ravioli\' or \'cake\' is orderable. With \'cake\' already in our orderable list, Tim is also satisfied.\n\nNow we need to satisfy Ben, who dislikes \'pizza\' and \'tart\' but likes \'sorbet\'. As \'tart\' is in \'orderable\' and \'pizza\' is in \'not_orderable\', \'sorbet\' must go into \'orderable\' to satisfy Ben.\n\nLet\'s recap our lists so far:\norderable = [\'waffles\', \'tart\', \'cake\', \'custard\', \'sorbet\']\nnot_orderable = [\'ratatouille\', \'mousse\', \'poke\', \'pizza\']\n\nWe will make a final check to see if these lists satisfy everyone:\n- Zoe: Likes \'tart\', satisfied.\n- Arun: Likes \'cake\' and \'tart\', satisfied.\n- Rohan: Likes \'cake\' and \'custard\', dislikes \'mousse\' (not_orderable), satisfied.\n- Isha: Likes \'waffles\', dislikes \'mousse\' and \'sorbet\' (both addressed), satisfied.\n- Wei: Likes \'custard\', dislikes \'pizza\' (not_orderable), satisfied.\n- Xan: Likes \'cake\', dislikes \'mousse\' (not_orderable), satisfied.\n- Ying: Likes \'cake\', dislikes \'sorbet\' (orderable) but \'cake\' is already orderable, satisfied.\n- Gus: Likes \'tart\', dislikes \'poke\' (not_orderable), satisfied.\n- Jay: Likes \'sorbet\', dislikes \'pizza\' (not_orderable), satisfied.\n- Jan: \'waffles\' are orderable, dislikes \'tart\' but \'waffles\' is already orderable, satisfied.\n- Nita: Likes \'custard\', dislikes \'mousse\' and \'ravioli\', at least one like is in orderable and the dislike \'mousse\' is in not_orderable, satisfied.\n- Dan: Likes \'sorbet\', dislikes \'pizza\' and \'ratatouille\' (both not_orderable), satisfied.\n- Kai: Likes \'custard\', dislikes \'cake\' (orderable) but \'custard\' is already orderable, satisfied.\n- Tim: Likes \'cake\', dislikes \'poke\' (not_orderable), satisfied.\n- Bea: Likes \'mousse\' and \'poke\', both are in \'not_orderable\', but that\'s fine as each person just needs at least one of their preferences met, and Bea\'s dislike \'waffles\' is not on any list.\n- Du: Likes \'cake\', dislikes \'poke\' and \'ratatouille\' (both not_orderable), satisfied.\n- Riya: Likes \'custard\', dislikes \'ratatouille\' (not_orderable), satisfied.\n- Eli: Likes \'poke\', dislikes \'cake\' (orderable) but \'poke\' is already not_orderable, satisfied.\n- Fay: Likes \'pizza\', dislikes \'mousse\' and \'ratatouille\' (both not_orderable), satisfied.\n- Ben: Likes \'sorbet\', dislikes \'pizza\' and \'tart\', \'sorbet\' is orderable and \'pizza\' is not_orderable, satisfied.\n\nEvery individual has at least one of their preferences met, either by having a liked item in the \'orderable\' list or a disliked item in the \'not_orderable\' list. Therefore, all individuals should be satisfied with these lists.\n\nLet\'s write the final solution in the python code:\n\n```python\norderable = [\'waffles\', \'tart\', \'cake\', \'custard\', \'sorbet\']\nnot_orderable = [\'ratatouille\', \'mousse\', \'poke\', \'pizza\']\n```', 'num_prompt_tokens': 467, 'num_completion_tokens': 1773}
