d by many or liked by someone who already has a satisfied like on the 'orderable' list:\n   - Salmon is disliked by many but liked by some who also like 'fries'. Since 'fries' can satisfy these individuals, 'salmon' can be a candidate for 'not_orderable'.\n\nContinuing this process:\n\n3. Wonton is widely disliked and only liked by those who have other likes (e.g., 'fries', 'ramen'). Therefore, 'wonton' can also be a candidate for 'not_orderable'.\n\nLet's check the likes and dislikes again with 'fries' in 'orderable' and 'salmon' and 'wonton' in 'not_orderable' to see if minimum one like or dislike is met for everyone:\n\n- All individuals who dislike 'salmon' or 'wonton' are satisfied by them being in 'not_orderable'.\n- Individuals who like 'fries' are satisfied by them being in 'orderable'.\n\nWe must now look at other items cautiously to prevent dissatisfaction. The remaining items to consider are 'naan', 'spaghetti', 'falafel', 'soup', and 'ramen'. Let's see if any of these can be added to 'orderable' or 'not_orderable' without causing dissatisfaction:\n\n4. 'Ramen' is liked by many and only disliked by people who either like 'fries' or dislike an item in 'not_orderable'. We can tentatively place 'ramen' in 'orderable'.\n\n5. 'Soup' is disliked by a few, but everyone who dislikes it either likes 'fries' or has another dislike such as 'salmon' or 'wonton' in 'not_orderable'. We can also tentatively place 'soup' in 'orderable'.\n\nBased on the tentative lists, let's check if any individuals are unsatisfied:\n\n- For 'naan', 'spaghetti', and 'falafel', multiple individuals only like these items and dislike 'fries', 'salmon', 'wonton', 'ramen', or 'soup', meaning none of 'naan', 'spaghetti', or 'falafel' can go into either list without causing dissatisfaction.\n\nAs of now, multiple rounds of cross-referencing are required to ensure no single placement causes a cascading dissatisfaction. After carefully considering the remaining items, we realize that 'naan', 'spa