Jan, Ty, Raj\n- Ravioli: Cal, Max, Yan\n- Peach: Cal, Veer, Yue, Rina, Ava\n- Macaron: Veer, Max, Sue\n- Guacamole: Veer, Ty\n- Brownies: Dan, Lei\n- Falafel: Sue, Ron\n- Kebab: Jan, Kavi, Ava\n- Fries: Kim\n\nThe goal is to ensure at least one liked item is in 'orderable' or one disliked item is in 'not_orderable', without any overlap. From this, we can notice:\n\n- Shawarma is disliked by Jaya, Kit, Jan, Ty, and Raj, making it a good candidate for 'not_orderable' since it satisfies many people by being excluded.\n- Falafel is liked by only Cal and Veer but disliked by Sue and Ron, and since it is disliked by two people, it might be safer to put it into 'not_orderable'.\n- Brownies are liked by many but only disliked by two, so it would be better placed in 'orderable'.\n- Ravioli is disliked by Cal, Max, and Yan, which means we might have to put it in 'not_orderable' to satisfy those individuals.\n\nI will follow these steps to reach the solution:\n1. Add 'Shawarma' to 'not_orderable', since it has many dislikes and no likes.\n2. Add 'Falafel' to 'not_orderable', because it has multiple dislikes and no strong preference otherwise.\n3. Add 'Brownies' to 'orderable', as it is liked by many.\n4. Add 'Ravioli' to 'not_orderable', considering the dislikes it garnered.\n\nLet's see if this satisfies everyone:\n\n- Jaya (satisfied with 'Shawarma' not orderable)\n- Kit (satisfied with 'Shawarma' not orderable)\n- Cal (satisfied with 'Falafel' not orderable)\n- Veer (satisfied with 'Falafel' not orderable)\n- Dan (satisfied with 'Brownies' orderable)\n- Jan (satisfied with 'Shawarma' not orderable)\n- Yue (satisfied with 'Brownies' orderable)\n- Rina (satisfied with 'Shawarma' not orderable)\n- Max (satisfied with 'Ravioli' not orderable)\n- Sue (satisfied with 'Falafel' not orderable)\n- Lei (satisfied with 'Brownies' orderable)\n- Ron (satisfied with 'Falafel' not orderable)\n- Ty (satisfied with 'Shawarma' not orderable)\n- Raj (satisfied with 'Shawarma' not orderable)\n- Yan (satisfied with 'Ravioli' not orderable)\n- Kavi (satisfied with 'Peach' orderable)\n- Ava (not satisfied yet - as 'Macaron' and 'Peach' are not determined)\n- Kim (not satisfied yet - 'Guacamole' not determined, 'Fries' not determined)\n\nNow we need to address Ava and Kim without violating the previous orderings:\n\nFor Ava and Kim, we cannot include 'Peach' in 'orderable' because it is disliked by too many (Cal, Veer, Yue, and Rina). So, 'Peach' should be in 'not_orderable', and as a result 'Macaron' can be in 'orderable' to satisfy Ava. This decision also satisfies Kim since 'Guacamole' (which Kim likes) remains unassigned and can be placed in 'orderable'\n\nFinally, we can put 'Fries' in 'orderable' as it is only disliked by Kim, and satisfied many others (Kit, Dan, Yan, Kavi).\n\nThe final lists would thus be:\n\norderable = ['Brownies', 'Guacamole', 'Macaron', 'Fries']\nnot_orderable = ['Shawarma', 'Falafel', 'Ravioli', 'Peach']\n\nLet's verify these choices:\n- Everyone who has an item in 'dislikes' is either satisfied by it appearing in 'not_orderable' or by another item they like appearing in 'orderable'.\n- Everyone who has an item in 'likes' is either satisfied by it appearing in 'orderable' or by another item they dislike appearing in 'not_orderable'.\n\nTherefore, this satisfies all conditions. Here are the lists in Python code format:\n\norderable=['Brownies', 'Guacamole', 'Macaron', 'Fries'], not_orderable=['Shawarma', 'Falafel', 'Ravioli', 'Peach']", 'num_prompt_tokens': 456, 'num_completion_tokens': 1213}
