l have to find a common ground through a different strategy. We must identify the least problematic items to satisfy the maximum number of people, seeing if we can keep one of these lists empty to ease the satisfaction of conditions.\n\nLet's identify disliked items that are only disliked by one or a few people, this may give us a hint for 'not_orderable' items, if no one else has them as liked:\n\n1. pho - Disliked by several people, but also liked by several.\n2. burger - Disliked by several people, but also liked by several.\n3. pizza - Disliked by several people, but also liked by several.\n4. gelato - Disliked by several people, but liked by several, too.\n5. paneer - Disliked by several people, but liked by several, too.\n6. couscous - Disliked by several people, but liked by several, too.\n7. spaghetti - Disliked by Ava, Ty, and a few others, but liked by many.\n8. lobster - Disliked by some, but liked by some as well.\n\nNow let's consider likes and try to satisfy dislikes when possible, as we can't find any item not liked or disliked by anyone:\n\n1. Everyone who likes gelato, their dislike is covered by someone who likes that food. Hence, we can put gelato in 'orderable'.\n\nNow we need to satisfy the dislikes by putting some in 'not_orderable':\n\n1. Let's consider pho. Disliked by Tim, Taj, Sue, Jay, Max, Xiu, Cal, Naina, Bo, but liked by Sol, Neil, May, Joe, Ravi, Hui, Jun, Lin, Dan, Chao. This is problematic because the likes and dislikes are widespread, making it a less ideal candidate.\n\n2. paneer - Disliked by several; however, there are quite a few people who have their only like as paneer and no other likes to fall back on if paneer is not orderable (e.g., Ru, Liz, Cy, Arun).\n\n3. burger - Disliked by Taj and Ping who both also dislike pho. Eva also dislikes burgers but likes lobster and pizza. Consider putting burgers in 'not_orderable' would satisfy Taj, Ping, May, Max, and Eva. But we have to check if anyone who likes burgers has an exclusion