re\'s no item that is universally liked by those who dislike the other items.\n\nOur strategy must shift—we cannot satisfy people by having their disliked items on the \'not_orderable\' list. We must ensure each person has something they like on the \'orderable\' list.\n\nHowever, we encounter a problem when trying to create an \'orderable\' list that satisfies everyone:\n\n- There are people who dislike vindaloo, sorbet, baklava, and guacamole (e.g., Kim and Raj), which means that if we place any of these items on the \'orderable\' list, they would be unsatisfied.\n- Every food item is disliked by at least one person.\n\nSince there\'s a person (or persons) who dislikes every item, and no person who dislikes an item has a common liked item that someone else does not dislike, it\'s impossible to create an \'orderable\' and \'not_orderable\' list combination that satisfies everyone. Therefore, we must output empty lists for both \'orderable\' and \'not_orderable\'.\n\nHere\'s the final solution:\n\n```python\norderable = []\nnot_orderable = []\n```', 'num_prompt_tokens': 712, 'num_completion_tokens': 657}
