sible to satisfy all the given conditions:\n\n- Since some people dislike specific items that are liked by others, it is impossible to have an item exclusively in 'not_orderable'. \n- Likewise, 'orderable' cannot include items that are disliked by some.\n  \nWe will iteratively try to find a combination that satisfies everyone starting with the most common dislikes and checking if we can balance those with the likes.\n\n1. Start with 'Custard' since it is disliked by many (7 people). However, Bo, Ned, Abe, Pat, Ying all like custard. This means we cannot put custard in 'not_orderable' unless there are other items in 'orderable' to satisfy these people.\n2. Next, 'Roti' is also disliked by many (8 people), but it is liked by Ned, Yan, Zac, Anil, and Zara, making it equally difficult to place in 'not_orderable'.\n3. 'Brownies' are a stronger candidate for 'not_orderable' since it is disliked by six people and only liked by Min, Arun, Abe, and Rina. However, since Min, Naina, and Leo all dislike three items and we have not yet found an item for 'not_orderable', one of their dislikes must end up in 'orderable' to meet the requirement. Min and Naina both dislike 'brownies', putting 'brownies' on the table for 'orderable'.\n4. 'Burrito' is disliked by four but has many likes; it cannot go into 'not_orderable' without making the 'orderable' list insufficient to satisfy those that like burritos.\n5. 'Salmon' is disliked by multiple persons but is also liked by multiple persons, making it impossible to satisfy everyone by putting it in 'not_orderable'.\n\nFrom the above iteration, we cannot find one item that can be placed in 'not_orderable' without upsetting someone else who likes it.\n\nAfter attempting to find a combination that works, it is clear that any item we would place in 'not_orderable' would need to be balanced by the likes of others in 'orderable'. Given the overlapping likes and dislikes for every single item, there does not exist a single combination that would satisfy everyone's requirements.\n\nThe list of likes and dislikes significantly overlap to the point that there is no possible way to order food without dissatisfying at least one person.\n\nTherefore, the final solution is:\n\n```python\norderable = []\nnot_orderable = []\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 628, 'num_completion_tokens': 989}
