ia.\n\n- Since "burger", "ravioli", and "waffles" are all disliked by many, it\'s safe to add them to the "not_orderable" list. This will satisfy a significant number of individuals who dislike them.\n- "udon", already in the "orderable" list, satisfies those who like it, and the dislikes are a minority, so we can disregard those dislikes for the greater good.\n- "ratatouille" has almost equal likes and dislikes, but as it has more dislikes, we might also consider adding it to "not_orderable".\n- "gelato" is liked by many and only disliked by a few, keeping it in "orderable" could satisfy people.\n\nHowever, the conflict lies in "roti", "ratatouille", and "quiche", which have preferences that are quite divided. We must check if we can satisfy everyone, but if it\'s impossible, it might be best to exclude items in order to satisfy the majority and minimize dissatisfaction.\n\nBased on this analysis, some items are clear:\n- "orderable": udon, gelato\n- "not_orderable": burger, ravioli, waffles, ratatouille\n\nNow let\'s go through each person and check if they would be satisfied with the current "orderable" and "not_orderable" lists:\n\nEvery individual who likes "udon" or "gelato" should be satisfied, same for those disliking "burger", "ravioli", "waffles", or "ratatouille". For those with preferences for other items, we need to ensure at least one food they like is on the "orderable" list or one they dislike on the "not_orderable".\n\nHowever, upon a closer inspection, we can observe that there is actually a conflicting preference with "roti". Some people like "roti" while others dislike it, which means we cannot categorically place it on either list without displeasing someone. Given the distribution of likes and dislikes, it seems impossible to cater to every preference without offending someone. No item can be added to either list without causing someone dissatisfaction, given that all conflicting items have at least one person who likes them and one who dislike