rson who mentions them and not liked by any), these can automatically be added to \'not_orderable\'.\n\nLooking at our list, there\'s no single item that is only disliked or only liked, so we can\'t easily choose some food items for \'orderable\' or \'not_orderable\'.\n\nLet\'s try a different strategy:\n\n- Choose an item or a combination of items that appears in the likes list of some and not in the dislikes list of others to add to \'orderable\'. For example, items like \'cake\', \'quiche\', \'tandoori\', \'vindaloo\' are liked by some but also disliked by others, which means they will cause a conflict if placed in \'orderable\'.\n- For \'not_orderable\', we are looking for items that are disliked but not liked by anyone else. For example, \'pie\' is both liked and disliked by a few, but \'falafel\' seems to be disliked by many and could be a candidate for \'not_orderable\', however, \'falafel\' is still liked by some. Therefore, it cannot be placed in \'not_orderable\' either.\n\nGiven how intertwined everyone\'s preferences are, it\'s clear that no item is universally disliked without being liked by someone else, and no item is universally liked without being disliked by someone else.\n\nTherefore, it is impossible to find an \'orderable\' and \'not_orderable\' list that satisfies all individuals in the given scenario. As such, the output should be two empty lists:\n\n```python\norderable = []\nnot_orderable = []\n```', 'num_prompt_tokens': 1836, 'num_completion_tokens': 656}
