ikes pho, so with sushi in `orderable`, Ying is satisfied.\n5. Gia likes pizza which is not yet in conflict, we could consider placing it in `orderable` later if needed.\n6. Max likes bread, which is in `orderable` now.\n7. Ian likes pizza and spaghetti with spaghetti in `orderable`, Ian will be satisfied.\n8. Qi likes sushi and bread with both potentially in `orderable`.\n9. Lou likes pho, which is left out entirely, so pho does not conflict with anyone.\n10. Cal dislikes bread but likes pho and spaghetti, with spaghetti in `orderable`.\n\nWe can see that if sushi is put into `orderable`, the conditions are met for everyone except for May, who would then need pizza to be satisfied.\n\nLastly, we can add pizza into `orderable` since Ravi, who dislikes pizza, would be satisfied with waffles which are also `orderable`.\n\nFinal lists:\n- `orderable`: spaghetti, waffles, bread, sushi, pizza\n- `not_orderable`: shrimps, doughnuts\n\nEveryone's satisfaction criteria are met with one liked item in `orderable` or one disliked item in `not_orderable`. No item appears on both lists, and all participants are satisfied.\n\nOutput:\n\n```python\norderable=['spaghetti', 'waffles', 'bread', 'sushi', 'pizza'], not_orderable=['shrimps', 'doughnuts']\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 508, 'num_completion_tokens': 1408}
