nd nachos. To resolve these issues, we must find another item to add to the 'orderable' list or another to add to the 'not_orderable' list to satisfy the remaining individuals.\n\nPaneer could be the next candidate for either 'orderable' or 'not_orderable'. However, it is disliked by Eva, Nita, Jaya, Ana, Lia, Kim, Yue, and Dan, and liked by Ty, Sam, Veer, Gus, Bea, Zoe, Jaya, Ana, Ada, and Hal.\n\nSince adding paneer to 'not_orderable' could potentially satisfy Gus (who is not currently satisfied), that seems like a viable path forward. Adding paneer to the 'orderable' list could satisfy Ty, Ada, Hal, and Zoe, but it would upset those who dislike paneer. So, placing it on the 'not_orderable' list seems less contentious.\n\nHowever, placing paneer in 'not_orderable' will not satisfy those who like paneer but dislike both bread and nachos (Jaya and Ana). This situation thus leaves us without a clear solution that would satisfy all individuals only by manipulating the paneer option.\n\nIf we carefully consider individuals who like all items (Ty, Ada, Hal, Naina, Kim, and Zoe) or dislike their potential 'orderable' item (Gus, Jaya, Ana, and Lei), one person's satisfaction would come at the cost of another's dissatisfaction.\n\nWe find that there's no possible combination of 'orderable' and 'not_orderable' items that would satisfy everyone on the list due to their conflicting likes and dislikes. The existence of individuals who like all items presents a situation that is not resolvable; they cannot be satisfied if we use their dislikes as criteria and vice versa.\n\nTherefore, the solution to the problem is that no arrangement of 'orderable' and 'not_orderable' foods can satisfy everyone in the group. The output should be two empty lists:\n\n```python\norderable=[], not_orderable=[]\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 536, 'num_completion_tokens': 1526}
