d Abe, since they also like fries).\n\nNow, we can place vegetables on the 'orderable' list, as those who dislike vegetables will have fries on the 'orderable' list and tiramisu on the 'not_orderable' list, satisfying their criteria. The only exception is Rina, who dislikes everything, but since no one dislikes all three items and likes something that Rina likes, we cannot satisfy Rina in any case. Since satisfaction of all participants is not possible due to Rina's preferences conflicting with everyone else's, we need to output empty lists for both 'orderable' and 'not_orderable'.\n\nHere is the Python code for the result:\n\n```python\norderable = []\nnot_orderable = []\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 543, 'num_completion_tokens': 750}
Data Sample: {'num_vars': 7, 'num_clauses': 28, 'formula': [[-2, -1, -4], [4, -7, -6], [3, -7, 4], [1, -7, -2], [1, -7, 6], [-4, -5, -1], [4, -1, 7], [4, -2, 7], [-7, 3, -1], [2, -1, -7], [2, 5, 1], [-5, -6, 2], [-2, 6, 7], [-2, 6, 1], [7, -3, -6], [5, 6, 2], [2, 6, 3], [-3, 2, -6], [7, -4, -3], [-1, -2, -6], [4, -7, -2], [7, -1, -2], [-7, 6, 4], [-6, 7, 3], [7, 1, -6], [4, -5, -3], [3, 7, -6], [3, 4, -6]], 'is_sat': False, 'preferences': 'Feng: Dislikes burger, doughnut, sushi. Nita: Likes sushi. Dislikes toast, pancakes. Ty: Likes curry, sushi. Dislikes toast. Jan: Likes doughnut. Dislikes toast, burger. Kim: Likes doughnut, pancakes. Dislikes toast. Cy: Dislikes sushi, hummus, doughnut. Lee: Likes sushi, toast. Dislikes doughnut. Arun: Likes sushi, toast. Dislikes burger. Tim: Likes curry. Dislikes toast, doughnut. Liv: Likes burger. Dislikes doughnut, toast. Zan: Likes burger, hummus, doughnut. Wes: Likes burger. Dislikes hummus, pancakes. Jay: Likes pancakes, toast. Dislikes burger. Xiu: Likes pancakes, doughnut. Dislikes burger. Ru: Likes toast. Dislikes curry, pancakes. Eli: Likes hummus, pancakes, burger. Diya: Likes burger, pancakes, curry. Yan: Likes burger. Dislikes curry, pancakes. Ava: Likes toast. Dislikes sushi, curry. Rina: Dislikes doughnut, burger, pancakes. May: Likes sushi. Dislikes toast, burger. Hal: Likes toast. Dislikes doughnut, burger. Dot: Likes pancakes, sushi. Dislikes toast. Sam: Likes toast, curry. Dislikes pancakes. Tom: Likes toast, doughnut. Dislikes pancakes. Gia: Likes sushi. Dislikes hummus, curry. Bao: Likes curry, toast. Dislikes pancakes. Yue: Likes curry, sushi. Dislikes pancakes. ', 'menu_items': ['doughnut', 'burger', 'curry', 'sushi', 'hummus', 'pancakes', 'toast'], 'gpt_out': "To solve this, we'll go step by step:\n\n1. Look for any items that must be in the 'orderable' list to satisfy someone's likes without causing conflicts with another's dislikes.\n2. Look for any items that must be in the 'not_orderable' list to satisfy someone's dislikes without causing conflicts with another's likes.\n3. Update the lists as necessary to accommodate these requirements, always making sure to keep at least one like or dislike for each person satisfied without contradiction.\n4. Try to maximize the 'orderable' list while keeping each participant satisfied according to the criteria.\n\nLet's start by listing individual preferences, focusing on unique likes (items that only one person likes and no one dislikes) and unique dislikes (items that only one person dislikes and no one likes):\n\n- Unique likes (must be orderable): None, as every liked item is liked by multiple individuals and also disliked by at least one.\n- Unique dislikes (can be not orderable but checking for conflicts): None, for the same reason listed above.\n\nSince there are no unique likes or unique dislikes that can be directly placed in either list without conflicts, we need to find a common ground where each person's preferences are met without contradicting someone else's.\n\nLet's consider the likes and dislikes of each person:\n\n1. Items liked by many people are sushi, toast, curry, burger, pancakes, and doughnut.\n2. Items disliked by many people include doughnut, toast, pancakes, and burger.\n\nFrom the top of the list, we can see:\n\n- Multiple people dislike the burger, but it's also liked by people, so it cannot be in the 'not_orderable' list.\n- Sushi is liked by multiple people, and only two dislike it, so this is likely to be 'orderable'.\n- Curry is also liked by many and only disliked by Ru, Ava, and Yue. However, since more people like it, including some who only like curry (e.g., Tim, Yue, Ty), it should likely be 'orderable'.\n- Toast is liked by some and only disliked by a few (Nita, Ty, Jan, and Kim), but we can see that Lee and Arun like both toast and sushi. Since we can satisfy them with sushi, we may not need toast to be 'orderable'.\n- Doughnut is disliked by many, which suggests it might be in the 'not_orderable' list, but Jan, Kim, Zan, Xiu, and Tom like it. We'll have to see if we can satisfy them with other items.\n\nWe'll begin by putting sushi in the 'orderable' list and doughnut in the 'not_orderable' list:\n\norderable = [sushi]\nnot_orderable = [doughnut]\n\nNow we need to check whether everyone's satisfaction criteria can still be met:\n\n- Feng dislikes sushi, which is 'orderable', so no conflicts here.\n- Nita likes sushi, which is 'orderable', so satisfied.\n- Ty is satisfied with both curry and sushi being 'orderable'.\n- Jan likes doughnut, which is 'not_orderable', we need to satisfy her with other likes (none in this case, so now we face a contradiction).\n- Kim likes pancakes, no conflict with doughnut being 'not_orderable', and she dislikes toast; therefore, pancakes need to be in the 'orderable' list.\n\nAt this point, it seems we cannot satisfy everyone because placing doughnut in 'not_orderable' conflicts with Jan's like. Given we're trying for a global solution and not being able to succeed with doughnut 'not_orderable', we need a new strategy.\n\nLet's consider another approach — we can try to put doughnut in 'orderable' to satisfy Jan, Kim, Zan, Xiu, and Tom. We should also place curry in 'orderable' to satisfy Tim, Ty, Yue, Bao, and Sam (this only conflicts with Ru, but we can satisfy them with toast):\n\norderable = [sushi, curry, doughnut]\nnot_orderable = []\n\nNow, we reassess:\n\n- Toast is disliked by a group but liked by another group where for some toast is their only like, so it cannot be 'not_orderable'.\n\nConsidering individual preferences, clearly, there is a high conflict over toast, burger, and pancakes with a nearly equal amount of likes and dislikes. Given that conflicts can't be resolved entirely, let's move to nondisliked (neutral) items.\n\nA better approach might be to look for items that are liked by some but not disliked by anyone, and since all items are either liked or disliked by someone, we'll try to maximize the inclusion of items that are 'orderable' and minimize excluding other items:\n\nCurry and sushi could be 'orderable' due to the several likes and fewer dislikes. To find out if May and Nita can be satisfied with toast, we need to see if anyone with only toast as a like can be satisfied; only Ru is in that situation, and they dislike pancakes and curry. It seems impossible to simultaneously satisfy everyone because people's unique likes get countered by someone's dislike or the only likes of other people.\n\nIt is impossible to satisfy everyone with the given criteria, as keeping an item in 'orderable' or 'not_orderable' would inevitably dissatisfy another individual. We must present an empty list for both orderable and not_orderable.\n\norderable = []\nnot_orderable = []\n\nThis is an impossible scenario to resolve.", 'num_prompt_tokens': 517, 'num_completion_tokens': 1131}
Data Sample: {'num_vars': 9, 'num_clauses': 18, 'formula': [[-6, -2, -1], [-8, -7, 5], [3, 5, -4], [8, -5, 7], [6, -2, 1], [1, -3, 9], [-6, -1, 3], [8, 1, 7], [-6, 2, 7], [-8, 1, -6], [2, -5, 9], [3, 4, -5], [-5, -1, 3], [-3, -2, -9], [-9, 3, 6], [9, 6, 4], [-4, 5, 2], [-3, 7, 5]], 'is_sat': True, 'preferences': 'Dan: Dislikes roti, waffles, nachos. Riya: Likes espresso. Dislikes bruschetta, lasagna. Ansh: Likes paneer, espresso. Dislikes toast. Ty: Likes bruschetta, lasagna. Dislikes espresso. Ian: Likes roti, nachos. Dislikes waffles. Taj: Likes nachos, falafel. Dislikes paneer. Lan: Likes paneer. Dislikes roti, nachos. Jun: Likes bruschetta, nachos, lasagna. Eli: Likes waffles, lasagna. Dislikes roti. Abe: Likes nachos. Dislikes bruschetta, roti. Lou: Likes waffles, falafel. Dislikes espresso. Sue: Likes paneer, toast. Dislikes espresso. Tara: Likes paneer. Dislikes espresso, nachos. Rob: Dislikes paneer, waffles, falafel. Gia: Likes paneer, roti. Dislikes falafel. Bea: Likes falafel, roti, toast. Max: Likes espresso, waffles. Dislikes toast. Zoe: Likes lasagna, espresso. Dislikes paneer. ', 'menu_items': ['nachos', 'waffles', 'paneer', 'toast', 'espresso', 'roti', 'lasagna', 'bruschetta', 'falafel'], 'gpt_out': "First, let's analyze the preferences to determine the 'orderable' and 'not_orderable' lists:\n\nWe start by identifying foods that are only liked by some and not disliked by others, which can safely be placed in 'orderable'. Similarly, we look for foods that are only disliked and not liked by anyone, which we can safely place in 'not_orderable'.\n\n**Orderable** candidates (only liked by some and not disliked by others):\n\n1. Espresso - Liked by Riya, Ansh, Max, Zoe. Disliked by Ty, Lou, Sue, Tara.\n2. Paneer - Liked by Ansh, Lan, Sue, Tara, Gia. Disliked by Taj, Rob, Zoe.\n3. Waffles - Liked by Ian, Lou, Max. Disliked by Dan, Rob.\n4. Falafel - Liked by Taj, Lou, Bea. Disliked by Rob, Gia.\n5. Toast - Liked by Sue, Bea. Disliked by Ansh, Max.\n\nFrom this list, no food item can directly go into 'orderable' because they are all disliked by at least one person.\n\n**Not_orderable** candidates (only disliked by some and not liked by any):\n\n1. Roti - Disliked by Dan, Lan, Abe. Liked by Ian, Gia, Bea.\n2. Bruschetta - Disliked by Riya, Abe. Liked by Ty, Jun.\n3. Lasagna - Disliked by Riya. Liked by Ty, Jun, Eli, Zoe.\n4. Nachos - Disliked by Dan, Tara. Liked by Ian, Taj, Abe, Jun.\n\nThere are no foods that are exclusively disliked and not liked by anyone, so nothing can go directly into 'not_orderable'.\n\nSince no items can immediately go into 'orderable' or 'not_orderable' without conflicts, we need to look for a combination that will satisfy everyone's likes and dislikes to the greatest possible extent.\n\nOne strategy is to satisfy the most restrictive preferences first:\n\n1. Espresso should be in 'orderable', as it has the largest group of people who like it and no one who likes it dislikes anything else that somebody else likes. This will satisfy Riya, Ansh, Max, Zoe.\n   - To satisfy Ty, who dislikes espresso, we must include bruschetta or lasagna in 'orderable'.\n   - To satisfy Lou who dislikes espresso, waffles or falafel should be in 'orderable'. We cannot use waffles (Max dislikes toast and there is no other selection to make Max happy if waffles get selected), so by selecting falafel we satisfy Lou and Taj also.\n   - To satisfy Sue, 'orderable' must include toast.\n   - Tara dislikes espresso and nachos but likes paneer. She will be dissatisfied since paneer cannot be ordered (disliked by more people).\n\n2. Sue likes toast, not included in 'orderable' and disliked by Ansh who likes espresso. We can satisfy Ansh, so Sue will be dissatisfied.\n\n3. Since Lan likes only paneer and dislikes roti and nachos which are in conflict with other likes, we cannot satisfy Lan without unsatisfying others.\n\n4. Ian likes roti and nachos and dislikes waffles, which conflicts with Max and Lou's preferences.\n\nIt seems there is no combination that will satisfy every single person simultaneously without conflict. We have individuals who have conflicting likes and dislikes that cannot be resolved within the criteria set forth. Thus, the 'orderable' and 'not_orderable' lists should be:\n\norderable=[]\nnot_orderable=[]\n\n```python\norderable=[], not_orderable=[]\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 438, 'num_completion_tokens': 761}
Data Sample: {'num_vars': 5, 'num_clauses': 35, 'formula': [[2, -3, 4], [3, 4, -5], [-1, 2, 4], [-2, -1, 3], [-3, -2, 5], [-5, 4, 2], [3, -1, 4], [1, 2, -3], [4, 3, 2], [-5, 3, -1], [-4, -5, 2], [3, 5, 4], [-1, 4, 5], [-1, -3, 4], [3, -1, -5], [3, -4, 5], [-5, 2, 3], [1, -4, 5], [-1, 3, 2], [4, 3, -2], [-5, 3, 2], [-2, 1, 4], [4, 3, 5], [-5, 3, 4], [-5, 3, 1], [4, 1, -5], [-2, 1, -3], [4, -5, -2], [1, 2, 3], [-2, 4, 5], [2, 4, -1], [-5, -3, 2], [3, 1, -5], [-4, -5, 2], [-4, -3, -2]], 'is_sat': True, 'preferences': 'Vic: Likes poke, burrito. Dislikes burger. Xan: Likes burger, burrito. Dislikes lobster. Tao: Likes poke, burrito. Dislikes salmon. Lei: Likes burger. Dislikes poke, salmon. Lan: Likes lobster. Dislikes burger, poke. Kavi: Likes burrito, poke. Dislikes lobster. Ying: Likes burger, burrito. Dislikes salmon. Ray: Likes salmon, poke. Dislikes burger. Fay: Likes burrito, burger, poke. Ula: Likes burger. Dislikes lobster, salmon. Ava: Likes poke. Dislikes burrito, lobster. Val: Likes burger, lobster, burrito. Li: Likes burrito, lobster. Dislikes salmon. Tia: Likes burrito. Dislikes salmon, burger. Zara: Likes burger. Dislikes salmon, lobster. Ravi: Likes burger, lobster. Dislikes burrito. Yan: Likes poke, burger. Dislikes lobster. Sal: Likes salmon, lobster. Dislikes burrito. Min: Likes burger, poke. Dislikes salmon. Mina: Likes burrito, burger. Dislikes poke. Amy: Likes burger, poke. Dislikes lobster. Xiu: Likes salmon, burrito. Dislikes poke. Ping: Likes burrito, burger, lobster. Wei: Likes burger, burrito. Dislikes lobster. Mel: Likes burger, salmon. Dislikes lobster. Om: Likes burrito, salmon. Dislikes lobster. Bo: Likes salmon. Dislikes poke, burger. Ansh: Likes burrito. Dislikes lobster, poke. Zoe: Likes salmon, poke, burger. Ada: Likes burrito, lobster. Dislikes poke. Chao: Likes poke, burrito. Dislikes salmon. Meg: Likes poke. Dislikes lobster, burger. Naina: Likes burger, salmon. Dislikes lobster. Jim: Likes poke. Dislikes burrito, lobster. Bea: Dislikes burrito, burger, poke. ', 'menu_items': ['salmon', 'poke', 'burger', 'burrito', 'lobster'], 'gpt_out': "We need to satisfy the conditions for each person by including at least one liked item in the 'orderable' list or one disliked item in the 'not_orderable' list. Also, the same item cannot appear in both lists. Let's go step by step:\n\nStep 1: Identify items that are universally disliked or liked as a starting point.\n- Only Bea has dislikes with no likes, and she dislikes burrito, burger, and poke. To satisfy Bea, at least one of these items must go on the 'not_orderable' list. There are no universally liked or disliked foods that all individuals agree upon, so we cannot fill any item in the 'orderable' or 'not_orderable' list just yet.\n\nStep 2: Analyze choices to find items that can be 'orderable' or 'not_orderable' based on overlapping likes and dislikes.\n- Poke is liked by many and disliked mainly by Bea and Lei, but Lei also likes burger, so we can't satisfy both Lei's like and Bea's dislike with burger. However, Bea dislikes all three options (burrito, burger, and poke), so whatever we choose to satisfy the others, Bea's satisfaction can still be met.\n- Lobster is disliked by many, and only a few like it. Making it 'not_orderable' will satisfy many, and we need to see if the ones who like it can be satisfied with another item. Lan, Li, Sal, Ping, Ada, and Ada can be satisfied with other options in their 'likes'.\n- Salmon is another item that is disliked by many and liked by some. If salmon is 'not_orderable', Ray, Bo, Xiu, Om, Zoe, and Naina who like salmon must be satisfied with other options.\n\nLet's now start making some decisions based on the likes and dislikes to create a preliminary list that meets the above points:\n- If we place lobster in 'not_orderable', we can satisfy Xan, Tao, Kavi, Ula, Zara, Om, Mel, Ansh, Wei, Amy, and Bea.\n- For those who like lobster, we'll have to satisfy them with other items. Lan can be satisfied with burrito, Li with burrito, Sal with salmon, Ping with burger, Ada with burrito, and Ravi with burger.\n\nTaking lobster as 'not_orderable', let's see if we can make a list that satisfies everyone:\n- We will tentatively put burrito in 'orderable' as it is liked by Vic, Xan, Tao, Kavi, Ying, Fay, Tia, Ansh, Chao, Ada, Li, Xiu, Ping, Wei, and Om without causing any direct conflicts.\n- We cannot satisfy Lei or Min with poke, because placing poke in 'orderable' would conflict with Bea's dislike. Therefore, we must ensure burger is orderable to meet Lei and Min's satisfaction.\n- We cannot satisfy Bea with poke because it would cause conflicts with others' likes. However, since Bea dislikes burrito, burger, and poke, we've already placed lobster in 'not_orderable', which doesn't satisfy Bea, so at least one of the items Bea dislikes (burrito, burger, or poke) will also go into 'not_orderable'.\n\nNow, let's analyze what we have so far:\n- Burrito and burger seem to be potentially 'orderable'. This satisfies many people.\n- Lobster is 'not_orderable', which also satisfies a good number of people.\n- Bea's condition doesn't affect the 'orderable' list because none of the items she likes are constrained.\n\nContinuing from here:\n- We're left to satisfy those who like poke (Vic, Tao, Kavi, Ray, Ava, Jim, Chao, and Meg) and salmon (Ray, Bo, Xiu, Om, Zoe, and Naina) without causing conflicts.\n- We can't satisfy everyone with salmon since Tao, Lei, Tia, Li, Chao, and Ana dislike salmon, and we don't want to place salmon in 'orderable'. Therefore, salmon should be 'not_orderable'.\n- Since poke is liked by several and disliked only by a few (Lei, Lan, Amy, Ansh, Ada, and Bea), and since we can't place it in 'not_orderable' because of Bea's condition, poke should be in 'orderable'.\n\nSo now we have:\n\norderable: poke, burrito, burger\nnot_orderable: lobster, salmon\n\nFinally, let's go through each individual to ensure everyone is satisfied:\n- Vic is satisfied (likes poke, burrito; poke is 'orderable').\n- Xan is satisfied (likes burger, burrito; dislikes lobster; lobster is 'not_orderable').\n- Tao is satisfied (likes poke, burrito; dislikes salmon; salmon is 'not_orderable').\n- Lei is satisfied (likes burger; dislikes salmon, poke; salmon is 'not_orderable').\n- Lan is satisfied (likes lobster; dislikes burger, poke; lobster is 'not_orderable').\n- Kavi is satisfied (likes poke, burrito; dislikes lobster; lobster is 'not_orderable').\n- Ying is satisfied (likes burger, burrito; dislikes salmon; salmon is 'not_orderable').\n- Ray is satisfied (likes salmon, poke; dislikes burger; poke is 'orderable').\n- Fay is satisfied (likes burrito, burger, poke; poke is 'orderable').\n- Ula is satisfied (likes burger; dislikes lobster, salmon; lobster is 'not_orderable').\n- Ava is satisfied (likes poke; dislikes lobster, burrito; poke is 'orderable').\n- Val is satisfied (likes burger, lobster, burrito; lobster is 'not_orderable').\n- Li is satisfied (likes burrito, lobster; dislikes salmon; lobster is 'not_orderable').\n- Tia is satisfied (likes burrito; dislikes salmon, burger; burrito is 'orderable').\n- Zara is satisfied (likes burger; dislikes salmon, lobster; lobster is 'not_orderable').\n- Ravi is satisfied (likes burger, lobster; dislikes burrito; lobster is 'not_orderable').\n- Yan is satisfied (likes poke, burger; dislikes lobster; poke is 'orderable').\n- Sal is satisfied (likes salmon, lobster; dislikes burrito; lobster is 'not_orderable').\n- Min is satisfied (likes burger, poke; dislikes salmon; poke is 'orderable').\n- Mina is satisfied (likes burrito, burger; dislikes poke; burrito is 'orderable').\n- Amy is satisfied (likes burger, poke; dislikes lobster; lobster is 'not_orderable').\n- Xiu is satisfied (likes salmon, burrito; dislikes poke; burrito is 'orderable').\n- Ping is satisfied (likes burrito, burger, lobster; lobster is 'not_orderable').\n- Wei is satisfied (likes burger, burrito; dislikes lobster; lobster is 'not_orderable').\n- Mel is satisfied (likes burger, salmon; dislikes lobster; lobster is 'not_orderable').\n- Om is satisfied (likes burrito, salmon; dislikes lobster; lobster is 'not_orderable').\n- Bo is satisfied (likes salmon; dislikes poke, burger; salmon is 'not_orderable').\n- Ansh is satisfied (likes burrito; dislikes lobster, poke; lobster is 'not_orderable').\n- Zoe is satisfied (likes salmon, poke, burger; salmon is 'not_orderable').\n- Ada is satisfied (likes burrito, lobster; dislikes poke; burrito is 'orderable').\n- Chao is satisfied (likes poke, burrito; dislikes salmon; poke is 'orderable').\n- Meg is satisfied (likes poke; dislikes lobster, burger; poke is 'orderable').\n- Naina is satisfied (likes burger, salmon; dislikes lobster; lobster is 'not_orderable').\n- Jim is satisfied (likes poke; dislikes burrito, lobster; poke is 'orderable').\n- Bea is satisfied (dislikes burrito, burger, poke; either burger or poke is in 'orderable' and the other can be in 'not_orderable' along with salmon and lobster.\n\nWith each person's preferences being met, we can conclude with the following lists:\n\norderable: ['poke', 'burrito', 'burger']\nnot_orderable: ['lobster', 'salmon']\n\nIn Python code, the output will be:\n\n```python\norderable=['poke', 'burrito', 'burger'], not_orderable=['lobster', 'salmon']\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 602, 'num_completion_tokens': 1757}
Data Sample: {'num_vars': 10, 'num_clauses': 20, 'formula': [[-6, 10, 1], [5, 10, -6], [9, 10, -6], [-3, 1, -10], [8, -5, -6], [1, -6, -9], [-6, -5, -2], [-5, 1, 9], [-4, 1, 5], [-8, 6, 7], [-9, 3, 1], [-2, 10, -9], [-1, -5, -10], [-5, -1, -3], [6, 1, -8], [1, -4, 3], [-9, -2, -10], [-5, -7, -8], [-5, 7, 10], [-4, 8, -2]], 'is_sat': True, 'preferences': 'Amy: Likes roti, custard. Dislikes shawarma. Jaya: Likes espresso, roti. Dislikes shawarma. Kai: Likes macaron, roti. Dislikes shawarma. Vic: Likes custard. Dislikes fries, roti. Bo: Likes kimchi. Dislikes espresso, shawarma. Sol: Likes custard. Dislikes shawarma, macaron. Neil: Dislikes shawarma, espresso, mousse. Wes: Likes custard, macaron. Dislikes espresso. Anil: Likes custard, espresso. Dislikes shrimps. Tara: Likes shawarma, bruschetta. Dislikes kimchi. Hui: Likes fries, custard. Dislikes macaron. Zoe: Likes roti. Dislikes mousse, macaron. Mel: Dislikes custard, espresso, roti. Qi: Dislikes espresso, custard, fries. Sue: Likes shawarma, custard. Dislikes kimchi. Yue: Likes custard, fries. Dislikes shrimps. Ping: Dislikes macaron, mousse, roti. Geet: Dislikes espresso, bruschetta, kimchi. Rohan: Likes bruschetta, roti. Dislikes espresso. Chao: Likes kimchi. Dislikes shrimps, mousse. ', 'menu_items': ['custard', 'mousse', 'fries', 'shrimps', 'espresso', 'shawarma', 'bruschetta', 'kimchi', 'macaron', 'roti'], 'gpt_out': 'To begin, let\'s split the likes and dislikes for each individual, analyze the data, and try to formulate lists that will satisfy everyone according to the criteria provided.\n\nHere, we\'ll create a set for \'likes\' and \'dislikes\' based on the preferences of all individuals:\n\nLikes: \n- roti (Amy, Jaya, Kai, Zoe, Rohan)\n- custard (Amy, Vic, Sol, Wes, Anil, Hui, Sue, Yue)\n- espresso (Jaya, Anil)\n- macaron (Kai, Wes)\n- kimchi (Bo, Chao)\n- fries (Hui, Yue)\n- bruschetta (Tara, Rohan)\n- shawarma (Tara, Sue)\n\nDislikes: \n- shawarma (Amy, Jaya, Kai, Bo, Sol, Neil, Sue)\n- fries (Vic, Qi)\n- roti (Vic, Mel, Ping)\n- espresso (Bo, Wes, Neil, Mel, Qi, Geet, Rohan)\n- macaron (Sol, Hui, Ping, Zoe)\n- mousse (Neil, Zoe)\n- shrimps (Anil, Yue, Chao)\n- kimchi (Tara, Sue, Geet)\n\nFrom this list, we can make the following observations:\n- Shawarma is the most disliked item, followed by espresso, and creates a common ground for disliking among many individuals.\n- Custard and roti are amongst the most liked items, but they are disliked by some too. Roti has more dislikers compared to custard, so it seems a better candidate for the not_orderable list.\n- Espresso is liked by only two and disliked by many, making it a prime candidate for the not_orderable list.\n\nWith these observations in mind, let\'s build our lists.\n\n1. Since Shawarma is disliked by seven individuals and liked by only two, "shawarma" goes into the \'not_orderable\' list to ensure the satisfaction of the majority.\n\n2. Espresso is liked by two individuals but disliked by seven. It should go into the \'not_orderable\' list to satisfy those seven people.\n\n3. Roti is liked by five people but disliked by three, and putting it in \'not_orderable\' will satisfy those three. However, it is not necessarily the case that custard or the other liked foods cannot be on the \'orderable\' list to satisfy those individuals. Therefore, we will hold off on deciding where to place roti for the moment.\n\n4. Custard is liked by eight people and disliked by only one, which makes it a strong candidate for the \'orderable\' list at first glance.\n\nLet\'s start by placing "shawarma" and "espresso" in the \'not_orderable\' list to satisfy participants who dislike them. Then we\'ll add "custard" to the \'orderable\' list to satisfy the many who like it. Between the people who like roti and dislike custard (Mel), custard\'s inclusion in \'orderable\' may suffice for Mel, due to criterion 1. Therefore, roti would also be a candidate for \'not_orderable\', but let\'s hold off as we said and examine further.\n\nBy placing these items on the respective lists, we are now able to satisfy all of the following individuals: \n- Amy, Jaya, Kai, Bo, Sol, Neil, Sue (shaowarma in \'not_orderable\'), \n- Anil (espresso in \'not_orderable\', likes custard),\n- Vic, Wes, Hui, Yue (custard in \'orderable\').\n\nWe must now ensure we are able to satisfy the remaining individuals via at least one of their liked items in \'orderable\' or one disliked item in \'not_orderable\'. \n\nMel dislikes custard, espresso, and roti. Since espresso is already not orderable, Mel is satisfied. Tara likes bruschetta and shawarma. Shawarma is not orderable, so Tara is not satisfied under the current configuration, but bruschetta can be and is not disliked by anyone, so we can add it to the \'orderable\' list to satisfy Tara.\n\nThis leaves Qi, who dislikes espresso, custard, and fries. We’ve placed two of Qi\'s dislikes in \'not_orderable\', which satisfies Qi\'s preferences. Everyone else\'s dislikes are not affected by the current lists, or their likes can be found in the \'orderable\' items, therefore they are also satisfied.\n\nSo far, our lists look like this:\n\'not_orderable\': ["shawarma", "espresso"]\n\'orderable\': ["custard", "bruschetta"]\n\nNow, let\'s check each remaining individual to see who is not satisfied:\n\n- Ping is not satisfied as none of their dislikes (macaron, mousse, roti) are in \'not_orderable\', and one of their dislikes (custard) is in \'orderable\'. Since their only dislike not countered by a like by someone else is mousse, we can put mousse in \'not_orderable\' to satisfy Ping.\n\nNow, \'orderable\': ["custard", "bruschetta"]\n\'not_orderable\': ["shawarma", "espresso", "mousse"]\n\nWe\'ll check again to ensure this latest addition doesn\'t conflict with any other individuals\' likes:\n- No one has mousse listed as a \'like,\' therefore, it is safe in the \'not_orderable\' list.\n\nWith mousse added to the \'not_orderable\' list, all individuals should now be satisfied. Individuals whose satisfactions are contingent on the \'not_orderable\' list now have at least one disliked item present, satisfying criterion 1. With the \'orderable\' list including \'custard\' and \'bruschetta\', we\'ve also satisfied individuals who liked these items.\n\nFinal lists to satisfy everyone\'s criteria:\n\'orderable\': ["custard", "bruschetta"]\n\'not_orderable\': ["shawarma", "espresso", "mousse"]\n\nNow formatted as requested:\n\n`orderable=["custard", "bruschetta"], not_orderable=["shawarma", "espresso", "mousse"]`', 'num_prompt_tokens': 474, 'num_completion_tokens': 1276}
