ai, disliked and liked by those who do not dislike it, so it's not universally disliked. Can't decide yet.\n\nIt looks like ***poke*** is the only item that is universally disliked and thus should be in 'not_orderable'.\n\nTo try and figure out which items should be in 'orderable', I will look for items that some participants like but no one dislikes. If there is an item liked by at least one person and not disliked by anyone, it is a good candidate for the 'orderable' list.\n\nWe have already determined that poke is universally disliked, so it is already on the 'not_orderable' list. Now, let's go through the rest of the preferences to see if we can satisfy everyone.\n\n1. **Tandoori** is liked by several people and disliked by many others, but since no one who dislikes it has it as their only dislike, we can put it in the 'orderable' list without failing to satisfy anyone. Adding ***tandoori*** to 'orderable'.\n\nNow let's check if the 'orderable' list (containing 'tandoori') and the 'not_orderable' list (containing 'poke') satisfy everyone's preferences.\n\n- Participants that are satisfied with 'tandoori' in 'orderable': Ula, Mia, Pia, Joe, Abe, Zac, Hal, Nic, Mina, Rob.\n- Participants that are satisfied with 'poke' in 'not_orderable': Hui, Xiu, Ken, Zara, Fay, Bob, Sue, Tom, Rohan, Wei, Joy, Li, Val, Kai, Ned, Tia, Jim, Eva, Anil, Mei, Qi.\n\nThere are still some participants we have yet to satisfy.\n\n2. We cannot decide on **espresso** or **baklava** since they are both liked and disliked by different participants with no clear way to satisfy everyone's preferences together.\n\n3. Similarly, **pizza**, **bread**, **lobster**, **shrimps**, **custard**, **cheesecake** are liked by some but disliked by others, so they cannot be placed in either list without more analysis.\n\nSince 'tandoori' and 'poke' alone do not satisfy all participants, we need to see if there's another food item that we can add to either of our lists to satisfy the remaining participants. We will choose an item that is liked by someone who isn't already satisfied and disliked by someone who is either indifferent to 'tandoori' or 'poke', or already satisfied by one of those items.\n\nBy examining each participant's preferences individually and trying to satisfy the remaining unsatisfied participants, we see that certain items like **espresso**, **baklava**, **pizza**, etc., are in opposition due to conflicting likes and dislikes. Therefore, we must look for compromise items that could be placed on either list without causing dissatisfaction for others.\n\nLet's focus first on 'orderable' to satisfy likes.\n\nFor anyone who isn't satisfied by 'tandoori' or 'poke', they must like at least one item that is not universally disliked. Items liked by those still unsatisfied include:\n\n- 'espresso': liked by Mel, Sky, Joy, Geet, Kavi, Rob, Diya, Tara\n- 'baklava': liked by Ian, Liz, Sol, Cal, Pat, Sal\n- 'pizza': liked by Sky, Hui, Isha, Aarav, Tim, Pat\n- 'custard': liked by Sky, Li, Qi, Om\n- 'cheesecake': liked by Xiu, Bea, Tim, Cal, Sue, Zac\n- 'lobster': liked by Ray, Tom, Bea, Lin, Lan, Mei, Gus\n\nAn item that could potentially satisfy all preferences doesn't appear evident, as each has opposites in the likes and dislikes lists among the currently unsatisfied participants. We are looking for items that are liked by someone unsatisfied, not disliked by anyone unsatisfied or someone who is satisfied by 'tandoori' or 'poke'.\n\nHowever, a thorough review of each participant's likes shows that there are items that are problematic to include on either list without causing dissatisfaction: espresso, baklava, pizza, custard, and lobster are contested. We need to find an item that, if added to 'orderable', doesn't satisfy someone who dislikes it without having another satisfying item.\n\nContested items' analysis:\n\n- Espresso is liked by many and disliked by a few. Including it in 'orderable' potentially satisfies some but not others who dislike it.\n- Baklava is liked by some and disliked by others. Including it in 'orderable' will satisfy a group but leave others unsatisfied due to their dislike.\n- Pizza is favored by several individuals, and if it is included in 'orderable', it will satisfy some but not others who explicitly dislike it.\n- Custard is liked by a group of people; however, including it in 'orderable' would go against the preferences of a different group.\n\nAs a result, finding a satisfying combination for every person seems impossible with the current constraints. No single item can be added to the 'orderable' list to meet these criteria without leaving at least one person unsatisfied.\n\nSo, looking at the constraints and the mentioned preferences, it seems that we have arrived at a point where no such combination exists that satisfies all conditions for the participants due to conflicting interests. Hence, we must conclude that the task is unfulfillable, and both lists should be empty. Our output will be:\n\n```python\norderable=[], not_orderable=[]\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 1017, 'num_completion_tokens': 1774}
