like pizza and have no other likes. For example, Rohan only likes doughnuts and dislikes pizza, meaning if pizza is orderable, doughnut must also be orderable, which in turn causes issues for Isha and Jay, who would then not have any disliked items in not_orderable or any liked items in orderable.\n\nUnfortunately, we quickly run into conflicts:\n- There are several people who have likes and dislikes that are mutually exclusive (e.g., disliking and liking pizza).\n- There are those with unique likes that nobody else dislikes, which conflicts with multiple people\'s dislikes.\n- Adding any disliked items to the orderable list without a backup for those individuals who also dislike them will make it impossible to satisfy everyone.\n\nGiven the interdependencies and the rule that a person is only satisfied if at least one of their liked items is orderable or disliked items is not_orderable, and considering the constraints that no item can appear on both lists, it appears that we cannot create a pair of lists that satisfies everyone according to the provided criteria.\n\nTherefore, the result should be:\n```python\norderable = []\nnot_orderable = []\n```', 'num_prompt_tokens': 648, 'num_completion_tokens': 716}
