e \'orderable\' list to satisfy the people who like it.\n\nNow, for the rest of the participants who do not like "macaron," we need to ensure at least one of their liked items or disliked items is in the corresponding list while making sure everyone is satisfied. Since "macaron" is now our only \'orderable\' item, we should find items that are not liked by anyone and put them on the \'not_orderable\' list.\n\nGiven that "macaron" is orderable and it is liked by almost all, only a few people are left who do not like it. By satisfying their dislikes, we can ensure that everyone else is satisfied as well. These individuals are Xan and Wes, who dislike "macaron." There are no other commonalities in likes or dislikes between Xan and Wes, which means the current state is that we have "macaron" on the \'orderable\' list, and we have not found a valid \'not_orderable\' list yet.\n\nThere are no more unanimous likes or dislikes that include Xan and Wes, so we move to iteratively consider more items and check whether they can satisfy others without causing dissatisfaction to anyone. Unfortunately, as we deal with multiple constraints and cross-liking-disliking patterns, a brute-force approach is required, which can be computational but cumbersome when done manually.\n\nGiven the complexity and the extensive list of participants with their individual likes and dislikes, a manual resolve would involve checking pairings of \'orderable\' and \'not_orderable\' items while cross-referencing all participants\' preferences, ensuring that for any item placed in one list, no participant who dislikes this item has all their liked items placed in the \'not_orderable\' list. \n\nThis would be an iterative process, requiring the checking of each item against the preferences of each individual, checking for any contradictions as new items are placed on the \'orderable\' and \'not_orderable\' lists. This could lead to multiple rounds of adding and removing items from lists as conflicts arise