y disliked by a few (Ned, Eli, Bea, Jan, Shan, Ana, Liz, Eva, and Arun). We will check if it can be placed on either list without issues.\n\n5. Ramen is liked by many, but also disliked by many. It could be more challenging to find the right list for ramen.\n\nLet's attempt to satisfy the preferences:\n\n- Risotto should be on the 'orderable' list to satisfy: Ying, Gen, Zan, Pat, Jon, May, Xiu, Raj, Geet, Tom, Rex, Yan, Riya, Kit, Lou, Ken, Qi, and Wei.\n- Tart and paneer can both be on the 'not_orderable' list, satisfying those who dislike them without causing dissatisfaction for those who like them, as the latter group also has other likes, such as risotto.\n- By the same logic, vindaloo should also be on the 'orderable' list, as it is the only liked food for some (Dan, Pat, Xiu, Raj, Zara, Ping, Wei, Aarav).\n\nAfter placing risotto and vindaloo in 'orderable' and tart and paneer in 'not_orderable', let's check ramen:\n\n- Ramen, if placed in 'orderable', will satisfy Zan, Kai, May, Tom, Cy, Joy, Val, Sal, Aarav.\n- However, by placing ramen in 'orderable', it will dissatisfy Dan, Zan, Kai, Nic, Jon, May, Raj, Tom, Ty, Yan, Eli, Ken, Qi, Sal, which is a larger unhappy group than those satisfied if ramen is in 'orderable'. So, it looks like ramen should be on the 'not_orderable' list.\n\nAfter establishing the tentative lists, it seems we can satisfy everyone:\n\n- Orderable: risotto, vindaloo\n- Not_orderable: tart, paneer, ramen\n\nNow, let's present the final solution in Python code:\n\n```python\norderable=['risotto', 'vindaloo']\nnot_orderable=['tart', 'paneer', 'ramen']\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 888, 'num_completion_tokens': 712}
