e will satisfy everyone who isn't satisfied by ‘brownies’ being on the ‘not_orderable’ list.\n\nIt seems like 'korma' is liked by a few (Geet, Dot, Cal, Sol, Hu, Ned, Zoe, Jan, Nita) and it doesn't show up as disliked as frequently as other items.\n\nWe will now assign 'korma' to the 'orderable' list and see if anyone is left unsatisfied:\n\n- Participants who liked 'korma' are now satisfied.\n- Participants who disliked brownies are also satisfied.\n\nChecking all individuals now:\n\n- Hui is satisfied due to brownies being 'not_orderable.'\n- Arun is unsatisfied since naan is not addressed.\n- Geet is satisfied with 'orderable' korma.\n- Zan is unsatisfied, as zan like 'ravioli' and dislike 'korma' but we add 'korma' to the 'orderable' list, contradicting Zan's likes and dislikes.\n- Ravi is satisfied due to brownies being 'not_orderable.'\n- Ian is unsatisfied due to brownies being 'not_orderable.'\n- Om is satisfied due to brownies being 'not_orderable.'\n- Isha is satisfied due to brownies being ‘not_orderable.'\n- Ava is unsatisfied since lobster is not addressed.\n- Dot is satisfied due to 'orderable' korma.\n- Mei is unsatisfied since naan is not addressed.\n- Fay is unsatisfied, as korma is 'orderable,' but it's on their dislike list.\n- Lou is unsatisfied, as korma is 'orderable,' but it's on their dislike list.\n- ...\n\nGiven that individuals like Zan, Ian, Ava, Mei, Fay, and Lou, among others, are unsatisfied, I've already identified an insurmountable problem with this configuration.\n\nTo satisfy the criteria, we might need to reevaluate our choice of 'orderable' item. However, looking at the preferences, it's a daunting task as no single liked item is universal, and someone's like is always someone else's dislike. It's even made more complex considering we have 40 individuals with overlapping yet conflicting preferences. The preferences are too conflicting, and there seems to be no possible combination to satisfy everyone's likes and dislikes in this 