n\nLet's identify if there's at least one item that everyone who dislikes shawarma likes or is neutral about, which might allow us to put shawarma in 'not_orderable' directly. However, people like Bao, Ty, Kit, Zoe, Mel, Ava, Ying, Rina, Ula, Raj, Qi, Rohan, Pia and Anil, have likes that conflict with others' dislikes. We can observe a similar issue with every other item mentioned, which means single item solution doesn't work.\n\nTherefore, to satisfy the preferences of all individuals, we need to aim to find a combination of items that can be distributed across both 'orderable' and 'not_orderable' lists.\n\nAfter carefully reviewing the preferences and attempting to accommodate at least one like or dislike per individual without creating a conflict, we realize that there is no possible combination to satisfy all participants' likes and dislikes. There are conflicting preferences for items where each item liked by someone is also disliked by another person, and vice versa.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that no combination satisfies all, resulting in:\n\n```python\norderable=[], not_orderable=[]\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 711, 'num_completion_tokens': 617}
