n- curry: Om, Taj, Lin, Raj (4 people)\n- frittata: Hui, Sky, Raj (3 people)\n- muffins: Hui, Sky, Ula, Riya (4 people)\n- custard: Liv, Li, Riya (3 people)\n- fries: Liv, Li (2 people)\n- pie: Sky (1 person)\n- couscous: Li (1 person)\n\nStep 1: Let's look for disliked food overlaps.\nCurry is disliked by 4 people and no one who likes it dislikes something else that could allow us to not order curry. So it's a candidate for the 'not_orderable' list. That will satisfy Om, Taj, Lin, and Raj.\n\nStep 2: Since shawarma is liked by the most people (8), and none of the individuals who like shawarma have it on their dislike list, it could be placed in the 'orderable' list to satisfy all those people.\n\nStep 3: Check for any conflicts.\nNo conflict in adding curry to 'not_orderable' since it satisfies multiple dislikes. Similarly, no conflict in adding shawarma to 'orderable' as it satisfies multiple likes.\n\nStep 4: Address individual requirements.\nUla is the only person whose likes and dislikes conflict with both 'orderable' (shawarma) and 'not_orderable' (curry) items chosen. Ula dislikes shawarma. Thus, it is not possible to satisfy Ula whilst also having shawarma on the 'orderable' list.\n\nConclusion:\nSince we cannot satisfy all participants due to Ula's conflicting requirements, we have to output empty lists for both 'orderable' and 'not_orderable'.\n\nThe final solution is:\n```\norderable=[], not_orderable=[]\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 413, 'num_completion_tokens': 629}
