 list or at least one item they dislike that can be placed on the 'not_orderable' list, while ensuring there's no overlap between the two lists. Let's try to satisfy these criteria by developing the lists iteratively.\n\nFirst step is to find common dislikes that could be placed in 'not_orderable' list, as this would automatically satisfy those who dislike those foods.\n\nDisliked by multiple people (and not liked by anyone else):\n- Tacos are disliked by Jon, Joe, Nic, Ada, Aarav, Bea, Ansh, and Vic. \n- Salad is disliked by Dan, Nic, Kavi, Zac, and Ben.\n- Caramel is disliked by Arun, Zara, and Gus.\n- Pizza is disliked by Bea, Ansh, Zara, and Kavi.\n- Quiche is disliked by Ada, Om, Aarav, Ben, and Feng.\n\nSince tacos are disliked by the most number of people and not listed as liked by any other members in the list, they're a candidate for 'not_orderable'. Thus, we can put tacos in the 'not_orderable' list. However, for the other dislikes, they are liked by at least one person, hence they cannot be in 'not_orderable' list.\n\nLet's go ahead and put tacos into 'not_orderable':\n\nnot_orderable = ['tacos']\n\nWith tacos in the 'not_orderable' list, this satisfies Jon, Joe, Nic, Ada, Aarav, Bea, Ansh, and Vic. We do not need to consider their preferences further because they have at least one dislike in the 'not_orderable' list.\n\nNext, we should check if all remaining individuals can be satisfied with an 'orderable' food item that they all like, and which no one dislikes.\n\nOrderable candidates based on remaining likes:\n- Biscuits are liked by Lex, Dan, Isha, Hui, but disliked by Ian, Arun, Naina.\n- Baklava is liked by Joe, Veer, Nic, Bea, Feng, but disliked by Lex, Dan.\n- Cake is liked by Jon, Isha, Hui, but disliked by Ada, Zac, Gus, Naina.\n- Tandoori is liked by Jon, Isha, Mel, but disliked by Joe, Veer, Kim, Vic.\n- Caramel is liked by Kim, Tia, Leo, Kavi, Aarav, Lan, Feng, Ansh, Mel, Yan, Ben, but disliked by Zara, Gus.\n- Biscuits, Cake, Tandoori, Pizza, and Quiche are all liked and disliked by multiple people, unable to be placed in 'orderable' list.\n\nLooking at the likes and considering the dislikes, caramel is the only item liked by several people which is not invalidated by being disliked by another person whose dislikes are already featured in 'not_orderable' (caramel is disliked by Arun, Zara, and Gus, but they don't conflict with our current 'not_orderable' list).\n\nTherefore, we can include caramel in the 'orderable' list to satisfy the remaining individuals.\n\norderable = ['caramel']\n\nFinally, we check if we have satisfied all the criteria for each individual with our current lists. Everyone who liked tacos has been satisfied by their dislikes, and everyone who likes caramel (that isn't already satisfied) is satisfied now that it is in the 'orderable' list.\n\nSo, the final solution is:\n\norderable = ['caramel'], not_orderable = ['tacos']", 'num_prompt_tokens': 565, 'num_completion_tokens': 758}
Data Sample: {'num_vars': 10, 'num_clauses': 40, 'formula': [[-7, -2, 6], [2, -10, -5], [6, 7, -4], [1, -3, 6], [-7, 6, -10], [3, 4, 8], [-9, -7, -4], [8, 1, 10], [10, -3, -5], [-3, -9, -6], [3, -5, 4], [-7, 5, 2], [4, 1, 7], [-5, 4, -2], [3, -5, 6], [7, 1, 4], [2, -1, -6], [-3, 1, -10], [-4, -10, 7], [-6, -2, -5], [9, 7, 5], [2, -3, 5], [4, -1, -6], [4, 10, -3], [-7, -5, -8], [-4, -5, -3], [-1, -10, 3], [4, 1, -10], [-5, 2, 1], [5, 1, -9], [3, 2, 8], [-6, -9, -7], [5, 10, -4], [-7, -10, 5], [-3, 1, -2], [10, -8, 5], [6, -2, -3], [-1, 9, -6], [-7, 1, -10], [3, -10, 9]], 'is_sat': False, 'preferences': 'Tom: Likes roti. Dislikes nachos, soup. Lia: Likes soup. Dislikes omelette, tiramisu. Qi: Likes roti, nachos. Dislikes pizza. Chen: Likes frittata, roti. Dislikes tacos. Jay: Likes roti. Dislikes nachos, omelette. Zoe: Likes tacos, pizza, cake. Chao: Dislikes spaghetti, nachos, pizza. Wei: Likes cake, frittata, omelette. Max: Likes omelette. Dislikes tacos, tiramisu. Joy: Dislikes tacos, spaghetti, roti. Hui: Likes tacos, pizza. Dislikes tiramisu. Rob: Likes tiramisu, soup. Dislikes nachos. Veer: Likes pizza, frittata, nachos. Vic: Likes pizza. Dislikes tiramisu, soup. Ansh: Likes tacos, roti. Dislikes tiramisu. Naina: Likes nachos, frittata, pizza. Cy: Likes soup. Dislikes frittata, roti. Rina: Likes frittata. Dislikes tacos, omelette. Lex: Likes nachos. Dislikes pizza, omelette. Gus: Dislikes roti, soup, tiramisu. Liv: Likes spaghetti, nachos, tiramisu. Du: Likes soup, tiramisu. Dislikes tacos. Pam: Likes pizza. Dislikes frittata, roti. Rohan: Likes pizza, omelette. Dislikes tacos. Bea: Dislikes nachos, tiramisu, cake. Abe: Dislikes pizza, tiramisu, tacos. Ravi: Likes tacos. Dislikes frittata, omelette. Jan: Likes pizza, frittata. Dislikes omelette. Ben: Likes soup, frittata. Dislikes tiramisu. Nita: Likes tiramisu, frittata. Dislikes spaghetti. Eva: Likes tacos, soup, cake. Jim: Dislikes roti, spaghetti, nachos. May: Likes tiramisu, omelette. Dislikes pizza. Val: Likes tiramisu. Dislikes nachos, omelette. Cal: Likes frittata. Dislikes tacos, soup. Yan: Likes omelette, tiramisu. Dislikes cake. Pat: Likes roti. Dislikes soup, tacos. Xiu: Likes spaghetti. Dislikes frittata, roti. Ned: Likes frittata. Dislikes nachos, omelette. Ty: Likes tacos, spaghetti. Dislikes omelette. ', 'menu_items': ['frittata', 'soup', 'tacos', 'pizza', 'tiramisu', 'roti', 'nachos', 'cake', 'spaghetti', 'omelette'], 'gpt_out': "To find the solution, we will need to go through various considerations. I will start by identifying any direct conflicts where a food item is liked by someone and disliked by someone else. Any such conflicts immediately disqualify that food item from being ordered for everyone.\n\nTo begin, let's tally the likes and dislikes and look for any food that is universally liked or disliked.\n\n## Step 1: Tallying preferences\n\nLike tally:\n- roti: liked by many, disliked directly by Joy and Cy (conflict)\n- nachos: liked and disliked by several (conflict)\n- soup: liked by some, disliked by Tom and Pat (conflict)\n- frittata: liked by several, disliked by Cal, etc. (conflict)\n- tacos: liked and disliked by several (conflict)\n- pizza: liked by many, disliked by Chao, Abe, etc. (conflict)\n- cake: liked by some, disliked by Bea (conflict)\n- spaghetti: liked by some, disliked by Chao, etc. (conflict)\n- tiramisu: liked and disliked by several (conflict)\n- omelette: liked by some, disliked by even more (conflict)\n\nRight off the bat, we can see that every item is part of a conflict. This means we are not going to find any food item that can be universally ordered or not ordered.\n\nHowever, we have a single goal that should lead us to a solution: **Every person must be satisfied by having at least one liked food that is orderable, or one disliked food that is not orderable**. Let's see how we might achieve that.\n\n## Step 2: Finding a solution\n\nSince each person must be satisfied by finding at least one liked item in the 'orderable' list or one disliked item in the 'not_orderable' list, we can try to find any item that is liked by at least someone and not disliked by others, or vice versa.\n\n### Finding 'orderable' items\n\n- **roti**: Liked by Tom, Qi, Chen, Jay, Ansh but disliked by Joy and Cy, cannot be in 'orderable'.\n- **nachos**: Liked by Qi, Lex, Naina, Veer, Liv but disliked by Tom, Jay, Bea, Chao, and Jim, cannot be in 'orderable'.\n- **soup**: Liked by Lia, Rob, Cy, Ben, Eva, Du but disliked by Tom and Pat, cannot be in 'orderable'.\n- **frittata**: Liked by Chen, Wei, Ned, Rina, Jan, Ben, Cal but disliked by others, cannot be in 'orderable'.\n- **tacos**: Liked by Zoe, Ansh, Hui, Eva, Ravi, Ty but disliked by many, cannot be in 'orderable'.\n- **pizza**: Liked by many but disliked by Chao, Abe, Pam, May, cannot be in 'orderable'.\n- **cake**: Liked by Zoe, Wei, Eva but disliked by Bea, cannot be in 'orderable'.\n- **spaghetti**: Liked by Liv, Xiu, Ty but disliked by Chao, Jim, Nita, cannot be in 'orderable'.\n- **tiramisu**: Liked by many but disliked by more, cannot be in 'orderable'\n- **omelette**: Liked by many but disliked by even more, cannot be in 'orderable'.\n\nNo direct inclusion in 'orderable' is possible given the conflicting preferences, so let's move on to finding 'not_orderable' items.\n\n### Finding 'not_orderable' items\n\nNow, we look for items that, if they are not orderable, will satisfy a person because it is an item they dislike.\n\n- **roti**: Disliking it are Joy and Cy. Everyone else who likes it can have an alternative, as they also like other things not disliked by others.\n- **nachos**: Disliking it are Tom, Jay, Bea, Chao, and Jim. Qi likes roti and nachos, but since roti can't be orderable due to conflicts, we can't use nachos for this purpose either. Others have additional likes.\n- **soup**: Disliking it are Tom and Pat. Tom also dislikes nachos, but we can satisfy him by having nachos in the 'not_orderable' list. Pat also dislikes tacos and roti, but since roti can't be orderable, she would not be satisfied if soup isn't orderable.\n- And so on for the rest of the list.\n\n### Conclusion\n\nGiven the complexity and extensive conflicts in food preferences, it's clear that it's impossible to satisfy everyone with the 'orderable' and 'not_orderable' lists given the current constraints. Each item liked by one or more individuals is disliked by another, creating a situation where no single item can be universally satisfactory.\n\nHence, the final solution would be:\n\n```python\norderable = [], not_orderable = []\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 737, 'num_completion_tokens': 1040}
