g "toast" and "udon" to the \'orderable\' list because everyone who dislikes those items is either already satisfied by "tacos" or will be satisfied by "pho" in the \'not_orderable\' list.\n- Remaining items disliked by multiple individuals (pho, toast) can be on the \'not_orderable\' list if it doesn\'t compromise anyone\'s satisfaction.\n\nFinalizing our lists as such:\n\n`orderable=["tacos", "toast", "udon"]`\n`not_orderable=["pho", "wonton"]`\n\nEveryone who likes tacos is already satisfied by the orderable list. Those who dislike tacos like at least one other item (toast or udon) from the orderable list or dislike pho, which is on the not_orderable list. All individuals who like pho like at least one item from the orderable list (e.g., tacos or udon), and all individuals who dislike pho are satisfied by having at least one like on orderable or a dislike on not_orderable.\n\nFinal output:\n```python\norderable=["tacos", "toast", "udon"], not_orderable=["pho", "wonton"]\n```', 'num_prompt_tokens': 573, 'num_completion_tokens': 1289}
