e need to identify a strategy that could satisfy all people based on their likes and dislikes.\n\nConsidering the likes and dislikes, we can use "shawarma" as a key to potentially satisfy a large group of people who like it and place it on the \'orderable\' list. However, due to the number of people who dislike shawarma, this means we need to satisfy those people with their likes or other dislikes.\n\nTo start, we place "shawarma" in the \'orderable\' list due to the high number of people liking it, and we\'ll make sure to satisfy those who dislike it through other means:\n\n- For Leo, who dislikes shawarma, we need to include pho or udon in the \'orderable\' list.\n- For Diya, who dislikes kimchi, placing kimchi in the \'not_orderable\' list would satisfy her.\n- For Kim, who also dislikes macaron, adding macaron to the \'not_orderable\' list would not work as it conflicts with many people liking macaron, so Kim will not be satisfied.\n- For Bea, macaron is a dislike too, again causing a conflict with many likes.\n- For Mel, who dislikes baklava, placing baklava in the \'not_orderable\' list could work.\n- For Sal, who dislikes shawarma, we can satisfy them with pho or korma in the \'orderable\' list.\n- For Wei, pie being \'orderable\' would satisfy them.\n- For Ana, udon or korma being \'orderable\' would be needed.\n- For Taj who dislikes kimchi and macaron, kimchi in \'not_orderable\' is a possible choice.\n- For Hui, who dislikes kimchi and baklava, kimchi in \'not_orderable\' could satisfy them.\n\nWe quickly encounter insurmountable conflicts, such as Kim and Bea\'s dislike of macaron which conflicts with many likes of macaron.\n\nGiven these conflicts, it becomes clear that there is no combination of items that can satisfy everyone in the group according to the constraints. Two major points of satisfaction conflict are macaron and shawarma – both liked by many but also disliked by some.\n\nThus, we must conclude that it\'s not possible to construct \'ordera