m unsatisfied.\n\nFirst, let's map out participants' preferences to see if we can identify any initial items for the 'orderable' and 'not_orderable' lists. To do that, we must find items that all liking individuals can agree upon and items that disliked by individuals not liked by anyone else.\n\nStarting with dislikes, let's check if there are any items that only appear in dislikes and not in any likes:\n- Fries are liked by Jon, Lex, Nic, Geet, Tia, Shan, Anil, Isha, Max, so we can't place it in 'not_orderable' as it's liked by several participants.\n- Nachos are liked by Geet, Jay, Yue, Ty, Max, Isha, Zan, so we can't place it in 'not_orderable'.\n- Burrito is liked by Jon, Zara, Yue, Tao, Ian, Gen, Zan, Isha, so we can't place it in 'not_orderable'.\n\nNow, let’s look at likes to place some in 'orderable' to ensure at least one liked food item for each participant:\n- Salmon is liked by Val, Lex, Li, Anil, Ty, Ada, Gen, Ian, which none of their dislikes are universally disliked.\n- Cake is liked by Lex, Nic, Li, Anil, Nita, Ian, which again none of their dislikes are universally disliked (lasagna is liked by several participants).\n- Lasagna is liked by Zara, Li, Jay, Lia, Tao, Zan, Max, which cannot be 'orderable' since Lia dislikes salmon and lasagna, and if lasagna is made 'orderable', Lia won’t have any satisfaction criterion met.\n- Fries can't be 'orderable' because Meg dislikes only the items fries, lasagna, and burrito and none of them can be placed into 'not_orderable'.\n- Nachos can't be 'orderable' because Wei dislikes nachos alongside lasagna and salmon, which both can’t be 'not_orderable'.\n- Burrito can't be 'orderable' because several participants have burrito as a dislike, and we can't place its counterpart likes exclusively in 'not_orderable'.\n\nWith the complexity of the preferences, it's challenging to satisfy everyone because quite a few items are both intensely liked and disliked. In this case, we do not find a straightforward solution that