 items that are universally liked by some but not disliked by any, or universally disliked by some but liked by none.\n\nSo, let\'s find the orderable items following these criteria:\n\n- "wonton" can be orderable because it\'s liked by some, but not expressly disliked by anyone – actually, it is, so we skip that.\n- "peach" can potentially be orderable because some like it and only a few dislike it – actually, it\'s also disliked, so we skip.\n- "shrimps" could be orderable because, again, it is liked by some and only a few dislike it. We will consider it later.\n\nFor \'not_orderable\', let\'s use a similar process:\n\n- "burger" could be not_orderable as it is disliked by many. We will consider this later since it\'s liked by some.\n- "cake" cannot be not_orderable as it is liked by many.\n- "kimchi" can potentially be not_orderable as it\'s disliked by some but liked by others, so it\'s excluded.\n- "salmon" is liked and disliked by too many to be in either list without causing dissatisfaction.\n\nWe now have two items to consider for orderable ("shrimps") and not_orderable ("burger"). Let\'s see if adding them satisfies all:\n\nOrderable:\n- "shrimps": Satisfies Sam, Bao, Mia, and Ned.\n\nNot_orderable:\n- "burger": Satisfies Oli, Nita, Sia, Taj, Bao, Chao, Ian, Naina.\n\nAll individuals who have \'shrimps\' as a dislike have another item that could be put in the not_orderable list to satisfy them. The ones for \'burger\' need to be checked to have alternative likes, and that creates a conflict due to burger being liked by Jon, Jan, and Qi. So, we cannot use burger in not_orderable list.\n\nAs we are not able to find a universally satisfying combination of orderable and not_orderable items, we can conclude that it is impossible to create lists that satisfy all individuals. Therefore, the solution to this problem must be empty lists for both \'orderable\' and \'not_orderable\':\n\n```python\norderable = []\nnot_orderable = []\n```', 'num_prompt_tokens': 721, 'num_completion_tokens': 993}
