d.**\norderable = [\'caramel\', \'soup\']\n\n**Let\'s try to find conflicts with adding \'soup\' to orderable:**\n  - Feng dislikes soup, that means if we add soup, Feng should have \'bruschetta\' as not_orderable to be satisfied.\n  - Wei also dislikes soup, so we need to have \'wonton\' in orderable or \'shawarma\' in not_orderable which is already there. So Wei is satisfied.\n  - Raj dislikes soup and muffins, but he is okay with \'caramel\'. So he is satisfied.\n  - May also dislikes caramel, but she is happy with \'pie\' or \'soup\', however, \'pie\' is disliked by many. Hence, we can\'t add \'pie\' to the orderable list here as it will dissatisfy others.\n  - Pam dislikes soup and shawarma, but \'shawarma\' is in \'not_orderable\'. She\'s satisfied.\n  - Ping dislikes soup, but \'caramel\' is in \'orderable\'.\n\nBased on this analysis, there are no conflicts with adding \'soup\' to \'orderable\', and we can do so to satisfy the maximum number of individuals.\n\n**Step 5: Check for any remaining unsatisfied individuals and attempt to add their likes to \'orderable\' while ensuring no conflicts with dislikes.**\n\nProceeding through the remaining individuals:\n\n- We look for \'muffins\', \'wonton\', \'pie\', and \'bruschetta\' to see if they can fit into the \'orderable\' or \'not_orderable\' without causing dissatisfaction. However, each of them is either liked or disliked by some individuals which poses a difficulty.\n\n**Step 6: Solve the remaining conflicts.**\n\nA key note here is that we cannot solve for individuals who have an exclusive like that is somebody\'s dislike. Our current \'orderable\' list (\'caramel\' and \'soup\') does not universally satisfy all individuals, with some individuals like Pia being dissatisfied. Given this complex scenario and limitations due to the mutual exclusivity of likes and dislikes, it might actually be impossible to find a perfect combination that will satisfy everyone.\n\nWe\'ll have to conclude that no combination c