l\n- Curry & Caramel conflict: Nic, Arun, Anil, Pat dislike Caramel but others like it; Nic, Xiu, Lex dislike Curry but others like it.\n\nDislikes:\n- Nachos: Isha, Oli, Ansh, Hui, Qi, Ned\n- Caramel: Nic, Eli, Anil, Pat\n- Curry: Nic, Pia, Sky, Xiu, Lex\n- Tiramisu: Shan, Oli, Jon, Tia, Qi, Ned\n- Peach: Jon, Eli, Om, Anil, Jay, Mel, Ned\n- Kimchi: Oli, Jon, Arun, Tia, Om, Pat, Mel, Qi, Lex\n- Kimchi & Curry conflict: Lex likes both but Qi dislikes both.\n\nFirst, let's check for potential orderable items that satisfy the most number of people without conflicting interests:\n\n1. Curry is liked by the largest number, however, it is disliked by Nic, Pia, Sky, Xiu, and Lex.\n2. Caramel is also well-liked, but it's disliked by Nic, Eli, Anil, and Pat.\n3. Kimchi is liked by several, but disliked by many as well.\n\nLooking at dislikes, we have conflicts with Tiramisu and Peach being widely disliked.\n\nNow let's try to construct lists that satisfy the conditions:\n\n- If we try to make Curry orderable, we will not satisfy Nic, Pia, Sky, Xiu, or Lex.\n- If we try to make Kimchi orderable, we will not satisfy Oli, Jon, Arun, Tia, Om, Pat, Mel, Qi, or Lex.\n- If we try to make Caramel orderable, Nic, Eli, Anil, and Pat will be dissatisfied.\n\nThere is a standoff between Curry and Caramel, where several people like one but dislike the other. Therefore, we must proceed by making sure each person is satisfied by either having something they like in the 'orderable' list or something they dislike in the 'not_orderable' list.\n\nOne satisfying solution could be:\n\nOrderable: Peach (because it is liked and not disliked by a majority).\nNot_orderable: Tiramisu (because it is widely disliked and liking it does not conflict with liking Peach).\n\nWe'll choose Peach as it's liked by Zara, Kavi, and Sky, and it doesn't conflict with other likes as much as other foods. (For example, nobody who likes Peach dislikes Caramel or Curry.)\n\nFor Tiramisu, Shan and Tia, who dislike Tiramisu, like Curry and have no issues with Peach. Similarly, Oli, Jon, Qi, and Ned have no issue with Peach. Om who likes Tiramisu also dislikes Peach, but since the dislike is not as strong as his like for Tiramisu, his satisfaction criteria will be met by having Tiramisu as 'not_orderable'.\n\nFollowing this logic, everyone should be satisfied as follows:\n- Everyone who likes Peach is satisfied by having Peach as 'orderable'.\n- Everyone who dislikes Tiramisu (as well as Oli and Qi who dislike both Peach and Tiramisu) is satisfied with Tiramisu as 'not_orderable'.\n- The rest of the individuals' satisfaction is either unaffected by these choices or is satisfied by the dislikes being honored.\n\nLet's output the solution:\n\n```python\norderable=['peach'], not_orderable=['tiramisu']\n```", 'num_prompt_tokens': 503, 'num_completion_tokens': 876}
Data Sample: {'num_vars': 8, 'num_clauses': 24, 'formula': [[5, 1, 6], [-3, 7, -5], [-6, 7, 5], [5, -1, -8], [7, -5, 3], [8, -3, -4], [-7, 8, -1], [-8, -4, 3], [8, 7, 6], [-5, 3, -2], [-7, 8, -1], [2, -3, 6], [-5, 1, -7], [3, 1, 7], [3, -6, 2], [6, -3, 7], [-6, -2, -7], [-1, -2, -3], [2, 3, 6], [-5, 1, -4], [-3, -6, 7], [-5, 2, 1], [-6, -1, -8], [-4, 8, 5]], 'is_sat': True, 'preferences': 'Zan: Likes peach, risotto, tandoori. Tao: Likes falafel. Dislikes lasagna, peach. Joe: Likes falafel, peach. Dislikes tandoori. Lex: Likes peach. Dislikes risotto, pizza. Ping: Likes falafel, lasagna. Dislikes peach. Jun: Likes pizza. Dislikes lasagna, ratatouille. Lou: Likes pizza. Dislikes falafel, risotto. Xiu: Likes lasagna. Dislikes pizza, ratatouille. Feng: Likes pizza, falafel, tandoori. Lei: Likes lasagna. Dislikes peach, baklava. Gus: Likes pizza. Dislikes falafel, risotto. Om: Likes baklava, tandoori. Dislikes lasagna. Ivy: Likes risotto. Dislikes peach, falafel. Cy: Likes lasagna, risotto, falafel. Isha: Likes lasagna, baklava. Dislikes tandoori. Ada: Likes tandoori, falafel. Dislikes lasagna. Leo: Dislikes tandoori, baklava, falafel. Bea: Dislikes risotto, baklava, lasagna. Rohan: Likes baklava, lasagna, tandoori. Val: Likes risotto. Dislikes peach, ratatouille. Gia: Likes falafel. Dislikes lasagna, tandoori. Kai: Likes baklava, risotto. Dislikes peach. Hui: Dislikes tandoori, risotto, pizza. Ru: Likes pizza, peach. Dislikes ratatouille. ', 'menu_items': ['risotto', 'baklava', 'lasagna', 'ratatouille', 'peach', 'tandoori', 'falafel', 'pizza'], 'gpt_out': "First, let's analyze the food items that are liked and disliked to see if we can determine a starting point. We need to find items that can satisfy the conditions and ensure that no person's likes are in the 'not_orderable' list and no person's dislikes are in the 'orderable' list.\n\nWe will create two sets for 'orderable' and 'not_orderable', and start filling them with items. If at any point we encounter a contradiction (an item cannot logically go into either list without upsetting someone), then we know we have reached an impasse and there is no satisfactory combination of 'orderable' and 'not_orderable' foods; our output would then be empty lists.\n\nLet's find an item that is liked by some but not disliked by anyone else as a reasonable candidate for 'orderable':\n\n1. Peach: Liked by Zan, Joe, Lex, Ru; Disliked by Tao, Ping, Lei, Ivy.\n2. Risotto: Liked by Zan, Ivy, Val, Kai; Disliked by Lex, Lou, Bea, Hui.\n3. Tandoori: Liked by Zan, Feng, Om, Rohan; Disliked by Joe, Isha, Leo, Hui.\n4. Falafel: Liked by Tao, Joe, Ping, Feng, Cy, Ada, Gia; Disliked by Lou, Leo, Ivy, Gus.\n5. Lasagna: Liked by Ping, Xiu, Lei, Cy, Isha, Rohan; Disliked by Tao, Jun, Om, Leo, Bea, Gia, Ada.\n6. Pizza: Liked by Jun, Lou, Feng, Gus, Ru; Disliked by Lex, Xiu, Hui.\n7. Baklava: Liked by Om, Isha, Rohan, Kai; Disliked by Lei, Leo, Bea.\n8. Ratatouille: No one likes ratatouille but many dislike it (Jun, Xiu, Val, Ru).\n\nWe can't initially place any liked food in 'orderable' without encountering someone who dislikes it. Therefore, let's see if we can place items in the 'not_orderable' list that are disliked by some but liked by none:\n\n1. Ratatouille has no likes but several dislikes, so it can be 'not_orderable' without upsetting anyone.\n\nNow let's use this information to start building our lists. We will try to place more items in 'not_orderable' with the idea that it seems less contentious than 'orderable':\n\n- 'not_orderable': [ratatouille]\n\nWe can see that the participants who dislike ratatouille do not have other dislikes that conflict with placing ratatouille in 'not_orderable'. So we can continue with ratatouille in that list.\n\nNow we are trying to see if adding ratatouille to 'not_orderable' can help us with any other items:\n\n1. Jun, Xiu, and Ru dislike both lasagna and ratatouille, but with ratatouille in 'not_orderable', they can now be satisfied without lasagna being present in 'orderable'.\n2. Val dislikes both peach and ratatouille, and with ratatouille in 'not_orderable', they don't require peach to be in 'orderable'.\n\nWith ratatouille off the table for these individuals, let's try finding a food item that can go into 'orderable', considering our current 'not_orderable' list:\n\n- 'orderable': [???]\n\nTo find a candidate for 'orderable', we need to find a food liked by some that isn't disliked by anyone else who hasn't been satisfied by the 'not_orderable' list. The dislikes have to be looked at in conjunction with the already 'not_orderable' items. For example, since Joe dislikes tandoori and is not satisfied by ratatouille being 'not_orderable', we cannot place tandoori in 'orderable'.\n\nLooking at likes and accounting for dislikes mitigated by ratatouille being 'not_orderable':\n\n- Peach: Remaining people who dislike peach and aren't satisfied by ratatouille are Tao and Lei.\n- Risotto: Remaining people who dislike risotto and aren't satisfied by ratatouille are Lex, Lou, Bea, Hui.\n- Tandoori: Remaining people who dislike tandoori and aren't satisfied by ratatouille are Joe, Isha, Leo, Hui.\n- Falafel: Remaining people who dislike falafel and aren't satisfied by ratatouille are Lou, Leo, Ivy, Gus; Tao and Gia are satisfied by ratatouille being 'not_orderable'.\n- Lasagna: Remaining people who dislike lasagna and aren't satisfied by ratatouille are Tao, Ado.\n- Pizza: No remaining unsatisfied dislikers with ratatouille 'not_orderable'.\n- Baklava: No remaining unsatisfied dislikers with ratatouille 'not_orderable'.\n\nPizza and Baklava could potentially be placed in 'orderable'. However, since no one dislikes baklava and hasn't been satisfied by ratatouille, we'll add it to 'orderable':\n\n- 'orderable': [baklava]\n- 'not_orderable': [ratatouille]\n\nLet's verify if everyone can be satisfied now:\n\nAdding baklava to 'orderable' satisfies Om, Isha, Rohan, and Kai. Since there's no conflict with ratatouille being 'not_orderable', they are now content.\n\nZan, who likes peach, risotto, and tandoori, is not yet satisfied. Since Tao and Lei are not satisfied by ratatouille being 'not_orderable' and they dislike peach, we cannot put peach in 'orderable'. Joe, Lex, Ping, Jun, Lou, Xiu, Feng, Cy, Ada, Leo, Bea, Val, Gia, Hui, Ru are in similar situations with their various likes and dislikes. They cannot be satisfied with just baklava in 'orderable' and ratatouille in 'not_orderable' as all have conflicting foods they dislike and needs not met.\n\nWithout a second item in 'orderable' that satisfies the likes of someone without conflicting with the dislikes of the already satisfied individuals (Om, Isha, Rohan, Kai), or the unsatisfied ones, we are stuck.\n\nGiven these conditions, it seems impossible to find a food combination that satisfies everyone's likes and dislikes as per our criteria. Therefore, our output is:\n\n`orderable=[], not_orderable=[]`", 'num_prompt_tokens': 541, 'num_completion_tokens': 1381}
