n the ‘orderable’ list. We have to ensure that those who like \'espresso\' and do not like \'korma\' have another liked item in the \'orderable\' list or disliked item in the \'not_orderable\' list.\n\n\'Waffles\' is liked by a few, but disliked by many. However, every person who likes \'waffles\' also likes something else that isn\'t universally disliked, so \'waffles\' can potentially be in the \'not_orderable\' list as long as everyone who likes it is still satisfied by another \'orderable\' item or \'not_orderable\' item they dislike.\n\n\'Cheesecake\' is liked by many and nobody has mentioned disliking it. It seems safe to add \'cheesecake\' to the \'orderable\' list.\n\n\'Risotto\' is liked by many, but \'Wei\', \'Ravi\', and \'Raj\' dislike \'risotto\' without liking \'korma\' or \'cheesecake\'. \'Risotto\' cannot be in the \'orderable\' list unless we can satisfy these individuals with other items.\n\n\'Frittata\' and \'shrimps\' are liked and disliked across the board, so we cannot place them in either list directly.\n\nLastly, \'risotto\' is problematic as it is liked by many but also disliked. To include it in \'orderable\', we must ensure that everyone who dislikes \'risotto\' is satisfied with other items. Since we are required to satisfy all people, and \'risotto\' cannot satisfy \'Wei\', \'Ravi\', and \'Raj\' (since they don\'t have other items that are liked by them and not disliked by others), we must reconsider our choices.\n\nAfter reviewing preferences and not finding any viable combination that avoids conflict, it doesn\'t look like there is a way to satisfy all people based on the criteria. Every attempt to satisfy one group seems to leave out others.\n\nGiven these constraints, the result is that there is no combination of \'orderable\' and \'not_orderable\' that can satisfy all participants:\n\n```python\norderable = []\nnot_orderable = []\n```\n\nWe come to the conclusion that it\'s impossible to formulate lists that satisfy all individuals\