ori", dislikes "sushi"): satisfied by \'orderable\' ("cheese")\n- Vic ("hummus", "sushi", dislikes "cheese"): satisfied by \'orderable\' ("cheese"), even though they dislike "cheese," and it\'s on the \'orderable\' list, they aren\'t unsatisfied as no direct conflicts are caused (i.e., none of their dislikes are on the \'orderable\' list)\n- Rohan ("tandoori", "hummus", dislikes "sushi"): satisfied by \'orderable\' ("cheese"), because we find a like represented there\n- Ben ("sushi", "tandoori", "hummus"): satisfied by \'orderable\' ("cheese"), because we find a like represented there\n- Zac ("paneer", dislikes "hummus", "sushi"): satisfied by \'orderable\' ("cheese") and \'not_orderable\' ("paneer")\n- Val ("cheese", dislikes "tandoori", "hummus"): satisfied by \'orderable\' ("cheese")\n- Bea ("cheese", "tandoori", "hummus"): satisfied by \'orderable\' ("cheese")\n- Jun ("hummus", "cheese", dislikes "paneer"): satisfied by \'orderable\' ("cheese") and \'not_orderable\' ("paneer")\n- Gen ("sushi", "hummus", "paneer"): satisfied by \'not_orderable\' ("paneer")\n- Pam ("hummus", dislikes "tandoori", "paneer"): satisfied by \'not_orderable\' ("paneer")\n- Ula ("paneer", "hummus", dislikes "sushi"): satisfied by \'not_orderable\' ("paneer")\n- Jon ("paneer", "sushi", dislikes "hummus"): satisfied by \'orderable\' ("cheese") and \'not_orderable\' ("paneer")\n- Jim ("paneer", "sushi", dislikes "tandoori"): satisfied by \'orderable\' ("cheese") and \'not_orderable\' ("paneer")\n\nGiven this evaluation, each person is satisfied because, for each individual, either a liked item appears on the \'orderable\' list or a disliked item appears on the \'not_orderable\' list. Therefore, we have found a feasible solution:\n\n```python\norderable=[\'cheese\'], not_orderable=[\'paneer\']\n```', 'num_prompt_tokens': 529, 'num_completion_tokens': 1657}
