tems, which are liked by some but not disliked by anyone. However, here too, every item is disliked by at least one person.\n\nSince we do not have universally disliked or liked items, we need to find a compromise:\n\n1. We can prioritize likes over dislikes because a person can be satisfied if they have at least one liked item on the \'orderable\' list.\n2. We need to minimize conflicts where someone\'s like is another person\'s dislike.\n\nLet\'s start with items that are liked by many but disliked by a few (to satisfy the most):\n\n- Peach is liked by many but disliked only by Nic, Neil, Sky, Val, Ray, Zoe, Jun, Taj, Bea, Ian, Ying, Pia.\n- Lasagna is liked by many but disliked by Neil, Yan, Ray, Diya, Feng, Rob, Min, Eva, Mia, Nita, Pam.\n\nAs naan and roti are both liked and disliked by many, choosing either could leave significant numbers unsatisfied, however, since Zac has only 1 like which is "roti", it must be on the \'orderable\' list in order to satisfy him, because there is no dislike overlap with anyone who doesn\'t have any other likes. For such users (with a single like), we must ensure their liked item is on the \'orderable\' list:\n\n- Zac likes only \'roti\' (this has to be in \'orderable\').\n- Nic dislikes only \'naan\', \'guacamole\', and \'peach\' (we need to pick \'naan\' or \'guacamole\' for \'not_orderable\').\n\nGiven the conflict surrounding \'naan\', \'guacamole\', and \'roti\', we need to consider dislikes. To minimize the number of people left unsatisfied, we should prioritize items that are disliked by individuals who have other likes to ensure they can still be satisfied:\n\n- \'Guacamole\' is disliked by many individuals who have other likes, making it a candidate for \'not_orderable\'.\n- \'Naan\' is disliked by many, but not as many as \'guacamole\', and those who dislike it often have other likes. However, Yue, Ada, Wei, Xiu, Lou, Om, and Veer only dislike \'naan\' and \'guacamole\', so we cannot put \'guacamole\' in \'not_orderable\' without also putting \'naan\'.\n- \'Roti\' is disliked by Neil, Ansh, Hu, Neil, Zac, Isha, Chen, Lee, Gia, Ru, Ian, Nita, Mia, Liz, and Meg but all of them except for Neil and Zac have other likes we can add to the \'orderable\' to satisfy them. Therefore, we will satisfy Neil by adding \'lasagna\' to \'orderable\' and satisfy Zac by adding \'roti\'.\n\nGiven these considerations, we can create the following initial lists:\n\norderable: [\'peach\', \'lasagna\', \'roti\']\nnot_orderable: [\'guacamole\', \'naan\']\n\nWe will now iterate through every individual to make sure they are satisfied:\n\n- Zac is satisfied (likes \'roti\' which is in \'orderable\').\n- Sal is satisfied (likes \'peach\' which is in \'orderable\').\n- Neil is satisfied (likes \'lasagna\' which is in \'orderable\').\n- Many others are satisfied by \'peach\', \'lasagna\', or \'roti\' being in the \'orderable\' list; we won’t list each explicitly.\n- Sid, Sol, Sky, Sid, Tim, Nic, Lei, Jan, Lou, Val, Li, Chen, Zoe, Ivy, Ray, Pia, Ying, Jun, Taj, Ian, and Bea have dislikes in the \'not_orderable\' list.\n\nWe particularly should check individuals who only like items now on the \'not_orderable\' list or only dislike items now on the \'orderable\' list. If there\'s a conflict, we need to adjust the lists accordingly.\n\nGoing through the list again, with the initial \'orderable\' and \'not_orderable\' setup, all individuals seem to have at least one item they like on the \'orderable\' list or one item they dislike on the \'not_orderable\' list.\n\nThus, the final solution that satisfies all the conditions appears to be:\n\n```python\norderable=[\'peach\', \'lasagna\', \'roti\'], not_orderable=[\'guacamole\', \'naan\']\n```', 'num_prompt_tokens': 1379, 'num_completion_tokens': 1107}
