nsure that each has a different item satisfying their criteria.\n\n3. **Items that Must Be in \'Not_Orderable\' List**:\n    - Pho must be in \'Not_Orderable\' because Isha, Amy, Mel, and Val dislike it with no other likes to satisfy them.\n    - Fries must be in \'Not_Orderable\' because Gen and Feng dislike it with no other likes to satisfy them.\n\n4. **Items that Must Be in \'Orderable\' List**:\n    - Since pho cannot satisfy Joe, Min, Sue, Gen, and Bao, we must find other items to satisfy them. \n    - Joe can be satisfied with an "unspecified" item because his only like is pho, and that\'s not orderable. The same applies to Sue.\n    - Min can be satisfied with roti.\n    - Gen can be satisfied with shrimps.\n    - Bao can be satisfied with cheesecake.\n    - Ava and Ada can now be satisfied with fries, given pho is not orderable.\n    - Meg can be satisfied with either cheesecake or espresso.\n    - Mel can be satisfied with omelette.\n    - Yan can be satisfied with shrimps.\n    - Eva can be satisfied with shrimps.\n    - Cheesecake must be orderable to satisfy Cy.\n    - Gia can be satisfied with omelette.\n    - Jaya can be satisfied with espresso.\n    - Jun can be satisfied with roti since both pho and fries are not orderable.\n\n5. **Universal Check**:\n    - By assigning roti to the \'Orderable\' list, we satisfy Min and Jun. \n    - Shrimps will satisfy Gen, Yan, and Eva, so shrimps must be \'Orderable\'.\n    - Cheesecake satisfies Bao and Cy, so it goes to \'Orderable\'.\n    - If we put espresso in \'Orderable\' we satisfy Meg, Mel, and Jaya. Since Sue dislikes espresso, it must be \'orderable\' to satisfy her because pho is \'not_orderable\'.\n    - Now we satisfy Joe, Min, Sue, Gen, Bao, Ava, Ada, Meg, Mel, Yan, Eva, Gia, Jaya, Jun with their likes.\n\n6. **Items still not considered**:\n    - Omelette satisfies Mel and Gia, so it must be \'Orderable\'. This won\'t affect Val and Rohan negatively since their dislikes also include items that are already in the \'Not_Orderable\' list.\n    - Rohan will be satisfied as long as fries and shrimps are \'Not_Orderable\'. Cheesecake is orderable, but his preference can already be met regardless.\n    - Rob, being satisfied with the Not_Orderable list containing espresso, shrimps, and fries, won\'t be negatively affected by other inclusions in the \'Orderable\' list.\n\nHere\'s the final solution:\n\n```python\norderable = [\'roti\', \'shrimps\', \'cheesecake\', \'espresso\', \'omelette\']\nnot_orderable = [\'pho\', \'fries\']\n```', 'num_prompt_tokens': 477, 'num_completion_tokens': 782}
